/* chalkcast question bank: Human Physiology.
   Built from the BIO 005 Human Physiology course files: multiple choice from the recall card bank,
   application cases, true or false, select all that apply, and from-memory prompts from the practice exam items,
   and drawing prompts from the competency study guide, with a few built from the competency statements. Built by build005.js. Edit the sources and rebuild. */
window.CHALKCAST_BANK={"course":"Human Physiology","units":[{"system":"Chemical Foundations","comps":[{"id":"w1-water-properties","name":"Water and solution properties","can":"Explain how the polarity and hydrogen bonding of water determine solubility and identify whether a given solute is hydrophilic or hydrophobic.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"When NaCl dissolves in water, which part of the water molecule faces the Na+ ion?\nA) The hydrogens, because they carry the partial positive charge\nB) Either end, since water is not oriented around dissolved ions\nC) The oxygen, because it carries the partial negative charge\nD) The oxygen, because it carries the partial positive charge\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The oxygen, because it carries the partial negative charge","why":"Na+ is positive, so it attracts the partially negative oxygen ends of surrounding water molecules, forming a hydration shell that keeps the ion in solution. The hydrogens orient toward Cl- instead, since that ion is negative. Water molecules are strongly oriented around ions rather than randomly arranged, and the oxygen is partially negative, not positive."},{"t":"mc","q":"What type of bond forms between the partially negative oxygen of one water molecule and the partially positive hydrogen of a neighboring water molecule?\nA) A hydrogen bond  |  B) An ionic bond\nC) A nonpolar covalent bond  |  D) A polar covalent bond\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A hydrogen bond","why":"A hydrogen bond is a weak attraction between molecules, not a sharing of electrons, and it is what gives water its cohesion and high heat capacity. Polar covalent bonds are the strong bonds inside a single water molecule, between its own oxygen and hydrogens. Ionic bonds require full charges, and nonpolar covalent bonds involve equal sharing, neither of which describes the attraction between two water molecules."},{"t":"mc","q":"A solute is described as hydrophilic. What does that tell you about it?\nA) It is uncharged and nonpolar, so it separates from water\nB) It has one polar end and one nonpolar end\nC) It dissolves only in lipid, not in water\nD) It is polar or charged, so it interacts with water and dissolves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It is polar or charged, so it interacts with water and dissolves","why":"Hydrophilic literally means water loving, and the physical basis is that polar or charged groups can form hydrogen bonds or ion dipole attractions with water. An uncharged nonpolar solute is hydrophobic and is excluded from the water network. A molecule with one polar end and one nonpolar end is amphipathic, a separate category, and dissolving only in lipid describes a hydrophobic solute."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the term for a molecule that has both a polar region and a nonpolar region?\nA) Amphipathic  |  B) Hydrophilic\nC) Hydrophobic  |  D) Nonpolar\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Amphipathic","why":"Amphipathic molecules such as phospholipids and bile salts carry both kinds of region, which is exactly why they line up at oil and water boundaries and form membranes and micelles. Hydrophilic and hydrophobic each describe a molecule that is one way throughout. Nonpolar is a description of bonding, and a nonpolar molecule by definition lacks the polar region an amphipathic molecule has."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these blood solutes is hydrophobic and therefore travels bound to a carrier protein rather than dissolved freely in plasma?\nA) Glucose  |  B) Na+\nC) Urea  |  D) Cholesterol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cholesterol","why":"Cholesterol is a largely nonpolar steroid ring structure, so plasma water will not dissolve it and it is carried inside lipoproteins. Glucose carries many hydroxyl groups and hydrogen bonds well with water. Na+ is a charged ion surrounded by a hydration shell, and urea is a small polar molecule, so both dissolve directly in plasma without a carrier."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Cholesterol dissolves freely in plasma water because it is a small molecule.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Cholesterol is nonpolar and hydrophobic, so it does not dissolve in plasma water; it is carried inside lipoproteins.","why":"Solubility in water depends on polarity, not on size. Water dissolves solutes that offer charges or polar groups it can hydrogen bond with. Cholesterol is a ring of hydrocarbon with a single hydroxyl group, so water excludes it, and the body packages it in lipoprotein particles with a polar surface to move it through blood."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one water molecule big, with the partial charges marked, then draw four more hydrogen bonding to it. Beside that, drop in a sodium ion and a drop of oil. Show what the water does around each one, arrows for how the molecules turn. Underneath, one line: why the same property that dissolves the salt refuses the oil."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cross section of a cell membrane with water on both sides. Put four solutes on your drawing: glucose, sodium, oxygen, and a steroid hormone. Mark each one hydrophilic or hydrophobic, then draw where each one sits relative to the membrane and whether it can pass. One line under it on which property of the solute decided that, not which property of the membrane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the polarity and hydrogen bonding of water determine solubility and identify whether a given solute is hydrophilic or hydrophobic."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Water and solution properties. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-ph-buffers","name":"pH and buffers","can":"Define pH, state the normal pH range of arterial blood, and explain how a buffer pair resists a change in pH when acid or base is added.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Use the relationship pH = 6.1 + log (HCO3- divided by 0.03 times PCO2). A patient has HCO3- of 12 mEq/L and PCO2 of 40 mmHg. What is the approximate pH?\nA) About 7.4  |  B) About 7.1\nC) About 7.3  |  D) About 6.8\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 7.1","why":"Dissolved CO2 is 0.03 times 40, which is 1.2, so the ratio is 12 divided by 1.2, or 10, and log 10 is 1, giving 6.1 plus 1, or 7.1. A pH of 7.4 comes from the normal 20 to 1 ratio, which needs a bicarbonate near 24. A value of 7.3 would require a ratio of about 16, and 6.8 would require a ratio of about 5, well below what these numbers give."},{"t":"mc","q":"A strong acid is added to plasma. What happens to the bicarbonate concentration and the amount of CO2 formed?\nA) Bicarbonate rises and CO2 formation falls\nB) Both bicarbonate and CO2 rise\nC) Both bicarbonate and CO2 fall\nD) Bicarbonate is consumed and CO2 formation rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bicarbonate is consumed and CO2 formation rises","why":"Added H+ is mopped up by HCO3-, forming carbonic acid that breaks down to CO2 and water, so the buffer is spent as bicarbonate falls and CO2 is generated for the lungs to blow off. Bicarbonate cannot rise while it is being consumed by the added acid. The reaction converts one into the other, so they move in opposite directions, which rules out both rising or both falling."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person hyperventilates during a panic attack. Predict the direction of change in arterial PCO2 and in blood pH.\nA) PCO2 rises and pH falls  |  B) PCO2 falls and pH falls\nC) PCO2 rises and pH rises  |  D) PCO2 falls and pH rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) PCO2 falls and pH rises","why":"Blowing off CO2 removes the acid side of the carbonic acid and bicarbonate pair, so H+ is consumed as the reaction shifts back toward CO2 and pH rises, a respiratory alkalosis. Rising PCO2 with falling pH describes hypoventilation, the opposite situation. Any answer that has PCO2 and pH moving in the same direction misreads a respiratory change, since a change in CO2 always moves pH the other way."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has been vomiting for two days and has lost a large amount of stomach acid (HCl). Labs: plasma bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (22 to 26), PCO2 40 mmHg (35 to 45). Use pH = 6.1 + log (HCO3- divided by 0.03 times PCO2).\nPredict the direction of the patient's arterial pH and the change in ventilation that follows, and explain why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"pH rises above 7.45; ventilation decreases so that CO2 is retained and pH moves back toward normal","why":"Losing HCl removes H+ from the body, which leaves bicarbonate unopposed and its concentration rises. Dissolved CO2 is 0.03 times 40, or 1.2, so the ratio is 36 divided by 1.2, or 30. Log 30 is about 1.48, so pH is about 7.58. The buffer pair is out of balance toward the base member. Chemoreceptors sense the high pH and reduce ventilation, letting CO2 build up, which raises the acid member of the pair and lowers the ratio back toward 20 to 1."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with long standing lung disease cannot exhale CO2 normally. Labs: PCO2 70 mmHg (35 to 45), plasma bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (22 to 26). Use pH = 6.1 + log (HCO3- divided by 0.03 times PCO2). Log 17 is about 1.23.\nEstimate the arterial pH and explain what the raised bicarbonate tells you about how the body has responded.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 7.33; the kidneys have retained bicarbonate to offset the high CO2, but the compensation is not complete because pH is still below 7.35","why":"Dissolved CO2 is 0.03 times 70, or 2.1. The ratio is 36 divided by 2.1, about 17. Log 17 is about 1.23, so pH is about 7.33. The extra CO2 is the acid member of the buffer pair and it drives pH down. Over days the kidneys have added bicarbonate, the base member, which lifts the ratio from what it would have been (24 divided by 2.1, about 11, pH about 7.15) most of the way back. The pH is much better than it would be uncompensated but still slightly acidic."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A buffer prevents any change in pH when acid is added to a solution.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A buffer limits the change in pH; it binds most of the added H+, but the pH still shifts slightly and the buffer can be used up.","why":"A buffer pair works by having its base member take up added H+, converting it to the weak acid member. Each H+ bound is one less free in solution, so the pH falls far less than in unbuffered water. It still falls, because some H+ stays free, and once the base member is consumed the buffer no longer helps. That is why blood pH does move during heavy exercise or in disease."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace what the bicarbonate buffer system does when lactic acid enters the blood during hard exercise, and explain what the lungs contribute.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"lactic acid releases H+ into plasma; HCO3- binds the H+ to form carbonic acid (H2CO3); carbonic acid converts to CO2 and water; plasma bicarbonate falls as it is consumed; pH drops only slightly instead of sharply; ventilation rises and the lungs exhale the extra CO2, which pulls pH back toward 7.4","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a pH scale as a horizontal line from 0 to 14. Mark arterial blood, stomach contents, and urine in the right places, with the actual numbers. Then draw the bicarbonate buffer pair as a two-way arrow above the blood mark, and show with arrows what the pair does when acid is added and when base is added. One line: what would happen to blood pH without it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same beaker twice, one with buffer and one without, and add the same amount of acid to each. Draw the pH before and after in both. Then draw a third panel: the buffered beaker after so much acid that the buffer runs out. Under it, one line naming what 'the buffer runs out' actually means at the molecular level."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define pH, state the normal pH range of arterial blood, and explain how a buffer pair resists a change in pH when acid or base is added."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: pH and buffers. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-protein-function","name":"Protein structure and function","can":"Relate the levels of protein structure to binding site shape and explain how denaturation by heat or pH change destroys function.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Dietary protein reaching the stomach is exposed to a pH near 2. Which level of protein structure survives that exposure intact?\nA) Quaternary structure  |  B) Primary structure\nC) Tertiary structure  |  D) Secondary structure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Primary structure","why":"Extreme pH changes the charge on side chains and breaks ionic and hydrogen bonds, so the higher levels of folding unravel while the covalent peptide bonds of the primary sequence hold. Quaternary and tertiary structure depend heavily on those side chain interactions and go first. Secondary structure depends on backbone hydrogen bonds and is also disrupted, even though the backbone itself remains a continuous chain until pepsin cleaves it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the primary structure of a protein?\nA) The coiling of the backbone into an alpha helix\nB) The overall three dimensional folded shape of one chain\nC) The sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds\nD) The assembly of several folded chains into one functional unit\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds","why":"Primary structure is the order of amino acids, and because that order dictates which R groups end up next to each other, it determines every higher level of folding. Coiling into an alpha helix is secondary structure. The full three dimensional shape of a single chain is tertiary structure, and combining several chains is quaternary structure."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bonds stabilize secondary structure such as the alpha helix and beta pleated sheet?\nA) Disulfide bonds between cysteine side chains\nB) Peptide bonds between adjacent amino acids\nC) Hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms\nD) Ionic bonds between charged side chains\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms","why":"Secondary structure is a backbone phenomenon: the carbonyl oxygen of one residue hydrogen bonds to the amide hydrogen of another, and repeating that pattern produces a helix or a sheet. Peptide bonds hold the chain together and define primary structure rather than its folding. Disulfide and ionic bonds involve R groups and belong to tertiary structure."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a globular protein dissolved in cytosol, where do the nonpolar amino acid side chains typically end up?\nA) On the outer surface, in contact with cytosolic water\nB) Distributed evenly between surface and interior\nC) Buried in the interior, away from water\nD) Attached to the peptide backbone hydrogen bonds\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Buried in the interior, away from water","why":"The hydrophobic effect drives nonpolar side chains inward so that water can maximize hydrogen bonding with itself, and this buried core is a major force holding tertiary structure together. Polar and charged side chains are the ones that face the water on the outside. An even distribution would leave nonpolar groups exposed and destabilize the fold, and side chains project from the backbone rather than participating in its hydrogen bonds."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Denaturation breaks the weak bonds that hold a protein's shape but leaves its amino acid sequence intact.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Shape is held by weak interactions that are easily disturbed. Heat adds motion that shakes them apart, and a pH change alters the charges on side chains so ionic and hydrogen bonds no longer form. The peptide bonds are covalent and need a protease or harsh chemistry to break. So a denatured protein is the right chain in the wrong shape, and shape is what determines function."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain in terms of protein structure why a fever of 41 C (105.8 F) and a blood pH of 7.0 can each cause cells to stop working properly.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"protein function depends on its folded three dimensional shape; shape is held by weak bonds: hydrogen, ionic, and hydrophobic interactions; heat increases molecular motion and breaks these weak bonds; a pH change alters the charge on amino acid side chains, disrupting ionic and hydrogen bonds; binding sites and active sites lose their shape; enzymes, transporters, and other proteins lose function, sometimes permanently","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick one protein you will meet later this term: hemoglobin, a sodium channel, or an enzyme. Draw it at all four levels of structure, one box per level, in order. On the fourth box, circle the binding site. Then draw the same protein after heat denaturation and mark exactly what changed. One line: which level of structure was lost first, and why the binding site stopped working."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a binding site and its ligand as two shapes that fit, like a lock and key, then draw three failed versions beside it: one where the ligand is the wrong shape, one where the site has been distorted by low pH, and one where a second molecule is already sitting in the site. Label which of the three is competition and which is denaturation. One line on why shape is the whole story here."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate the levels of protein structure to binding site shape and explain how denaturation by heat or pH change destroys function."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Protein structure and function. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-enzyme-function","name":"Enzyme activity and regulation","can":"Describe how enzymes lower activation energy and predict the effect of substrate concentration, temperature, pH, and competitive or allosteric inhibition on reaction rate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A competitive inhibitor is added to an enzyme reaction. Predict its effect on apparent Km and on Vmax.\nA) Apparent Km falls and Vmax is unchanged\nB) Apparent Km is unchanged and Vmax falls\nC) Apparent Km rises and Vmax is unchanged\nD) Both apparent Km and Vmax fall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Apparent Km rises and Vmax is unchanged","why":"A competitive inhibitor occupies the active site, so more substrate is needed to reach half maximal rate, but flooding the system with substrate still outcompetes the inhibitor and Vmax is eventually reached. A falling Km would mean tighter binding, the opposite of competition. Unchanged Km with reduced Vmax is the signature of a noncompetitive or allosteric inhibitor, which binds elsewhere and cannot be outcompeted."},{"t":"mc","q":"A human enzyme is assayed at 37 C and then at 55 C. Predict the rate at 55 C.\nA) Roughly double, because rate rises with temperature\nB) Much lower, because the enzyme denatures above its optimum\nC) Slightly higher, because molecules collide more often\nD) Unchanged, because enzymes are temperature independent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Much lower, because the enzyme denatures above its optimum","why":"Rate does rise with temperature up to about 37 C for human enzymes, but past that the added kinetic energy breaks the weak bonds holding the fold and the active site is destroyed, so activity drops steeply. Doubling or a modest increase would only apply below the optimum. Enzymes are strongly temperature dependent, which is why body temperature is regulated so tightly."},{"t":"mc","q":"With substrate held at a saturating concentration, the amount of enzyme in the tube is doubled. Predict the reaction rate.\nA) It stays at the same Vmax as before\nB) It roughly doubles\nC) It rises only slightly, because substrate is limiting\nD) It falls, because enzyme molecules compete for substrate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It roughly doubles","why":"Vmax is set by how much enzyme is present, so twice as many active sites working at full speed convert twice as much substrate per minute. The rate would stay flat only if something other than enzyme quantity were limiting. Substrate is saturating here rather than limiting, and enzyme molecules working in parallel do not interfere with one another."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has swallowed methanol. The enzyme alcohol dehydrogenase converts methanol to formaldehyde, which is what causes the damage. Ethanol binds the active site of the same enzyme with about ten times the affinity of methanol. The emergency team gives the patient ethanol.\nPredict the effect of the ethanol on the rate at which methanol is converted to formaldehyde, and explain the mechanism.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Conversion slows, because ethanol occupies the active site and methanol must compete for it","why":"This is competitive inhibition used as a treatment. Both alcohols fit the same active site. With ethanol present at high concentration and higher affinity, most enzyme molecules are busy with ethanol at any moment, so methanol is processed slowly and the kidneys have time to excrete it before much formaldehyde forms."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An enzyme has a Km of 1 mM for its substrate. A student measures the reaction rate at substrate concentrations of 0.5 mM, 5 mM, and 50 mM, keeping enzyme amount and temperature the same.\nPredict how the rate at 50 mM compares to the rate at 5 mM, and why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Only slightly higher, because at 5 mM the enzyme is already close to saturated","why":"Km is the substrate concentration that gives half of Vmax. At 5 mM, five times Km, the enzyme is running at about 83 percent of Vmax. At 50 mM it reaches about 98 percent. So a tenfold jump in substrate buys only a small gain because most active sites were already occupied. The curve is a hyperbola, not a straight line."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Raising the substrate concentration increases the rate of an enzyme catalyzed reaction without limit.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Rate rises with substrate only until the enzyme molecules are saturated; after that the rate plateaus at Vmax.","why":"Each enzyme molecule can process only so many substrate molecules per second. At low substrate, many active sites sit empty and adding substrate fills them, so rate climbs. Once nearly every active site is occupied all the time, extra substrate has nowhere to bind and the rate levels off. The only way to raise Vmax is to add more enzyme."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how a competitive inhibitor and an allosteric (noncompetitive) inhibitor each slow an enzyme, and predict what happens to the reaction rate in each case when substrate concentration is raised.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"competitive inhibitor binds at the active site and competes with substrate; raising substrate overcomes competitive inhibition, rate approaches normal Vmax; competitive inhibitor raises apparent Km but leaves Vmax unchanged; allosteric inhibitor binds a site away from the active site and changes the enzyme's shape; raising substrate does not overcome allosteric inhibition; allosteric inhibitor lowers Vmax","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an energy diagram with reactants, products, and the activation energy hump, then draw the same reaction with enzyme on top of it in a second color. Show what the enzyme changed and what it did not. Underneath, draw four small graphs in a row: rate against substrate concentration, against temperature, against pH, and with a competitive inhibitor added. Label the axes on every one. One line on which of the four curves has a plateau and what the plateau means."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an enzyme with its active site, then draw three copies of it: one with a competitive inhibitor in the active site, one with an allosteric inhibitor at a second site, and one with an allosteric activator. In each copy show what happened to the active site's shape. Then write which one you could overcome by adding more substrate, and why that works for one and not the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe how enzymes lower activation energy and predict the effect of substrate concentration, temperature, pH, and competitive or allosteric inhibition on reaction rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Enzyme activity and regulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-atp-energy","name":"ATP and energy coupling","can":"Explain how ATP hydrolysis is coupled to endergonic cellular work and name three categories of work that require it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cell's ATP supply falls sharply. Predict what happens to intracellular Na+ and to cell volume.\nA) Intracellular Na+ falls and the cell shrinks\nB) Intracellular Na+ rises and the cell shrinks\nC) Intracellular Na+ is unchanged, because the pump does not use ATP\nD) Intracellular Na+ rises and the cell swells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Intracellular Na+ rises and the cell swells","why":"Without ATP the sodium potassium pump stops, so Na+ that leaks in is no longer extruded, it accumulates, and water follows the added solute osmotically into the cell. Falling intracellular Na+ would require a working pump. Rising intracellular solute draws water in rather than out, so the cell cannot shrink, and the pump is one of the largest ATP consumers in the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does ATP most often transfer energy to a protein that is doing work?\nA) By transferring its terminal phosphate to the protein, changing its shape\nB) By donating an electron that chemically reduces the protein\nC) By binding permanently to the protein and remaining in place\nD) By raising the local temperature so that the protein moves faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) By transferring its terminal phosphate to the protein, changing its shape","why":"Phosphorylation adds a bulky, doubly negative group that forces a conformational change, and that shape change is the work, whether it moves an ion, a filament, or a substrate. Electron transfer is what NADH and FADH2 do, a different energy carrier system. Permanent binding would leave the protein stuck in one state, and the energy is delivered chemically rather than as local heat."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes ATP as an energy carrier?\nA) ATP is stored in large quantities in muscle and liver for later use\nB) ATP is recycled continuously, with ADP rephosphorylated as fast as ATP is spent\nC) ATP is produced once each day and then spent slowly over that day\nD) ATP is exported into the blood so that other tissues can use it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) ATP is recycled continuously, with ADP rephosphorylated as fast as ATP is spent","why":"The cellular ATP pool is small and turns over extremely fast, so the body cycles through many times its own weight in ATP each day rather than stockpiling it. Muscle and liver store glycogen, and adipose stores triglyceride, but neither stores ATP in bulk. Slow use over a day contradicts the seconds long ATP reserve, and ATP is used where it is made rather than shipped through the blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the products when ATP is hydrolyzed in a typical energy releasing step?\nA) ADP and inorganic phosphate  |  B) AMP and two inorganic phosphates\nC) Adenosine and three inorganic phosphates  |  D) ADP and glucose phosphate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) ADP and inorganic phosphate","why":"Most cellular work removes the terminal phosphate, leaving ADP and free Pi, and the released energy is captured by the protein doing the work. Hydrolysis to AMP plus pyrophosphate happens in a few reactions such as amino acid activation, but it is not the usual step. Stripping all three phosphates to adenosine is not a normal energy yielding reaction, and glucose phosphate is a product of a transfer reaction, not of plain hydrolysis."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: The first step of glycolysis attaches a phosphate to glucose. Adding phosphate to glucose by itself has a free energy change of about plus 14 kJ per mole. Hydrolysis of ATP has a free energy change of about minus 30 kJ per mole. The enzyme hexokinase carries out the step.\nPredict whether the phosphorylation of glucose proceeds in the cell, and explain how hexokinase makes it possible.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"It proceeds, because hexokinase couples the two reactions by transferring the phosphate directly from ATP to glucose, giving a net change of about minus 16 kJ per mole","why":"This is energy coupling. The endergonic step (plus 14) and the exergonic step (minus 30) are made into a single reaction in one active site. The phosphate never floats free; hexokinase hands it from ATP to glucose. The combined free energy change is minus 30 plus 14, which is minus 16 kJ per mole, so the coupled reaction is exergonic and runs forward. This same trick, direct phosphate transfer from ATP, powers pumps and many synthesis steps."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The energy released by ATP hydrolysis can drive a reaction that would not happen on its own.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Reactions run in the direction that releases free energy. Building glucose 6 phosphate or pumping Na+ uphill absorbs energy and would not go forward alone. When the cell links that step to ATP hydrolysis, usually by transferring the phosphate onto the substrate or the pump, the total energy change is negative and the pair runs. This is energy coupling, and it is how ATP pays for all three kinds of cellular work."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw ATP, then draw the hydrolysis to ADP plus phosphate with the energy released as an arrow leaving. Now draw three cellular jobs that arrow can go and do: a pump moving sodium uphill, a muscle filament sliding, and a molecule being built. In each one show where the phosphate or the energy actually goes. One line: why the cell cannot just store the energy as heat instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw ATP as a rechargeable battery on one side of a cycle and draw the cycle closed, with what charges it and what drains it. Then break the cycle at one point of your choosing, either the charging half or the draining half, and write what fails first in a real cell within seconds, and what fails within minutes. Be specific about which cell you picked."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how ATP hydrolysis is coupled to endergonic cellular work and name three categories of work that require it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ATP and energy coupling. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-lab-enzyme-assay","name":"Enzyme assay","can":"Measure enzyme activity across a range of temperature or pH using a spectrophotometric or colorimetric assay, plot the results, and identify the optimum.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student assays a human enzyme and records rates of 0.02, 0.09, 0.21, 0.08, and 0.01 absorbance units per minute at 5, 20, 37, 50, and 70 C. What is the optimum temperature?\nA) 20 C  |  B) 50 C\nC) 70 C  |  D) 37 C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 37 C","why":"The highest rate, 0.21 per minute, occurs at 37 C, which matches the body temperature this enzyme evolved to work at. The rate at 20 C is still climbing toward the peak. Rates at 50 and 70 C are low because the enzyme is denaturing, so higher temperature does not keep improving performance."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pH series gives rates of 0.01, 0.05, 0.22, 0.19, and 0.03 absorbance units per minute at pH 3, 5, 7, 8, and 11. What is the optimum pH for this enzyme?\nA) pH 5  |  B) pH 3\nC) pH 11  |  D) pH 7\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) pH 7","why":"The peak rate of 0.22 per minute falls at pH 7, with pH 8 close behind on the shoulder of the curve. Rates at pH 5 and pH 3 are low because the enzyme is progressively denatured on the acid side. pH 11 gives the near zero activity expected when strong alkali has unfolded the protein."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two tubes are plotted as absorbance against time. Tube A gives a steeper line than tube B. What does that tell you?\nA) Tube A started with more product already present\nB) Tube A contains a larger volume of solution\nC) Tube A was read at a longer wavelength\nD) The enzyme in tube A is producing product faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The enzyme in tube A is producing product faster","why":"Slope is change in absorbance over change in time, which is exactly the definition of reaction rate, so a steeper line means faster product formation. Starting product would raise the intercept of the line rather than tilt it. Volume differences are eliminated by using matched cuvettes and standard volumes, and the wavelength is held constant for all tubes in an assay."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A lab group assays an enzyme at six temperatures and records the initial rate as change in absorbance per minute: 20 C (68 F) 0.010, 30 C (86 F) 0.020, 37 C (98.6 F) 0.030, 45 C (113 F) 0.034, 55 C (131 F) 0.012, and 65 C (149 F) 0.001.\nIdentify the temperature optimum from these data and explain why the rate drops between 45 and 55 C.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The optimum is near 45 C; above it the enzyme denatures, so fewer active molecules remain","why":"The optimum is whatever temperature gives the highest measured rate, here 45 C. Below that, warming speeds the reaction by increasing collision energy. Past it, heat breaks the weak bonds that hold the fold, the active site is lost, and the rate collapses even though the remaining molecules are colliding faster than ever. Not every enzyme peaks at exactly 37 C, and a data driven optimum beats an assumption."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A student assays amylase at pH 3, 5, 7, and 9 with excess substrate and gets initial rates of 0.002, 0.018, 0.030, and 0.015 absorbance units per minute. She then repeats the pH 7 tube with twice the amount of enzyme, all else unchanged.\nPredict the initial rate in the doubled enzyme tube and explain why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 0.060 per minute, because with substrate in excess the rate is proportional to the amount of enzyme","why":"When substrate is in excess, every enzyme molecule is working at close to its full speed, so the total rate is simply the number of enzyme molecules times the rate per molecule. Doubling the enzyme doubles Vmax. The pH optimum determines how fast each molecule works, and pH 7 is the best of the tested values, but it does not cap how many molecules you add."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a temperature series, the fall in reaction rate above the optimum is caused by denaturation of the enzyme.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Below the optimum, warming speeds the reaction the way it speeds any chemistry, with more frequent and more energetic collisions. Above it, the protein begins to unfold and the loss of active enzyme outweighs the gain in collision energy. The drop is usually steep and often not reversible, which is why the high temperature tubes in an assay show almost no activity."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, describe how you would find the pH optimum of an enzyme using a colorimetric assay. Include what you hold constant, what control you run, how you get a rate from the readings, and how you identify the optimum.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"prepare buffers spanning a range of pH values; hold enzyme amount, substrate concentration, and temperature the same in every tube; run a blank with no enzyme to subtract background color; read absorbance over time and take the rate from the initial linear slope; plot rate against pH; the optimum is the pH that gives the highest rate","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you run anything, draw the graph you predict: rate on the y axis, whichever variable you are testing on the x, with your predicted optimum marked. Run the assay, then draw the real curve on the same axes in a second color. Label the optimum you measured. Underneath, write one line on where your prediction and the data disagreed, and one line on what in the method could have moved the measured optimum away from the true one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw your assay as a flow of steps, one box each, from tube to number, with an arrow between every step. Under each box write the one thing that step controls. Then circle the single step where a mistake would change your answer the most, and write what that mistake would do to the shape of your curve, not just to one point."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Enzyme assay. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Metabolism and Energy Balance","comps":[{"id":"w14-atp-pathways","name":"ATP production pathways","can":"Compare glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation by location, oxygen requirement, and ATP yield.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a normal resting lactate, but mild exercise sends lactate climbing steeply and peak oxygen consumption is very low. Which step is limited?\nA) Glucose uptake into muscle  |  B) Glycolysis in the muscle cytosol\nC) Oxidative phosphorylation  |  D) Oxygen carriage by hemoglobin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Oxidative phosphorylation","why":"Muscle that cannot use oxygen at the mitochondria meets any extra ATP demand by accelerating glycolysis, so lactate rises out of proportion to a low workload while peak oxygen consumption stays small. A glycolytic block would do the opposite and produce little or no lactate. Limited glucose uptake would lower fuel supply rather than force lactate production, and a hemoglobin problem would limit oxygen delivery but would also lower venous oxygen content, whereas a mitochondrial defect leaves venous oxygen unusually high."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mature red blood cell has no mitochondria. Which pathway supplies its ATP?\nA) The citric acid cycle  |  B) Oxidative phosphorylation\nC) Beta oxidation  |  D) Anaerobic glycolysis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Anaerobic glycolysis","why":"Glycolysis is entirely cytosolic, so it is the only pathway a red cell can run, and the pyruvate it makes is converted to lactate and exported. The citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation and beta oxidation all require mitochondrial compartments the red cell does not have. This is also why a red cell cannot consume any of the oxygen it carries."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cyanide blocks the final complex of the electron transport chain. Which ATP producing process can still run?\nA) Oxidative phosphorylation  |  B) Proton pumping by complex I\nC) The citric acid cycle in the matrix  |  D) Glycolysis producing lactate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Glycolysis producing lactate","why":"Glycolysis survives the block because it makes ATP by substrate level phosphorylation and regenerates its own NAD+ by reducing pyruvate to lactate. Oxidative phosphorylation is exactly what cyanide stops. Complex I halts as well, because with the chain blocked downstream the carriers stay reduced and cannot accept more electrons, and for the same reason NADH piles up and the citric acid cycle stalls."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A runner completes a 400 meter sprint in 50 seconds. Oxygen delivery to the leg muscles cannot keep up with demand during the run. Blood lactate rises from 1.0 mmol/L to 12 mmol/L (resting 0.5 to 2.0) by the finish.\nExplain why lactate rises, in terms of what the muscle cell needs to keep making ATP.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Pyruvate is converted to lactate to regenerate NAD+, which glycolysis must have to keep producing ATP without oxygen","why":"Glycolysis needs NAD+ to accept electrons at one of its steps. Normally the resulting NADH hands those electrons to the mitochondrion, which needs oxygen at the end of the chain. Without enough oxygen, NADH accumulates and NAD+ runs out, which would stop glycolysis and ATP production. Converting pyruvate to lactate takes the electrons back off NADH and regenerates NAD+, so glycolysis can keep making its 2 ATP per glucose at high speed. Lactate is the cost of that shortcut."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A child has an inherited defect in pyruvate dehydrogenase, the enzyme that converts pyruvate to acetyl CoA for entry into the citric acid cycle. Resting blood lactate is 8 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.0). Oxygen delivery to the tissues is normal.\nPredict the ATP yield per glucose in this child's cells and explain why lactate is high even at rest with plenty of oxygen.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Yield falls to about 2 ATP per glucose; pyruvate cannot enter the mitochondrion's pathways, so it is diverted to lactate","why":"The block sits at the doorway between glycolysis and the citric acid cycle. Glucose still makes pyruvate and 2 ATP in the cytosol, but the pyruvate cannot become acetyl CoA, so it never reaches the cycle or the electron transport chain. The cell handles the pile of pyruvate the same way an oxygen starved muscle does: it converts it to lactate to regenerate NAD+. Oxygen is available but glucose derived carbon cannot get to where it would be used."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When oxygen is absent, a cell makes about the same amount of ATP per glucose as when oxygen is present, because glycolysis speeds up to compensate.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Without oxygen, ATP yield falls from about 30 to 32 per glucose to only 2, because the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation cannot run.","why":"Almost all the ATP from glucose comes from oxidative phosphorylation, which needs oxygen as the final electron acceptor. Without it, only glycolysis works. Glycolysis can run faster to make ATP more quickly, but it still gets only 2 ATP from each glucose, so the cell burns through glucose rapidly and piles up lactate. Faster is not the same as more efficient."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare what happens to one glucose molecule with and without oxygen. Name where each pathway runs, roughly how much ATP results, and what becomes of pyruvate in each case.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"glycolysis runs in the cytosol in both cases and nets 2 ATP; with oxygen, pyruvate enters the mitochondrion and feeds the citric acid cycle in the matrix; electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation on the inner membrane use oxygen as the final electron acceptor; total with oxygen is about 30 to 32 ATP per glucose; without oxygen, pyruvate is converted to lactate to regenerate NAD+ so glycolysis can continue; without oxygen the yield is only 2 ATP per glucose","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cell outline with the cytosol and one mitochondrion. Place glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation where each actually happens, and draw arrows for what passes from one to the next. Beside each, write its ATP yield and whether it needs oxygen. One line: which of the three keeps running when oxygen runs out, and what the cell pays for that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same three pathways as a single fuel line from one glucose molecule to the ATP total, with the running count written on the line as it climbs. Then draw a second version of the line for the same glucose under anaerobic conditions and mark where it stops. Under both, one line on why a sprinting muscle and a resting one are on different lines, and what the sprinter accumulates."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation by location, oxygen requirement, and ATP yield."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ATP production pathways. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-absorptive-state","name":"Absorptive state","can":"Describe the fate of glucose, amino acids, and fats in the absorptive state and identify insulin as the dominant hormone.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Insulin increases the activity of lipoprotein lipase on the capillary walls of adipose tissue. What does this do to fat handling after a meal?\nA) It increases fatty acid uptake into adipocytes\nB) It releases stored fat into the blood\nC) It moves chylomicrons intact into adipocytes\nD) It converts fatty acids into glucose\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It increases fatty acid uptake into adipocytes","why":"Lipoprotein lipase sits on the endothelium and strips triglyceride out of circulating chylomicrons, freeing fatty acids that diffuse into the adipocyte and are re esterified for storage. Releasing stored fat is the job of hormone sensitive lipase inside the adipocyte, which insulin inhibits rather than stimulates. Whole chylomicrons are far too large to cross the capillary wall, and fatty acids with an even number of carbons cannot be converted to glucose."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person eats a large carbohydrate meal. Predict what happens to glycogen synthase and glycogen phosphorylase activity in the liver.\nA) Both activities rise together  |  B) Synthase falls and phosphorylase rises\nC) Both activities fall together  |  D) Synthase rises and phosphorylase falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Synthase rises and phosphorylase falls","why":"Insulin drives dephosphorylation of both enzymes, which activates glycogen synthase and inactivates glycogen phosphorylase, so the liver stores glucose instead of releasing it. Running both at once would create a futile cycle that burns ATP and stores nothing. Synthase down with phosphorylase up is exactly the fasting pattern set by glucagon, and shutting both down would leave the liver unable to handle the meal at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a person whose muscle GLUT4 vesicles cannot fuse with the plasma membrane, what happens to plasma glucose after a meal?\nA) It falls faster than normal\nB) It is unaffected, because the liver compensates\nC) It stays elevated longer than normal\nD) It falls below the fasting value\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It stays elevated longer than normal","why":"Skeletal muscle is the largest single sink for a glucose load, and it takes glucose up only when insulin puts GLUT4 into the membrane, so blocking that step leaves glucose in the plasma much longer. This is the mechanism behind insulin resistance in muscle. The liver takes up glucose through insulin independent GLUT2, but liver uptake alone cannot substitute for the muscle mass, so glucose cannot fall faster or below fasting."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does plasma sometimes look milky a few hours after a fatty meal?\nA) Free fatty acids released from adipose scatter light\nB) Cholesterol crystals precipitate out of plasma\nC) Bile salts absorbed from the gut scatter light\nD) Chylomicrons entering from the lymph scatter light\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Chylomicrons entering from the lymph scatter light","why":"Long chain fats leave the intestine as chylomicrons through the lymphatics and enter the bloodstream at the thoracic duct, and these large triglyceride rich particles scatter light enough to make plasma turbid until lipoprotein lipase clears them. Free fatty acids rise in fasting, are bound to albumin, and do not cloud plasma. Cholesterol does not precipitate in normal plasma, and bile salts are reabsorbed into the portal blood at a concentration far too low to be visible."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person eats a meal of pure protein with almost no carbohydrate. Why does glucagon rise along with insulin?\nA) Glucagon helps move amino acids into cells\nB) Glucagon prevents insulin from lowering glucose too far\nC) Glucagon stimulates further insulin release\nD) Glucagon slows amino acid absorption from the gut\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Glucagon prevents insulin from lowering glucose too far","why":"Amino acids stimulate insulin secretion, but with no carbohydrate in the meal that insulin would push plasma glucose down; the simultaneous glucagon rise stimulates hepatic glucose output and holds glucose steady. This is a nice example of two opposing hormones being co secreted to achieve a net result neither could achieve alone. Glucagon does not move amino acids into cells, does not slow their absorption, and its islet effect on insulin is minor compared with the glucose it liberates."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In the absorptive state, adipose tissue releases fatty acids into the blood so that glucose is spared for the brain.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. In the absorptive state insulin inhibits lipolysis and adipose tissue stores fat, because glucose is plentiful and there is no need to spare it.","why":"Releasing stored fat to spare glucose is the strategy of the postabsorptive state, when glucagon is high and insulin is low. After a meal the situation is reversed. Insulin turns off hormone sensitive lipase, turns on lipoprotein lipase at the capillary wall, and drives fatty acids into the adipocyte for storage."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the small intestine at the left with three fuels leaving it, glucose, amino acids, and fat. Follow each to the tissues that take it up, liver, muscle, adipose, and draw what it is turned into there, glycogen, protein, stored triglyceride. Draw insulin arriving from the pancreas and mark every arrow it is driving. One line: say which single tissue in your drawing takes up glucose without needing insulin, and why that matters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three panels, liver, skeletal muscle, and adipose tissue, all in the hour after a large meal. In each, draw the transporters and the storage form actually used there, and show insulin at the cell surface. Mark which panels have an insulin sensitive glucose transporter moving to the membrane and which does not. One line: say why muscle glycogen cannot raise your plasma glucose later, and which panel can."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the fate of glucose, amino acids, and fats in the absorptive state and identify insulin as the dominant hormone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Absorptive state. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-postabsorptive-state","name":"Postabsorptive state","can":"Explain how glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis maintain plasma glucose during fasting and name the hormones that drive each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A healthy person has fasted for three days. Predict liver glycogen content, the rate of gluconeogenesis, and plasma ketone concentration.\nA) Glycogen depleted, gluconeogenesis low, ketones high\nB) Glycogen depleted, gluconeogenesis high, ketones high\nC) Glycogen normal, gluconeogenesis high, ketones low\nD) Glycogen depleted, gluconeogenesis high, ketones low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Glycogen depleted, gluconeogenesis high, ketones high","why":"Liver glycogen is spent within roughly a day, so by day three the liver is making glucose from glycerol, lactate and amino acids at a high rate. At the same time the low insulin state has adipose tissue releasing fatty acids so fast that the liver converts the excess acetyl CoA into ketone bodies, and plasma ketones climb. Gluconeogenesis cannot be low when it is the only glucose source left, and ketones cannot stay low when fatty acid delivery to the liver is maximal."},{"t":"mc","q":"A child with a deficiency of glucose 6 phosphatase is fasted overnight. Predict plasma glucose, liver glycogen content, and plasma lactate.\nA) Glucose falls, glycogen is depleted, lactate falls\nB) Glucose is normal, glycogen accumulates, lactate rises\nC) Glucose falls, glycogen accumulates, lactate rises\nD) Glucose rises, glycogen accumulates, lactate falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Glucose falls, glycogen accumulates, lactate rises","why":"Without glucose 6 phosphatase the liver can break glycogen down but cannot free the glucose to export it, so plasma glucose falls during any fast while glucose 6 phosphate and glycogen pile up inside hepatocytes. The trapped glucose 6 phosphate is pushed on through glycolysis to pyruvate and lactate, so plasma lactate rises. Glycogen cannot be depleted when the exit is blocked, and glucose cannot stay normal or rise when the liver's only export route is closed."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person with type 1 diabetes stops taking insulin. Trace the effect on adipose lipolysis, hepatic ketone production, and blood pH.\nA) Lipolysis accelerates, ketones rise, pH falls\nB) Lipolysis slows, ketones fall, pH rises\nC) Lipolysis accelerates, ketones rise, pH rises\nD) Lipolysis slows, ketones rise, pH falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lipolysis accelerates, ketones rise, pH falls","why":"Insulin is the brake on hormone sensitive lipase, so removing it lets adipose tissue release fatty acids without restraint, and the liver converts the flood of acetyl CoA into acetoacetate and beta hydroxybutyrate. Those ketone bodies are acids, so as they accumulate they consume bicarbonate and blood pH falls, producing a metabolic acidosis. Note the paradox worth remembering: plasma glucose is very high, yet the cells behave as though they are starving, because without insulin they cannot use that glucose."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has fasted for 36 hours. Which process is now the main supplier of plasma glucose?\nA) Hepatic glycogenolysis  |  B) Muscle glycogenolysis\nC) Gluconeogenesis  |  D) Intestinal absorption\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Gluconeogenesis","why":"Liver glycogen is essentially gone by about 24 hours, so beyond that point the liver has to build glucose from lactate, glycerol and amino acids. Muscle glycogenolysis never contributes glucose to the blood because muscle lacks glucose 6 phosphatase. Intestinal absorption stopped when the last meal was absorbed, which is what started the postabsorptive state in the first place."},{"t":"mc","q":"Working muscle sends lactate to the liver, which converts it back to glucose and returns it to the blood. What is this loop called?\nA) The urea cycle  |  B) The Cori cycle\nC) The citric acid cycle  |  D) The enterohepatic cycle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The Cori cycle","why":"The Cori cycle lets muscle unload the lactate it makes during anaerobic work and lets the liver recycle that carbon into glucose, at a net cost in ATP that the liver pays. The urea cycle disposes of the nitrogen from amino acid breakdown, not of lactate carbon. The citric acid cycle oxidizes acetyl CoA inside mitochondria, and the enterohepatic circulation recycles bile salts between gut and liver."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the body keeps plasma glucose in range during the 24 hours after the last meal. Name the pathways in the order they take over and the hormones that drive them.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"insulin falls and glucagon rises as absorption ends; liver glycogenolysis supplies glucose during the first hours; liver glycogen is largely gone by about 24 hours; gluconeogenesis from amino acids, glycerol, and lactate takes over, driven by glucagon and cortisol; lipolysis in adipose tissue releases fatty acids and most tissues switch to burning fat; the liver begins converting fatty acids to ketone bodies; glucose is spared for the brain and red blood cells","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a time axis from the last meal out to several days of fasting. Above it, draw stacked bands showing which source is supplying plasma glucose at each point: liver glycogen first, then glucose made new from amino acids, glycerol and lactate. Below the axis, draw fat breakdown rising and ketone bodies appearing later. Name the hormone driving each band. One line: say what runs out first and roughly when, and what takes over."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one hepatocyte in a fasting person with four labeled pathways running out of it or through it: glycogen breakdown, glucose made from noncarbohydrate precursors, and arriving from elsewhere, fatty acids from adipose and their conversion to ketone bodies. Draw the precursors arriving from muscle and fat with arrows. Write the hormone driving each pathway beside it. One line: name the tissue that switches to ketones during a long fast, and say what it is protecting."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis maintain plasma glucose during fasting and name the hormones that drive each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Postabsorptive state. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-glucose-regulation","name":"Integrated glucose regulation","can":"Predict the hormonal and metabolic response to a carbohydrate meal and to a prolonged fast and explain how plasma glucose stays within range in both.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In a glucose tolerance test, plasma glucose two hours after the drink is 165 mg/dL. How is this value classified?\nA) Impaired glucose tolerance  |  B) Normal glucose tolerance\nC) Hypoglycemia  |  D) Normal fasting glucose\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Impaired glucose tolerance","why":"A normal two hour value is under 140 mg/dL, and values from 140 up to 199 fall in the impaired range, meaning disposal is slower than it should be but not yet in the diabetic range of 200 or above. The value is far too high to be hypoglycemia, and it cannot be called a fasting value at all because the person deliberately drank glucose two hours earlier."},{"t":"mc","q":"Closure of the ATP sensitive K+ channel in a beta cell has what immediate effect on its membrane potential?\nA) The cell hyperpolarizes\nB) The membrane potential is unchanged\nC) The cell repolarizes toward the K+ equilibrium potential\nD) The cell depolarizes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cell depolarizes","why":"Resting membrane potential is negative largely because K+ leaks out, so closing a K+ channel removes that outward positive current and the interior becomes less negative, which is depolarization. Hyperpolarization would require more K+ leaving, not less. Moving toward the K+ equilibrium potential is the same thing as hyperpolarizing, since that potential is more negative than rest, so it is the opposite of what happens here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Contracting skeletal muscle takes up glucose even when insulin is low. What does regular exercise therefore do to a person's insulin requirement?\nA) It raises it  |  B) It lowers it\nC) It has no effect on it  |  D) It abolishes the need for glucose entirely\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It lowers it","why":"Muscle contraction moves GLUT4 into the membrane by a pathway independent of insulin, so working muscle pulls glucose out of the blood without insulin having to force it, and less insulin is needed for the same disposal. Exercise also increases insulin sensitivity for hours afterward. Muscle still needs glucose, and indeed uses much more of it during exercise, so the demand for the fuel rises even as the demand for the hormone falls."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a prolonged fast, plasma glucose settles near 60 to 70 mg/dL rather than continuing to fall. What holds it there?\nA) Gluconeogenesis matched to reduced glucose demand\nB) Continued absorption of glucose from the gut\nC) Renal reabsorption of glucose above the threshold\nD) Release of glucose from muscle glycogen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gluconeogenesis matched to reduced glucose demand","why":"The liver keeps building glucose from glycerol, lactate and amino acids while most tissues switch to fatty acids and the brain switches partly to ketones, so supply and a shrinking demand meet at a low but stable value. Absorption from the gut ended long ago. Renal reabsorption prevents loss but adds no new glucose, and muscle cannot release glucose to the blood at any time because it lacks glucose 6 phosphatase."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A healthy student eats a large bowl of pasta. Thirty minutes later her plasma glucose is 145 mg/dL (fasting normal 70 to 99), her plasma insulin is 400 pmol/L (fasting normal 20 to 100), and her plasma glucagon is half its fasting value.\nPredict what her liver, muscle, and adipose tissue are doing over the next hour.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The liver stores glucose as glycogen and stops making new glucose, muscle and fat take up glucose through GLUT4, and fat breakdown is suppressed","why":"This is the absorptive state and the hormone values say so: insulin up, glucagon down. Insulin tells the liver to switch from producing glucose to storing it, tells muscle and fat to insert GLUT4 and take glucose in, and shuts off hormone sensitive lipase in adipose tissue. Over the next hour plasma glucose comes back down as these pathways clear the load, and insulin falls with it."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During a prolonged fast, plasma glucose falls close to zero once liver glycogen is used up.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Plasma glucose stays in the low normal range (roughly 60 to 70 mg/dL) through a prolonged fast because glucagon and cortisol drive gluconeogenesis from amino acids, glycerol, and lactate, and ketones reduce the brain's need for glucose.","why":"Liver glycogen lasts about a day, but the brain cannot tolerate a glucose of zero for even a few minutes. Gluconeogenesis takes over as glycogen runs out, and the switch of most tissues to fatty acids and of the brain partly to ketones lowers the amount of glucose that has to be made. The regulated variable is defended, just at a slightly lower level."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one wide set of axes covering 24 hours, plasma glucose on the vertical with a shaded normal band across it. Plot the trace through three meals and an overnight fast. Underneath, on the same time axis, draw insulin and glucagon as two traces. Mark every point where a correction is being made and by which hormone. One line: say what the shaded band would look like if only one of the two hormones existed, and which one you would keep."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two negative feedback loops side by side, each with a stimulus, a sensor, the islet cell that responds, the hormone, the target tissue, and the response that returns glucose to range. Loop one starts with a carbohydrate meal. Loop two starts with 18 hours without food. Draw the return arrow on both. One line: say which loop has more backup hormones behind it, and offer a reason why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the hormonal and metabolic response to a carbohydrate meal and to a prolonged fast and explain how plasma glucose stays within range in both."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Integrated glucose regulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-metabolic-rate","name":"Energy balance and metabolic rate","can":"Define basal metabolic rate, state the factors that raise and lower it, and explain how indirect calorimetry estimates energy expenditure.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A subject exercises hard enough to produce lactate. Why does indirect calorimetry underestimate their true energy expenditure during that period?\nA) Lactate binds oxygen and hides it from the analyzer\nB) Ventilation falls during heavy exercise\nC) Anaerobic ATP is produced without consuming oxygen\nD) Carbon dioxide production stops during anaerobic work\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Anaerobic ATP is produced without consuming oxygen","why":"Indirect calorimetry assumes all ATP comes from oxidative metabolism, so any ATP made by anaerobic glycolysis is energy released that the oxygen measurement never sees, and the estimate comes in low. Part of that debt is paid back after exercise, when oxygen consumption stays elevated. Lactate does not bind oxygen, ventilation rises steeply rather than falling, and CO2 output actually increases as bicarbonate buffers the acid load."},{"t":"mc","q":"Metabolic rate rises about 10 percent for each 1 C rise in body temperature. If a person with a basal rate of 1500 kcal per day develops a fever 2 C above normal, what is the approximate new rate?\nA) About 3000 kcal per day  |  B) About 1800 kcal per day\nC) About 1650 kcal per day  |  D) About 1550 kcal per day\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 1800 kcal per day","why":"Two degrees at roughly 10 percent each is about a 20 percent increase, and 20 percent of 1500 is 300, giving about 1800 kcal per day. This is a real clinical burden: a sustained fever measurably increases energy and fluid needs. About 1650 would correspond to only 1 C, and 3000 would require a doubling, which no realistic fever produces."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two people weigh the same, but one has considerably more skeletal muscle and less fat. Predict their basal metabolic rates.\nA) The person with more fat has the higher rate\nB) The person with more muscle has the higher rate\nC) The rates are identical because body mass is equal\nD) The rates cannot differ unless thyroid hormone differs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The person with more muscle has the higher rate","why":"Basal metabolic rate tracks lean body mass because muscle, liver and other lean tissues are metabolically active even at rest, while stored triglyceride uses very little energy. This is also why basal rate tends to be higher in men and to fall with age as lean mass is lost. Equal total mass does not mean equal active mass, and body composition changes metabolic rate without any change in thyroid hormone."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient secretes far too little thyroid hormone. Predict their basal metabolic rate, heat production, and tolerance of cold.\nA) Rate down, heat down, cold tolerated poorly\nB) Rate up, heat up, cold tolerated well\nC) Rate down, heat up, cold tolerated well\nD) Rate unchanged, heat down, cold tolerated poorly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Rate down, heat down, cold tolerated poorly","why":"Thyroid hormone sets the resting rate at which cells burn fuel, so too little slows metabolism, reduces the heat generated as a byproduct, and leaves the person intolerant of cold, often with weight gain and fatigue. Heat production cannot rise while metabolic rate falls, since the heat is a byproduct of that same metabolism. An unchanged rate would not explain the symptoms at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person who has fasted overnight has a measured respiratory quotient of 0.71. Which fuel are they mainly burning?\nA) Carbohydrate  |  B) Protein\nC) An equal mix of carbohydrate and fat  |  D) Fat\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fat","why":"A quotient near 0.7 means CO2 output is well below oxygen uptake, which is the signature of fat oxidation because fatty acids are hydrogen rich and require extra oxygen. Pure carbohydrate gives 1.0 and protein about 0.8. An even mix would land near 0.85, so 0.71 tells you this person has already shifted almost entirely to fat, exactly as expected after an overnight fast."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Indirect calorimetry estimates energy expenditure by measuring the heat that leaves the body.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Indirect calorimetry measures oxygen consumption and converts it to energy expenditure, since nearly all the energy the body releases comes from oxidative metabolism.","why":"Measuring heat directly requires an insulated chamber and is called direct calorimetry. The indirect method works because burning fuel with oxygen releases a predictable amount of energy, roughly 4.8 kcal for every liter of oxygen consumed. That is why a mouthpiece and a gas analyzer are enough to estimate metabolic rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw energy in on one side and energy out on the other, balanced on a pivot. Split energy out into its parts, the resting cost of staying alive, physical activity, and the cost of digesting the food itself. Beside the pivot, draw a row of short bars showing what raises and lowers basal metabolic rate: thyroid hormone, lean muscle mass, fever, age, prolonged food restriction. One line: say which side of the pivot is easier to change by a large amount, and what that means for weight change."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person breathing through a mouthpiece into a measuring device, with inspired and expired gas drawn as two streams. Show the difference in oxygen between them being measured. Write the conversion beside it, roughly 4.8 to 5 kilocalories of energy released per liter of oxygen consumed. Add a small scale for the ratio of carbon dioxide made to oxygen used, near 1.0 for carbohydrate and near 0.7 for fat. One line: say what the ratio tells you that the oxygen number alone does not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define basal metabolic rate, state the factors that raise and lower it, and explain how indirect calorimetry estimates energy expenditure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Energy balance and metabolic rate. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-thermoregulation","name":"Thermoregulation","can":"Trace the negative feedback response to heat and to cold through the hypothalamus and the effectors and explain the mechanism of fever.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Cold exposure triggers cutaneous vasoconstriction. How does this conserve heat?\nA) It increases the insulating fat layer\nB) It reduces blood flow to the skin surface\nC) It raises the metabolic rate of skin cells\nD) It closes the sweat ducts mechanically\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It reduces blood flow to the skin surface","why":"Skin blood flow is the main variable resistor in heat loss, and constricting those vessels keeps warm blood in the core where its heat cannot escape by radiation or convection. The fat layer is a fixed insulator and does not change acutely. Skin metabolism contributes very little heat either way, and sweat production is controlled by sympathetic cholinergic fibers to the glands rather than by mechanical closure of ducts."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person stands in still air at 40 C, which is above their skin temperature. Which mechanism can still remove heat from the body?\nA) Radiation  |  B) Convection\nC) Conduction  |  D) Evaporation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Evaporation","why":"When the surroundings are hotter than the skin, radiation, convection and conduction all run backwards and deliver heat into the body. Evaporation works against that gradient because vaporizing water absorbs heat regardless of which way the temperature difference points. This is why sweating becomes the sole defense in a very hot environment, and why fluid replacement becomes critical."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two people exercise at the same intensity at 32 C, one in dry air and one in very humid air. Predict how core temperature differs.\nA) It rises more in the dry condition\nB) It rises equally in both, since air temperature is the same\nC) It rises more in the humid condition\nD) It falls in the humid condition\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises more in the humid condition","why":"Sweat only cools when it evaporates, and evaporation depends on the water vapor gradient between skin and air, so humid air lets sweat drip off without taking heat with it. The person in humid air therefore loses less heat despite sweating just as much or more, and core temperature climbs faster. Air temperature alone does not determine heat loss when evaporation is the dominant route."},{"t":"mc","q":"Vigorous shivering begins in a cold room. Predict what happens to oxygen consumption and metabolic rate.\nA) Both decrease as the body conserves energy\nB) Oxygen consumption rises while metabolic rate falls\nC) Both increase substantially\nD) Both stay the same, since no external work is done\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both increase substantially","why":"Shivering is muscle contraction, and contraction costs ATP, so shivering can raise metabolic rate and oxygen consumption several fold above basal. Because no useful external work is done, essentially all of that energy appears as heat, which is exactly the point of the response. Metabolic rate and oxygen consumption cannot move in opposite directions in aerobic tissue, and the absence of external work makes the response more thermally efficient, not free."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 25 year old man with influenza has a core temperature of 39.5 C (103.1 F) (normal about 37 C or 98.6 F). His hands and feet are cold and pale, he is shivering, and he says he feels freezing and wants more blankets.\nWhy is he shivering and feeling cold when his temperature is already high?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Cytokines from the infection raised the hypothalamic set point through prostaglandin E2, so his core temperature is below the new set point and the hypothalamus is running cold responses to raise it","why":"Fever is not a failure of thermoregulation. It is thermoregulation aimed at a new target. Pyrogenic cytokines stimulate prostaglandin E2 production in the hypothalamus, which raises the set point, say to 40 C (104 F). A core of 39.5 C is now too cold relative to the target, so the hypothalamus does exactly what it would do in a snowstorm: vasoconstrict the skin to conserve heat and shiver to produce more. He will feel cold until his core reaches the new set point."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace the negative feedback response of a person who steps outside into 0 C (32 F) air wearing a T shirt. Include the sensor, the integrating center, and each effector response.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"skin thermoreceptors and central thermoreceptors send input to the hypothalamus; the hypothalamus compares core temperature to its set point of about 37 C (98.6 F); sympathetic vasoconstriction of skin vessels reduces heat loss; shivering increases heat production by skeletal muscle; nonshivering thermogenesis rises through catecholamines and thyroid hormone; behavioral responses such as curling up and seeking shelter; the responses shut off when core temperature returns to the set point","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two negative feedback loops meeting at the hypothalamus in the middle. On the hot side, run the loop from a rise in core temperature through the hypothalamus to the effectors, skin vessel dilation and sweating, and back to core temperature. On the cold side, run it to vessel constriction, shivering, and heat generated without shivering. Draw the sensors in both skin and core. One line: say which effector on the hot side stops working in high humidity, and what that does to core temperature."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time on the horizontal, temperature on the vertical, and plot two lines through a fever: the set point and the actual core temperature. Show the set point jumping first, the gap that opens under it, and the core temperature climbing to meet it. Then show the set point dropping back and the second gap that opens above it. Mark where the person shivers and where they sweat. One line: say why a person with a rising fever feels cold at 39 degrees Celsius."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the negative feedback response to heat and to cold through the hypothalamus and the effectors and explain the mechanism of fever."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Thermoregulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-lab-metabolic-rate","name":"Metabolic rate measurement","can":"Estimate metabolic rate from measured or simulated oxygen consumption and compare resting values with values after activity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A thirty minute exercise session is calculated to have cost 300 kcal. Using 5 kcal per liter of oxygen, what was the average oxygen consumption during the session?\nA) About 10 L/min  |  B) About 2 L/min\nC) About 1 L/min  |  D) About 0.5 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 2 L/min","why":"Work backwards in two steps: 300 kcal over 30 minutes is 10 kcal/min, and dividing by 5 kcal per liter gives 2 L/min. That is about eight times resting oxygen consumption, or roughly 8 METs, which is a plausible vigorous workout and a good check that the answer is sensible. About 10 L/min would exceed the maximum oxygen uptake of nearly any human, so it can be ruled out on physiological grounds alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"A subject consumes oxygen at 0.30 L/min. Using 4.8 kcal per liter, what is their energy expenditure per minute?\nA) About 16 kcal per minute  |  B) About 14.4 kcal per minute\nC) About 1.44 kcal per minute  |  D) About 0.16 kcal per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 1.44 kcal per minute","why":"Multiply the oxygen flow by the caloric equivalent: 0.30 L/min times 4.8 kcal/L gives 1.44 kcal/min. Sanity check the answer against a day: 1.44 times 1440 minutes is about 2070 kcal, a plausible daily figure for a person slightly above basal. About 0.16 comes from dividing instead of multiplying, and 14.4 is off by a factor of ten."},{"t":"mc","q":"A 70 kg subject rests at 1 MET. What is their absolute oxygen consumption?\nA) About 35 mL/min  |  B) About 245 mL/min\nC) About 700 mL/min  |  D) About 2450 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 245 mL/min","why":"One MET is 3.5 mL/kg/min, so 3.5 times 70 kg gives 245 mL/min, which is right at the expected resting value of about a quarter liter per minute. That agreement is not a coincidence: the MET was defined so that resting equals one. About 2450 mL/min would be roughly 10 METs, near maximal effort for many adults, and 35 mL/min drops a factor of ten."},{"t":"mc","q":"A subject's oxygen consumption rises from 0.25 L/min at rest to 1.50 L/min during a step test. By what factor has their metabolic rate increased?\nA) About threefold  |  B) About twelvefold\nC) About twentyfold  |  D) About sixfold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About sixfold","why":"Because energy expenditure is directly proportional to oxygen consumption, the ratio of the two oxygen values is the ratio of the metabolic rates, and 1.50 divided by 0.25 is 6. In MET terms the subject is working at about 6 METs, typical of a vigorous stair climb. There is no need to convert to kilocalories first, since the same conversion factor appears in numerator and denominator and cancels."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a subject stops exercising, oxygen consumption stays above resting for several minutes. What accounts for this?\nA) Energy stores are being restored and lactate cleared\nB) The gas analyzer drifts after heavy breathing\nC) Oxygen is still trapped in the exercising muscles\nD) Metabolic rate has risen permanently\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Energy stores are being restored and lactate cleared","why":"During exercise some ATP came from anaerobic sources, and afterward the body must use oxygen to rebuild creatine phosphate, convert lactate back to usable substrate, and restore elevated body temperature and catecholamine driven metabolism to baseline. That extra oxygen use is the recovery debt. Analyzer drift would be an equipment artifact rather than physiology, and the elevation resolves within minutes to hours rather than being permanent."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 75 kg subject breathes through a gas analyzer. At rest her oxygen consumption is 0.30 L/min. After 10 minutes of stepping it is 1.50 L/min. When she stops and sits down, oxygen consumption falls but stays above the resting value for several minutes. Use 4.8 kcal per liter of oxygen.\nWhat is her resting energy expenditure, and why does oxygen consumption stay above resting after she stops?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 1.4 kcal/min at rest; oxygen consumption stays elevated because the body is rebuilding creatine phosphate, clearing lactate, and refilling oxygen stores used during the exercise","why":"Resting expenditure is 0.30 times 4.8, which is 1.44 kcal/min. Exercise is 1.50 times 4.8, or 7.2 kcal/min, a fivefold increase. The elevated consumption after exercise is the oxygen debt, or excess postexercise oxygen consumption. During the first minutes of exercise, ATP came partly from creatine phosphate and anaerobic glycolysis, which need no oxygen at the time but must be paid for afterward. Restoring creatine phosphate, converting lactate back to glucose or oxidizing it, and reloading myoglobin and hemoglobin all consume oxygen after the work has stopped."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a bar chart with oxygen consumption in liters per minute on the vertical and four bars across the bottom: lying quietly, sitting, walking, and hard exercise. Draw the bars at believable relative heights, not exact values. Beside the chart, write out the conversion from liters of oxygen to kilocalories per minute and carry one bar through it. One line: say which bar comes closest to a true basal measurement, and name one condition it still fails."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time on the horizontal, oxygen consumption on the vertical, and plot a person through rest, then three minutes of exercise, then recovery. Show the rise, the plateau during work, and the slow fall after stopping. Shade the area above resting level after exercise ended. One line: say what the body is still paying for in that shaded area, and why oxygen use does not drop the moment the work stops."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to estimate metabolic rate from measured or simulated oxygen consumption and compare resting values with values after activity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Metabolic rate measurement. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Foundations of Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w1-levels-function","name":"Physiology and levels of function","can":"Define physiology and place a given process at the correct level of organization from molecule to organism, then state the level at which that process is best explained.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A researcher measures how fast a single isolated intestinal epithelial cell takes up glucose. At what level of organization is this measurement made?\nA) Molecular  |  B) Tissue\nC) Cellular  |  D) Organ\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cellular","why":"The unit being measured is one whole cell performing a function, which places the measurement at the cellular level. Molecular would be the behavior of the SGLT transporter protein by itself. Tissue would be a sheet of epithelium, and organ would be the intestine as a whole, both of which involve many cells acting together."},{"t":"mc","q":"Swallowing moves food from the mouth through the pharynx and esophagus into the stomach. Which level of organization best explains this process?\nA) Cellular  |  B) Molecular\nC) Organ system  |  D) Tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Organ system","why":"Swallowing requires several organs acting in a coordinated sequence, which is exactly what an organ system does, so the digestive system level is the right one. Cellular and molecular events certainly occur in each muscle fiber, but neither one explains the ordered movement from one organ to the next. A single tissue cannot produce a sequence spanning three separate organs."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student says that the left ventricle generates enough pressure to eject blood into the aorta. This statement describes function at which level?\nA) Tissue  |  B) Organ system\nC) Cellular  |  D) Organ\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Organ","why":"The left ventricle is one chamber of the heart and the heart is a single organ, so pressure generation by the ventricle is organ level function. Tissue level would describe cardiac muscle contracting. Cellular level would describe a myocyte shortening. Organ system level would include the vessels and the blood, not the ventricle alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Insulin binds its receptor on a liver cell and changes the shape of the receptor protein. Where does this event belong?\nA) The tissue level  |  B) The molecular level\nC) The organ level  |  D) The organ system level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The molecular level","why":"A hormone and a receptor are both molecules, and the binding event is a change in molecular shape, so it is molecular. Tissue and organ levels describe what the liver does after the signal is received, such as storing glucose. The organ system level would describe the endocrine control of blood glucose across the whole body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sheets of alveolar epithelium and the capillary endothelium next to them form the surface across which gases diffuse in the lung. That diffusion surface is best described at which level?\nA) Organelle  |  B) Molecular\nC) Organ system  |  D) Tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Tissue","why":"Two thin layers of similar cells acting as one barrier is a tissue level description of the respiratory membrane. Organelle and molecular levels are far too small to describe a diffusion surface. Organ system would be the whole respiratory system including airways and the muscles of breathing."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Every physiological process is best explained at the molecular level, because all higher functions are built from molecules.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Each process is best explained at the level where the important interactions happen. Blood pressure control, for example, is explained at the organ system level, even though molecules are involved at every step.","why":"Molecules are the building blocks, but knowing the molecules does not tell you how blood pressure is defended. That needs baroreceptors, nerves, the heart, and vessels acting together. Physiology works across levels, and a good explanation picks the level where the mechanism actually operates."},{"t":"draw","q":"Build your own ladder. Pick a process, not oxygen delivery, and draw it at all six levels, molecule up to organism. One small box per level, labeled. Then break one rung and write, level by level, what stops working above it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick your own process, not oxygen delivery. Draw the same thing twice. First as anatomy sees it, the structures and their parts. Then as physiology sees it, the same thing working, with arrows for what moves and what changes. Under each drawing, one line naming the question it answers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define physiology and place a given process at the correct level of organization from molecule to organism, then state the level at which that process is best explained."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Physiology and levels of function. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-structure-function","name":"Structure and function relationship","can":"Predict how a change in the structure of a molecule, cell, tissue, or organ alters its function, using examples from two different organ systems.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A disease strips myelin from peripheral motor axons. What happens to conduction velocity and the strength of muscle activation?\nA) Conduction slows and muscle activation weakens\nB) Conduction speeds up and muscle activation strengthens\nC) Conduction slows but muscle activation is unchanged\nD) Conduction is unchanged but muscle activation weakens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Conduction slows and muscle activation weakens","why":"Without myelin the action potential must be regenerated continuously along the membrane, which slows it and can cause the signal to fail before it reaches the terminal, so fewer fibers are recruited on time and force drops. Demyelination never speeds conduction, since the insulation is what allows the jump between nodes. Slowing and failure occur together, so muscle activation cannot stay normal, and conduction cannot be normal while the axon is bare."},{"t":"mc","q":"An arteriole constricts so that its radius is halved. Given that resistance varies with the fourth power of the radius, resistance in that vessel becomes about how many times greater?\nA) Two times greater  |  B) Four times greater\nC) Sixteen times greater  |  D) Eight times greater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Sixteen times greater","why":"Halving the radius means multiplying resistance by two raised to the fourth power, which is sixteen, so small changes in arteriolar diameter produce very large changes in flow. Two times would apply if resistance varied directly with radius. Four times would apply to a squared relationship, and eight times to a cubed one. The fourth power dependence is what makes arterioles the main control point for blood flow."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the wall between an alveolus and a pulmonary capillary extremely thin?\nA) A thin wall increases the resistance of the airway\nB) A thin wall lets alveoli store more air\nC) A short diffusion distance speeds gas exchange\nD) A thin wall keeps blood from entering the capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A short diffusion distance speeds gas exchange","why":"Diffusion rate falls as the distance the gas must cross rises, so a barrier only a fraction of a micrometer thick lets oxygen and carbon dioxide move quickly between air and blood. Air volume is set by alveolar size, not wall thickness. Airway resistance is a property of the conducting tubes, not the exchange surface. Blood must enter the capillary for exchange to happen at all."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a defect in a red blood cell membrane protein that makes her red cells small and spherical instead of biconcave discs. Her hemoglobin is 10 g/dL (normal 12 to 16) and her spleen is enlarged.\nPredict why her red cells are destroyed faster than normal ones as they pass through the narrow channels of the spleen.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"A sphere has no spare membrane, so the cell cannot deform to squeeze through narrow spaces and is trapped and destroyed","why":"A biconcave disc has extra membrane relative to its volume, and that slack is what lets a red cell fold to pass through channels narrower than itself. A sphere is the shape with the least surface for a given volume, so there is no slack. The cell cannot deform, gets stuck in the spleen's narrow passages, and is removed. Fewer surviving cells means a lower hemoglobin."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In one patient a mutation makes the cilia on airway epithelial cells unable to beat. In another patient a different mutation makes airway mucus so thick and sticky that it cannot be moved. Both patients have repeated lung infections.\nWhat do the two structural changes have in common at the level of function?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Both stop the mucus escalator, so trapped particles and bacteria are not cleared from the airways","why":"Airway clearance needs two structures working together: a mucus layer that traps particles and cilia that sweep it upward. Break either one and the function is lost. Cilia that cannot beat leave normal mucus sitting still. Mucus that is too sticky cannot be moved by normal cilia. Either way, bacteria stay in the lung and infection follows. Different structures, same functional loss."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a person with emphysema (loss of alveolar walls) and a person whose intestinal villi have been flattened by celiac disease share the same kind of functional problem. Name the shared problem and trace it in each organ.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"both structural changes reduce surface area; exchange across a membrane depends on the surface area available; fewer alveolar walls means less oxygen diffuses into the blood per breath; flattened villi means less nutrient absorbed per meal; the shared problem is reduced surface for transfer across a membrane; the amount of function a structure can do is set by its shape","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one structure from this week and label the single feature that makes its job possible. Then sketch what would stop working if that feature changed."},{"t":"draw","q":"List three structures from this week. Beside each, write the one property its function depends on, in six words or fewer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict how a change in the structure of a molecule, cell, tissue, or organ alters its function, using examples from two different organ systems."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Structure and function relationship. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-homeostasis","name":"Homeostasis defined","can":"Define homeostasis, regulated variable, and setpoint, and distinguish homeostasis from chemical equilibrium and from steady state.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which described situation requires continuous energy input to stay constant?\nA) Plasma calcium held near 10 mg/dL while calcium moves between bone, gut, and urine\nB) Oxygen and nitrogen concentrations in a sealed tank of air\nC) The pH of a buffer solution sitting on a bench\nD) The salt concentration of seawater in a closed jar\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Plasma calcium held near 10 mg/dL while calcium moves between bone, gut, and urine","why":"Plasma calcium is constant only because hormones drive absorption, bone exchange, and renal handling, and every one of those transport steps costs energy. The sealed tank, the bench buffer, and the closed jar are all at equilibrium and stay constant with nothing being spent. Recognizing the cost of stability is what separates steady state from equilibrium."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does homeostasis mean?\nA) Keeping every internal variable at exactly one fixed number\nB) The state in which forward and reverse reactions proceed at equal rates\nC) Active maintenance of the internal environment within a narrow range compatible with life\nD) The tendency of body fluids to match the composition of the outside world\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Active maintenance of the internal environment within a narrow range compatible with life","why":"Homeostasis is regulation, meaning the body senses deviations and acts to bring a variable back into an acceptable range, which is why the range matters more than a single number. Perfect constancy never occurs, since regulated variables oscillate around their setpoints. Equal forward and reverse rates describe chemical equilibrium, which is a different idea. Matching the outside world would abolish the gradients life depends on."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a regulated variable?\nA) Any quantity that changes during the day\nB) A quantity the body monitors and actively holds within a narrow range\nC) A quantity that is altered in order to hold something else constant\nD) A quantity that cannot be measured in the clinic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A quantity the body monitors and actively holds within a narrow range","why":"A regulated variable such as core temperature, plasma osmolarity, or mean arterial pressure is sensed by receptors and defended by reflexes. Many quantities change during the day without being defended, so mere variation is not the test. A quantity that is changed in order to protect something else is a controlled variable, such as heart rate serving blood pressure. Measurability in the clinic has nothing to do with whether the body regulates it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a setpoint?\nA) The highest value a variable can reach before damage occurs\nB) The average of all measurements taken over one day\nC) The point at which a reflex pathway crosses the spinal cord\nD) The value of a regulated variable that the control system defends\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The value of a regulated variable that the control system defends","why":"The setpoint is the target the integrating center compares incoming information against, and any deviation from it drives a corrective response. The upper limit before damage is a tolerance, not a target. Where a reflex crosses the cord is anatomy of the pathway. A daily average may sit near the setpoint but it is a description of data rather than the value being defended."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A person can be in steady state for a variable, with intake equal to output, while that variable sits outside its homeostatic range.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"A patient with kidney disease may hold plasma potassium steady at 5.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0) for months. Intake matches output, so it is a steady state, but the control system has failed to bring the value back into range. Stable is not the same as healthy."},{"t":"bd","q":"Compare a healthy person's plasma glucose with glucose in a sealed beaker where glucose has stopped moving between two compartments. Explain why one is homeostasis and the other is equilibrium, and predict what happens to each if the energy supply is cut.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"plasma glucose is a regulated variable held near a setpoint by sensors and effectors; holding it there costs energy (insulin, glucagon, transport, liver metabolism); in the beaker no energy is being spent, the gradient has run down, and net movement has stopped; equilibrium has no sensor, setpoint, or correction; cutting energy in the body lets glucose drift out of range toward equilibrium; cutting energy changes nothing in the beaker because none was being used","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Write the definition of homeostasis, then underline the three words doing the real work. Beside each underlined word, one line on why it matters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two graphs side by side, a value in steady state and a value at equilibrium. On each, label what is still moving and what it costs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define homeostasis, regulated variable, and setpoint, and distinguish homeostasis from chemical equilibrium and from steady state."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Homeostasis defined. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-feedback-components","name":"Feedback loop components","can":"Diagram a negative feedback loop labeling stimulus, sensor, afferent path, integrating center, efferent path, effector, and response, and trace body temperature or blood glucose through every step.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person becomes overheated and sweat glands begin to secrete. Which loop component are the sweat glands?\nA) The effector  |  B) The afferent pathway\nC) The sensor  |  D) The integrating center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The effector","why":"Sweat glands perform the action that removes heat, which is the definition of an effector. Sensors detect temperature and do not act on it. The afferent pathway carries information inward. The integrating center for this loop is the hypothalamus, not the gland."},{"t":"mc","q":"Insulin released by the pancreas travels in the blood to the liver and muscle. Which component of the feedback loop is that travel?\nA) The afferent pathway  |  B) The stimulus\nC) The response  |  D) The efferent pathway\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The efferent pathway","why":"The efferent limb carries the command from the integrating center to the effectors, and a hormone in the bloodstream does exactly that job. The afferent limb would carry information toward the center instead. The stimulus was the rise in glucose. The response is the fall in glucose that follows insulin action."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a meal, blood glucose rises from 90 to 140 mg/dL. In the glucose feedback loop, what is that rise?\nA) The stimulus  |  B) The response\nC) The effector output  |  D) The setpoint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The stimulus","why":"The stimulus is the change in the regulated variable that starts the loop, and here it is the postprandial rise in glucose. The response is the later fall back toward normal. Effector output would be glucose uptake and storage by liver and muscle. The setpoint is the target value the system defends, not the deviation itself."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sensory neuron carries impulses from a cold thermoreceptor in the skin up to the hypothalamus. Which component is this neuron?\nA) The efferent pathway  |  B) The afferent pathway\nC) The integrating center  |  D) The effector\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The afferent pathway","why":"Information traveling from a receptor toward the comparator is the afferent limb, and this is the classic place students reverse the terms. The efferent limb would run from the hypothalamus out to skin blood vessels or to skeletal muscle. The integrating center is the hypothalamus itself, and the effectors are the vessels and muscles that respond."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A drug destroys the cold sensitive thermoreceptors in the skin but leaves the temperature sensitive neurons in the hypothalamus intact. A person taking the drug steps out into 0 C (32 F) air.\nPredict the effect on the shivering response.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Shivering starts later, only after core temperature has fallen enough for the hypothalamic sensors to detect it","why":"The loop has two sets of sensors. Skin receptors detect cold at the surface almost immediately, before core temperature moves, and that early warning lets the hypothalamus start shivering while core temperature is still normal. Without them the integrating center must wait until the core itself cools. The loop still works, but it responds later, so core temperature falls further before the correction begins."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In the blood glucose loop after a meal, insulin is the afferent signal that carries information from the sensor to the integrating center.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Insulin is the efferent signal. The beta cell senses the glucose and integrates the information itself, then releases insulin, which carries the command out to the liver and muscle.","why":"Afferent means toward the center, efferent means away from it. In an endocrine reflex the sensor and integrating center are often the same cell, so there is no separate afferent path at all. The hormone is the outgoing signal to the effector. Confusing the two puts the response before the decision."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the five component reflex pathway top down and label all five plus the feedback arrow. Labels only, no sentences."},{"t":"draw","q":"Fill in the five components for thermoregulation on a cold day. Then mark which component you would suspect first if the patient never shivered."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to diagram a negative feedback loop labeling stimulus, sensor, afferent path, integrating center, efferent path, effector, and response, and trace body temperature or blood glucose through every step."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Feedback loop components. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-feedback-types","name":"Negative and positive feedback","can":"Distinguish negative from positive feedback by the direction of the response and give a physiological example of each, explaining why a positive feedback loop needs an outside event to end it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Blood pressure falls, baroreceptors fire less, and heart rate rises to bring pressure back up. Which type of feedback is this?\nA) Positive feedback, because heart rate rose\nB) Positive feedback, because two variables changed together\nC) Feedforward, because the heart acted before pressure changed\nD) Negative feedback, because the response opposes the fall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Negative feedback, because the response opposes the fall","why":"The sign of a loop is judged by whether the response opposes or amplifies the original change, and here a fall in pressure produced a correction that raises pressure. Heart rate rising is the mechanism of the correction, not evidence of amplification. The response came after the pressure fell, so it is not anticipatory. Two variables changing together says nothing about the sign of the loop."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma osmolarity rises, antidiuretic hormone is released, the kidney conserves water, and osmolarity falls back toward normal. Classify this loop.\nA) Positive feedback  |  B) Negative feedback\nC) Feedforward control  |  D) Acclimatization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Negative feedback","why":"The response lowered the variable that had risen, which is opposition and therefore negative feedback. Positive feedback would have driven osmolarity higher still. Feedforward would act before osmolarity changed, for instance thirst triggered by the taste of salty food. Acclimatization is a slow adaptive change over days, not a single corrective loop."},{"t":"mc","q":"In negative feedback, how does the response relate to the stimulus?\nA) The response moves the variable further in the same direction\nB) The response moves the variable in the opposite direction from the stimulus\nC) The response has no effect on the variable that started the loop\nD) The response changes the setpoint instead of the variable\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The response moves the variable in the opposite direction from the stimulus","why":"Negative feedback opposes change, so a rise triggers a response that lowers the variable and a fall triggers a response that raises it, which is what produces stability. Reinforcing the change is positive feedback. A response with no effect on the variable would not close a loop at all. Moving the setpoint is a separate event, as happens in fever."},{"t":"mc","q":"In positive feedback, how does the response relate to the stimulus?\nA) The response cancels the original change\nB) The response amplifies the original change\nC) The response holds the variable exactly at the setpoint\nD) The response alternates between opposing and amplifying the change\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The response amplifies the original change","why":"Positive feedback drives the variable further from where it started, which is useful when the body needs a process to run to completion quickly. Canceling the change describes negative feedback. Holding a variable at a setpoint is the goal of negative feedback, not positive. No physiological loop alternates between opposing and amplifying its own stimulus."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A positive feedback loop shuts itself off once the regulated variable returns to its setpoint.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A positive feedback loop has no setpoint to return to. It drives the variable further in the same direction and only stops when an event outside the loop ends it, such as delivery of the baby or inactivation of the sodium channels.","why":"In negative feedback the response reduces the stimulus, so the loop winds down on its own. In positive feedback the response increases the stimulus, so the loop speeds up. Nothing inside the loop can stop it. That is why every positive feedback loop in the body is paired with an external stopping event."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain why blood clotting is a positive feedback loop, what stops it, and why the body could not use positive feedback to control body temperature.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"each step produces more of the signal that started it (platelets recruit more platelets, thrombin activates more clotting); the response amplifies the original change instead of opposing it; it ends with an outside event: the wound is sealed, no new surface is exposed, or anticoagulants limit spread; positive feedback drives the variable away from its start, not back toward a setpoint; temperature needs a response that opposes the change (negative feedback) to stay near 37 C (98.6 F); a positive loop on temperature would push it higher or lower without limit","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one negative feedback loop and one positive feedback loop as arrow diagrams. On each, mark what makes it stop."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw clotting as a positive feedback loop, then show what ends it. Underneath, write one line on why the body can afford to use positive feedback here."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish negative from positive feedback by the direction of the response and give a physiological example of each, explaining why a positive feedback loop needs an outside event to end it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Negative and positive feedback. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-feedforward","name":"Feedforward control and acclimatization","can":"Explain anticipatory feedforward control and acclimatization and identify which one is operating in a given scenario.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An athlete's heart rate rises from 65 to 90 beats per minute in the moments before a race begins, while still standing still. Which mechanism is operating?\nA) Acclimatization\nB) Feedforward control\nC) Negative feedback triggered by rising carbon dioxide\nD) Positive feedback in the sinoatrial node\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Feedforward control","why":"No exercise has occurred yet, so no metabolic error has been detected; the nervous system is acting on the expectation of exercise to prepare the circulation. Acclimatization would take days and would not appear in a single moment. Carbon dioxide has not yet risen, so a feedback response to it is not possible. The sinoatrial node is being driven by autonomic input rather than by an amplifying loop."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person who moves to a city at 3500 meters has a hematocrit of 52 percent after three weeks, compared with 44 percent on arrival. Which mechanism explains the change?\nA) Acclimatization  |  B) Feedforward control\nC) Positive feedback  |  D) A reset of the oxygen setpoint by the medulla\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Acclimatization","why":"Weeks of sustained low arterial PO2 raised erythropoietin and red cell production, which is a reversible adjustment to a new environment. Feedforward would require an anticipatory cue and would act in seconds to minutes. Nothing in the sequence amplifies the original stimulus. Arterial oxygen is not defended around a setpoint in the way temperature or pressure are."},{"t":"mc","q":"After ten days of outdoor work in the desert, a laborer sweats sooner, sweats more, and the sweat contains less Na+. Which mechanism is this?\nA) Acclimatization  |  B) Negative feedback within a single work shift\nC) Feedforward control  |  D) Positive feedback driving sweat production\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Acclimatization","why":"Repeated heat exposure over days raises aldosterone driven sodium reabsorption in the sweat duct and lowers the threshold for sweating, both of which improve heat tolerance and reverse after weeks indoors. Feedforward would be an anticipatory response to a cue rather than a change in gland behavior over days. A single shift of negative feedback cannot change the composition of sweat. Nothing about sweating amplifies the heat load."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing correctly matches the mechanism to the timescale on which it acts?\nA) Feedforward acts over days to weeks, acclimatization in seconds\nB) Feedforward acts in seconds to minutes, acclimatization over days to weeks\nC) Both act within seconds of the triggering cue\nD) Both require weeks of repeated exposure before they appear\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Feedforward acts in seconds to minutes, acclimatization over days to weeks","why":"Feedforward is a fast nervous or hormonal response to a predictive cue, while acclimatization requires changes in gene expression and tissue composition that take much longer to build and to fade. Assigning days to weeks to feedforward and seconds to acclimatization reverses the two. Acclimatization cannot occur in seconds because protein synthesis and cell production are involved. Feedforward needs no prior exposure period, only a recognized cue."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A trained runner wears a heart rate monitor. Standing still at the start line, her heart rate climbs from 60 to 95 beats per minute (normal resting 60 to 100). The start is delayed 5 minutes, and during the wait her heart rate settles back to 70.\nWhat kind of control raised her heart rate at the line, and what does the fall during the delay show?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Feedforward control. The brain anticipated exercise and raised heart rate before any error existed, and the anticipatory signal faded when the expected exercise did not come","why":"She has not moved, so no regulated variable has changed and there is no error for feedback to correct. The rise comes from the brain predicting the coming demand and preparing the heart in advance. That is feedforward. When the cue (the imminent start) goes away, the preparation fades. Feedforward exists to shrink the deviation that would otherwise occur when exercise begins."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Acclimatization to altitude takes days to weeks because it depends on slow changes such as making more red blood cells, whereas the rise in ventilation at the start of a race begins within seconds.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The two are easy to mix up because both help the body cope before trouble is severe. The difference is timescale and mechanism. Feedforward uses an existing pathway triggered by a predictive cue and acts at once. Acclimatization changes the amount of machinery, such as red cell mass or sweat gland output, and that takes time to build and time to fade."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a timeline of eating a meal. Mark where feedforward starts and where feedback takes over."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a setpoint as a horizontal line. Show what fever does to it, and mark at each stage what the patient feels."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain anticipatory feedforward control and acclimatization and identify which one is operating in a given scenario."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Feedforward control and acclimatization. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-control-pathways","name":"Local and reflex control pathways","can":"Distinguish a local control pathway from a long distance reflex pathway and classify a given response as one or the other.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Blood pressure falls when a person stands, and within seconds arterioles throughout the body constrict. Classify this pathway.\nA) Local control by each arteriole sensing its own pressure\nB) A long distance neural reflex\nC) A paracrine response of endothelial cells\nD) Acclimatization to the upright posture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A long distance neural reflex","why":"Baroreceptors in the great vessels detect the fall, the medulla integrates it, and sympathetic nerves reach vessels all over the body, so a signal travels a long way to widespread effectors. Local sensing could not produce a coordinated body wide response within seconds. Paracrine signals cannot reach vessels in distant limbs. Acclimatization takes days and would not appear in a single act of standing."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mast cell in the skin releases histamine after an insect bite, and the capillaries a few micrometers away become leaky. Classify this response.\nA) Long distance endocrine reflex\nB) Autocrine feedback on the mast cell\nC) Long distance neural reflex\nD) Local control through paracrine signaling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Local control through paracrine signaling","why":"Histamine diffuses a very short distance to act on neighboring vessels, which is paracrine signaling and therefore local. An endocrine reflex would require histamine to travel in blood to distant targets. No sensor, nerve, or brain center is involved in this step. The response occurs on vessels rather than on the mast cell itself, so it is not autocrine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Blood glucose falls to 60 mg/dL, alpha cells secrete glucagon, and the liver releases glucose into the blood. Classify this pathway.\nA) Local control within the pancreas\nB) A long distance neural reflex\nC) A long distance endocrine reflex\nD) Paracrine signaling between liver cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A long distance endocrine reflex","why":"The sensor sits in the pancreas, the signal travels in blood, and the effector is the liver, so the pathway spans two organs using a hormone. Local control would keep both sensing and response inside the pancreas. No neuron is required for this loop, although the nervous system can modulate it. Liver cells respond to glucagon rather than signaling one another."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature most reliably tells you a response used a long distance pathway rather than local control?\nA) The effector is in a different organ from the sensor\nB) The response involved a chemical signal\nC) The response was fast\nD) The response corrected a deviation from a setpoint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The effector is in a different organ from the sensor","why":"Distance between where the change is detected and where the response occurs is the defining test, because bridging that gap requires nerves or hormones. Speed does not separate the two, since local responses are often the faster of the pair. Chemical signals occur in both categories, as paracrines and as hormones. Correcting a deviation describes negative feedback, which both local and long distance pathways can perform."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with a small skin burn has redness and warmth confined to the burned area. His core temperature is 37 C (98.6 F), heart rate 72 beats per minute (normal 60 to 100), and blood pressure 118/76 mmHg (normal below 120/80).\nThe response is confined to the injured tissue with no change in whole body variables. What does that pattern tell you about the control pathway?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Local control. Chemicals released by the injured cells act on nearby blood vessels, without involving the nervous or endocrine systems","why":"Injured cells release paracrine signals such as histamine that dilate the arterioles a few cell widths away. The message never leaves the tissue, so the effect stays put. If the nervous or endocrine system were carrying the signal, you would expect effects far from the burn, such as changes in heart rate or blood pressure, and there are none."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A reflex pathway is faster than local control because it does not need the nervous system.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A reflex pathway uses the nervous system or the endocrine system to carry information over a long distance. Local control acts within the tissue where the change happened, using paracrine signals, and it does not need either system.","why":"The defining feature of a reflex is distance: sensor in one place, integrating center somewhere else, effector in a third place, with a nerve or hormone connecting them. Local control keeps everything in one neighborhood. Neither is always faster, but the statement reverses which one needs the nervous system."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a local control example and a reflex control example side by side. On each, mark how far the signal travels."},{"t":"draw","q":"Standing up suddenly: draw the reflex, label all five components, and write one line on why local control could not have handled it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish a local control pathway from a long distance reflex pathway and classify a given response as one or the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Local and reflex control pathways. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-mass-balance","name":"Mass balance","can":"Apply the mass balance equation to a solute or to body water and calculate the intake, production, and output combination that holds the amount in the body constant.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An adult takes in 2200 mL of water in food and drink and produces 300 mL of metabolic water. Losses are 1500 mL urine, 800 mL from skin and lungs, and 100 mL in feces. What is the daily water balance?\nA) Negative by 100 mL  |  B) Positive by 400 mL\nC) Balanced at zero  |  D) Positive by 100 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Positive by 100 mL","why":"Total input is 2200 plus 300, which is 2500 mL, and total output is 1500 plus 800 plus 100, which is 2400 mL, leaving a gain of 100 mL. Getting a negative answer means the terms were subtracted in the wrong order. Zero would require the two totals to match exactly. Four hundred milliliters results from forgetting the fecal and insensible losses."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person eats 150 mmol of sodium per day and loses 5 mmol in sweat and 5 mmol in feces. How much sodium must appear in the urine each day to stay in balance?\nA) 140 mmol  |  B) 160 mmol\nC) 150 mmol  |  D) 145 mmol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 140 mmol","why":"Output must equal the 150 mmol taken in, and 10 mmol already leaves by non renal routes, so the kidney must excrete the remaining 140 mmol. Excreting 150 mmol would put the person in negative balance by 10 mmol a day. One hundred sixty would add the non renal losses instead of subtracting them. One hundred forty five accounts for only one of the two other routes."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient drinks 3.0 L of water per day and loses 1.0 L per day through skin, lungs, and feces combined. What urine output holds total body water constant?\nA) 1.0 L per day  |  B) 3.0 L per day\nC) 4.0 L per day  |  D) 2.0 L per day\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 2.0 L per day","why":"Total output must match the 3.0 L input, and 1.0 L is already accounted for elsewhere, so the kidney must produce 2.0 L. Producing 1.0 L would leave a gain of 1.0 L per day. Three liters of urine on top of the other losses would create a 1.0 L daily deficit. Four liters would deepen that deficit further."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A hiker sweats 1.2 L per hour for 3 hours. During the hike she drinks 1.5 L total, loses 0.2 L in breath, produces no urine, and makes 0.1 L of metabolic water.\nWhat is the net change in her body water at the end of the hike?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"A loss of 2.2 L","why":"Work it in order. Output is sweat 3.6 L plus breath 0.2 L, which is 3.8 L. Input is drink 1.5 L plus metabolic water 0.1 L, which is 1.6 L. Change equals input minus output, so 1.6 minus 3.8 gives a deficit of 2.2 L. With sweat being hypo-osmotic to plasma, her plasma osmolarity is also rising, which is why she is thirsty."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient on a fixed hospital diet takes in 100 mmol of potassium per day. His urine contains 80 mmol per day and his feces 10 mmol per day. His plasma potassium today is 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0).\nPredict what happens over the coming days if nothing changes.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Total body potassium rises by about 10 mmol per day, and plasma potassium will drift upward unless output increases","why":"Intake is 100 mmol and output is 90 mmol, so the body gains 10 mmol per day. A normal plasma value today only tells you where he is now, not which way he is moving. Positive balance means the amount in the body is climbing, and sooner or later plasma potassium will follow unless the kidneys raise excretion to match intake, which is what a healthy kidney does."},{"t":"bd","q":"A person eats 200 mmol of sodium per day and has had a stable weight for months. Write the mass balance equation for sodium, state how much sodium must leave the body each day and by what main route, then predict what happens to extracellular fluid volume on the first day if intake suddenly drops to 50 mmol while the kidneys keep excreting 200 mmol.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"change in body amount equals intake plus production minus output; the body does not make sodium, so output must equal intake, 200 mmol per day; most sodium leaves in urine, with small amounts in sweat and feces; after the drop, output exceeds intake by about 150 mmol on day one; body sodium falls, water follows sodium, so extracellular fluid volume shrinks; the kidneys then cut excretion until output again matches intake","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Write the mass balance equation and label every term. Then draw a body outline with arrows in and out for sodium, labeling which arrow is which term."},{"t":"draw","q":"Work a mass balance for one substance across one day. Then change a single term and show the new result underneath."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to apply the mass balance equation to a solute or to body water and calculate the intake, production, and output combination that holds the amount in the body constant."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Mass balance. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-fluid-compartments","name":"Body fluid compartments","can":"State the approximate volumes of total body water, intracellular fluid, extracellular fluid, plasma, and interstitial fluid in a 70 kg adult and compare the dominant solutes of the intracellular and extracellular compartments.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A 300 mg dose of a marker that stays inside blood vessels is injected. After mixing, its plasma concentration is 100 mg/L. What is the plasma volume?\nA) 0.3 L  |  B) 10 L\nC) 30 L  |  D) 3 L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 3 L","why":"Volume equals the amount given divided by the resulting concentration, so 300 mg divided by 100 mg/L is 3 L, which matches the expected plasma volume in an adult. Zero point three liters would come from dividing by 1000 mg/L. Thirty liters would come from a concentration of 10 mg/L. Ten liters matches no plausible plasma volume and does not follow from these numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Using 60 percent of body weight as total body water, how much water does a 50 kg adult have?\nA) About 20 L  |  B) About 25 L\nC) About 42 L  |  D) About 30 L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 30 L","why":"Sixty percent of 50 kg is 30 kg of water, which is about 30 L because a liter of water weighs about a kilogram. Twenty liters would be 40 percent, and 25 L would be 50 percent, both below the usual fraction. Forty two liters is the value for a 70 kg adult and cannot apply to a smaller person."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has 36 L of total body water. Using the usual two thirds to one third split, what is the intracellular volume?\nA) 12 L  |  B) 18 L\nC) 24 L  |  D) 9 L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 24 L","why":"Two thirds of 36 L is 24 L inside cells, leaving 12 L outside. Twelve liters is the extracellular volume, which is the other third. Eighteen liters would be half, which is not the standard division. Nine liters corresponds to a quarter and matches no compartment here."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an extracellular fluid volume of 12 L. Using the usual proportions, what is the plasma volume?\nA) 3 L  |  B) 6 L\nC) 9 L  |  D) 1.5 L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 3 L","why":"Plasma is about one quarter of extracellular fluid, and one quarter of 12 L is 3 L, leaving 9 L in the interstitium. Six liters would be half of the extracellular volume, far more plasma than the circulation holds. Nine liters is the interstitial share. One point five liters would be an eighth, which is too small."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which body fluid compartment is the largest by volume?\nA) Interstitial fluid  |  B) Plasma\nC) Intracellular fluid  |  D) Extracellular fluid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Intracellular fluid","why":"The intracellular compartment holds about two thirds of body water, roughly 28 L in a 70 kg adult, more than everything outside cells combined. Interstitial fluid is about 11 L and plasma about 3 L. Extracellular fluid at 14 L is the sum of those two and is still only half the intracellular volume."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a healthy adult, the extracellular fluid holds more of the total body water than the intracellular fluid does.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The intracellular fluid holds about two thirds of total body water (about 28 L of 42 L); the extracellular fluid holds about one third (14 L).","why":"Most body water is inside cells. The extracellular fluid is smaller but it is the compartment we can sample and treat, so it gets most of the clinical attention. Students often flip the two because plasma and interstitial fluid are discussed so much more often than cytosol."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the fluid compartments top down with the fractions. Use one border style for inside cells and a different one for outside."},{"t":"draw","q":"On a compartment drawing, mark where a blood sample is taken and which compartment you are actually drawing a conclusion about."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the approximate volumes of total body water, intracellular fluid, extracellular fluid, plasma, and interstitial fluid in a 70 kg adult and compare the dominant solutes of the intracellular and extracellular compartments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Body fluid compartments. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-compartment-shifts","name":"Compartment separation and clinical volume shifts","can":"Predict the direction of water movement between compartments when extracellular osmolarity rises or falls and name a clinical situation that produces each shift.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular osmolarity rises from 290 to 310 mOsm/L. Which way does water move, and what happens to cell volume?\nA) Water enters the cells and cell volume rises\nB) Water leaves the cells and cell volume falls\nC) Water does not move because the cell membrane blocks it\nD) Water leaves the cells and cell volume rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Water leaves the cells and cell volume falls","why":"Water follows the nonpenetrating solute, so a more concentrated extracellular fluid pulls water out of cells until osmolarity is equal again, and the cells shrink. Water entering cells would require the outside to be more dilute, not more concentrated. Membranes are highly permeable to water, so it does move. Losing water and gaining volume at the same time is a contradiction."},{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular osmolarity falls from 290 to 270 mOsm/L. What happens to the cells?\nA) They shrink as water moves out of them\nB) They shrink because low osmolarity dehydrates the cytoplasm\nC) They stay the same size because osmolarity affects only plasma\nD) They swell as water moves into them\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They swell as water moves into them","why":"With the outside now more dilute than the inside, water moves into the cells until the two osmolarities match, and the cells swell, which is dangerous in the fixed volume of the skull. Shrinkage requires the extracellular fluid to be the more concentrated side. Osmolarity changes reach interstitial fluid and therefore every cell in the body. Low extracellular osmolarity adds water to cells rather than removing it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which clinical situation raises extracellular osmolarity and shrinks cells?\nA) Prolonged sweating in the heat with no fluid intake\nB) Drinking several liters of plain water quickly\nC) Infusion of a large volume of isotonic saline\nD) A slow bleed that removes whole blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Prolonged sweating in the heat with no fluid intake","why":"Sweat is dilute compared with plasma, so sweating loses proportionally more water than salt and concentrates the extracellular fluid, pulling water out of cells. Drinking plain water dilutes the extracellular fluid and swells cells. Isotonic saline expands the extracellular volume without changing osmolarity. Whole blood loss removes fluid at the osmolarity of plasma, so it lowers volume without shifting water between compartments."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An 82 year old nursing home resident has eaten and drunk very little for four days. Her plasma sodium is 160 mmol/L (normal 135 to 145) and her plasma osmolarity is 330 mOsm/L (normal 280 to 296). She is confused.\nWhich way has water moved between her compartments, and what has that done to the volume of her brain cells?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Out of her cells into the extracellular fluid; brain cells have shrunk","why":"She has lost water without losing much solute, so her extracellular fluid is concentrated. Na+ cannot enter cells, so it acts as an impermeant solute and pulls water out of cells until the two compartments match. Her brain cells lose volume, which is why she is confused. Both compartments end up smaller and more concentrated."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient receives 1 L of 0.9 percent NaCl (isotonic saline, about 300 mOsm/L) by IV over one hour. Before and after the infusion her plasma sodium is 140 mmol/L (normal 135 to 145). Her plasma volume is about 3 L and her interstitial volume is about 11 L.\nWhere will most of this liter be an hour after the infusion, and what happens to her cell volume?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About three quarters moves into the interstitial fluid and one quarter stays in plasma; cells do not change volume","why":"Saline is isotonic, so it changes nothing about osmolarity and gives cells no reason to gain or lose water. The Na+ it contains is held out of cells by the pump, so the whole liter stays in the extracellular fluid. Within that space, water and ions cross capillary walls freely, so the liter distributes between plasma and interstitial fluid in proportion to their sizes, about 1 to 3. Roughly 250 mL stays in the blood and 750 mL ends up in the interstitium."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what happens to the extracellular and intracellular compartments when a patient is given one liter of 3 percent (hypertonic) saline through an IV. Trace the direction of water movement and the change in cell volume, and name one clinical reason this would be done.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"NaCl stays in the extracellular fluid because the pump keeps it out of cells; extracellular osmolarity rises; water moves out of cells into the extracellular fluid; cells shrink (intracellular volume falls); extracellular volume increases by more than the liter given; used to shrink swollen brain cells in cerebral edema or severe hyponatremia","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a patient with swollen ankles and a low blood pressure. Use arrows to show where the water went."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three scenarios: water gained in the vessels, water lost from the body, water moved into tissue. Predict blood pressure under each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the direction of water movement between compartments when extracellular osmolarity rises or falls and name a clinical situation that produces each shift."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Compartment separation and clinical volume shifts. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Quantitative Skills for Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w1-units-conversion","name":"Units and unit conversion","can":"Convert among the units used in physiology including molarity, osmolarity, milliequivalents, mmHg, liters per minute, and percent solutions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"During a treadmill test a subject's heart rate rises from 60 to 90 beats per minute while stroke volume falls from 80 mL to 60 mL. What happens to cardiac output?\nA) It falls from 4.8 to 4.2 L/min  |  B) It is unchanged at 4.8 L/min\nC) It rises from 4.8 to 5.4 L/min  |  D) It rises from 4.8 to 7.2 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises from 4.8 to 5.4 L/min","why":"Multiply each pair: 60 times 80 is 4800 mL/min, or 4.8 L/min, and 90 times 60 is 5400 mL/min, or 5.4 L/min, so output rises even though stroke volume dropped. 7.2 L/min comes from applying the new heart rate to the old stroke volume and forgetting that stroke volume changed. Saying it is unchanged assumes the two changes cancel exactly, which they would only if rate and volume moved by the same factor. A fall would require the drop in stroke volume to outweigh the rise in rate, and here it does not."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lab makes a solution containing NaCl at 140 mmol/L plus glucose at 5 mmol/L. NaCl dissociates completely and glucose does not. What is the total osmolarity?\nA) 145 mOsm/L  |  B) 285 mOsm/L\nC) 290 mOsm/L  |  D) 150 mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 285 mOsm/L","why":"Handle each solute by its own particle count and then add: NaCl gives 140 times 2, or 280 mOsm/L, and glucose gives 5 mOsm/L, for 285 mOsm/L in total. 145 mOsm/L adds the millimolar figures without doubling the salt. 290 mOsm/L doubles the glucose too, which no nondissociating sugar does. 150 mOsm/L drops the glucose contribution and mishandles the salt at the same time."},{"t":"mc","q":"A bag of 0.9 percent NaCl is hanging at the bedside. How many grams of NaCl does one liter of it contain?\nA) 0.9 grams  |  B) 9 grams\nC) 90 grams  |  D) 0.09 grams\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 9 grams","why":"Percent solutions are grams per 100 mL, and a liter is ten of those, so 0.9 g times 10 gives 9 g/L. Answering 0.9 g means reading the percent as grams per liter and skipping the conversion. 90 g multiplies by 100 instead of 10, which would be the answer if percent meant grams per 10 mL. 0.09 g divides where you should multiply."},{"t":"mc","q":"Normal saline is 154 mmol/L NaCl and NaCl dissociates completely. What is its approximate osmolarity?\nA) 77 mOsm/L  |  B) 154 mOsm/L\nC) 231 mOsm/L  |  D) 308 mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 308 mOsm/L","why":"Each NaCl gives two particles, so 154 mmol/L becomes 308 mOsm/L, which is why saline sits close to plasma at about 290 mOsm/L. Answering 154 treats NaCl as if it stayed intact in solution. 77 halves instead of doubling, the mistake you get from dividing by the number of ions. 231 would follow from assuming only half the NaCl dissociates, but dissociation of NaCl in dilute solution is essentially complete."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient's lab report lists plasma calcium as 5.0 mEq/L. The reference range on the sheet is given in different units, 2.2 to 2.6 mmol/L.\nConvert the value and predict whether this patient's calcium is in range.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"2.5 mmol/L, within range, because each calcium ion carries two charges so mEq is twice mmol","why":"Milliequivalents count charges, millimoles count ions. Calcium is Ca2+, so each ion carries two charges and 5.0 mEq/L is 2.5 mmol/L. That sits inside the 2.2 to 2.6 range, so the patient's calcium is normal. Reading the raw number against the range without converting would wrongly suggest a dangerous excess."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A 1 mmol/L solution of calcium chloride (CaCl2) has an osmolarity of about 1 mOsm/L.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. CaCl2 dissociates into one calcium ion and two chloride ions, so 1 mmol/L gives about 3 mOsm/L.","why":"Osmolarity counts every dissolved particle, not every molecule you started with. Glucose stays one particle, NaCl becomes two, CaCl2 becomes three. This matters because water follows particle count, so a solution's effect on cell volume depends on osmolarity, not molarity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Take one real lab value and walk it through every unit physiology reports it in. Glucose in mg/dL to mmol/L, or a pressure in mmHg to kPa. Show the conversion as a drawn chain, one box per step, with the factor written on the arrow between them. Then write one line on which unit a clinician would actually say out loud, and why that one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a number line for one quantity, say volume, and mark liters, milliliters and microlitres on it in the right places. Put three real physiological volumes on that line: a tidal breath, a drop of blood taken for a glucose stick, and the water in an adult body. Under it, write what goes wrong in a patient if you read one of them off by a factor of a thousand."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to convert among the units used in physiology including molarity, osmolarity, milliequivalents, mmHg, liters per minute, and percent solutions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Units and unit conversion. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-lab-graphing","name":"Graphing and data interpretation","can":"Construct a labeled graph with the independent variable on the x axis, and read slope, direction, and trend from a physiological data set.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Heart rate rises from 70 to 130 beats per minute as cycling workload rises from 0 to 120 watts, and the points fall on a straight line. What is the slope?\nA) 0.5 beats per minute per watt  |  B) 2 beats per minute per watt\nC) 60 beats per minute per watt  |  D) 0.5 watts per beat per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 0.5 beats per minute per watt","why":"Slope is the rise over the run: 130 minus 70 is 60 beats per minute, divided by 120 watts, giving 0.5 beats per minute per watt. Answering 2 divides the workload by the heart rate change and inverts the calculation. Answering 60 reports the rise alone and never divides by the run. The last option has the right number but the units upside down, which would describe watts per heartbeat rather than the response per unit of work."},{"t":"mc","q":"When you construct a graph of experimental data, which variable belongs on the x axis?\nA) The variable measured as the outcome\nB) The variable that showed the largest change\nC) The variable the experimenter set or manipulated\nD) The variable held constant in every trial\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The variable the experimenter set or manipulated","why":"The x axis carries the independent variable, the one you chose the values for, so the graph reads left to right as cause then effect. The measured outcome is the dependent variable and belongs on the y axis. Size of change has nothing to do with axis placement. A variable held constant does not appear on either axis, because it has only one value and would plot as a single point."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does the dependent variable belong on a properly constructed graph?\nA) On the y axis, the vertical axis\nB) On the x axis, the horizontal axis\nC) In the legend rather than on an axis\nD) On either axis, as long as both are labeled\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) On the y axis, the vertical axis","why":"The dependent variable is the response you measured, and by convention it goes on the vertical axis so that height reads as magnitude of the response. The x axis is reserved for the independent variable you controlled. A legend identifies which line is which when several conditions are plotted together, not which variable was measured. The convention is not optional, because a reader interprets slope as the change in the response per unit of the manipulated variable."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a graph with lung volume on the y axis and time on the x axis, what does the slope of the line represent?\nA) The total volume of air moved during the trial\nB) The volume present in the lungs at the start of the trace\nC) The average volume across the whole recording\nD) The rate at which volume is changing, that is, airflow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The rate at which volume is changing, that is, airflow","why":"Slope is the change on the y axis divided by the change on the x axis, so volume over time gives liters per second, which is flow. The total volume moved is read as the vertical distance between the start and the end of the trace, not its steepness. Starting volume is the y intercept. The average volume is a single summary height and carries no information about how fast air was moving."},{"t":"mc","q":"Besides the name of the variable, what must each axis of a physiology graph carry?\nA) The number of subjects tested\nB) The units in which the variable was measured\nC) The equation of the best fit line\nD) The name of the instrument used\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The units in which the variable was measured","why":"Without units the numbers are meaningless: a pressure of 90 could be mmHg or kPa, and a volume of 3 could be liters or milliliters. Sample size, instrument, and any fitted equation belong in the figure caption or the methods, where a reader looks for them. An axis label is doing its job when someone can read a value off the graph and state it correctly with its unit."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When plotting how heart rate changes with exercise intensity, heart rate belongs on the x axis.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Exercise intensity is the independent variable and belongs on the x axis. Heart rate responds to it, so heart rate is the dependent variable and goes on the y axis.","why":"The x axis carries the variable the experimenter set, and the y axis carries the response. Putting them the wrong way round flips the meaning of the slope: instead of beats per minute gained per watt, you would be reading watts per beat, which is not the question being asked."},{"t":"draw","q":"Plot the mission patient's sodium across the three days. Label both axes with units and mark the reference range on the graph."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a saturation curve. Label the plateau, then write one line on what the plateau means physiologically."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to construct a labeled graph with the independent variable on the x axis, and read slope, direction, and trend from a physiological data set."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Graphing and data interpretation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-lab-experimental-design","name":"Experimental design and controls","can":"Identify the hypothesis, independent variable, dependent variable, and control condition in a physiology experiment and state what the control rules out.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Students give volunteers 0, 100, or 200 mg of caffeine and record resting heart rate 30 minutes later. What is the independent variable?\nA) The resting heart rate recorded at the end\nB) The 30 minute waiting period before measuring\nC) The dose of caffeine given\nD) The number of volunteers who took part\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The dose of caffeine given","why":"The dose is the one thing set to different values by the team, which makes it the independent variable and puts it on the x axis. Resting heart rate is what they measured, so it is the dependent variable. The 30 minute wait is the same for everyone, which makes it a controlled variable rather than a manipulated one. Sample size is a feature of the study design, not a variable measured or manipulated within it."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a cold pressor test, one hand is placed in ice water for one minute while blood pressure is recorded every 15 seconds. Which is the dependent variable?\nA) The temperature of the ice water bath\nB) The blood pressure recorded during the immersion\nC) The one minute duration of the exposure\nD) The hand chosen for immersion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The blood pressure recorded during the immersion","why":"Blood pressure is what the team measures and what the cold stimulus is expected to change through sympathetic activation, so it is the dependent variable. The water temperature is the manipulated condition, the independent variable. The one minute exposure and the choice of hand are both fixed by the protocol, which makes them controlled variables that must be identical for every subject if the comparison is to mean anything."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a physiology experiment, what is the independent variable?\nA) The variable measured as the outcome of the experiment\nB) The variable the experimenter deliberately changes\nC) The variable held constant across every group\nD) The variable that differs between subjects by chance\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The variable the experimenter deliberately changes","why":"The independent variable is what you set: the dose, the temperature, the stimulus frequency, the workload. The measured outcome is the dependent variable, which is what you hope will respond. A variable held constant across groups is a controlled variable, deliberately prevented from varying. Differences that arise by chance between subjects are a source of variability you try to balance across groups, not a variable you manipulate."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A study tests whether a new drug lowers blood pressure. Forty patients take the drug, and over 4 weeks their average blood pressure falls from 150/95 mmHg to 135/85 mmHg (normal below 120/80). There is no other group.\nWhich control would let the team credit the drug rather than something else, and what would that control rule out?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"A matched group taking a look alike pill with no drug on the same schedule, which rules out the passage of time, the effect of being watched, and the tendency of high readings to drift back toward average","why":"A control condition matches everything except the independent variable. Patients enrolled because their pressure was high tend to drift downward anyway, they often change habits once they are being measured, and 4 weeks of time passes for everyone. A placebo group experiences all of that without the drug, so the difference between groups is what the drug did."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A lab tests whether epinephrine raises the rate of an isolated frog heart. They add 1 mL of epinephrine dissolved in saline to the bath, and heart rate rises from 40 to 60 beats per minute. In a second run on the same heart they add 1 mL of plain saline, and heart rate stays at 40.\nWhat is the role of the second run, and what does it rule out?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"It is the control. It holds everything constant except the epinephrine, so it rules out the added volume or the disturbance of the bath as the cause of the rise","why":"Adding anything to the bath changes volume, temperature, and mixing. Without the saline run, the team could not tell whether the heart sped up because of epinephrine or because of the act of adding liquid. The saline run is identical in every way except the drug. Since heart rate did not change, the rise in the first run can be credited to epinephrine."},{"t":"bd","q":"A team wants to know whether putting one hand in cold water raises blood pressure. Design the experiment. State the hypothesis, the independent and dependent variables, the control condition, and what the control rules out.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"hypothesis: cold water immersion of the hand raises blood pressure; independent variable: water temperature; dependent variable: blood pressure; control: the same hand in water at a neutral temperature for the same time; the control rules out effects of immersion itself, time, posture, and anticipation; measure blood pressure before and during in both conditions and repeat in several subjects","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Sketch an experiment as three labeled boxes: what you changed, what you measured, and your control."},{"t":"draw","q":"Take a claim from this week and design the control group that would test it. Draw both groups and label the one difference between them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the hypothesis, independent variable, dependent variable, and control condition in a physiology experiment and state what the control rules out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Experimental design and controls. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w1-lab-measurement-error","name":"Measurement error and variability","can":"Distinguish random from systematic error and explain why physiological measurements are repeated and averaged.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is random error in a physiological measurement?\nA) An error that shifts every reading in the same direction\nB) A mistake in the arithmetic used to analyze the collected data\nC) A real change in the variable that is being measured\nD) Unpredictable scatter that pushes readings above and below the true value\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Unpredictable scatter that pushes readings above and below the true value","why":"Random error has no preferred direction, so repeated readings straddle the true value and their mean converges on it as trials accumulate. An error that always shifts one way is systematic and averaging will never remove it. An arithmetic mistake is a blunder, correctable by rechecking rather than by repeating the measurement. A real change in the variable is biological signal, not error, even though it also makes the numbers move."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is systematic error?\nA) Scatter that is equally likely to fall high or low on any given trial\nB) A consistent bias that shifts every reading in the same direction\nC) A single mistyped value entered into the data table\nD) Normal biological variation from one subject to the next\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A consistent bias that shifts every reading in the same direction","why":"Systematic error is a bias built into the instrument or the technique, such as a transducer with an offset or a cuff of the wrong size, and it moves every reading the same way by roughly the same amount. Scatter with no preferred direction is random error. A mistyped value is a one time recording mistake rather than a property of the method. Variation between subjects is real physiology and belongs in the analysis, not in the error budget of the instrument."},{"t":"mc","q":"Repeating a measurement many times and averaging mainly reduces which problem?\nA) Random scatter among repeated readings\nB) Systematic bias built into the instrument\nC) True biological variation between different subjects\nD) An incorrect choice of dependent variable\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Random scatter among repeated readings","why":"Random deviations are as likely to be high as low, so they partly cancel in a mean, and the more trials you average the closer the mean sits to the true value. A built in bias survives averaging untouched, because it pushes every trial the same way. Variation between subjects is not removed by repeating measurements on one subject, and it is often the thing you actually want to describe. Choosing the wrong dependent variable is a design flaw that no amount of repetition will repair."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does accuracy describe in a measurement?\nA) How closely repeated readings agree with one another\nB) How many decimal places the instrument displays\nC) How close a reading is to the true value\nD) How quickly the instrument produces a reading\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) How close a reading is to the true value","why":"Accuracy is about truth: an accurate blood pressure reading matches what an arterial line would show. Agreement among repeats is precision, and an instrument can be beautifully precise while consistently wrong. Displayed decimal places are resolution, and adding digits to a biased reading does not bring it closer to the truth. Speed of measurement is a practical property with no bearing on either accuracy or precision."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A student measures a classmate's resting heart rate five times by counting the radial pulse for 15 seconds and multiplying by 4. The readings are 64, 72, 68, 76, and 60 beats per minute (normal resting 60 to 100). A chest strap monitor worn at the same time reads 68 the entire time.\nWhat kind of error is the student's method showing, and what would reduce it?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Random error. Counting for a full 60 seconds and averaging several trials would tighten the readings around 68","why":"The readings scatter on both sides of 68 and their average is 68, which matches the monitor. That is random error: a one beat miscount in 15 seconds becomes 4 beats per minute, and it can go either way. A longer count shrinks the effect of each miscount, and averaging cancels the scatter. There is no fixed offset to correct, so it is not systematic."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Taking a blood pressure reading three times and averaging the results reduces random error but does not remove a systematic error such as a cuff that is too small.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Physiological measurements are repeated because the body itself fluctuates and because each reading carries small chance errors. Averaging handles those. A bias built into the method shows up identically in every trial, so the only fix is to correct the method: use the right cuff, calibrate the instrument, or compare against a known standard."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two sets of repeated measurements, one with high variability and one with low. Mark which difference between readings is real."},{"t":"draw","q":"List three sources of variation in a single measurement. Beside each, one way to reduce it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish random from systematic error and explain why physiological measurements are repeated and averaged."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Measurement error and variability. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Membrane Structure and Diffusion","comps":[{"id":"w2-membrane-structure","name":"Membrane composition and fluidity","can":"Describe the fluid mosaic membrane and state how phospholipids, cholesterol, glycolipids, and integral and peripheral proteins contribute to its properties.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What forms the continuous structural framework of the plasma membrane?\nA) A phospholipid bilayer\nB) A sheet of cholesterol molecules\nC) A woven network of peripheral proteins\nD) A surface layer of membrane carbohydrate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A phospholipid bilayer","why":"The bilayer of phospholipids is the one component that is continuous around the whole cell, and it is what makes the membrane a barrier to water soluble substances. Cholesterol is inserted between phospholipids and does not form a sheet of its own. Peripheral proteins sit on the surfaces and can be removed without opening the cell. Membrane carbohydrate is attached to lipids and proteins on the outer face only, so it cannot be the framework."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an intact plasma membrane, where do the fatty acid tails of the phospholipids sit?\nA) Facing the cytosol on both leaflets\nB) Facing each other in the interior of the bilayer, away from water\nC) Facing the extracellular fluid on both leaflets\nD) Alternating with the phosphate heads along each membrane surface\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Facing each other in the interior of the bilayer, away from water","why":"The tails are nonpolar, so water drives them together into the core of the membrane while the polar heads face the watery fluid on each side. Tails cannot face the cytosol or the extracellular fluid, because both are water and the tails are excluded from water. Tails do not alternate with the heads along the surface, because each phospholipid keeps its head at the surface and its tails pointing inward."},{"t":"mc","q":"Calling a phospholipid amphipathic means that one molecule has which combination?\nA) Two polar ends separated by a neutral middle\nB) A charged head and a charged tail, both attracted to water\nC) A polar head that interacts with water and nonpolar tails that do not\nD) A nonpolar head and polar tails that face the cytosol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A polar head that interacts with water and nonpolar tails that do not","why":"Amphipathic means one molecule carries both a water loving and a water avoiding region, and that split is exactly what makes a bilayer form spontaneously. A molecule polar at both ends would dissolve rather than assemble into a sheet. A fully charged molecule would stay in solution. The head is the polar end and the tails are the nonpolar end, not the reverse."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which class of membrane protein extends completely through the bilayer and touches both the cytosol and the extracellular fluid?\nA) Peripheral proteins bound to one membrane surface\nB) Lipid anchored proteins tethered to a single leaflet\nC) Secreted proteins that rest in the glycocalyx\nD) Integral transmembrane proteins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Integral transmembrane proteins","why":"Only a transmembrane protein has a hydrophobic stretch long enough to cross the lipid core, which is what lets channels and transporters connect the two sides of the cell. Peripheral proteins bind to a surface and never enter the core. Lipid anchored proteins are attached to a fatty acid in one leaflet and stay on that side. Secreted proteins are outside the membrane entirely."},{"t":"mc","q":"How is cholesterol positioned within the plasma membrane?\nA) Wedged between phospholipids, with its hydroxyl near the polar heads\nB) Floating free in the cytosol just under the inner leaflet\nC) Coating the outer surface as a continuous waterproof film\nD) Packed in the center of the bilayer with no contact with the heads\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Wedged between phospholipids, with its hydroxyl near the polar heads","why":"Cholesterol is itself amphipathic, so its single hydroxyl group sits with the polar heads while its stiff ring structure sits among the fatty acid tails, and that position is what lets it control packing. Cytosolic cholesterol would not affect membrane fluidity. A surface film would block the transport proteins. Burying it entirely in the center would leave the hydroxyl group in the hydrophobic core, which it avoids."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"At body temperature, cholesterol makes the plasma membrane less fluid by restricting the movement of the phospholipid tails.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer. At warm temperatures it restrains the tails and makes the membrane firmer and less leaky. At cold temperatures it does the opposite, keeping tails from packing into a gel. The statement is true because it specifies body temperature."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a patch of membrane in cross section, big enough to fill half your box. Put in phospholipids with heads and tails clearly different, cholesterol wedged between the tails, a transmembrane protein, a peripheral protein on the inner face, and a glycolipid with its sugar chain. Label which face is extracellular. One line: name the one component whose position alone tells you which side of the membrane you are looking at."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same membrane patch three times in a row: at normal body temperature, chilled, and warmed. In each, show how tightly the phospholipid tails pack and where cholesterol sits. Draw one tail with a double bond kink in it and one straight saturated tail. One line: cholesterol is called a fluidity buffer and not a stiffener, so say what the word buffer is doing there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the fluid mosaic membrane and state how phospholipids, cholesterol, glycolipids, and integral and peripheral proteins contribute to its properties."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Membrane composition and fluidity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-permeability","name":"Determinants of permeability","can":"Rank molecules by their ability to cross a lipid bilayer unaided using size, charge, and lipid solubility, and predict which will require a protein.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A new drug is small, uncharged, and highly lipid soluble. What do you predict about how it enters cells?\nA) It needs a specific carrier protein and will saturate at high dose\nB) It enters only by ATP driven pumping\nC) It cannot enter cells and stays in the extracellular fluid\nD) It diffuses straight through the bilayer without a transporter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It diffuses straight through the bilayer without a transporter","why":"Small, uncharged, and lipid soluble is the exact profile that crosses by simple diffusion, so the rate will rise in proportion to the concentration gradient with no ceiling. A carrier would impose saturation, which simple diffusion does not show. No pump is needed for a molecule that has no charge to fight and no gradient to climb. Being lipid soluble is the opposite of being trapped outside."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fluorescent dye is injected into a cell and stays inside for hours. What does that tell you about the dye?\nA) It is polar or charged and has no transporter to carry it out\nB) It is highly lipid soluble and dissolves in the membrane\nC) It is pumped inward continuously by the Na+/K+ ATPase\nD) It has been covalently bonded to the phospholipid tails\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is polar or charged and has no transporter to carry it out","why":"A dye that stays put is one the bilayer will not admit and no carrier recognizes, which is exactly why polar or charged dyes are chosen for this purpose. A lipid soluble dye would leak straight back out within minutes. The Na+/K+ ATPase moves only Na+ and K+, so it cannot trap a dye. Covalent bonding to tails would place the dye in the membrane rather than in the cytosol."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which substance crosses a pure lipid bilayer most readily without help from a protein?\nA) Sodium ion  |  B) Glucose\nC) Chloride ion  |  D) Oxygen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Oxygen","why":"Oxygen is small and nonpolar, so it dissolves in the fatty acid core and passes straight through. Na+ and Cl- are charged and carry a shell of water, which the hydrophobic core will not admit, so both need channels. Glucose is uncharged but large and heavily hydroxylated, so it needs a transporter such as GLUT."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which property of a molecule most increases its ability to cross a lipid bilayer unaided?\nA) High lipid solubility  |  B) A large net negative charge\nC) A high molecular weight  |  D) A large shell of bound water\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) High lipid solubility","why":"Crossing the membrane means dissolving into the fatty acid core and out again, so the more lipid soluble a molecule is, the faster it goes. Net charge does the opposite, since the nonpolar core repels charged particles. High molecular weight slows a molecule down at any given lipid solubility. A shell of bound water makes the effective particle much larger and more polar, which also slows crossing."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Aspirin is a weak acid. Below a pH of about 3.5 most of it is in its uncharged form. Above that pH most of it is charged. A student swallows a tablet. Stomach contents are at pH 2. The inside of a stomach lining cell is at pH 7.4.\nPredict how aspirin moves from the stomach into the lining cells and what happens once it is inside.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Mostly uncharged in the stomach, it crosses the lipid bilayer easily, then becomes charged inside the cell and is trapped there","why":"Charge is the main barrier to crossing a lipid bilayer. At pH 2 aspirin is mostly uncharged, so it dissolves in the membrane and diffuses into the cell. Inside, at pH 7.4, it gives up its proton and becomes charged. The charged form cannot get back through the lipid, so aspirin accumulates in the lining cells. This ion trapping is part of why aspirin irritates the stomach lining."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why oxygen crosses a cell membrane with no help, while glucose and sodium ions each need a protein. Use size, charge, and lipid solubility in your answer, and say what kind of protein each one needs.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the core of the bilayer is hydrophobic (oily), so a molecule must dissolve in lipid to cross unaided; oxygen is small and nonpolar, so it dissolves in the core and diffuses through; glucose is large and polar (many hydroxyl groups), so it cannot enter the lipid core; glucose needs a carrier (GLUT or SGLT); sodium is charged and carries a shell of water, so it cannot enter the lipid core at all; sodium needs a channel or a carrier or pump","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a bilayer as a vertical wall down the middle of your page. Line up six molecules on the left: oxygen, carbon dioxide, water, a steroid hormone, glucose, and a sodium ion. Draw an arrow straight through the wall for each one that crosses unaided, a blocked arrow for each that cannot, and a protein set into the wall for each that needs help. One line: sodium is tiny and still cannot cross, so say what actually stopped it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a ranking ladder, most permeable at the top and least at the bottom, and place at least six molecules of your own choosing on it. Beside each rung write the properties that put it there: size, lipid solubility, and charge where it applies. One line: name the pair on your ladder that are close in size but far apart in rank, and say what separates them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to rank molecules by their ability to cross a lipid bilayer unaided using size, charge, and lipid solubility, and predict which will require a protein."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Determinants of permeability. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-fick-diffusion","name":"Simple diffusion and Fick's law","can":"State the variables in Fick's law of diffusion and predict how a change in concentration gradient, surface area, membrane thickness, or distance changes the rate of transfer.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A barrier becomes three times thicker while its surface area doubles. What happens to the rate of diffusion across it?\nA) It rises to about six times the original rate\nB) It falls to about one sixth of the original rate\nC) It falls to about two thirds of the original rate\nD) It is unchanged, since the two effects cancel exactly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It falls to about two thirds of the original rate","why":"Area multiplies the rate and thickness divides it, so the new rate is the old rate times two divided by three, which is about 0.67 of the original. Multiplying the two factors together would treat thickness as if it helped, which it does not. Dividing by both would treat area as if it hurt. Exact cancellation would require the two factors to be equal, and two and three are not."},{"t":"mc","q":"The surface area available for exchange doubles while everything else stays the same. What happens to the rate of diffusion?\nA) It halves  |  B) It quadruples\nC) It doubles  |  D) It is unchanged\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It doubles","why":"Surface area is in the numerator of Fick's law and enters as a simple multiplier, so twice the area gives twice the transfer. Halving would be the answer if the thickness had doubled instead. Quadrupling would require a squared relationship, which applies to diffusion time and distance, not to area. Area is a real variable, so the rate cannot stay put."},{"t":"mc","q":"The concentration difference across a membrane is tripled while area and thickness stay the same. What happens to the rate of diffusion?\nA) It triples\nB) It falls to one third\nC) It is unchanged, since diffusion has a fixed maximum\nD) It rises ninefold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It triples","why":"The concentration difference is the driving force and enters Fick's law as a direct multiplier, so three times the gradient gives three times the flux. A fall would require the gradient to be in the denominator, which it is not. Simple diffusion has no transport maximum, since there is no carrier to saturate, which is one way it differs from facilitated diffusion. Nothing in Fick's law is squared."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell increases in radius so that the distance from its surface to its center goes from 10 micrometers to 20 micrometers. How does the time for a solute to diffuse to the center change?\nA) It doubles  |  B) It is halved\nC) It increases about fourfold  |  D) It stays the same\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It increases about fourfold","why":"Diffusion time rises with the square of distance, so doubling the distance multiplies the time by four, and this is the main reason cells stay small. Doubling would be the answer only if time were directly proportional to distance. Time cannot fall as the distance grows. Distance is the dominant term here, so it certainly matters."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Doubling the thickness of a membrane doubles the rate of diffusion across it.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Doubling the thickness cuts the rate of diffusion in half, because thickness is in the denominator of Fick's law.","why":"Thickness and distance always slow diffusion. Surface area and concentration gradient speed it. Students who memorize Fick's law as a list of variables without noting which ones multiply and which ones divide will get this backwards. Pulmonary edema is the clinical example: fluid thickens the alveolar wall and oxygen transfer falls."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, list the variables in Fick's law of diffusion and use them to explain why emphysema (loss of alveolar walls) and pulmonary edema (fluid in the alveolar wall) both reduce oxygen transfer, but for different reasons. Then explain why giving supplemental oxygen helps in both.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"rate is proportional to surface area times concentration gradient times permeability; rate is inversely proportional to membrane thickness or diffusion distance; emphysema destroys alveolar walls and lowers surface area; edema adds fluid to the alveolar wall and increases diffusion distance (thickness); supplemental oxygen raises alveolar oxygen and steepens the concentration gradient; a bigger gradient partly makes up for less area or greater thickness","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the Fick's law setup as a picture, not an equation: two compartments, a membrane between them, and label on the drawing the surface area, the concentration difference, and the membrane thickness. Then draw four small graphs in a row, rate of diffusion against each of those, plus against diffusion distance. Label both axes on every graph. One line: one variable sits in the denominator, so say which and how the shape of its graph shows that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a real gas exchange surface, one alveolus against one capillary, and mark on it every Fick variable the body has already optimized. Then draw the same surface in pulmonary edema, with fluid thickening the barrier. Show what changed and what did not. One line: name the single Fick variable the disease changed, and say whether breathing faster fixes it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the variables in Fick's law of diffusion and predict how a change in concentration gradient, surface area, membrane thickness, or distance changes the rate of transfer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Simple diffusion and Fick's law. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-osmolarity-tonicity","name":"Osmolarity and tonicity","can":"Calculate the osmolarity of a solution, distinguish osmolarity from tonicity, and classify a solution as isotonic, hypotonic, or hypertonic to a cell.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the osmolarity of a 300 mM glucose solution?\nA) 600 mOsm/L  |  B) 300 mOsm/L\nC) 150 mOsm/L  |  D) 900 mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 300 mOsm/L","why":"Glucose does not dissociate, so each molecule contributes one particle and the millimolar and milliosmolar numbers are the same. Doubling to 600 would apply to a salt that splits into two ions, such as NaCl. Halving is never correct, since dissolving cannot reduce the particle count. Tripling would apply to a salt like CaCl2 that yields three ions."},{"t":"mc","q":"A solution contains 150 mM NaCl plus 100 mM glucose. What is its total osmolarity?\nA) 250 mOsm/L  |  B) 500 mOsm/L\nC) 350 mOsm/L  |  D) 400 mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 400 mOsm/L","why":"NaCl contributes 300 mOsm/L because it dissociates into two particles, and glucose contributes 100 mOsm/L because it does not dissociate, giving 400 mOsm/L in total. The value of 250 comes from adding the millimolar figures without doubling the salt. The value of 500 would come from doubling the glucose as well, which does not dissociate. The value of 350 mixes the two errors."},{"t":"mc","q":"A red blood cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L is placed in a 300 mOsm/L urea solution. Urea crosses the cell membrane. How is this solution best described?\nA) Isosmotic but hypotonic  |  B) Isosmotic and isotonic\nC) Hyperosmotic and hypertonic  |  D) Hyposmotic and hypotonic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Isosmotic but hypotonic","why":"The total particle count matches the cell, so the solution is isosmotic, but urea enters the cell and stops holding water outside, leaving an effective impermeant osmolarity of zero, so the cell swells and eventually lyses. Calling it isotonic treats urea as if it were trapped outside, which is the classic error. It is not hyperosmotic, because 300 equals 300. It is not hyposmotic either, since the measured osmolarity is normal, and only its tonicity is low."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell of 300 mOsm/L is placed in a 400 mOsm/L NaCl solution. How is this solution described?\nA) Hyperosmotic but isotonic  |  B) Hyperosmotic and hypertonic\nC) Isosmotic and isotonic  |  D) Hyperosmotic but hypotonic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hyperosmotic and hypertonic","why":"NaCl does not cross the membrane appreciably, so all 400 mOsm/L is effective and the solution both measures higher than the cell and pulls water out of it. A hyperosmotic solution is isotonic only when the excess above 300 is made of penetrating solute. It is not isosmotic, since 400 is not 300. It could be hypotonic only if its impermeant fraction were below 300, which it is not."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Any solution that is hyperosmotic to a cell must also be hypertonic to that cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A hyperosmotic solution is hypertonic only if the extra solute cannot enter the cell; a solution of 300 mOsm/L NaCl plus 100 mOsm/L urea is hyperosmotic but isotonic, because urea equilibrates across the membrane.","why":"Osmolarity counts every particle. Tonicity counts only the impermeant ones, because only they can hold water on one side for the long term. A penetrating solute like urea raises the osmolarity of the bottle but has no lasting effect on cell volume. This distinction is why clinicians care about effective osmoles rather than total osmoles."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, calculate the osmolarity of a solution containing 100 mmol/L NaCl and 100 mmol/L glucose. Say whether it is isosmotic to body fluids. Then predict its tonicity to a red blood cell, given that red cells take up glucose through GLUT1 and metabolize it, and explain what happens to the cell.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"NaCl contributes 200 mOsm/L (two particles per molecule); glucose contributes 100 mOsm/L; total osmolarity is 300 mOsm/L, which is isosmotic; only the impermeant solute (NaCl, 200 mOsm/L) counts toward tonicity; glucose enters the cell and is used up, so it does not hold water outside; the solution is hypotonic to the red cell; water enters and the cell swells and may hemolyze","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three beakers labeled 150 mM NaCl, 300 mM sucrose, and 300 mM urea. Under each, write the osmolarity calculation and the answer in mOsm/L. Then draw a red blood cell in each beaker and show its final volume. One line: all three have the same osmolarity and one of them lyses the cell, so name the property of the solute that decides tonicity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three cells that have just come out of three unknown solutions: one shrunken, one swollen but intact, and one lysed. Beside each, write two different solutions that could have produced that result, one differing in osmolarity and one differing only in penetrating solute content. One line: say what osmolarity alone failed to tell you about the lysed cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate the osmolarity of a solution, distinguish osmolarity from tonicity, and classify a solution as isotonic, hypotonic, or hypertonic to a cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Osmolarity and tonicity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-osmosis-cell-volume","name":"Osmosis and cell volume","can":"Predict the direction of water movement and the resulting change in cell volume when a cell is placed in a solution of stated osmolarity and penetrating solute content.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm/L is placed in 150 mOsm/L NaCl. Which way does water move, and what happens to the cell?\nA) Water enters and the cell swells\nB) Water leaves and the cell shrinks\nC) No net water movement occurs\nD) Water enters and then leaves, so volume returns to normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Water enters and the cell swells","why":"The outside has fewer impermeant particles than the inside, so water moves toward the cell interior and volume rises. Water cannot leave, since that would require a higher effective osmolarity outside. There is a gradient here, so net movement occurs. Volume does not recover on its own in this case, because NaCl cannot enter the cell to erase the gradient."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell is given many extra aquaporins and then placed in a hypotonic solution. How does this change the outcome?\nA) The cell swells faster, but the final volume is the same\nB) The cell swells to a larger final volume\nC) The cell no longer swells, because aquaporins let solute escape\nD) The cell shrinks instead, because water can now leave more easily\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cell swells faster, but the final volume is the same","why":"Aquaporins change how quickly water moves, while the osmotic gradient decides how far it moves, so the endpoint is set by the solute concentrations and is unaffected. A larger endpoint would require a bigger gradient, which aquaporins do not create. Aquaporins pass water and exclude solute, so nothing escapes through them. Water moves both ways through aquaporins, and the net direction still follows the gradient, which favors entry here."},{"t":"mc","q":"During osmosis, which way does water move across a membrane that blocks the solute?\nA) Toward the side with more impermeant solute\nB) Toward the side with less impermeant solute\nC) Toward the side with the higher hydrostatic pressure\nD) Toward the warmer side of the membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Toward the side with more impermeant solute","why":"Water diffuses down its own concentration gradient, and adding solute lowers the water concentration, so water always moves toward the more concentrated side. Moving toward the dilute side would be backward and would concentrate that solution further. Hydrostatic pressure pushes water away from the high pressure side, which is filtration rather than osmosis. Temperature changes how fast osmosis happens but not which way water goes."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with kidney failure has a blood urea nitrogen of 150 mg/dL (normal 7 to 20). Urea crosses most cell membranes freely, but it crosses into and out of brain cells slowly over hours. Rapid dialysis pulls his blood urea down to 40 mg/dL in three hours. Brain cell urea has not had time to fall.\nPredict what happens to his brain cells during and just after the dialysis session, and why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Brain cells swell, because they are now hyperosmotic to plasma and water moves into them","why":"Whenever a solute is removed from one side faster than it can cross the membrane, it acts as an impermeant solute for that time. Plasma urea fell fast. Brain urea is still high. So for a few hours the brain cells hold more osmoles than the plasma, and water moves into them. Brain swelling after fast dialysis is called dialysis disequilibrium. It is prevented by dialyzing slowly so brain urea has time to keep up."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When a cell is placed in a hypotonic solution, water enters the cell and the cell swells.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Water moves toward the side with more solute that cannot cross the membrane. Hypotonic means the outside has less of that solute than the cell, so the inside wins and the cell swells. If it swells enough, the membrane breaks. This is the basis for hemolysis in distilled water."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, a cell with 300 mOsm/L of impermeant solute inside is placed in a solution of 150 mOsm/L NaCl plus 150 mOsm/L urea. Predict the cell volume at the first instant, describe what happens over the next several minutes, and predict the final result. Explain each step.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"at first the total osmolarity is 300 outside and 300 inside, so there is no immediate water movement; urea crosses the membrane and enters the cell down its gradient; water follows urea into the cell; at equilibrium urea is equal on both sides and no longer counts; only 150 mOsm/L of impermeant solute remains outside against 300 inside, so the solution is hypotonic; the cell swells to about twice its volume or lyses","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cell at normal volume in the middle of your page, 290 mOsm/L inside. Around it draw four more cells, in 150 mOsm/L NaCl, 290 mOsm/L NaCl, 450 mOsm/L NaCl, and 290 mOsm/L urea. For each, draw the water arrow with its direction and the final cell volume. One line: for the urea cell, say what happens at the first instant and what happens over the next few minutes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, cell volume on the y and time on the x, with the moment of immersion marked. Plot three traces on it: a cell in 450 mOsm/L NaCl, a cell in 150 mOsm/L NaCl, and a cell in 600 mOsm/L urea. One line: one trace goes down and then climbs back past where it started, so say which solute movement caused each half of that curve."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the direction of water movement and the resulting change in cell volume when a cell is placed in a solution of stated osmolarity and penetrating solute content."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Osmosis and cell volume. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-lab-diffusion-osmosis","name":"Diffusion and osmosis experiment","can":"Measure diffusion and osmotic movement across a selectively permeable membrane and relate the observed rate to molecular size and concentration gradient.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A dialysis bag containing 30 percent sucrose is placed in a beaker of distilled water. What happens to the mass of the bag?\nA) It decreases, because sucrose leaves the bag\nB) It stays the same, because sucrose cannot cross\nC) It decreases, because water moves out of the bag\nD) It increases, because water moves into the bag\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It increases, because water moves into the bag","why":"The bag holds the higher solute concentration and sucrose cannot escape, so water enters by osmosis and the bag gains mass. Sucrose is too large for the pores, so it does not leave. The fact that sucrose cannot cross is exactly why water moves, so it does not keep the mass constant. Water moves toward solute, not away from it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A dialysis bag filled with distilled water is placed in a beaker of 30 percent sucrose. Predict the change in bag mass.\nA) It decreases, because water leaves the bag\nB) It increases, because sucrose enters the bag\nC) It increases, because water enters the bag\nD) It stays the same, because water crosses equally in both directions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It decreases, because water leaves the bag","why":"Now the higher solute concentration is outside, so water leaves the bag and it loses mass. Sucrose cannot enter through the pores, so it cannot add weight. Water moves toward the concentrated beaker, not into the dilute bag. Water does cross both ways, but the fluxes are unequal whenever a gradient exists."},{"t":"mc","q":"A bag of 10 percent sucrose is placed in a beaker of 30 percent sucrose. What happens?\nA) The bag gains mass, because it contains sucrose too\nB) The bag loses mass, because the beaker is hypertonic to the bag\nC) The bag is unchanged, because both contain the same solute\nD) The bag gains mass, because water always moves into a bag\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The bag loses mass, because the beaker is hypertonic to the bag","why":"What matters is the difference in concentration, not whether both sides contain sucrose, and the more concentrated beaker draws water out of the bag. Simply containing sucrose does not pull water in if the outside has more. Identical solutes at different concentrations still produce an osmotic gradient. Direction is set by the gradient, so there is no rule that bags always gain."},{"t":"mc","q":"A dialysis bag weighs 20.0 g at the start and 23.0 g after 30 minutes. What is the percent change in mass?\nA) An increase of 3 percent  |  B) An increase of 13 percent\nC) An increase of 15 percent  |  D) An increase of 115 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) An increase of 15 percent","why":"The gain of 3.0 g divided by the starting mass of 20.0 g is 0.15, or 15 percent. Reporting 3 percent uses the raw gain in grams and ignores the denominator. Using the final mass of 23.0 g as the denominator gives about 13 percent, which compares the change to where the bag ended rather than where it began. The figure of 115 percent is the final mass as a percentage of the initial mass, not the change."},{"t":"mc","q":"A bag of starch solution sits in a beaker of iodine solution for 30 minutes. What do you expect to see?\nA) The beaker turns blue black while the bag stays white\nB) Both the bag and the beaker turn blue black\nC) Neither changes color, since neither substance can cross\nD) The bag contents turn blue black while the beaker stays amber\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The bag contents turn blue black while the beaker stays amber","why":"Iodine is small enough to pass through the pores and meets the starch inside, so color develops in the bag, while starch is too large to reach the iodine outside. A blue black beaker would mean starch had escaped, which the pore size prevents. Both turning would require both molecules to cross. Iodine does cross, so a total absence of color change is not expected."},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you run anything, draw your predicted result: the bag or the plate as it will look at the start and at the end, with each test molecule drawn where you expect it to be. Run it, then draw the actual result beside your prediction in a second color. One line: name the substance that moved fastest and say whether molecular size or starting gradient explains it better in your own data."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a graph of distance moved on the y against molecular weight on the x, one labeled point for every substance you tested. Beside it draw a second small graph, distance moved against starting concentration. One line: your membrane passed some things and stopped others, so write the size cutoff your data actually supports and how confident you are in that number."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Diffusion and osmosis experiment. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-lab-rbc-tonicity","name":"Tonicity and red blood cells","can":"Observe erythrocytes in solutions of different tonicity, identify crenation, normal shape, and hemolysis, and explain each result.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Red blood cells are placed in 0.45 percent NaCl. Predict what happens.\nA) They crenate, since the solution is hypertonic\nB) They keep their normal shape, since the solution is isotonic\nC) They swell and some may hemolyze, since the solution is hypotonic\nD) They shrink at first and then return to normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They swell and some may hemolyze, since the solution is hypotonic","why":"Half strength saline is about 150 mOsm/L, well below the cell's 300 mOsm/L, so water moves into the cells and they round up and can burst. Crenation would require the outside to be more concentrated. Isotonic saline is 0.9 percent, twice this concentration. There is no initial shrinkage, because the gradient favors water entry from the first moment."},{"t":"mc","q":"Under the microscope a technician sees shrunken, spiky red blood cells. Which solution was most likely added?\nA) 1.8 percent NaCl  |  B) 0.9 percent NaCl\nC) 0.3 percent NaCl  |  D) Distilled water\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 1.8 percent NaCl","why":"Crenation requires a hypertonic solution, and 1.8 percent NaCl is twice the isotonic concentration, so water leaves the cells. The 0.9 percent solution is isotonic and leaves the cells looking normal. Both 0.3 percent NaCl and distilled water are hypotonic and would swell or lyse the cells rather than shrink them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Red blood cells sit in isotonic saline and show no change in volume. What is happening to water at the membrane?\nA) Water has stopped crossing the membrane entirely\nB) Water crosses both ways at equal rates, so there is no net movement\nC) Water enters continuously and is broken down inside the cell\nD) Water enters and is pumped back out by the Na+/K+ ATPase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Water crosses both ways at equal rates, so there is no net movement","why":"Osmotic equilibrium is a balance of two equal fluxes, not a halt, and water molecules keep moving through aquaporins constantly. A complete stop would require an impermeable membrane, which the red cell does not have. Water is not consumed inside the cell in any amount that matters here. The Na+/K+ ATPase moves ions and cannot pump water."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the term for the shrunken, scalloped appearance of a red blood cell placed in a hypertonic solution?\nA) Hemolysis  |  B) Agglutination\nC) Crenation  |  D) Phagocytosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Crenation","why":"Water leaves the cell, the membrane loses its smooth curve, and the surface takes on spiky projections, which is crenation. Hemolysis is the opposite result and happens in hypotonic solutions. Agglutination is clumping caused by antibodies and has nothing to do with tonicity. Phagocytosis is engulfment by a white blood cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to a red blood cell placed in a strongly hypotonic solution?\nA) It shrinks and crenates  |  B) It keeps its biconcave shape\nC) It divides into two smaller cells  |  D) It swells and undergoes hemolysis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It swells and undergoes hemolysis","why":"Water enters until the membrane can stretch no further, and the cell bursts and releases its hemoglobin, which is hemolysis. Crenation requires water to leave, which needs a hypertonic solution. The biconcave shape is preserved only in an isotonic solution. Mature red blood cells have no nucleus and do not divide at all."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Red blood cells are placed in a tube of 0.9 percent NaCl (300 mOsm/L, isotonic) to which 200 mmol/L glycerol has been added, giving a total osmolarity of 500 mOsm/L. Glycerol crosses the red cell membrane slowly, over several minutes. NaCl does not cross.\nPredict the sequence of changes in the red cells over the next 15 minutes.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"They shrink at first, then return to normal volume as glycerol enters and water follows it","why":"At the first instant all 500 mOsm/L is outside against 300 inside, so water leaves and the cells crenate. Then glycerol diffuses in over a few minutes and water follows. Once glycerol is equal on both sides it no longer counts. What remains is 300 mOsm/L of impermeant NaCl outside against 300 mOsm/L of impermeant solute inside, which is isotonic, so the cells end at their original volume. The solution is hyperosmotic but isotonic."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Red blood cells crenate in 3 percent NaCl because sodium chloride enters the cell and damages the membrane.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Red blood cells crenate in 3 percent NaCl because the solution is hypertonic, so water leaves the cell by osmosis and the shrunken membrane wrinkles; NaCl does not enter the cell.","why":"The red cell membrane is essentially impermeable to Na+ and Cl-. Crenation is purely a water problem: water leaves, the cell loses volume, and the membrane has too much surface for the smaller contents. Nothing chemical is happening to the membrane. If NaCl could enter, it would equilibrate and the cell would return to normal size, which is what happens with a penetrating solute like urea."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three microscope fields, one per solution: distilled water, 0.9 percent NaCl, and 3 percent NaCl. In each, draw the cells as you actually see them, keeping the biconcave disc, the spiky crenated form, and the ghosts distinct. Under each field write the direction of net water movement. One line: name the slide that is hardest to count cells on, and say why that difficulty is itself the result."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one red cell in the center of your box and three arrows out to three versions of it: crenated, normal, and hemolysed. Beside each write the tonicity that produced it and the water arrow. Then draw what the membrane itself did in the hemolysed one. One line: crenation is reversible and hemolysis is not, so say what makes the difference."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Tonicity and red blood cells. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Membrane Transport","comps":[{"id":"w2-facilitated-diffusion","name":"Facilitated diffusion","can":"Describe carrier and channel mediated diffusion and explain why carrier mediated transport shows saturation and specificity while simple diffusion does not.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular glucose is raised from a very low value to twice that value while the GLUT carriers remain far from saturated. What happens to the rate of glucose entry?\nA) It roughly doubles  |  B) It jumps immediately to the transport maximum\nC) It stays the same  |  D) It falls by about half\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It roughly doubles","why":"Well below saturation most carriers are free, so the rate rises in near proportion to the gradient and doubling the outside concentration roughly doubles entry. The rate would only stay flat if the carriers were already saturated. Nothing here reduces the gradient, so a fall makes no sense, and the transport maximum is reached only when carrier sites are fully occupied."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell is moved into a solution in which glucose is lower outside the cell than inside. Which way does GLUT move glucose?\nA) Neither way, because a carrier needs ATP to run in reverse\nB) Into the cell until the concentrations are equal\nC) Into the cell, because carriers only work inward\nD) Out of the cell, down its concentration gradient\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Out of the cell, down its concentration gradient","why":"A facilitated diffusion carrier has no preferred direction. It simply follows the gradient, so with more glucose inside, net movement is outward. Carriers are not one way valves, and reversing does not cost ATP because no energy is being added in either direction. Inward movement would require glucose to climb its gradient, which passive transport cannot do."},{"t":"mc","q":"Galactose is added at high concentration to a cell taking up glucose through a carrier that binds both sugars. What happens to glucose uptake?\nA) It rises, because more total sugar is available\nB) It is unchanged, because carriers handle only one sugar\nC) It stops permanently\nD) It falls, because galactose occupies the shared binding sites\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls, because galactose occupies the shared binding sites","why":"When two solutes fit the same site, they compete, and the more abundant one wins more cycles, so glucose uptake drops. Uptake does not rise because the number of carriers is fixed. It is not unchanged, because the premise says the carrier binds both sugars. The block is not permanent either, since raising glucose again would win back sites."},{"t":"mc","q":"Doubling the oxygen concentration outside a cell doubles the rate of oxygen entry, and the rate keeps climbing with no plateau at any concentration tested. Which process is this?\nA) Simple diffusion  |  B) Facilitated diffusion\nC) Primary active transport  |  D) Secondary active transport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Simple diffusion","why":"A rate that keeps rising in direct proportion with no ceiling means there is no limited number of binding sites, which is the signature of simple diffusion through the lipid. Facilitated diffusion would plateau once carriers were saturated. Both active transport types would plateau as well and would also require an energy source, which a gas moving downhill does not use."},{"t":"mc","q":"A treatment denatures every carrier protein in a cell membrane. Which solute still crosses at nearly its original rate?\nA) A steroid hormone  |  B) Fructose\nC) Glucose  |  D) An amino acid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A steroid hormone","why":"Steroid hormones are lipid soluble and dissolve straight through the bilayer, so losing carriers barely affects them. Glucose and fructose both depend on GLUT family carriers, and amino acids depend on their own carrier proteins, so all three would slow sharply. The general rule is that only small nonpolar or lipid soluble molecules are carrier independent."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Facilitated diffusion requires ATP because a membrane protein is involved.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Facilitated diffusion is passive and uses no ATP; the protein only provides a path, and the concentration gradient supplies all the energy.","why":"Involving a protein does not make a process active. What makes a process active is moving solute against its gradient. GLUT carriers and ion channels both use proteins and both are passive. The common confusion is treating carrier and pump as the same thing. A carrier lets solute slide downhill. A pump pushes it uphill and pays for it with ATP."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a channel protein and a carrier protein in the same stretch of membrane, with a molecule crossing through each. Make the difference visible in the drawing: the channel as an open pore, the carrier changing shape around its cargo. Underneath, draw one graph of transport rate against solute concentration with two traces, simple diffusion and carrier mediated. One line: only one trace plateaus, so say what has run out at the plateau."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a carrier protein with glucose bound, then draw three failures beside it: galactose competing for the same carrier, a molecule of the wrong shape bouncing off, and every carrier already occupied. Label which drawing is competition, which is specificity, and which is saturation. One line: adding more glucose helps in one of those three and does nothing in another, so name each and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe carrier and channel mediated diffusion and explain why carrier mediated transport shows saturation and specificity while simple diffusion does not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Facilitated diffusion. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-primary-active-transport","name":"Primary active transport","can":"Explain how the sodium potassium ATPase uses ATP to move three sodium out and two potassium in, and state the two gradients it maintains.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Ouabain is applied to a cell. Predict what happens to intracellular Na+ over the next several minutes.\nA) It rises, because Na+ leaks in and is no longer pumped out\nB) It falls, because Na+ leaks out unopposed\nC) It is unchanged, because the membrane is impermeable to Na+\nD) It rises briefly and then returns to normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It rises, because Na+ leaks in and is no longer pumped out","why":"Na+ constantly leaks inward down a steep electrochemical gradient, and the pump is the only thing removing it, so blocking the pump lets Na+ pile up inside. It cannot fall, because there is no other route that carries Na+ out against the gradient. The membrane is leaky to Na+, not impermeable, and nothing restores the gradient while the pump stays blocked."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell's Na+/K+ ATPase is blocked. What is the immediate effect on the resting membrane potential, before the ion gradients have had time to run down?\nA) A small depolarization of a few mV\nB) A small hyperpolarization of a few mV\nC) No change at all under any circumstances\nD) A large hyperpolarization of about 30 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A small depolarization of a few mV","why":"The pump exports one net positive charge per cycle, so it contributes a few mV of negativity directly. Removing that contribution makes the inside slightly less negative, a small depolarization. Losing an outward positive current cannot hyperpolarize the cell. The larger change comes later, as the K+ and Na+ gradients themselves decay, so saying nothing changes ignores both the direct and the delayed effect."},{"t":"mc","q":"The Ca2+ ATPase of the sarcoplasmic reticulum hydrolyzes ATP to move Ca2+ from the cytosol into the SR against its gradient. How is this transporter classified?\nA) Simple diffusion  |  B) Primary active transport\nC) Facilitated diffusion  |  D) Secondary active transport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Primary active transport","why":"The transporter itself splits ATP to drive uphill movement, which is the definition of primary active transport, the same category as the Na+/K+ ATPase. Secondary active transport would use an existing ion gradient rather than ATP, as the sodium calcium exchanger does. Both diffusion categories are passive and could never build a thousandfold Ca2+ gradient across the SR membrane."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the direction each ion moves relative to its own concentration gradient during one pump cycle?\nA) Both Na+ and K+ move up their gradients\nB) Both Na+ and K+ move down their gradients\nC) Na+ moves down its gradient and K+ moves up its gradient\nD) Na+ moves up its gradient and K+ moves down its gradient\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Both Na+ and K+ move up their gradients","why":"Na+ is already high outside and is pushed further out, and K+ is already high inside and is pushed further in, so both ions are moved uphill and that is why ATP is required. If either ion were moving downhill, that leg of the cycle would need no energy. If both moved downhill, the pump would be a channel and the gradients would collapse instead of being maintained."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many K+ ions does the Na+/K+ ATPase bring into the cell per transport cycle?\nA) One  |  B) Two\nC) Three  |  D) Four\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Two","why":"Each cycle imports two K+ while exporting three Na+, so more positive charge leaves than enters. Three is the Na+ number, not the K+ number, and mixing the two up is the most common error with this pump. One and four do not match the stoichiometry and would not give the pump its small direct contribution to the resting membrane potential."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The Na+/K+ ATPase moves two sodium ions out of the cell and three potassium ions into the cell for each ATP used.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The Na+/K+ ATPase moves three sodium ions out and two potassium ions in for each ATP, which is why it leaves the cell with one net positive charge fewer per cycle.","why":"The numbers matter because the imbalance is what makes the pump electrogenic. Three positives out and two in removes one net positive charge from the cell each cycle, which contributes a few millivolts to the resting membrane potential. Flipping the numbers would make the pump add positive charge to the cell, which is not what happens."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the sodium potassium ATPase in a membrane with inside and outside labeled. Sketch the cycle in three or four steps: ions binding, ATP splitting, the shape change, ions releasing. Write 3 and 2 on the correct arrows in the correct directions. Beside it note the sodium and potassium concentrations inside and out that the pump maintains. One line: it moves three out for every two in, so say what that does to charge across the membrane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cell with the two gradients the pump maintains, sodium high outside and potassium high inside, as tall and short bars on each side. Now poison the pump with ouabain and draw the same bars ten minutes later and an hour later. One line: pick one downstream process that fails, not the resting membrane potential, and say how many steps removed from the pump it is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the sodium potassium ATPase uses ATP to move three sodium out and two potassium in, and state the two gradients it maintains."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Primary active transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-secondary-active-transport","name":"Secondary active transport","can":"Distinguish symport from antiport and trace how the sodium gradient powers glucose uptake by SGLT and calcium removal by the sodium calcium exchanger.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A transporter in a cell membrane moves Cl- into the cell while moving HCO3- out. How should it be classified?\nA) Primary active transport  |  B) Channel mediated diffusion\nC) Symport  |  D) Antiport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Antiport","why":"Two solutes crossing in opposite directions is the definition of an antiport, no matter which ions are involved. A symport would move both the same way. Nothing states that the protein hydrolyzes ATP, so primary active transport is not supported. A channel carries one species through a pore and does not couple two solutes to each other."},{"t":"mc","q":"The Na+ gradient across a cell membrane is reduced by half. Predict the effect on Ca2+ export by the sodium calcium exchanger.\nA) Ca2+ export rises, so intracellular Ca2+ falls\nB) Ca2+ export falls, so intracellular Ca2+ rises\nC) Ca2+ export is unchanged, because the exchanger uses ATP\nD) Ca2+ export stops completely and cannot resume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Ca2+ export falls, so intracellular Ca2+ rises","why":"The exchanger is powered by Na+ falling into the cell, so a shallower Na+ gradient supplies less energy and less Ca2+ is pushed out, letting cytosolic Ca2+ climb. Export cannot rise when its driving force has shrunk. The exchanger does not use ATP directly, and a partial reduction in gradient slows the exchanger rather than halting it permanently."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell becomes acidic inside. The Na+/H+ exchanger responds by moving H+ out. Which gradient pays for moving H+ against its concentration gradient?\nA) The outward K+ gradient  |  B) The inward Cl- gradient\nC) The outward HCO3- gradient  |  D) The inward Na+ gradient\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The inward Na+ gradient","why":"Na+ is far more concentrated outside than inside, so its inward movement releases energy the exchanger uses to push H+ out. K+ is concentrated inside, so its gradient points the wrong way to pull Na+ or H+ anywhere useful here. Cl- and HCO3- gradients drive other exchangers, such as the chloride bicarbonate antiport, but not this one."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a symport, the two transported solutes move in which directions?\nA) Both move only when ATP is bound to the carrier\nB) Both in the same direction across the membrane\nC) In opposite directions across the membrane\nD) One moves and the other stays bound to the carrier\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both in the same direction across the membrane","why":"Symport, also called cotransport, carries both solutes the same way at once, as SGLT does when it brings Na+ and glucose into the cell together. Opposite directions define an antiport, such as the sodium calcium exchanger. A solute that stayed bound would never be delivered, and direct ATP binding would make the protein a primary active pump rather than a coupled carrier."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with cholera is losing several liters of watery stool a day. His intestinal lining cells are intact. He is given an oral rehydration solution containing 75 mmol/L Na+ and 75 mmol/L glucose. Plain salt water at the same sodium concentration does not rehydrate cholera patients nearly as well.\nWhy does adding glucose to the drink increase the absorption of sodium and water?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Glucose and Na+ enter the cell together through SGLT, so each glucose pulls a Na+ in with it and water follows the absorbed salt","why":"SGLT is a symporter. It cannot move glucose without moving Na+ at the same time. Giving glucose in the drink drives the SGLT cycle, and every glucose absorbed brings Na+ into the cell with it. The basolateral pump throws that Na+ into the interstitial fluid, and water follows by osmosis. The cholera toxin blocks a different Na+ route, so SGLT is the pathway still working, and glucose is the key that turns it on."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace how the Na+/K+ ATPase powers calcium removal by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger in a heart muscle cell. Then predict what happens to intracellular Ca2+ and to contraction strength if the pump is partly inhibited, and explain why.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the pump uses ATP to keep intracellular Na+ low, storing energy in the inward Na+ gradient; the exchanger is an antiporter that moves Na+ in and Ca2+ out; Na+ flowing downhill into the cell provides the energy to push Ca2+ out against its gradient; pump inhibition lets intracellular Na+ rise and flattens the Na+ gradient; the exchanger has less driving force, so Ca2+ removal slows; intracellular Ca2+ rises and contraction becomes stronger","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cell membrane with the sodium potassium ATPase keeping sodium low inside. Beside it draw SGLT carrying sodium and glucose in together, and the sodium calcium exchanger sending calcium out as sodium comes in. Draw every ion arrow, and mark the sodium gradient as the shared energy source. Label which one is symport and which is antiport. One line: no ATP touches either of these two proteins, so say where their energy came from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the sodium calcium exchanger working normally, then draw the same exchanger in a cell whose sodium potassium pump has been stopped. Use three panels with arrows between them: what happens to intracellular sodium, then to the exchanger's driving force, then to intracellular calcium. One line: this is how digoxin strengthens a failing heart, so write the last step of that chain in your own words."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish symport from antiport and trace how the sodium gradient powers glucose uptake by SGLT and calcium removal by the sodium calcium exchanger."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Secondary active transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-transport-maximum","name":"Transport maximum and saturation","can":"Interpret a transport rate curve, identify the transport maximum, and apply the concept to renal glucose handling in hyperglycemia.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma glucose is 600 mg/dL, GFR is 100 mL/min, and the transport maximum for glucose is 375 mg/min. How much glucose is excreted per minute?\nA) 125 mg/min  |  B) 600 mg/min\nC) 375 mg/min  |  D) 225 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 225 mg/min","why":"The filtered load is 6 mg/mL times 100 mL/min, or 600 mg/min. Carriers reclaim 375 mg/min, so 600 minus 375 leaves 225 mg/min in the urine. Six hundred is the filtered load, which would only all be excreted if reabsorption were zero, and 375 is the reabsorbed amount. The 125 value comes from a different case with a normal GFR and a plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug lowers the transport maximum for glucose from 375 mg/min to 150 mg/min. With a GFR of 125 mL/min and a plasma glucose of 200 mg/dL, how much glucose is excreted per minute?\nA) 50 mg/min  |  B) 100 mg/min\nC) 250 mg/min  |  D) 0 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 100 mg/min","why":"The filtered load is 2 mg/mL times 125 mL/min, or 250 mg/min, and only 150 mg/min can now be reabsorbed, so 100 mg/min is excreted. Zero would be right with the normal maximum of 375, which is the point of the comparison. The 250 value is the filtered load before any reabsorption, and 50 comes from subtracting in the wrong direction."},{"t":"mc","q":"GFR is 125 mL/min and plasma glucose is 100 mg/dL. What is the filtered load of glucose?\nA) 12.5 mg/min  |  B) 125 mg/min\nC) 375 mg/min  |  D) 1250 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 125 mg/min","why":"A plasma glucose of 100 mg/dL is 1 mg/mL, so 125 mL/min carries 125 mg/min into the tubule. The 12.5 value comes from slipping a decimal place during the dL to mL conversion. The 375 value is the transport maximum rather than the filtered load, and 1250 would require a plasma glucose ten times higher than the one given."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has a plasma glucose of 120 mg/dL with a normal GFR. Will glucose appear in the urine?\nA) No, because the filtered load is well below the transport maximum\nB) No, because glucose is too large to be filtered\nC) Yes, because any glucose in plasma spills into urine\nD) Yes, because 120 mg/dL exceeds the transport maximum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) No, because the filtered load is well below the transport maximum","why":"At 120 mg/dL the filtered load is about 150 mg/min, comfortably under the 375 mg/min capacity, so the proximal tubule reclaims all of it. Plasma glucose is always present in the filtrate without spilling into urine, so the idea that any plasma glucose appears in urine is wrong on its face. The transport maximum is a rate, not a plasma concentration, so 120 mg/dL cannot exceed it. Glucose is small and is freely filtered."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a plasma glucose of 200 mg/dL (normal 70 to 100 fasting) and a glomerular filtration rate of 125 mL/min. Assume the kidney's transport maximum for glucose is 375 mg/min and that the carriers reabsorb everything up to that limit.\nHow much glucose appears in the urine each minute, and why?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"None, because the filtered load of 250 mg/min is below the transport maximum","why":"First find the filtered load. 200 mg/dL is 2 mg/mL, times 125 mL/min gives 250 mg/min filtered. The carriers can handle 375 mg/min, so they reabsorb all 250 and nothing is left for the urine. Glucose only spills when the filtered load climbs past the transport maximum, which for this patient would take a plasma glucose of about 300 mg/dL."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Once plasma glucose is high enough that the filtered load exceeds the transport maximum, any further rise in plasma glucose causes more glucose to appear in the urine.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Reabsorption cannot rise above the transport maximum because every carrier is already busy. Filtered load keeps rising with plasma glucose, so the difference between filtered and reabsorbed, which is the excreted amount, rises too. This is why urine glucose tracks plasma glucose in uncontrolled diabetes once the renal threshold is crossed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, transport rate on the y and solute concentration on the x. Draw a carrier mediated curve that rises and then flattens, and mark the transport maximum with a labeled dashed line. On the same axes draw a straight simple diffusion line for contrast. One line: past the transport maximum the curve is flat, so say what is happening to every extra solute molecule that arrives."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a kidney tubule with glucose filtered in, reabsorbed by carriers, and any leftover carried on to urine. Draw it three times: normal plasma glucose, plasma glucose at the renal threshold near 180 mg/dL, and plasma glucose well above that. Show the urine in each. Write the glucose transport maximum, about 375 mg/min, beside them. One line: say why a person can be losing glucose in urine while every carrier is working perfectly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to interpret a transport rate curve, identify the transport maximum, and apply the concept to renal glucose handling in hyperglycemia."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Transport maximum and saturation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-vesicular-transport","name":"Vesicular transport","can":"Compare phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor mediated endocytosis, and exocytosis by trigger, cargo, and energy requirement.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A neutrophil extends pseudopods around a bacterium and encloses it in a vesicle. Which process is this?\nA) Exocytosis  |  B) Pinocytosis\nC) Receptor mediated endocytosis  |  D) Phagocytosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Phagocytosis","why":"Surrounding a large solid target with pseudopods and sealing it into a vesicle is phagocytosis, the specialty of neutrophils and macrophages. Pinocytosis brings in fluid droplets rather than particles this large. Receptor mediated endocytosis concentrates specific molecules in coated pits and does not use pseudopods. Exocytosis would send material outward instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"A liver cell binds LDL particles to surface receptors that cluster in coated pits, then internalizes them. Which process is this?\nA) Receptor mediated endocytosis  |  B) Phagocytosis\nC) Pinocytosis  |  D) Simple diffusion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Receptor mediated endocytosis","why":"Binding to a specific receptor and concentrating in a coated pit before internalization defines receptor mediated endocytosis, which lets the cell gather scarce cargo efficiently. Phagocytosis handles large particles with pseudopods and is not receptor selective in this way. Pinocytosis is nonselective fluid uptake. A lipoprotein particle is far too large to diffuse through the bilayer."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell is treated so that ATP production stops. Which process continues at close to its normal rate?\nA) Phagocytosis of bacteria\nB) Exocytosis of secretory granules\nC) Receptor mediated endocytosis of LDL\nD) Diffusion of oxygen across the membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Diffusion of oxygen across the membrane","why":"Oxygen crosses by simple diffusion down its own partial pressure gradient, so it needs no cellular energy. All three vesicular processes require ATP for membrane remodeling, coat assembly, and fusion, so each of them slows or stops when ATP is gone. That shared energy requirement is what groups them together as active transport."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which form of endocytosis takes in large solid particles such as bacteria and cell debris?\nA) Exocytosis  |  B) Pinocytosis\nC) Phagocytosis  |  D) Receptor mediated endocytosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Phagocytosis","why":"Phagocytosis extends pseudopods around a solid particle and encloses it in a large vesicle, which is how a macrophage clears a bacterium. Pinocytosis takes in droplets of extracellular fluid, not solid particles. Receptor mediated endocytosis takes in specific molecules bound to surface receptors. Exocytosis moves material out of the cell rather than in."},{"t":"mc","q":"Pinocytosis brings which kind of cargo into the cell?\nA) Only lipid soluble hormones\nB) Solid particles the size of bacteria\nC) Small droplets of extracellular fluid and whatever is dissolved in them\nD) Only molecules bound to specific surface receptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Small droplets of extracellular fluid and whatever is dissolved in them","why":"Pinocytosis, sometimes called cell drinking, pinches off small vesicles of extracellular fluid, so the cargo is whatever happens to be dissolved nearby and the process is nonselective. Solid particles of bacterial size go by phagocytosis. Selection by receptor is receptor mediated endocytosis. Lipid soluble hormones cross the bilayer directly and need no vesicle at all."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Pinocytosis and receptor mediated endocytosis both require the cargo to bind a specific surface receptor before a vesicle forms.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Only receptor mediated endocytosis requires receptor binding; pinocytosis is nonselective and takes in a droplet of extracellular fluid along with whatever is dissolved in it.","why":"The receptor step is what makes receptor mediated endocytosis specific and efficient. Ligand binding gathers receptors into a clathrin coated pit and the cell captures a concentrated dose of one molecule. Pinocytosis has no such step, so it takes in fluid in proportion to what is in the fluid. This is why the cell uses receptor mediated endocytosis for scarce cargo like LDL and iron bound transferrin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one cell with four events happening on its surface at once: phagocytosis of a bacterium, pinocytosis of a droplet of fluid, receptor mediated endocytosis of a particle held in a coated pit, and exocytosis of a secretory vesicle. Draw the membrane actually deforming in each. Beside each write the trigger and the cargo. One line: all four cost ATP, so name the one that is completely unselective about what it takes in, and say what that costs the cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one particle's whole journey by receptor mediated endocytosis, left to right across your page: binding to receptors, the coated pit, the vesicle pinching off, fusion with a lysosome, and the receptors returning to the surface. Underneath, draw the reverse journey for a molecule leaving by exocytosis. One line: pick a cell type, not a neuron, and say which direction it spends more energy on."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor mediated endocytosis, and exocytosis by trigger, cargo, and energy requirement."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Vesicular transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-transepithelial-transport","name":"Transepithelial transport","can":"Trace glucose or sodium from lumen to blood across a polarized epithelium and identify which step occurs at the apical membrane and which at the basolateral membrane.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Glucose enters an intestinal epithelial cell from the lumen. Which type of transport is that first step?\nA) Facilitated diffusion  |  B) Secondary active transport\nC) Primary active transport  |  D) Simple diffusion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Secondary active transport","why":"Glucose is carried in by SGLT together with Na+, using the Na+ gradient rather than ATP, which makes the apical step secondary active transport and lets glucose move against its own gradient. Facilitated diffusion happens later, at the basolateral membrane through GLUT2. Simple diffusion cannot move polar glucose, and primary active transport would require the carrier itself to hydrolyze ATP."},{"t":"mc","q":"Glucose leaves the epithelial cell across the basolateral membrane on its way to the blood. Which type of transport is that step?\nA) Facilitated diffusion through GLUT2\nB) Secondary active transport through SGLT\nC) Primary active transport by an ATPase\nD) Vesicular transport by exocytosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Facilitated diffusion through GLUT2","why":"Apical uptake concentrates glucose inside the cell, so glucose can then move downhill through GLUT2, a passive carrier that needs no energy. SGLT is on the apical side and works in the opposite direction. No ATPase carries glucose, and packaging glucose into vesicles would be a wasteful way to move a small sugar."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which membrane of an absorptive epithelial cell holds the transporter that keeps intracellular Na+ low?\nA) The basolateral membrane\nB) Both membranes equally\nC) Neither, since Na+ is kept low by binding proteins in the cytosol\nD) The apical membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The basolateral membrane","why":"The basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase exports Na+ toward the interstitial fluid, which is what holds cytosolic Na+ low and sustains apical Na+ coupled uptake. An apical pump would return Na+ to the lumen and defeat absorption. Equal distribution would eliminate directionality, and cytosolic binding proteins are not how cells maintain their Na+ gradient."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sodium is absorbed across an intestinal epithelium and water follows. What drives the water movement?\nA) Water is carried inside vesicles with the Na+\nB) Water moves because tight junctions actively squeeze it through\nC) Water is pumped by an ATP driven water pump\nD) Water follows the osmotic gradient created by solute absorption\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Water follows the osmotic gradient created by solute absorption","why":"Moving Na+ and its accompanying solutes to the blood side raises osmolarity there, and water follows passively through aquaporins and between cells. There is no water pump in human cell membranes, since water movement is always driven by osmotic and hydrostatic forces. Vesicular carriage would be far too slow for the volumes involved, and junctions are passive seals rather than motors."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An infant is born with a mutation that makes apical SGLT1 nonfunctional in the small intestine. Basolateral GLUT2, the Na+/K+ ATPase, and the fructose carrier GLUT5 all work normally. Every feeding with milk (which contains glucose and galactose after digestion) produces severe watery diarrhea. Fructose based formula does not.\nExplain the mechanism of the diarrhea.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Glucose cannot enter the cells, so it stays in the lumen as an impermeant solute and holds water there osmotically, and the Na+ that would have entered with it is not absorbed either","why":"Glucose absorption has one door in from the lumen, SGLT1. Close it and dietary glucose stays in the gut. A solute that cannot be absorbed acts like an osmotic laxative, holding water in the lumen. On top of that, each glucose normally carried a Na+ in with it, so that salt and its water are also lost. Fructose uses GLUT5, a separate apical carrier, so it is still absorbed and does not cause the problem."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace one glucose molecule from the lumen of the small intestine to the blood. Name the transporter at each membrane, say which membrane it sits on, state whether each step is uphill or downhill for glucose, and explain why the Na+/K+ ATPase is essential even though it never touches glucose.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"SGLT on the apical membrane moves glucose into the cell with Na+; the apical step is uphill for glucose, powered by Na+ moving downhill; glucose accumulates inside the cell to a high concentration; GLUT2 on the basolateral membrane moves glucose out by facilitated diffusion, downhill; glucose then diffuses into the capillary; the Na+/K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low, which is what makes the apical Na+ step downhill","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one polarized epithelial cell, lumen on the left and blood on the right, tight junctions at the top, apical and basolateral membranes labeled. Trace one glucose molecule all the way through: SGLT at the apical membrane, GLUT at the basolateral membrane, and the sodium potassium pump on the basolateral side. Mark each step active or passive. One line: the pump never touches glucose, so say how it powers the apical step."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same epithelium twice. In the first, show what would go wrong if the basolateral pump drifted around to the apical side, and use that to say what tight junctions are for. In the second, draw the cell with apical SGLT blocked. Show where glucose ends up in each. One line: oral rehydration solution contains both salt and sugar, so say which of your two drawings explains why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace glucose or sodium from lumen to blood across a polarized epithelium and identify which step occurs at the apical membrane and which at the basolateral membrane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Transepithelial transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-lab-transport-sim","name":"Transport simulation","can":"Use a membrane transport simulation to distinguish simple diffusion from facilitated diffusion and active transport by their response to gradient reversal and metabolic poison.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In the simulation, solute X shows these results: the transport rate plateaus at high concentration, a metabolic poison does not change it, and reversing the gradient reverses the direction of net movement. Which transport type is solute X using?\nA) Simple diffusion  |  B) Facilitated diffusion\nC) Primary active transport  |  D) Osmosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Facilitated diffusion","why":"The plateau shows a limited number of transport proteins, while ATP independence and gradient following both show the process is passive, which together identify facilitated diffusion. Simple diffusion is ruled out by the plateau. Active transport is ruled out by the poison result and by the willingness to reverse with the gradient. Osmosis refers to water rather than a solute."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a simulation run, doubling the solute gradient doubles the transport rate, and the rate keeps rising with no ceiling at any concentration tested. Which process is this?\nA) Simple diffusion  |  B) Facilitated diffusion\nC) Primary active transport  |  D) Secondary active transport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Simple diffusion","why":"A rate that rises in direct proportion with no ceiling means nothing is saturating, which points to movement through the lipid rather than through a limited set of proteins. Facilitated diffusion would flatten once the carriers were full. Both active transport types would also flatten and would require energy, which a strictly gradient driven result does not indicate."},{"t":"mc","q":"A metabolic poison is added in the simulation and the transport rate for solute X does not change. Which conclusion is best supported?\nA) Solute X is being actively transported\nB) Solute X is moving by a passive process\nC) Solute X must be crossing without any protein\nD) Solute X is being moved against its gradient\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Solute X is moving by a passive process","why":"Insensitivity to ATP loss rules out active transport and marks the process as passive, but the poison test alone cannot say whether the passive route is simple or facilitated diffusion. Deciding between those two requires the saturation test. Uphill movement is also ruled out, since a passive process cannot build a gradient."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a membrane transport simulation, which transport type stops when a metabolic poison removes ATP from the model cell?\nA) Active transport  |  B) Osmosis of water\nC) Simple diffusion  |  D) Facilitated diffusion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Active transport","why":"Only active transport spends ATP, so it is the one process the poison shuts down. Simple and facilitated diffusion run on the solute gradient and keep going, which is why the poison run is the standard test for distinguishing them. Osmosis is likewise passive and follows the water gradient regardless of ATP."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the simulation, which transport type shows no plateau no matter how high the solute concentration is raised?\nA) Primary active transport  |  B) Secondary active transport\nC) Simple diffusion  |  D) Facilitated diffusion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Simple diffusion","why":"Simple diffusion has no carriers to saturate, so its rate keeps rising as long as the gradient rises. All three of the other processes depend on a limited number of transport proteins, so each one flattens into a plateau once every binding site is occupied. Watching for that plateau is how the simulation separates protein mediated transport from movement through the lipid."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, describe the two tests the transport simulation uses (reversing the concentration gradient and adding a metabolic poison) and predict how simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and active transport each respond to them. Add one more observation that separates simple from facilitated diffusion.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"reversing the gradient reverses the direction of net movement for simple and facilitated diffusion; active transport keeps moving solute in the same direction even against a reversed gradient; metabolic poison (no ATP) has no effect on simple or facilitated diffusion; metabolic poison stops active transport; facilitated diffusion and active transport plateau at high concentration because carriers saturate; simple diffusion never plateaus, its rate keeps rising with concentration","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you run it, draw three predicted graphs in a row, transport rate against time, one each for simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and active transport. Then draw a fourth showing what a metabolic poison does to whichever of the three it affects. Run the simulation and draw the real curves underneath in a second color. One line: say which transport type the poison left untouched, and why it could not touch it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a decision tree with three endpoints: simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, and active transport. At each branch put one test you can actually run in the simulation, reversing the gradient, raising solute until the rate stops climbing, or adding a metabolic poison. Draw the result that sends you down each branch. One line: name the single test that separates active transport from both of the others in one step."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to use a membrane transport simulation to distinguish simple diffusion from facilitated diffusion and active transport by their response to gradient reversal and metabolic poison."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Transport simulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Cell Signaling","comps":[{"id":"w3-signal-types","name":"Signal types and range","can":"Classify a chemical signal as autocrine, paracrine, neurotransmitter, neurohormone, or hormone by its route and distance of travel.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A mast cell in the skin releases histamine, which binds receptors on capillary endothelial cells a few micrometers away. How is histamine acting here?\nA) As a paracrine  |  B) As an autocrine\nC) As a neurohormone  |  D) As a hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) As a paracrine","why":"The signal is secreted into interstitial fluid and acts on nearby cells of a different type without entering the blood, which is the definition of paracrine action. Autocrine would require histamine to act back on the mast cell itself, which also happens but is not what is described here. It is not a hormone because it is not carried by blood to distant targets, and it is not a neurohormone because a mast cell is not a neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"An activated T cell secretes interleukin 2, which binds interleukin 2 receptors on that same T cell and drives it to divide. Classify this signal.\nA) Paracrine  |  B) Autocrine\nC) Hormone  |  D) Neurotransmitter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Autocrine","why":"The secreting cell is also the responding cell, so this is autocrine, and it creates a positive feedback loop that expands the clone. It would be paracrine only if the responding cells were neighbors rather than the secretor itself. It is not a hormone because no blood transport is involved, and it is not a neurotransmitter because there is no neuron and no synapse."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sympathetic neuron releases norepinephrine into a narrow gap where it binds beta 1 receptors on cardiac cells. Classify norepinephrine in this situation.\nA) Neurohormone  |  B) Paracrine\nC) Neurotransmitter  |  D) Hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Neurotransmitter","why":"A neuron is releasing the molecule directly onto an identified target across a synapse, so it is functioning as a neurotransmitter. The same molecule counts as a neurohormone only when a neuron releases it into blood. Paracrine would require release into general interstitial fluid rather than a synapse, and hormone would require an endocrine cell and blood transport."},{"t":"mc","q":"Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are modified sympathetic neurons that release epinephrine straight into the blood. Epinephrine released this way is best classified as which of the following?\nA) A neurotransmitter  |  B) A paracrine\nC) An autocrine  |  D) A neurohormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A neurohormone","why":"The releasing cell is neural in origin and the molecule enters the circulation to reach distant targets, and that combination defines a neurohormone. Calling it a neurotransmitter would require a synapse and a single postsynaptic cell. Paracrine and autocrine both require the molecule to stay in local interstitial fluid rather than being carried away by blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"Beta cells of the pancreas release insulin into pancreatic capillaries, and insulin then acts on muscle, liver, and fat throughout the body. Classify insulin.\nA) A hormone  |  B) A neurohormone\nC) A paracrine  |  D) An autocrine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A hormone","why":"An endocrine cell secretes it into blood and it acts on many distant tissues, which is the classical hormone pattern. It is not a neurohormone because a beta cell is an endocrine cell, not a neuron. It is not paracrine or autocrine in this description, although insulin does also act locally on neighboring islet cells, which shows one molecule can occupy more than one class depending on route."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A paracrine signal must enter the bloodstream to reach its target cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A paracrine signal diffuses through the interstitial fluid to nearby cells and never enters the blood. A signal that travels in the blood is a hormone or neurohormone.","why":"Paracrines act locally because they are broken down or taken up before they can travel far. That short range is the point. If a signal reaches the blood, it is by definition a hormone, and the whole body becomes a possible target."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one target cell in the middle of the box. Draw an arrow in from each of the five signal types, autocrine, paracrine, neurotransmitter, neurohormone, hormone. Label each arrow with how far it traveled and what carried it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Make a five row table. Signal type, where it is released, how far it goes, what it travels through. Fill it from memory, then check one row against the book."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a chemical signal as autocrine, paracrine, neurotransmitter, neurohormone, or hormone by its route and distance of travel."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Signal types and range. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-receptor-location","name":"Receptor location and ligand solubility","can":"Predict whether a signal molecule binds a surface receptor or an intracellular receptor from its lipid solubility and relate that to the speed and duration of the response.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Cells are pretreated with a protein synthesis inhibitor, then exposed to epinephrine and to cortisol. Predict the result for each.\nA) Both responses are lost\nB) The cortisol response persists and the epinephrine response is lost\nC) The epinephrine response persists and the cortisol response is lost\nD) Both responses persist unchanged\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The epinephrine response persists and the cortisol response is lost","why":"Epinephrine works through a surface receptor and second messengers that modify enzymes already present, so it does not need the ribosome. Cortisol works through an intracellular receptor whose entire output is new protein, so blocking translation removes its effect. Reversing the two swaps the mechanisms, and saying both survive ignores cortisol's dependence on gene expression."},{"t":"mc","q":"A hormone changes the activity of an existing enzyme within 10 seconds of exposure. Which receptor type is it most likely using?\nA) A surface receptor\nB) A cytosolic steroid receptor\nC) A nuclear thyroid hormone receptor\nD) A DNA binding receptor in the nucleus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A surface receptor","why":"Ten seconds is far too short for transcription and translation, so the hormone must be modifying proteins that are already present, which is the signature of surface receptor signaling. The other three are all transcription linked receptors whose effects require new protein and cannot appear that fast. Those receptors would instead explain a response measured in hours."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug that blocks RNA polymerase abolishes a hormone's effect on a target cell. What does that tell you about the receptor?\nA) It is a surface receptor coupled to a G protein and a second messenger\nB) It is an intracellular receptor working through gene transcription\nC) It is a ligand gated ion channel\nD) It is a surface receptor with kinase activity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is an intracellular receptor working through gene transcription","why":"If stopping transcription stops the response, the response depends on new messenger RNA, and that is the mechanism of a cytosolic or nuclear receptor. G protein pathways, ion channels, and receptor kinases all act on proteins that already exist, so their immediate effects survive a transcription blocker. Those pathways can change gene expression eventually, but their fast effects would remain."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pharmacologist wants a molecule that can be swallowed, cross membranes freely, and act on a nuclear receptor. Which property must the molecule have?\nA) A large positive charge\nB) A permanent covalent bond to a carrier protein\nC) A long chain of polar amino acids\nD) High lipid solubility\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) High lipid solubility","why":"Only a lipophilic molecule can slip through the bilayer of intestinal cells and then of target cells to reach a nuclear receptor. Strong charge and polar peptide chains both trap the molecule in water, which is exactly why peptide hormones must be injected and must use surface receptors. A permanent bond to a carrier would prevent the free molecule from ever entering a cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lipid soluble signal molecule most often binds a receptor located where?\nA) On the outer face of the plasma membrane\nB) Bound to the outside of a plasma protein in blood\nC) In the extracellular matrix outside the cell\nD) Inside the cell, in the cytosol or nucleus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Inside the cell, in the cytosol or nucleus","why":"Being lipid soluble lets the molecule pass straight through the phospholipid bilayer, so its receptor can sit in the cytosol or nucleus where the complex can act on DNA. A receptor on the outer membrane face is what a water soluble signal needs, since that signal cannot cross. Receptors are not free in the matrix, and plasma binding proteins carry hormones in blood but do not transmit a signal."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Two hormones are injected into a dog at time zero. Epinephrine, a water soluble amine, raises heart rate within 30 seconds, and the effect is gone in 5 minutes. Aldosterone, a steroid, does nothing measurable to sodium reabsorption for 60 minutes. Then reabsorption climbs and stays elevated for 8 hours.\nWhich explanation accounts for both time courses?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Epinephrine binds a surface receptor and uses second messengers to modify proteins already in the cell. Aldosterone enters the cell, binds an intracellular receptor, and changes gene transcription, so new proteins take time to appear and then persist","why":"Receptor location follows solubility, and time course follows receptor location. A water soluble hormone cannot enter the cell, so it signals from the surface, and second messengers act on existing proteins within seconds. The signal ends as soon as the hormone is cleared. A lipid soluble hormone enters the cell and changes gene expression. The lag is transcription and translation, and the long tail is the lifetime of the new proteins."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cell with one surface receptor and one intracellular receptor. Show the path a lipid soluble signal takes and the path a water soluble one takes. Mark which response is faster and which lasts longer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Write the two questions you would ask about any new signal molecule to predict where its receptor sits. Then answer both for a steroid and for a peptide hormone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict whether a signal molecule binds a surface receptor or an intracellular receptor from its lipid solubility and relate that to the speed and duration of the response."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Receptor location and ligand solubility. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-gpcr-pathway","name":"G protein coupled receptors","can":"Trace a G protein coupled receptor pathway from ligand binding through the G protein and amplifier enzyme to the second messenger and the cellular response.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An agonist binds a Gs coupled receptor on a liver cell. Predict what happens to cytosolic cyclic AMP and to protein kinase A activity.\nA) Cyclic AMP rises and protein kinase A activity falls  |  B) Both fall\nC) Both rise  |  D) Cyclic AMP falls and protein kinase A activity rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both rise","why":"Gs turns on adenylyl cyclase, cyclic AMP accumulates, and that cyclic AMP releases the catalytic subunits of protein kinase A, so the two move together. Cyclic AMP does not inhibit protein kinase A, which rules out the split answers. Both falling is the prediction for a Gi coupled agonist instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"An agonist activates a Gi coupled receptor. Predict what happens to cyclic AMP in that cell.\nA) It rises sharply  |  B) It stays constant while IP3 rises\nC) It falls below the resting level  |  D) It rises slowly over several hours\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It falls below the resting level","why":"Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase, so ongoing production drops while phosphodiesterase keeps degrading what is there, and the net level falls. A sharp rise is the Gs result. IP3 belongs to the Gq branch and is not generated by Gi, and a slow rise over hours describes a transcription driven response, not a second messenger."},{"t":"mc","q":"Adenylyl cyclase is completely inhibited in a cell. A Gs coupled agonist is then applied. Which step still occurs?\nA) Cyclic AMP still accumulates\nB) Protein kinase A is still activated\nC) Target proteins are still phosphorylated by protein kinase A\nD) The G protein alpha subunit still exchanges GDP for GTP\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The G protein alpha subunit still exchanges GDP for GTP","why":"The block sits at the amplifier enzyme, so everything upstream of it, including ligand binding and nucleotide exchange on the G protein, proceeds normally. Cyclic AMP cannot accumulate because the enzyme that makes it is disabled. Without cyclic AMP, protein kinase A stays inhibited by its regulatory subunits and no downstream phosphorylation follows."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which nucleotide is bound to the alpha subunit of a G protein when the G protein is inactive?\nA) ATP  |  B) GTP\nC) GDP  |  D) Cyclic AMP\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) GDP","why":"The resting G protein holds GDP on its alpha subunit, and activation is the exchange of that GDP for GTP after the receptor binds ligand. GTP marks the active state, so it is the answer to the opposite question. ATP is the substrate adenylyl cyclase uses, and cyclic AMP is the product further down the pathway, neither of which sits on the G protein."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a rare mutation in the adrenal cortex. The ACTH receptor is present and binds ACTH normally, but it cannot activate its Gs protein. Plasma ACTH is 180 pg/mL (normal 10 to 60). Plasma cortisol is 2 micrograms/dL (normal 5 to 25).\nPredict the cyclic AMP level in these adrenal cells and explain why ACTH is so high.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Cyclic AMP stays low despite high ACTH because the receptor cannot turn on adenylyl cyclase. ACTH is high because low cortisol removes negative feedback on the pituitary","why":"Trace the pathway. ACTH binds the receptor, but a receptor that cannot activate Gs cannot activate adenylyl cyclase, so cyclic AMP does not rise and cortisol synthesis is not stimulated. The pituitary senses the low cortisol, loses its negative feedback, and pours out ACTH. High trophic hormone with low peripheral hormone is the signature of a gland that cannot respond."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace what happens from the moment glucagon binds its receptor on a liver cell to the release of glucose into the blood. Include how the signal is turned off.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"glucagon binds a G protein coupled receptor on the cell surface; the receptor activates Gs, and the alpha subunit swaps GDP for GTP; active Gs alpha turns on adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cyclic AMP; cyclic AMP activates protein kinase A; protein kinase A phosphorylates enzymes that increase glycogen breakdown and decrease glycogen synthesis; glucose leaves the liver cell into the blood; the signal ends when Gs alpha hydrolyzes GTP and phosphodiesterase breaks down cyclic AMP","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the membrane with a receptor crossing it, and trace the pathway in numbered order: ligand binds, the G protein swaps GDP for GTP, the alpha subunit leaves, it switches on the amplifier enzyme, the enzyme makes second messenger, the messenger switches on a kinase, the kinase changes a target protein. Beside each step write roughly how many molecules that one step produced from one. One line: name the step where the biggest multiplication happened."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a Gs pathway and a Gq pathway side by side, starting from the same membrane and ending at two different cellular responses. Label the amplifier enzyme and the second messenger on each. Then draw the off switch on both, GTP being hydrolyzed on the alpha subunit. One line: cholera toxin jams that off switch open, so write what the gut cell keeps doing and why the person loses fluid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace a G protein coupled receptor pathway from ligand binding through the G protein and amplifier enzyme to the second messenger and the cellular response."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: G protein coupled receptors. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-second-messengers","name":"Second messengers","can":"Identify cyclic AMP, IP3, diacylglycerol, and calcium as second messengers and state the enzyme that generates each and one target it activates.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug mimics diacylglycerol at its binding site. Which enzyme would you expect to become active?\nA) Protein kinase A  |  B) Adenylyl cyclase\nC) Protein kinase C  |  D) Phosphodiesterase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Protein kinase C","why":"Diacylglycerol recruits and activates protein kinase C at the membrane, so a mimic occupies that same site and switches the kinase on without any ligand at the receptor. Protein kinase A responds to cyclic AMP, a different messenger. Adenylyl cyclase is activated by Gs, and phosphodiesterase is a degrading enzyme rather than a diacylglycerol target."},{"t":"mc","q":"You measure a rise in cyclic AMP in a cell. Which downstream activity should rise if the pathway is intact?\nA) Phosphorylation of proteins by protein kinase A\nB) Cleavage of PIP2 by phospholipase C\nC) Release of calcium through IP3 receptors\nD) Transcription driven by a steroid receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Phosphorylation of proteins by protein kinase A","why":"Cyclic AMP has one main job, activating protein kinase A, so its rise should be followed by increased phosphorylation of that kinase's substrates. PIP2 cleavage and IP3 mediated calcium release belong to the Gq branch and are not triggered by cyclic AMP. Steroid receptor transcription requires a lipophilic hormone and an intracellular receptor, not a second messenger."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing of messenger, generating enzyme, and target is correct?\nA) Cyclic AMP, phospholipase C, protein kinase C in the plasma membrane\nB) Calcium, adenylyl cyclase, protein kinase A\nC) Diacylglycerol, adenylyl cyclase, calmodulin\nD) IP3, phospholipase C, endoplasmic reticulum calcium release channel\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) IP3, phospholipase C, endoplasmic reticulum calcium release channel","why":"Phospholipase C makes IP3, and IP3 opens the release channel that lets stored calcium into the cytosol, so all three parts belong together. Cyclic AMP comes from adenylyl cyclase and acts on protein kinase A. Diacylglycerol comes from phospholipase C and acts on protein kinase C, and calcium is released from stores or enters through channels rather than being made by a cyclase."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme generates cyclic AMP?\nA) Phospholipase C  |  B) Adenylyl cyclase\nC) Phosphodiesterase  |  D) Protein kinase A\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Adenylyl cyclase","why":"Adenylyl cyclase sits in the plasma membrane and converts ATP into cyclic AMP once a Gs protein activates it. Phospholipase C generates IP3 and diacylglycerol instead. Phosphodiesterase breaks cyclic AMP down, and protein kinase A is the enzyme cyclic AMP activates rather than the one that makes it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main target activated by cyclic AMP?\nA) Calmodulin  |  B) Protein kinase C\nC) Protein kinase A  |  D) Phospholipase C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Protein kinase A","why":"Cyclic AMP binds the regulatory subunits of protein kinase A and releases the catalytic subunits, which then phosphorylate a long list of cellular proteins. Protein kinase C is activated by diacylglycerol and calcium. Calmodulin is the calcium binding partner, and phospholipase C is an amplifier enzyme in the other branch of the pathway."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Cyclic AMP is produced by phospholipase C.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Cyclic AMP is produced by adenylyl cyclase. Phospholipase C produces IP3 and diacylglycerol.","why":"Each G protein pathway has its own amplifier enzyme. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cyclic AMP. Gq activates phospholipase C, which cuts a membrane phospholipid into IP3 and diacylglycerol. Mixing these up is the most common second messenger error."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a membrane and put all four second messengers on one page: cyclic AMP, IP3, diacylglycerol, and calcium. For each, draw where it comes from, the enzyme or channel that produced it, and an arrow to one thing it switches on. Keep DAG in the membrane and IP3 out in the cytosol, because that is where they actually go. One line: one of the four is not made by an enzyme at all, so name it and say where it comes from instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw phospholipase C cutting PIP2 in the membrane and follow both halves. Send IP3 to the endoplasmic reticulum and draw what comes out. Leave DAG where it is and draw what it activates. Show the two halves converging on one response. Draw the adenylyl cyclase and cyclic AMP route beside it for contrast. One line: cyclic AMP is destroyed by phosphodiesterase, so say what a drug that blocks that enzyme does to the signal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify cyclic AMP, IP3, diacylglycerol, and calcium as second messengers and state the enzyme that generates each and one target it activates."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Second messengers. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-catalytic-intracellular-receptors","name":"Catalytic receptors and intracellular receptors","can":"Compare a receptor enzyme such as the insulin receptor with an intracellular steroid receptor by mechanism and by time course of the response.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A receptor has its ligand binding site facing the extracellular fluid and a catalytic domain projecting into the cytosol. How should it be classified?\nA) A receptor enzyme  |  B) An intracellular receptor\nC) A G protein coupled receptor  |  D) A ligand gated ion channel\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A receptor enzyme","why":"A single protein that both binds ligand outside and catalyzes a reaction inside is the definition of a receptor enzyme, the family that includes the insulin receptor. An intracellular receptor would have no extracellular domain at all. A G protein coupled receptor has no catalytic domain of its own, and an ion channel forms a conducting pore rather than a catalytic site."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mutation damages the DNA binding domain of a receptor. Which hormone's signaling would be impaired?\nA) Insulin  |  B) Cortisol\nC) Epinephrine  |  D) Acetylcholine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cortisol","why":"Only receptors that act as transcription factors have a DNA binding domain, so a steroid such as cortisol is the one that loses its pathway. Insulin acts through a membrane kinase and epinephrine through a G protein, neither of which touches DNA directly. Acetylcholine works through ion channels or G protein coupled receptors, also without a DNA binding step."},{"t":"mc","q":"The insulin receptor belongs to which class of receptor?\nA) A receptor enzyme with tyrosine kinase activity\nB) A G protein coupled receptor\nC) A ligand gated ion channel\nD) A nuclear transcription factor receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A receptor enzyme with tyrosine kinase activity","why":"The insulin receptor spans the membrane and its cytosolic portion is itself an enzyme, a tyrosine kinase, so binding insulin switches on catalytic activity directly with no G protein in between. A G protein coupled receptor would need a separate amplifier enzyme. Ligand gated channels conduct ions rather than phosphorylate proteins, and a nuclear receptor would require insulin to enter the cell, which a peptide cannot do."},{"t":"mc","q":"What chemical change does a tyrosine kinase make to its target proteins?\nA) It removes a phosphate group from tyrosine residues\nB) It adds a phosphate group to tyrosine residues\nC) It cleaves the protein at tyrosine residues\nD) It adds a methyl group to tyrosine residues\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It adds a phosphate group to tyrosine residues","why":"Kinases transfer a phosphate from ATP onto a specific amino acid, and adding that bulky negative charge changes the target protein's shape and activity. Removing phosphate is the job of a phosphatase, which is how these signals are reversed. Cleaving the protein is proteolysis, and methylation is a different modification used mainly in gene regulation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the receptor for a steroid hormone such as cortisol located?\nA) On the outer surface of the plasma membrane\nB) In the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum\nC) In the cytosol or nucleus of the target cell\nD) On the surface of circulating carrier proteins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In the cytosol or nucleus of the target cell","why":"Cortisol crosses the membrane on its own, so its receptor waits inside the cell and the hormone receptor complex then acts on DNA. A plasma membrane receptor is needed only by signals that cannot cross. The endoplasmic reticulum lumen is not a signaling compartment for steroids, and carrier proteins transport hormone in blood without transmitting a message."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A steroid hormone response takes longer to begin than a peptide hormone response because the steroid must first be converted to a water soluble form before it can enter the cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A steroid response is slow because the hormone receptor complex must change gene transcription and the cell must translate new protein. The steroid enters the cell easily because it is lipid soluble, no conversion is needed.","why":"Time course follows mechanism. Peptides work through surface receptors that modify proteins already in the cell, so the response is fast. Steroids work by making new protein, which takes an hour or more. Lipid solubility is what lets a steroid cross the membrane, so making it water soluble would block entry, not permit it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one cell with the insulin receptor in the membrane on one side and a steroid receptor sitting inside the cell on the other. Trace both from hormone arrival to final response, the steroid crossing the membrane and reaching DNA, the insulin receptor phosphorylating itself and moving a glucose transporter to the surface. Put a clock beside each showing how long the response takes. One line: name the one you could not use if you needed the effect ten seconds from now."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis with two response curves on it, a receptor enzyme response and a steroid response, each from hormone arrival through peak to fade. Make the onsets and durations honestly different. Under the axis, draw what is physically being changed in each case, an existing protein being modified or a new protein being built. One line: say which of the two you would want for the next thirty seconds and which for the next week."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare a receptor enzyme such as the insulin receptor with an intracellular steroid receptor by mechanism and by time course of the response."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Catalytic receptors and intracellular receptors. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-signal-amplification","name":"Signal amplification","can":"Explain how a cascade amplifies a signal and estimate the size of the amplification across the steps of a given pathway.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"One receptor activates 20 G proteins, each activated cyclase makes 200 cyclic AMP molecules, and each protein kinase A molecule phosphorylates 100 target enzymes. Estimate the number of enzymes affected by one binding event.\nA) About 400,000  |  B) About 4,000\nC) About 320  |  D) About 40,000,000\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 400,000","why":"Multiplying 20 by 200 by 100 gives 4 times 10 to the fifth power, so roughly four hundred thousand enzyme molecules are modified from a single ligand. The value 320 comes from adding the three numbers instead of multiplying them. Four thousand drops the hundredfold kinase step, and forty million would need another hundredfold factor that this pathway does not have."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pathway has three amplifying steps with gains of 10, 100, and 100. What is the total amplification?\nA) 210  |  B) 1,000\nC) 10,000  |  D) 100,000\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 100,000","why":"Multiplying 10 by 100 by 100 gives 10 to the fifth power, or 100,000. The sum of 210 treats sequential steps as if they were independent contributions. One thousand and ten thousand each drop one of the hundredfold steps, which shows how quickly the estimate collapses when a step is left out."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a pathway with gains of 20, 50, and 200, a drug doubles the gain of the middle step. What happens to total amplification?\nA) It doubles\nB) It increases by 50\nC) It increases fourfold\nD) It is unchanged, because the other steps are limiting\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It doubles","why":"Because the steps multiply, doubling any single factor doubles the product, no matter which step it is. Adding 50 would apply if the steps summed, which they do not. A fourfold rise would require two steps to double, and no step is limiting in a simple multiplicative chain, so the total cannot be unchanged."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a G protein coupled receptor pathway, where does amplification begin?\nA) One ligand binds many receptors at once\nB) One second messenger molecule binds many receptors at the surface\nC) One G protein binds many ligands\nD) One activated receptor activates many G protein molecules\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) One activated receptor activates many G protein molecules","why":"A single occupied receptor can cycle through and switch on many G proteins before the ligand leaves, so the first multiplication happens right at the membrane. A single ligand molecule occupies one receptor at a time, so binding itself does not amplify. G proteins do not bind ligand, and second messengers act downstream on kinases rather than back on receptors."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature of a cascade makes it capable of large amplification?\nA) Each step uses a different second messenger\nB) Each active enzyme acts catalytically on many substrate molecules\nC) Each step is reversible, so signal can flow both directions\nD) Each step requires a new receptor to be synthesized\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Each active enzyme acts catalytically on many substrate molecules","why":"Turnover number is the engine of amplification: one enzyme molecule handles many substrate molecules per second, and stacking such steps multiplies the gain. Using different messengers adds versatility but not gain by itself. Cascades flow in one direction under normal conditions, and no step waits for new receptor synthesis, which would slow rather than amplify the signal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a three step cascade as a widening funnel. Put a number on each step and multiply them down the side to get the total amplification."},{"t":"draw","q":"Start from one ligand and write the count at each step of a cascade until you reach the response. One line per step, numbers only, no sentences."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how a cascade amplifies a signal and estimate the size of the amplification across the steps of a given pathway."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Signal amplification. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-receptor-modulation","name":"Receptor modulation","can":"Define agonist, antagonist, competitive inhibition, up regulation, and down regulation and predict the effect of chronic ligand excess on target cell sensitivity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug binds beta 1 receptors on cardiac cells but produces no change in heart rate, and it prevents norepinephrine from acting. How should it be classified?\nA) An antagonist  |  B) A full agonist\nC) A partial agonist  |  D) An up regulator\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) An antagonist","why":"Binding with no response of its own plus blockade of the natural ligand is the definition of an antagonist. A full agonist would raise heart rate on its own. A partial agonist would produce some response, just less than the full agonist, and up regulation refers to receptor number rather than to a drug action at the binding site."},{"t":"mc","q":"A competitive antagonist is present. What happens if the agonist concentration is raised high enough?\nA) The maximal response stays permanently reduced\nB) The full response can still be reached\nC) The receptor is destroyed and cannot respond\nD) The antagonist becomes an agonist at high concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The full response can still be reached","why":"Competition is a matter of odds at a shared site, so flooding the system with agonist wins back the receptors and restores the maximum, though it takes more agonist to do it. A permanently reduced maximum is what a noncompetitive or irreversible blocker produces. Competitive binding is reversible and does not destroy receptors, and an antagonist does not change into an agonist with concentration."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has chronically elevated insulin levels for months. Predict what happens to insulin receptor number and to target cell sensitivity.\nA) Receptor number rises and sensitivity increases\nB) Receptor number is unchanged and sensitivity increases\nC) Receptor number falls but sensitivity increases\nD) Receptor number falls and sensitivity decreases\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Receptor number falls and sensitivity decreases","why":"Sustained ligand excess drives internalization and degradation of receptors, so the cell reads the same insulin level as a weaker signal, which is one contributor to insulin resistance. Rising receptor number is the response to chronic deficiency instead. Fewer receptors cannot make a cell more sensitive, so any answer pairing falling or unchanged receptor number with increased sensitivity has the relationship backward."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change would make a target cell more sensitive to a given hormone concentration?\nA) An increase in the rate of receptor internalization\nB) An increase in the number of receptors on the cell\nC) A decrease in the affinity of the receptor for the hormone\nD) An increase in the rate of hormone degradation in plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) An increase in the number of receptors on the cell","why":"More receptors means more binding events at any given concentration, so the same signal produces a larger response. Faster internalization lowers receptor number and reduces sensitivity. Lower affinity requires more hormone for the same effect, and faster plasma degradation lowers the concentration reaching the cell rather than changing how the cell responds."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with asthma has been using a beta 2 agonist inhaler 8 to 10 times a day for 3 weeks. She reports that each puff now gives less relief. A lung function test shows her FEV1 improves by only 5 percent after a dose (a normal response to this drug is 12 percent or more).\nWhat has happened at the receptor level, and what would be expected to restore her response?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Chronic agonist exposure has down regulated beta 2 receptors on airway smooth muscle. Reducing how often she uses the drug for several days lets receptor number recover","why":"Continuous agonist exposure tells the cell it is being overstimulated, and the cell responds by pulling receptors out of the membrane and degrading them. Fewer receptors means less response per dose. Receptor number is regulated in both directions, so removing the excess stimulus lets the cell rebuild its receptor population over days."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient uses a beta 2 agonist inhaler many times a day for weeks and finds it works less and less. Explain what has happened at the receptor level, and predict what happens to sensitivity if the patient stops using it for a week and then takes one dose.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the inhaler drug is an agonist, it activates the receptor; chronic high ligand exposure causes down regulation of the receptor; receptors are removed from the membrane by endocytosis and degraded, so receptor number falls; fewer receptors means lower target cell sensitivity, so the same dose gives a smaller response; after the drug is stopped, the cell makes new receptors and receptor number recovers over days; a single dose after the break produces a larger response than the last dose during heavy use","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one target cell three times. Normal, after a long stretch of scarce signal, after a long stretch of excess signal. Show the receptor count changing in each and label which is up regulation and which is down."},{"t":"draw","q":"Define agonist, antagonist, up regulation and down regulation in your own words, one line each. Then explain in two lines why a patient who needs more opioid for the same effect is usually showing down regulation rather than drug seeking."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define agonist, antagonist, competitive inhibition, up regulation, and down regulation and predict the effect of chronic ligand excess on target cell sensitivity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Receptor modulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-signal-termination","name":"Signal termination","can":"Name three mechanisms that end a chemical signal and explain why a signal that cannot be terminated produces pathology.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme ends the action of acetylcholine at a synapse?\nA) Monoamine oxidase  |  B) Phosphodiesterase\nC) Adenylyl cyclase  |  D) Acetylcholinesterase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Acetylcholinesterase","why":"Acetylcholinesterase sits in the synaptic cleft and splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline within milliseconds, which is why each nerve impulse produces a separate muscle response. Phosphodiesterase degrades cyclic AMP inside the cell. Adenylyl cyclase makes a second messenger rather than ending a signal, and monoamine oxidase breaks down catecholamines, not acetylcholine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme terminates a cyclic AMP signal?\nA) Phosphodiesterase  |  B) Adenylyl cyclase\nC) Protein kinase A  |  D) Phospholipase C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Phosphodiesterase","why":"Phosphodiesterase hydrolyzes cyclic AMP to AMP, so the messenger disappears within seconds once production stops. Adenylyl cyclase makes cyclic AMP, the opposite job. Protein kinase A is the target of cyclic AMP, and phospholipase C belongs to a different branch of signaling entirely."},{"t":"mc","q":"What kind of enzyme reverses the changes a protein kinase has made?\nA) A cyclase  |  B) A phosphatase\nC) A protease  |  D) A ligase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A phosphatase","why":"Phosphatases remove the phosphate groups kinases add, returning target proteins to their resting activity and ending the response. Cyclases make cyclic nucleotides rather than removing phosphate from proteins. A protease would destroy the protein instead of resetting it, and a ligase joins molecules together."},{"t":"mc","q":"How is a calcium signal in the cytosol normally terminated?\nA) Calcium is degraded by an enzyme in the cytosol\nB) Calcium binds permanently to calmodulin in the cytosol and is inactivated\nC) Calcium ATPase pumps move calcium back into stores and out of the cell\nD) Calcium diffuses into the nucleus where it has no effect\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Calcium ATPase pumps move calcium back into stores and out of the cell","why":"Calcium is an element, so it cannot be broken down and must instead be physically moved, which the endoplasmic reticulum and plasma membrane pumps do at the cost of ATP. There is no enzyme that degrades an ion. Calmodulin binding is reversible and is part of the signal rather than its end, and calcium entering the nucleus does not remove it from the cytosolic pool."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is reuptake as a mechanism of signal termination?\nA) The neurotransmitter diffuses out of the cleft into the blood\nB) An enzyme in the cleft splits the neurotransmitter into inactive pieces\nC) The receptor is pulled into the cell along with its ligand\nD) A transporter carries the neurotransmitter back into the cell that released it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A transporter carries the neurotransmitter back into the cell that released it","why":"Reuptake uses a membrane transporter to recapture intact transmitter for reuse, which both clears the cleft and saves the cell the cost of making more. Enzymatic splitting in the cleft is degradation, a separate mechanism. Pulling in the receptor is internalization, which removes the receptor rather than the ligand, and simple diffusion away is a third and slower route."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"If the enzyme that degrades a neurotransmitter is blocked, the signal ends faster because the transmitter diffuses away sooner.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Blocking the degrading enzyme makes the signal last longer, because the transmitter stays in the cleft and keeps binding receptors.","why":"Degradation is one of the main ways a signal is ended. Remove it and ligand accumulates, receptors stay occupied, and the target keeps responding. Diffusion does not speed up to compensate. This is why acetylcholinesterase inhibitors cause prolonged muscle stimulation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one signal running from receptor to response across a cell, then add three different off switches to that same drawing at three different points: the ligand removed or destroyed outside the cell, the second messenger broken down inside, and the response protein having its phosphate taken off. Name the enzyme doing each. One line: pick the switch nearest the response and say what still goes wrong if only that one fails."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a signal, not insulin, and draw it working normally, then draw it again with its termination broken. In the broken version show what piles up and where. Underneath, draw the effect on the tissue as two panels, one over minutes and one over weeks. One line: a signal that never shuts off eventually produces less response rather than more, so write what the cell did to itself."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name three mechanisms that end a chemical signal and explain why a signal that cannot be terminated produces pathology."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Signal termination. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w3-lab-dose-response","name":"Dose response relationships","can":"Plot a dose response curve, identify threshold, maximal response, and EC50, and compare a full agonist with a partial agonist on the same axes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A tissue reaches a maximal tension of 80 grams. At what tension would you read the EC50 off the curve?\nA) 80 grams  |  B) 40 grams\nC) 20 grams  |  D) 8 grams\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 40 grams","why":"EC50 is defined against the maximal response of that same preparation, so half of 80 grams is 40 grams and the corresponding dose is read down to the horizontal axis. Eighty grams is the maximum itself. Twenty grams is one quarter of maximum, and 8 grams is 10 percent, neither of which is the halfway point."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two drugs act at the same receptor. Drug A has an EC50 of 1 nanomolar and drug B an EC50 of 100 nanomolar. Which statement is correct?\nA) Drug B is more potent, because it acts at a higher concentration\nB) Drug B must be a partial agonist\nC) Drug A must also produce a larger maximal response\nD) Drug A is one hundred times more potent than drug B\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Drug A is one hundred times more potent than drug B","why":"Potency is inversely related to EC50, so needing one hundredth as much drug for the same fractional effect makes drug A one hundred times more potent. Requiring a higher concentration means less potent, not more. Potency and efficacy are independent, so nothing about the maximal response or partial agonism follows from EC50 alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug's curve has reached its plateau. Why does doubling the dose again produce no further response?\nA) The receptors and the downstream machinery are already saturated\nB) The drug has been fully metabolized by the tissue at that dose\nC) The receptor affinity falls as dose rises\nD) The response axis cannot record higher values\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The receptors and the downstream machinery are already saturated","why":"Once essentially every available receptor is occupied and the response system is running at capacity, extra drug molecules have nothing left to bind or drive. Metabolism does not switch on selectively at the plateau dose. Affinity is a fixed property of the drug receptor pair at a given condition, and the flat top is a biological ceiling rather than a limit of the measuring device."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a bath experiment, doses of 0.1, 1, 10, and 100 micromolar produce responses of 0, 0, 18, and 62 percent of maximum. What is the best estimate of the threshold dose?\nA) About 0.1 micromolar  |  B) About 10 micromolar\nC) About 1 micromolar  |  D) About 100 micromolar\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 10 micromolar","why":"Threshold is the lowest tested dose that produces a measurable response, and 10 micromolar is the first dose to move the response off zero. The two lower doses produced nothing at all, so neither can be the threshold. One hundred micromolar produces a large response but is well above the point where the response first appeared."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a dose response curve, what is plotted on the horizontal axis?\nA) The magnitude of the response\nB) The dose or concentration of the drug, usually on a log scale\nC) The time since the drug was given\nD) The number of receptors present on the target cell membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The dose or concentration of the drug, usually on a log scale","why":"Dose is the independent variable and goes on the horizontal axis, and a log scale is used because effective doses span several orders of magnitude. Response magnitude is the dependent variable and belongs on the vertical axis. Time belongs on a time course plot, and receptor number is a property of the tissue rather than something varied along the axis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a dose response curve with log dose on the x axis and response as a percentage of maximum on the y. Mark threshold, EC50, and the maximal response, with construction lines dropped to the axes. Then plot a partial agonist on the same axes. One line: the partial agonist never reaches the top no matter how much you add, so say what that tells you about the drug that raising the dose cannot fix."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three curves on one set of axes: a full agonist alone, the same agonist with a competitive antagonist added, and the same agonist with an antagonist that will not come off the receptor. Show which curve slides sideways and which one gets shorter. One line: only one of the two antagonists can be beaten by giving more agonist, so name it and say what feature of the graph proves it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to plot a dose response curve, identify threshold, maximal response, and EC50, and compare a full agonist with a partial agonist on the same axes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Dose response relationships. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Membrane Potential","comps":[{"id":"w2-electrochemical-gradient","name":"Ion distribution and electrochemical gradients","can":"State the typical intracellular and extracellular concentrations of sodium, potassium, chloride, and calcium and separate the chemical from the electrical component of the driving force on each ion.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron sits at a resting membrane potential of -70 mV. Which way do the chemical and the electrical forces push Na+?\nA) Chemical inward, electrical inward\nB) Chemical inward, electrical outward\nC) Chemical outward, electrical inward\nD) Chemical outward, electrical outward\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Chemical inward, electrical inward","why":"Na+ is about ten times more concentrated outside, so diffusion pushes it in, and the negative interior attracts the positive ion, so the electrical force pushes it in as well. That is why Na+ has the largest net driving force of any ion at rest and why opening Na+ channels depolarizes so sharply. The options that put a force outward describe K+ or Cl- instead, whose two components oppose each other at rest."},{"t":"mc","q":"At a resting membrane potential of -70 mV, which way does the electrical force alone push K+?\nA) Into the cell, because K+ is concentrated outside\nB) Into the cell, because the interior is negative\nC) Out of the cell, because K+ is concentrated inside\nD) Out of the cell, because the interior is negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Into the cell, because the interior is negative","why":"The electrical force is about voltage and charge only: a negative interior attracts the positive K+ ion inward. The option saying K+ leaves because it is concentrated inside states the chemical force correctly but answers the wrong half of the question, and that is exactly the confusion this card is testing. A negative interior cannot push a cation out. K+ is not concentrated outside, so that reasoning fails on the facts."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these ions is present at a much higher concentration inside a cell than in the extracellular fluid?\nA) K+  |  B) Cl-\nC) Ca2+  |  D) Na+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) K+","why":"Intracellular K+ runs about 140 mEq/L against roughly 4 mEq/L outside, a gradient the Na+/K+ ATPase rebuilds continuously. Na+ is the mirror image, about 145 mEq/L outside and 15 mEq/L inside. Cl- is also higher outside, near 105 mEq/L against about 10 mEq/L inside. Ca2+ shows the steepest gradient of all, but it points inward: free Ca2+ inside a resting cell is only about 0.0001 mM."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the typical extracellular sodium concentration in a healthy adult?\nA) 45 mEq/L  |  B) 145 mEq/L\nC) 290 mEq/L  |  D) 15 mEq/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 145 mEq/L","why":"Plasma Na+ is held near 145 mEq/L, and it is the dominant extracellular cation. The value of 15 mEq/L is the intracellular Na+ concentration, which is the other half of the gradient. 45 mEq/L does not correspond to either compartment. 290 is the number to remember for plasma osmolarity in mOsm/L, a total particle count rather than a sodium concentration."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the typical intracellular potassium concentration?\nA) 14 mEq/L  |  B) 40 mEq/L\nC) 140 mEq/L  |  D) 4 mEq/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 140 mEq/L","why":"Cells hold roughly 140 mEq/L of K+, which makes K+ the dominant intracellular cation. 4 mEq/L is the extracellular value, so picking it flips the gradient. 14 and 40 mEq/L do not match either compartment; 14 is close to intracellular Na+, which is a different ion entirely."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a resting neuron, the chemical gradient and the electrical gradient on K+ point in the same direction, both pushing K+ out of the cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The chemical gradient pushes K+ out, but the negative interior pulls K+ in, so the two forces on K+ oppose each other at rest.","why":"K+ is high inside, so concentration drives it out. But the inside of the cell is negative, and a negative interior attracts a positive ion, so the electrical force pulls K+ back in. At the K+ equilibrium potential (about -90 mV) the two forces balance exactly. At -70 mV the chemical force is a little stronger, so K+ leaks out slowly. The ion whose two forces do point the same way at rest is Na+, which is why Na+ entry is so fast when channels open."},{"t":"draw","q":"Which ion has the largest driving force at rest, and what keeps it from moving? Draw four vertical voltage scales side by side, one per ion, from minus 100 mV up to plus 150 mV. Mark each ion's equilibrium potential: potassium near minus 90 mV, sodium near plus 60 mV, calcium near plus 120 mV, chloride near minus 65 mV. Draw one horizontal line at minus 70 mV across all four. Each gap from that line is a driving force. In one line: name the ion with the largest gap and say why it barely moves at rest."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: State the typical intracellular and extracellular concentrations of sodium, potassium, chloride, and calcium and separate the chemical from the electrical component of the driving force on each ion. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the typical intracellular and extracellular concentrations of sodium, potassium, chloride, and calcium and separate the chemical from the electrical component of the driving force on each ion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ion distribution and electrochemical gradients. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-nernst","name":"Nernst equation","can":"Explain what an ion's equilibrium potential means, predict its sign from the direction of the ion's concentration gradient and its charge, and use it to predict which way that ion will move when its channels open.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cell rests at -70 mV. E_Na is +60 mV, E_K is -90 mV, E_Cl is -70 mV and E_Ca is +120 mV. Which ion has the largest driving force pushing it into the cell?\nA) Na+  |  B) K+\nC) Cl-  |  D) Ca2+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Ca2+","why":"The driving force is the gap between the membrane potential and each ion's equilibrium potential. From -70 mV, Ca2+ is 190 mV away from its balance point, Na+ is 130 mV away, K+ is 20 mV away in the outward direction, and Cl- is at its balance point with no net push. So Ca2+ has the largest inward driving force, which is why even a few open Ca2+ channels let Ca2+ flood in, as happens at the axon terminal. Na+ has a large inward push too, but smaller than Ca2+. K+ is pushed out, not in."},{"t":"mc","q":"In terms of its effect on the membrane potential, Cl- entering a cell is most like which of these?\nA) Na+ entering the cell  |  B) Ca2+ entering the cell\nC) K+ leaving the cell  |  D) K+ entering the cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) K+ leaving the cell","why":"What changes the membrane potential is the movement of charge. Cl- carries a negative charge, so bringing it in makes the inside more negative. Taking positive charge out does the same thing, which is exactly what K+ leaving does. Na+ or Ca2+ entering brings positive charge in and depolarizes the cell, and so would K+ entering. This is why the direction rule for an anion looks reversed compared with a cation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change would make the equilibrium potential for Na+ more positive?\nA) Opening more Na+ channels\nB) Raising the extracellular Na+ concentration\nC) Raising the intracellular Na+ concentration\nD) Lowering the extracellular Na+ concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Raising the extracellular Na+ concentration","why":"E_Na depends only on the Na+ gradient and its charge. Making the outside even richer in Na+ makes the gradient steeper, so a more positive interior would be needed to hold Na+ out, and E_Na becomes more positive. Raising inside Na+ or lowering outside Na+ flattens the gradient and makes E_Na less positive. Opening more channels changes how strongly Na+ pulls the membrane toward E_Na, but it does not move E_Na itself."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A researcher studies a neuron at 37 C (98.6 F). Cl- is about 20 times more concentrated outside the cell than inside, and the equilibrium potential for Cl- in this neuron is about -82 mV. The neuron's resting potential is -70 mV. A neurotransmitter then opens Cl- channels on the cell.\nWhy is E_Cl negative, and what happens when the Cl- channels open at -70 mV?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Because Cl- is a negative ion that is high outside; Cl- enters the cell and the membrane hyperpolarizes toward -82 mV","why":"Cl- is crowded outside, so its gradient pushes it in. Because Cl- is negative, only a negative interior can repel it enough to stop that entry, which is why E_Cl is negative. At -70 mV the cell is less negative than -82 mV, so the electrical force pushing Cl- out is weaker than the concentration force pushing it in. Cl- enters, carrying negative charge in, and the membrane moves toward -82 mV. That is an inhibitory response."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"If a neuron's membrane were permeable only to Na+, its membrane potential would settle near +60 mV.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"An equilibrium potential is the voltage at which the electrical force exactly balances the concentration force on one ion. Na+ is crowded outside, so it would keep entering until the inside became positive enough, about +60 mV, to stop further net entry. A real neuron never gets there because K+ leak channels stay open, but the peak of the action potential does approach this value, which is why the overshoot exists."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what the equilibrium potential for K+ means. Use the fact that K+ is about 30 times more concentrated inside the cell than outside to predict whether E_K is positive or negative, give its approximate value, and explain which way K+ moves when its channels open in a neuron resting at -70 mV.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the equilibrium potential is the membrane potential at which the electrical force on K+ exactly balances its concentration gradient, so there is no net K+ movement; K+ is high inside, so its concentration gradient pushes it out of the cell; to hold a positive ion in, the inside must be negative, so E_K is negative; E_K is about -90 mV; at -70 mV the inside is not negative enough to hold K+ in, so K+ leaves when its channels open; K+ leaving pulls the membrane potential toward -90 mV, which hyperpolarizes the cell","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Why is one equilibrium potential negative and the other positive? Draw a cell with potassium high inside and sodium high outside. For each ion, draw the chemical arrow (down its concentration gradient) and the electrical arrow that would balance it. Beside each ion, write its equilibrium potential and whether the sign is positive or negative. In one line: for each ion, say why the sign comes out that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"When a channel opens, which way does the ion move, and which way does the membrane potential go? Draw a vertical voltage scale from minus 100 to plus 70 mV. Mark E_K, E_Na, and a resting potential of minus 70 mV. Open potassium channels. Draw an arrow for which way potassium moves and which way the membrane potential moves. Now open sodium channels and do the same. In one line: label which ion has the bigger driving force and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain what an ion's equilibrium potential means, predict its sign from the direction of the ion's concentration gradient and its charge, and use it to predict which way that ion will move when its channels open."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Nernst equation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-resting-potential","name":"Resting membrane potential","can":"Explain why the resting membrane potential sits near the potassium equilibrium potential, explain in words how the Goldman equation combines each ion's concentration gradient and permeability, and predict how the potential shifts when membrane permeability to potassium or sodium changes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular K+ rises from 4 to 8 mEq/L. Which way does the resting membrane potential move?\nA) It does not change, because intracellular K+ is unchanged\nB) It depolarizes past 0 mV\nC) It depolarizes, becoming less negative\nD) It hyperpolarizes, becoming more negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It depolarizes, becoming less negative","why":"Raising extracellular K+ shrinks the K+ gradient, so E_K becomes less negative, and because the resting membrane follows E_K closely the resting potential follows it up. This is the mechanism behind the cardiac and neuromuscular effects of hyperkalemia. Hyperpolarization would require lowering extracellular K+ instead. The resting potential tracks a ratio, so a change on either side matters, and a doubling of extracellular K+ shifts it by roughly 18 mV, nowhere near 0 mV."},{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular K+ falls from 4 to 2 mEq/L. Which way does the resting membrane potential move?\nA) It stays the same, because K+ permeability did not change\nB) It moves toward E_Na\nC) It depolarizes toward threshold\nD) It hyperpolarizes, becoming more negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It hyperpolarizes, becoming more negative","why":"Halving extracellular K+ steepens the K+ gradient, so E_K becomes more negative and the resting potential follows, sitting further from threshold. That extra negativity is why hypokalemia makes excitable cells harder to fire. Depolarization is the hyperkalemic direction, not the hypokalemic one. Permeability did not change, but the driving force did, and Vm moves toward E_K, not toward E_Na, whenever K+ dominates."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell's membrane permeability to K+ increases while all concentrations stay the same. What happens to the resting membrane potential?\nA) It hyperpolarizes toward E_K\nB) It depolarizes toward E_Na\nC) It moves to exactly 0 mV\nD) It is unchanged, because the K+ gradient did not change\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It hyperpolarizes toward E_K","why":"In the Goldman weighting, the ion with the highest permeability dominates the membrane potential, so raising K+ permeability pulls Vm closer to E_K near -90 mV. It cannot pass E_K, because at that point K+ movement stops. Depolarization toward E_Na would follow a rise in Na+ permeability instead. A value of 0 mV would require the gradients to vanish, which is not what a permeability change does."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A neuron rests at -70 mV. Its K+ equilibrium potential is -90 mV and its Na+ equilibrium potential is +60 mV, and its threshold is -55 mV. A drug opens extra K+ channels in the membrane, tripling the resting K+ permeability without changing Na+ permeability or the ion gradients.\nPredict the new resting potential and the effect on how easily the neuron fires.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The resting potential moves toward -90 mV and the neuron becomes harder to fire","why":"The resting potential is a weighted average of the equilibrium potentials, weighted by permeability. At rest K+ already dominates, which is why -70 mV sits close to -90 mV. Tripling K+ permeability gives K+ even more of a say, so the potential moves closer to -90 mV. That widens the gap to threshold from 15 mV to something larger, so a bigger stimulus is now needed. This is how many inhibitory signals and some anesthetics work."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The resting membrane potential is negative mainly because the Na+/K+ ATPase pumps three Na+ out for every two K+ it brings in.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The resting potential is negative mainly because the membrane is far more permeable to K+ than to Na+ at rest, so K+ leaks out and pulls the potential toward the K+ equilibrium potential; the pump's unequal exchange contributes only a few millivolts directly.","why":"The pump is essential, but for a different reason: it builds and maintains the K+ and Na+ gradients. The potential itself comes from what those gradients do through open channels. Because K+ leak channels outnumber Na+ leak channels many times over, K+ exit dominates and the inside becomes negative. If you blocked the pump, the immediate change would be only about 3 to 4 mV; the big collapse of the resting potential would come later, as the gradients ran down."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a neuron's resting membrane potential sits at about -70 mV rather than at the K+ equilibrium potential (about -90 mV) or the Na+ equilibrium potential (about +60 mV). Then predict what happens to the resting potential if K+ permeability suddenly increases.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the resting membrane is much more permeable to K+ than to Na+ because of open K+ leak channels; K+ leaving the cell makes the inside negative and pulls the potential toward the K+ equilibrium potential; a small Na+ leak inward pulls the potential a little positive of -90 mV, which is why rest is -70 rather than -90 mV; the Na+/K+ ATPase maintains the gradients and adds only a few mV of negativity directly; the potential never approaches +60 mV because Na+ permeability at rest is very low; increasing K+ permeability moves the potential closer to -90 mV, a hyperpolarization","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Which changes move the resting potential a lot, and which barely move it at first? Draw a voltage scale from minus 90 to plus 60 mV with the resting potential marked. Draw four labeled arrows off it showing where the potential moves when you: open more potassium channels, open sodium channels, block the potassium leak, stop the sodium potassium pump. In one line: one of those four shifts the potential only a few mV even though it eventually ruins the cell. Name it and say why the immediate effect is so small."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Explain why the resting membrane potential sits near the potassium equilibrium potential, explain in words how the Goldman equation combines each ion's concentration gradient and permeability, and predict how the potential shifts when membrane permeability to potassium or sodium changes. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why the resting membrane potential sits near the potassium equilibrium potential, explain in words how the Goldman equation combines each ion's concentration gradient and permeability, and predict how the potential shifts when membrane permeability to potassium or sodium changes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Resting membrane potential. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-ion-channels","name":"Ion channel gating","can":"Compare leak, voltage gated, ligand gated, and mechanically gated channels by what opens each one and give a physiological location for each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction opens its pore when acetylcholine binds it. How is this channel classified?\nA) Voltage gated  |  B) Ligand gated\nC) Mechanically gated  |  D) Nongated leak\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Ligand gated","why":"The stimulus that opens the pore is a bound chemical messenger, so by definition the channel is ligand gated, even though the ions that flow through it then change the voltage. Classification follows the stimulus that opens the gate, not the consequence. A voltage gated channel would need a change in membrane potential first, a mechanically gated one would need deformation, and a leak channel would need nothing at all because it is already open."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron ending in the wall of the carotid sinus fires more rapidly when a rise in arterial pressure distends the vessel. Which channel type is doing the initial transduction?\nA) Voltage gated  |  B) Nongated leak\nC) Mechanically gated  |  D) Ligand gated\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Mechanically gated","why":"The stimulus here is physical stretch of the vessel wall pulling on the nerve ending, so the first channels to open are mechanically gated ones, and the resulting depolarization is what later recruits voltage gated channels to fire action potentials. Ligand gated channels would require a chemical messenger, and none is being applied. Voltage gated channels do participate, but downstream, after the mechanical event has already depolarized the ending. Leak channels do not respond to stimuli at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"A channel is closed at -70 mV and opens as the membrane potential moves to -50 mV. How is it classified?\nA) Ligand gated  |  B) Nongated leak\nC) Mechanically gated  |  D) Voltage gated\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Voltage gated","why":"The only thing that changed was the membrane potential, and the channel responded, which is the definition of voltage gating. A leak channel would already have been open at -70 mV. A mechanically gated channel would require deformation of the membrane, and a ligand gated channel would require a messenger to bind, neither of which was supplied in this description."},{"t":"mc","q":"A Pacinian corpuscle in the skin depolarizes when the overlying tissue is pressed. Which channel type opens first?\nA) Mechanically gated  |  B) Ligand gated\nC) Voltage gated  |  D) Nongated leak\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Mechanically gated","why":"Pressure deforms the lamellae and the nerve ending inside them, and that deformation pulls mechanically gated channels open, producing the receptor potential. Voltage gated channels come next, converting a large enough receptor potential into action potentials, but they are not the transducer. No chemical messenger is involved, so ligand gating does not apply, and leak channels have no stimulus to respond to."},{"t":"mc","q":"A channel in a cell membrane opens when intracellular Ca2+ binds to a site on its cytoplasmic side. How is it classified?\nA) Leak, because Ca2+ is always present in the cytosol\nB) Voltage gated, because Ca2+ carries charge\nC) Ligand gated, with an intracellular ligand\nD) Mechanically gated, because Ca2+ influx stretches the membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Ligand gated, with an intracellular ligand","why":"Ligand gating is defined by a chemical binding to the channel, and nothing in the definition requires the chemical to arrive from outside the cell. Calcium activated K+ channels work exactly this way and help repolarize cells after Ca2+ entry. The fact that Ca2+ is charged does not make the channel voltage gated, since the trigger is binding rather than a field change. Ca2+ influx does not stretch the membrane, and a channel that requires a trigger is not a leak channel."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A voltage gated Na+ channel has a single gate that opens when the membrane depolarizes and closes again when the membrane repolarizes.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A voltage gated Na+ channel has two gates: a fast activation gate that opens on depolarization and a slow inactivation gate that closes shortly afterward, and the channel cannot open again until the membrane repolarizes and both gates reset.","why":"The two gate design is what gives the action potential its shape and its refractory period. The activation gate opens within a fraction of a millisecond and lets Na+ rush in. The inactivation gate then swings shut about a millisecond later and stops the current even though the membrane is still depolarized. Because the inactivation gate only reopens after repolarization, the channel is unavailable for a while, which is the absolute refractory period. A single gate channel could not produce a refractory period on its own."},{"t":"draw","q":"What opens each type of channel, and where in the body does each one work? Draw four channels in a row in a strip of membrane: leak, voltage gated, ligand gated, and mechanically gated. Draw each one twice, closed and open. Beside each, draw whatever opens it: a voltage change, a bound molecule, a stretch or a bend. Under each, write one real place in the body where it is found. In one line: only one of the four is open at rest. Say what job that gives it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Where along one neuron does each channel type work, and in what order do they open? Draw one neuron stretched across the whole width of your box. Place each channel type where it actually works: ligand gated on the dendrites, voltage gated sodium and potassium along the axon, leak channels everywhere, voltage gated calcium at the terminal. Add a mechanically gated channel on a sensory ending drawn off to the side. In one line: number the order these open during one real signal and say which one starts it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare leak, voltage gated, ligand gated, and mechanically gated channels by what opens each one and give a physiological location for each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ion channel gating. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-potential-terms","name":"Depolarization and hyperpolarization","can":"Define depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and overshoot and label each on a membrane potential tracing.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A tracing rises from -70 mV to +30 mV and then falls back to -70 mV. What is the falling portion called?\nA) Repolarization  |  B) Hyperpolarization\nC) Depolarization  |  D) Overshoot\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Repolarization","why":"The membrane is traveling back toward its resting value after being displaced, which is the definition of repolarization, and it is driven mostly by K+ leaving through delayed rectifier channels. It would only count as hyperpolarization if the tracing continued past -70 mV into more negative territory. Depolarization is the rising portion. Overshoot names the segment above 0 mV, which occurs during the rise and the earliest part of the fall."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a tracing that rises from -70 mV to a peak of +30 mV, which part is the overshoot?\nA) The whole rise from -70 mV to +30 mV\nB) The segment where the membrane potential is above 0 mV\nC) The segment between -70 mV and threshold\nD) The peak value of +30 mV only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The segment where the membrane potential is above 0 mV","why":"Overshoot means the membrane potential has gone past zero and reversed polarity, so the interior is briefly positive. The whole rise from -70 mV is the depolarizing phase, of which the overshoot is only the upper part. The stretch between rest and threshold is the subthreshold portion of the depolarization. The single peak value is the maximum reached, not the whole region above zero."},{"t":"mc","q":"A wave of Na+ rushes into a neuron through open channels. What change in membrane potential results?\nA) No change, because Cl- follows Na+ and cancels it\nB) Hyperpolarization, because charge is moving across the membrane\nC) Depolarization, because positive charge is added to the interior\nD) Repolarization, because Na+ restores the resting value\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Depolarization, because positive charge is added to the interior","why":"Adding positive charge to a negative interior reduces the charge separation, so the membrane potential moves toward and possibly past zero. Hyperpolarization would require the interior to become more negative, which Na+ entry cannot do. Na+ entry displaces the membrane from rest rather than restoring it, so it is not repolarization. Cl- movement is a separate event that depends on its own driving force and does not automatically cancel a Na+ current."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron's membrane potential moves from -85 mV back to -70 mV. Is this depolarization or hyperpolarization?\nA) Depolarization, because the interior became less negative\nB) Neither, because the cell only returned to rest\nC) Hyperpolarization, because the cell has not reached threshold\nD) Hyperpolarization, because the value is still negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Depolarization, because the interior became less negative","why":"These terms describe the direction of change, not the absolute value, so any move toward zero is depolarization even when the potential stays negative the whole time. A value being negative does not make the change hyperpolarizing. Returning to rest still counts as a change with a direction, and here that direction is depolarizing. Threshold is a separate landmark and has no bearing on what to call the movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stimulus moves a neuron's membrane potential from -70 mV to -62 mV, and it decays back to -70 mV without any spike. How should this be classified?\nA) An after-hyperpolarization  |  B) A subthreshold graded depolarization\nC) An action potential of reduced size  |  D) A graded hyperpolarization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A subthreshold graded depolarization","why":"The membrane became less negative, so the change was depolarizing, and because it never reached threshold it stayed graded and simply decayed. Action potentials are all or none, so there is no such thing as a smaller one; either threshold is reached and a full spike fires or it is not. Hyperpolarization would move the value below -70 mV. An after-hyperpolarization only occurs following a spike, and no spike occurred here."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Hyperpolarization means the membrane potential moves closer to zero.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Hyperpolarization means the membrane potential becomes more negative than the resting value, moving away from zero and away from threshold.","why":"Moving toward zero from a negative resting potential is depolarization, and it brings the neuron closer to firing. Hyperpolarization does the opposite: it pushes the potential further from threshold, so the neuron is harder to fire. Inhibitory synapses work this way, opening K+ or Cl- channels to hyperpolarize the cell. The afterhyperpolarization at the end of an action potential is the same idea, caused by K+ channels that are slow to close."},{"t":"draw","q":"Where does each named phase sit on a membrane potential tracing? Draw a tracing with time on the x axis and mV on the y axis, starting at minus 70. Take it up past zero to about plus 30, then back down below minus 70 before it returns to rest. Draw zero mV as a dashed line. Bracket and label depolarization, overshoot, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and the return to rest. In one line: define overshoot using the dashed line, not using the peak."},{"t":"draw","q":"Which changes in membrane potential make a cell easier to fire, and which make it harder? Draw one set of axes with a dashed line at minus 70 mV and another at zero. Plot four traces starting from rest: a small depolarization that fades away, a hyperpolarization, a depolarization that crosses threshold, a repolarization from plus 30 back down. Label each with its correct term. In one line: say which traces make the cell easier to fire and which make it harder, and how you can tell from direction alone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and overshoot and label each on a membrane potential tracing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Depolarization and hyperpolarization. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w2-lab-membrane-potential","name":"Membrane potential simulation","can":"Manipulate extracellular potassium and sodium in a simulation, record the resulting change in resting membrane potential, and explain why changing potassium moves it far more than changing sodium.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Intracellular K+ is 150 mEq/L and extracellular K+ is 5 mEq/L. If the membrane were permeable only to K+, where would the membrane potential settle?\nA) At about -70 mV, the normal resting potential  |  B) At 0 mV\nC) At E_K, about -90 mV  |  D) At E_Na, about +60 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At E_K, about -90 mV","why":"With only one ion able to cross, the membrane potential settles at that ion's equilibrium potential, here E_K, about -90 mV. A real neuron rests near -70 mV instead because the membrane also leaks some Na+, which pulls the potential a little positive of E_K. 0 mV would require equal K+ on both sides, and E_Na matters only when the membrane is permeable to Na+."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the membrane potential simulation you change extracellular K+ and record the resting membrane potential. Which is the independent variable?\nA) The intracellular K+ concentration\nB) The number of open K+ channels\nC) The recorded resting membrane potential\nD) The extracellular K+ concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The extracellular K+ concentration","why":"The independent variable is the one you set, and here that is the extracellular K+ concentration you type into the simulation. The recorded membrane potential is what responds, so it is the dependent variable. Intracellular K+ is held fixed as a control variable in this protocol. The number of open channels is a property of the model rather than something the exercise asks you to manipulate."},{"t":"mc","q":"In that same protocol, which is the dependent variable?\nA) The recorded resting membrane potential\nB) The Nernst constant of 61 mV\nC) The extracellular K+ concentration\nD) The temperature of the simulated bath\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The recorded resting membrane potential","why":"The dependent variable is the measurement that changes in response to what you set, and that is the membrane potential the electrode reports. Extracellular K+ is what you manipulate, so it is independent. Bath temperature is held at 37 C as a control. The 61 mV constant is fixed by temperature and physical constants, so it is not a variable in this experiment at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the intracellular microelectrode in the simulation actually measure?\nA) The total electrical charge stored in the cytosol\nB) The potential inside the cell relative to the outside bath\nC) The concentration of K+ in the cytosol of the cell\nD) The rate at which ions cross the cell membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The potential inside the cell relative to the outside bath","why":"A membrane potential is always a difference between two points, so the recording electrode inside is compared against a reference electrode in the bath, and the bath is defined as zero. It does not report concentrations; those are what you set in the simulation. It does not report flux rate, which would require a current measurement instead. And it reports a potential difference across the membrane, not an absolute quantity of charge in the cytosol."},{"t":"mc","q":"In what units should you record the membrane potential values from this simulation?\nA) mmHg  |  B) mEq/L\nC) mV  |  D) mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) mV","why":"Membrane potential is a voltage, and physiological voltages are small enough that millivolts are the working unit. mEq/L is the unit for the ion concentrations you set, not for the response you record. mOsm/L measures osmolarity, a particle count. mmHg measures pressure and belongs to cardiovascular and respiratory recordings."},{"t":"bd","q":"In the membrane potential simulation you raise extracellular K+ from 5 to 20 mmol/L with intracellular K+ fixed at 150 mmol/L. Without notes, predict which way the K+ equilibrium potential moves, what you expect the simulated resting potential to do, and why raising extracellular Na+ by the same factor would barely change the resting potential.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"more K+ outside means a smaller gradient pushing K+ out of the cell; the K+ equilibrium potential becomes less negative; the resting potential depolarizes in the same direction as E_K; the resting potential follows K+ because the resting membrane is highly permeable to K+; changing extracellular Na+ shifts the Na+ equilibrium potential, but resting Na+ permeability is so low that the membrane barely responds; an ion's equilibrium potential only moves the membrane when the membrane is permeable to that ion","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"What happens to the resting potential as extracellular potassium rises? Before you touch the simulation, sketch the graph you predict: resting membrane potential on the y axis, extracellular potassium on the x axis. Show which way the line should go as potassium rises. Run the simulation at several potassium values. Plot the measured points on the same axes in a second color. In one line: say which way the resting potential moved as potassium rose, and why potassium has such a strong effect at rest."},{"t":"draw","q":"Why does changing potassium move the resting potential so much more than changing sodium? Draw two panels side by side that share the same y axis. Left panel: plot resting membrane potential against extracellular potassium from your runs. Right panel: plot resting membrane potential against extracellular sodium over a comparable range. In one line: one plot is nearly flat and one is not. Say what that tells you about how permeable the resting membrane is to each ion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Membrane potential simulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Neurons and Neuroglia","comps":[{"id":"w4-neuron-classes","name":"Neuron structural and functional classes","can":"Map the organization of the nervous system into the central nervous system, the sensory and efferent divisions of the peripheral nervous system, the somatic motor and autonomic branches, and the enteric nervous system, then classify a neuron as multipolar, bipolar, or pseudounipolar by structure and as sensory, motor, or interneuron by function, and match each class to a location in the nervous system.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A toxin destroys most interneurons in the spinal cord gray matter but spares afferent and efferent neurons. Which response is most impaired?\nA) Transduction at the sensory receptor in the skin\nB) Transmission at the neuromuscular junction\nC) The monosynaptic stretch reflex, such as the knee jerk\nD) Polysynaptic withdrawal reflexes from a painful stimulus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Polysynaptic withdrawal reflexes from a painful stimulus","why":"A withdrawal reflex routes the afferent signal through one or more interneurons before reaching motor neurons, so removing interneurons breaks the circuit. Receptor transduction is a property of the afferent ending and needs no interneuron. Neuromuscular transmission happens between a motor neuron and muscle, well outside the cord. The stretch reflex is the useful contrast: its afferent synapses directly on the motor neuron, so it survives when interneurons are lost."},{"t":"mc","q":"The central nervous system can produce activity with no measurable output to the efferent divisions. Which example stays entirely inside the CNS?\nA) Pulling a hand away from a hot stove\nB) Salivating at the smell of food\nC) Thinking through a problem while sitting still\nD) The heart speeding up during exercise\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Thinking through a problem while sitting still","why":"Thinking and dreaming are complex brain functions that can take place entirely within the CNS, with no signal sent out to muscles or glands. Pulling a hand from a stove uses sensory input and a somatic motor output to skeletal muscle. Salivating uses a smell signal coming in and an autonomic output to a gland going out. A faster heart rate is an autonomic output to cardiac muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A retinal neuron has one short process ending on photoreceptors and a single axon leaving the opposite side of the cell body. Which structural class is it?\nA) Multipolar  |  B) Bipolar\nC) Pseudounipolar  |  D) Anaxonic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Bipolar","why":"Two processes leaving opposite poles is the definition of bipolar, and the retina is one of the few places this class appears. Multipolar would require many dendrites converging on the soma. Pseudounipolar would show a single stalk splitting into two branches with the soma set off to the side. An anaxonic cell would have no identifiable axon at all, so the described axon rules it out."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron has its cell body in a dorsal root ganglion, a peripheral branch ending in a muscle spindle, and a central branch entering the spinal cord. Which structural class is it?\nA) Pseudounipolar  |  B) Multipolar\nC) Anaxonic  |  D) Bipolar\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Pseudounipolar","why":"One stalk that divides into a peripheral and a central branch, with the soma parked in a ganglion off to the side, is the pseudounipolar plan used by essentially all first-order somatic sensory neurons. Bipolar cells have their soma between the two processes rather than off to the side. Multipolar cells have many dendrites and are the integrating cells of the CNS. Anaxonic cells lack an axon and cannot project a meter to the cord."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The autonomic division of the nervous system controls skeletal muscles.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The somatic motor division controls skeletal muscles. The autonomic division controls cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, exocrine glands, some endocrine glands, and some adipose tissue.","why":"Both divisions carry commands out from the CNS, so they are easy to mix up. The difference is the target. Somatic motor neurons go to skeletal muscle, and autonomic neurons go to internal organs and glands, which is why the autonomic division is also called the visceral nervous system."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, draw the organization of the nervous system as a branching map. Start with the whole nervous system, split it into its two main parts, and keep splitting until you reach the enteric nervous system. Put an arrow on each peripheral branch showing which way information travels, and write one target under each efferent branch.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"central nervous system: the brain and the spinal cord, the integrating center; peripheral nervous system splits into a sensory, or afferent, division and an efferent division; sensory neurons carry information from receptors toward the CNS; efferent neurons carry commands from the CNS out to target cells; efferent splits into somatic motor neurons, which control skeletal muscle, and autonomic neurons; autonomic splits into sympathetic and parasympathetic branches, which control cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, exocrine glands, some endocrine glands, and some adipose tissue; enteric nervous system: a network in the wall of the digestive tract that can act as its own integrating center and is often controlled by the autonomic division","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How do the three neuron shapes differ, and where do you find each one? Draw a multipolar, a bipolar, and a pseudounipolar neuron side by side. Give each its dendrites, cell body, and axon in the right arrangement, with the cell body in the right position relative to the signal path. Under each, write one real location in the body where you would find it. In one line: one of the three has its cell body off to the side of the signal path. Say what advantage that gives a sensory neuron."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Map the organization of the nervous system into the central nervous system, the sensory and efferent divisions of the peripheral nervous system, the somatic motor and autonomic branches, and the enteric nervous system, then classify a neuron as multipolar, bipolar, or pseudounipolar by structure and as sensory, motor, or interneuron by function, and match each class to a location in the nervous system. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to map the organization of the nervous system into the central nervous system, the sensory and efferent divisions of the peripheral nervous system, the somatic motor and autonomic branches, and the enteric nervous system, then classify a neuron as multipolar, bipolar, or pseudounipolar by structure and as sensory, motor, or interneuron by function, and match each class to a location in the nervous system."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Neuron structural and functional classes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-neuron-regions","name":"Functional regions of a neuron","can":"Label the dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, trigger zone, axon, and axon terminal and state which signal type each region carries.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron rests at -70 mV and fires at -55 mV. One excitatory input starts as a 15 mV depolarization at a dendrite but has decayed to 5 mV by the time it reaches the trigger zone. How many such inputs must arrive together to fire the cell?\nA) Two  |  B) Five\nC) One, because the dendritic value is what counts  |  D) Three\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Three","why":"The cell needs 15 mV of depolarization at the trigger zone to move from -70 to -55, and each input delivers 5 mV there, so three simultaneous inputs are required. Two would give only 10 mV and leave the membrane at -60 mV, still below threshold. Using the 15 mV starting value ignores the decay that happens on the way, which is the whole point of the calculation. Five would be more than needed, since 15 mV is reached at three."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sensory stimulus is doubled in strength. What happens to the action potentials the neuron sends down its axon?\nA) They become twice as large but keep the same frequency\nB) They become both larger and more frequent\nC) They stay the same size but occur more frequently\nD) They stop, because the axon is overloaded by the input\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They stay the same size but occur more frequently","why":"The all-or-none rule fixes action potential amplitude, so intensity has to be coded some other way, and the nervous system uses firing frequency. Doubling the amplitude would violate the all-or-none property of the axon membrane. Since amplitude cannot change, the combined option fails for the same reason. Very strong input increases firing rate up to a limit set by the refractory period, but it does not silence the axon."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two excitatory synapses on different dendrites of the same neuron are active at the same instant, and together they bring the trigger zone to threshold. What is this called?\nA) Temporal summation  |  B) Saltatory conduction\nC) Accommodation  |  D) Spatial summation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Spatial summation","why":"Inputs arriving at the same moment from different places on the cell add together at the trigger zone, which is spatial summation. Temporal summation is the other route to threshold, where one input repeats quickly enough that each graded potential builds on the last. Saltatory conduction describes impulses jumping between nodes along a myelinated axon, a different process entirely. Accommodation is the loss of excitability during a slow depolarization."},{"t":"mc","q":"You want to record a signal whose amplitude changes when you change how much transmitter is applied. Which region should you place the electrode in?\nA) The middle of the axon\nB) A dendrite or the cell body\nC) The initial segment, just past the hillock\nD) A node of Ranvier along the axon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A dendrite or the cell body","why":"Only the graded signals of the dendrites and soma scale with input size, so that is where a dose-dependent amplitude shows up. The axon carries all-or-none impulses whose amplitude is fixed no matter how strong the input was. A node of Ranvier is simply a point on the axon and follows the same all-or-none rule. The initial segment is where the graded signal is converted into a fixed-size impulse, so recordings there already show the all-or-none output."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of electrical signal do the dendrites of a neuron carry?\nA) Graded potentials that vary in size with the input\nB) All-or-none action potentials of fixed amplitude\nC) End plate potentials generated by acetylcholine\nD) Pacemaker potentials that depolarize on a set schedule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Graded potentials that vary in size with the input","why":"Dendritic membrane responds to transmitter with graded potentials whose amplitude tracks how much transmitter bound and which fade as they spread toward the soma. Action potentials are all-or-none and begin only at the trigger zone, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are dense. End plate potentials are the graded response of skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction, not of a dendrite. Pacemaker potentials belong to cardiac nodal and some smooth muscle cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"What kind of signal does each region of a neuron carry, and where does it change? Draw one motor neuron big, across the whole width of your box. Label the dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, trigger zone, axon, myelin, nodes, and axon terminal. Above each region, write the signal type it carries (graded or all or none) and sketch the shape of that signal there. In one line: mark the spot where graded becomes all or none and say what has to be true at that spot for the change to happen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Label the dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, trigger zone, axon, and axon terminal and state which signal type each region carries. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, trigger zone, axon, and axon terminal and state which signal type each region carries."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Functional regions of a neuron. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-glia-functions","name":"Glial cell functions","can":"Match astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, ependymal cells, satellite cells, and Schwann cells to their physiological roles.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Bacteria reach the brain tissue of a patient. Which glial cell population responds first by multiplying and engulfing the invaders?\nA) Oligodendrocytes  |  B) Microglia\nC) Satellite cells  |  D) Ependymal cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Microglia","why":"Microglia are derived from the same lineage as macrophages, and they proliferate and phagocytose when they detect pathogens or debris in nervous tissue. Oligodendrocytes are highly specialized for making myelin and cannot phagocytose. Ependymal cells line fluid spaces and provide no defense. Satellite cells exist only in peripheral ganglia, so they cannot respond to an infection in the brain."},{"t":"mc","q":"A peripheral nerve is crushed but the connective tissue sheath stays intact. Which glial cell makes regrowth of the severed axons possible?\nA) The oligodendrocyte  |  B) The astrocyte\nC) The Schwann cell  |  D) The ependymal cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The Schwann cell","why":"Schwann cells survive the injury, clear debris with macrophages, and line up in columns that guide the regrowing axon back toward its target, which is why peripheral nerves can recover. Oligodendrocytes are central cells, and the molecules they leave behind actively inhibit regrowth. Astrocytes react to central injury by forming a dense scar that also blocks regeneration. Ependymal cells have no role in peripheral repair."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell forms the myelin sheath around axons in the brain and spinal cord?\nA) The oligodendrocyte  |  B) The astrocyte\nC) The Schwann cell  |  D) The microglial cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The oligodendrocyte","why":"Oligodendrocytes wrap central axons, sending out several arms so that one cell myelinates segments on many different axons. Astrocytes support synapses and the blood brain barrier but make no myelin. Schwann cells do make myelin, but only in the peripheral nervous system. Microglia are the immune cells of the CNS and remove debris rather than insulate axons."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell myelinates axons in peripheral nerves?\nA) The satellite cell  |  B) The oligodendrocyte\nC) The Schwann cell  |  D) The ependymal cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The Schwann cell","why":"Schwann cells wrap peripheral axons, one cell per internode, and the same cell type also ensheathes unmyelinated peripheral fibers. Satellite cells are the other peripheral glia, but they surround cell bodies in ganglia rather than axons. Oligodendrocytes do the equivalent job in the CNS only. Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and have no role in myelination."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell lines the ventricles of the brain and helps produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid?\nA) The oligodendrocyte  |  B) The astrocyte\nC) The microglial cell  |  D) The ependymal cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ependymal cell","why":"Ependymal cells form the epithelium lining the ventricles and central canal, and their cilia keep cerebrospinal fluid moving while specialized ependymal cells of the choroid plexus secrete it. Astrocytes touch capillaries and help regulate what enters the brain, but they do not secrete cerebrospinal fluid. Microglia are phagocytes. Oligodendrocytes are the myelinating cells of the CNS."},{"t":"draw","q":"Which glial cells work in the CNS, which work in the PNS, and what does each one do? Draw a CNS scene and a PNS scene side by side. In the CNS scene, draw: an astrocyte touching both a capillary and a neuron, an oligodendrocyte myelinating more than one axon, a microglial cell, ependymal cells lining a fluid space. In the PNS scene, draw a Schwann cell on one axon segment and satellite cells wrapped around a cell body. In one line: two of these six make myelin. Say the one structural difference in how they do it."},{"t":"draw","q":"What does each glial cell do for a neuron, and what goes wrong without one? Draw one neuron in the middle of your box with six arrows pointing at it, one from each glial type. On each arrow, write the single job that glial cell does for the neuron or its surroundings. Cross out one arrow of your choice and draw what goes wrong. In one line: name the glial cell you removed and the first symptom you would expect, not the last."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, ependymal cells, satellite cells, and Schwann cells to their physiological roles."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Glial cell functions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-myelin","name":"Myelin and its loss","can":"Explain how myelin and the nodes of Ranvier speed conduction and predict the functional consequence of demyelination.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A demyelinating disease affects the largest myelinated sensory fibers first. Which sensation is most likely to be lost earliest?\nA) Slow burning pain  |  B) Itch from the skin surface\nC) Warm temperature sensation  |  D) Vibration and position sense\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Vibration and position sense","why":"Vibration and proprioception travel on the largest, most heavily myelinated afferents, so they are the first modalities to fail when myelin is the target. Slow burning pain runs on unmyelinated C fibers, which have no myelin to lose and are therefore spared early. Warm temperature also travels on unmyelinated fibers. Itch is likewise carried by small unmyelinated afferents, so it persists while position sense fades."},{"t":"mc","q":"A signal must travel 1 m from the toe to the spinal cord. How long does it take on a fiber conducting at 50 m/s compared with one conducting at 1 m/s?\nA) About 50 ms versus about 100 ms  |  B) About 2 ms versus about 100 ms\nC) About 20 ms versus about 50 ms  |  D) About 20 ms versus about 1000 ms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 20 ms versus about 1000 ms","why":"Time equals distance divided by velocity, so 1 m at 50 m/s takes 1/50 of a second, which is 20 ms, and 1 m at 1 m/s takes a full second, or 1000 ms. That fiftyfold gap is why a sharp prick registers almost instantly while the aching pain behind it arrives about a second later. The values of 50 ms, 2 ms and 100 ms come from dividing in the wrong direction or from mixing seconds with milliseconds."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a node of Ranvier?\nA) A short gap in the myelin sheath where the axon membrane is exposed\nB) The junction between an axon terminal and its target cell\nC) The point where an axon branches into several collaterals\nD) The layer of myelin laid down by a single glial cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A short gap in the myelin sheath where the axon membrane is exposed","why":"Nodes are the bare patches between successive myelin segments, and they are where ions can actually cross the membrane. The junction with a target cell is a synapse, a different structure entirely. A branch point is a collateral and has nothing to do with myelin. The stretch of myelin made by one glial cell is the internode, which is the insulated region between two nodes."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the name for the way an action potential travels along a myelinated axon?\nA) Continuous conduction  |  B) Electrotonic spread\nC) Saltatory conduction  |  D) Antidromic conduction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Saltatory conduction","why":"Saltatory comes from the Latin for jumping, and it describes the impulse being regenerated only at the nodes so that it appears to leap from node to node. Continuous conduction is the slower pattern seen in unmyelinated axons, where every patch of membrane must fire in sequence. Electrotonic spread is the passive, decremental flow of current, which is what happens under the myelin between nodes. Antidromic conduction means travel in the direction opposite the normal one."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the myelin sheath do to the electrical properties of the axon membrane it covers?\nA) It lowers membrane resistance and raises membrane capacitance\nB) It raises membrane resistance and lowers membrane capacitance\nC) It raises both membrane resistance and membrane capacitance\nD) It leaves both unchanged and simply protects the axon from injury\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It raises membrane resistance and lowers membrane capacitance","why":"Stacking many lipid layers around the axon makes it much harder for ions to leak out, which is high resistance, and separates the charges farther apart, which is low capacitance. Both changes push more of the local current forward inside the axon to the next node. Lowering resistance would do the opposite and let current escape. Myelin does provide some mechanical protection, but the reason it speeds conduction is electrical."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how myelin and the nodes of Ranvier speed up conduction, and predict what happens to an action potential when it reaches a stretch of axon that has lost its myelin.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"myelin insulates the axon, reducing current leak across the membrane between nodes; voltage gated Na+ channels are concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier; current spreads quickly through the cytoplasm from one node to the next and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes (saltatory conduction); a demyelinated segment leaks current and has few Na+ channels, so the depolarization decays as it crosses it; the signal reaches the next node smaller and later, so conduction slows; if the depolarization falls below threshold at the next node, conduction fails (conduction block); the functional result is delayed or lost signals, such as weakness, numbness or blurred vision depending on the pathway","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How does myelin make a signal travel faster? Draw two axons of the same length side by side, one bare and one myelinated with nodes of Ranvier. On each, mark with dots every place the action potential has to be regenerated. Put a clock reading beside each showing which signal arrives first. In one line: myelin is an insulator and does nothing electrical itself. Say how adding insulation makes a signal faster."},{"t":"draw","q":"What happens to conduction when a stretch of axon loses its myelin? Draw a myelinated axon with three intact internodes. Draw the same axon again with the middle internode stripped bare. On both, draw the sodium channel distribution: dense at the nodes, sparse under the myelin. Show the action potential traveling along each and mark where it slows or fails. In one line: the demyelinated stretch has few sodium channels. Say why conduction fails there rather than simply slowing down."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how myelin and the nodes of Ranvier speed conduction and predict the functional consequence of demyelination."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Myelin and its loss. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-axonal-transport","name":"Axonal transport","can":"Distinguish fast anterograde and retrograde axonal transport from slow transport and state what each carries.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A virus is taken up at a nerve terminal in the skin and later appears in the neuron cell body in the spinal cord. Which transport process moved it?\nA) Fast anterograde transport, driven by kinesin\nB) Slow transport, driven by cytoskeletal assembly\nC) Simple diffusion along the length of the axon\nD) Fast retrograde transport, driven by dynein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fast retrograde transport, driven by dynein","why":"Movement from the terminal back to the soma is retrograde by definition, and dynein is the motor that walks in that direction, which is how viruses such as rabies and toxins such as tetanus reach the central nervous system. Anterograde transport would carry the virus the wrong way, out toward the terminal. Slow transport is anterograde only and far too slow. Diffusion over a distance of a meter would take years and is not a viable route."},{"t":"mc","q":"A severed peripheral axon regrows at roughly 1 mm per day. Which transport process sets that pace?\nA) Fast anterograde transport of synaptic vesicles\nB) Diffusion of ions into the growing tip\nC) Fast retrograde transport of growth factor signals\nD) Slow transport of cytoskeletal proteins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Slow transport of cytoskeletal proteins","why":"A regrowing axon has to build new cytoskeleton along its whole new length, and tubulin and neurofilaments arrive only by slow transport at about a millimeter per day, which matches the observed regeneration rate almost exactly. Fast anterograde transport runs hundreds of times faster and is not the bottleneck. Retrograde signals report back to the soma but do not supply building material. Ion movement is not what limits the construction of a new axon segment."},{"t":"mc","q":"A motor neuron axon is 1000 mm long and fast anterograde transport runs at 400 mm per day. About how long does a newly made vesicle take to reach the terminal?\nA) About 2.5 hours  |  B) About 25 days\nC) About 2.5 days  |  D) About 400 days\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 2.5 days","why":"Dividing 1000 mm by 400 mm per day gives 2.5 days, a reminder that even the fast system takes real time over the length of a limb. Getting hours instead of days means the rate was read as millimeters per hour. Twenty-five days would follow from a rate of 40 mm per day, ten times too slow. Four hundred days confuses the rate with the answer."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which motor protein carries cargo down the axon away from the cell body?\nA) Dynein  |  B) Kinesin\nC) Myosin  |  D) Tubulin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Kinesin","why":"Kinesin walks toward the plus end of a microtubule, which in an axon points away from the soma, so kinesin is the anterograde motor. Dynein walks the other way and handles retrograde traffic. Myosin is a motor too, but it moves along actin filaments and drives muscle contraction and short-range movements. Tubulin is not a motor at all; it is the subunit that polymerizes into the microtubule track."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which motor protein carries cargo back up the axon toward the cell body?\nA) Kinesin  |  B) Dynein\nC) Actin  |  D) Myosin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Dynein","why":"Dynein moves toward the minus end of a microtubule, which in an axon points back toward the soma, making it the retrograde motor. Kinesin is its anterograde counterpart. Actin is a filament protein, not a motor, and it forms the track that myosin uses. Myosin does not carry cargo the length of an axon, since actin filaments do not run continuously along it."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Fast anterograde axonal transport carries synaptic vesicles and mitochondria from the cell body toward the axon terminal along microtubules, using the motor protein kinesin.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Fast transport moves organelles and vesicles at up to about 400 mm per day. Kinesin walks toward the terminal (anterograde), and dynein walks back toward the cell body (retrograde), returning worn organelles and growth factor signals. Slow transport is a separate process that moves cytoskeletal proteins and enzymes at only millimeters per day, which is why a cut axon takes so long to regrow."},{"t":"draw","q":"What moves each way along the axon, and why does the retrograde direction exist? Draw a long neuron with the cell body on the left and the terminal on the right. Run a microtubule track down the axon. On the track, draw fast anterograde transport with kinesin and fast retrograde transport with dynein, arrows pointing in opposite directions. Write the cargo beside each. In one line: the cell body makes everything. Say what the retrograde direction is even for."},{"t":"draw","q":"How fast is fast transport compared with slow transport, and how can a virus use it? Draw the same axon and add slow transport as a third, much slower stream, with its cargo written beside it. Draw a scale bar showing the real speed difference: hundreds of mm per day for fast transport, a few mm per day for slow. Draw a virus entering at the terminal and mark the route it takes to the cell body. In one line: say which transport system the virus hijacked and why that is bad news for the neuron."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish fast anterograde and retrograde axonal transport from slow transport and state what each carries."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Axonal transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Electrical Signaling","comps":[{"id":"w4-graded-potentials","name":"Graded potentials","can":"Describe how a graded potential is produced and explain why it varies with stimulus strength and decays with distance, using the terms current leak and cytoplasmic resistance, and relate current, voltage, and resistance with Ohm's law, including how opening channels raises conductance.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A stimulus opens twice as many chemically gated channels as before on a dendrite. What is the result?\nA) A graded potential of the same amplitude that spreads faster\nB) A graded potential of larger amplitude\nC) A graded potential of the same amplitude repeated twice\nD) An action potential of larger amplitude at the trigger zone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A graded potential of larger amplitude","why":"More open channels means more ions crossing per unit time, more charge moved, and therefore a bigger voltage change, which is exactly what makes graded potentials graded. Passive spread speed is set by the cable properties of the cell, not by how many channels the stimulus opened. Nothing about a stronger stimulus splits the response into two events. The action potential option is the classic trap: a bigger graded potential can make an action potential more likely, but it never makes the action potential itself bigger, because that response is all or none."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two EPSPs arrive at the same synapse two milliseconds apart, and the second builds on the tail of the first. What is this called?\nA) Spatial summation  |  B) Temporal summation\nC) Divergence to two targets  |  D) Convergence of two pathways\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Temporal summation","why":"Two inputs at one location arriving close together in time overlap before the first decays, and their depolarizations add, which is temporal summation. Spatial summation is the same addition, but of inputs arriving at the same moment from different locations on the neuron. Convergence and divergence describe wiring patterns, how many neurons feed into one cell or how many cells one neuron contacts, not the adding of overlapping potentials."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two different presynaptic neurons fire at the same instant onto separate dendrites, and their EPSPs add at the trigger zone. What is this called?\nA) Temporal summation  |  B) Facilitation of a single synapse\nC) Spatial summation  |  D) Recruitment of additional axons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Spatial summation","why":"Inputs at different places on the same neuron arriving at the same moment sum where their spreading currents meet, and that is spatial summation. Temporal summation would require the inputs to be separated in time at one site rather than separated in space at one instant. Facilitation describes a synapse becoming more effective with use, which is a change in one synapse rather than the addition of two. Recruitment refers to bringing more axons into a population response, which happens across cells, not within one postsynaptic neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"An EPSP of 6 mV and an IPSP of 4 mV reach the trigger zone at the same moment. What is the net change in membrane potential?\nA) About 10 mV of depolarization\nB) No net change, since opposite inputs cancel completely\nC) About 2 mV of hyperpolarization\nD) About 2 mV of depolarization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 2 mV of depolarization","why":"Graded potentials sum algebraically, so a 6 mV depolarization plus a 4 mV hyperpolarization leaves roughly 2 mV of net depolarization. Adding the two magnitudes to get 10 mV would treat the inhibitory input as if it were excitatory. A net hyperpolarization would require the IPSP to be the larger of the two, which it is not. Complete cancellation happens only when the two are equal in size, so it is the right answer for a 4 mV EPSP paired with a 4 mV IPSP, not for this pair."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A graded potential that begins as a 20 mV depolarization at a dendrite arrives at the trigger zone still at 20 mV, as long as no other input interferes.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A graded potential loses amplitude as it spreads, because current leaks out through the membrane and the cytoplasm resists current flow, so it arrives at the trigger zone smaller than it started.","why":"Graded potentials are not regenerated. The charge that enters at the synapse spreads passively, and along the way some of it exits through open leak channels while the rest is slowed by the narrow cytoplasm. The farther the synapse from the trigger zone, the smaller the signal that arrives. This is why a synapse on the cell body has more influence than one on a distant dendrite, and why several inputs often must add together (summation) to reach threshold."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a graded potential started on the cell body gets smaller as it spreads toward the trigger zone. Use Ohm's law, and name both sources of resistance in the neuron.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"Ohm's law: current equals voltage divided by resistance, so more resistance means less current; positive charge that enters spreads through the cytoplasm as local current flow; current leak: open leak channels let some positive charge escape across the membrane; cytoplasmic resistance: the cytoplasm resists current flow inside the cell; the membrane is the other main source of resistance, and opening channels lowers it; together, current leak and cytoplasmic resistance make the signal weaker with distance; a larger diameter lowers internal resistance, so current spreads farther","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How does a graded potential change with stimulus strength, and what is its job? Draw one set of axes, membrane potential against time, with threshold marked as a dashed line. From the same spot, plot three graded potentials produced by a weak, a medium, and a strong stimulus. Then plot two of them arriving close together and summing. In one line: a graded potential changes size with stimulus strength and an action potential does not. Say what the graded potential's actual job is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe how a graded potential is produced and explain why it varies with stimulus strength and decays with distance, using the terms current leak and cytoplasmic resistance, and relate current, voltage, and resistance with Ohm's law, including how opening channels raises conductance. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe how a graded potential is produced and explain why it varies with stimulus strength and decays with distance, using the terms current leak and cytoplasmic resistance, and relate current, voltage, and resistance with Ohm's law, including how opening channels raises conductance."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Graded potentials. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-action-potential","name":"Action potential phases","can":"Diagram an action potential and state the channel state and ion movement responsible for depolarization to peak and for repolarization and after-hyperpolarization.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A recorded action potential has a normal upstroke and a normal peak, but repolarization takes three times as long as usual and there is no afterhyperpolarization. Which channel is affected?\nA) The voltage gated Na+ channel  |  B) The K+ leak channel\nC) The voltage gated K+ channel  |  D) The chemically gated Na+ channel\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The voltage gated K+ channel","why":"A normal upstroke says the Na+ channels are working, and slow repolarization with no afterhyperpolarization points straight at the outward current that normally ends the action potential, the voltage gated K+ channel. If Na+ channels were affected, the upstroke and peak would be the first things to change. Leak channels set the resting potential and would show up as a shifted baseline rather than a stretched repolarization. Chemically gated channels act at synapses and do not shape the axonal waveform at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"At a resting potential of -70 mV, what is the position of the two gates on a voltage gated Na+ channel?\nA) Activation gate open, inactivation gate open\nB) Activation gate open, inactivation gate closed\nC) Activation gate closed, inactivation gate closed\nD) Activation gate closed, inactivation gate open\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Activation gate closed, inactivation gate open","why":"At rest the channel is closed but ready: the activation gate blocks the pore, while the inactivation gate is standing open so the channel can conduct the instant depolarization swings the activation gate aside. Both gates open is the brief conducting state during the upstroke. Activation open with inactivation closed is the inactivated state that follows the peak and blocks further firing. Both gates closed is not a state the channel occupies, because the inactivation gate closes only after depolarization has opened the activation gate."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks most voltage gated K+ channels in an axon. What happens to the action potential?\nA) The upstroke is abolished and no action potential occurs\nB) The resting potential becomes more negative than usual\nC) The peak is lower but the timing is unchanged\nD) Repolarization is slowed and the action potential is prolonged\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Repolarization is slowed and the action potential is prolonged","why":"Repolarization depends on K+ leaving through those channels, so blocking them removes the main outward current and the membrane crawls back toward rest, stretching the action potential and blunting or erasing the afterhyperpolarization. Abolishing the upstroke would require blocking Na+ channels instead. The peak is set by Na+ entry and by how quickly it is opposed, so it is unchanged or slightly higher, not lower. Losing K+ permeability moves the resting potential in the depolarized direction if anything, not the hyperpolarized one."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the overshoot phase of the action potential mean about the membrane?\nA) The membrane potential has become more negative than the resting value\nB) The inside of the cell is briefly positive relative to the outside\nC) The Na+ concentration inside has briefly exceeded that outside\nD) The membrane potential has reached the K+ equilibrium potential\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The inside of the cell is briefly positive relative to the outside","why":"Overshoot names the part of the trace above 0 mV, where so much Na+ has entered that the inside of the cell carries a net positive charge relative to the outside, reversing the usual polarity. Becoming more negative than rest is afterhyperpolarization, which is at the other end of the trace. Concentrations are essentially unchanged, because a swing of 100 mV requires only a minuscule number of ions to cross. Reaching the K+ equilibrium potential would mean about -90 mV, which is as far from overshoot as the trace ever gets."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mutation makes voltage gated K+ channels close faster than normal after repolarization. What happens to the trace?\nA) The peak of the action potential is higher\nB) The afterhyperpolarization is reduced or absent\nC) The upstroke takes longer to reach threshold\nD) The resting potential settles closer to -90 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The afterhyperpolarization is reduced or absent","why":"Afterhyperpolarization exists only because K+ channels linger open past the point where the membrane has returned to rest, so closing them promptly removes the extra K+ efflux and the trace stops at rest instead of dipping below it. The peak is determined by Na+ entry before these channels matter much, so it is unaffected. The time to reach threshold depends on the stimulus and on the graded potential, not on how K+ channels close. A resting potential nearer -90 mV would require more K+ permeability at rest, not less."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Repolarization during an action potential is caused mainly by the Na+/K+ ATPase pumping the Na+ that entered back out of the cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Repolarization is caused by K+ leaving the cell through voltage gated K+ channels after the Na+ channels inactivate; the pump is far too slow to shape a millisecond event and only restores the gradients over time.","why":"Each action potential moves very few ions relative to the total present, so the gradients barely change during a single spike. What returns the potential to rest is a change in permeability: Na+ channels shut off and K+ channels open, so positive charge leaves the cell. The pump works in the background, over seconds to minutes, to keep the gradients from running down after many action potentials."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Diagram an action potential and state the channel state and ion movement responsible for depolarization to peak and for repolarization and after-hyperpolarization. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to diagram an action potential and state the channel state and ion movement responsible for depolarization to peak and for repolarization and after-hyperpolarization."},{"t":"draw","q":"Trace the cause and effect. Draw a flow chart for this topic: Action potential phases. Show each step in order, with an arrow for what causes what. Explain every arrow out loud."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Action potential phases. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-threshold","name":"Threshold and all or none","can":"Explain what threshold represents at the trigger zone and why action potential amplitude does not change with a stronger stimulus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A sensory axon is stimulated at three times threshold strength instead of once. What changes in its output?\nA) It fires larger action potentials at the same rate\nB) It fires action potentials with a longer duration each\nC) It fires action potentials that travel faster along the axon\nD) It fires more action potentials per second, each the same size\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It fires more action potentials per second, each the same size","why":"A stronger stimulus produces a larger graded potential at the trigger zone, which drives the membrane back to threshold sooner after each action potential, so the firing rate rises while every individual action potential stays the same size. Larger action potentials would violate the all or none principle. Conduction velocity is set by axon diameter and myelination, which a stronger stimulus does not change. Duration is determined by the kinetics of the Na+ and K+ channels and is likewise fixed for a given axon."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these changes will alter the amplitude of the action potentials recorded from a single axon?\nA) A doubling of stimulus intensity\nB) A longer duration of the applied stimulus\nC) An increase in the frequency of stimulation within the axon's limits\nD) A large drop in extracellular Na+ concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A large drop in extracellular Na+ concentration","why":"Amplitude depends on how far the membrane can move toward the Na+ equilibrium potential, so cutting extracellular Na+ lowers that target and shrinks the action potential. Stimulus intensity, stimulus duration, and firing frequency are all properties of the input, and the all or none principle means none of them scales the response as long as threshold is reached. Frequency can reduce amplitude if it is pushed into the relative refractory period, but within the axon's normal limits it does not."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron is hyperpolarized from -70 mV to -80 mV by an inhibitory input. What size depolarization is now needed to reach a threshold of -55 mV?\nA) 10 mV, less than before  |  B) 15 mV, the same as before\nC) 80 mV, the full distance from zero  |  D) 25 mV, up from the usual 15 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 25 mV, up from the usual 15 mV","why":"The membrane now sits 25 mV below threshold instead of 15 mV, so a larger depolarization is required and the same synaptic input that used to fire the cell may no longer do so. Fifteen millivolts would be the answer only if the resting potential were still -70 mV. Needing less depolarization would be true of a cell depolarized toward threshold, not hyperpolarized away from it. Measuring from zero misreads the scale; what matters is the distance from the current potential to threshold."},{"t":"mc","q":"A recorded response grows from 5 mV to 12 mV when the stimulus is doubled, and it never overshoots zero. What kind of response is it?\nA) An action potential recorded near its origin\nB) A graded potential\nC) An action potential with partial Na+ channel block\nD) An afterhyperpolarization following a subthreshold stimulus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A graded potential","why":"Scaling with stimulus strength is the defining behavior of a graded potential, and the absence of an overshoot confirms that no regenerative Na+ current ever took over. An action potential recorded anywhere along its path is the same size, so a response that doubles cannot be one. Partial Na+ channel block does lower the peak, but the remaining action potentials would still be all or none rather than proportional to the stimulus. Afterhyperpolarization follows an action potential and is a hyperpolarization, not the depolarizing response described here."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the typical threshold potential for a neuron?\nA) About -90 mV  |  B) About -55 mV\nC) About -70 mV  |  D) About -30 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About -55 mV","why":"Threshold sits near -55 mV, roughly 15 mV of depolarization above the usual resting potential, and reaching it commits the cell to an action potential. About -70 mV is the resting potential, the starting point rather than the tipping point. About -90 mV is the K+ equilibrium potential, approached during afterhyperpolarization. About -30 mV is well past threshold, on the rising phase of an action potential that has already been triggered."},{"t":"draw","q":"Why is every action potential the same size, no matter how strong the stimulus? Draw one set of axes. Along a bottom trace, draw four stimuli of increasing strength as bars. Above them, plot the membrane potential each one produces, and mark threshold. Show the first two failing to reach threshold and the last two producing identical full sized action potentials. In one line: the fourth stimulus was far stronger than the third and the spike came out the same size. Say what actually fixes the amplitude."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Explain what threshold represents at the trigger zone and why action potential amplitude does not change with a stronger stimulus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain what threshold represents at the trigger zone and why action potential amplitude does not change with a stronger stimulus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Threshold and all or none. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-intensity-coding","name":"Coding of stimulus intensity","can":"Explain how the nervous system encodes stimulus strength when action potential amplitude is fixed.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An axon has an absolute refractory period of 2 ms. What is its approximate maximum firing frequency?\nA) About 50 action potentials per second\nB) About 200 action potentials per second\nC) About 500 action potentials per second\nD) About 2000 action potentials per second\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 500 action potentials per second","why":"If no action potential can start until 2 ms after the last one, the shortest possible interval is 2 ms and the ceiling is one divided by 0.002 seconds, which is 500 per second. Fifty per second would correspond to a 20 ms refractory period. Two hundred per second corresponds to 5 ms. Two thousand per second would require a 0.5 ms refractory period, which is far shorter than any real axon achieves."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature of a sensory signal tells the brain what kind of stimulus occurred rather than how strong it was?\nA) How many action potentials arrive per second\nB) Which pathway the action potentials arrive on\nC) How tall the arriving action potentials are\nD) How fast the action potentials conducted\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Which pathway the action potentials arrive on","why":"Modality is carried by the labeled line: the brain interprets activity in the optic pathway as light and activity in a pain pathway as pain, regardless of what actually stimulated the receptor. Firing rate is the intensity code and says how much, not what kind. Action potential height is constant and carries no information at all. Conduction velocity varies between fiber types and affects timing, but the identity of the sensation comes from the destination of the pathway."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a single sensory axon, what feature of the action potential train carries information about stimulus strength?\nA) The number of action potentials per second\nB) The height of each action potential\nC) The conduction velocity of each action potential\nD) The duration of each action potential\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The number of action potentials per second","why":"Because every action potential in an axon is the same size, the only thing left to vary is how often they occur, so the nervous system reads stimulus strength from firing frequency. Height is fixed by the ion gradients and the all or none principle. Conduction velocity is a fixed property of the axon set by diameter and myelination, so it cannot vary with the stimulus. Duration is set by channel kinetics and stays essentially constant from one action potential to the next."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is frequency coding?\nA) Representing stimulus intensity by the rate of action potential firing\nB) Representing stimulus type by the rate of action potential firing\nC) Representing stimulus intensity by the size of each action potential\nD) Representing stimulus location by the speed of conduction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Representing stimulus intensity by the rate of action potential firing","why":"Frequency coding is the strategy of letting the interval between action potentials carry the intensity information, since the action potentials themselves are all identical. Stimulus type, or modality, is carried instead by which pathway the signal travels, the labeled line principle. Coding intensity by action potential size is exactly what the all or none principle rules out. Conduction velocity differs between fiber types but is a fixed axon property and does not encode location."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A pressure receptor in the skin is tested with a recording electrode on its sensory axon. A light touch produces 10 action potentials per second. A firm press produces 80 per second. Every action potential is 100 mV in amplitude in both trials. The axon's absolute refractory period is 1 ms.\nPredict what happens to the signal from this axon and from the skin as a whole when the pressure is increased far beyond a firm press.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Firing rate rises toward a ceiling of about 1000 per second set by the refractory period; beyond that, extra intensity is signaled by recruiting more sensory axons, not by this one","why":"Amplitude is all or none, so it stays at 100 mV. Intensity is coded as frequency, and a larger receptor potential drives the trigger zone back to threshold sooner after each spike. But the axon cannot fire again until its Na+ channels recover, so 1 divided by 0.001 s, about 1000 per second, is the most this axon can do. Once it saturates, the only remaining way to signal more pressure is for the stimulus to spread and activate additional receptors, which is population coding."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A stronger stimulus produces larger action potentials in a sensory neuron, and the brain reads the amplitude to judge stimulus strength.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Action potential amplitude is fixed (all or none), so a stronger stimulus is coded by a higher frequency of action potentials in each neuron and by recruiting more neurons.","why":"Every action potential in a given axon has the same size because it uses the same channels and the same gradients. What a stronger stimulus does change is the size of the graded potential at the receptor. A larger graded potential keeps the trigger zone above threshold longer and drives it back to threshold sooner after each refractory period, so the neuron fires more often. Stronger stimuli also spread to more receptors, so more axons fire. Frequency and population, not amplitude, carry intensity."},{"t":"draw","q":"If every spike is the same size, how does one neuron signal a stronger stimulus? Draw three stimulus bars of increasing strength across the bottom of your box: weak, medium, and strong. Above each, draw the spike train one sensory neuron sends, as vertical marks on a time line. Keep every spike exactly the same height. In one line: say precisely what changed between your three trains, and what could not have changed."},{"t":"draw","q":"What are the two ways the nervous system signals a stronger stimulus? Draw a patch of skin with a stimulus pressed into it, gentle on the left and hard on the right. In each, draw how many receptors and how many axons get recruited. Underneath, draw the spike train on one of those axons. In one line: name both ways from your drawing and say which one a single axon can do on its own."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the nervous system encodes stimulus strength when action potential amplitude is fixed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Coding of stimulus intensity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-refractory","name":"Refractory periods","can":"Distinguish the absolute from the relative refractory period by channel state and explain how the refractory period sets maximum firing frequency and prevents backward conduction.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron can follow a 50 Hz drive faithfully but starts dropping action potentials at 200 Hz. What does this indicate about its refractory period?\nA) It is shorter than 5 ms\nB) It is longer than 5 ms but shorter than 20 ms\nC) It is longer than 20 ms\nD) It cannot be estimated from firing rates\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is longer than 5 ms but shorter than 20 ms","why":"Following 50 Hz means the neuron can fire at 20 ms intervals, so the refractory period is under 20 ms, and failing at 200 Hz means it cannot fire at 5 ms intervals, so the refractory period is longer than 5 ms. A period under 5 ms would have allowed the neuron to follow 200 Hz. A period over 20 ms would have caused failure at 50 Hz as well. The refractory period is precisely what the maximum following frequency measures, so it can certainly be estimated this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"An axon cannot fire again at normal stimulus strength until about 4 ms after the previous action potential. What is its maximum firing frequency?\nA) About 250 action potentials per second\nB) About 40 action potentials per second\nC) About 4 action potentials per second\nD) About 400 action potentials per second\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 250 action potentials per second","why":"The shortest usable interval is 4 ms, so the ceiling is 1 divided by 0.004 seconds, which is 250 per second. Reading 4 ms as 4 per second confuses an interval with a rate. Forty per second would correspond to a 25 ms interval, far longer than any refractory period. Four hundred per second would require a 2.5 ms interval, which is shorter than the 4 ms the question specifies."},{"t":"mc","q":"An experimenter stimulates an axon in the middle of its length and records from both ends. What is observed?\nA) Action potentials travel only toward the axon terminal\nB) Action potentials travel in both directions from the stimulus site\nC) No action potential occurs, because the trigger zone was bypassed\nD) Action potentials travel only toward the cell body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Action potentials travel in both directions from the stimulus site","why":"Nothing about the axon membrane is inherently one way; the direction of normal traffic is set by where the action potential starts, and a stimulus in the middle finds unrefractory membrane on both sides, so two action potentials propagate away from each other. Believing the axon can only conduct toward the terminal confuses a normal starting point with a structural rule. The trigger zone is where action potentials normally begin, but any patch of axon membrane with voltage gated Na+ channels can be excited directly. Conduction toward the cell body alone has no basis either."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A sensory axon has an absolute refractory period of 2 ms followed by a relative refractory period lasting a further 3 ms. In the lab, the axon is stimulated with a strong pulse and then a second pulse of the same strength is delivered at different intervals.\nWhat is the highest firing rate this axon can sustain, and what will a second pulse delivered 3 ms after the first do?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 500 per second; a pulse at 3 ms lands in the relative refractory period and produces a smaller action potential only if it is strong enough","why":"The absolute refractory period sets the ceiling. One action potential every 2 ms is 1 divided by 0.002, or 500 per second. At 3 ms the absolute period is over, but the axon is 1 ms into its relative period: only some Na+ channels have recovered and K+ channels are still open, so it takes a stronger than usual stimulus to reach threshold and the resulting action potential is smaller because less Na+ current is available."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During the absolute refractory period, no stimulus of any strength can trigger a second action potential, because the voltage gated Na+ channels are inactivated.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The absolute refractory period lasts from the start of the action potential until enough Na+ channels have recovered from inactivation, about 1 to 2 ms. During the relative refractory period that follows, some Na+ channels have reset but the K+ channels are still open, so a stronger than normal stimulus can fire a smaller action potential. The absolute period sets the maximum firing rate and, because the membrane just behind a traveling spike is refractory, it keeps the action potential from turning around."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, describe the absolute and relative refractory periods in terms of channel states. Then explain how the refractory period sets the maximum firing frequency of an axon and why it keeps an action potential from traveling backward.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"absolute refractory period: Na+ channels are inactivated, so no stimulus of any strength can start a second action potential; relative refractory period: some Na+ channels have recovered but K+ channels are still open, so a stronger than normal stimulus is needed and the action potential is smaller; maximum firing frequency is about 1 divided by the absolute refractory period (about 1 ms gives roughly 1000 per second); the membrane just behind the traveling action potential is refractory; current spreading backward cannot re-excite that refractory membrane, so the action potential moves only forward from the trigger zone","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How does the refractory period keep the signal moving one way and cap how fast a neuron can fire? Draw an axon with an action potential halfway along it and an arrow for the direction it is traveling. Shade the membrane behind it and mark what state its sodium channels are in. Draw the local current spreading both forward and backward, and show why only one direction succeeds. In one line: an absolute refractory period of about 1 to 2 ms sets a ceiling on firing rate. Work out that ceiling in spikes per second."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the absolute from the relative refractory period by channel state and explain how the refractory period sets maximum firing frequency and prevents backward conduction. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the absolute from the relative refractory period by channel state and explain how the refractory period sets maximum firing frequency and prevents backward conduction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Refractory periods. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-conduction-velocity","name":"Conduction velocity","can":"Rank axons by conduction velocity using diameter and myelination and contrast continuous with saltatory conduction.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An action potential travels 15 cm along a nerve in 3 ms. What is the conduction velocity?\nA) 5 m/s  |  B) 45 m/s\nC) 50 m/s  |  D) 500 m/s\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 50 m/s","why":"Convert first: 15 cm is 0.15 m and 3 ms is 0.003 s, and 0.15 divided by 0.003 gives 50 m/s, a value typical of a medium sized myelinated fiber. Five meters per second would come from dividing 15 by 3 and then mishandling the unit conversion by a factor of ten. Forty five is what you get by subtracting rather than dividing. Five hundred is off by a factor of ten in the other direction and exceeds any biological conduction velocity."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a myelinated axon, why does the spacing of the nodes of Ranvier matter?\nA) Local current must still be above threshold when it reaches the next node\nB) The Na+/K+ ATPase can only work at evenly spaced intervals\nC) Widely spaced nodes would raise the amplitude of the action potential\nD) Nodes must be spaced closely enough for myelin to remain attached\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Local current must still be above threshold when it reaches the next node","why":"Current spreading under the internode decays as it goes, so nodes have to be close enough that the depolarization arriving at the next one is still large enough to open its Na+ channels, and spacing them too far apart causes conduction to fail. The pump is distributed along the membrane and does not require particular spacing. Node spacing does not change action potential amplitude, which is all or none. Myelin attachment is a matter of the supporting cell wrapping the axon, not of a threshold current requirement."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a myelinated axon, where is the action potential regenerated?\nA) Beneath the thickest part of the myelin sheath\nB) At the nodes of Ranvier\nC) At the axon terminal only\nD) Uniformly along the entire internode\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) At the nodes of Ranvier","why":"Nodes of Ranvier are short gaps in the myelin packed with voltage gated Na+ channels, and they are the only stretches of a myelinated axon where the membrane can produce a fresh action potential. Under the myelin there are almost no voltage gated channels, so nothing can be regenerated there. The axon terminal converts the arriving action potential into transmitter release rather than serving as the site of propagation. Regeneration along the whole internode describes continuous conduction in an unmyelinated axon."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which fiber type conducts action potentials most rapidly?\nA) Small diameter unmyelinated fibers  |  B) Large diameter myelinated fibers\nC) Large diameter unmyelinated fibers  |  D) Small diameter myelinated fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Large diameter myelinated fibers","why":"Speed rises with diameter and with myelination, so the fastest axons in the body combine both, reaching roughly 100 m/s in the largest myelinated fibers that serve proprioception and skeletal muscle. Small unmyelinated fibers sit at the other extreme, near 1 m/s, and carry slow pain and autonomic signals. A small myelinated fiber or a large unmyelinated one has one advantage but not the other, so each falls in between."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does myelin do to current leak across the internodal membrane?\nA) It has no effect on leak but lowers cytoplasmic resistance\nB) It increases leak, which speeds repolarization at each node\nC) It reduces leak, so more current reaches the next node\nD) It reduces leak only in unmyelinated regions of the axon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It reduces leak, so more current reaches the next node","why":"Wrapping the axon in many layers of lipid membrane drastically raises the resistance of the path out of the cell, so the current spreading forward inside the axon stays inside and arrives at the next node with enough strength to reach threshold. Increased leak would be a handicap, draining the very current that has to reach the next node. Myelin acts on the membrane, not on the cytoplasm, so cytoplasmic resistance is unchanged. Describing unmyelinated regions as myelinated is a contradiction."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A person steps on a tack. The foot is 1.2 m from the spinal cord. Three kinds of sensory axons carry the news: large myelinated A beta touch fibers at 60 m/s, small myelinated A delta sharp pain fibers at 15 m/s, and unmyelinated C fibers for burning pain at 1 m/s.\nPredict the order and timing of arrival of these three signals at the spinal cord.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Touch at about 20 ms, sharp pain at about 80 ms, burning pain at about 1200 ms; large myelinated fibers first, unmyelinated last","why":"Time equals distance divided by velocity. For 1.2 m: 1.2 over 60 is 0.02 s (20 ms), 1.2 over 15 is 0.08 s (80 ms), and 1.2 over 1 is 1.2 s (1200 ms). This matches the everyday experience of feeling the contact, then a sharp jab, then a slow burning ache well over a second later. Diameter and myelin set the order: the biggest myelinated fibers use saltatory conduction with low internal resistance, and the thin unmyelinated fibers must regenerate the signal at every point."},{"t":"draw","q":"Which axons conduct fastest, and which buys more speed: diameter or myelin? Draw four axons stacked vertically: large myelinated, small myelinated, large unmyelinated, and small unmyelinated. Rank them fastest to slowest. Write a rough velocity beside each, using the real range of about 0.5 up to about 120 m per second. In one line: say which buys more speed for the space it takes up, diameter or myelin, and why that matters for a nerve running down a limb."},{"t":"draw","q":"How is saltatory conduction different from continuous conduction? Draw one myelinated and one unmyelinated axon of similar diameter and the same length, side by side. On each, shade where the membrane actually depolarizes: continuously along one, node to node on the other. Draw the local current loops on both. In one line: saltatory means jumping, but the action potential does not really jump. Say what is actually moving between the nodes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to rank axons by conduction velocity using diameter and myelination and contrast continuous with saltatory conduction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Conduction velocity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-ion-disturbance","name":"Effects of altered extracellular ions","can":"Predict the effect of hyperkalemia and hypokalemia on resting potential and excitability and explain why local anesthetics that block sodium channels abolish the action potential.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An axon in an experimental bath has a resting potential of -60 mV instead of -70 mV, and its action potentials have a lower peak than normal. Which change to the bath explains both findings?\nA) Extracellular Na+ has been raised  |  B) Extracellular K+ has been raised\nC) Extracellular Cl- has been lowered  |  D) Extracellular K+ has been lowered\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Extracellular K+ has been raised","why":"Raising extracellular K+ makes the K+ equilibrium potential less negative and depolarizes the resting membrane, and that sustained depolarization inactivates a fraction of the Na+ channels so fewer are available for the upstroke, which lowers the peak, so one change accounts for both observations. Raising extracellular Na+ would raise the peak rather than lower it and would barely touch the resting potential. Lowering Cl- has little effect in an axon where Cl- carries small resting current. Lowering K+ would hyperpolarize the cell, moving the resting potential the wrong way."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell is bathed in a solution with extracellular K+ lowered from 4 to 2 mEq/L. Which way does the K+ equilibrium potential move?\nA) It becomes more negative\nB) It becomes less negative\nC) It becomes positive\nD) It does not change, since intracellular K+ is unchanged\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It becomes more negative","why":"The Nernst equation depends on the ratio of outside to inside concentration, and halving the outside value makes that ratio smaller, driving the equilibrium potential further below zero, toward roughly -108 mV. Becoming less negative is the direction taken when extracellular K+ rises. A positive value would require extracellular K+ to exceed intracellular K+, which is far from the case. Holding intracellular K+ constant does not protect the equilibrium potential, because the ratio is what matters."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which single measurement best distinguishes a cell in hyperkalemia from a cell in hypokalemia?\nA) The peak of the action potential\nB) The resting membrane potential\nC) The conduction velocity of the axon\nD) The duration of the absolute refractory period\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The resting membrane potential","why":"The two conditions push the resting potential in opposite directions, depolarized in hyperkalemia and hyperpolarized in hypokalemia, so the baseline voltage separates them immediately. The action potential peak can fall in hyperkalemia through Na+ channel inactivation, but it does not move in a clean opposite direction in hypokalemia. Conduction velocity and refractory duration can both be affected indirectly, but neither gives the clear, opposite signed answer that the resting potential does."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the normal range for plasma K+ concentration?\nA) About 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L  |  B) About 8 to 12 mEq/L\nC) About 135 to 145 mEq/L  |  D) About 95 to 105 mEq/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L","why":"Plasma K+ is held in a narrow band near 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L, and even small departures shift the resting potential enough to change excitability in nerve and cardiac muscle. The range of 135 to 145 mEq/L belongs to plasma Na+, and 95 to 105 mEq/L belongs to Cl-. Eight to twelve is close to the intracellular Cl- value and is not a plasma potassium range."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with kidney failure arrives with muscle weakness. Plasma K+ is 7.2 mmol/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0). The team gives insulin with glucose, which drives K+ from the plasma into cells. Two hours later plasma K+ is 5.0 mmol/L. Intracellular K+ was about 150 mmol/L throughout and changed very little.\nPredict what the treatment did to the resting potential and the voltage gated Na+ channels of the patient's muscle cells.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The resting potential moved back toward -70 mV (more negative), Na+ channels recovered from inactivation, and the cells can fire normally again","why":"At 7.2 mmol/L the outside to inside ratio was 7.2 over 150, giving a K+ equilibrium potential of about -80 mV instead of the usual -90 mV, and the resting potential had depolarized with it. Holding the membrane depolarized had inactivated a fraction of the Na+ channels, which is why the muscles were weak. Lowering plasma K+ to 5.0 mmol/L steepens the gradient again, the resting potential returns toward its normal value, and once the membrane repolarizes the inactivation gates reset. Excitability returns."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient with kidney failure has a plasma K+ of 7.5 mmol/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0). Without notes, explain step by step what this does to the resting potential and excitability of neurons and muscle cells, both at first and if the high K+ persists.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the outside to inside K+ ratio rises, so the K+ equilibrium potential becomes less negative; the resting membrane potential depolarizes toward threshold; at first the cell needs less depolarization to fire, so excitability rises (tingling, cramps); a sustained depolarization drives voltage gated Na+ channels into the inactivated state; inactivated Na+ channels cannot reopen until the membrane repolarizes, so the cell can no longer fire; the lasting result is reduced excitability: muscle weakness and, in the heart, conduction failure or arrhythmia","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How do high and low blood potassium change the resting potential and excitability? Draw a voltage scale from minus 100 to plus 30 mV. Mark the resting potential at minus 70 and threshold at about minus 55. Using the Nernst logic for potassium, draw where the resting potential moves in hyperkalemia and in hypokalemia. Show the remaining gap to threshold in each. In one line: hyperkalemia first makes the cell more excitable and then less. Say what happens to the sodium channels in between."},{"t":"draw","q":"Why does a local anesthetic abolish the action potential when low extracellular sodium only shrinks it? Draw a normal action potential tracing. Draw the same axon after a local anesthetic such as lidocaine has blocked its voltage gated sodium channels, and show what is left of the trace. Beside it, draw the axon in low extracellular sodium and show what changes about the peak, not about the resting level. In one line: say why blocking the channel abolishes the signal while low extracellular sodium only shrinks it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the effect of hyperkalemia and hypokalemia on resting potential and excitability and explain why local anesthetics that block sodium channels abolish the action potential."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Effects of altered extracellular ions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-lab-ap-simulation","name":"Action potential simulation","can":"Run a neuron simulation with sodium and potassium channel blockers and interpret the resulting change in the action potential tracing.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A simulated action potential has a normal peak and normal repolarization but no afterhyperpolarization at all. Which manipulation best explains this?\nA) Partial block of the voltage gated Na+ channels\nB) An increase in extracellular Na+\nC) Partial block of the voltage gated K+ channels\nD) A decrease in extracellular Na+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Partial block of the voltage gated K+ channels","why":"Afterhyperpolarization exists because voltage gated K+ channels remain open past the return to rest, so reducing their number removes the extra K+ efflux and the trace stops at the resting potential, and a partial block can do this while leaving repolarization close to normal. Na+ channel block would show up in the upstroke and the peak. Changing extracellular Na+ shifts the peak by changing the Na+ equilibrium potential and does not selectively remove the tail of the trace."},{"t":"mc","q":"A simulated response grows steadily as the stimulus is increased and never rises above 0 mV. What is being recorded?\nA) A graded potential rather than an action potential\nB) An action potential with partial Na+ channel block\nC) An action potential recorded far from the trigger zone\nD) An afterhyperpolarization following a subthreshold stimulus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A graded potential rather than an action potential","why":"Growing in proportion to the stimulus is the definition of a graded response, and the absence of any overshoot confirms that the regenerative Na+ current never took over. A partially blocked action potential has a lower peak but is still all or none, so it does not scale smoothly with the stimulus. Action potentials are the same size wherever along the axon they are recorded, so distance does not explain a graded response. Afterhyperpolarization is a hyperpolarization and requires a preceding action potential."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a neuron simulation, tetrodotoxin (TTX) is applied. Which channel does it block?\nA) K+ leak channels  |  B) Voltage gated K+ channels\nC) Voltage gated Na+ channels  |  D) The Na+/K+ ATPase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Voltage gated Na+ channels","why":"TTX plugs the pore of the voltage gated Na+ channel, which is why it is the standard tool for removing the depolarizing upstroke in a simulation. Voltage gated K+ channels are blocked by tetraethylammonium instead. Leak channels are not the target of either classic blocker. The Na+/K+ ATPase is inhibited by ouabain, a different agent working on a pump rather than a channel."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a neuron simulation, tetraethylammonium (TEA) is applied. Which channel does it block?\nA) Voltage gated Na+ channels  |  B) Chemically gated Na+ channels\nC) Voltage gated K+ channels  |  D) Voltage gated Ca2+ channels\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Voltage gated K+ channels","why":"TEA blocks the delayed rectifier voltage gated K+ channel, so in a simulation it is used to slow repolarization and remove the afterhyperpolarization. Voltage gated Na+ channels are the target of tetrodotoxin. Chemically gated channels respond to transmitter and are not part of the axonal action potential machinery being tested. Voltage gated Ca2+ channels matter at the axon terminal but do not shape the axonal action potential in these simulations."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an action potential simulation, what does the stimulus setting control?\nA) The number of Na+ channels present in the membrane\nB) The size of the depolarization delivered to the membrane\nC) The resting potential the membrane returns to\nD) The conduction velocity of the simulated axon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The size of the depolarization delivered to the membrane","why":"The stimulus control sets how large a depolarizing current is injected, which determines whether the membrane reaches threshold and, with repeated stimuli, how often it fires. Channel numbers are properties of the simulated membrane, adjusted separately or by applying a blocker. The resting potential is set by the ion concentrations and leak permeabilities in the model. Conduction velocity depends on the modeled diameter and myelination, not on how hard the membrane is pushed."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In the neuron simulation, a control action potential peaks at +35 mV, returns to -70 mV within 2 ms, and shows an afterhyperpolarization to -78 mV. After a drug is added, the action potential peaks at +33 mV, takes 9 ms to return to -70 mV, and shows no afterhyperpolarization.\nWhich channel does the drug block, and what happens to the maximum firing frequency of this neuron?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Voltage gated K+ channels; maximum firing frequency falls because the prolonged action potential lengthens the refractory period","why":"A normal upstroke and peak say the Na+ channels are fine. Slow repolarization with no afterhyperpolarization is the signature of a missing voltage gated K+ current; the membrane repolarizes only through Na+ inactivation and leak channels, which is slow. Na+ channels recover from inactivation only after the membrane repolarizes, so a 9 ms action potential keeps the neuron refractory far longer than a 2 ms one. The next action potential cannot start until then, so the firing ceiling drops."},{"t":"bd","q":"In the neuron simulation you apply tetrodotoxin (TTX) in one run and tetraethylammonium (TEA) in another. Without notes, predict how each drug changes the action potential tracing and explain each change in terms of the channel it blocks.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"TTX blocks voltage gated Na+ channels; with TTX there is no upstroke and no action potential (a partial block gives a higher threshold and a smaller peak); TEA blocks voltage gated K+ channels; with TEA the upstroke and peak are normal because Na+ channels are untouched; with TEA repolarization is slow and prolonged and the afterhyperpolarization disappears; the membrane still repolarizes eventually with TEA because Na+ channels inactivate and K+ leak channels remain open","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"What happens to the action potential when you block sodium channels, and when you block potassium channels? Before you run it, draw three predicted tracings on one set of axes: a normal action potential, sodium channels blocked, potassium channels blocked. Run the simulation. Draw the actual tracings underneath in a second color. In one line: name the phase that changed most under the potassium blocker, and say what that tells you about which channel ends the spike."},{"t":"draw","q":"What does a partial sodium channel block do to the action potential? Draw one set of axes and plot your normal action potential. Overlay the tracing you got with a partial sodium blocker rather than a full one. Mark exactly where the two curves separate and where they rejoin. Beside the plot, sketch the sodium and potassium channel populations at the moment the curves separate. In one line: say what the partial block did to threshold in your run, and whether the spikes that still happened came out smaller."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Action potential simulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w4-lab-nerve-conduction","name":"Nerve conduction measurement","can":"Measure conduction velocity from a recorded compound action potential and account for the difference between the fastest and slowest fibers.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A compound action potential recorded 12 cm from the stimulus begins at 2 ms and its last component arrives at 6 ms. What are the velocities of the fastest and slowest contributing fibers?\nA) About 6 m/s and about 2 m/s  |  B) About 60 m/s and about 20 m/s\nC) About 20 m/s and about 60 m/s  |  D) About 24 m/s and about 72 m/s\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 60 m/s and about 20 m/s","why":"Each velocity is 0.12 m divided by the relevant time, so 0.12 divided by 0.002 gives 60 m/s for the earliest arrivals and 0.12 divided by 0.006 gives 20 m/s for the last, a range consistent with a mix of large and medium myelinated fibers. The values near 6 and 2 m/s drop a factor of ten in the length conversion. Assigning 20 m/s to the fastest fibers reverses the relationship, since arriving earlier means moving faster. The pair near 24 and 72 m/s comes from multiplying instead of dividing."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two recording sites are 12 cm apart and the response onset differs by 2 ms between them. What is the conduction velocity of the fastest fibers?\nA) 6 m/s  |  B) 24 m/s\nC) 600 m/s  |  D) 60 m/s\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 60 m/s","why":"Convert to base units first: 12 cm is 0.12 m and 2 ms is 0.002 s, and 0.12 divided by 0.002 is 60 m/s, a normal value for a large myelinated nerve. Six meters per second comes from dropping a factor of ten in the length conversion. Twenty four comes from multiplying rather than dividing. Six hundred exceeds any biological conduction velocity by roughly a factor of six."},{"t":"mc","q":"The stimulus to a nerve is increased and the compound action potential grows larger. What does this show?\nA) Each axon is producing a larger action potential\nB) The axons are conducting more rapidly\nC) More axons are being recruited into the response\nD) The refractory period has shortened\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) More axons are being recruited into the response","why":"Individual axons are all or none, so the only way the summed wave can grow is for additional axons, with higher thresholds, to start contributing. Larger individual action potentials are ruled out by the all or none principle. Conduction velocity is a fixed property of each axon and does not rise with stimulus strength. Refractory period is a membrane property and is not affected by how hard the nerve is stimulated."},{"t":"mc","q":"A nerve conducts at 50 m/s. How long does a signal take to travel 0.5 m?\nA) 1 ms  |  B) 10 ms\nC) 25 ms  |  D) 100 ms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 10 ms","why":"Time is distance divided by velocity, so 0.5 m divided by 50 m/s gives 0.01 s, which is 10 ms. One millisecond would require a velocity of 500 m/s. Twenty five milliseconds comes from multiplying 0.5 by 50 and misplacing the units. One hundred milliseconds corresponds to a velocity of 5 m/s, which is ten times too slow for this nerve."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which point on the compound action potential should be used to calculate the velocity of the fastest fibers?\nA) The onset of the response\nB) The peak of the response\nC) The point where the response returns to baseline\nD) The midpoint of the falling phase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The onset of the response","why":"The fastest fibers are the first to arrive, so the very beginning of the deflection marks their arrival time and is the correct point for calculating their velocity. The peak reflects the arrival of the largest group of fibers rather than the quickest ones. The return to baseline marks the arrival of the slowest contributors. The falling phase midpoint has no clear physiological meaning as an arrival time."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: You stimulate a frog sciatic nerve and record the compound action potential 10 cm away. The first, largest deflection begins at 2.5 ms. A second, smaller deflection begins at 5.0 ms. A single stimulus pulse was delivered.\nWhat are the conduction velocities of the fibers producing the two deflections, and what accounts for the difference?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The first deflection is from fibers conducting at 40 m/s and the second from fibers conducting at 20 m/s; the nerve contains large and small myelinated axons that conduct at different speeds","why":"Velocity is distance over time. The distance is 0.10 m, so 0.10 divided by 0.0025 s is 40 m/s and 0.10 divided by 0.0050 s is 20 m/s. A nerve is a bundle of axons of different diameters and myelination, and each group arrives at its own time. The large myelinated fibers get there first and make the biggest deflection; smaller fibers arrive later. A compound action potential is just the sum of all of these."},{"t":"bd","q":"A compound action potential is recorded 8 cm from the stimulating electrode. The first deflection appears at 1.6 ms and the last at 8 ms. Without notes, show how to calculate the conduction velocity of the fastest and slowest fibers and explain why the response is spread out over time instead of arriving all at once.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"velocity equals distance divided by time, with distance converted to meters (8 cm is 0.08 m); fastest fibers: 0.08 m divided by 0.0016 s is 50 m/s; slowest fibers: 0.08 m divided by 0.008 s is 10 m/s; a nerve contains axons of different diameters and degrees of myelination; large myelinated axons conduct fastest and arrive first; small or unmyelinated axons conduct slowest and arrive last; the compound action potential is the sum of many single axon action potentials, so different arrival times spread it out","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How do you get conduction velocity from a recording? Draw the nerve with a stimulating electrode at one end and a recording electrode a measured distance away. Draw the trace you record, marking the stimulus artifact, the latency, and the compound action potential. Write your distance and your latency on the drawing. Show the division that gives velocity in m per second. In one line: say what would have to go wrong in your setup to make the measured velocity come out too high."},{"t":"draw","q":"Why does the compound action potential get lower and wider the farther you record from the stimulus? On the same time base, draw the compound action potential recorded close to the stimulus. Below it, draw the one recorded much farther along: lower and wider. Underneath, draw several individual fibers of different diameters arriving at different times to account for that shape. In one line: say which fibers set the leading edge of the wave and which fibers make it broaden with distance."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Nerve conduction measurement. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Synaptic Transmission","comps":[{"id":"w5-synaptic-sequence","name":"Sequence at a chemical synapse","can":"Order the events of chemical synaptic transmission from action potential arrival through calcium entry and vesicle fusion to receptor binding and postsynaptic response.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An axon terminal is bathed in a solution containing no calcium. An action potential arrives at the terminal. What happens?\nA) The terminal releases neurotransmitter normally\nB) The action potential fails to reach the terminal\nC) The terminal releases neurotransmitter continuously\nD) The terminal depolarizes but releases no neurotransmitter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The terminal depolarizes but releases no neurotransmitter","why":"The action potential is carried by Na+ and K+, so it still invades the terminal, but with no Ca2+ outside there is nothing to enter and trigger fusion, and release fails. Normal release is impossible without the Ca2+ trigger. Conduction to the terminal does not require external Ca2+. Continuous release would require a sustained rise in terminal Ca2+, which is exactly what is missing."},{"t":"mc","q":"Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins in the axon terminal. Which step of transmission fails first?\nA) Conduction of the action potential into the terminal\nB) Fusion of vesicles with the terminal membrane\nC) Binding of transmitter to postsynaptic receptors\nD) Entry of Ca2+ through voltage-gated channels\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Fusion of vesicles with the terminal membrane","why":"SNARE proteins are the fusion machinery, so cleaving them stops vesicles from merging with the terminal membrane and no transmitter reaches the cleft. Conduction into the terminal is unaffected because it depends on voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels. Ca2+ still enters normally, since its channels are intact. Receptor binding never gets tested, because nothing is released for the receptors to bind."},{"t":"mc","q":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels along an axon. Which event in synaptic transmission fails first?\nA) Neurotransmitter is not loaded into vesicles\nB) Postsynaptic receptors no longer bind transmitter\nC) The action potential no longer reaches the terminal\nD) Transmitter is not cleared from the cleft\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The action potential no longer reaches the terminal","why":"Without voltage-gated Na+ channels the axon cannot regenerate the spike, so the signal never arrives to depolarize the terminal and everything downstream stops. Vesicle loading uses a proton gradient and vesicular transporters and continues in the dark. Postsynaptic receptors remain fully functional, which is why applied transmitter still works. Clearance mechanisms are also untouched."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which step accounts for most of the roughly 0.5 ms synaptic delay?\nA) Diffusion of transmitter across the cleft\nB) Conduction of the action potential along the axon\nC) Opening of the postsynaptic ligand-gated channel\nD) Calcium entry and vesicle fusion in the terminal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Calcium entry and vesicle fusion in the terminal","why":"Ca2+ channels take time to open and the fusion machinery takes more time to act, and together they consume most of the delay. Diffusion across a 20 to 40 nm cleft is nearly instantaneous. Axonal conduction happens before the delay is measured, so it is not counted. Channel opening after binding is fast, on the order of tens of microseconds."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the sequence at a chemical synapse, which event comes immediately before transmitter binds its receptor?\nA) Opening of postsynaptic ion channels\nB) Entry of Ca2+ into the terminal\nC) Diffusion of transmitter across the synaptic cleft\nD) Recycling of vesicle membrane by endocytosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Diffusion of transmitter across the synaptic cleft","why":"Once exocytosis dumps transmitter into the cleft, it must diffuse the short distance to the postsynaptic membrane before it can bind. Postsynaptic channels open after binding, not before. Ca2+ entry is two steps earlier, before fusion. Endocytosis recovers membrane after release and runs on a slower timescale than the events of a single transmission."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Neurotransmitter release from a presynaptic terminal is triggered by Na+ entering the terminal during the action potential.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Release is triggered by Ca2+ entering the terminal through voltage gated Ca2+ channels that open when the action potential depolarizes the terminal.","why":"The action potential itself is carried by Na+, but Na+ does not talk to the vesicles. The depolarization opens voltage gated Ca2+ channels, and it is the Ca2+ that binds vesicle proteins and drives fusion. This is why lowering extracellular Ca2+ or blocking Ca2+ channels stops release while the action potential still arrives normally."},{"t":"draw","q":"What happens, step by step, from the action potential arriving to the postsynaptic channel opening? Draw one axon terminal big enough to fill half your space, with the cleft and a patch of postsynaptic membrane. Number the events in order, one arrow per step. Show where calcium enters and what it does once inside. Redraw the terminal sitting in a calcium free bath. In one line: name the step where the chain stops, and say what still happens normally before it."},{"t":"draw","q":"What is happening during the synaptic delay, when nothing shows on either trace? Draw two traces stacked on one shared time axis in milliseconds: the presynaptic action potential on top, the postsynaptic membrane potential below. Mark the synaptic delay between them, about 0.5 ms. In the gap under that delay, draw the events that are happening. In one line: say which single step you think takes up most of that delay, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the events of chemical synaptic transmission from action potential arrival through calcium entry and vesicle fusion to receptor binding and postsynaptic response."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Sequence at a chemical synapse. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-neurotransmitters","name":"Neurotransmitter classes","can":"Group neurocrines into their seven chemical classes, acetylcholine, amines, amino acids, peptides, purines, gases, and lipids, and match acetylcholine, the catecholamines, serotonin, glutamate, GABA, glycine, and the neuropeptides to their usual excitatory or inhibitory effect and to a site of action.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug activates the cannabinoid receptors in the brain. Which neurocrine class is it imitating, and through what type of receptor does that class act?\nA) Lipids, acting through G protein coupled receptors\nB) Peptides, acting through ion channel receptors\nC) Purines, acting through ion channel receptors\nD) Amines, acting through G protein coupled receptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lipids, acting through G protein coupled receptors","why":"The body's own ligands for cannabinoid receptors are lipid neurocrines, eicosanoids, and all lipid neurocrines bind G protein coupled receptors. Peptides such as the opioids are a different class. Some purine receptors are ion channels, but purines do not act on cannabinoid receptors. Many amines use G protein coupled receptors, but amines are not the cannabinoid ligands."},{"t":"mc","q":"Loss of dopamine-releasing neurons in the substantia nigra is expected to disturb which function most directly?\nA) Fast excitatory transmission at the neuromuscular junction\nB) Conduction speed along myelinated axons\nC) Inhibition in the spinal cord\nD) Control of movement\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Control of movement","why":"The nigrostriatal dopamine pathway shapes the basal ganglia circuits that select and scale movement, so losing it produces rigidity, tremor, and slowed movement. The neuromuscular junction uses acetylcholine and is unaffected by nigral dopamine loss. Spinal inhibition relies on glycine and GABA. Conduction speed depends on myelin and axon diameter, not on dopamine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Strychnine blocks glycine receptors in the spinal cord. What is the predicted effect on spinal motor neurons?\nA) They become silent, because excitatory input is removed\nB) They become overexcited, because inhibitory input is removed\nC) They hyperpolarize, because Cl- entry increases\nD) They are unaffected, because glycine acts only in the brain\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) They become overexcited, because inhibitory input is removed","why":"Glycine is the brake on spinal motor circuits, so blocking its receptor releases motor neurons from inhibition and produces spasm and convulsion. Glycine is inhibitory, so blocking it removes a brake rather than an accelerator. Blocking the receptor reduces Cl- entry rather than increasing it. Glycine receptors are concentrated in the cord and brainstem, not absent from them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why do gases such as nitric oxide not need specific membrane receptors?\nA) They are released only into the blood as hormones\nB) They are broken down before they reach a target cell\nC) They act only on the neuron that released them\nD) They diffuse into target cells and bind directly to molecules inside\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They diffuse into target cells and bind directly to molecules inside","why":"The gases have no specific receptors because they diffuse into neurons or glial cells and bind directly to target molecules. They act as neurotransmitters and neuromodulators, not only as hormones in the blood. They do reach and affect other cells rather than breaking down first, and they are not limited to the cell that made them."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Nitric oxide acts by binding to a specific receptor on the outside of its target cell.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Nitric oxide is a gas with no specific receptor. It diffuses into the target cell and binds directly to molecules inside.","why":"Most neurocrines need a membrane receptor because they cannot cross the membrane. The gases, nitric oxide, carbon monoxide, and hydrogen sulfide, are small enough to diffuse straight into neurons and glial cells."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, list the seven chemical classes of neurocrine molecules and give one example of each. Then name the three major neurocrines of the peripheral nervous system.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"acetylcholine, a class by itself; amines, such as norepinephrine, dopamine, serotonin, or histamine; amino acids, such as glutamate, GABA, or glycine; peptides, such as substance P or the opioid peptides; purines, such as adenosine or ATP; gases, such as nitric oxide, which have no specific receptors; lipids, such as the eicosanoids that bind cannabinoid receptors; peripheral nervous system: acetylcholine and norepinephrine as neurotransmitters, epinephrine as a neurohormone","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How can the same neurotransmitter excite one cell and inhibit another? Draw acetylcholine arriving at two postsynaptic membranes side by side: a skeletal muscle fiber with nicotinic receptors, a cardiac pacemaker cell with muscarinic receptors. Show the ion moving at each. Draw the membrane potential trace under each. In one line: using both drawings, state what actually decides whether a synapse is excitatory or inhibitory."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Group neurocrines into their seven chemical classes, acetylcholine, amines, amino acids, peptides, purines, gases, and lipids, and match acetylcholine, the catecholamines, serotonin, glutamate, GABA, glycine, and the neuropeptides to their usual excitatory or inhibitory effect and to a site of action. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to group neurocrines into their seven chemical classes, acetylcholine, amines, amino acids, peptides, purines, gases, and lipids, and match acetylcholine, the catecholamines, serotonin, glutamate, GABA, glycine, and the neuropeptides to their usual excitatory or inhibitory effect and to a site of action."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Neurotransmitter classes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-neurotransmitter-removal","name":"Neurotransmitter removal","can":"Name the three routes that clear a neurotransmitter from the synaptic cleft, degradation, reuptake, and diffusion, and give a drug that blocks one of them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor is applied at the neuromuscular junction. What happens to the postsynaptic response to a single nerve stimulus?\nA) It becomes smaller and briefer\nB) It disappears completely\nC) It is unchanged in size but delayed in onset\nD) It becomes larger and lasts longer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It becomes larger and lasts longer","why":"With the enzyme blocked, acetylcholine stays in the cleft, rebinds receptors, and keeps channels open, so the endplate potential grows and decays more slowly. A smaller and briefer response would follow from faster clearance or from receptor blockade. Onset is set by release and diffusion, which are unchanged. Complete loss of the response would require blocking release or blocking the receptor."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft?\nA) Acetylcholinesterase  |  B) Monoamine oxidase\nC) Catechol O methyltransferase  |  D) Carbonic anhydrase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Acetylcholinesterase","why":"Acetylcholinesterase sits in the cleft, particularly at the neuromuscular junction, and splits acetylcholine into acetate and choline within about a millisecond. Monoamine oxidase degrades catecholamines and serotonin, and it works inside the terminal rather than in the cleft. Catechol O methyltransferase also acts on catecholamines. Carbonic anhydrase handles the reaction of CO2 with water and has nothing to do with transmitters."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme inside the axon terminal degrades catecholamines that are not repackaged into vesicles?\nA) Acetylcholinesterase  |  B) Adenylyl cyclase\nC) Monoamine oxidase  |  D) Sodium potassium ATPase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Monoamine oxidase","why":"Monoamine oxidase sits on the outer mitochondrial membrane of the terminal and destroys cytosolic norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin. Acetylcholinesterase acts on acetylcholine and works in the cleft. Adenylyl cyclase makes cyclic AMP in second messenger signaling. The Na+/K+ ATPase builds the sodium gradient that powers reuptake, so it supports clearance without degrading anything."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main route by which glutamate is cleared from a central synapse?\nA) Reuptake by transporters into astrocytes and neurons\nB) Degradation by a specific enzyme released into the cleft\nC) Filtration into the cerebrospinal fluid\nD) Endocytosis of intact receptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Reuptake by transporters into astrocytes and neurons","why":"Glutamate transporters on surrounding astrocytes and on the terminal pull glutamate out of the cleft, which both ends the signal and keeps concentrations low enough to avoid excitotoxicity. There is no cleft enzyme that destroys glutamate the way acetylcholinesterase destroys acetylcholine. Cerebrospinal fluid is not filtered at individual synapses. Receptor endocytosis changes sensitivity rather than removing transmitter."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A person is exposed to an organophosphate insecticide that irreversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase. Within an hour they have profuse salivation and sweating, a heart rate of 48 beats per minute (normal 60 to 100), and muscle twitching that then progresses to weakness.\nWhich mechanism explains both the early twitching and the later weakness at the skeletal neuromuscular junction?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Acetylcholine accumulates in the cleft and overstimulates nicotinic receptors, then persistent depolarization inactivates the muscle Na+ channels so the muscle can no longer fire","why":"Acetylcholinesterase is the only fast route for clearing acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. With the enzyme blocked, every release lingers and the endplate is stimulated over and over, giving fasciculations. A membrane held depolarized cannot reset its voltage gated Na+ channels, so the muscle becomes unable to generate action potentials and weakness follows. The slow heart and salivation are the same excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A drug that blocks reuptake of a neurotransmitter shortens the postsynaptic response, because less transmitter is recycled for the next release.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Blocking reuptake prolongs and enlarges the postsynaptic response, because the transmitter stays in the cleft and keeps binding receptors.","why":"Reuptake is one of the routes that ends a signal. Take it away and the transmitter lingers, so the response lasts longer, which is the basis of how SSRIs and cocaine act. Recycling matters for long term supply, but the immediate effect of a reuptake blocker is more signal, not less."},{"t":"draw","q":"How does a neurotransmitter get cleared out of the cleft, and what happens if one route is blocked? Draw a synaptic cleft with transmitter still in it. Draw all three exit routes off the cleft: enzymatic breakdown, reuptake, and simple diffusion. Show where each one physically happens: the postsynaptic side, the presynaptic side, or out the edges. Name the transmitter you picked and put a real drug on the route it blocks. In one line: say what the postsynaptic cell experiences once that route is shut."},{"t":"draw","q":"How does blocking removal change the postsynaptic potential? Draw the postsynaptic potential from one normal release event, membrane potential on the y axis and time on the x axis. On the same axes, draw that trace again under two drugs: one that blocks degradation and one that blocks reuptake. Show what changes: the height, the width, or both. In one line: say which drug you expect to change the trace more, and what you would need to measure to be sure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three routes that clear a neurotransmitter from the synaptic cleft, degradation, reuptake, and diffusion, and give a drug that blocks one of them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Neurotransmitter removal. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-postsynaptic-potentials","name":"Excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials","can":"Distinguish an EPSP from an IPSP by the ion channel opened and the direction of the membrane potential change, and contrast fast responses through ionotropic receptor-channels with slow responses through metabotropic G protein coupled receptors, including how a neuromodulator differs from a neurotransmitter.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A transmitter opens K+ channels on a neuron resting at -70 mV, with the K+ equilibrium potential at -90 mV. Which way does the membrane potential move?\nA) Toward -55 mV, so this is an EPSP\nB) Toward -90 mV, so this is an IPSP\nC) Toward 0 mV, so this is an EPSP\nD) It does not move, because K+ is already at equilibrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Toward -90 mV, so this is an IPSP","why":"Opening a channel drives the membrane toward that ion's equilibrium potential, and -90 mV is below rest, so K+ leaves, the cell hyperpolarizes, and the event is inhibitory. Moving toward -55 mV or 0 mV would require an inward cation current. K+ is not at equilibrium at -70 mV, which is exactly why the driving force exists."},{"t":"mc","q":"A recording shows the membrane potential of a neuron moving from -70 mV to -76 mV after a synapse is activated. How should this event be classified?\nA) An IPSP\nB) An EPSP\nC) An action potential\nD) A resting potential shift caused by the sodium potassium pump\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) An IPSP","why":"A 6 mV hyperpolarization in response to synaptic activation is an inhibitory postsynaptic potential, which moves the cell further from the roughly -55 mV threshold. An action potential is a large all or none spike toward positive voltages. An EPSP would move the potential upward toward threshold. The pump contributes only a few millivolts of steady offset and does not produce a stimulus locked event."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuromodulator binds a G protein coupled receptor, and a second messenger closes K+ channels in the postsynaptic cell. What results?\nA) An IPSP, because less K+ enters the cell\nB) No change, because closing channels cannot change the membrane potential\nC) An EPSP, because less K+ leaves and the cell depolarizes\nD) An IPSP, because Cl- now enters the cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) An EPSP, because less K+ leaves and the cell depolarizes","why":"K+ normally leaks out of the cell, so closing K+ channels keeps positive charge inside, and the cell depolarizes, a slow EPSP. K+ leaves the cell rather than entering it, so less K+ entering is the wrong direction. Closing channels does change membrane potential, since it changes ion movement. Nothing in this pathway opens a Cl- channel."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change in a postsynaptic cell produces an IPSP?\nA) Opening channels that let Cl- into the cell\nB) Opening channels that let more Na+ into the cell\nC) Closing K+ channels so that less K+ leaves\nD) Opening channels that let Ca2+ into the cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Opening channels that let Cl- into the cell","why":"Cl- entering adds negative charge, hyperpolarizing the cell and moving it away from threshold, which is an IPSP. More K+ leaving would also do this. Na+ or Ca2+ entering adds positive charge and depolarizes the cell, an EPSP. Keeping K+ inside by closing K+ channels also depolarizes the cell."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A response that works through a G protein coupled receptor begins faster than one that works through a receptor-channel.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A response through a receptor-channel is faster. A G protein coupled receptor has to work through a second messenger system, so its response starts later and lasts longer.","why":"A receptor-channel opens as soon as the neurotransmitter binds, so ions move within milliseconds. A G protein coupled receptor adds steps between binding and the effect, which slows the start but lets the response last seconds to minutes and even change proteins in the cell."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare a fast and a slow postsynaptic response. For each one, name the receptor type, explain how it changes the membrane potential, and say how long it lasts. Then explain the difference between a neurotransmitter and a neuromodulator.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"fast responses come from ionotropic receptors, which are receptor-channels, ligand-gated ion channels; fast synaptic potentials begin quickly and last a few milliseconds; slow responses come from metabotropic, G protein coupled receptors working through second messengers; slow synaptic potentials take longer to begin and last seconds to minutes; slow responses can also modify existing proteins or regulate the making of new proteins; depolarizing potentials are EPSPs and hyperpolarizing potentials are IPSPs; neuromodulators act at synaptic and nonsynaptic sites, act more slowly, and mostly bind G protein coupled receptors; the receptor a chemical binds decides whether it acts as a neurotransmitter or a neuromodulator","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"What makes a postsynaptic potential excitatory or inhibitory? Draw a postsynaptic neuron with two synapses on one dendrite, one excitatory and one inhibitory. At each synapse, draw the channel that opened and the ion moving through it, with an arrow for direction. Under each synapse, draw its own trace starting from about minus 70 mV and label the peak value. In one line: say how an inhibitory input can still be inhibitory even when the membrane potential barely moves."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish an EPSP from an IPSP by the ion channel opened and the direction of the membrane potential change, and contrast fast responses through ionotropic receptor-channels with slow responses through metabotropic G protein coupled receptors, including how a neuromodulator differs from a neurotransmitter. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish an EPSP from an IPSP by the ion channel opened and the direction of the membrane potential change, and contrast fast responses through ionotropic receptor-channels with slow responses through metabotropic G protein coupled receptors, including how a neuromodulator differs from a neurotransmitter."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-summation-integration","name":"Summation and integration","can":"Distinguish temporal from spatial summation and determine whether a stated combination of EPSPs and IPSPs will bring the trigger zone to threshold.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"One presynaptic neuron fires twice, 2 ms apart, onto the same synapse. What type of summation is at work?\nA) Temporal summation  |  B) No summation, because the interval is too short\nC) Spatial summation  |  D) Presynaptic facilitation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Temporal summation","why":"A single input firing repeatedly in rapid succession produces overlapping potentials that add in time, the definition of temporal summation. Spatial summation requires inputs at different locations. A 2 ms interval is short enough for strong overlap, so summation certainly occurs. Presynaptic facilitation is a change in transmitter release caused by a third neuron, not simple addition of postsynaptic potentials."},{"t":"mc","q":"Three separate presynaptic neurons fire at the same instant onto three different dendritic synapses of one cell. What type of summation is this?\nA) Temporal summation  |  B) Spatial summation\nC) Long term potentiation  |  D) Postsynaptic inhibition\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Spatial summation","why":"Different synapses at different places delivering current at the same moment add across space, which is spatial summation. Temporal summation requires repeated firing over time at one input. Long term potentiation is a lasting strengthening of synapses, not the immediate addition of inputs. Postsynaptic inhibition would require inhibitory inputs, which are not described here."},{"t":"mc","q":"A neuron rests at -70 mV with a threshold of -55 mV. Three EPSPs of 5 mV each arrive together at the trigger zone. Does the cell fire?\nA) Yes, the summed potential reaches -55 mV, exactly threshold\nB) No, the summed potential reaches only -60 mV\nC) No, because EPSPs cannot summate spatially\nD) Yes, but only if an IPSP arrives at exactly the same moment\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Yes, the summed potential reaches -55 mV, exactly threshold","why":"Three inputs of 5 mV give 15 mV of depolarization, and -70 mV plus 15 mV is -55 mV, which is threshold, so the cell fires. Reaching only -60 mV would require just 10 mV of summed input. EPSPs summate spatially all the time, and that is the whole point of convergence. An IPSP would subtract from the total and make firing less likely."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A motor neuron rests at -70 mV with a threshold of -55 mV. Input A is a single synapse on the soma that produces a 4 mV EPSP. When input A fires once, the neuron does not reach threshold. The experimenter then stimulates input A five times in 10 ms, and each EPSP lasts about 15 ms.\nPredict what happens at the trigger zone during the burst, and name the process responsible.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The EPSPs stack before each one decays, the membrane passes -55 mV, and the neuron fires; this is temporal summation","why":"Five 4 mV EPSPs delivered within 10 ms from one synapse overlap, because each one lasts longer than the gap between stimuli. Their sum at the soma approaches 20 mV, well past the 15 mV needed to reach threshold, so the neuron fires. Adding inputs from one synapse in time is temporal summation."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A neuron rests at -70 mV with threshold at -55 mV. Three excitatory synapses on the soma each produce a 6 mV EPSP, and one inhibitory synapse on the soma produces a 5 mV IPSP. All four fire at the same instant.\nDoes the neuron fire, and what is the approximate membrane potential at the trigger zone?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"No; the inputs sum to about -57 mV, just below threshold","why":"Excitatory and inhibitory inputs add algebraically. Three EPSPs of 6 mV give 18 mV of depolarization, the IPSP subtracts 5 mV, leaving 13 mV net. From -70 mV that brings the trigger zone to about -57 mV, two millivolts short of threshold, so no action potential."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how a postsynaptic neuron decides whether to fire an action potential when it is receiving several excitatory and inhibitory inputs at once. Include where the decision is made and why timing and location of the inputs matter.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"EPSPs and IPSPs are graded potentials that add algebraically; spatial summation combines inputs from different synapses arriving together; temporal summation combines repeated inputs from one synapse close in time; graded potentials decay with distance, so distant dendritic inputs count for less; the summed potential is tested at the trigger zone (axon hillock or initial segment); an action potential fires only if the trigger zone reaches threshold","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How are temporal and spatial summation different? Draw the same neuron twice. First drawing: one presynaptic neuron fires four times in quick succession. Second drawing: four separate presynaptic neurons fire at the same moment. Under each, draw the trace at the trigger zone, with a resting line near minus 70 mV and a dashed threshold line near minus 55 mV. Label which is temporal and which is spatial. In one line: say what has to be true about timing for the first one to work at all."},{"t":"draw","q":"Will this combination of inputs reach threshold, and what change would flip the outcome? Draw a neuron with five inputs: three excitatory worth about 4 mV each and two inhibitory worth about 5 mV each. Choose which inputs fire and in what order. Draw the trace at the trigger zone against a threshold line near minus 55 mV, writing your running total along the trace. Change one input, its timing or its sign, so the outcome flips. In one line: say which change you made and why it was enough."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish temporal from spatial summation and determine whether a stated combination of EPSPs and IPSPs will bring the trigger zone to threshold."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Summation and integration. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-presynaptic-modulation","name":"Presynaptic modulation","can":"Compare presynaptic inhibition and facilitation with postsynaptic modulation and state the advantage of presynaptic control at a single input, which is selectivity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Where does a neuron producing presynaptic inhibition make its synapse?\nA) On the dendrite of the postsynaptic neuron\nB) On the initial segment of the postsynaptic neuron\nC) On the soma of the postsynaptic neuron\nD) On the axon terminal of another presynaptic neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) On the axon terminal of another presynaptic neuron","why":"Presynaptic inhibition works through an axoaxonic synapse, one terminal contacting another terminal, so it changes how much transmitter that one terminal releases. Contacts on a dendrite or soma of the target cell produce postsynaptic inhibition instead, which affects the whole cell. A synapse on the initial segment is also postsynaptic and is a particularly powerful form of it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main mechanism by which presynaptic inhibition reduces transmitter release?\nA) It blocks postsynaptic receptors\nB) It destroys transmitter inside the vesicles\nC) It reduces Ca2+ entry into the terminal\nD) It hyperpolarizes the postsynaptic cell body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It reduces Ca2+ entry into the terminal","why":"Less Ca2+ per spike means fewer vesicles fuse, so less transmitter is released from that terminal alone. Destroying vesicular transmitter is what a depleting drug does, not what a modulating neuron does. Blocking postsynaptic receptors would be a postsynaptic effect and would weaken every input using that receptor. Hyperpolarizing the postsynaptic soma is postsynaptic inhibition, a different arrangement entirely."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does presynaptic facilitation do at the terminal it acts on?\nA) It decreases the amount of transmitter released per action potential\nB) It increases the number of postsynaptic receptors\nC) It increases the amount of transmitter released per action potential\nD) It shortens the refractory period of the postsynaptic cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It increases the amount of transmitter released per action potential","why":"Facilitation raises terminal Ca2+, often by prolonging the terminal action potential, so more vesicles fuse and the postsynaptic potential from that input grows. Reducing release is presynaptic inhibition, the opposite. Adding postsynaptic receptors is a postsynaptic change seen in long term potentiation. The refractory period is set by Na+ channel inactivation and is not a target of presynaptic modulation."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main advantage of presynaptic control over postsynaptic control?\nA) Speed, because it acts before the action potential arrives\nB) Selectivity, because only one input to the target cell is changed\nC) Strength, because it always abolishes the response completely\nD) Permanence, because its effect cannot be reversed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Selectivity, because only one input to the target cell is changed","why":"Acting on a single terminal turns one input up or down while every other input to the same neuron continues unchanged, which is the point of the arrangement. It is not faster, since it still requires transmitter release and second messenger action at the terminal. It is graded rather than all or nothing. Its effect is reversible and lasts only as long as the modulating input is active."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which receptor on a presynaptic terminal is commonly responsible for presynaptic inhibition in the central nervous system?\nA) The nicotinic receptor  |  B) The AMPA receptor\nC) The NMDA receptor  |  D) The GABA-B receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The GABA-B receptor","why":"GABA-B receptors on terminals act through G proteins to reduce Ca2+ channel opening and increase K+ conductance, cutting release from that terminal. Nicotinic receptors are excitatory cation channels. AMPA and NMDA receptors are the excitatory glutamate receptors of the postsynaptic membrane and do not mediate this inhibition."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The main advantage of presynaptic inhibition over postsynaptic inhibition is that it can turn down one specific input to a neuron while leaving the neuron's other inputs at full strength.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Postsynaptic inhibition changes the membrane of the target cell, so every input to that cell is dampened together. Presynaptic inhibition acts on one terminal and edits one message without touching the rest, which is what selectivity means here. The trade off is that the modulated input is affected regardless of what else the postsynaptic cell is doing."},{"t":"draw","q":"How can one input be turned down without affecting the others? Draw a neuron with three excitatory inputs converging on it. Add a modulatory neuron that synapses on one input's terminal, not on the cell body. Show what it does to calcium entry and to transmitter release at that terminal only. Draw the postsynaptic response to all three inputs, before and after. In one line: say what postsynaptic inhibition would have done to the other two inputs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Why is presynaptic control the right tool when you need to change just one input? Your job: cut one input's effect by half and leave the other inputs untouched. Draw two attempts side by side: one acting on that input's presynaptic terminal, one acting on the postsynaptic membrane. On each drawing, shade every synapse the attempt affected. Mark the attempt that failed the job. In one line: name the property that makes presynaptic control the right tool for this job."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare presynaptic inhibition and facilitation with postsynaptic modulation and state the advantage of presynaptic control at a single input, which is selectivity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Presynaptic modulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-synaptic-plasticity","name":"Synaptic plasticity","can":"Explain long term potentiation as a mechanism of learning, tracing how glutamate acts on AMPA and NMDA receptors, how strong depolarization removes the magnesium block, how calcium entry starts second messenger pathways, and how repeated stimulation leads to insertion of new AMPA receptors.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A postsynaptic membrane depolarizes strongly during a burst of stimulation. What does this do at NMDA receptors that have glutamate bound?\nA) Mg2+ leaves the pore and Ca2+ flows into the cell\nB) Mg2+ enters the pore and current stops\nC) Cl- flows into the cell and the synapse weakens\nD) The receptor is removed from the membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Mg2+ leaves the pore and Ca2+ flows into the cell","why":"Depolarization pushes the positively charged Mg2+ out of the pore, unblocking the channel so that Na+ and Ca2+ can enter, and the Ca2+ signal starts the potentiating cascade. Mg2+ block deepens with hyperpolarization, not depolarization. NMDA receptor channels are cation channels and do not pass Cl-. Receptor removal from the membrane is a feature of long term depression rather than of unblocking."},{"t":"mc","q":"A synapse is stimulated at 1 Hz for fifteen minutes. What is the expected outcome?\nA) Long term depression, with AMPA receptors removed from the membrane\nB) Long term potentiation, with AMPA receptors added to the membrane\nC) No change at all, because the stimulation is too weak to matter\nD) Presynaptic facilitation lasting several minutes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Long term depression, with AMPA receptors removed from the membrane","why":"Prolonged low frequency stimulation produces a small sustained rise in postsynaptic Ca2+ that favors phosphatases over kinases and drives receptor removal, weakening the synapse. Potentiation requires the brief, intense Ca2+ rise that only high frequency stimulation delivers. This protocol changes the synapse durably rather than leaving it untouched. Presynaptic facilitation requires a modulating input at the terminal, which is not part of this protocol."},{"t":"mc","q":"In which brain region has long term potentiation been studied most thoroughly?\nA) The cerebellum  |  B) The hypothalamus\nC) The medulla oblongata  |  D) The hippocampus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The hippocampus","why":"Hippocampal circuits are laid out in orderly layers and are central to forming new memories, which made them the standard preparation for studying LTP. The cerebellum shows its own plasticity, better known for long term depression. The medulla handles autonomic reflexes such as breathing and blood pressure. The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis and endocrine output."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which receptor acts as the coincidence detector in long term potentiation?\nA) The NMDA receptor  |  B) The GABA-A receptor\nC) The AMPA receptor  |  D) The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The NMDA receptor","why":"The NMDA receptor opens only when glutamate is bound and the postsynaptic membrane is already depolarized, so it reports that presynaptic and postsynaptic activity happened together. AMPA receptors open on glutamate binding alone and carry the ordinary fast EPSP. GABA-A receptors are inhibitory Cl- channels. Nicotinic receptors respond to acetylcholine and are not part of this pathway."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ion blocks the NMDA receptor channel while the postsynaptic membrane is at its resting potential?\nA) K+  |  B) Mg2+\nC) Cl-  |  D) HCO3-\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Mg2+","why":"Mg2+ sits in the pore at negative membrane potentials and is expelled when the membrane depolarizes, which is the voltage dependence that makes the receptor a coincidence detector. K+ moves through many channels but does not plug the NMDA pore. Cl- passes through inhibitory receptor channels. HCO3- matters in acid base handling and has no role here."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how repeated stimulation strengthens a glutamate synapse (long term potentiation). Include why a single weak stimulus does not do it, and what is physically different about the synapse afterward.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"AMPA receptors carry the normal EPSP; NMDA receptors are blocked by Mg2+ at resting potential; high frequency stimulation depolarizes the postsynaptic cell enough to expel Mg2+; Ca2+ enters through the NMDA receptor only when glutamate is bound and the membrane is depolarized; Ca2+ activates kinases that insert more AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic membrane; afterward the same release produces a larger EPSP, which is the cellular basis of learning","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"How does repeated stimulation make a synapse stronger? Panel 1: draw a glutamate synapse with both AMPA and NMDA receptors in the postsynaptic membrane. Show the magnesium sitting in the NMDA channel at rest. Panel 2: draw the same synapse during repeated high frequency stimulation, with the magnesium displaced and calcium entering. Panel 3: draw what the membrane looks like an hour later. In one line: say why one presynaptic action potential on its own cannot do this."},{"t":"draw","q":"What does long term potentiation look like on a graph, and which receptor is needed to start it? Draw a graph of postsynaptic response size against time, with a burst of high frequency stimulation marked on the time axis. Plot the response before, during, and long after the burst. On the same axes, draw a second curve for the identical experiment with NMDA receptors blocked. In one line: say which receptor carries the ordinary moment to moment signal, and which one carries the signal that something should change."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain long term potentiation as a mechanism of learning, tracing how glutamate acts on AMPA and NMDA receptors, how strong depolarization removes the magnesium block, how calcium entry starts second messenger pathways, and how repeated stimulation leads to insertion of new AMPA receptors."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Synaptic plasticity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-electrical-synapses","name":"Electrical synapses","can":"Compare an electrical synapse at a gap junction with a chemical synapse by speed and by capacity for modulation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A group of neurons must fire together in near perfect synchrony. Which arrangement best achieves this?\nA) Electrical synapses linking them through gap junctions\nB) Chemical synapses that use glutamate as their transmitter at each contact\nC) Chemical synapses that use GABA as their transmitter\nD) Presynaptic inhibition acting among the members of the group\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Electrical synapses linking them through gap junctions","why":"Direct electrical coupling shares current instantly across the group, so the cells depolarize together and fire as a unit. Glutamate synapses would excite the group but each with its own synaptic delay, blurring the timing. GABA synapses would inhibit rather than synchronize firing. Presynaptic inhibition damps selected inputs and does nothing to synchronize."},{"t":"mc","q":"What structure joins the two cells at an electrical synapse?\nA) A synaptic cleft of 20 to 40 nm  |  B) A tight junction\nC) A desmosome  |  D) A gap junction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A gap junction","why":"A gap junction is a set of paired channels that lines up across a 2 to 4 nm gap and creates a continuous pore between the cytoplasm of the two cells. A tight junction seals the space between cells and blocks leakage rather than conducting current. A desmosome is a mechanical anchor. A wide cleft of 20 to 40 nm is the arrangement at a chemical synapse."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which protein forms the channels of a gap junction?\nA) Connexin  |  B) Actin\nC) Synaptotagmin  |  D) Dystrophin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Connexin","why":"Six connexin subunits form a connexon in each membrane, and two connexons align to make one continuous pore. Actin is a cytoskeletal filament. Synaptotagmin is the vesicle calcium sensor at chemical synapses. Dystrophin links the muscle cytoskeleton to the membrane and is unrelated to junctional channels."},{"t":"mc","q":"In which direction does current usually flow at an electrical synapse?\nA) Only in one direction, from the presynaptic to the postsynaptic cell\nB) Only in one direction, from the postsynaptic back to the presynaptic cell\nC) In either direction, since the junction is usually bidirectional\nD) Only when both of the coupled cells are hyperpolarized\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In either direction, since the junction is usually bidirectional","why":"The pore is an open channel between two cytoplasms, so current flows whichever way the voltage difference drives it, and either cell can influence the other. One way transmission is the rule at chemical synapses, where transmitter is released from only one side. Restricting flow to a single direction in either sense misses the symmetry of the junction. Current flows whenever a voltage difference exists, not only during hyperpolarization."},{"t":"mc","q":"About how long is the synaptic delay at an electrical synapse?\nA) About 0.5 ms  |  B) About 2 ms\nC) About 10 ms  |  D) Essentially zero\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Essentially zero","why":"Current passes directly through the open junction, so there is no release step and no measurable delay. A delay near 0.5 ms is the value at a fast chemical synapse, where Ca2+ entry and vesicle fusion take time. Two milliseconds and ten milliseconds are longer than even most chemical synapses require."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Electrical synapses are slower than chemical synapses because current must pass through two membranes.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Electrical synapses are faster than chemical synapses, because current flows directly between cells through gap junction channels with no release, diffusion or receptor binding step.","why":"The synaptic delay of a chemical synapse comes from Ca2+ entry, vesicle fusion and diffusion across the cleft, about half a millisecond or more. A gap junction skips all of that. The price of the speed is that an electrical synapse cannot easily be made inhibitory or modulated by drugs."},{"t":"draw","q":"How do electrical and chemical synapses compare in speed, and what does the slower one gain? Draw two synapses side by side in cross section at the same scale: an electrical one at a gap junction and a chemical one. Show what physically crosses at each, and make clear which gap is wider. Underneath both, draw one shared time axis and mark when the postsynaptic response begins for each. In one line: say what the chemical synapse gains in exchange for its delay."},{"t":"draw","q":"When is an electrical synapse the right choice, and when is a chemical one? Pick two real jobs: one where many cells must fire as a single unit, and one where a signal must be turned up, turned down, or blocked outright. Draw the synapse you would build for each and label it electrical or chemical. On each drawing, circle the feature that made it right for that job. In one line: say what fails if you swap the two."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare an electrical synapse at a gap junction with a chemical synapse by speed and by capacity for modulation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Electrical synapses. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-lab-synapse-sim","name":"Synapse simulation","can":"Alter neurotransmitter release and receptor availability in a synapse simulation and interpret the resulting postsynaptic recording.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In the simulation the extracellular calcium is set to zero and the presynaptic axon is stimulated. What does the postsynaptic trace show?\nA) A normal EPSP, because release does not need Ca2+\nB) An action potential, because the terminal depolarizes\nC) A flat trace at rest, because no transmitter is released\nD) A hyperpolarization below -80 mV\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A flat trace at rest, because no transmitter is released","why":"Vesicle fusion depends on Ca2+ entering the terminal, so with none available nothing is released and the postsynaptic cell sees nothing at all. Release absolutely requires Ca2+, which is the point of the manipulation. The presynaptic terminal does depolarize, but that event is not recorded by the postsynaptic electrode. Nothing in this setting opens inhibitory channels."},{"t":"mc","q":"The simulation is set so that each presynaptic action potential releases twice as many vesicles as the control run. What changes on the postsynaptic trace?\nA) The EPSP amplitude increases\nB) The EPSP amplitude decreases\nC) The resting potential becomes more negative\nD) The synaptic delay lengthens noticeably\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The EPSP amplitude increases","why":"More vesicles mean more transmitter, more receptors bound, more channels open, and a larger depolarization, since postsynaptic potentials are graded. Releasing more transmitter cannot shrink the response. Resting potential depends on the leak conductances rather than on how much transmitter arrives. Delay is set by the release machinery and does not lengthen when release increases."},{"t":"mc","q":"An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor is switched on in the simulation of a cholinergic synapse. What changes in the shape of the postsynaptic potential?\nA) It decays more quickly and ends sooner\nB) It decays more slowly and lasts longer\nC) It reverses direction and becomes a hyperpolarization\nD) It disappears entirely from the trace\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It decays more slowly and lasts longer","why":"With the degrading enzyme inhibited, transmitter lingers in the cleft, keeps rebinding, and holds channels open, which stretches out the falling phase. Faster decay would follow from more clearance, not less. The sign of the response depends on which ions the receptor channel passes and is not changed by an enzyme. The response persists and in fact grows, so it does not vanish."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a synapse simulation, what does the vertical axis of the postsynaptic recording show?\nA) Transmitter concentration in mmol/L\nB) Current in nanoamperes through the stimulating electrode\nC) Membrane potential in mV\nD) Time in ms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Membrane potential in mV","why":"The postsynaptic trace plots the recorded membrane potential, which is why the baseline sits near -70 mV and events are read in millivolts. Transmitter concentration is a variable you set, not what the electrode reports. Stimulating current is an input to the presynaptic side. Time is plotted on the horizontal axis."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In the synapse simulation, the control EPSP is 10 mV. A student adds drug X and the EPSP falls to 3 mV. She then doubles extracellular Ca2+ and the EPSP rises to 9 mV. In a separate run without drug X, doubling Ca2+ alone raises the EPSP from 10 mV to 14 mV.\nWhat does this pattern indicate about where drug X acts?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Drug X reduces transmitter release from the presynaptic terminal, because extra Ca2+ largely rescues the response","why":"Extra Ca2+ works by increasing the amount of transmitter released per action potential. If the drug had removed receptors, releasing more transmitter would not help much because there would be nothing extra to bind. A response that goes from 3 mV back to 9 mV when Ca2+ is raised was limited by release, so the drug is acting at the terminal."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In the synapse simulation, the control EPSP has a peak of 10 mV and a duration of 20 ms. After a drug is added, the peak is 12 mV and the duration is 80 ms. Presynaptic Ca2+ entry per action potential is measured and is unchanged.\nWhat is the most likely action of the drug?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"It blocks a route that clears the transmitter from the cleft, so transmitter stays bound longer","why":"A response that lasts four times longer with a slightly larger peak is the signature of transmitter that is not being cleared. Release is normal, since Ca2+ entry is unchanged, and receptors must be working because the response is at least as big as before. Blocking degradation or reuptake leaves transmitter in the cleft to keep binding."},{"t":"bd","q":"In the synapse simulation you can change extracellular Ca2+ and change the number of available postsynaptic receptors. Without notes, explain how you would use these two controls to decide whether an unknown drug that shrinks the EPSP is acting on the presynaptic terminal or on the postsynaptic receptors, and what result you expect in each case.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the EPSP size depends on both amount of transmitter released and number of receptors available; raising extracellular Ca2+ increases transmitter release per action potential; if the drug acts presynaptically, extra Ca2+ restores the EPSP at least partly; if the drug blocks receptors, extra Ca2+ does not restore the EPSP; adding receptors (or applying transmitter directly) restores the response only if the receptors were the problem; the peak of the EPSP, not its duration, is the measure to compare","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"What do you predict happens to the postsynaptic signal when you reduce transmitter release, and when you reduce receptors? Before you run anything, draw the recording you predict for three conditions on the same axes: normal, less transmitter released, fewer receptors available. Label the height (amplitude) you expect on each. Run the simulation. Draw the real traces over your prediction in a second color. In one line: say where your prediction was off, if it was, and what you had assumed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Can you tell from the recording alone whether a change was presynaptic or postsynaptic? On one shared time axis, draw four postsynaptic recordings from your simulation: the control run and three altered runs. Under each altered trace, draw the synapse and mark the single thing you changed, presynaptic or postsynaptic. In one line: point to a trace that two different changes could both have produced, and name the extra run you would need to tell them apart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Synapse simulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Central Integration and Reflexes","comps":[{"id":"w5-reflex-arc","name":"Reflex arc components","can":"Diagram a reflex arc naming the receptor, sensory neuron, integrating center, motor neuron, and effector, and classify the reflex as monosynaptic or polysynaptic.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A reflex arc has an afferent path of 0.8 m conducting at 80 m/s, an efferent path of 0.8 m conducting at 80 m/s, and one central synapse costing about 0.5 ms. Roughly how long is the delay from stimulus to the arrival of the signal at the muscle?\nA) About 21 ms  |  B) About 41 ms\nC) About 2 ms  |  D) About 11 ms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 21 ms","why":"Each 0.8 m leg takes 0.8 divided by 80, which is 0.01 s or 10 ms, so the two legs total 20 ms, and adding the 0.5 ms synaptic delay gives about 21 ms. About 11 ms counts only one leg of the trip. About 41 ms would double the true conduction distance. About 2 ms is off by a factor of ten and would require conduction distances of centimeters rather than most of a limb."},{"t":"mc","q":"The dorsal root of a spinal nerve is cut. What happens to a reflex whose arc runs through that segment?\nA) The reflex is exaggerated because inhibition has been removed\nB) The reflex is lost because the sensory limb is interrupted\nC) The reflex is lost because the motor limb is interrupted\nD) The reflex is normal because the interneurons are intact\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The reflex is lost because the sensory limb is interrupted","why":"The dorsal root carries afferent axons into the cord, so cutting it means the stimulus never reaches the integrating center and the arc cannot be completed. The motor limb leaves through the ventral root and is untouched, which is why the muscle can still be activated voluntarily. Intact interneurons cannot help when nothing arrives to excite them. Removing input removes excitation, so exaggeration is the opposite of what happens."},{"t":"mc","q":"The ventral root of a spinal nerve is cut. What is the expected pattern of findings in the region it supplies?\nA) Sensation is preserved but the reflex response is absent\nB) Both sensation and the reflex response are lost\nC) Sensation is lost but the reflex response is preserved\nD) Both sensation and the reflex response are preserved\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Sensation is preserved but the reflex response is absent","why":"The ventral root carries motor axons out, so cutting it removes the output limb while leaving the afferents in the dorsal root intact: the stimulus is still felt, but no contraction follows. Losing sensation while keeping the reflex is impossible, since the same afferents serve both. Preserving both would require an intact motor limb. Losing both is what a cut spinal nerve distal to the union of the two roots would produce, not a ventral root lesion alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"An autonomic reflex such as the baroreceptor reflex differs from a somatic reflex mainly in which component?\nA) The receptor, which produces no receptor potential\nB) The sensory neuron, which conducts without action potentials\nC) The integrating center, which must be the cerebral cortex\nD) The effector, which is cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, or gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The effector, which is cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, or gland","why":"Autonomic and somatic reflexes share the same general plan, and the clearest difference is the effector: autonomic output goes to cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands rather than to skeletal muscle. Autonomic receptors transduce stimuli into receptor potentials just as somatic ones do. The integrating center for the baroreceptor reflex is in the medulla, not the cortex. Autonomic afferents conduct action potentials like any other sensory neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change to a reflex arc would slow the reflex the most?\nA) Increasing the strength of the stimulus\nB) Warming the nerve by 1 C\nC) Demyelination of the afferent axons\nD) Insertion of one additional interneuron in the pathway\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Demyelination of the afferent axons","why":"Myelin is what makes saltatory conduction fast, so losing it can drop conduction velocity from tens of meters per second to a few, adding tens of milliseconds over a limb length. One extra interneuron adds only about half a millisecond to a millisecond of synaptic delay. Warming a nerve slightly speeds conduction rather than slowing it. A stronger stimulus recruits more fibers and can shorten latency a little, but it does not slow the reflex."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A monosynaptic reflex has one synapse in the spinal cord, between the sensory neuron and the motor neuron, and therefore no interneuron in its excitatory pathway.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Most reflexes are polysynaptic, with one or more interneurons between input and output, and each extra synapse adds delay and a place for modulation. The stretch reflex is the textbook exception. Note that the inhibition of the antagonist in the same reflex does use an interneuron, so the whole response is not monosynaptic, only its excitatory core."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a spinal cord cross section and build one real reflex on it, your own choice, not the patellar. Label all five components in order, receptor, sensory neuron, integrating center, motor neuron, effector. Put the dorsal root and ventral root in their correct places and count the synapses inside the cord. Write monosynaptic or polysynaptic on the drawing. One line: what that synapse count buys and what it costs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one reflex arc, then cut it in four separate places, at the receptor, at the dorsal root, inside the cord, and at the ventral root. For each cut, draw what the person can still do and what they cannot, keeping sensation and movement separate. One line: which two cuts look the same from the outside, and the one test that would separate them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to diagram a reflex arc naming the receptor, sensory neuron, integrating center, motor neuron, and effector, and classify the reflex as monosynaptic or polysynaptic."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Reflex arc components. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-stretch-reflex","name":"Muscle spindle and stretch reflex","can":"Explain how the muscle spindle detects stretch and trace the stretch reflex including reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The patellar tendon is tapped. Predict what the quadriceps and the hamstrings do.\nA) Both contract together and stiffen the knee\nB) Both relax and the leg drops\nC) The hamstrings contract and the quadriceps relax\nD) The quadriceps contracts and the hamstrings relax\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The quadriceps contracts and the hamstrings relax","why":"The tap stretches the quadriceps spindles, and their Ia fibers excite quadriceps motor neurons directly while inhibiting the hamstring motor neurons through an inhibitory interneuron, so the knee extends smoothly. Contraction of the hamstrings would be the reflex of a muscle that had been stretched, which the tap did not do. Co-contraction would occur only if reciprocal inhibition were blocked. Both muscles relaxing describes loss of the reflex, not the normal response."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many synapses in the spinal cord does reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist require?\nA) One, the same synapse the agonist uses\nB) At least two, because an inhibitory interneuron is inserted\nC) None, because the inhibition occurs at the muscle\nD) Three or more, because the signal must cross to the other side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) At least two, because an inhibitory interneuron is inserted","why":"The Ia fiber synapses on an inhibitory interneuron, which in turn synapses on the antagonist motor neuron, so this limb of the reflex is polysynaptic even though the agonist limb is monosynaptic. A single synapse would deliver excitation, since Ia afferents are excitatory. Inhibition of skeletal muscle never occurs at the muscle itself, only in the cord. The pathway stays on the same side, so no midline crossing is involved."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which spindle ending responds best to the rate at which a muscle is being stretched?\nA) The tendon organ on its group Ib fiber\nB) The free nerve ending in the muscle belly\nC) The secondary ending on its group II fiber\nD) The primary ending on its group Ia fiber\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The primary ending on its group Ia fiber","why":"The primary ending is strongly dynamic: it fires a burst proportional to how fast the muscle is lengthening, which is why a brisk tap works so much better than a slow pull. The secondary ending is mainly static and reports steady length. The tendon organ reports force and is not part of the spindle. Free nerve endings in muscle carry pain and metabolic signals, not length information."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a lesion that removes descending inhibition from the brain onto the spinal reflex circuits of the right leg. The alpha motor neurons, gamma motor neurons, muscle spindles, and Ia afferents of the right leg are intact.\nPredict what a tap on the right patellar tendon will produce, and why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"An exaggerated knee jerk, because the intact monosynaptic arc is no longer damped by descending inhibition","why":"The stretch reflex is a spinal loop and does not need the brain to work. Under normal conditions descending pathways hold the reflex in check. Take that braking away and the same Ia input drives a larger motor response, so the jerk is brisker than normal. This is the pattern seen after upper motor neuron lesions."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: During a reflex exam, a student cannot get a knee jerk from a relaxed subject. The instructor has the subject hook the fingers together and pull hard (the Jendrassik maneuver), and now the tap produces a clear kick. The subject reports no change in sensation over the leg.\nWhat is the most likely explanation for why the reflex appeared?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Descending activity during the effort raised gamma motor neuron drive and spinal motor neuron excitability, making the spindles more sensitive and the arc easier to bring to threshold","why":"The reinforcement maneuver works through the nervous system, not through mechanics. Voluntary effort elsewhere in the body raises descending excitatory drive to spinal circuits, including gamma motor neurons. Tighter intrafusal fibers make the spindle respond more strongly to the same tap, and more excitable alpha motor neurons reach threshold with that stronger Ia volley."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During a voluntary contraction, gamma motor neurons contract the ends of the intrafusal fibers so that the muscle spindle keeps firing even as the muscle shortens.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Alpha and gamma motor neurons are activated together in a voluntary movement. Without gamma coactivation the shortening muscle would unload the spindle, the Ia afferent would fall silent, and the nervous system would lose its length information in the middle of the movement. Gamma drive also sets how sensitive the spindle is, which is why it changes the size of a tendon jerk."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace what happens from the moment a reflex hammer taps the patellar tendon to the moment the leg kicks, including what happens to the hamstrings and why.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the tap stretches the quadriceps and its muscle spindles; spindle Ia afferents fire in proportion to the stretch; Ia afferents synapse directly on quadriceps alpha motor neurons in the spinal cord (monosynaptic); alpha motor neurons fire and the quadriceps contracts, extending the knee; Ia afferents also excite an inhibitory interneuron that inhibits hamstring motor neurons (reciprocal inhibition); the relaxed antagonist lets the kick happen without resistance","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a muscle with its extrafusal fibers and one spindle drawn in parallel with them, plus the antagonist muscle beside it. Stretch the muscle on your drawing, then trace the circuit from the spindle afferent into the cord and back out, showing both the excitatory path to its own muscle and the inhibitory interneuron to the antagonist. One line: what the limb would do if that inhibitory interneuron were missing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same spindle three times, at rest, during a sudden stretch, and during a shortening contraction with no gamma motor neuron activity. In each, show the spindle's own length and its firing rate. Add a fourth panel with gamma motor neurons firing alongside the alpha motor neurons. One line: what the gamma neurons are protecting the spindle from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the muscle spindle detects stretch and trace the stretch reflex including reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Muscle spindle and stretch reflex. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-golgi-tendon","name":"Golgi tendon organ","can":"Contrast the stimulus and the reflex response of the Golgi tendon organ with those of the muscle spindle.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle contracts hard isometrically against an immovable bar. Which receptor increases its firing the most?\nA) The muscle spindle, because muscle length rises\nB) Neither, because nothing moves\nC) Both equally, because both receptors sense force\nD) The Golgi tendon organ, because tension rises sharply\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The Golgi tendon organ, because tension rises sharply","why":"An isometric contraction changes force without changing whole muscle length, which is exactly the condition that loads a series receptor and leaves a parallel one nearly unchanged. Muscle length does not rise during an isometric contraction, so the spindle has no reason to fire more. The absence of movement does not mean the absence of a stimulus, since tension is a stimulus. The two receptors sense different variables, so treating them as equal misses the whole contrast."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which receptor most directly helps hold grip force steady while carrying a heavy bag?\nA) The Golgi tendon organ, which monitors force\nB) The muscle spindle, which monitors force\nC) The Golgi tendon organ, which monitors length\nD) The muscle spindle, which monitors joint angle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The Golgi tendon organ, which monitors force","why":"Steady force is a force problem, and the tendon organ is the receptor whose firing is proportional to the tension the muscle is producing, so it supplies the feedback that a force controller needs. The spindle does not monitor force, so pairing it with force is the common mix up. Length is the spindle variable and does not belong to the tendon organ. Joint angle is reported by joint receptors and again is not what a spindle measures directly."},{"t":"mc","q":"A receptor lies in series with the muscle fibers and inhibits its own muscle when it fires. Which receptor is described?\nA) The nociceptor in the muscle belly  |  B) The joint capsule receptor\nC) The muscle spindle  |  D) The Golgi tendon organ\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The Golgi tendon organ","why":"Series placement and autogenic inhibition are the two defining features of the tendon organ, and they belong together because a series receptor is the one loaded by force. A spindle is in parallel and excites its own muscle. A joint capsule receptor is outside the muscle entirely and reports position. A muscle nociceptor triggers withdrawal and pain responses and is not arranged in series to measure force."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a maximal lift the load suddenly feels as though it gives way and the muscle relaxes. Which reflex best explains this?\nA) Reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist muscle\nB) The stretch reflex driven by muscle spindles\nC) Autogenic inhibition driven by Golgi tendon organs\nD) The crossed extensor reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Autogenic inhibition driven by Golgi tendon organs","why":"At very high force the Ib input becomes strong enough to override the excitatory drive to the motor pool, and the muscle abruptly relaxes, unloading the tendon before it tears. The stretch reflex causes contraction, not sudden relaxation, so it cannot explain a give. Reciprocal inhibition acts on the opposing muscle and would not silence the one doing the lifting. The crossed extensor reflex involves the opposite limb and a painful stimulus."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When a Golgi tendon organ fires strongly, it causes the muscle it sits in to contract harder to protect the tendon.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A strongly firing Golgi tendon organ inhibits the alpha motor neurons of its own muscle through an interneuron, so the muscle relaxes and tension on the tendon falls.","why":"The tendon organ is a tension sensor in series with the muscle. High tension threatens the tendon, so the reflex is negative feedback: the Ib afferent excites an inhibitory interneuron, the motor pool is quieted, and force drops. Contracting harder would raise tension further and is the opposite of what the reflex does."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare what the muscle spindle and the Golgi tendon organ each detect, how their placement in the muscle makes that possible, and what reflex each one produces. Then explain what happens to the muscle if the tendon organ pathway is lost.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"spindle lies in parallel with extrafusal fibers and detects muscle length and rate of stretch; tendon organ lies in series with the muscle at the tendon and detects tension; spindle afferents excite the same muscle (stretch reflex); tendon organ afferents inhibit the same muscle through an inhibitory interneuron; the tendon organ reflex is negative feedback that limits force and protects the tendon; without it, force during a strong contraction is not braked and tendon injury is more likely","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one muscle with its tendon. Place the spindle and the Golgi tendon organ each in its correct physical position, one in parallel with the fibers and one in series at the tendon. Beside each, write the stimulus it actually detects and draw its reflex output back to that same muscle with a plus or a minus sign. One line: why something in series can report what a parallel structure cannot."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person holding a load with the elbow bent and still. Underneath, draw two firing rate traces on one shared time axis, the spindle afferent and the Golgi tendon organ afferent, as the load is slowly increased. Beside each, draw what its reflex is trying to make the muscle do. One line: which one wins if the load keeps climbing, and what you would see happen to the arm."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to contrast the stimulus and the reflex response of the Golgi tendon organ with those of the muscle spindle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Golgi tendon organ. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-withdrawal-reflex","name":"Withdrawal and crossed extensor reflexes","can":"Trace the flexor withdrawal reflex and the crossed extensor reflex and explain how the two together preserve balance.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person standing barefoot steps on a tack with the right foot. Predict what the left leg does.\nA) The left leg extends to bear the body weight\nB) The left leg flexes so that both feet lift\nC) The left leg does nothing until the person decides to shift\nD) The left leg relaxes completely\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The left leg extends to bear the body weight","why":"Crossed extensor output excites the contralateral extensors at the same time the right leg withdraws, so the left leg stiffens and supports the shifted weight. Flexing both legs would drop the person to the floor. Complete relaxation would collapse the supporting limb. The response is spinal and begins well before any voluntary decision can be made."},{"t":"mc","q":"A hand touches a hot pan. Which muscles are activated first in the withdrawal response?\nA) The elbow extensors, which push the hand away\nB) The shoulder abductors acting alone\nC) The flexors and extensors together, which hold the arm steady\nD) The elbow flexors, which pull the hand away\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The elbow flexors, which pull the hand away","why":"Withdrawal is a flexion pattern, and flexing the elbow is the fastest way to move the hand off the source of injury. Extending the elbow would drive the hand further into the pan. The shoulder often joins the movement, but it does so through the same spreading interneurons rather than acting on its own. Co-contraction would freeze the arm in contact with the heat."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which reflex depends on interneurons that cross to the opposite side of the spinal cord?\nA) The crossed extensor reflex  |  B) The Golgi tendon organ reflex\nC) The patellar stretch reflex  |  D) The flexor withdrawal reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The crossed extensor reflex","why":"Only the crossed extensor reflex sends its signal through commissural interneurons to motor pools on the other side of the body. The flexor withdrawal reflex is ipsilateral, even though it spans several segments. The stretch reflex acts on one muscle and its antagonist on the same side. The tendon organ reflex likewise stays within the same side of the cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which receptor starts the flexor withdrawal reflex?\nA) A Golgi tendon organ  |  B) A Pacinian corpuscle\nC) A nociceptor  |  D) A muscle spindle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A nociceptor","why":"Withdrawal is triggered by tissue damaging stimuli, and nociceptors are the free nerve endings that detect them. A muscle spindle detects stretch and starts the stretch reflex instead. A Golgi tendon organ detects tension and produces autogenic inhibition. A Pacinian corpuscle detects vibration and rapid pressure change, which does not by itself drive withdrawal."},{"t":"mc","q":"Is the flexor withdrawal reflex monosynaptic or polysynaptic?\nA) Polysynaptic\nB) Monosynaptic on the stimulated side and polysynaptic on the other side\nC) It uses no synapse within the spinal cord\nD) Monosynaptic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Polysynaptic","why":"Nociceptive afferents reach flexor motor neurons only through chains of interneurons, and those interneurons also spread the signal across several segments, so the arc is polysynaptic throughout. A monosynaptic arrangement is limited to the stretch reflex. There is no side of this reflex that is monosynaptic, since even the ipsilateral limb runs through interneurons. Every spinal reflex has at least one synapse in the cord, so a synapse free arc is not possible."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a standing person from the waist down, both legs, who has just stepped on something sharp with the right foot. Put the cord in the middle and trace the circuit to all four muscle groups, right flexors, right extensors, left flexors, left extensors. Mark each excited or inhibited and draw an interneuron everywhere one is needed. One line: what the left leg is doing, and why it has to happen at the same moment."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw this response twice, once complete and once with the crossed extensor half deleted. Show the body position a fraction of a second later in each drawing. Then count the synapses along the shortest path in your complete version. One line: using your own count, say why this reflex cannot be monosynaptic."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the flexor withdrawal reflex and the crossed extensor reflex and explain how the two together preserve balance."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Withdrawal and crossed extensor reflexes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-spinal-pathways","name":"Ascending and descending pathways","can":"Compare the dorsal column, spinothalamic, and corticospinal pathways by the information carried, the side of decussation, and the deficit produced by a lesion.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The dorsal column on the left side of the spinal cord is damaged at T6. Where is vibration sense lost?\nA) On both sides of the body below T6\nB) On the left side of the body above T6\nC) On the right side of the body below T6\nD) On the left side of the body below T6\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) On the left side of the body below T6","why":"Dorsal column fibers ascend on the same side they entered and do not cross until the medulla, so a lesion in the cord takes out sensation from the same side, below the level of the injury. A contralateral deficit would require the fibers to have crossed already, which happens only above the medulla. Segments above the lesion send their fibers into the column above the damage, so they are unaffected. A bilateral loss would require damage to both columns."},{"t":"mc","q":"The lateral corticospinal tract is damaged on the left side of the spinal cord at C7. Which side of the body is weak?\nA) The left side, above the level of the lesion\nB) The left side, below the level of the lesion\nC) Both sides equally\nD) The right side, below the level of the lesion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The left side, below the level of the lesion","why":"These fibers crossed at the pyramids in the medulla, well above the cord, so by the time they reach C7 they are on the same side as the muscles they control and the weakness is ipsilateral to the cord lesion. A contralateral weakness would require the crossing to happen below C7, which it does not. Segments above the lesion receive their descending fibers before the damage. A bilateral deficit would require both lateral columns to be involved."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stroke damages corticospinal fibers in the right internal capsule, above the pyramids. Which side of the body is weak?\nA) The left side of the body\nB) The right side of the body\nC) Neither side, because the fibers have not yet crossed\nD) Both sides of the body equally\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The left side of the body","why":"The internal capsule lies above the pyramidal decussation, so fibers there still have their crossing ahead of them and control the opposite side of the body, giving a contralateral weakness. An ipsilateral weakness would be the answer for a lesion in the cord, below the crossing. A single sided capsular lesion cannot weaken both sides. The fact that the fibers have not yet crossed is exactly why the deficit appears on the other side, not why it is absent."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a lesion confined to the right dorsal column of the spinal cord at the T6 level. Strength is normal in all four limbs.\nPredict the sensory findings below the lesion.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Loss of vibration and position sense in the right leg, with pain and temperature intact on both sides","why":"The dorsal column pathway carries fine touch, vibration and proprioception up the same side of the cord it entered and does not cross until the medulla. A right dorsal column lesion therefore removes those senses from the right side below the lesion. The spinothalamic tract runs in a different part of the cord and is untouched, so pain and temperature are preserved."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a small lesion in the left side of the medulla that damages the corticospinal tract above the point where it crosses. Sensory pathways are spared.\nPredict the motor deficit and explain the side.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Weakness on the right side of the body, because the tract has not yet crossed at the level of the lesion","why":"Corticospinal fibers from the left cortex control the right side of the body and cross at the lower medulla. A lesion above that crossing removes the fibers while they are still on the left, so the muscles that lose their command are on the right. Lesions below the crossing, in the cord, give same side weakness."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature and crosses to the opposite side within a segment or two of where it enters the spinal cord.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The dorsal column pathway for fine touch, vibration and position sense stays on the same side until it crosses in the medulla, and the corticospinal tract crosses in the medulla on its way down. The spinothalamic tract crosses early. This difference in crossing level is why a hemisection of the cord gives a split pattern of deficits, with weakness and vibration loss on one side and pain loss on the other."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a lesion of the left half of the spinal cord produces some deficits on the left side of the body and others on the right. Name the pathways involved and where each one crosses.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"dorsal column pathway carries fine touch, vibration and proprioception and crosses in the medulla; spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature and crosses near its entry level in the cord; corticospinal tract carries voluntary motor commands and crosses in the medulla; below the lesion, weakness and loss of vibration and position sense are on the same side (left); pain and temperature loss is on the opposite side (right); the side of a deficit tells you whether the pathway had already crossed at the level of the lesion","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a tall diagram with spinal cord at the bottom, medulla in the middle, and cortex on top. Run three tracts up or down it, dorsal column, spinothalamic, and corticospinal. Mark where each one crosses the midline and put an arrow on each showing which way information travels. Beside each, write what it carries. One line: two of them cross at very different levels, say why that matters at the bedside."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a spinal cord cross section and cut clean through the left half only. Below it, draw a body outline and shade what is lost on each side, using a different mark for fine touch and position sense, for pain and temperature, and for voluntary movement. One line: name the finding on your drawing that tells you the lesion is in the cord and not in the brain."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the dorsal column, spinothalamic, and corticospinal pathways by the information carried, the side of decussation, and the deficit produced by a lesion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ascending and descending pathways. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-csf-bbb","name":"Cerebrospinal fluid and the blood brain barrier","can":"State the functions of cerebrospinal fluid and explain how the blood brain barrier selects what reaches neurons, including why lipid soluble drugs cross readily.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The cerebral aqueduct becomes blocked. Predict what happens.\nA) CSF is rerouted through the arachnoid granulations with no change in pressure\nB) CSF drains into the spinal canal and the brain shrinks\nC) CSF production stops at once, so pressure falls\nD) CSF accumulates above the block and pressure rises in the ventricles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) CSF accumulates above the block and pressure rises in the ventricles","why":"Choroid plexus secretion continues largely independent of pressure, so fluid keeps entering the ventricles upstream of the block while it can no longer reach the absorption sites, and the ventricles distend. Production does not shut off promptly when pressure rises, which is why obstruction is dangerous. Reaching the granulations requires passing the block, so no rerouting is available. Drainage downward is also blocked, so the brain tissue is compressed rather than allowed to shrink."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid produced?\nA) By the pia mater covering the cortex\nB) At the choroid plexus in the ventricles\nC) At the arachnoid granulations\nD) By the endothelium of the dural venous sinuses\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) At the choroid plexus in the ventricles","why":"Choroid plexus epithelium actively secretes CSF into the ventricles at roughly 500 mL per day. Arachnoid granulations are where the fluid is absorbed, the opposite end of the circuit. The pia is a thin covering membrane and does not secrete the fluid. The dural venous sinuses receive CSF rather than make it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these is a function of cerebrospinal fluid?\nA) It establishes the resting membrane potential of central neurons\nB) It supplies the main metabolic fuel used by neurons in the cortex\nC) It delivers oxygen to neurons in place of the cerebral blood supply\nD) It supports the brain so that its effective weight is greatly reduced\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It supports the brain so that its effective weight is greatly reduced","why":"Floating in CSF reduces the effective weight of the brain from about 1400 g to roughly 50 g, which protects the base of the brain and its vessels from being crushed. Oxygen is delivered by blood through cerebral capillaries, not by CSF. Resting membrane potential is set by ion gradients and membrane permeability at each cell. Glucose is the fuel and arrives through the blood, although CSF composition does help keep the neuronal environment stable."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does cerebrospinal fluid return to the blood?\nA) Across the blood brain barrier into the brain capillaries\nB) At the capillary beds of the choroid plexus in the ventricles\nC) At the arachnoid granulations projecting into the dural sinuses\nD) Through lymphatic vessels running within the pia mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At the arachnoid granulations projecting into the dural sinuses","why":"Arachnoid granulations act as one way valves that let CSF pass into venous blood once CSF pressure exceeds venous pressure. Choroid plexus capillaries supply the fluid that becomes CSF, so flow there runs the other way. The blood brain barrier restricts movement between blood and brain tissue and is not the bulk drainage route. Classic lymphatic vessels do not run in the pia."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with a bacterial infection of the meninges is treated with an antibiotic that normally crosses the blood brain barrier poorly. Her CSF drug level on day 2 is 40 percent of the plasma level (expected in healthy adults less than 5 percent). By day 10, as the inflammation resolves, her CSF drug level has fallen to 8 percent of plasma while the dose is unchanged.\nWhat best explains the change between day 2 and day 10?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Inflammation loosened endothelial tight junctions and opened a paracellular route; as the junctions resealed, the drug was excluded again","why":"The barrier is the tight junctions between brain capillary endothelial cells. Inflammation makes those junctions leaky, so a water soluble drug that normally cannot pass between the cells slips through. As the infection resolves and the junctions tighten, the paracellular route closes and the drug level in the CSF drops back toward its usual low value."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the blood brain barrier decides which substances in the blood reach neurons, and use it to predict why a lipid soluble sedative acts on the brain within minutes while a water soluble antibiotic barely gets in.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"brain capillary endothelial cells are joined by tight junctions; tight junctions block the paracellular route that most capillaries allow; substances must cross the endothelial cell membranes (transcellular route); lipid soluble molecules dissolve through membranes and enter freely; polar or water soluble molecules need a specific transporter (for example glucose) or are excluded; the sedative crosses by diffusion; the antibiotic has no transporter and stays out","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the brain in section with the ventricles, and trace cerebrospinal fluid with arrows all the way from where it is made to where it is reabsorbed. Label the choroid plexus and the arachnoid villi. Beside the drawing, write the total volume, about 150 mL, and the amount produced per day, about 500 mL. One line: what that turnover rate means for a patient with a blockage in the loop."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a brain capillary in cross section beside a capillary from elsewhere in the body, same scale. Show the junctions between endothelial cells in each and the astrocyte feet on the brain one. Now send four things at the brain capillary, oxygen, glucose, a large plasma protein, and a lipid soluble anesthetic. Draw how each gets through or fails. One line: why the drug that crosses easiest is also the hardest to keep out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the functions of cerebrospinal fluid and explain how the blood brain barrier selects what reaches neurons, including why lipid soluble drugs cross readily."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cerebrospinal fluid and the blood brain barrier. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w5-lab-reflex-testing","name":"Reflex testing and reaction time","can":"Elicit and grade deep tendon reflexes and measure reaction time to distinguish a reflex response from a voluntary response by latency.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A ruler is caught after falling 20 cm. Using d equal to one half g t squared, with g of 9.8 m/s squared, what is the reaction time?\nA) About 0.45 s  |  B) About 0.20 s\nC) About 0.02 s  |  D) About 2.0 s\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 0.20 s","why":"Rearranging gives t equal to the square root of two times 0.20 m divided by 9.8, which is the square root of about 0.041, or roughly 0.20 s, a typical simple reaction time. About 0.02 s would be shorter than a spinal reflex latency and is physically impossible for a visual response. About 0.45 s corresponds to a drop of roughly a meter. About 2.0 s is off by a factor of ten and would require a fall of nearly 20 m."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an absent ankle jerk while the knee jerk is a normal 2+. What does this pattern suggest?\nA) A normal variant that carries no localizing information\nB) An upper motor neuron lesion above the lumbar segments\nC) A problem in the S1 reflex arc\nD) A problem in the L2 to L4 reflex arc\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A problem in the S1 reflex arc","why":"Each tendon jerk tests a defined segment, so an isolated loss at the ankle with a normal knee jerk points at S1 and spares L2 to L4. A problem at L2 to L4 would show up as a diminished knee jerk instead. An upper motor neuron lesion removes descending inhibition and makes reflexes brisker, not absent. The pattern is segmental and therefore localizing, which is the reason reflexes are tested at several levels."},{"t":"mc","q":"A reflex appears absent on repeated testing. What should be tried before recording it as 0?\nA) Testing the opposite limb and recording that value instead\nB) Reinforcement with the Jendrassik maneuver\nC) Tapping the muscle belly instead of the tendon\nD) Asking the subject to contract the muscle during the tap\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Reinforcement with the Jendrassik maneuver","why":"Reinforcement raises motor pool excitability and often brings out a reflex that a tense or inattentive subject was suppressing, so it must be tried before recording a grade of 0. Tapping the muscle belly stretches the fibers differently and does not give a standard tendon jerk. A voluntary contraction during the tap contaminates the response and makes the reflex impossible to grade. The opposite limb is worth comparing but cannot substitute for the value on the side being tested."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is a choice reaction time longer than a simple reaction time?\nA) Nerve fibers conduct more slowly when two choices are present\nB) The receptor needs longer to detect a stimulus when there are two of them\nC) The added decision step requires more central processing time\nD) The muscle takes longer to contract for a chosen movement\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The added decision step requires more central processing time","why":"Both tasks share the same conduction and contraction times, so the extra 100 ms or so of a choice task comes from selecting between responses in the brain. Conduction velocity is fixed by fiber diameter and myelination and does not depend on the task. Contraction time is a property of the muscle and is unchanged. Receptor transduction takes the same few milliseconds regardless of how many stimuli might appear."},{"t":"mc","q":"On the standard deep tendon reflex scale, which grade is recorded for a normal response?\nA) 4+  |  B) 2+\nC) 0  |  D) 1+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 2+","why":"The scale runs from 0 to 4+, and 2+ is the expected brisk but not exaggerated response. A grade of 0 means no response at all even with reinforcement. A grade of 1+ means present but diminished, which is below normal though not always abnormal. A grade of 4+ means very brisk with clonus and points to loss of descending inhibition."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how measuring the delay from stimulus to response lets you tell a spinal reflex from a voluntary response, and give the approximate timing you expect for each.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"a monosynaptic spinal reflex takes only a few tens of milliseconds (about 20 to 40 ms); the reflex delay is conduction to the cord, one synapse, and conduction back to the muscle; a voluntary response takes well over 100 ms (typically 150 to 250 ms); the extra time is ascending pathway, cortical processing and descending command; anticipation of the stimulus shortens a voluntary reaction time and invalidates the measurement; reflex latency does not depend on attention, but reaction time does","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a horizontal grading scale for deep tendon reflexes from 0 to 4 with every grade labeled. Mark where your own measured reflexes fell and where a hyperreflexic patient would fall. Beside it, draw the arc you were actually testing with its synapses shown. One line: a 0 and a 4 point at damage in different places, say which is which and how you would tell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis in milliseconds. Mark the stimulus, then plot two responses on it using your own numbers, your measured tendon reflex latency and your measured reaction time from the ruler drop. Under each, draw the path that signal took and count the structures it passed through. One line: what accounts for the gap between the two, judging from your own drawings."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Reflex testing and reaction time. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"General Sensory Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w7-transduction","name":"Sensory transduction","can":"Explain how a stimulus is converted into a receptor potential and how that graded potential becomes a train of action potentials in the sensory neuron.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A pressure stimulus on the skin doubles in strength. What changes in the sensory axon?\nA) The action potentials come at a higher frequency\nB) The action potentials become taller\nC) The action potentials travel faster along the axon\nD) The action potentials last longer in duration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The action potentials come at a higher frequency","why":"A stronger stimulus makes a larger receptor potential, which drives the trigger zone back to threshold sooner after each spike, so spikes come closer together. Height is fixed by the all or none rule, and both conduction velocity and spike duration are set by axon diameter, myelination, and channel kinetics, not by how hard the skin is pressed."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sensory neuron is firing at its maximum rate, and further increases in stimulus strength do not raise that rate. What sets the ceiling?\nA) The absolute refractory period of the axon\nB) The size of the receptor potential\nC) The number of mechanically gated channels in the ending\nD) The conduction velocity of the axon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The absolute refractory period of the axon","why":"During the absolute refractory period the voltage gated Na+ channels are inactivated and no stimulus of any size can produce another spike, so the refractory period puts a hard ceiling on frequency. The receptor potential and channel number set how quickly the neuron climbs toward that ceiling, not where the ceiling sits. Conduction velocity governs how fast a spike travels, not how often one can be launched."},{"t":"mc","q":"In sensory physiology, what does transduction mean?\nA) Conversion of stimulus energy into a change in membrane potential\nB) Propagation of an action potential along a sensory axon toward the cord\nC) Release of neurotransmitter onto a second order neuron\nD) Amplification of a weak signal by the somatosensory cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Conversion of stimulus energy into a change in membrane potential","why":"Transduction happens at the receptor membrane, where pressure, light, heat, or a chemical opens or closes ion channels and changes the voltage across that membrane. Propagation comes later, after the graded potential reaches the trigger zone. Transmitter release is the synaptic handoff further along the pathway, and cortical processing is central, not a receptor event."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does the size of a receptor potential change as the stimulus gets stronger?\nA) It stays fixed, because the response is all or none\nB) It gets larger in proportion to the stimulus\nC) It gets smaller as the receptor is loaded\nD) It stays flat until threshold and then jumps\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It gets larger in proportion to the stimulus","why":"Receptor potentials are graded: a stronger stimulus opens more channels and produces a larger depolarization. All or none describes the action potential, not the receptor potential. Nothing about a stronger stimulus shrinks the response, and the sudden jump at threshold belongs to the trigger zone, not to the graded potential itself."},{"t":"mc","q":"In most somatosensory receptors, which ion movement produces the depolarizing receptor potential?\nA) K+ moving out of the cell  |  B) Cl- moving into the cell\nC) Ca2+ being pumped out of the cell  |  D) Na+ moving into the cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Na+ moving into the cell","why":"Stimulus energy opens nonselective cation channels, and because the electrochemical gradient for Na+ is large and inward, Na+ entry dominates and depolarizes the ending. K+ leaving and Cl- entering both drive the membrane away from threshold, so they hyperpolarize instead. Pumping Ca2+ out is a recovery process and carries no significant part of the receptor potential."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how pressing on the skin over a touch receptor ends up as action potentials traveling toward the spinal cord. Include what happens at the receptor ending and what happens at the trigger zone, and say how a harder press is represented.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"pressure deforms the ending and opens mechanically gated cation channels; inward current produces a graded depolarization, the receptor potential; receptor potential size depends on stimulus strength and it can sum; the receptor potential spreads to the trigger zone (first node of Ranvier); if threshold is reached, voltage gated Na+ channels open and action potentials fire; a larger receptor potential gives a higher action potential frequency, not bigger action potentials","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one sensory neuron stretched across your space, from its receptor ending to a point well along the axon. Above it, draw the membrane potential recorded at three places, the receptor ending, the trigger zone, and further down the axon. Label which traces are graded and which are all or none, and mark threshold. One line: what happens to the information at the trigger zone that cannot be undone afterwards."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three stimulus strengths as three bars of different height on a time axis, weak, medium, and strong. Under each, draw the receptor potential it produces, and under that, the train of action potentials leaving the trigger zone. Keep every spike exactly the same height. One line: since the spikes are all identical, name what actually carries the message that the third stimulus was the strongest."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how a stimulus is converted into a receptor potential and how that graded potential becomes a train of action potentials in the sensory neuron."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Sensory transduction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-receptor-classes","name":"Receptor classification","can":"Classify a receptor by stimulus type as a mechanoreceptor, chemoreceptor, thermoreceptor, photoreceptor, or nociceptor and give a body location for each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Hair cells in the cochlea are bent by fluid movement set up by sound waves. How should they be classified?\nA) As mechanoreceptors  |  B) As chemoreceptors\nC) As photoreceptors  |  D) As nociceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) As mechanoreceptors","why":"Sound reaches the hair cell as physical motion of fluid and membrane, and bending the stereocilia pulls open mechanically gated channels, so the adequate stimulus is deformation. Classify by the energy the receptor actually transduces, not by the everyday name of the sense. The chemical synapse the hair cell makes onto the auditory nerve comes after transduction and does not make it a chemoreceptor."},{"t":"mc","q":"Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus fire when plasma osmolarity rises. What are they responding to?\nA) The temperature of the blood perfusing the hypothalamus\nB) Damage to the neurons caused by concentrated plasma\nC) Light reaching the hypothalamus through the retinohypothalamic tract\nD) Cell shrinkage, which deforms the membrane and opens ion channels\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cell shrinkage, which deforms the membrane and opens ion channels","why":"When osmolarity rises, water leaves the osmoreceptor cell, the cell shrinks, and the membrane deformation opens mechanically gated channels, so the transduction step is mechanical even though the variable being monitored is chemical. This is the same lesson as the baroreceptor: the measured quantity and the transduced energy can differ. No damage, temperature change, or light is involved in the response."},{"t":"mc","q":"Muscle spindles fire when a muscle is stretched, reporting muscle length to the CNS. Which class do they belong to?\nA) Chemoreceptors  |  B) Nociceptors\nC) Mechanoreceptors  |  D) Thermoreceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Mechanoreceptors","why":"Stretch deforms the spindle ending and opens mechanically gated channels, so the spindle is a mechanoreceptor even though it lies deep in muscle rather than in skin. Location does not determine class. Muscle also contains chemoreceptors that report metabolites and nociceptors that report damage, so all three classes share the tissue but respond to different energies."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person steps barefoot on a tack. Which receptor class is activated by the puncture itself?\nA) Photoreceptors  |  B) Nociceptors\nC) Chemoreceptors  |  D) Thermoreceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Nociceptors","why":"A puncture is mechanical energy at a damaging intensity, and that is exactly the definition of a nociceptor stimulus, so the high threshold free nerve endings fire. Low threshold mechanoreceptors in the sole also fire, which is why the foot is felt as well as hurt, but they are not what makes it painful. Chemoreceptors join in a few seconds later as damaged cells release K+ and bradykinin, and temperature and light play no part."},{"t":"mc","q":"What kind of stimulus does a mechanoreceptor transduce?\nA) A change in local temperature\nB) A change in the concentration of a dissolved chemical in the tissue\nC) Physical deformation such as pressure, stretch, or vibration\nD) Tissue damage severe enough to be felt as pain\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Physical deformation such as pressure, stretch, or vibration","why":"Mechanoreceptors have channels that open when the membrane is physically pulled or pushed, so pressure, stretch, and vibration are all adequate stimuli for them. Temperature is the domain of thermoreceptors, dissolved molecules of chemoreceptors, and damaging stimuli of nociceptors. Classification is always by the form of energy the receptor is tuned to, not by where in the body it sits."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline and place one real receptor from each of the five classes on it, mechanoreceptor, chemoreceptor, thermoreceptor, photoreceptor, and nociceptor. Put each where it genuinely exists, inside the body as well as on the surface. Beside each, draw the stimulus arriving as an arrow and name the form of energy it carries. One line: which class is spread over the most locations, and what that says about its job."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a moment, drinking something hot, walking into the cold, or one of your own. Draw the scene and mark every receptor class firing during it, with an arrow from each stimulus to its receptor. Some classes will appear more than once at different sites. One line: name one class that is not firing in your scene, and the change that would recruit it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a receptor by stimulus type as a mechanoreceptor, chemoreceptor, thermoreceptor, photoreceptor, or nociceptor and give a body location for each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Receptor classification. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-stimulus-coding","name":"Stimulus coding","can":"Explain how the nervous system encodes modality, location, intensity, and duration and identify the labeled line principle.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"As a pinprick becomes progressively sharper, receptors that were silent begin to fire. What is this called?\nA) Recruitment, the basis of population coding\nB) Adaptation, the decline of firing during a steady stimulus\nC) Lateral inhibition, the sharpening of a stimulus border\nD) Convergence, the pooling of afferents onto one central neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Recruitment, the basis of population coding","why":"Receptors differ in threshold, so raising intensity brings the higher threshold ones above their thresholds and the active population grows, which is recruitment. Adaptation runs the other way, reducing firing during a maintained stimulus. Lateral inhibition sharpens where a stimulus is, and convergence is an anatomical arrangement that blurs location rather than a code for intensity."},{"t":"mc","q":"In sensory coding, what does the term modality refer to?\nA) The strength of the sensation the person reports\nB) The type of sensation, such as touch, cold, or pain\nC) The part of the body where the sensation is felt\nD) The length of time the sensation lasts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The type of sensation, such as touch, cold, or pain","why":"Modality is the what of a stimulus, the qualitative category the brain assigns it, and it is set by which pathway carries the signal. Strength is intensity, place is location, and length is duration, and the nervous system codes each of those four features by a different means. Keeping the four straight is the whole point of this competency."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does a single sensory neuron code the intensity of a stimulus?\nA) By the height of its action potentials\nB) By the conduction velocity of its axon\nC) By the duration of each action potential\nD) By its firing frequency\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) By its firing frequency","why":"Stronger stimuli make a larger receptor potential, the trigger zone returns to threshold sooner, and spikes come faster, so intensity lives in spikes per second. Spike height and duration are fixed by the all or none properties of the membrane, and conduction velocity is set by axon diameter and myelination, all of which stay constant while intensity changes."},{"t":"mc","q":"Besides firing frequency, what is the second way the nervous system codes a stronger stimulus?\nA) The action potentials grow larger in the strongest fibers\nB) The stimulus is switched onto a faster conducting pathway instead\nC) More receptors are recruited, so more afferent fibers are active\nD) The refractory period shortens so spikes can overlap\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) More receptors are recruited, so more afferent fibers are active","why":"A stronger stimulus spreads farther and reaches receptors with higher thresholds, so the population of active afferents grows, and this population code works alongside frequency coding. Spike amplitude never changes with intensity, pathways are fixed rather than switched by strength, and the refractory period is a property of the channels and does not shorten with a bigger stimulus."},{"t":"mc","q":"How is the duration of a stimulus coded in a slowly adapting receptor?\nA) By the total number of afferent fibers recruited\nB) By the size of the first action potential in the train\nC) By the conduction velocity of the afferent fiber\nD) By how long the train of action potentials continues\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) By how long the train of action potentials continues","why":"A tonic receptor keeps firing for as long as the stimulus is applied, so the length of the train maps onto the length of the stimulus. The number of fibers recruited codes intensity, spike size is fixed, and conduction velocity does not vary with how long a stimulus is held. Rapidly adapting receptors give up this duration information, which is the tradeoff for reporting change sharply."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A researcher places a fine electrode on the median nerve at the wrist and stimulates a single cutaneous afferent that normally serves the tip of the index finger. At 10 pulses per second the subject reports a light tap on the tip of the index finger. At 50 pulses per second the subject reports a firm press at the same spot. Nothing is touching the finger.\nWhich coding principles explain the two reports?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Location and modality come from which line is active (labeled line), and intensity comes from firing frequency","why":"The CNS does not know where along the axon a spike started. It reads activity in that afferent as touch at the fingertip because that is where the line normally comes from and where its cortical target is mapped. Firing frequency is read as intensity, so more pulses per second feel like a harder press. Nothing about the receptor or the finger changed; only the traffic on the line changed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four panels in a row, one each for modality, location, intensity, and duration. In each, draw the actual mechanism, a labeled line running to a specific cortical area, a receptive field on the skin, a firing rate, and a firing pattern held over time. Use spikes on a time axis wherever a rate is the answer. One line: which of the four still works if you stimulate the nerve trunk directly with an electrode."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw what happens when you press hard on your own closed eyelid. Trace the path from the pressed photoreceptors to the cortical area that receives them, and draw what the person actually perceives. Beside it, draw the same mechanical pressure applied to the back of the hand and what that produces. One line: the same stimulus gave two different sensations, say what decided which one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the nervous system encodes modality, location, intensity, and duration and identify the labeled line principle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Stimulus coding. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-receptive-fields","name":"Receptive fields and acuity","can":"Relate receptive field size and lateral inhibition to two point discrimination and predict which body regions have the finest acuity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the receptive field of a sensory neuron?\nA) The region of the body where a stimulus changes that neuron's firing\nB) The region of cortex where the signal from that neuron finally arrives\nC) The range of stimulus intensities the neuron can code\nD) The range of modalities the neuron is able to transduce\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The region of the body where a stimulus changes that neuron's firing","why":"A receptive field is a patch of skin, or of retina, and stimulating anywhere in that patch alters the firing of the one neuron that owns it. The cortical destination is a separate idea, the target of the labeled line rather than the field itself. Intensity range and modality are properties of the receptor, not a description of where it listens."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does a two point discrimination test measure?\nA) The smallest force that can be detected anywhere on the skin\nB) The time between two touches needed to feel them as separate\nC) The smallest separation at which two simultaneous touches are felt as two\nD) The number of touch receptors present in a square centimeter of skin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The smallest separation at which two simultaneous touches are felt as two","why":"The examiner sets two points a known distance apart, touches the skin with both at once, and finds the distance at which the subject stops feeling one point and starts feeling two, which is the two point threshold. The smallest detectable force is the touch threshold, a different measurement. Temporal separation and receptor counts are related ideas but neither is what this test reports."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does receptive field size relate to tactile acuity?\nA) Larger receptive fields give finer acuity\nB) Smaller receptive fields give finer acuity\nC) Receptive field size has no effect on acuity\nD) Acuity is best when fields are of medium size\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Smaller receptive fields give finer acuity","why":"If each afferent watches a tiny patch of skin, two nearby points are likely to fall in two different fields and activate two different lines, which the brain reads as two points. A large field swallows both points into one line and they are felt as one. This single relationship is what makes fingertips sharp and the back coarse."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does receptor density in a patch of skin relate to two point threshold?\nA) Higher density gives a larger two point threshold\nB) Higher density gives a smaller two point threshold\nC) Density affects sensitivity to force but not two point threshold\nD) Density and two point threshold are unrelated\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Higher density gives a smaller two point threshold","why":"Packing more afferents into the same area means each one covers less skin, so two nearby points are more likely to stimulate separate afferents and be felt separately, and the threshold distance shrinks. Density and field size are two ways of describing the same anatomy. Density does also affect force sensitivity, but that is an additional effect and not a substitute for this one."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab a student records two point thresholds: lip 2 mm, fingertip 3 mm, forearm 35 mm. A classmate has just had a partial nerve block of one fingertip. About half of the afferents from that fingertip are silenced, but the fingertip still clearly feels a single touch.\nPredict the classmate's two point threshold on the blocked fingertip and explain.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Higher than 3 mm, because with fewer working afferents two nearby points are more likely to fall on the same surviving line and be felt as one","why":"Two point discrimination needs two points to activate two separate afferents. When half the lines are silenced, the surviving lines are spread farther apart, so the points must be moved farther apart before they land on different ones. The skin still feels touch, which is why a threshold can be measured, but it is coarser. Cortex can only separate points the periphery reports separately."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The back has a smaller two point threshold than the fingertip, because the back has more skin over which to spread its receptors.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The fingertip has the smaller two point threshold, because its receptors have small receptive fields and are densely packed, so two nearby points land on separate afferents.","why":"Two point threshold tracks receptive field size. Two points inside one receptive field stimulate one afferent and are felt as one point. The fingertip has many small fields packed together, so points a few millimeters apart are felt as two. The back has few, large fields, so points must be several centimeters apart. Skin area has nothing to do with it; what matters is how many separate lines a patch of skin sends."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two patches of skin side by side, a fingertip and the middle of the back, with the sensory neuron receptive fields on each drawn to scale as circles. Set the same pair of caliper points on both and show why one is felt as two points and the other as one. Write your own measured thresholds in millimeters on each patch. One line: what would have to change on the back to make it match the fingertip."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three sensory neurons in a row receiving a stimulus that is strongest on the middle one. Add the inhibitory interneurons that connect them sideways. Then draw the firing rate of all three as bars, once before the lateral connections act and once after. One line: what the sharpened pattern lets the brain do that the raw pattern did not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate receptive field size and lateral inhibition to two point discrimination and predict which body regions have the finest acuity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Receptive fields and acuity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-receptor-adaptation","name":"Receptor adaptation","can":"Distinguish tonic from phasic receptors by their adaptation rate and match each to a stimulus the body needs to monitor continuously or only at onset.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Imagine a hand in which every mechanoreceptor is phasic and none are tonic. Which everyday task becomes hardest?\nA) Feeling a fly land on the skin\nB) Detecting the buzz of a phone in the palm\nC) Noticing the exact moment a held object begins to slip in the hand\nD) Holding a glass with steady grip force without watching it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Holding a glass with steady grip force without watching it","why":"Grip force control depends on a continuous report of how hard the fingers are pressing, and with only rapidly adapting receptors that report vanishes a moment after the grasp is stable, so the glass is squeezed or dropped without visual feedback. The other three tasks are all detections of change, which is precisely what phasic receptors do best. This is the clearest demonstration of why the body keeps both types rather than choosing one."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does receptor adaptation mean?\nA) A rise in firing rate as a stimulus is held constant\nB) A decline in firing rate during a stimulus that is held constant\nC) A change in the modality a receptor reports over time\nD) A shift of the receptor's response to a different body region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A decline in firing rate during a stimulus that is held constant","why":"Adaptation is the fall in action potential frequency that occurs while the stimulus itself stays the same, so the receptor stops reporting a condition it has already reported. Firing that increases during a steady stimulus is sensitization, which happens at nociceptors during inflammation. Modality is fixed by the labeled line, and receptive field position is anatomical, so neither drifts with time."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does a tonic receptor behave during a maintained stimulus?\nA) It fires once at onset and then falls silent\nB) It fires only when the stimulus is removed\nC) It adapts within a few milliseconds and then stops firing entirely\nD) It adapts slowly and fires for as long as the stimulus lasts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It adapts slowly and fires for as long as the stimulus lasts","why":"Tonic receptors are the slowly adapting group, and their sustained firing is what lets the CNS know a condition is still present, though the rate may drift down somewhat. Firing only at onset, only at offset, or for a few milliseconds all describe phasic receptors. The distinction between the two groups is the rate of adaptation, not the strength of the response."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does a phasic receptor behave during a maintained stimulus?\nA) It fires at the onset, adapts quickly, and often fires again at the offset\nB) It fires steadily from onset until the stimulus is removed\nC) It fires with slowly increasing frequency the longer the stimulus lasts\nD) It fires only if the stimulus lasts longer than one second\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It fires at the onset, adapts quickly, and often fires again at the offset","why":"Phasic receptors are rapidly adapting and are built to report change, so they signal that something started and that something stopped and stay quiet in between. Steady firing throughout is the tonic pattern. Firing that grows over time would be sensitization, and phasic receptors respond best to brief and changing stimuli rather than requiring long ones."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these is a slowly adapting, tonic receptor?\nA) The Pacinian corpuscle  |  B) The Meissner corpuscle\nC) The muscle spindle  |  D) The olfactory receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The muscle spindle","why":"The muscle spindle reports muscle length continuously, which posture requires, so it must adapt slowly. Pacinian and Meissner corpuscles are both rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors tuned to vibration and to moving light touch, and olfactory receptors adapt so quickly that a smell fades within minutes. The match is always between how long the body needs the information and how slowly the receptor adapts."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Muscle spindles are phasic receptors, so they stop firing when a muscle is held at a constant length.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Muscle spindles are tonic receptors that keep firing at a constant length, so the CNS always knows where the limb is.","why":"Limb position must be known continuously, not just when it changes, so spindles adapt very little. Phasic receptors such as Pacinian corpuscles and olfactory receptors are the ones that fall silent during a steady stimulus. A phasic spindle would leave you with no idea where your arm was once it stopped moving."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one stimulus bar on a time axis, held perfectly steady from start to finish. Under it, on the same axis, draw two firing rate traces responding to that same stimulus, a tonic receptor and a phasic receptor. Show clearly where the phasic one falls silent while the stimulus is still on, and mark any response when it switches off. Name a real receptor for each trace. One line: what the phasic receptor is reporting that the tonic one is not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick two things the body monitors, one it needs to know continuously and one it only needs at the moment of change. Choose your own, not the shirt on your skin. Draw the receptor you would assign to each and its firing over a long steady stimulus. Then swap the two assignments and draw what goes wrong in each case. One line: which swap is actually dangerous, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish tonic from phasic receptors by their adaptation rate and match each to a stimulus the body needs to monitor continuously or only at onset."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Receptor adaptation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-somatosensory-pathways","name":"Somatosensory pathways","can":"Trace touch and pain information from receptor to cortex and explain why the somatosensory homunculus is disproportionate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A pin is felt in the right hand. Which hemisphere processes it, and why?\nA) The left hemisphere, because the pathway crossed in the spinal cord\nB) The right hemisphere, because pain pathways stay on the same side\nC) The left hemisphere, because pain pathways cross in the thalamus\nD) Both hemispheres equally, because pain is not localized to one side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The left hemisphere, because the pathway crossed in the spinal cord","why":"Pain from the right hand enters the right dorsal horn, the second order axon crosses within a segment or two, and the spinothalamic tract ascends on the left to the left thalamus and left cortex. Pain pathways do cross, so a same side answer is wrong, and the crossing happens in the cord rather than in the thalamus, which relays without crossing. Pain localization is imprecise, but it is still lateralized."},{"t":"mc","q":"The left dorsal column is damaged at the C5 level. Which deficit should you predict?\nA) Loss of pain and temperature in the left arm\nB) Loss of vibration and fine touch in the left arm\nC) Loss of vibration and fine touch in the right arm\nD) Loss of all sensation in both arms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Loss of vibration and fine touch in the left arm","why":"Dorsal column fibers ascend on the same side until they reach the medulla, so a lesion below that crossing point causes a deficit on the same side as the damage and below the level of the injury. Pain and temperature are spared because they have already crossed and are traveling in the spinothalamic tract on the other side. A contralateral deficit would require the lesion to be above the medullary crossing."},{"t":"mc","q":"The lips occupy a far larger strip of somatosensory cortex than the entire back. What does that reflect?\nA) The lips move more often than the back\nB) The lips are closer to the brain than the back\nC) The lips contain more muscle tissue per gram than the back does\nD) The lips send far more afferent fibers per square centimeter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The lips send far more afferent fibers per square centimeter","why":"Cortical territory scales with the number of incoming lines, and densely innervated skin with small receptive fields needs many cortical neurons to keep those lines separate. Movement frequency belongs to the motor homunculus rather than the sensory one, distance to the brain is irrelevant to allocation, and muscle mass predicts neither map. This is the same anatomy that gives the lips a two point threshold of a few millimeters."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the cell body of the first order neuron of a somatosensory pathway?\nA) In the dorsal horn of the spinal cord\nB) In the dorsal root ganglion\nC) In the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus\nD) In the postcentral gyrus of the cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In the dorsal root ganglion","why":"The first order neuron runs from the receptor to the CNS with its cell body parked in the dorsal root ganglion, off to the side of the conducting axon. The dorsal horn is where many first order neurons synapse onto second order neurons, not where their cell bodies sit. The thalamus holds third order cell bodies, and the postcentral gyrus is the final destination rather than a cell body location for the pathway neurons."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sensations travel in the dorsal column medial lemniscus pathway?\nA) Fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception\nB) Pain, temperature, and crude touch\nC) Pain and vibration only\nD) Temperature and conscious proprioception but nothing else\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception","why":"The dorsal columns carry the large fiber, high acuity information: two point discrimination, vibration, and joint position. Pain, temperature, and crude touch travel instead in the spinothalamic pathway on small diameter fibers. Keeping the two lists separate is what makes it possible to predict which sensations a given cord lesion will take."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Fine touch information from the left hand crosses the midline in the medulla, so it reaches the right somatosensory cortex.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Each somatosensory pathway crosses once, and the level of crossing is what differs. Fine touch and vibration cross in the medulla; pain and temperature cross in the spinal cord. Either way the information from one side ends up in the opposite cortex, which is why a stroke on one side produces sensory loss on the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one diagram running from a fingertip up to the cortex with two tracts on it, one carrying fine touch and one carrying pain. Mark where each crosses the midline, where each synapses on the way, and where each arrives in the postcentral gyrus. Use two colors or two line styles. One line: name a level where a single lesion would knock out one of them and spare the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a strip of postcentral gyrus in cross section and lay the homunculus along it, sizing each body part by the cortical area it gets rather than by its real size. Beside it, draw the same body parts at true relative size. Use your own two point discrimination data to decide what to enlarge. One line: given both drawings, state what cortical area is actually proportional to."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace touch and pain information from receptor to cortex and explain why the somatosensory homunculus is disproportionate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Somatosensory pathways. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-pain-modulation","name":"Pain and its modulation","can":"Distinguish fast and slow pain by fiber type, explain referred pain by convergence, and describe how gate control and endogenous opioids reduce pain transmission.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why is fast pain localized more precisely than slow pain?\nA) A delta fibers have smaller receptive fields and less convergence\nB) A delta fibers travel in the dorsal columns, which preserve fine detail\nC) A delta fibers are more numerous than C fibers in every tissue\nD) A delta fibers fire at higher frequencies, which improves localization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A delta fibers have smaller receptive fields and less convergence","why":"Localization is a spatial code, so it depends on how narrow each line is and how much information is thrown away at the first synapse, and the A delta system keeps both tighter than the C fiber system. Both pain fiber types ascend in the spinothalamic tract, not the dorsal columns. C fibers actually outnumber A delta fibers in most tissues, and firing rate carries intensity rather than position."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which fiber type carries fast pain?\nA) Small myelinated A delta fibers  |  B) Unmyelinated C fibers\nC) Large myelinated A beta fibers  |  D) Preganglionic autonomic B fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Small myelinated A delta fibers","why":"A delta fibers are thin but myelinated, so they conduct at roughly 5 to 30 m/s and deliver the first, sharp signal from an injury. C fibers are unmyelinated and much slower, carrying slow pain. A beta fibers are the large touch and vibration fibers, and B fibers are autonomic, so neither carries pain at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which fiber type carries slow pain?\nA) Large myelinated A alpha fibers  |  B) Small myelinated A delta fibers\nC) Unmyelinated C fibers  |  D) Large myelinated A beta fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Unmyelinated C fibers","why":"C fibers have no myelin, conduct at about 0.5 to 2 m/s, and produce the dull, aching pain that follows an injury and lingers. A delta fibers carry the sharp first pain. A alpha fibers serve proprioception from muscle and A beta fibers serve touch, and both are far too fast and too large to be the slow pain line."},{"t":"mc","q":"How would a patient describe fast pain?\nA) Dull and hard to localize  |  B) Sharp and easy to localize\nC) Burning and slow to build  |  D) Cramping and spread over a wide area\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sharp and easy to localize","why":"Fast pain arrives on A delta fibers with relatively little convergence in the cord, so it is felt as a sharp prick at a definite spot, which is the sensation that makes a person pull the hand back. Dull, burning, and diffuse descriptions all belong to slow pain on C fibers. The two qualities often follow one another from the same injury, which is why a single stimulus can be felt twice."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 58 year old man develops crushing chest pain during exertion that spreads down the inner side of his left arm. There is no injury to the arm, arm pulses are normal, and pressing on the arm does not change the pain. His ECG shows changes consistent with reduced blood flow to heart muscle.\nWhy is the pain felt in the arm?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Cardiac nociceptor afferents enter the cord at T1 to T5 and converge on the same dorsal horn neurons as afferents from the arm skin, and the brain attributes the input to the skin","why":"This is referred pain by convergence. Visceral nociceptors are few and their afferents share second order neurons with the far more numerous somatic afferents from the same spinal segments. The brain has learned that activity in those neurons usually means skin injury, so it projects the pain to the arm and chest wall. The normal arm pulses and the fact that pressing on the arm changes nothing tell you the arm itself is not the source."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain two things. First, why pain from the heart is often felt in the left arm and chest wall. Second, why rubbing a bumped shin makes it hurt less. Name the fibers and the neurons involved in each.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"visceral nociceptor afferents and somatic afferents converge on the same second order neurons in the dorsal horn; the brain attributes the input to the skin, the more common source, so cardiac pain is referred to the arm and chest dermatomes; rubbing activates large myelinated A beta touch fibers; A beta input excites inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn; the interneurons reduce transmission from C and A delta fibers to the projection neuron, closing the gate; the pain signal reaching the brain is weaker while the touch input continues","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one painful stimulus and the two fibers carrying it away, an A delta and a C fiber, drawn to show the difference in diameter and myelination. On a shared time axis, mark when each signal arrives and write the sensation each produces. Then add the dorsal horn gate, a large touch fiber, the inhibitory interneuron, and the projection neuron. One line: why rubbing a bruise helps, judging from your own circuit."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a spinal cord segment with a visceral afferent from the heart and a somatic afferent from the left arm skin converging on the same projection neuron. Follow that one neuron up to the cortex and draw where the brain places the pain. Then draw an endogenous opioid acting somewhere on this circuit and mark whether it works before or after the synapse. One line: why the brain guesses skin and not heart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish fast and slow pain by fiber type, explain referred pain by convergence, and describe how gate control and endogenous opioids reduce pain transmission."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pain and its modulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-tactile-mapping","name":"Tactile mapping and adaptation testing","can":"Measure two point discrimination at several body sites and show receptor adaptation and referred sensation experimentally.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which instrument is used to measure two point discrimination in the lab?\nA) A tuning fork of known frequency\nB) A two point caliper with an attached millimeter scale\nC) A monofilament of calibrated bending force applied to the skin\nD) A thermode set to a fixed temperature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A two point caliper with an attached millimeter scale","why":"Two point discrimination is a distance measurement, so the tool must present two contacts a known number of millimeters apart, which is what the caliper or aesthesiometer does. A tuning fork tests vibration sense and a monofilament tests the force threshold for light touch, both useful but neither spatial. A thermode tests thermal thresholds and has nothing to do with spatial resolution."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why are trials with only one caliper point randomly mixed into the series?\nA) To let the skin recover between two point trials\nB) To measure the subject's reaction time as a second dependent variable\nC) To check that the subject is discriminating rather than guessing\nD) To calibrate the caliper against a known separation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) To check that the subject is discriminating rather than guessing","why":"A subject who says two on every trial will appear to have superb acuity unless the protocol can catch it, and single point catch trials do exactly that. Recovery time is handled by pacing the trials, not by changing the stimulus. Reaction time is not part of this measurement, and the caliper is calibrated against its own scale rather than against the subject."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the coin demonstration of adaptation, what does the subject report?\nA) The sensation of the coin grows stronger as the skin warms\nB) The sensation of the coin fades within a minute although it has not moved\nC) The coin feels heavier the longer it rests on the skin\nD) The coin is felt in a location a few centimeters away from where it rests\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sensation of the coin fades within a minute although it has not moved","why":"Rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors stop firing during a steady, unchanging pressure, so the CNS receives no continuing touch signal and the coin drops out of awareness. A growing or heavier sensation would mean sensitization rather than adaptation. Mislocalization to a distant site describes referred sensation, which is a separate demonstration in this lab."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the lab demonstration of referred sensation, tapping the ulnar nerve where it crosses the elbow produces a sensation felt where?\nA) In the little finger and the ring finger\nB) At the elbow itself, where the tap was applied\nC) In the thumb and the index finger\nD) Diffusely over the whole forearm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the little finger and the ring finger","why":"The ulnar nerve carries afferents from the medial hand, and stimulating those fibers anywhere along their length sends traffic on lines labeled for the little and ring fingers, so that is where the tingle is felt. Feeling it at the elbow would require the brain to track where along the axon the stimulus occurred, which it cannot do. The thumb and index finger are served by the median nerve, and the sensation is felt in a defined territory rather than diffusely."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does the subject keep their eyes closed throughout the two point testing?\nA) Vision raises the firing rate of touch afferents\nB) Closed eyes reduce the subject's overall skin temperature\nC) Vision activates the same cortical region as touch\nD) Seeing the caliper would let the subject infer the answer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Seeing the caliper would let the subject infer the answer","why":"The measurement is a report of what the skin resolves, so any visual cue about how far apart the points are turns the trial into an inference rather than a discrimination. Vision does not change afferent firing or skin temperature. Visual and somatosensory cortex are distinct regions, so cortical overlap is not the concern here."},{"t":"bd","q":"Describe how you measured two point discrimination on the fingertip and on the back in lab, state the result you expect at each site, and explain the difference in terms of receptive fields and lateral inhibition.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"touch the skin with two points, changing the separation, while the subject cannot see; the threshold is the smallest separation still reported as two points; include single point trials so the subject cannot simply guess two; fingertip threshold is a few millimeters, back threshold is several centimeters; fingertip receptors have small receptive fields and dense innervation, back receptors have large sparse fields; two points inside one receptive field are felt as one point; lateral inhibition sharpens the borders and helps separate two nearby points","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline and write your own measured two point threshold in millimeters at every site you tested. Before looking at anyone else's data, circle the site you predicted would be finest. Then draw a bar for each site ordered from finest to coarsest with the real numbers on them. One line: name a site where your measurement and your prediction disagreed, and one method error that could explain it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a time axis and plot your own adaptation data on it, how long the sensation took to fade for each stimulus you tested. Beside it, draw a body outline with your referred sensation result on it, marking where the stimulus was applied and where it was actually felt. One line: what the distance between those two marks tells you about where in the pathway the mistake is being made."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Tactile mapping and adaptation testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Special Senses","comps":[{"id":"w7-vision-optics","name":"Optics and accommodation","can":"Explain refraction by the cornea and lens and describe how accommodation and the pupillary reflex focus near and far images.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A light is shone into the right eye only. What happens to the left pupil?\nA) It constricts, because of the consensual reflex\nB) It dilates, because that eye is still in relative darkness\nC) It does not change, because each optic nerve drives only its own eye\nD) It constricts only if the light is moved to the left eye as well\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It constricts, because of the consensual reflex","why":"Signals from one optic nerve reach the pretectal area and then both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, so a light in one eye constricts both pupils. That crossed connection is the whole point of testing the direct and consensual responses separately. The idea that each eye works alone would predict no consensual response at all, and dilation of the unlit eye would require sympathetic drive that the reflex does not produce."},{"t":"mc","q":"In dim light the pupil dilates. What happens to the depth of focus?\nA) It decreases, because a wide pupil admits more peripheral rays\nB) It increases, because a wide pupil narrows the cone of light entering the eye\nC) It is unchanged, because depth of focus depends only on lens power\nD) It increases, because more light reaching the retina sharpens the image\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It decreases, because a wide pupil admits more peripheral rays","why":"A wide pupil lets in rays that strike the lens far from its center, and those rays converge over a wider spread of distances, so only a narrow band of object distances stays sharp. A small pupil does the opposite and acts like the small aperture on a camera. Total light and lens power both change the image, but neither one is what sets depth of focus, which is governed by aperture size."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the eye does most of the bending of incoming light?\nA) The vitreous humor  |  B) The lens\nC) The cornea  |  D) The aqueous humor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The cornea","why":"Light passes from air into the cornea, and that is the largest jump in refractive index anywhere in the eye, so the cornea supplies roughly two thirds of the total refractive power. The lens supplies the rest, and it matters because it is the part that can change shape, but its power is smaller. The vitreous humor and the aqueous humor have refractive indexes close to the tissues around them, so light passes through them with very little bending."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle contracts so that the lens can round up for near vision?\nA) The ciliary muscle  |  B) The sphincter pupillae\nC) The dilator pupillae  |  D) The levator palpebrae superioris\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The ciliary muscle","why":"The ciliary muscle is a ring of smooth muscle, and when it contracts the ring narrows, which slackens the zonular fibers and lets the elastic lens spring into a rounder shape. The sphincter pupillae narrows the pupil during the near response but does not change lens shape. The dilator pupillae widens the pupil under sympathetic drive. The levator palpebrae raises the eyelid and has nothing to do with focusing."},{"t":"mc","q":"During accommodation for a near object, how does the lens change?\nA) It flattens and loses refractive power\nB) It becomes more rounded and gains refractive power\nC) It slides backward toward the retina\nD) It stays the same shape while the cornea steepens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It becomes more rounded and gains refractive power","why":"A rounder lens has more sharply curved surfaces, so it bends light more strongly, which is exactly what the diverging rays from a near object need. Flattening is what happens for distance vision, when the ciliary muscle relaxes. The human lens does not move forward or backward to focus, unlike a camera lens. The cornea has a fixed curvature and does not participate in accommodation."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The lens provides most of the refractive power of the eye, because it is the part whose shape can change.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The cornea provides about two thirds of the refractive power; the lens supplies the rest and is only the adjustable part.","why":"Refraction depends on the change in refractive index at a surface, and the biggest change in the eye is from air to cornea. The lens sits in fluid on both sides, so it bends light less, but it can round up or flatten and that adjustment is what lets us focus at different distances. Being adjustable is not the same as being powerful."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an eye in cross section twice at the same scale with light rays coming in. In the first, the object is far away. In the second, it is close. In each, draw the lens shape, the ciliary muscle, the zonule fibers taut or slack, and where the rays come to a focus. One line: name which of the two states has the ciliary muscle working, and why that catches most people out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the pupillary light reflex as a circuit, light into one eye, the afferent nerve, the brainstem, and the efferent nerve running back out to both irises. Show the muscle that contracts and draw both pupils before and after. Then move the light to the other eye and draw the result. One line: what a response missing in one eye only tells you about where the lesion sits."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain refraction by the cornea and lens and describe how accommodation and the pupillary reflex focus near and far images."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Optics and accommodation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-phototransduction","name":"Phototransduction","can":"Trace phototransduction from photon absorption by retinal through the change in cyclic GMP to the decrease in glutamate release and explain why photoreceptors hyperpolarize to light.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In complete darkness, what is happening at the cyclic GMP gated channels of a rod?\nA) Cyclic GMP is high, the channels are open, and Na+ and Ca2+ flow in\nB) Cyclic GMP is low, the channels are closed, and no current flows\nC) Cyclic GMP is high, the channels are open, and K+ flows out\nD) Cyclic GMP is low, the channels are open, and Cl- flows in\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cyclic GMP is high, the channels are open, and Na+ and Ca2+ flow in","why":"With no light there is nothing to activate phosphodiesterase, so cyclic GMP stays high, holds the channels open, and lets a steady inward flow of Na+ and Ca2+ called the dark current keep the cell near minus 40 mV. That is a depolarized resting state compared with most neurons. Low cyclic GMP with closed channels describes the illuminated cell instead. The channels are cation channels, so they do not carry outward K+ or inward Cl- as their main current."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which molecule inside a rod actually absorbs the photon?\nA) Retinal  |  B) Opsin\nC) Transducin  |  D) Guanylyl cyclase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Retinal","why":"Retinal is the light absorbing chromophore, a derivative of vitamin A, and its shape change is the only step in vision that light itself performs. Opsin is the protein that holds retinal and shapes which wavelengths it absorbs best, but opsin alone does not capture the photon. Transducin is the G protein activated afterward, and guanylyl cyclase is the enzyme that rebuilds cyclic GMP during recovery."},{"t":"mc","q":"What two parts make up rhodopsin?\nA) The protein opsin plus all-trans retinal\nB) The protein transducin plus cyclic GMP\nC) The protein opsin plus 11-cis retinal\nD) The enzyme phosphodiesterase plus 11-cis retinal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The protein opsin plus 11-cis retinal","why":"Rhodopsin is a G protein coupled receptor, opsin, with 11-cis retinal bound in its core as a built in ligand waiting to be changed by light. All-trans retinal is the product formed after a photon is absorbed, and once it forms, the pigment is bleached rather than resting. Transducin and phosphodiesterase are downstream signaling proteins, not components of the pigment itself."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An experimental drug inhibits the phosphodiesterase in rods, so cyclic GMP cannot be broken down even when rhodopsin is activated. Retinal recordings are taken from a treated animal, first in a dark room and then during a flash of light.\nPredict the rod's membrane potential and glutamate release in the dark and in the light.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"In the dark the rod is depolarized and releasing glutamate as normal; in the light it stays depolarized and keeps releasing glutamate, so no light signal is sent","why":"The only way light changes a rod's membrane potential is by lowering cyclic GMP. In the dark the drug changes nothing that matters: cyclic GMP is already high, channels are open, the cell is depolarized near minus 40 mV, and glutamate is released steadily. In the light rhodopsin and transducin still activate, but the phosphodiesterase they turn on is blocked, so cyclic GMP stays high, the channels stay open, and the rod behaves as if it were still dark."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In the dark, photoreceptors are hyperpolarized and release very little glutamate.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. In the dark, cyclic GMP is high, the cation channels are open, the photoreceptor sits depolarized near minus 40 mV, and it releases glutamate steadily; light is what hyperpolarizes it.","why":"Photoreceptors are the exception among sensory cells: the stimulus turns them off. In the dark the cyclic GMP gated channels carry the dark current, keeping the cell depolarized and its glutamate release high. Light activates a phosphodiesterase that destroys cyclic GMP, the channels close, the cell hyperpolarizes, and glutamate release drops. That drop is the signal to the bipolar cells."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain step by step why a rod hyperpolarizes when light hits it. Start with the state of the rod in the dark and end with what the bipolar cell sees.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"in the dark cyclic GMP is high, the cyclic GMP gated cation channels are open, and the rod sits depolarized near minus 40 mV; the depolarized rod releases glutamate continuously; light converts 11 cis retinal to all trans retinal, which activates rhodopsin; rhodopsin activates transducin, which activates phosphodiesterase; phosphodiesterase breaks down cyclic GMP, so the channels close; less inward cation current, so the rod hyperpolarizes; glutamate release falls, and that decrease is the signal to the bipolar cell","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a photoreceptor twice, side by side, once in darkness and once in bright light. In each, show the cyclic GMP level, whether the cation channels are open or closed, the membrane potential, and how much glutamate is leaving the base of the cell. One line: name the state in which the cell releases the most transmitter, and say why that is the opposite of what you would expect."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the cascade as a chain of labeled boxes from photon absorption through retinal, the pigment, the G protein, the enzyme, cyclic GMP, and the channel, with arrows between every box. On each arrow, mark whether the next thing goes up or down. Then break one box of your choosing and write what the cell now does when light hits it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace phototransduction from photon absorption by retinal through the change in cyclic GMP to the decrease in glutamate release and explain why photoreceptors hyperpolarize to light."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Phototransduction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-visual-processing","name":"Rods cones and visual processing","can":"Compare rods and cones by sensitivity and acuity and trace the visual pathway to predict the field deficit produced by a lesion at the optic nerve or the optic chiasm.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has lost the left half of the visual field in both eyes, with normal pupillary light reflexes. Where is the lesion?\nA) In the left optic nerve  |  B) In the optic chiasm\nC) In the right optic tract  |  D) In the left optic tract\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In the right optic tract","why":"A deficit in the same half of the field in both eyes must be behind the chiasm, because only there are fibers grouped by field rather than by eye, and losing the left field means losing the pathway on the right. The left optic tract would remove the right half of both fields instead. A chiasm lesion would produce bitemporal loss, and a left optic nerve lesion would blind that eye alone rather than half of each field."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is rod vision less sharp than cone vision?\nA) Rods are spread evenly across the retina instead of clustering near the center\nB) Rods respond more slowly to a change in light intensity\nC) Many rods converge onto one bipolar cell, so fine detail is pooled away\nD) Rods lack the pigment needed to absorb photons efficiently\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Many rods converge onto one bipolar cell, so fine detail is pooled away","why":"Convergence sums the output of a wide patch of retina onto a single output neuron, which raises sensitivity but destroys information about which rod in the patch was hit. Foveal cones approach a one to one line to the brain, which preserves position. Rods are actually more sensitive and carry more pigment than cones, so the explanation cannot be a shortage of pigment, and their slower response affects motion blur rather than spatial resolution."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion completely destroys the left optic nerve. What visual deficit results?\nA) Loss of the left half of the field in both eyes\nB) Loss of the temporal field of both eyes\nC) Total blindness of the left eye\nD) Loss of the right half of the field in both eyes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Total blindness of the left eye","why":"In front of the chiasm every fiber in the nerve comes from one eye, so cutting it removes all input from that eye and nothing from the other, and the patient is blind in the left eye alone. Losing one half of the field in both eyes requires a lesion behind the chiasm, where fibers from the two eyes have already been sorted by field rather than by eye. Loss of both temporal fields is the signature of a lesion at the chiasm itself."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which photoreceptor type operates in dim light?\nA) Cones  |  B) Bipolar cells\nC) Rods  |  D) Ganglion cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Rods","why":"Rods carry far more pigment per cell and pool their signals, so they respond to very few photons and are the only photoreceptors working under starlight. Cones need much brighter light and go silent in dim conditions, which is why color drains out of a dark room. Bipolar and ganglion cells are second and third order neurons in the retina and do not absorb light at all."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient keeps walking into door frames on her right side. Visual field testing shows loss of the right half of the visual field in both eyes. Acuity in the remaining field is normal in each eye, and shining a light into either eye constricts both pupils.\nWhere must the damaged fibers be, and what did they carry?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"In the left optic tract, behind the chiasm, carrying fibers from the left temporal retina and the right nasal retina, both of which viewed the right visual field","why":"The same half of the field is missing in both eyes, which can only happen behind the chiasm, where fibers are sorted by visual field rather than by eye. The right visual field lands on the left half of each retina: the temporal retina of the left eye and the nasal retina of the right eye. Those fibers meet in the left optic tract. Each eye still sees, so neither optic nerve is cut, and the preserved pupil reflexes fit a lesion that spares part of the pathway."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Cutting the right optic nerve blinds the right eye completely but leaves the full visual field of the left eye intact.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The place a lesion sits along the visual pathway decides the pattern of loss. Before the chiasm the fibers are grouped by eye, so an optic nerve lesion blinds one eye. At the chiasm the crossing nasal fibers from both eyes are damaged and both temporal fields go. Behind the chiasm the fibers are grouped by visual field, so an optic tract lesion takes the same half of the field from both eyes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a patch of retina at the fovea and a patch from the far periphery, side by side, each showing photoreceptors, bipolar cells, and one ganglion cell. Make the convergence ratio visible by how many photoreceptors feed that one ganglion cell. Beside each, write which receptor type dominates, its sensitivity, and its acuity. One line: why the same wiring cannot give you both sensitivity and acuity at once."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the visual pathway from both eyes through the chiasm to the cortex, marking which fibers cross and which do not. Then cut it in two places, once at the left optic nerve and once through the middle of the chiasm. For each cut, draw the two visual fields as a pair of circles and shade what is lost. One line: which lesion the patient notices first, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare rods and cones by sensitivity and acuity and trace the visual pathway to predict the field deficit produced by a lesion at the optic nerve or the optic chiasm."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Rods cones and visual processing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-vision-clinical","name":"Refractive errors and clinical vision","can":"Explain myopia, hyperopia, presbyopia, and astigmatism in terms of focal point and eyeball or lens shape and state the corrective lens for each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A refractive surgeon removes too much corneal tissue while treating a myopic patient. What is the expected new refractive state and its correction?\nA) Myopia that is worse than before, corrected with a stronger concave lens\nB) Emmetropia, needing no correction\nC) Astigmatism, corrected with a cylindrical lens\nD) Hyperopia, corrected with a convex lens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Hyperopia, corrected with a convex lens","why":"Flattening the cornea subtracts refractive power, so removing more tissue than needed pushes the focal point past the retina and turns an overpowered eye into an underpowered one. The fix is then a converging lens, the same correction a naturally short eye needs. More myopia would require added power rather than less. Astigmatism would follow only if the tissue removal were uneven between meridians, and hitting the retina exactly is the intended outcome that did not happen here."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's eyeball is longer than average from cornea to retina, and the cornea and lens are normal. Which condition is this and which vision is blurred?\nA) Hyperopia, with distance vision blurred\nB) Myopia, with distance vision blurred\nC) Myopia, with near vision blurred\nD) Hyperopia, with near vision blurred\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Myopia, with distance vision blurred","why":"A long eye puts the retina behind the focal point of parallel rays, so distant objects blur while near objects, whose rays arrive diverging and focus farther back, can still land correctly. That is why nearsighted people can often read without glasses. Hyperopia comes from an eye that is too short, and blurred near vision with clear distance vision is the hyperopic or presbyopic pattern."},{"t":"mc","q":"A 50-year-old with previously excellent vision now holds the newspaper at arm's length to read it. What has happened and what correction helps?\nA) Myopia, corrected with a concave lens\nB) Astigmatism, corrected with a cylindrical lens\nC) Presbyopia, corrected with a convex reading lens\nD) Hyperopia from a shortened eyeball, corrected with a prism\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Presbyopia, corrected with a convex reading lens","why":"Holding print farther away is the classic sign that the near point has receded, which happens as the aging lens stiffens and can no longer add power for close work. A convex lens supplies the extra convergence the lens can no longer produce. Myopia would blur distance rather than near vision, astigmatism blurs one orientation at every distance, and the eyeball does not shorten in adulthood."},{"t":"mc","q":"In myopia, where do parallel rays from a distant object come to a focus?\nA) In front of the retina  |  B) Exactly on the retina\nC) Behind the retina  |  D) On the optic disc\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In front of the retina","why":"A myopic eye has too much refractive power for its length, usually because the eyeball is too long or the cornea too steep, so distant rays converge early and are already spreading again by the time they reach the retina. Focus behind the retina is the hyperopic pattern. Focus exactly on the retina defines the normal eye, and the optic disc is a location on the retina rather than a focal plane."},{"t":"mc","q":"In hyperopia, where do parallel rays from a distant object come to a focus?\nA) In front of the retina  |  B) Behind the retina\nC) On the lens surface  |  D) Exactly on the retina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Behind the retina","why":"A hyperopic eye is optically too weak for its length, usually because the eyeball is short, so the rays are still converging when they pass the photoreceptor layer and the theoretical focal point lies behind it. Focus in front of the retina is myopia, the opposite error. Light never focuses on the lens surface, since the lens is part of the focusing system rather than a screen."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 16 year old can read the board only from the front row, but reads a phone screen at 25 cm with no trouble. An eye exam shows a normal cornea and a normal, elastic lens. The eyeball measures about 1.5 mm longer than average from front to back.\nWhere does light from a distant object focus in her eye, and which lens corrects it?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"In front of the retina, because the eye is too long for its refractive power; a concave (diverging) lens moves the focal point back onto the retina","why":"Her optics are normal, so parallel rays from a distant object are brought to a focus at the normal distance behind the lens. The retina is farther back than that, so the image forms in front of it and has spread out again by the time it lands. Near objects focus farther back and can land on the retina, which is why near vision is fine. A diverging lens weakens the eye's total power and pushes the focal point back to the retina."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four eyes in a row with parallel rays entering each, normal, myopic, hyperopic, and astigmatic. In each, mark where the rays come to a focus relative to the retina and mark whether the eyeball shape or the corneal surface is the problem. Then draw the corrective lens in front of each, bending the rays back onto the retina. One line: why the astigmatic eye needs a differently shaped lens than the other two."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one eye reading a page at 25 cm at age 20 and the same eye at age 60, showing the lens shape and where the image falls in each. Beside them, draw a hyperopic 20 year old reading the same page. Both people end up holding it further away. One line: name the one structure that differs between the two cases, and a test that would tell them apart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain myopia, hyperopia, presbyopia, and astigmatism in terms of focal point and eyeball or lens shape and state the corrective lens for each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Refractive errors and clinical vision. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-hearing-transduction","name":"Auditory transduction","can":"Trace sound from the tympanic membrane through the ossicles and cochlear fluid to hair cell bending and auditory nerve firing and explain how pitch and loudness are coded.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"When the stapes pushes inward at the oval window, what must the round window do?\nA) Open a channel that lets perilymph drain into the middle ear\nB) Push inward at the same time, to double the pressure wave\nC) Stay rigid, so that pressure builds inside the cochlea\nD) Bulge outward, because cochlear fluid cannot be compressed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bulge outward, because cochlear fluid cannot be compressed","why":"Perilymph is essentially incompressible in a bony tube, so unless something yields, the stapes cannot move and no traveling wave forms, and the flexible round window membrane is that release point. This is why scarring of the round window impairs hearing. Both windows moving inward together, or a rigid round window, would lock the fluid in place, and the window is a membrane rather than a drainage route."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pure low frequency tone is played. Where along the cochlea will the basilar membrane displacement be greatest?\nA) Near the base, where the membrane is narrow and stiff\nB) Near the apex, where the membrane is wide and floppy\nC) Uniformly along the entire length of the membrane\nD) At the round window, where the wave is reflected\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Near the apex, where the membrane is wide and floppy","why":"Low frequencies travel farthest before their traveling wave peaks, because the apex has the mechanical properties that resonate slowly. The stiff base is tuned to high frequencies and passes low ones along without peaking. A uniform response would erase the place code, and the round window is a pressure release membrane rather than a tuned resonator."},{"t":"mc","q":"Years of loud noise exposure destroy hair cells near the base of the cochlea. What hearing loss is expected?\nA) Loss of high frequency hearing, with low frequencies relatively spared\nB) Loss of low frequency hearing, with high frequencies relatively spared\nC) Equal loss across all frequencies\nD) Loss of the ability to judge loudness, with pitch intact\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Loss of high frequency hearing, with low frequencies relatively spared","why":"The base of the cochlea is tuned to high frequencies, so damage there takes those frequencies out first while the apex, which handles low tones, keeps working. This is why noise damage and age related loss both show up first as trouble with consonants and high voices. Low frequency loss would require apical damage, and losing loudness perception while keeping pitch would require a coding failure rather than a loss of a tuned region."},{"t":"mc","q":"In what order does vibration pass through the auditory ossicles?\nA) Stapes, then incus, then malleus  |  B) Malleus, then incus, then stapes\nC) Incus, then malleus, then stapes  |  D) Malleus, then stapes, then incus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Malleus, then incus, then stapes","why":"The malleus is attached to the tympanic membrane and receives the vibration first, passes it to the incus, and the incus drives the stapes against the oval window. Reversing the order would send energy from the inner ear outward, which is not how airborne sound is collected. Any order that puts the stapes before the incus breaks the physical chain, since the stapes is the only ossicle that contacts the cochlea."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has damage to the stria vascularis, the tissue that secretes endolymph. Her endolymph K+ is measured at 60 mmol/L (normal about 150 mmol/L). The ossicles, the basilar membrane, and the hair cell stereocilia are all structurally intact.\nPredict the effect on hearing and explain the mechanism.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Hearing is reduced, because bending the stereocilia normally lets K+ flow into the hair cell down a steep gradient; a smaller gradient gives a smaller receptor potential and less transmitter release","why":"Hair cells are unusual: their receptor current is carried by K+ flowing in from the K+ rich, positively charged endolymph bathing the stereocilia. The size of that current depends on the electrochemical gradient across the tip of the stereocilium. Cutting endolymph K+ to less than half shrinks the gradient, so the same amount of bending produces a smaller depolarization, less glutamate, and less nerve firing across all frequencies. The mechanics are intact; the electrochemistry is not."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain how the cochlea codes the pitch and the loudness of a sound. Include how the basilar membrane behaves and what happens at the hair cell.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the stapes pushes on the oval window and starts a pressure wave in the perilymph; the basilar membrane is narrow and stiff near the oval window and wide and flexible at the apex; high frequencies displace the base most and low frequencies the apex, so pitch is coded by which hair cells along the membrane are activated (place coding); membrane movement bends the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane and opens mechanically gated channels; K+ enters from the endolymph and depolarizes the hair cell, which releases transmitter onto the cochlear nerve; louder sounds move the membrane more, open more channels, and produce more transmitter release, a higher firing rate, and recruitment of more fibers","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the path of one sound from air to auditory nerve, tympanic membrane, the three ossicles named in order, oval window, cochlear fluid, basilar membrane, hair cell, nerve. Draw the round window and show what it is doing. Enlarge one hair cell and draw its stereocilia bending and the channel that opens. One line: name what the ossicles are correcting for at the boundary between air and fluid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the basilar membrane unrolled as one long strip, narrow and stiff at the end nearest the oval window and wide and floppy at the far end. Mark where a high pitched sound peaks and where a low one peaks. Then draw two hair cell firing traces for the same pitch at two loudness levels. One line: pitch and loudness are coded two different ways, say which one is place and which is rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace sound from the tympanic membrane through the ossicles and cochlear fluid to hair cell bending and auditory nerve firing and explain how pitch and loudness are coded."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Auditory transduction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-hearing-clinical","name":"Conductive and sensorineural hearing loss","can":"Distinguish conductive from sensorineural hearing loss by the site of the lesion and by the expected Weber and Rinne results.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On testing, the Weber tone lateralizes to the left ear while both ears show air conduction longer than bone conduction. What is the diagnosis?\nA) A conductive loss in the left ear\nB) A sensorineural loss in the left ear\nC) A conductive loss in the right ear\nD) A sensorineural loss in the right ear\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A sensorineural loss in the right ear","why":"Normal Rinne results on both sides rule out a conductive block, so any hearing loss present must be sensorineural, and lateralization to the left means the left cochlea is the better one, placing the damage on the right. Reading the Weber without the Rinne is what leads students to name the wrong side here. A left sided lesion of either type would send the tone away from the left, and a conductive loss would have reversed a Rinne."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a conductive loss in the right ear only. Predict the Weber result.\nA) The tone lateralizes to the left ear\nB) The tone is not heard on either side\nC) The tone is heard equally in both ears\nD) The tone lateralizes to the right ear\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tone lateralizes to the right ear","why":"With a healthy cochlea on both sides, the bone conducted tone is detected equally well, but the blocked right middle ear keeps the sound from leaking back out and shields that ear from background noise, so the tone seems louder on the blocked side. Lateralizing away from the affected ear would mean that cochlea was damaged, which is the sensorineural pattern. A one sided problem of either type rarely gives a midline result."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a sensorineural loss in the left ear only. Predict the Rinne result on that side.\nA) Air conduction still outlasts bone conduction, but both are reduced\nB) Bone conduction is heard longer than air conduction on the affected side\nC) Air and bone conduction are both normal in duration\nD) Only bone conduction is heard at all\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Air conduction still outlasts bone conduction, but both are reduced","why":"The middle ear is intact, so it still amplifies airborne sound better than the skull transmits it, and the normal air over bone relationship survives even though the damaged cochlea raises the threshold for both routes. That is why the Rinne alone can look deceptively normal in a sensorineural loss and must be read alongside the Weber. Bone conduction beating air conduction requires a conductive block, and truly normal durations would mean no loss at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which single finding best separates a conductive loss from a sensorineural loss in the same ear?\nA) Whether the loss is worse for high frequencies than for low\nB) Whether the loss came on suddenly or gradually\nC) Whether the patient also reports ringing in the ear\nD) Whether bone conduction exceeds air conduction in that ear\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Whether bone conduction exceeds air conduction in that ear","why":"Comparing the two delivery routes isolates the question of whether sound is getting in, which is exactly the difference between the two categories, and only a conductive block reverses the normal air over bone relationship. High frequency predominance, tinnitus, and speed of onset are all common in sensorineural loss but occur in conductive cases too, so none of them decides the question on its own."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the lesion in a conductive hearing loss?\nA) In the cochlear hair cells  |  B) In the outer or middle ear\nC) In the cochlear nerve  |  D) In the auditory cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In the outer or middle ear","why":"Conductive loss means sound energy is not reaching the cochlea, so the fault lies in the ear canal, the tympanic membrane, or the ossicular chain. The cochlea and nerve are working normally, which is why bone conduction still delivers sound well. Hair cell and nerve lesions define sensorineural loss, and a cortical lesion causes trouble interpreting sound rather than a simple threshold shift."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 70 year old woman says voices sound muffled, worse in her right ear. Weber: the tone lateralizes to the left. Rinne: air conduction lasts longer than bone conduction in both ears, but both durations are shorter on the right than on the left. Otoscopy shows clear ear canals and normal eardrums on both sides.\nWhich site of damage fits all of the results, and why?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The right cochlea or cochlear nerve, because the Rinne is in normal order on both sides (no conductive block) and the Weber goes to the better ear","why":"Read the Rinne first. Air still beats bone on both sides, so sound is getting through both outer and middle ears and neither side has a conductive block; the shorter durations on the right just mean the right side hears less of everything. Then read the Weber: the tone goes to the left, the better ear, which is the pattern of a sensorineural loss on the right. Clear canals and normal eardrums confirm the outer and middle ear are not the problem."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the ear in one cross section from the canal through to the auditory nerve, with outer, middle, and inner ear marked off. Put an X at a site that would cause conductive loss and a different X at a site that would cause sensorineural loss. Beside each X, draw the tuning fork on the mastoid and beside the ear, and mark which position is heard louder. One line: name what bone conduction bypasses."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a head from the front three times with a tuning fork on the midline of the skull in each, once with normal hearing, once with a conductive loss on the right, and once with a sensorineural loss on the right. Draw an arrow from the fork to the ear where the sound is heard louder in each. One line: those arrows point opposite ways, say what it costs you to run Weber alone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish conductive from sensorineural hearing loss by the site of the lesion and by the expected Weber and Rinne results."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Conductive and sensorineural hearing loss. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-equilibrium","name":"Equilibrium","can":"Explain how the semicircular canals detect angular acceleration and how the utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and head position.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has lost hair cell function in both maculae but has intact semicircular canals. Which task will be hardest?\nA) Detecting the start of a head turn to the left\nB) Hearing a conversation in a quiet room\nC) Sensing which way is up with the eyes closed\nD) Judging the pitch of a musical note\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Sensing which way is up with the eyes closed","why":"Static head orientation relative to gravity is reported by the otolith organs, so losing them removes the internal sense of upright, and without vision to substitute the patient becomes disoriented. The onset of a head turn is a job for the canals, which are intact here. Hearing and pitch discrimination belong to the cochlea, a separate part of the inner ear that this lesion does not touch."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vestibular structures detect rotational, or angular, acceleration of the head?\nA) The tympanic cavity  |  B) The utricle and saccule\nC) The cochlear duct  |  D) The semicircular canals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The semicircular canals","why":"The three semicircular canals are fluid filled loops, and rotating the head sets up relative motion of that fluid, which is only produced by a change in rotational speed. The utricle and saccule instead carry weighted membranes that respond to linear acceleration and to gravity. The cochlear duct handles hearing, and the tympanic cavity is the air filled middle ear."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the crista ampullaris located?\nA) In the wall of the utricle near the macula\nB) In the ampulla of each semicircular canal\nC) At the apex of the cochlear spiral\nD) In the roof of the saccule near the macula\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In the ampulla of each semicircular canal","why":"Each canal widens into an ampulla, and the crista ampullaris is the ridge of hair cells and supporting cells inside it, topped by the cupula. Putting it in the utricle or saccule confuses it with the macula, the sensory patch of those two chambers. The cochlear apex belongs to hearing and contains no vestibular receptors."},{"t":"mc","q":"What sits on top of the hair cells in the macula of the utricle and saccule?\nA) A cupula of the same density as endolymph\nB) A thin sheet of stiff collagen with no added weight\nC) A gel layer studded with calcium carbonate otoliths\nD) A layer of endolymph secreting cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A gel layer studded with calcium carbonate otoliths","why":"The otoliths make the overlying membrane denser than the fluid around it, so it lags behind during linear acceleration and slides downhill when the head tilts, dragging the stereocilia with it. Without that extra mass the structure could not sense gravity at all, which is exactly why the cupula, matched in density to endolymph, cannot. Secretory cells maintain endolymph composition but do not form the sensory covering."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vestibular organ is best positioned to detect horizontal linear acceleration, such as a car pulling away from a stoplight?\nA) The utricle, whose macula lies roughly horizontally\nB) The saccule, whose macula lies roughly vertically\nC) The lateral semicircular canal\nD) The cochlear duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The utricle, whose macula lies roughly horizontally","why":"A horizontal macula puts the otolithic membrane free to slide forward and back across the hair cells, so forward acceleration shears it in the plane that the utricle monitors. The saccular macula stands vertically and is better suited to up and down acceleration, such as an elevator start. Semicircular canals respond to rotation, not straight line motion, and the cochlear duct serves hearing."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A person spun at a constant speed in the dark with the eyes closed stops feeling the rotation after a short time.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The canals sense angular acceleration by inertia: the fluid lags when rotation starts and deflects the cupula. At constant speed there is no lag, so nothing bends the hair cells. Without vision to report the moving world, the person feels stationary, and when the chair stops the fluid keeps going and produces a false sense of spinning the other way."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one semicircular canal with its cupula and one macula with its otolith membrane and otoconia, side by side and both enlarged. On each, draw the head movement it detects as an arrow and show what physically pushes on the hair cells in that case. One line: name a head movement that bends the cupula but leaves the otoliths sitting where they are."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the endolymph and cupula inside one horizontal canal at four moments, sitting still, at the start of a spin, during a long steady spin at constant speed, and the instant you stop. Show the cupula position in every panel. One line: using your fourth panel, say why you still feel like you are turning after you have stopped."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the semicircular canals detect angular acceleration and how the utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and head position."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Equilibrium. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-chemical-senses","name":"Taste and smell","can":"Compare gustatory and olfactory transduction and explain why olfactory input reaches the limbic system without a thalamic relay.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What are the five basic taste qualities?\nA) Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and spicy\nB) Sweet, salty, bitter, umami, and fatty\nC) Sweet, sour, salty, metallic, and umami\nD) Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami","why":"Each of the five has its own receptor mechanism on the taste cell, and umami, the savory taste of glutamate, was the last to be confirmed. Spiciness is not a taste at all but activation of pain and temperature endings by capsaicin through the trigeminal nerve. Metallic and fatty sensations are studied but are not among the established five."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which second messenger carries the olfactory signal inside an olfactory receptor neuron?\nA) Cyclic AMP  |  B) Cyclic GMP\nC) Inositol trisphosphate  |  D) Diacylglycerol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cyclic AMP","why":"An odorant binds a G protein coupled receptor, Golf activates adenylyl cyclase, and the cyclic AMP produced opens cation channels directly. Cyclic GMP is the messenger in photoreceptors, where the light response closes channels instead of opening them. Inositol trisphosphate and diacylglycerol are messengers in other G protein pathways, including some taste transduction, but not in the main olfactory cascade."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cranial nerve carries taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue?\nA) The facial nerve, CN VII  |  B) The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX\nC) The trigeminal nerve, CN V  |  D) The vagus nerve, CN X\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The facial nerve, CN VII","why":"Taste from the front of the tongue travels with CN VII, while CN IX carries taste from the posterior third and CN X picks up the few taste buds on the epiglottis and upper esophagus. CN V supplies general touch, temperature, and pain to the anterior tongue, which is why it carries the burn of chili but not the taste."},{"t":"mc","q":"Through which bony structure do olfactory receptor neuron axons pass on their way to the brain?\nA) The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone\nB) The optic canal of the sphenoid bone\nC) The foramen magnum of the occipital bone\nD) The internal acoustic meatus of the temporal bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone","why":"The cribriform plate is perforated by many small holes, and bundles of unmyelinated olfactory axons thread through them to reach the olfactory bulb sitting just above. That thin, perforated bone is also why a blow to the head can shear the axons and abolish smell. The optic canal transmits CN II, the internal acoustic meatus transmits CN VII and CN VIII, and the foramen magnum transmits the spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where do olfactory receptor neurons first synapse?\nA) In the glomeruli of the olfactory bulb\nB) In the solitary nucleus of the medulla\nC) In the ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus\nD) In the piriform cortex of the temporal lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the glomeruli of the olfactory bulb","why":"Receptor neurons expressing the same odorant receptor converge on the same glomerulus, where they synapse on mitral cells, and that sorting is the first step in coding which odorant is present. The solitary nucleus is the first relay for taste, not smell. The ventral posteromedial nucleus is the thalamic taste relay, and the piriform cortex receives olfactory information only after the bulb has processed it."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Taste information, like smell, reaches the cortex without passing through the thalamus.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Taste relays in the solitary nucleus and then in the thalamus before reaching the gustatory cortex; smell is the one sense that skips the thalamus.","why":"Olfactory receptor neurons project into the olfactory bulb, and the bulb sends axons straight to olfactory cortex and the amygdala, which is why smells have such direct emotional and memory effects. Taste travels a conventional route through cranial nerves VII, IX, and X, the solitary nucleus, and the thalamus. The two chemical senses are often lumped together, but only one of them bypasses the thalamus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a taste bud with one taste cell enlarged beside an olfactory epithelium with one olfactory receptor neuron enlarged, at the same scale. On each, show the stimulus arriving, what it binds to or passes through, and how the cell signals the next neuron along. Mark which of your two cells is itself a neuron. One line: name a taste that works by an ion entering the cell directly rather than by a receptor protein."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw both pathways from receptor to cortex on one page, taste on one side and smell on the other. Put the thalamus in the middle and route each pathway correctly, through it or around it. Mark where the smell pathway reaches the limbic system. One line: why a smell can drop you into a memory before you have even named the smell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare gustatory and olfactory transduction and explain why olfactory input reaches the limbic system without a thalamic relay."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Taste and smell. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-vision-tests","name":"Vision testing","can":"Perform visual acuity, blind spot, accommodation, and color vision testing and interpret each result.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A subject reads the 20/15 line correctly. What does this indicate?\nA) Acuity is worse than average, because the smaller number means less detail\nB) Acuity is better than average, since a normal eye must come to 15 feet\nC) The subject stood 15 feet from the chart instead of 20\nD) The result is invalid, because 20/20 is the best possible score\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Acuity is better than average, since a normal eye must come to 15 feet","why":"The subject is resolving from 20 feet what an average eye needs to approach to 15 feet to see, so the eye is doing better than the reference standard, and results down to about 20/10 are seen in healthy young subjects. The bottom number describes the reference eye, not the subject's position. Because 20/20 is an average rather than a ceiling, better scores are perfectly valid."},{"t":"mc","q":"At what distance is a standard Snellen chart read during acuity testing?\nA) 10 feet, or about 3 meters\nB) 14 inches, or about 35 centimeters\nC) 20 feet, or about 6 meters\nD) At whatever distance the letters first become clear\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 20 feet, or about 6 meters","why":"Twenty feet is treated as optical infinity, so the rays arriving at the eye are essentially parallel and no accommodation is needed, which is what makes the measurement a clean test of distance vision. Fourteen inches is the standard distance for a handheld near card, which tests a different thing. Ten feet is used only with a mirror system that doubles the path, and letting the subject choose the distance would make results impossible to compare."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does a Snellen result of 20/20 mean?\nA) The subject read 20 letters correctly out of the 20 that were tried\nB) The subject sees 20 percent better than average\nC) At 20 feet the subject reads what a normal eye reads at 20 feet\nD) The subject's near point is 20 centimeters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At 20 feet the subject reads what a normal eye reads at 20 feet","why":"The top number is the subject's testing distance and the bottom number is the distance at which a normal eye could read that same line, so matching numbers means normal acuity. Counting letters correct is not how the fraction is built. The fraction is not a percentage of normal, and the near point is measured by a separate accommodation test."},{"t":"mc","q":"What do Ishihara plates test?\nA) Visual acuity at near\nB) The size and position of the blind spot in each eye\nC) Color vision, especially red and green discrimination\nD) The near point of accommodation for each eye separately\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Color vision, especially red and green discrimination","why":"Each plate hides a number in dots that differ from the background only in hue, so a subject who cannot distinguish those hues reads no figure or a different one. The plates are designed mainly around the red and green confusions produced by the common cone pigment deficiencies. Acuity, blind spot mapping, and near point each have their own test in the lab."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate near point of accommodation for a healthy young adult?\nA) About 1 centimeter  |  B) About 1 meter\nC) About 50 centimeters  |  D) About 10 centimeters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 10 centimeters","why":"A young elastic lens can add enough power to keep an object sharp until it is roughly a hand's width from the eye, so about 10 cm is the expected result in the lab. One centimeter is far closer than any human lens can manage. Values near 50 cm or a meter are what you record in older subjects whose lenses have stiffened, so finding one in a 20 year old should prompt a repeat measurement."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four panels in a row, one for each test you ran, acuity, blind spot, accommodation, and color vision. In each panel, draw the setup and your own result, with real numbers where you have them, including your acuity fraction and your near point in centimeters. One line: name the test whose result depends most on the subject being honest, and what that means for trusting the number."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the eye in cross section and mark the optic disc and the fovea in their correct positions relative to each other. Draw rays from the target in your blind spot experiment to work out where the spot falls in the visual field and on which side. Then do the same for the other eye. One line: why you never notice the blind spot in ordinary life."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Vision testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-hearing-tests","name":"Hearing and equilibrium testing","can":"Perform Weber and Rinne tuning fork tests and a balance test and interpret the findings.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A subject's Weber tone lateralizes to the right ear and the right Rinne shows bone conduction outlasting air conduction. How is this recorded?\nA) A sensorineural loss on the right  |  B) A conductive loss on the right\nC) A conductive loss on the left  |  D) Normal hearing with a technique error\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A conductive loss on the right","why":"Bone beating air on the right says the sound is not getting through that middle ear, and the Weber pointing to the same ear says the cochlea underneath is healthy, so the two findings agree on a right sided conductive block. A right sensorineural loss would send the Weber to the left and leave the Rinne in normal order. Nothing in the findings implicates the left ear, and consistent results across two tests argue against a technique error."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the vibrating tuning fork placed to perform the Weber test?\nA) On the mastoid process behind one ear\nB) On the midline of the forehead or the top of the skull\nC) Just outside one ear canal without touching the head\nD) On the angle of the jaw on the affected side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) On the midline of the forehead or the top of the skull","why":"A midline placement delivers the same bone conducted stimulus to both inner ears, which is the only way a difference in perceived loudness can be attributed to the ears rather than to the placement. Putting the fork on one mastoid tests bone conduction on that side and is the first half of the Rinne. Holding it beside the ear tests air conduction, and the jaw is not a standard site."},{"t":"mc","q":"In tuning fork testing, what does lateralization mean?\nA) Air conduction outlasts bone conduction in the same ear\nB) The fork is moved from one side of the head to the other\nC) The tone changes pitch as the fork slows down\nD) The tone is heard louder in one ear than the other\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tone is heard louder in one ear than the other","why":"During the Weber test the sound reaches both cochleas equally, so if the subject reports it as louder on one side the result is said to lateralize to that side, and the direction is what identifies the type of loss. Moving the fork is a step in the procedure rather than a finding. A fading fork loses loudness but not pitch, and comparing air with bone conduction is the Rinne comparison."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tuning fork frequency is standard for the Weber and Rinne tests?\nA) 128 Hz  |  B) 256 Hz\nC) 512 Hz  |  D) 2048 Hz\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 512 Hz","why":"A 512 Hz fork sits in the speech range and gives a tone that lasts long enough to time while still being carried well by air, which is why it is the clinical standard. Lower frequency forks such as 128 Hz are felt as vibration as much as heard, so subjects confuse touch with sound. Very high frequency forks fade too quickly for a reliable comparison."},{"t":"mc","q":"How should the tuning fork be set vibrating before testing?\nA) Struck sharply against the hard edge of the lab bench or a metal table\nB) Struck gently against the heel of the hand or another soft surface\nC) Squeezed between the fingers and released\nD) Tapped against the subject's mastoid process\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Struck gently against the heel of the hand or another soft surface","why":"A soft strike produces a clean fundamental tone at a moderate intensity, which is what the comparison depends on. Striking a hard surface adds overtones and can start the tone so loud that a mild difference between the two routes is missed. Squeezing the tines produces almost no vibration, and striking the fork against the subject is uncomfortable and starts the test before the timing begins."},{"t":"bd","q":"Describe how to perform the Weber and Rinne tests. Then state and explain the results you expect in a person with a right sided conductive loss and in a person with a right sided sensorineural loss.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"Weber: vibrating fork on the midline of the skull, ask which ear hears it louder; Rinne: fork on the mastoid until the tone disappears, then hold it beside the ear canal, compare which lasts longer; normal Rinne is air conduction longer than bone conduction; right conductive loss: Weber lateralizes to the right and the right Rinne shows bone longer than air; right sensorineural loss: Weber lateralizes to the left and the Rinne stays air longer than bone on both sides; bone conduction bypasses the middle ear, and a conductive block also shields that cochlea from room noise, which is why the Weber goes toward it","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the Weber and Rinne procedures as you actually performed them, with the fork position marked on a head for each and your own subject's result written beside it. Then add your balance test, drawing the subject's stance and marking how the sway changed when the eyes closed. One line: name which system takes over when vision is removed, judging from your own sway result."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a branching chart of boxes and arrows that starts at a Weber result and ends at a named finding, with Rinne as the second branch. Fill in every ending, normal, conductive on one side, sensorineural on one side. Then run your own subject's results through it and mark the path they took. One line: name an ending your chart cannot reach, and what you would need to add to reach it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hearing and equilibrium testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Skeletal Muscle Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w6-nmj","name":"Neuromuscular junction","can":"Trace transmission at the neuromuscular junction from the motor neuron action potential to the end plate potential and explain why every action potential in the motor neuron produces one in the muscle fiber.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which neurotransmitter does a somatic motor neuron release onto a skeletal muscle fiber?\nA) Norepinephrine  |  B) Acetylcholine\nC) Glutamate  |  D) Glycine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Acetylcholine","why":"Every somatic motor neuron in the body releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, which is why one drug class can affect all voluntary muscle. Norepinephrine is the transmitter of most sympathetic postganglionic fibers onto smooth muscle and glands, not onto skeletal muscle. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter inside the central nervous system, and glycine is an inhibitory transmitter in the spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"When the action potential reaches the axon terminal, which ion enters the terminal and triggers vesicle fusion?\nA) Ca2+  |  B) Na+\nC) K+  |  D) Cl-\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Ca2+","why":"Depolarization of the terminal opens voltage gated Ca2+ channels, and the Ca2+ that enters is the direct signal for synaptic vesicles to fuse with the membrane and release acetylcholine. Na+ entry carries the action potential along the axon but does not itself trigger release. K+ efflux repolarizes the terminal, and Cl- movement plays no role in the release step."},{"t":"mc","q":"What kind of receptor does acetylcholine bind on the motor end plate?\nA) A beta adrenergic receptor coupled to adenylyl cyclase\nB) A muscarinic cholinergic receptor coupled to a G protein\nC) A nicotinic cholinergic receptor that is itself an ion channel\nD) A ryanodine receptor in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A nicotinic cholinergic receptor that is itself an ion channel","why":"The end plate receptor is nicotinic: the binding site and the channel pore are parts of the same protein, so the channel opens within a fraction of a millisecond of acetylcholine binding. Muscarinic receptors also bind acetylcholine but act through G proteins and are found on cardiac and smooth muscle and glands, not on the skeletal end plate. The ryanodine receptor sits on the sarcoplasmic reticulum and is opened later in the sequence, and beta adrenergic receptors bind catecholamines rather than acetylcholine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme in the synaptic cleft breaks down acetylcholine?\nA) Monoamine oxidase  |  B) Acetylcholinesterase\nC) Catechol O methyltransferase  |  D) Choline acetyltransferase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Acetylcholinesterase","why":"Acetylcholinesterase is anchored in the basal lamina of the cleft and splits acetylcholine into choline and acetate within a couple of milliseconds, which is what allows the end plate to repolarize between action potentials. Choline acetyltransferase does the opposite job, building acetylcholine inside the terminal from choline and acetyl CoA. Monoamine oxidase and catechol O methyltransferase degrade catecholamines, not acetylcholine."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is an end plate potential?\nA) A slow depolarization produced by a G protein coupled second messenger\nB) An all or none depolarization produced by voltage gated Na+ channels\nC) A graded hyperpolarization produced by ligand gated Cl- channels\nD) A graded depolarization produced by ligand gated channels\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A graded depolarization produced by ligand gated channels","why":"The end plate potential is graded: its size depends on how much acetylcholine was released and how many receptor channels opened, and it spreads passively to the surrounding sarcolemma. It is not all or none, because the end plate membrane itself is packed with ligand gated channels rather than voltage gated Na+ channels, which live in the folds just outside the end plate and generate the muscle action potential. Nothing at the end plate hyperpolarizes the fiber, and the response is far too fast to involve a second messenger cascade."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The end plate potential is produced by acetylcholine opening voltage gated Na+ channels on the motor end plate.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The end plate potential is produced by acetylcholine opening ligand gated nicotinic receptor channels; the voltage gated Na+ channels sit in the sarcolemma around the end plate and generate the muscle action potential.","why":"Two different channels do two different jobs. The nicotinic receptor is opened by the chemical, not by voltage, and it produces a graded depolarization. That depolarization then spreads to the surrounding membrane and opens voltage gated Na+ channels, which fire the all or none action potential. Mixing these up hides why the end plate potential is graded and why a receptor blocker such as curare stops transmission without touching the Na+ channels."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the axon terminal, the cleft, and the motor end plate large enough to number six steps in order: action potential arrives, calcium channels open, vesicles fuse, ACh crosses, receptor channels open, end plate potential. Mark which ion moves at each channel and which direction. Then write one line: the end plate potential is far bigger than it needs to be, so say what that margin buys the muscle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same junction three times, normal, then with the receptors on the end plate blocked, then with the calcium channels in the terminal blocked. In each version show how far the signal gets before it dies and circle the last structure still working. One line: two of the three look identical to someone watching the patient, so write what you would measure to tell those two apart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace transmission at the neuromuscular junction from the motor neuron action potential to the end plate potential and explain why every action potential in the motor neuron produces one in the muscle fiber."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Neuromuscular junction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-ec-coupling","name":"Excitation contraction coupling","can":"Trace excitation contraction coupling from the T tubule through the DHP and ryanodine receptors to calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the dihydropyridine receptor located and what does it sense?\nA) In the sarcolemma at the end plate, where it senses acetylcholine\nB) In the thin filament, where it senses tropomyosin position\nC) In the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane, where it senses Ca2+\nD) In the T tubule membrane, where it senses voltage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) In the T tubule membrane, where it senses voltage","why":"The dihydropyridine receptor is an L type Ca2+ channel sitting in the T tubule membrane, and in skeletal muscle its main job is to act as a voltage sensor that changes shape when the T tubule depolarizes. Placing it in the sarcoplasmic reticulum confuses it with the ryanodine receptor, which is the release channel it controls. It does not bind acetylcholine and it is a membrane protein, not part of the thin filament."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the ryanodine receptor do?\nA) It pumps Ca2+ from the cytosol back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum\nB) It is the Ca2+ release channel in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane\nC) It binds Ca2+ inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum lumen\nD) It is the voltage sensor in the T tubule membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is the Ca2+ release channel in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane","why":"The ryanodine receptor is a large channel in the terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and when it opens Ca2+ pours out into the cytosol. Pumping Ca2+ back in is the job of SERCA, and buffering Ca2+ inside the lumen is the job of calsequestrin. The T tubule voltage sensor is the dihydropyridine receptor, which physically opens the ryanodine receptor."},{"t":"mc","q":"What structures form a triad in skeletal muscle?\nA) One T tubule and the two terminal cisternae beside it\nB) Three sarcomeres arranged end to end within a single myofibril\nC) One sarcolemma with two mitochondria pressed against it\nD) Three myofibrils bundled inside one sarcolemma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One T tubule and the two terminal cisternae beside it","why":"The triad is the functional unit of excitation contraction coupling: the central T tubule carries the electrical signal and the two flanking cisternae hold the Ca2+ that will be released. This close apposition is what allows the voltage sensor and the release channel to touch. Counting myofibrils, sarcomeres, or mitochondria describes other features of muscle organization and none of them make the electrical to chemical link."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which protein inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds Ca2+ and keeps the free Ca2+ concentration in the lumen low?\nA) Troponin C  |  B) Calmodulin\nC) Calsequestrin  |  D) Titin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Calsequestrin","why":"Calsequestrin sits in the lumen and binds large amounts of Ca2+ loosely, which lets the sarcoplasmic reticulum store a big total load while keeping free luminal Ca2+ low enough that SERCA can keep pumping against it. Troponin C is the cytosolic Ca2+ sensor on the thin filament, and calmodulin is the Ca2+ binding protein that regulates smooth muscle rather than skeletal thin filaments. Titin is the elastic structural protein running from the Z disc to the thick filament."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one T tubule diving into the fiber with a terminal cisterna of sarcoplasmic reticulum on each side. Put the DHP receptor in the T tubule membrane and the ryanodine receptor in the SR membrane, touching each other. Draw the action potential arriving and use arrows to show what moves and what only changes shape. Mark where the calcium comes from. One line: say why the T tubule has to go inside the fiber rather than stay on the surface."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw skeletal and cardiac coupling side by side with the same structures in both. In the skeletal one, show the DHP receptor opening the ryanodine receptor by physical contact. In the cardiac one, show calcium entering from outside first and that calcium opening the ryanodine receptor. Label the calcium source in each. One line: one of the two stops working if you remove calcium from the fluid outside the cell, so name it and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace excitation contraction coupling from the T tubule through the DHP and ryanodine receptors to calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Excitation contraction coupling. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-crossbridge-cycle","name":"Crossbridge cycle","can":"Order the steps of the crossbridge cycle, binding, power stroke, detachment, and reactivation, and state where ATP binds and where it is hydrolyzed.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle fiber in rigor is bathed in a solution containing ATP but no Ca2+. What happens?\nA) The heads detach and the fiber relaxes\nB) The fiber contracts strongly, because ATP is the energy source\nC) The fiber stays rigid, because Ca2+ is needed for detachment\nD) The heads detach and immediately reattach, producing sustained cycling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The heads detach and the fiber relaxes","why":"ATP binding alone is enough to break the actin myosin bond, which is why adding ATP releases rigor even when Ca2+ is absent. Without Ca2+ the heads cannot reattach, so the fiber goes limp instead of contracting or cycling. Detachment requires ATP rather than Ca2+, and this experiment separates the two roles cleanly."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change would allow a muscle to hold a given force while using less ATP?\nA) Myosin heads that stay attached longer during each cycle\nB) A larger Ca2+ transient with each action potential\nC) A faster SERCA pump in the sarcoplasmic reticulum\nD) Myosin heads that hydrolyze ATP faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Myosin heads that stay attached longer during each cycle","why":"Force depends on how many heads are attached at any instant, so a head that spends a longer fraction of its cycle attached contributes the same tension for fewer ATP molecules per second. Slow postural muscle uses exactly this strategy. Faster hydrolysis speeds cycling and raises the ATP cost, a bigger Ca2+ transient does not change the cost of each cycle, and a faster pump increases ATP use rather than reducing it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of which protein carries the ATPase activity that powers contraction?\nA) The troponin complex on the thin filament  |  B) The actin monomer itself\nC) The head of the myosin molecule  |  D) The tail of the myosin molecule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The head of the myosin molecule","why":"The globular myosin head contains both the actin binding site and the site that binds and hydrolyzes ATP, which is why the same structure that grabs the thin filament is also the motor. The myosin tail packs into the backbone of the thick filament and does no chemistry. Troponin is the Ca2+ switch on the thin filament and actin provides the track, but neither hydrolyzes the ATP that drives cycling."},{"t":"mc","q":"What causes the myosin head to let go of actin at the end of a crossbridge cycle?\nA) A fresh ATP molecule binds to the myosin head\nB) Ca2+ leaves troponin C\nC) Phosphate is released from the myosin head\nD) ATP is hydrolyzed on the myosin head\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A fresh ATP molecule binds to the myosin head","why":"Binding, not hydrolysis, is what breaks the bond: when ATP occupies the nucleotide site the affinity of the head for actin drops sharply and the crossbridge detaches. Hydrolysis comes immediately afterward and re cocks the detached head. Ca2+ leaving troponin ends the whole contraction rather than a single cycle, and phosphate release is the trigger for the power stroke earlier in the cycle."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: About six hours after death, a body's skeletal muscles have become stiff and the joints resist being moved. Muscle ATP, measured in a sample, is below 5 percent of its living value, and cytosolic Ca2+ is high because the sarcoplasmic reticulum has leaked its store.\nWhy are the muscles stiff, and which single change would allow the fibers to relax?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Without ATP the myosin heads cannot detach from actin, so nearly every head is locked in the bound state; supplying ATP would let the heads release","why":"Detachment is the step that costs ATP. With Ca2+ high and binding sites open, every head that attaches completes its power stroke and then waits for an ATP that never arrives, so the fibers fill with bound heads and become rigid. This is rigor. Only ATP can break the bond; removing Ca2+ would stop new attachments but would not release the heads already bound. In life, rigor is what a muscle would do if it ever ran completely out of ATP, which is why cells guard their ATP so carefully."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, walk through one turn of the crossbridge cycle. Say where ATP binds, where it is hydrolyzed, which step generates force, and what happens to the cycle when ATP runs out.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"ATP binds the myosin head, and binding makes the head detach from actin; the head hydrolyzes ATP to ADP and Pi, which cocks the head into its high energy position; the cocked head binds actin when Ca2+ has exposed the binding site; release of Pi triggers the power stroke, which pulls the thin filament toward the center of the sarcomere; ADP is released and the head stays tightly bound until a new ATP arrives; without ATP the heads cannot detach, so the muscle locks in rigor; the cycle repeats as long as ATP is present and Ca2+ keeps the binding sites exposed","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the cycle as four myosin heads around a loop in order: binding, power stroke, detachment, reactivation. At each head show the thin filament position and what is sitting on the head, nothing, ATP, or ADP and phosphate. Put a star where ATP binds and a different mark where ATP is hydrolyzed, because they are not the same step. One line: name the step that stalls in rigor mortis and what the head is stuck to."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a stretch of thin filament with a ruler under it, and draw the myosin head at all four points of the cycle above it, showing how far the filament has traveled by each point. Mark the one step where movement actually happens and the three where the filament does not move. Then write one line on why taking ATP away leaves the muscle stiff rather than limp, using the step you marked."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the steps of the crossbridge cycle, binding, power stroke, detachment, and reactivation, and state where ATP binds and where it is hydrolyzed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Crossbridge cycle. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-calcium-regulation","name":"Calcium and the regulatory proteins","can":"Explain how calcium binding to troponin moves tropomyosin off the myosin binding site and predict what happens to contraction if calcium cannot be released.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A treatment removes troponin and tropomyosin from the thin filament while leaving actin, myosin, and ATP intact. What do you predict?\nA) Cycling only when Ca2+ is high, exactly as before\nB) Cycling only when Ca2+ is below the resting level\nC) No crossbridge cycling at any Ca2+ concentration\nD) Continuous crossbridge cycling at any Ca2+ concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Continuous crossbridge cycling at any Ca2+ concentration","why":"Troponin and tropomyosin exist to keep myosin off actin, so stripping them leaves the binding sites permanently exposed and the heads cycle as long as ATP lasts. This is the strongest evidence that Ca2+ does not activate myosin directly, it merely removes an inhibition. Cycling cannot become Ca2+ dependent when the Ca2+ sensing protein is gone, and it certainly does not reverse to require low Ca2+."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which subunit of the troponin complex binds Ca2+?\nA) Troponin M  |  B) Troponin T\nC) Troponin I  |  D) Troponin C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Troponin C","why":"Troponin C is the Ca2+ sensor of the thin filament, and the C in its name is a useful reminder. Troponin T is the subunit that binds tropomyosin, and troponin I is the subunit that holds the complex against actin and inhibits binding when Ca2+ is low. There is no troponin M in skeletal muscle, although calmodulin plays the Ca2+ sensing role in smooth muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"What covers the myosin binding sites on actin in a resting skeletal muscle fiber?\nA) Tropomyosin  |  B) Troponin C\nC) Titin  |  D) Calsequestrin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Tropomyosin","why":"Tropomyosin is a long fibrous protein that lies in the groove of the actin helix and physically blocks the sites where myosin heads would attach. Troponin C does not block anything, it senses Ca2+ and repositions tropomyosin through the rest of the complex. Titin is the elastic protein running from the Z disc to the thick filament, and calsequestrin buffers Ca2+ inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which three proteins make up the thin filament of skeletal muscle?\nA) Actin, tropomyosin, and troponin\nB) Myosin, tropomyosin, and calsequestrin\nC) Actin, troponin, and calmodulin\nD) Actin, myosin, and titin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Actin, tropomyosin, and troponin","why":"The thin filament is a double helix of actin monomers with tropomyosin lying along it and one troponin complex attached every seven actin monomers, and that arrangement is what makes the whole filament a Ca2+ controlled switch. Myosin belongs to the thick filament, and titin links the thick filament to the Z disc rather than forming part of the thin filament. Calsequestrin is inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and calmodulin is the Ca2+ sensor of smooth muscle."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 42 year old has a plasma Ca2+ of 1.6 mmol/L (normal 2.2 to 2.6) after thyroid surgery. She has tingling around her mouth and painful cramping spasms of her hands and feet. A classmate reasons that low Ca2+ should mean less Ca2+ for troponin, so her muscles ought to be weak, not spasming.\nWhy does low plasma Ca2+ produce spasms rather than weakness?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The Ca2+ that binds troponin comes from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, not the plasma, so contraction is intact; low extracellular Ca2+ instead makes motor neurons and muscle membranes fire more easily, so the muscles are stimulated spontaneously","why":"The two pools of Ca2+ have to be kept apart. Contraction uses Ca2+ stored inside the fiber in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and that store is not depleted by a low plasma level. Extracellular Ca2+ has a separate job: it stabilizes voltage gated Na+ channels. When it falls, the channels open with less depolarization, so nerves and muscle fibers fire spontaneously. The spasms are therefore a sign of hyperexcitable membranes driving a normal contractile system, and the fix is to raise plasma Ca2+, not to alter troponin."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Ca2+ triggers skeletal muscle contraction by binding directly to the myosin heads and switching on their ATPase.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Ca2+ binds troponin on the thin filament, which shifts tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin; the myosin heads are already energized and simply gain access to actin.","why":"In skeletal muscle the switch is on the thin filament, not the thick one. Myosin heads hydrolyze ATP and sit cocked whether or not Ca2+ is present; what stops them at rest is tropomyosin covering their binding sites. Ca2+ binding to troponin moves tropomyosin aside and lets the cycle run. The statement describes something closer to smooth muscle, where Ca2+ acts on the myosin side through calmodulin and a kinase, and even there Ca2+ does not bind myosin itself."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a stretch of thin filament twice, with actin, tropomyosin, and the troponin complex on both. In the first, no calcium, with tropomyosin lying over the myosin binding sites. In the second, calcium bound to troponin and tropomyosin rolled away. Draw a myosin head reaching in on both and show what it meets. One line: calcium never touches actin or myosin here, so name what it does bind and what that binding moves."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a resting sarcomere with a full sarcoplasmic reticulum, then draw the same sarcomere in a fiber whose ryanodine receptors will not open. In both, show the calcium, the troponin, the tropomyosin, and the myosin heads. The nerve, the end plate potential, and the ATP supply are all normal in the second one. One line: say whether that muscle feels stiff or limp to a doctor moving the limb, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how calcium binding to troponin moves tropomyosin off the myosin binding site and predict what happens to contraction if calcium cannot be released."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Calcium and the regulatory proteins. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-relaxation","name":"Relaxation and calcium removal","can":"Explain how SERCA and acetylcholinesterase end a contraction and predict the result if either one fails.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug that specifically inhibits SERCA is applied to a muscle fiber. Predict the twitch.\nA) Force is reduced and relaxation is faster than normal\nB) Force never develops, because the store cannot be refilled in time\nC) Force develops normally but relaxation is slowed or incomplete\nD) Force develops and relaxes normally, because release is unaffected\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Force develops normally but relaxation is slowed or incomplete","why":"For the first twitch the store is already loaded, so release and force development are normal, and the drug shows itself in the falling phase, where cytosolic Ca2+ lingers and the crossbridges keep cycling. Over repeated stimulation force would also fall as the store fails to refill, but the immediate and defining sign is slowed relaxation. Nothing about blocking a pump makes relaxation faster."},{"t":"mc","q":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase. What is the immediate effect at the junction?\nA) The nicotinic receptors are blocked, so the end plate potential shrinks\nB) Acetylcholine release from the terminal increases\nC) Acetylcholine lingers in the cleft, so the end plate potential is prolonged\nD) The Ca2+ transient inside the fiber is prolonged directly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Acetylcholine lingers in the cleft, so the end plate potential is prolonged","why":"The enzyme is the only fast route for clearing transmitter, so inhibiting it leaves acetylcholine free to rebind and hold receptor channels open past the normal couple of milliseconds. The resulting long end plate potential can drive several muscle action potentials from one nerve impulse, which is why small doses strengthen contraction in myasthenia gravis. Release from the nerve is unchanged, the receptors are not blocked, and the Ca2+ transient lengthens only as a downstream consequence of extra action potentials."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle fiber is cooled from 37 C to 25 C, slowing SERCA. Which part of the twitch changes most?\nA) The contraction phase becomes steeper  |  B) Peak force doubles\nC) The latent period disappears  |  D) The relaxation phase lengthens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The relaxation phase lengthens","why":"Relaxation time tracks how fast Ca2+ is cleared from the cytosol, so slowing the pump stretches that phase out most obviously. The latent period reflects excitation contraction coupling and cannot disappear, and the rise of force depends mainly on crossbridge attachment rather than on the pump. Cooling does not double peak force, although a longer Ca2+ transient can raise twitch force somewhat by keeping the filament activated longer."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle relaxes normally in every respect except that the end plate potential lasts much longer than usual. Which component has failed?\nA) Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft\nB) SERCA in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane\nC) Calsequestrin in the sarcoplasmic reticulum lumen\nD) The ryanodine receptor in the terminal cisternae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft","why":"A prolonged end plate potential is a synaptic finding, and the only listed protein that works in the cleft is the enzyme that clears acetylcholine. All the other candidates operate on Ca2+ inside the fiber, and their failure would show up as an abnormal Ca2+ transient or abnormal relaxation of force, not as a longer electrical event at the end plate. Being able to place a defect from the measurement that is abnormal is the point of this comparison."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fiber is modified to express extra SERCA pumps. Predict the effect on its twitch.\nA) Relaxation is faster, because cytosolic Ca2+ falls sooner\nB) Relaxation is slower, because more ATP is diverted from the crossbridges\nC) The latent period is shorter, because the store is fuller\nD) Peak force is higher, because more Ca2+ is released\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Relaxation is faster, because cytosolic Ca2+ falls sooner","why":"More pumps clear Ca2+ from the cytosol more quickly, so troponin empties sooner and the crossbridges shut down earlier, giving a briefer twitch. Fast twitch fibers really do have a higher density of SERCA than slow fibers for this reason. Pumping does consume ATP, but not at a rate that starves the crossbridges in a single twitch, and the number of pumps does not shorten excitation contraction coupling or raise the amount of Ca2+ released per impulse."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how a skeletal muscle contraction ends. Include the roles of acetylcholinesterase and SERCA, and predict what happens to the muscle if each one stops working.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"acetylcholinesterase breaks down acetylcholine in the cleft, so the end plate repolarizes and no new action potentials are triggered; SERCA pumps Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum using ATP; as cytosolic Ca2+ falls, Ca2+ leaves troponin and tropomyosin covers the binding sites again; crossbridges stop forming and the fiber relaxes; relaxation therefore needs ATP; if acetylcholinesterase fails, the end plate stays depolarized, causing twitching and then weakness because Na+ channels stay inactivated; if SERCA fails, Ca2+ stays high in the cytosol, relaxation is slow, and repeated stimulation produces sustained contraction","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw both shut off jobs on one page: acetylcholinesterase chopping ACh in the synaptic cleft, and SERCA in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane pumping calcium back in. Mark which of the two costs ATP. Under each, draw a small graph of cytosolic calcium against time and shade the part of the falling limb that job is responsible for. One line: say why relaxation burns energy when it looks like nothing is happening."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two failures side by side, acetylcholinesterase blocked by an organophosphate, and SERCA stopped. For each, show where the signal is stuck, ACh piled up in the cleft or calcium piled up in the cytosol, and draw the muscle underneath in the state it ends up in. One line: both muscles are contracted and will not release, so name the one thing you could measure or do that separates the two."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how SERCA and acetylcholinesterase end a contraction and predict the result if either one fails."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Relaxation and calcium removal. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-twitch","name":"The muscle twitch","can":"Label the latent period, contraction phase, and relaxation phase on a twitch tracing and explain what limits each phase.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A fiber is cooled so that SERCA works at half speed. Which phase of the twitch lengthens most?\nA) The relaxation phase\nB) None, because temperature does not affect a single twitch\nC) The latent period\nD) The contraction phase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The relaxation phase","why":"Relaxation waits on the removal of Ca2+ from the cytosol, so halving the pump rate stretches that phase noticeably. The latent period depends on release rather than removal, and the rise of tension depends mainly on crossbridge attachment, so both change much less. Cooling does slow every protein somewhat, but the pump dependent phase is where the effect shows up most clearly."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug prolongs the opening of ryanodine receptors so Ca2+ release lasts longer. Which change to the twitch do you predict?\nA) A longer contraction phase and a higher peak tension\nB) A shorter relaxation phase and an unchanged peak tension\nC) No change, because the amount of Ca2+ released is unchanged\nD) A longer latent period and a lower peak tension\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A longer contraction phase and a higher peak tension","why":"Keeping the release channel open longer keeps troponin occupied longer, so crossbridges continue to attach and tension keeps climbing before Ca2+ removal takes over. Peak force in a single twitch is normally submaximal precisely because Ca2+ is withdrawn before all binding sites are engaged, so extending release raises it. The latent period depends on how quickly release begins, not on how long it lasts, and relaxation gets longer rather than shorter when there is more Ca2+ to clear."},{"t":"mc","q":"An experiment slows conduction of the action potential into the T tubules without changing anything else. Which part of the twitch changes?\nA) The relaxation phase lengthens  |  B) The latent period lengthens\nC) Peak tension rises  |  D) The contraction phase shortens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The latent period lengthens","why":"Everything that happens between the stimulus and the first rise in tension belongs to the latent period, and T tubule conduction is one of those steps, so delaying it pushes the start of force later. Relaxation is governed by Ca2+ removal and is untouched. Slower delivery of the signal does not increase force, and the contraction phase reflects crossbridge attachment rather than how the signal arrived."},{"t":"mc","q":"A twitch tracing shows a latent period of 3 ms, a contraction phase of 25 ms, and a relaxation phase of 55 ms. Roughly how long after the stimulus does tension peak?\nA) About 25 ms  |  B) About 28 ms\nC) About 55 ms  |  D) About 83 ms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 28 ms","why":"Tension peaks at the end of the contraction phase, so you add the latent period to the contraction phase, giving 3 plus 25, or 28 ms. Using 25 alone ignores the delay before force begins. The value of 55 is the relaxation phase alone, and 83 ms is the end of the whole twitch, when tension has returned to baseline."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a muscle twitch?\nA) The electrical response of a fiber to a single nerve impulse\nB) A sustained contraction produced by repeated stimulation\nC) The smallest force a whole muscle can produce\nD) The mechanical response of a fiber to a single action potential\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The mechanical response of a fiber to a single action potential","why":"A twitch is one contraction and relaxation cycle following one action potential, and it is the basic mechanical unit from which all larger responses are built. The electrical event is the action potential itself, which is a separate and much briefer signal. A sustained response to repeated stimulation is tetanus, and the smallest force a whole muscle can produce depends on how many motor units are recruited, not on the twitch of a single fiber."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The latent period of a twitch is the delay between the stimulus and the start of tension, during which the action potential spreads and Ca2+ is released.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The latent period is not dead time. The action potential is propagating along the sarcolemma and into the T tubules, the ryanodine receptors are opening, Ca2+ is binding troponin, and heads are beginning to attach. Only after all of that does tension at the tendon begin to rise. That is why the latent period is nearly fixed and does not depend on load or on how fast the myosin heads cycle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a twitch tracing with time on the x axis and tension on the y, and put an arrow on the axis at the stimulus. Bracket and label the latent period, the contraction phase, and the relaxation phase. Beside each bracket write the one event inside the fiber that sets how long that phase lasts. One line: the tension trace is flat through the latent period, so say what is going on during it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three traces stacked on one shared time axis: the muscle action potential on top, cytosolic calcium in the middle, and tension at the bottom, all lined up. Show that the action potential has finished long before tension peaks. One line: say which of the upper two curves the tension curve actually follows, and what that lag lets you do by stimulating again."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the latent period, contraction phase, and relaxation phase on a twitch tracing and explain what limits each phase."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: The muscle twitch. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-summation-tetanus","name":"Summation and tetanus","can":"Explain wave summation and distinguish unfused from fused tetanus by stimulus frequency and calcium accumulation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A fiber with an 80 ms twitch is stimulated three ways: every 100 ms, every 30 ms, and every 10 ms. Match the three tracings.\nA) Unfused tetanus, separate twitches, fused tetanus\nB) Separate twitches, unfused tetanus, fused tetanus\nC) Fused tetanus, unfused tetanus, separate twitches\nD) Separate twitches, fused tetanus, unfused tetanus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Separate twitches, unfused tetanus, fused tetanus","why":"Compare each interval with the 80 ms twitch. At 100 ms the twitch has ended before the next stimulus, so the responses are separate. At 30 ms the fiber has started to relax but not finished, so tension builds with visible dips, which is unfused tetanus. At 10 ms there is no time for any relaxation at all, so the tracing is a smooth plateau. Ordering these correctly is just a matter of holding the twitch duration next to each interval."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fiber whose twitch lasts 100 ms is stimulated every 200 ms. What does the tracing show?\nA) Separate twitches with a full return to baseline\nB) Unfused tetanus with oscillating tension\nC) Fused tetanus with a smooth plateau\nD) A single twitch, because the later stimuli fall in the refractory period\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Separate twitches with a full return to baseline","why":"An interval of 200 ms is twice the twitch duration, so each contraction is completely over before the next stimulus arrives and there is nothing left to add to. Summation requires the stimulus interval to be shorter than the twitch, not longer. The membrane refractory period is only a few milliseconds, far shorter than any of these intervals, so every stimulus produces its own twitch."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fiber has a twitch lasting 40 ms. Approximately what is the lowest stimulus frequency that will produce any wave summation?\nA) About 5 Hz  |  B) About 25 Hz\nC) About 100 Hz  |  D) About 400 Hz\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 25 Hz","why":"Summation begins when the interval between stimuli becomes shorter than the twitch duration, and an interval of 40 ms corresponds to 25 stimuli per second. At 5 Hz the interval is 200 ms and each twitch is long finished. Higher frequencies such as 100 Hz will certainly summate and probably fuse, but the question asks for the lowest frequency at which summation appears at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is unfused tetanus?\nA) A contraction in which only some motor units are active\nB) A smooth maximal contraction with no fluctuation in tension\nC) A high tension response with visible dips between stimuli\nD) A single twitch of unusually large amplitude and duration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A high tension response with visible dips between stimuli","why":"In unfused, or incomplete, tetanus the stimuli come fast enough that the fiber cannot relax fully between them, so tension builds but the tracing still oscillates. When the frequency rises further and no relaxation at all is possible, the oscillations disappear and the response is fused. A single twitch has one peak, and partial activation of a muscle is a question of recruitment rather than of stimulus frequency."},{"t":"mc","q":"What distinguishes fused tetanus from unfused tetanus on a tracing?\nA) Fused tetanus shows a return to baseline between each stimulus\nB) Fused tetanus shows a slow decline in tension from the first stimulus onward\nC) Fused tetanus shows larger oscillations at a lower frequency\nD) Fused tetanus shows a smooth plateau with no relaxation between stimuli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fused tetanus shows a smooth plateau with no relaxation between stimuli","why":"Fusion means the stimuli arrive so close together that relaxation never begins, so individual twitches are no longer distinguishable and the tracing is a flat maximal plateau. Oscillations are the signature of unfused tetanus, and a full return to baseline between stimuli means the stimuli are too far apart for summation at all. A steady decline from the first stimulus describes fatigue rather than fusion."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A fast twitch fiber has a twitch lasting 60 ms and fuses into a smooth tetanus at about 80 Hz. A drug that halves the rate of SERCA is applied. The single twitch now reaches the same peak tension, but its total duration doubles to 120 ms.\nPredict how the drug changes the stimulus frequency needed for fused tetanus, and why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Fusion now occurs at a lower frequency, because each twitch lasts longer, so stimuli do not need to arrive as close together to catch the fiber before it relaxes","why":"Fusion happens when the next stimulus arrives before relaxation is complete. What sets that interval is the twitch duration, which depends mostly on how fast Ca2+ is pumped away. Halve the pump rate and the twitch lasts twice as long, so a stimulus arriving at half the old frequency still lands inside the previous twitch. The fiber therefore summates and fuses at a lower frequency. This is also why slow oxidative fibers, which have slower Ca2+ removal, fuse at lower frequencies than fast fibers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis with stimulus marks below it, and above it draw four tension traces at rising frequency: a single twitch, two twitches close enough to add, unfused tetanus, and fused tetanus. Keep the y axis identical on all four so the heights are comparable. Mark where the trace dips between stimuli and where the dips disappear. One line: say what stops dipping first, the tension or the cytosolic calcium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw cytosolic calcium against time for a low frequency train and a high frequency train, one above the other, with SERCA pulling calcium back between stimuli in both. Show why calcium stacks up in one and clears in the other. Under each, draw the tension trace it produces. One line: the action potentials themselves never add together, so write what does."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain wave summation and distinguish unfused from fused tetanus by stimulus frequency and calcium accumulation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Summation and tetanus. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-motor-units","name":"Motor units and recruitment","can":"Define the motor unit and explain how size of the unit and orderly recruitment grade the force a whole muscle produces.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which motor units are active while a person simply stands still for several minutes?\nA) No units at all, since standing requires no muscle activity\nB) Large, fast, fatigable units\nC) Small, slow, fatigue resistant units\nD) All units in the muscle, firing at a low rate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Small, slow, fatigue resistant units","why":"Quiet standing is a low force task that must be sustained for a long time, which is exactly the job description of the small units recruited first by the size principle. Their oxidative metabolism lets them work for hours without fatiguing. Large fatigable units would exhaust themselves within a minute and are held in reserve for high force efforts, and posture certainly does require continuous low level contraction."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a motor unit?\nA) One motor neuron and all of the muscle fibers it innervates\nB) One muscle fiber and all of the motor neurons that innervate it\nC) All of the motor neurons that supply a single muscle\nD) One sarcomere and the portion of the sarcoplasmic reticulum around it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One motor neuron and all of the muscle fibers it innervates","why":"A motor unit is the smallest part of a muscle the nervous system can control, because every fiber in the unit is reached by the same axon and contracts whenever that neuron fires. A skeletal muscle fiber is normally innervated by exactly one motor neuron, so the reverse definition does not describe anything real. The full set of neurons supplying a muscle is called the motor neuron pool, and the sarcomere is a structural unit rather than a functional one."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does innervation ratio mean?\nA) The number of muscle fibers controlled by a single motor neuron\nB) The ratio of fast to slow fibers within one muscle\nC) The proportion of motor units active during a contraction\nD) The number of motor neurons supplying a single muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The number of muscle fibers controlled by a single motor neuron","why":"Innervation ratio counts fibers per neuron, so a low ratio means a small motor unit and finer control while a high ratio means a large unit and coarser but more powerful steps of force. The number of neurons supplying a muscle describes the size of the motor neuron pool. Fiber type composition and the fraction of units currently active are both important properties of a muscle, but neither is what this term names."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle would you expect to have the smallest motor units?\nA) The gluteus maximus of the hip  |  B) The gastrocnemius of the calf\nC) The quadriceps of the thigh  |  D) The extraocular muscles of the eye\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The extraocular muscles of the eye","why":"Muscles that make small, precisely graded movements have very low innervation ratios, sometimes only a handful of fibers per neuron, so each additional unit adds only a tiny increment of force. The eye muscles are the classic example, along with the small muscles of the hand and the larynx. The large limb and hip muscles are built for force rather than precision and have hundreds to over a thousand fibers per neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is meant by recruitment in a skeletal muscle?\nA) Increasing the amount of Ca2+ released per action potential\nB) Increasing the firing rate of the motor units already active\nC) Activating additional motor units to increase whole muscle force\nD) Adding new muscle fibers to a motor unit through training\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Activating additional motor units to increase whole muscle force","why":"Recruitment adds units, and it is one of the two ways the nervous system grades force, the other being the firing rate of the units already active. Raising the firing rate is rate coding and produces summation within a unit. Training changes fiber size rather than adding fibers to existing units, and the Ca2+ released per action potential is not under moment to moment neural control."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, define a motor unit and explain how the nervous system grades the force of a whole muscle from a light touch to a maximal effort.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"a motor unit is one motor neuron plus every muscle fiber it innervates; all fibers in a unit contract together, so the unit is the smallest amount of force the nervous system can add; small units (few fibers, slow oxidative) give fine control; large units (many fibers, fast) give big force; units are recruited in order of size, small first and large last (the size principle); force is also graded by raising the firing rate of active units toward tetanus (rate coding); asynchronous recruitment lets units take turns during sustained submaximal effort, which delays fatigue","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one alpha motor neuron with its axon branching onto every fiber it owns, then draw a much larger motor unit beside it. Name a real muscle for each and label which is which. Underneath, draw a force trace as units are switched on one at a time, smallest first. One line: say why an eye muscle and a calf muscle are built with different unit sizes, in terms of the job each one has."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a rising force curve for a whole muscle, from a light hold up to maximum, with force on the y axis and effort on the x. Along the curve, mark the stretch where new motor units are still being added and the stretch where the units already firing are simply firing faster. At three points along it, draw the fiber type being brought in. One line: name the fibers that lifted the coffee cup, and say why those and not the others."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define the motor unit and explain how size of the unit and orderly recruitment grade the force a whole muscle produces."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Motor units and recruitment. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-length-tension","name":"Length tension relationship","can":"Interpret a length tension curve and explain why tension falls at very short and very long sarcomere lengths.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle is tested isometrically at four lengths. Length A: total tension 100, passive 0. Length B: total 110, passive 20. Length C: total 120, passive 60. Length D: total 70, passive 0. Which length is closest to the optimal length for active tension?\nA) Length B  |  B) Length C\nC) Length D  |  D) Length A\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Length A","why":"Subtract passive from total to get active tension: A gives 100, B gives 90, C gives 60, and D gives 70, so A produces the most crossbridge force and sits closest to optimal length. Length C is the trap, because its total tension is the highest recorded, but most of that is elastic recoil rather than crossbridge force. Length B is already onto the descending limb, with passive tension starting to appear. Length D has no passive tension at all, which marks it as a short length on the ascending limb."},{"t":"mc","q":"A relaxed muscle is stretched progressively beyond its optimal length. Predict what happens to passive tension.\nA) It falls toward zero as the filaments separate\nB) It stays flat until active tension reaches zero\nC) It rises steeply as the muscle is stretched further\nD) It rises briefly and then returns to zero\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises steeply as the muscle is stretched further","why":"Titin and the surrounding connective tissue behave like nonlinear springs, so passive tension is near zero around resting length and then climbs steeply the further the muscle is stretched. It cannot fall as the muscle lengthens, since that is the direction that loads the elastic elements. It does not wait for active tension to disappear before rising; the two curves overlap across a wide range of lengths. And elastic recoil does not release on its own while the stretch is maintained."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does shortening a sarcomere from 2.2 to 2.0 micrometers fail to increase active tension?\nA) The extra sliding crosses the bare zone, which has no myosin heads\nB) Ca2+ release falls as the sarcomere shortens\nC) Titin begins to resist any further shortening\nD) Thin filaments begin to detach from the Z discs at very short lengths\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The extra sliding crosses the bare zone, which has no myosin heads","why":"Between roughly 2.0 and 2.2 micrometers the thin filaments advance over the head free bare zone, so no new crossbridges are gained and tension holds flat. Ca2+ release is set by the action potential, not by length over this range. Titin is slack rather than resistive at lengths at or below optimal. Thin filaments stay anchored to the Z discs throughout, which is what allows them to transmit force at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the length tension relationship, what sets the amount of active tension a sarcomere can generate at a given length?\nA) The number of myosin heads that can reach binding sites on actin\nB) The amount of ATP stored inside the sarcomere\nC) The resting membrane potential of the muscle fiber\nD) The length of the thick filament itself\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The number of myosin heads that can reach binding sites on actin","why":"Active tension tracks the overlap between thick and thin filaments, because overlap decides how many crossbridges can form, and each crossbridge adds a fixed increment of force. Stored ATP is not limiting in a rested fiber, so it does not shape the curve. Resting membrane potential decides whether the fiber fires at all, not how much force one contraction produces. Thick filament length is fixed near 1.6 micrometers and does not change when the sarcomere is stretched."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A student squeezes a grip dynamometer with the wrist in a neutral position and records 180 N. She repeats the test with the wrist fully flexed, which shortens the finger flexor muscles in the forearm, and records only 95 N. Her effort and motor unit activation, checked by EMG, are the same in both trials.\nExplain the drop in force in the flexed position.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"At the shorter length thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments run into the Z discs, so fewer effective crossbridges form and active tension falls","why":"Force at a given activation depends on sarcomere length. In the neutral position the flexors sit near optimal length, where every myosin head faces a thin filament. Flexing the wrist shortens them onto the ascending limb of the length tension curve: thin filaments from opposite sides begin to overlap and interfere with each other, and the thick filaments press against the Z discs, so crossbridges pull less effectively. EMG confirms the nervous system did its part; the deficit is mechanical, in the sarcomere."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A sarcomere generates its greatest active tension when it is stretched as far as possible, because titin is then fully extended and pulls hardest.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A sarcomere generates its greatest active tension at an intermediate resting length where thick and thin filament overlap is optimal; at long lengths overlap is lost and active tension falls, while the rising tension from titin is passive, not active.","why":"Active tension comes from crossbridges, and the number that can form depends on how much thin filament lies beside the thick filament. Stretch the sarcomere past optimal length and thin filaments pull away from the myosin heads, so active force drops toward zero. The tension you feel in an overstretched muscle is passive tension from titin and connective tissue, which is real force but not contraction. Separating the two curves is the whole point of the length tension relationship."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the length tension curve with sarcomere length on the x axis and active tension on the y, and mark the plateau at about 2.0 to 2.2 micrometers. Above three points on the curve, one short, one on the plateau, one long, draw the sarcomere at that length with the thick and thin filaments overlapping correctly. One line: tension falls at both ends of this curve, so say why the two ends fall for different reasons."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a sarcomere at a very short length and at a very long length, big enough to show the Z discs, the thick filaments, and where the thin filaments end. In the short one, show the two things that physically get in the way. In the long one, show what has run out. One line: say which of the two you can actually reach in a joint in your own body, and what stops you reaching the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to interpret a length tension curve and explain why tension falls at very short and very long sarcomere lengths."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Length tension relationship. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-contraction-types","name":"Isometric and isotonic contraction","can":"Distinguish isometric, concentric, and eccentric contractions and predict shortening velocity from the load applied.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of contraction is most strongly associated with delayed onset muscle soreness one or two days after unfamiliar exercise?\nA) Concentric contractions\nB) Isometric contractions held at short muscle length\nC) Low load concentric contractions at high velocity\nD) Eccentric contractions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Eccentric contractions","why":"Eccentric work loads a small number of attached crossbridges with high force per bridge, which produces microscopic damage to the sarcomeres and the inflammatory response felt a day or two later. Concentric contractions distribute lower force across the working bridges and cause much less damage. Isometric holds at short lengths produce lower tension still, and short lengths are the least damaging. Fast, light concentric work generates the lowest tension of any of these options."},{"t":"mc","q":"A hiker walks steadily downhill. What is the quadriceps doing at each step as the leading knee bends under body weight?\nA) Contracting concentrically to drive the knee forward\nB) Contracting eccentrically to control knee flexion\nC) Contracting isometrically to lock the knee in place\nD) Relaxing completely while the ligaments absorb the load\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Contracting eccentrically to control knee flexion","why":"Body weight bends the loaded knee, which lengthens the quadriceps while it keeps generating force to slow the descent, the textbook eccentric contraction and the reason downhill walking causes soreness. Concentric quadriceps work extends the knee, which is what happens going uphill. The knee is flexing rather than holding a fixed angle, so it is not isometric. If the quadriceps relaxed the knee would buckle."},{"t":"mc","q":"What defines an isometric contraction?\nA) The muscle shortens while tension stays constant\nB) The muscle lengthens while tension stays constant\nC) The muscle changes length without developing tension\nD) The muscle develops tension but its length does not change\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The muscle develops tension but its length does not change","why":"Isometric means same length: crossbridges cycle and tension climbs, but the load is too large to move, so the muscle holds its length. Shortening at constant tension is a concentric isotonic contraction. Lengthening at constant tension is an eccentric contraction. A length change without tension is not a contraction at all; it is passive stretch or recoil."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is happening to the muscle during a concentric contraction?\nA) It lengthens while generating force\nB) It holds a fixed length while generating force\nC) It shortens without generating force\nD) It shortens while generating force\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It shortens while generating force","why":"Concentric contraction is the familiar case in which crossbridge force exceeds the load, so the muscle shortens and does positive work on the load. Lengthening under load is eccentric. Holding a fixed length is isometric. Shortening with no force generation is elastic recoil, not contraction."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the load velocity relationship for skeletal muscle, what happens to shortening velocity as the load on the muscle increases?\nA) Shortening velocity decreases\nB) Shortening velocity increases\nC) Shortening velocity is unchanged until the load exceeds maximal tension\nD) Shortening velocity increases and then plateaus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Shortening velocity decreases","why":"Heavier loads mean each crossbridge spends more of its cycle bearing force, so the filaments slide more slowly and the load velocity curve falls steadily from left to right. Velocity cannot rise with load, since more work must be done per unit distance. It also does not stay flat, because even a small load slows shortening measurably. A plateau would imply that load stops mattering, which the curve does not show."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"As the load on a muscle increases, the muscle shortens faster because more crossbridges are recruited to move it.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. As the load increases, the muscle shortens more slowly, and at a load equal to its maximal tension it does not shorten at all.","why":"This is the force velocity relationship. A heavier load takes longer to lift because tension must first rise isometrically to match it, and the heads then cycle against more resistance, so shortening velocity falls as load rises. Recruiting more motor units raises the force available but does not reverse the relationship for a given muscle. At maximal load velocity is zero and the contraction is isometric."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same arm and dumbbell three times: holding it still, curling it up, and lowering it under control. Label each isometric, concentric, or eccentric, and draw an arrow for whether the active muscle is staying the same length, shortening, or lengthening. Beside each, write whether the load is smaller than, equal to, or larger than the tension the muscle is making. One line: say which of the three leaves you sorest two days later."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a force velocity curve with load on the x axis and shortening velocity on the y. Mark the point where velocity is highest and the point where velocity hits zero, and write what the load equals at that second point. Beside the curve, draw the arm at three loads along it. One line: the muscle is fully activated at every point on this curve, so say what is actually changing as the load climbs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish isometric, concentric, and eccentric contractions and predict shortening velocity from the load applied."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Isometric and isotonic contraction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-fiber-types","name":"Muscle fiber types","can":"Compare slow oxidative, fast oxidative glycolytic, and fast glycolytic fibers by speed, ATP source, fatigue resistance, and typical task.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The soleus muscle works continuously at low force to keep a standing person from falling forward. Which fiber type would you expect to predominate?\nA) Slow oxidative\nB) Fast glycolytic\nC) Fast oxidative glycolytic\nD) An even mix of fast glycolytic and slow oxidative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Slow oxidative","why":"A postural task demands low force sustained for hours, which is exactly what fatigue resistant, oxygen dependent slow oxidative fibers deliver. Fast glycolytic fibers would fatigue within a minute and are wasteful for a low force job. Fast oxidative glycolytic fibers hold up better than fast glycolytic but still fatigue faster than the task requires. A mix weighted toward fast fibers would not match the continuous demand."},{"t":"mc","q":"A biopsy shows fibers with a fast myosin ATPase, a high mitochondrial density, and moderate glycogen. Which fiber type is this?\nA) Slow oxidative\nB) Fast glycolytic\nC) A denervated fiber reverting to an immature type\nD) Fast oxidative glycolytic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fast oxidative glycolytic","why":"Fast ATPase with strong oxidative machinery is the signature of the intermediate type, which contracts quickly yet resists fatigue reasonably well. Slow oxidative fibers would have the oxidative machinery but a slow ATPase. Fast glycolytic fibers would have the fast ATPase but very few mitochondria. Denervated fibers atrophy rather than adopt a distinct hybrid profile, and nothing in the description points to denervation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which skeletal muscle fiber type contains the highest density of mitochondria?\nA) Slow oxidative\nB) Fast glycolytic\nC) Fast oxidative glycolytic\nD) All three carry the same mitochondrial density\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Slow oxidative","why":"Slow oxidative fibers are built for sustained aerobic work, so they are packed with mitochondria to keep oxidative phosphorylation supplying ATP for hours. Fast glycolytic fibers sit at the other extreme, with few mitochondria and a reliance on stored glycogen. Fast oxidative glycolytic fibers are intermediate, with a substantial but smaller mitochondrial complement than slow oxidative fibers. The three types differ sharply on this measure, which is one of the main ways they are told apart."},{"t":"mc","q":"High myoglobin content, which gives a muscle its red appearance, is characteristic of which fiber type?\nA) Fast glycolytic  |  B) Fast oxidative glycolytic only\nC) Slow oxidative  |  D) Fibers with the largest diameter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Slow oxidative","why":"Myoglobin stores oxygen inside the fiber and speeds its diffusion to the mitochondria, so the fibers that depend most on oxygen carry the most, and slow oxidative fibers are the reddest. Fast glycolytic fibers have little myoglobin and appear pale or white. Fast oxidative glycolytic fibers do contain myoglobin, but less than slow oxidative fibers, so naming them alone is wrong. Diameter tracks with force capacity, and the largest fibers are actually the palest."},{"t":"mc","q":"Fast glycolytic fibers depend mainly on which source of ATP during activity?\nA) Anaerobic glycolysis using stored glycogen\nB) Oxidative phosphorylation using fatty acids\nC) Oxidative phosphorylation using blood glucose\nD) Creatine phosphate alone for the entire contraction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Anaerobic glycolysis using stored glycogen","why":"Fast glycolytic fibers have few mitochondria and a poor capillary supply, so they break down their large glycogen stores anaerobically to lactate, which supplies ATP quickly but only briefly. Fatty acid oxidation requires abundant mitochondria and oxygen, which describes slow oxidative fibers. Oxidative use of blood glucose has the same requirement and the same problem. Creatine phosphate is used by all fiber types, but it lasts only seconds and cannot carry a contraction on its own."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Fast glycolytic fibers are the most fatigue resistant fiber type because they contain the most mitochondria.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Slow oxidative fibers are the most fatigue resistant because they have the most mitochondria; fast glycolytic fibers have few mitochondria and fatigue the fastest.","why":"Fatigue resistance tracks oxidative capacity. Fast glycolytic fibers are built for brief powerful effort, so they carry a fast myosin, a large diameter, and little oxidative machinery, and they run down within a minute. Slow oxidative fibers trade speed and force for a dense supply of mitochondria, capillaries, and myoglobin, which is why they can hold posture all day."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three fibers in cross section at the same scale, slow oxidative, fast oxidative glycolytic, and fast glycolytic. Draw the differences you could actually see: fiber diameter, mitochondria, capillaries wrapped around it, and myoglobin shading. Under each, write its main ATP source, its fatigue resistance, and one task it does. One line: name the single feature on your drawing that best predicts fatigue resistance, and defend picking that one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a sport or a job, not marathon running, and draw the muscle that person needs as a bundle in cross section with the fiber types shaded in the proportions you expect. Beside it, draw a bundle for someone doing the opposite kind of work. Under both, draw force against time out to ten minutes. One line: say where your two curves cross and what that crossing point means for choosing between the two athletes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare slow oxidative, fast oxidative glycolytic, and fast glycolytic fibers by speed, ATP source, fatigue resistance, and typical task."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Muscle fiber types. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-muscle-energetics","name":"ATP sources during activity","can":"Order creatine phosphate, anaerobic glycolysis, and oxidative phosphorylation by how quickly each supplies ATP and how long each can sustain it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An athlete performs an all out 8 second effort from rest. Which ATP source supplies most of the energy?\nA) Anaerobic glycolysis  |  B) Oxidative phosphorylation\nC) Fatty acid oxidation  |  D) Creatine phosphate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Creatine phosphate","why":"Eight seconds falls inside the creatine phosphate window, since that system responds instantly and covers roughly the first 10 to 15 seconds of maximal work. Anaerobic glycolysis is contributing by then but has not yet become the main supplier. Oxidative phosphorylation takes tens of seconds to ramp up and cannot match the ATP demand of an all out sprint. Fatty acid oxidation is slower still and dominates only during prolonged low intensity work."},{"t":"mc","q":"Anaerobic glycolysis yields 2 ATP per glucose and complete oxidation yields about 32. About how many times more glucose per minute must a muscle consume to make the same ATP anaerobically?\nA) About 2 times more  |  B) About 16 times more\nC) About 8 times more  |  D) About 32 times more\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 16 times more","why":"Dividing 32 by 2 gives 16, so an anaerobic fiber must run through roughly sixteen glucose molecules to match the ATP from one oxidized aerobically, which is why glycogen empties so fast during intense work. Two is the anaerobic yield itself rather than the ratio. Eight would be the ratio if aerobic yield were 16 ATP. Thirty two is the aerobic yield, not the ratio between the pathways."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ATP source supplies the working muscle fastest at the very start of a contraction?\nA) Anaerobic glycolysis  |  B) Oxidative phosphorylation\nC) Fatty acid oxidation  |  D) Creatine phosphate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Creatine phosphate","why":"Creatine phosphate transfers its phosphate to ADP in a single enzymatic step, so it regenerates ATP faster than any other route, though the store is small. Anaerobic glycolysis is next fastest but takes several reactions to get going. Oxidative phosphorylation is the slowest to respond, since it depends on delivering oxygen and on many mitochondrial steps. Fatty acid oxidation is the slowest of all, because the fatty acids must first be mobilized and transported into mitochondria."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme transfers a phosphate group from creatine phosphate to ADP in a working muscle fiber?\nA) Myosin ATPase  |  B) Phosphofructokinase\nC) Creatine kinase  |  D) ATP synthase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Creatine kinase","why":"Creatine kinase catalyzes the reversible transfer that rebuilds ATP from ADP and creatine phosphate, which is why it is the enzyme of the first few seconds of effort. Myosin ATPase splits ATP at the crossbridge rather than regenerating it. Phosphofructokinase is the rate limiting enzyme of glycolysis, a different pathway. ATP synthase makes ATP in mitochondria using the proton gradient, not using creatine phosphate."},{"t":"mc","q":"For roughly how long can stored creatine phosphate sustain maximal muscle activity?\nA) About 1 to 2 seconds  |  B) About 2 to 3 minutes\nC) About 10 to 15 seconds  |  D) About 20 to 30 minutes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 10 to 15 seconds","why":"Creatine phosphate stores cover the first 10 to 15 seconds of maximal effort, roughly the length of a short sprint, before glycolysis has to take over. One to two seconds is closer to the amount of preformed ATP already sitting in the fiber. Two to three minutes is the range covered by anaerobic glycolysis. Twenty to thirty minutes and beyond belongs to oxidative phosphorylation."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During the first few seconds of a sprint, creatine phosphate is the main source of new ATP because oxidative phosphorylation cannot increase its output quickly enough.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Stored ATP lasts only a few seconds, so muscle needs an immediate backup. Creatine phosphate provides it: one enzyme, no oxygen, and a rate that matches even maximal ATP use. It runs out in 10 to 15 seconds, by which time anaerobic glycolysis has taken over, and oxidative phosphorylation gradually becomes dominant as the circulation and mitochondria catch up. This is why a 100 meter sprint barely uses oxygen while running."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis running from a few seconds out to an hour, and draw three curves above it for the rate of ATP supply from stored creatine phosphate, from anaerobic glycolysis, and from oxidative phosphorylation. Show where each peaks and where each fades out. Label which of the three needs oxygen. One line: the fastest source is also the shortest lived, so write what a sprinter has already used up by the ten second mark."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three fuel tanks in a row, each sized to how long it can run the muscle, and put a tap on each sized to how fast it delivers. Keep the tank size and the tap size clearly separate, because they are not the same property. Draw the order the muscle opens them in. One line: name the biggest tank and say why the muscle still cannot start a sprint on it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order creatine phosphate, anaerobic glycolysis, and oxidative phosphorylation by how quickly each supplies ATP and how long each can sustain it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ATP sources during activity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-fatigue","name":"Fatigue and oxygen debt","can":"Distinguish central from peripheral fatigue and explain the metabolic basis of excess post exercise oxygen consumption.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Peripheral fatigue is located where in the pathway from brain to force?\nA) In the motor cortex and descending motor pathways\nB) At or beyond the neuromuscular junction, within the muscle fiber itself\nC) In the sensory pathways carrying pain signals to the brain\nD) In the cardiovascular centers of the medulla\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) At or beyond the neuromuscular junction, within the muscle fiber itself","why":"Peripheral fatigue covers everything from the neuromuscular junction outward, including excitation contraction coupling and the crossbridge machinery inside the fiber. Motor cortex and descending pathways are the site of central fatigue. Sensory pain pathways can contribute to the central component but are not the peripheral site. Medullary cardiovascular centers control blood pressure and heart rate rather than force production."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does excess post exercise oxygen consumption describe?\nA) Oxygen consumption that stays above resting level after exercise has ended\nB) The oxygen deficit that builds during the first minutes of exercise\nC) The maximal rate of oxygen consumption a person can achieve\nD) The difference between arterial and venous oxygen content during exercise\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Oxygen consumption that stays above resting level after exercise has ended","why":"After exercise stops, oxygen uptake falls only gradually, and that elevated consumption is used to restore the systems that ran ahead of oxygen delivery during the effort. The oxygen deficit is the mirror image and occurs at the start of exercise. Maximal oxygen consumption is a capacity measure, not a recovery phenomenon. The arteriovenous oxygen difference describes extraction by tissue rather than what happens after exercise."},{"t":"mc","q":"Accumulation of which ion in the T tubule lumen contributes to peripheral fatigue by reducing the size of the action potential?\nA) Ca2+  |  B) K+\nC) Cl-  |  D) HCO3-\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) K+","why":"Repeated action potentials pump K+ out of the fiber into the narrow T tubule lumen, where it accumulates, depolarizes the membrane, and inactivates Na+ channels so later action potentials are smaller. Ca2+ accumulates in the sarcoplasm rather than in the T tubule and its problem is impaired release, not impaired excitation. Cl- movement stabilizes the membrane rather than accumulating to a fatiguing level. HCO3- is a buffer and plays no role in T tubule excitation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Accumulating inorganic phosphate inside a fatiguing muscle fiber reduces force mainly by what mechanism?\nA) It lowers Ca2+ release and reduces crossbridge force\nB) It blocks the myosin binding site on actin directly\nC) It prevents the action potential from spreading into the T tubules\nD) It prevents ATP from binding to the myosin head\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It lowers Ca2+ release and reduces crossbridge force","why":"Inorganic phosphate rises as ATP and creatine phosphate are broken down, and it both precipitates with Ca2+ inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum and shifts crossbridge kinetics toward lower force. It does not sit on the actin binding site; that is the job of tropomyosin under resting conditions. Action potential conduction into the T tubules is affected by K+ rather than by phosphate. ATP still binds myosin during fatigue, which is why the muscle relaxes rather than going into rigor."},{"t":"mc","q":"During intense exercise, what happens to intracellular pH in a working muscle fiber?\nA) It rises, because lactate consumes H+ as it forms\nB) It stays constant, because muscle has no significant buffering capacity\nC) It rises, because carbon dioxide is exhaled faster during exercise\nD) It falls, because H+ accumulates alongside high rates of glycolysis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls, because H+ accumulates alongside high rates of glycolysis","why":"High rates of ATP turnover and glycolysis release H+ faster than the fiber can buffer or export it, so intracellular pH drops during intense work. Lactate formation actually consumes some H+, but the net effect of the whole pathway during intense exercise is acidification. Muscle does have substantial buffering, which slows but does not prevent the fall. Increased ventilation removes carbon dioxide from blood but cannot keep pace with intramuscular H+ production during maximal work."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the path from motor cortex to crossbridge as a row of boxes: brain, spinal cord, motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, T tubule, sarcoplasmic reticulum, crossbridge. Draw a vertical line through the row separating central from peripheral. Put a cross at two plausible failure points on each side of your line. One line: name the test you would run on a tired person to tell you which side of that line has failed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw oxygen consumption against time across three stages, rest, a hard bout of exercise, then recovery, and shade the region after exercise where consumption is still above resting. Inside that shaded region, draw the things being restored or paid for, one small item each. One line: the muscle stopped working minutes ago, so say why oxygen use has not come back to baseline yet."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish central from peripheral fatigue and explain the metabolic basis of excess post exercise oxygen consumption."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Fatigue and oxygen debt. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-muscle-adaptation","name":"Muscle plasticity","can":"Predict the change in fiber size, capillary supply, and mitochondrial content produced by endurance training, resistance training, and disuse.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A leg is immobilized in a cast for six weeks. Predict what happens to fiber cross sectional area and to mitochondrial content.\nA) Both decrease\nB) Cross sectional area decreases and mitochondrial content increases\nC) Cross sectional area increases and mitochondrial content decreases\nD) Both are unchanged, because the nerve supply remains intact\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Both decrease","why":"Disuse removes both the mechanical load that maintains contractile protein and the metabolic demand that maintains mitochondria, so the fiber loses on both fronts. Mitochondria do not increase when demand disappears. Cross sectional area cannot grow without loading, so the third option has the direction backward. An intact nerve slows the loss compared with denervation, but it does not prevent it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Predict the fiber type shift produced by several months of endurance training.\nA) A shift from slow oxidative toward fast glycolytic properties\nB) A shift from fast oxidative glycolytic toward fast glycolytic properties\nC) A shift from fast glycolytic toward fast oxidative glycolytic properties\nD) No shift at all, because fiber type is fixed by genetics\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A shift from fast glycolytic toward fast oxidative glycolytic properties","why":"Chronic aerobic activity pushes fibers toward the oxidative end of the spectrum, so fast glycolytic fibers acquire more mitochondria and capillaries and take on fast oxidative glycolytic properties. Shifting toward the glycolytic end is what disuse and sprint style training encourage, not endurance work. Fiber type has a strong genetic component but is clearly modifiable by chronic activity pattern, as cross innervation and training studies both show."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the principal change in a muscle fiber produced by months of heavy resistance training?\nA) An increase in fiber diameter from added myofibrils\nB) An increase in the number of fibers from cell division\nC) An increase in mitochondrial density within each fiber\nD) An increase in the number of sarcomeres in series along the fiber\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) An increase in fiber diameter from added myofibrils","why":"Resistance training drives synthesis of new actin and myosin, which are assembled into additional myofibrils, so the fiber grows thicker and produces more force. Mature skeletal muscle fibers do not divide, so the fiber count changes very little. Mitochondrial density is the hallmark of endurance training and often falls slightly with hypertrophy because the fiber grows around the existing mitochondria. Adding sarcomeres in series lengthens a fiber and follows chronic stretch rather than heavy loading."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the two most characteristic adaptations to months of endurance training within the trained muscle?\nA) Larger fiber diameter and greater glycogen storage\nB) More motor units and larger motor neurons\nC) More mitochondria and a denser capillary network\nD) More sarcomeres in series and a longer resting length\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) More mitochondria and a denser capillary network","why":"Endurance training improves the muscle's ability to make ATP aerobically, so mitochondrial content and capillary supply both rise, which raises oxygen delivery and oxidative enzyme capacity together. Fiber diameter changes little with endurance work and is the resistance training adaptation. Motor unit number is fixed by the number of motor neurons and does not increase with training. Sarcomere number in series changes with chronic length rather than with endurance activity."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to fiber diameter with several months of pure endurance training?\nA) It increases markedly, similar to resistance training\nB) It changes little and may decrease slightly\nC) It doubles, because oxidative fibers grow fastest\nD) It increases in fast fibers only and is unchanged in slow fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It changes little and may decrease slightly","why":"Endurance training improves the aerobic machinery rather than the contractile mass, and a slightly smaller fiber actually shortens the oxygen diffusion path, so diameter stays flat or drops a little. Marked growth in diameter is the resistance training signature. Doubling of diameter does not happen with any training program in that timeframe. Endurance work does not selectively enlarge fast fibers; if anything it shifts them toward a more oxidative profile."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one fiber in cross section as your baseline, then draw three more at the same scale beside it: after months of endurance training, after months of heavy resistance training, and after six weeks in a cast. In each, draw mitochondria, capillaries, and myofibrils, and keep the diameters honest. One line: say which of the four could lift the most and which could go the longest, and why those are not the same fiber."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three arrows leaving one starting fiber, labeled endurance training, resistance training, and disuse. Along each arrow, draw what changes early and what changes only after months. Then draw what happens on each path when the stimulus stops. One line: one of these adaptations is lost far faster than it was gained, so name it and say what that means for someone coming back from an injury."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the change in fiber size, capillary supply, and mitochondrial content produced by endurance training, resistance training, and disuse."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Muscle plasticity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-lab-emg-fatigue","name":"Electromyography and grip fatigue","can":"Record grip force and surface EMG over a sustained contraction and relate the decline in force to recruitment and fatigue.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What does a surface electromyogram record?\nA) The force the muscle is producing at the tendon\nB) The action potentials traveling down the motor nerve to the muscle\nC) The concentration of Ca2+ released inside the fibers\nD) The summed electrical activity of the fibers under the electrodes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The summed electrical activity of the fibers under the electrodes","why":"Surface EMG picks up the electrical field created by many muscle fiber action potentials occurring under the skin electrodes, which is why its amplitude reflects how much muscle is electrically active. Force is measured separately by a dynamometer, and the relationship between the two signals is exactly what the lab examines. Motor nerve action potentials are far too small and too deep to register on surface electrodes. Intracellular Ca2+ cannot be detected from the skin surface at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does a hand dynamometer measure in the grip fatigue lab?\nA) The electrical activity of the forearm flexors\nB) The force the hand exerts during a grip\nC) The rate of ATP use in the forearm muscles\nD) The number of motor units recruited in the forearm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The force the hand exerts during a grip","why":"A dynamometer is a force transducer, so it reports how hard the hand squeezes, usually in kilograms or newtons. Electrical activity is what the EMG electrodes report, and pairing the two signals is the point of the experiment. ATP use is not measured directly in this lab. Motor unit number can only be inferred, and then only indirectly, from the EMG signal rather than from the force record."},{"t":"mc","q":"Surface EMG amplitude increases mainly because of which two changes in the muscle?\nA) The fibers shorten faster and the tendon stretches further\nB) More motor units are recruited and active units fire faster\nC) More Ca2+ is released per action potential and troponin binds more of it\nD) The muscle warms up and its electrical resistance falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) More motor units are recruited and active units fire faster","why":"EMG amplitude is a summed electrical signal, so it grows when more fibers are firing and when each active unit fires more often, which are the same two mechanisms the nervous system uses to increase force. Shortening velocity and tendon stretch are mechanical events and do not add electrical signal. Ca2+ release and troponin binding occur after the action potential and are not detected by the electrodes. Warming does change tissue impedance slightly, but that is a minor effect compared with recruitment and rate coding."},{"t":"mc","q":"Grip force in this lab is most commonly reported in which units?\nA) Kilograms or newtons  |  B) Millivolts or microvolts\nC) Milliseconds  |  D) Hertz\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Kilograms or newtons","why":"Force is force, so the dynamometer reads in kilograms of force or in newtons. Millivolts and microvolts are the units of the EMG voltage signal, which is recorded on a separate channel. Milliseconds measure the duration of events such as the latent period. Hertz measures stimulus or firing frequency rather than force."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a maximal voluntary contraction, usually abbreviated MVC?\nA) The force produced when the motor nerve is stimulated supramaximally\nB) The greatest force a subject can produce voluntarily against the transducer\nC) The force at which the EMG signal first becomes detectable\nD) The force a subject can hold for at least five minutes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The greatest force a subject can produce voluntarily against the transducer","why":"MVC is a voluntary measure and serves as the reference against which submaximal target forces such as 30 percent or 60 percent are set. Force from supramaximal nerve stimulation is an evoked measure and can exceed MVC when central fatigue is present. The point where EMG first appears is closer to a threshold of activation than to a maximum. A force sustainable for five minutes is an endurance measure, far below MVC."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab, a student's maximal grip is 400 N. Asked to hold 50 percent (200 N), she keeps it steady for 55 seconds while her surface EMG amplitude climbs from 0.4 mV to 0.7 mV, then force falls below target. After a 10 minute rest she holds 20 percent (80 N). This time she lasts over 6 minutes, and EMG amplitude rises only slowly.\nExplain why the 20 percent hold lasts so much longer than the 50 percent hold.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"At 20 percent the force is supplied by small, fatigue resistant oxidative units, and units can be rotated in and out, so the reserve of fresh units lasts far longer than at 50 percent, where large fast units must be recruited and fatigue quickly","why":"Recruitment follows the size principle. A 20 percent target is met almost entirely by small slow oxidative units, which make ATP aerobically and can run for a long time, and because the pool is far from exhausted the nervous system can alternate units and let some rest. At 50 percent the fast, less oxidative units are already needed from the start; they fatigue within a minute, the rising EMG shows the system recruiting whatever reserve remains, and once that reserve is gone the target cannot be held. Time to failure therefore falls steeply as the target force rises."},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you squeeze anything, draw the two traces you predict for a long hold at a constant submaximal grip force: force on top, EMG amplitude below, both against time. Then run it and draw the real traces on the same axes in a second color. Mark where prediction and data part company. One line: the force was held flat on purpose, so say what the EMG had to do to keep it there and what that tells you about the units doing the work."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the raw EMG burst from the start of a maximal squeeze and from the end of it, side by side at the same scale, with the grip force trace running underneath across the whole hold. Mark on the force trace the moment you first felt it slipping. One line: say whether the falling force proves the motor neurons stopped firing, and name what else could produce the same trace."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Electromyography and grip fatigue. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-lab-muscle-sim","name":"Twitch and tetanus simulation","can":"Generate twitch and tetanus tracings in a muscle simulation by varying stimulus voltage and frequency and identify threshold and maximal stimulus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In a muscle stimulation experiment, what is the threshold stimulus?\nA) The smallest stimulus voltage that activates every motor unit in the muscle\nB) The stimulus frequency at which individual twitches begin to fuse\nC) The stimulus voltage above which further increases damage the muscle\nD) The smallest stimulus voltage that produces a measurable contraction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The smallest stimulus voltage that produces a measurable contraction","why":"Threshold is the voltage at which the first and most excitable motor units reach their firing threshold, so it is the lowest voltage that produces any tension at all. The voltage that activates every motor unit is the maximal stimulus, which is much higher. The frequency at which twitches begin to fuse is a frequency phenomenon, not a voltage one. Damaging voltages are a practical concern in a real preparation but are not what threshold means."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the maximal stimulus in a muscle stimulation experiment?\nA) The highest voltage the stimulator can deliver\nB) The lowest voltage that produces any detectable contraction in the muscle at all\nC) The voltage that produces complete tetanus in a single stimulus\nD) The lowest voltage that activates every motor unit, so force stops rising\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The lowest voltage that activates every motor unit, so force stops rising","why":"As voltage rises, progressively less excitable motor units are recruited, and once the last one is included, further voltage adds nothing, which defines the maximal stimulus. The stimulator's ceiling is a property of the equipment rather than of the muscle. The lowest voltage producing any contraction is threshold, at the other end of the recruitment range. Tetanus depends on stimulus frequency, and no single stimulus can produce it at any voltage."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the three phases of a single muscle twitch as recorded on a tracing?\nA) Latent period, contraction phase, relaxation phase\nB) Threshold, summation, tetanus\nC) Depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization\nD) Recruitment, treppe, fatigue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Latent period, contraction phase, relaxation phase","why":"A twitch tracing starts with a latent period in which tension has not yet appeared, then a contraction phase as tension rises to peak, then a relaxation phase as tension returns to baseline. Threshold, summation, and tetanus describe how twitches respond to stimulus intensity and frequency rather than the shape of one twitch. Depolarization and repolarization are phases of the action potential, an electrical event that precedes the mechanical one. Recruitment, treppe, and fatigue are separate phenomena of repeated stimulation."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is treppe, sometimes called the staircase effect?\nA) A progressive rise in twitch force over the first several identical stimuli\nB) The fusion of successive twitches into a smooth tetanus\nC) The progressive fall in force during a sustained tetanus\nD) The stepwise increase in whole muscle force as the stimulus voltage is raised\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A progressive rise in twitch force over the first several identical stimuli","why":"When a rested muscle is stimulated repeatedly at a low frequency, the first several twitches get stronger even though the stimulus is unchanged, mainly because Ca2+ accumulates in the sarcoplasm and enzyme activity rises with warming. Fusion of twitches is summation leading to tetanus, which is a different phenomenon. Force falling during a sustained tetanus is fatigue. Stepwise increases with rising voltage are multiple motor unit recruitment."},{"t":"mc","q":"What defines complete or fused tetanus on a tracing?\nA) A smooth sustained plateau with no relaxation between stimuli\nB) A tension record showing distinct peaks separated by periods of partial relaxation\nC) The maximal tension a single twitch can produce\nD) A tension record that falls steadily throughout the stimulus train\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A smooth sustained plateau with no relaxation between stimuli","why":"At high enough stimulus frequency there is no time for any relaxation between stimuli, so Ca2+ stays elevated and tension holds a smooth maximal plateau. Distinct peaks with partial relaxation describe unfused tetanus, one step below. A single twitch produces far less tension than tetanus, typically a third to a fifth as much. Steadily falling tension during a train is fatigue, not fusion."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what a voltage series on a simulated whole muscle shows. Say why force is zero at low voltage, why it rises with voltage, and why it stops rising. Define threshold and maximal stimulus as you go.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"below threshold no fiber reaches its firing threshold, so force is zero; threshold is the lowest voltage that produces any measurable force; each fiber is all or none, so rising voltage raises force by exciting more fibers (recruitment), not by making fibers contract harder; maximal stimulus is the lowest voltage at which every fiber is activated and force plateaus; voltages above maximal add no force because there are no fibers left to recruit; a whole muscle is graded by the number of active fibers even though each fiber is all or none","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw peak twitch tension against stimulus voltage, from zero up past the point where the curve flattens. Put arrows on the x axis at threshold and at maximal stimulus. Above three voltages along the curve, draw the twitch tracing you would get there. One line: nothing about any single fiber changed as you turned the voltage up, so write what did change to make the whole muscle pull harder."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a grid with stimulus voltage on one axis and frequency on the other, and sketch the tracing you would get at four corners of it: low voltage low frequency, low voltage high frequency, maximal voltage low frequency, maximal voltage high frequency. Label each tracing twitch, tetanus, or something in between. One line: say which knob you turn for the strongest contraction available, and why the other knob on its own cannot get you there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Twitch and tetanus simulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Motor Control","comps":[{"id":"w8-motor-hierarchy","name":"Motor control hierarchy","can":"Order the levels of motor control from spinal reflex through brainstem to cortex and state what each level contributes to a voluntary movement.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Damage limited to the brainstem reticulospinal and vestibulospinal pathways would be expected to impair which movements the most?\nA) Buttoning a shirt with the fingertips\nB) Holding the trunk upright and keeping balance while standing\nC) Detecting a light touch on the sole of the foot while standing\nD) Deciding which of two objects to pick up\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Holding the trunk upright and keeping balance while standing","why":"These brainstem pathways run to axial and proximal muscle pools, so losing them costs postural tone and balance while leaving distal dexterity comparatively intact. Fine finger movement depends on the lateral corticospinal tract, which is a separate pathway. Light touch is a sensory function carried by ascending tracts. Choosing between objects is a cortical planning function above the brainstem level."},{"t":"mc","q":"Recording from a person about to make a self-initiated movement, which area shows activity earliest, before the movement begins?\nA) The muscle spindles of the antagonist muscle\nB) The ventral horn alpha motor neurons\nC) The neuromuscular junctions of the muscle involved\nD) The supplementary motor and premotor areas\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The supplementary motor and premotor areas","why":"Planning precedes execution, so the supplementary motor and premotor areas become active first, well before the command reaches the cord. Alpha motor neurons and neuromuscular junctions fire only once the descending command arrives, so they are late in the sequence, not early. Spindles in the antagonist respond to stretch that occurs after the movement has already started, making them the last of these to change."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which level of the motor hierarchy selects the goal of a voluntary movement and plans the strategy for reaching it?\nA) The spinal cord interneuron pools\nB) The association and premotor areas of the cortex\nC) The brainstem reticular formation\nD) The alpha motor neurons of the ventral horn\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The association and premotor areas of the cortex","why":"The highest level of the hierarchy decides what to do and roughly how to do it, and that planning work belongs to the prefrontal association cortex together with the premotor and supplementary motor areas. The brainstem sets postural background rather than choosing the goal. Spinal interneurons and alpha motor neurons execute whatever command arrives; they carry out the plan and do not form it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main contribution of the brainstem level to a voluntary movement?\nA) It generates the action potential in the muscle fiber itself\nB) It chooses which object the movement will be aimed at\nC) It sets postural tone and balance so the movement has a stable base\nD) It stores the memory of how the movement felt last time\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It sets postural tone and balance so the movement has a stable base","why":"Brainstem centers act mostly through the vestibulospinal and reticulospinal tracts on axial and proximal muscles, holding the trunk and limb girdles steady so the hand can be moved accurately. Choosing the target is cortical planning. The muscle action potential is generated at the neuromuscular junction by the lower motor neuron. Motor memory is built in cortical and cerebellar circuits, not in the postural brainstem pathways."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where do the vestibulospinal and reticulospinal tracts originate?\nA) In the brainstem  |  B) In the primary motor cortex\nC) In the dorsal root ganglia  |  D) In the basal ganglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the brainstem","why":"Both are brainstem pathways: the vestibulospinal tract arises from the vestibular nuclei and the reticulospinal tracts from the reticular formation, and together they form the middle level of the hierarchy for posture and gross proximal control. Corticospinal fibers, not these tracts, arise in the primary motor cortex. The dorsal root ganglia hold sensory cell bodies and send nothing down the cord. The basal ganglia have no direct descending projection to the spinal cord at all."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The spinal cord only relays commands from the brain to muscle and cannot generate a movement pattern on its own.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Spinal cord circuits produce reflexes and rhythmic patterns such as stepping on their own; the brain starts, stops, and adjusts these patterns rather than building every movement from scratch.","why":"The lowest level of the motor hierarchy does real work. Stretch and withdrawal reflexes are complete within the cord, and central pattern generators can produce alternating flexion and extension of the legs after the cord has been disconnected from the brain. Descending pathways from cortex and brainstem select which spinal circuits run and how strongly. That division of labor is why a cortical lesion leaves reflexes present, and even exaggerated."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the levels stacked, spinal cord at the bottom, brainstem in the middle, cortex on top, and trace one voluntary movement of your own choosing, not walking, from the decision down to the muscle. At each level write what that level contributed. Draw the cerebellum and basal ganglia off to the side with arrows showing they adjust rather than command. One line: remove the top level only, and say what movement the person can still produce."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same three levels and drop a hot pan into the picture. Trace the withdrawal reflex through the lowest level alone, then trace the slower conscious response that arrives afterwards through all three. Put both on a shared time axis underneath. One line: the reflex is faster because it skipped something, so name what it skipped and what the person gave up by skipping it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the levels of motor control from spinal reflex through brainstem to cortex and state what each level contributes to a voluntary movement."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Motor control hierarchy. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-corticospinal","name":"Corticospinal pathway","can":"Trace the corticospinal pathway from the primary motor cortex to the skeletal muscle and identify where it crosses.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion destroys the hand region of the left primary motor cortex. Which hand becomes weak?\nA) Neither hand, because brainstem pathways take over hand control\nB) The left hand, because the tract has already crossed above the lesion\nC) Both hands equally, because the tract supplies both sides\nD) The right hand, because the tract crosses below the lesion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right hand, because the tract crosses below the lesion","why":"The decussation is in the medulla, well below the cortex, so fibers leaving the left cortex have not yet crossed and will end up serving the right side of the body. The left hand would be weak only if the lesion sat below the crossing. Bilateral weakness would require the tract to supply both sides equally, which the small uncrossed anterior tract does only for axial muscles. Brainstem pathways cannot substitute for fine hand control."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion damages the right medullary pyramid, just above the pyramidal decussation. Where is the resulting weakness?\nA) On the left side of the body\nB) On the right side of the body\nC) On both sides of the body below the neck\nD) In the right arm and the left leg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) On the left side of the body","why":"Fibers in the right pyramid have not crossed yet, and they are destined to cross a few millimeters lower and supply the left side, so the weakness appears contralateral to the lesion. Weakness on the same side would require a lesion below the decussation, in the lateral corticospinal tract of the cord. A one-sided pyramid lesion cannot weaken both sides. A split pattern with one arm and the opposite leg does not match a tract that crosses all at one point."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion damages the right lateral corticospinal tract at spinal level C5. Where is the weakness?\nA) In the left arm and left leg, on the side opposite the lesion\nB) In the right arm and right leg, on the same side as the lesion\nC) In both arms but neither leg\nD) In the right arm only, with the right leg spared\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In the right arm and right leg, on the same side as the lesion","why":"By C5 these fibers have already crossed in the medulla, so the tract on the right side of the cord is serving right sided muscles and a lesion there produces ipsilateral weakness below the level. Contralateral weakness is the pattern for a lesion above the decussation. One-sided cord damage does not affect both arms. Fibers for the leg travel in the same tract and are cut too, so the leg is not spared."},{"t":"mc","q":"A small infarct in the posterior limb of the left internal capsule interrupts a dense bundle of corticospinal fibers. What deficit is expected?\nA) Weakness of the right hand alone\nB) Weakness of the left arm and left leg together\nC) Weakness of the right arm and right leg together\nD) Weakness confined to the muscles of the trunk\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Weakness of the right arm and right leg together","why":"Fibers from the whole body map are squeezed into a small volume in the capsule, so a small lesion there strikes arm and leg fibers at once and produces contralateral weakness of the whole side. Weakness of the hand alone is the signature of a small lesion up in the cortex, where the body map is spread out. Left sided weakness would require a lesion below the decussation or on the right. Trunk muscles receive substantial uncrossed input and are the least affected."},{"t":"mc","q":"If the lateral corticospinal tract alone were selectively interrupted, which task would suffer the most?\nA) Standing still with the eyes open\nB) Breathing quietly at rest\nC) Holding the head upright while seated\nD) Picking up a small coin with the fingertips\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Picking up a small coin with the fingertips","why":"The lateral tract carries the fractionated commands that let individual fingers move independently, so precision grip is what fails first and most completely. Standing still and holding the head up rely on axial and proximal pools driven by brainstem pathways and the anterior corticospinal tract. Quiet breathing is generated by brainstem respiratory centers and does not need the corticospinal tract at all."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Most corticospinal axons cross the midline in the medulla, so the right motor cortex controls muscles on the left side of the body.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The upper motor neuron starts in the cortex on one side and ends on lower motor neurons on the opposite side of the cord. The crossing happens in the medulla, so a lesion above the decussation weakens the opposite side of the body while a lesion below it, in the cord, weakens the same side. Knowing where the tract crosses is what lets you locate a lesion from the pattern of weakness."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a coronal outline of the brain, brainstem, spinal cord, and one arm. Run the corticospinal neuron from the precentral gyrus down through the internal capsule, midbrain, and pons, and mark the pyramidal decussation in the medulla where most fibers cross. Synapse it in the ventral horn on the lower motor neuron and continue out to the muscle. Label upper and lower motor neuron. One line: name the side of the body the left motor cortex moves, and the structure that decided that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the pathway once, then place a lesion on it at three sites: in the motor cortex, in the medulla just above the crossing, and in the spinal cord below the crossing. For each, shade the side of the body that ends up weak. One line: two of your three lesions weaken the same side, so write what a clinician would look for to separate those two."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the corticospinal pathway from the primary motor cortex to the skeletal muscle and identify where it crosses."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Corticospinal pathway. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-cerebellum-basal-ganglia","name":"Cerebellum and basal ganglia","can":"Contrast the contributions of the cerebellum and the basal ganglia to movement and match a described motor sign to the structure involved.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A stroke damages the right cerebellar hemisphere. On which side would you expect the finger to nose test to be inaccurate?\nA) On both sides equally\nB) On the left\nC) On the right\nD) On neither side, since accuracy does not depend on the cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) On the right","why":"Cerebellar signs are ipsilateral because the cerebellar output crossing and the corticospinal crossing cancel each other, so a right hemisphere lesion disturbs the right arm. A left sided deficit would follow damage to the right motor cortex instead. Damage on one side does not disturb both arms. Finger to nose accuracy is a direct test of cerebellar error correction, so it is very much affected."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's hand is steady while resting in the lap, but it begins to oscillate as she reaches for a cup and the shaking is worst just before she grasps it. Which structure is implicated?\nA) The basal ganglia  |  B) The primary motor cortex\nC) The lower motor neuron pool of the forearm  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cerebellum","why":"The timing is the clue. A tremor that appears only during a goal directed movement and peaks near the target is an intention tremor and reflects failed cerebellar correction of ongoing error. A basal ganglia tremor behaves in the opposite way, present at rest and damped by voluntary movement. A motor cortex lesion produces weakness rather than oscillation. Lower motor neuron damage gives wasting and twitching at rest, not a tremor tied to reaching."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's fingers show a slow rhythmic tremor while the hands rest on the knees, and the tremor stops when he picks up a glass. Which structure is implicated?\nA) The basal ganglia  |  B) The cerebellum\nC) The muscle spindle  |  D) The dorsal root ganglion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The basal ganglia","why":"A tremor that is present at rest and quiets with voluntary movement is the classic basal ganglia pattern seen with nigrostriatal dopamine loss. The cerebellar tremor is the mirror image, absent at rest and worst as the hand nears the target. Spindle malfunction alters reflex gain rather than producing a rhythmic resting tremor. Dorsal root ganglion damage causes sensory loss and sensory clumsiness, not tremor."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient walks with a wide based, staggering gait and sways badly when standing with the feet together, yet strength and reflexes in the legs are normal. Which structure is most likely involved?\nA) The internal capsule  |  B) The midline cerebellum\nC) The lateral corticospinal tract  |  D) The globus pallidus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The midline cerebellum","why":"Normal strength and normal reflexes rule out the descending motor pathways, and a wide base with truncal instability points to the midline cerebellum, which coordinates balance and gait. A capsular or corticospinal lesion would leave the leg weak with brisk reflexes. Pallidal disease produces rigidity and slowness or involuntary movements rather than this pattern of unsteadiness with a wide base."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has continuous, irregular, dance like movements of the limbs that he cannot suppress. How is this best classified?\nA) A lower motor neuron disorder\nB) A hypokinetic basal ganglia disorder\nC) A cerebellar coordination disorder\nD) A hyperkinetic basal ganglia disorder\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A hyperkinetic basal ganglia disorder","why":"Chorea is an excess of unwanted movement, which means the gate that normally suppresses competing movements has been weakened, and that gating belongs to the basal ganglia. A hypokinetic disorder shows the opposite problem, too little movement. Cerebellar disease makes voluntary movement inaccurate but does not generate involuntary movements at rest. Lower motor neuron disease produces weakness and wasting, and its fasciculations are small twitches within a muscle rather than movements of a whole limb."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, contrast what the cerebellum and the basal ganglia contribute to movement. For each, describe one motor sign a lesion produces and explain why that sign follows from the structure's job.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"cerebellum compares the intended movement with sensory feedback about the actual movement and corrects errors in timing and coordination; cerebellar lesion produces overshoot or undershoot, intention tremor, or clumsy rapid alternating movements, because errors are no longer corrected; cerebellar signs appear on the same side as the lesion; basal ganglia help select and initiate wanted movements and suppress unwanted ones, acting through the thalamus on the cortex; basal ganglia lesion produces slow, hard to start movement or unwanted involuntary movement, depending on which pathway is affected; neither structure drives spinal motor neurons directly, so strength and reflexes stay normal with either lesion","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a hand reaching toward a target three times: normally, with cerebellar damage, and with basal ganglia damage. Draw the actual path the hand takes each time rather than writing about it. Beside each path, draw the tremor if there is one and mark the point in the movement where it shows up. One line: both patients tremble, so say when each one's tremor appears and what that timing tells you about the structure involved."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a loop diagram with the motor cortex at the top, the cerebellum on one side and the basal ganglia on the other, each with its arrows out to and back from the cortex. Label what each loop does to the command, comparing it against feedback or selecting and scaling it. One line: name the loop that needs sensory information from the moving limb to work, and say what happens to it when that information is lost."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to contrast the contributions of the cerebellum and the basal ganglia to movement and match a described motor sign to the structure involved."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cerebellum and basal ganglia. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-umn-lmn","name":"Upper and lower motor neuron signs","can":"Distinguish upper from lower motor neuron lesions by tone, reflexes, and muscle bulk and assign a set of findings to the correct level.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A limb is flaccid and markedly wasted, the tendon reflexes are absent, and twitches are visible in the muscle at rest. Which level is damaged?\nA) The lower motor neuron  |  B) The upper motor neuron\nC) The cerebellum  |  D) The basal ganglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The lower motor neuron","why":"All four findings point the same way: low tone, absent reflexes, severe wasting, and fasciculations are exactly what happens when the final common pathway to the muscle is destroyed. An upper motor neuron lesion would give the opposite tone and reflexes, spasticity and hyperreflexia, along with a Babinski sign and little wasting. Cerebellar disease causes incoordination with normal strength and bulk. Basal ganglia disease alters the amount of movement rather than abolishing reflexes or wasting the muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A limb is weak and stiff, the tendon reflexes are brisk, the great toe goes up when the sole is stroked, and muscle bulk is nearly normal. Which level is damaged?\nA) The lower motor neuron  |  B) The upper motor neuron\nC) The neuromuscular junction  |  D) The muscle fibers themselves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The upper motor neuron","why":"Increased tone, hyperreflexia, an extensor plantar response, and preserved bulk are the four features of an upper motor neuron lesion, and they occur together because the spinal reflex machinery survives and is released from descending control. A lower motor neuron lesion would show flaccidity, lost reflexes, fasciculations, and obvious wasting. Neuromuscular junction disease causes fatigable weakness with normal or reduced reflexes and no Babinski sign. Primary muscle disease weakens and wastes muscle without making reflexes brisk."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which neuron is the lower motor neuron?\nA) The ventral horn cell whose axon leaves the cord and reaches the muscle\nB) The cortical cell whose axon descends to the spinal cord\nC) The dorsal root ganglion cell that carries stretch information inward\nD) The spinal interneuron that connects one side of the cord to the other\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The ventral horn cell whose axon leaves the cord and reaches the muscle","why":"The lower motor neuron is the cell that actually contacts skeletal muscle, so its cell body sits in the ventral horn of the cord or in a cranial nerve motor nucleus and its axon runs out in a peripheral nerve. The cortical cell that descends to the cord is the upper motor neuron and stops in the spinal gray matter. Dorsal root ganglion cells are sensory. Interneurons stay inside the central nervous system and never reach muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to muscle tone after an upper motor neuron lesion?\nA) Tone falls, producing flaccidity\nB) Tone increases, producing spasticity\nC) Tone is unchanged from normal\nD) Tone rises for a day and then falls permanently\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Tone increases, producing spasticity","why":"Descending pathways normally restrain the spinal stretch reflex, so removing them leaves the reflex circuitry overactive and the limb resists passive stretch, which is spasticity. Flaccidity is the tone change of a lower motor neuron lesion, where the reflex arc itself is broken. Tone does change, and durably, so unchanged tone does not fit. The time course given in the last option is backwards: tone is low at first after an acute lesion and rises over the following weeks."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 55 year old is examined three months after a stroke. His right leg is weak. Tone in the leg is increased and catches when the examiner moves it quickly. The right knee reflex is graded 4+ (normal 2+), and stroking the sole of the right foot makes the big toe extend. Muscle bulk in the right leg is nearly the same as the left, and there are no fasciculations.\nExplain why the reflexes in the weak leg are exaggerated rather than lost.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The lesion is above the spinal cord, so the reflex arc and its lower motor neurons are intact but have lost descending inhibition, and the released spinal circuits overreact to stretch","why":"The findings are a set that goes together. Weakness with preserved bulk and no fasciculations says the lower motor neurons still supply the muscle. Increased tone, a brisk reflex, and an extensor plantar response say the descending pathways that normally damp the spinal reflex circuits are gone. That combination places the lesion in the upper motor neuron, here the corticospinal system damaged by the stroke. The reflex arc survives and, freed from inhibition, produces more response to the same stretch than it should."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, contrast an upper motor neuron lesion with a lower motor neuron lesion. Cover muscle tone, reflexes, muscle bulk, and any extra signs, and explain the mechanism behind each difference.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"lower motor neuron lesion: flaccid, reduced tone, because the muscle has lost its only source of activation; lower motor neuron lesion: stretch reflex lost or reduced, because the efferent limb of the reflex arc is gone; lower motor neuron lesion: marked wasting and fasciculations, because the fiber loses the neuron's trophic support and denervated fibers fire spontaneously; upper motor neuron lesion: spasticity with increased tone, because descending inhibition of the spinal reflex circuits is lost; upper motor neuron lesion: brisk reflexes and an extensor plantar response, because the reflex arc is intact but released from descending control; upper motor neuron lesion: only mild disuse atrophy and no fasciculations, because the lower motor neuron still supplies the muscle","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the two neuron chain from cortex to muscle once, and mark two lesion sites, one on the upper neuron and one on the lower. Beside each, draw the limb as it behaves: the tone, the reflex hammer response drawn as a big or a small kick, the muscle bulk, and whether the muscle twitches on its own at rest. One line: both patients are weak, so name the single finding you would check first and say why that one."},{"t":"draw","q":"You are handed a limb with raised tone, brisk reflexes, normal bulk, and a big toe that goes up when you stroke the sole. Draw the neuron chain and mark where the lesion has to be. Then draw a second chain with the opposite findings and mark that lesion. Label both. One line: one of these two patients wastes away quickly and the other does not, so say what that muscle lost contact with."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish upper from lower motor neuron lesions by tone, reflexes, and muscle bulk and assign a set of findings to the correct level."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Upper and lower motor neuron signs. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Autonomic Nervous System","comps":[{"id":"w8-ans-organization","name":"Autonomic and somatic organization","can":"Contrast the somatic motor system with the autonomic two neuron pathway by neuron count and by effector tissue.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A toxin blocks acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction only. What happens to intestinal smooth muscle?\nA) It becomes flaccid along with skeletal muscle\nB) It contracts continuously without relaxing\nC) It loses all spontaneous activity\nD) It continues to contract normally\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It continues to contract normally","why":"Intestinal smooth muscle is an autonomic effector, so a toxin restricted to the neuromuscular junction never touches it and gut motility carries on. Flaccidity along with skeletal muscle mistakes smooth muscle for a somatic target. Continuous contraction and loss of spontaneous activity both assume the toxin altered autonomic or enteric input, which it did not."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure would you find in a pathway to the stomach but never in a pathway to the quadriceps?\nA) A myelinated axon leaving the spinal cord\nB) A peripheral ganglion containing a synapse\nC) A terminal that releases acetylcholine\nD) A neuronal cell body located inside the central nervous system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A peripheral ganglion containing a synapse","why":"The peripheral ganglion is the structural signature of the autonomic route, since the somatic neuron reaches its muscle without relaying anywhere. Myelinated axons leaving the cord, acetylcholine release, and CNS cell bodies all occur in both systems, so none of them can separate the two. Acetylcholine in particular is used by every preganglionic fiber and by every somatic motor neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many neurons carry the signal from the central nervous system to the effector in a somatic motor pathway?\nA) One  |  B) Two\nC) Three  |  D) Four\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One","why":"A single somatic motor neuron runs the whole way, from its cell body in the CNS out to the skeletal muscle fiber, with no synapse in between. Two is the autonomic arrangement, where a preganglionic and a postganglionic neuron are linked at a ganglion. Three and four do not describe any motor output pathway, although a sensory pathway climbing to the cortex does use three neurons in series."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many neurons in series make up an autonomic motor pathway from the central nervous system to the effector?\nA) One  |  B) Four\nC) Two  |  D) Three\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Two","why":"Every autonomic pathway uses a preganglionic neuron whose cell body sits in the CNS and a postganglionic neuron whose cell body sits in a ganglion outside the CNS. One neuron is the somatic pattern that runs straight to skeletal muscle. Three and four overcount, since only one peripheral synapse exists between the CNS and the target organ."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where do the two neurons of an autonomic pathway synapse with each other?\nA) In the ventral horn of the spinal cord\nB) In an autonomic ganglion outside the central nervous system\nC) In the dorsal root ganglion\nD) Within the wall of the effector cell itself\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In an autonomic ganglion outside the central nervous system","why":"The peripheral synapse happens in an autonomic ganglion, a cluster of postganglionic cell bodies sitting outside the CNS, which is exactly the relay the somatic system lacks. The ventral horn holds the cell bodies of preganglionic and somatic motor neurons, not the relay itself. The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies and has no synapse in it at all, and no synapse occurs inside the effector cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the somatic motor pathway and the autonomic pathway side by side, both starting in the CNS and ending at an effector. Count the neurons in each and put the ganglion in its correct place on the autonomic one. Label the transmitter released at every synapse and at both endings. Name the effector tissue at the end of each. One line: what the extra neuron buys the autonomic system."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one somatic motor neuron reaching skeletal muscle, and one autonomic pathway branching to cardiac muscle, a smooth muscle, and a gland. Then cut every nerve on your drawing and show what happens to each effector. One line: one of these effectors is completely paralyzed by the cut while the others keep working, say which and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to contrast the somatic motor system with the autonomic two neuron pathway by neuron count and by effector tissue."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Autonomic and somatic organization. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-divisions","name":"Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions","can":"Compare the two autonomic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and predict the effect of each on heart rate and on the gastrointestinal tract.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student begins to sprint and sympathetic output to the heart rises sharply. Predict the change in heart rate.\nA) Heart rate falls\nB) Heart rate rises\nC) Heart rate is unchanged while stroke volume falls\nD) Heart rate rises only after the sprint ends\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Heart rate rises","why":"Sympathetic fibers to the sinoatrial node speed its pacemaker depolarization, so more sympathetic traffic means a faster rate, and the change begins within a few beats. A fall in rate would require increased vagal activity instead. Stroke volume rises rather than falls under sympathetic drive, and the response happens during the effort, not after it."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a large meal, parasympathetic outflow to the gut increases. Predict what happens to intestinal motility and glandular secretion.\nA) Both decrease  |  B) Motility increases while secretion falls\nC) Both increase  |  D) Motility falls while secretion rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both increase","why":"Parasympathetic activity is the digestive setting, raising smooth muscle activity in the gut wall and stimulating secretion so food is moved and broken down. Decreases in both would follow sympathetic activation instead. The mixed options split two responses that this division always drives in the same direction."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an anesthetized animal the vagus nerve is stimulated electrically. Predict the effect on the sinoatrial node.\nA) It depolarizes more slowly, so the heart rate falls\nB) It depolarizes faster, so the heart rate rises\nC) It stops responding to any input at all\nD) It is unaffected, since the vagus supplies only the lungs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It depolarizes more slowly, so the heart rate falls","why":"Vagal stimulation releases acetylcholine on the sinoatrial node, slowing its pacemaker drift toward threshold and lowering the rate. Faster depolarization is what sympathetic stimulation produces. The node keeps responding to other inputs during vagal stimulation, and the vagus supplies the heart as well as the lungs and abdominal viscera."},{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion destroys the lateral horn neurons from T1 to L2 while leaving the brainstem and sacral cord intact. Which division is lost?\nA) The parasympathetic division  |  B) Both divisions equally\nC) The sympathetic division  |  D) Neither, since these neurons are somatic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The sympathetic division","why":"T1 to L2 is exactly the thoracolumbar outflow, so destroying those lateral horn cell bodies removes the sympathetic preganglionic supply. The parasympathetic division survives because its cell bodies are craniosacral and the lesion spares both the brainstem and S2 to S4. Lateral horn neurons are autonomic preganglionic, not somatic, which is why voluntary movement would be relatively preserved."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person is startled by a loud noise. Predict the simultaneous change in heart rate and in gastrointestinal motility.\nA) Heart rate falls and gastrointestinal motility rises\nB) Both heart rate and gastrointestinal motility rise\nC) Both heart rate and gastrointestinal motility fall\nD) Heart rate rises and gastrointestinal motility falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Heart rate rises and gastrointestinal motility falls","why":"A startle recruits the sympathetic division, which speeds the sinoatrial node while inhibiting gut smooth muscle, shifting resources from digestion to circulation. Falling rate with rising motility is the parasympathetic pattern seen after a meal. The two options that move both variables the same way miss the point that this division pushes the heart and the gut in opposite directions."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Because the sympathetic division uses norepinephrine, blocking acetylcholine receptors would have no effect on sympathetic output.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Sympathetic preganglionic fibers release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors in the ganglion, so a nicotinic blocker stops sympathetic output at the ganglion even though the postganglionic transmitter is norepinephrine.","why":"Both autonomic divisions are cholinergic at the ganglion. What differs is the postganglionic transmitter: norepinephrine for most sympathetic fibers, acetylcholine for parasympathetic fibers. A ganglionic blocker therefore shuts down both divisions, and the visible effect on each organ depends on which division had the greater resting tone there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the CNS as one tall outline from brainstem to sacrum and mark the outflow region for each division on it. From each region, draw a preganglionic and a postganglionic neuron with the ganglion at its true distance from the cord, and make the fiber lengths honestly different between the two divisions. One line: what the ganglion position does to how widely one preganglionic neuron can spread its signal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a heart and a length of gut side by side. Run a sympathetic fiber and a parasympathetic fiber to each, four pathways in total. On each pathway, write the effect with a direction, what happens to heart rate and what happens to gut motility and secretion. One line: name the whole body situation each of your two columns is built for."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the two autonomic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and predict the effect of each on heart rate and on the gastrointestinal tract."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-receptors","name":"Autonomic neurotransmitters and receptors","can":"Match nicotinic, muscarinic, alpha, and beta receptors to their neurotransmitter, their location, and the response each produces.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A substance raises heart rate and relaxes bronchiolar smooth muscle but produces no constriction of skin arterioles. Which receptor family is it acting on?\nA) Beta receptors  |  B) Alpha receptors\nC) Muscarinic receptors  |  D) Nicotinic receptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Beta receptors","why":"Working back from the results, a faster heart points to beta-1 and relaxed airways to beta-2, while the absence of skin vasoconstriction rules out alpha-1 activity, so the agent is beta selective. Alpha activation would have constricted the skin vessels. Muscarinic activation would have slowed the heart and narrowed the airways, the reverse of both findings, and nicotinic activation would have driven both divisions at the ganglia rather than producing this clean pattern."},{"t":"mc","q":"Acetylcholine is released onto muscarinic receptors on the sinoatrial node. Predict the change in heart rate.\nA) Heart rate rises\nB) Heart rate falls\nC) Heart rate is unchanged but contractility rises\nD) Heart rate rises and then falls below baseline\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Heart rate falls","why":"Muscarinic activation on the node slows its pacemaker depolarization, so threshold is reached less often and the rate drops. A rise in rate would require beta-1 activation by norepinephrine instead. Contractility is a beta-1 effect on the ventricles, and there is no biphasic rise before the fall."},{"t":"mc","q":"Norepinephrine binds beta-1 receptors in the heart. Predict the effect on rate and on force of contraction.\nA) Rate increases and force decreases  |  B) Both rate and force decrease\nC) Both rate and force increase  |  D) Rate is unchanged and force increases\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both rate and force increase","why":"Beta-1 activation raises cyclic AMP in cardiac cells, which speeds the pacemaker current in the node and increases calcium entry in working myocardium, so the heart beats both faster and harder. Falling rate and force would follow muscarinic activation. Rate and force move together under beta-1 stimulation, so the split options describe a dissociation that does not occur here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Epinephrine activates beta-2 receptors on the arterioles supplying skeletal muscle. Predict the change in vessel diameter and in flow to that muscle.\nA) The vessels dilate and flow increases\nB) The vessels constrict and flow decreases\nC) The vessels constrict and flow increases\nD) Diameter is unchanged and flow falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The vessels dilate and flow increases","why":"Beta-2 receptors relax vascular smooth muscle in skeletal muscle beds, so the arterioles widen, resistance falls, and more blood reaches the working muscle. Constriction is what alpha-1 activation would do, and alpha-1 is the dominant receptor in most other vascular beds. Constriction with increased flow is internally contradictory, since narrowing a vessel raises resistance and lowers flow at a given pressure."},{"t":"mc","q":"Norepinephrine reaches alpha-1 receptors on arterioles in the skin. Predict the change in vessel radius and in resistance in that bed.\nA) Radius rises and resistance falls  |  B) Radius falls and resistance falls\nC) Radius rises and resistance rises  |  D) Radius falls and resistance rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Radius falls and resistance rises","why":"Alpha-1 activation contracts vascular smooth muscle, narrowing the arteriole, and resistance climbs steeply as radius falls. Widening with falling resistance is the beta-2 response, which occurs in skeletal muscle vessels rather than skin. The two mismatched options break the inverse relationship between radius and resistance that holds in every vascular bed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four complete pathways from the cord out to an effector, sympathetic to a blood vessel, sympathetic to sweat glands, sympathetic to the adrenal medulla, and parasympathetic to the heart. At every synapse and every ending, write the transmitter and the receptor type. One line: name the two places where your labels broke the pattern, and state what the pattern was."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four postsynaptic membranes in a row, one each for nicotinic, muscarinic, alpha, and beta receptors. On each, draw the transmitter that binds it, name one real location in the body, and draw the response it produces, contract, relax, speed up, or secrete. One line: two of your four bind the same transmitter, name them and say what makes their responses different."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match nicotinic, muscarinic, alpha, and beta receptors to their neurotransmitter, their location, and the response each produces."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Autonomic neurotransmitters and receptors. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-tone","name":"Dual innervation and tonic control","can":"Explain dual innervation and autonomic tone and predict the effect of removing one division from a dually innervated organ.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A resting person has a heart rate of 72 beats per minute while the intrinsic rate of the sinoatrial node is about 100. What does this difference indicate?\nA) Sympathetic tone is holding the rate down\nB) Parasympathetic tone is holding the rate down\nC) The sinoatrial node is damaged\nD) Both divisions are silent at rest\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Parasympathetic tone is holding the rate down","why":"A resting rate below the intrinsic rate can only mean an active brake, and vagal tone is that brake. Sympathetic tone pushes the rate up, so more of it would move the heart above 100 rather than below. A node that is damaged would not still be pacing normally, and if both divisions were silent the rate would sit at the intrinsic value."},{"t":"mc","q":"Both vagus nerves to the heart are blocked in a resting person while sympathetic fibers are left intact. Predict the heart rate.\nA) It falls toward 40 beats per minute\nB) It stays at the previous resting rate\nC) It rises above the previous resting rate\nD) It becomes irregular but averages the same\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises above the previous resting rate","why":"Removing the dominant resting brake unmasks the intrinsic pacemaker rate plus whatever sympathetic tone remains, so the rate climbs. A fall would require losing sympathetic rather than vagal input. Staying the same would only happen if vagal tone had been absent, and while beat to beat variability does drop after vagal block, the average rate clearly increases."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sympathetic fibers to the heart are blocked in a resting person while vagal fibers stay intact. Predict the change in heart rate.\nA) It falls to about half the resting value\nB) It rises toward the intrinsic rate\nC) It is unchanged, since only exercise uses sympathetic fibers\nD) It falls modestly, because sympathetic tone at rest is small\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls modestly, because sympathetic tone at rest is small","why":"At rest the sympathetic contribution to heart rate is the smaller of the two, so removing it lowers the rate somewhat but leaves the vagally braked pace largely intact. Halving the rate overstates a tone that is minor at rest. A rise would follow vagal block, not sympathetic block, and sympathetic fibers do fire at rest rather than only during exercise."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sympathetic firing to the arterioles of the skin decreases. Predict what happens to those vessels.\nA) They dilate, because the constricting tone is reduced\nB) They constrict, because parasympathetic input takes over\nC) They stay the same, since tone is fixed\nD) They dilate, because parasympathetic fibers are activated\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) They dilate, because the constricting tone is reduced","why":"The resting vessel is held partly closed by sympathetic tone, so a fall in firing releases that grip and the vessel widens, which is exactly how skin flow rises when the body needs to lose heat. No parasympathetic supply exists in these vessels, so both options invoking it are wrong regardless of the direction they predict. Tone is a variable firing rate, not a fixed setting."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"If the parasympathetic supply to the heart is cut, resting heart rate falls, because the sympathetic division now has nothing to oppose.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Resting heart rate rises toward the intrinsic rate of the SA node, about 100 beats per minute, because resting vagal tone was holding the rate down.","why":"At rest the vagus dominates the heart. The SA node on its own fires about 100 times a minute, and vagal tone brakes it to around 70. Removing the brake lets the rate climb. Sympathetic tone to the heart at rest is small, so removing it instead would lower the rate only slightly. Removing one division reveals the tone of the other, and the direction of change tells you which division was doing the work."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain what autonomic tone means. Then use the heart and the arterioles to show why removing one autonomic division has different consequences in a dually innervated organ and in an organ with a single innervation.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"tone is a resting level of firing in an autonomic division that can be raised or lowered from baseline; the heart has dual innervation, and at rest vagal tone dominates, holding the rate below the intrinsic SA node rate; cutting the vagus raises heart rate; cutting the sympathetic supply lowers it only slightly; most arterioles receive sympathetic innervation only, so diameter is controlled by raising or lowering sympathetic tone; removing sympathetic input to arterioles causes dilation and there is no parasympathetic input to compensate; dual innervation allows faster and finer control because one division can be raised while the other is lowered","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an organ with both divisions on it as a dial with a pointer, sympathetic pushing one way and parasympathetic the other. Set the pointer at rest and mark which division is pulling harder there. Beside it, draw a blood vessel, which gets only one division, and show how its diameter is adjusted with a single input. One line: define tone using both of your drawings."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a heart with both divisions attached and write its resting rate on it. Then draw it twice more, once with the parasympathetic supply cut and once with the sympathetic supply cut, putting a rate on each and an arrow for the direction it moved. The pacemaker's own intrinsic rate is higher than the resting rate. One line: say what that fact tells you about which division dominates at rest."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain dual innervation and autonomic tone and predict the effect of removing one division from a dually innervated organ."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Dual innervation and tonic control. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-adrenal-medulla","name":"Adrenal medulla","can":"Explain why the adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion and compare the duration of its circulating catecholamine effect with direct sympathetic innervation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The secretory cells of the adrenal medulla are best described as modified versions of which cell?\nA) Postganglionic sympathetic neurons  |  B) Preganglionic sympathetic neurons\nC) Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons  |  D) Skeletal muscle fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Postganglionic sympathetic neurons","why":"Chromaffin cells develop from the same neural crest tissue as postganglionic sympathetic neurons and sit in the same position in the pathway, one synapse downstream of the CNS. Preganglionic neurons are the cells that innervate them, not the cells themselves. There is no parasympathetic component to the medulla, and chromaffin cells are secretory rather than contractile."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which fibers innervate the cells of the adrenal medulla?\nA) Sympathetic postganglionic fibers  |  B) Sympathetic preganglionic fibers\nC) Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers  |  D) Somatic motor fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sympathetic preganglionic fibers","why":"Preganglionic sympathetic axons run through the chain without synapsing and end directly on the chromaffin cells, which is exactly the arrangement seen at any sympathetic ganglion. Postganglionic fibers would imply a synapse had already happened somewhere else. The medulla receives no parasympathetic or somatic supply."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which transmitter and receptor are used where the nerve meets the adrenal medullary cells?\nA) Norepinephrine acting on beta-1 receptors\nB) Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors\nC) Acetylcholine acting on nicotinic receptors\nD) Epinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Acetylcholine acting on nicotinic receptors","why":"This synapse follows the ganglionic rule exactly, acetylcholine released by a preganglionic fiber onto nicotinic receptors, which is the strongest evidence that the medulla is a modified ganglion. Muscarinic receptors would place the synapse at a parasympathetic target instead. Norepinephrine and epinephrine act on adrenergic receptors at the far end of the pathway, not at the ganglionic step."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which catecholamine makes up the majority of adrenal medullary secretion?\nA) Norepinephrine  |  B) Dopamine\nC) Acetylcholine  |  D) Epinephrine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Epinephrine","why":"About eighty percent of the output is epinephrine and most of the rest is norepinephrine, so the medulla is the body main source of circulating epinephrine. Norepinephrine is the minority product here even though it is the transmitter at ordinary sympathetic nerve endings. Dopamine is released in trace amounts, and acetylcholine is the input to these cells rather than their product."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does the adrenal medulla release its secretions?\nA) Into the bloodstream\nB) Into a synaptic cleft at the target organ\nC) Into the interstitial fluid of the kidney only\nD) Into the lumen of a duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Into the bloodstream","why":"Chromaffin cells secrete into capillaries, so their catecholamines act as hormones and reach every tissue with the appropriate receptors. Release into a synaptic cleft is what an ordinary postganglionic neuron does, and it is the difference that makes the medullary response broader and longer. The medulla is not a duct gland and its secretion is not confined to the kidney, despite the location of the adrenal glands."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The adrenal medulla is controlled by postganglionic sympathetic neurons that release norepinephrine onto its chromaffin cells.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The adrenal medulla is controlled by preganglionic sympathetic neurons that release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors on the chromaffin cells, which are themselves modified postganglionic cells.","why":"The chromaffin cells are the postganglionic cells of this pathway. They just never grew axons, so they release their catecholamines into the blood instead of onto a target. Because they occupy the postganglionic position, the fiber that reaches them is preganglionic and cholinergic, the same as the input to any sympathetic ganglion. Students often assume the medulla receives norepinephrine because it produces catecholamines, but the input signal and the output signal are different transmitters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a preganglionic sympathetic neuron running to an ordinary sympathetic ganglion, and below it the same kind of neuron running to the adrenal medulla. Show the transmitter and receptor at each synapse, and show what leaves the second one into a blood vessel (about 80 percent epinephrine, about 20 percent norepinephrine). Mark which cell is the postganglionic neuron that never grew an axon. One line: what the medulla gains by dumping into blood instead of onto a target."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis in seconds and put two response curves on it starting at the same instant: one target reached by direct sympathetic nerve endings, one reached by adrenal catecholamines in the blood. Make the hormonal curve outlast the neural one by roughly five to ten times. On each, mark what ends it, fast reuptake at the synapse against slow clearance from blood over two to four minutes. One line: why a bloodborne signal cannot be switched off quickly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why the adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion and compare the duration of its circulating catecholamine effect with direct sympathetic innervation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Adrenal medulla. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-pharmacology","name":"Autonomic pharmacology","can":"Predict the effect of a beta blocker or a muscarinic antagonist on a named organ from the receptor it targets.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person receives a muscarinic antagonist. Predict the effect on heart rate and on gastrointestinal motility.\nA) Heart rate falls and gastrointestinal motility rises\nB) Both heart rate and gastrointestinal motility fall\nC) Heart rate rises and gastrointestinal motility falls\nD) Both heart rate and gastrointestinal motility rise\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Heart rate rises and gastrointestinal motility falls","why":"Removing parasympathetic influence produces opposite changes in the two organs, because that division normally slows the heart and speeds the gut, so blocking it releases the heart and quiets the gut. Falling rate with rising motility describes adding parasympathetic activity rather than blocking it. The two options that move both in the same direction miss the fact that the same division has opposite effects on these targets."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a drug is given, a subject shows a faster heart rate, reduced gastric secretion, and slowed intestinal motility, while arteriolar resistance is unchanged. Which receptor was blocked?\nA) The beta-1 receptor  |  B) The alpha-1 receptor\nC) The muscarinic receptor  |  D) The nicotinic receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The muscarinic receptor","why":"Every finding points to loss of parasympathetic effect at target organs, and unchanged arteriolar resistance fits because most vessels have no parasympathetic supply, so a muscarinic block leaves them alone. Blocking beta-1 receptors would have slowed the heart rather than speeding it. Blocking alpha-1 receptors would have lowered arteriolar resistance, and blocking nicotinic receptors would have shut down both divisions and dropped vascular resistance as well."},{"t":"mc","q":"A beta blocker reaches the heart of a resting person. Predict the effect on heart rate.\nA) Heart rate rises  |  B) Heart rate becomes irregular but averages the same\nC) Heart rate is unchanged  |  D) Heart rate falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Heart rate falls","why":"Blocking beta-1 receptors removes the sympathetic push on the sinoatrial node, so the node depolarizes more slowly and the rate drops toward or below the value set by vagal tone alone. A rise would follow removal of the vagal brake, which a beta blocker does not touch. Some sympathetic tone is present even at rest, so the rate does change."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscarinic antagonist reaches the heart of a resting person. Predict the effect on heart rate.\nA) Heart rate falls  |  B) Heart rate is unchanged\nC) Heart rate rises  |  D) Heart rate falls, then returns to baseline\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Heart rate rises","why":"Muscarinic receptors carry the vagal signal to the sinoatrial node, so blocking them removes the resting brake and the rate climbs toward the intrinsic pacemaker rate. A fall would follow blockade of beta-1 receptors instead. Because vagal tone dominates the resting heart, this change is substantial rather than absent."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Before an eye exam a patient is given eye drops containing a muscarinic antagonist. Twenty minutes later her pupils are 8 mm wide (normal in room light about 3 to 4 mm) and she cannot read a form held 30 cm away, although her distance vision is unchanged.\nExplain both effects from the receptor that was blocked.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The drug blocks muscarinic receptors on the pupillary sphincter, so the pupil cannot constrict, and on the ciliary muscle, so the lens cannot round up for near focus","why":"Both the pupillary sphincter and the ciliary muscle are smooth muscles driven by parasympathetic fibers through muscarinic receptors. Blocking those receptors removes constriction, so the sympathetic radial muscle wins and the pupil dilates. It also removes the ciliary contraction that rounds the lens, so accommodation fails and near objects blur. Distance vision needs a flat lens, which is the relaxed state, so it is untouched."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient with asthma is prescribed a nonselective beta blocker for high blood pressure. Using receptors, explain what the drug does to the heart, why that lowers blood pressure, what it may do to the airways, and what kind of drug would be a safer choice.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"beta-1 receptors on the SA node and ventricular muscle: blocking them lowers heart rate and contractility; lower heart rate and stroke volume lower cardiac output, which lowers blood pressure; beta-2 receptors on bronchiolar smooth muscle mediate relaxation in response to epinephrine; blocking beta-2 removes that bronchodilation, so airways narrow and asthma can worsen; a beta-1 selective blocker lowers blood pressure while sparing the airway beta-2 receptors","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline with four organs on it, heart, airways, blood vessels, and gut, and label the autonomic receptor type that dominates at each. Then draw a non selective beta blocker arriving and mark every organ it lands on with the effect it produces there. One line: name the organ where this drug does something the prescriber did not want, and the receptor responsible for it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a muscarinic antagonist and draw its effect at four places at once, pupil, salivary gland, heart, and gut. In each drawing, show the parasympathetic input being blocked and the change that follows. Then write which of the four you were actually treating and which three are side effects. One line: what would have to change about the drug to keep only the effect you wanted."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the effect of a beta blocker or a muscarinic antagonist on a named organ from the receptor it targets."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Autonomic pharmacology. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w8-lab-autonomic-testing","name":"Autonomic function testing","can":"Measure heart rate variability and the response to a cold pressor or orthostatic challenge and interpret the result as sympathetic or parasympathetic dominance.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A resting subject shows large beat to beat variation in R to R interval. How should this be interpreted?\nA) As parasympathetic dominance at the heart\nB) As sympathetic dominance at the heart\nC) As loss of all autonomic input to the heart\nD) As a faulty electrode connection\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) As parasympathetic dominance at the heart","why":"High variability at rest reflects strong, rapidly changing vagal influence on the sinoatrial node, which is the hallmark of parasympathetic dominance. Sympathetic dominance produces a faster and more regular rhythm because its effects change slowly. A heart stripped of autonomic input beats with very little variability, which is the opposite finding, and a genuine variability pattern tracks the breathing cycle rather than appearing as random noise."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a cold pressor test, blood pressure rises. Which mechanism accounts for most of the rise?\nA) Alpha-1 mediated arteriolar constriction raising peripheral resistance\nB) Beta-2 mediated dilation of the vessels supplying skeletal muscle\nC) Withdrawal of resting vagal tone from the arterioles\nD) Increased compliance of the veins in the legs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Alpha-1 mediated arteriolar constriction raising peripheral resistance","why":"Sympathetic outflow triggered by the painful stimulus reaches alpha-1 receptors on arterioles throughout the body, and the resulting rise in total peripheral resistance is the main driver of the pressure increase. Beta-2 dilation lowers resistance and would work against the response. Arterioles have essentially no vagal supply, so there is no vagal tone there to withdraw, and greater venous compliance would reduce venous return rather than raise pressure."},{"t":"mc","q":"A subject stands up from lying down and heart rate rises by 15 beats per minute within 15 seconds. How is this interpreted?\nA) As an abnormal parasympathetic surge\nB) As a complete failure of the baroreceptor reflex\nC) As clear evidence of orthostatic hypotension\nD) As a normal sympathetic response with vagal withdrawal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) As a normal sympathetic response with vagal withdrawal","why":"Standing unloads the baroreceptors, and the reflex answer is less vagal traffic and more sympathetic traffic to the heart, so a prompt rise of this size is the expected healthy result. A parasympathetic surge would slow the heart. Orthostatic hypotension is defined by a fall in blood pressure rather than by a rise in rate, and a working reflex is exactly what this response demonstrates."},{"t":"mc","q":"An electrocardiogram shows a steady R to R interval of 800 ms. What is the heart rate?\nA) 60 beats per minute  |  B) 80 beats per minute\nC) 48 beats per minute  |  D) 75 beats per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 75 beats per minute","why":"There are 60000 ms in a minute, so dividing 60000 by an 800 ms interval gives 75 beats per minute. Choosing 80 comes from dividing 800 into a rounded number of seconds incorrectly, and 60 would be the rate if the interval were a full 1000 ms. A rate of 48 corresponds to an interval of about 1250 ms, much longer than the one recorded."},{"t":"mc","q":"A resting subject shows a fast heart rate with almost no beat to beat variation. How should this be interpreted?\nA) As parasympathetic dominance with strong vagal influence\nB) As an entirely normal finding during quiet breathing\nC) As the sign of an unusually fit cardiovascular system\nD) As sympathetic dominance with reduced vagal influence\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) As sympathetic dominance with reduced vagal influence","why":"Losing the fast fluctuations means losing the vagal modulation that creates them, and a fast baseline rate points the same way, toward sympathetic dominance. Strong vagal influence produces the opposite pattern, a slower rate with wide variation. Trained endurance athletes typically show high variability with a low resting rate, and quiet breathing normally produces visible respiratory variation rather than none."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab, two subjects rest lying down for five minutes and then stand. Subject A: lying heart rate 64, blood pressure 118/76; after one minute standing, heart rate 82, blood pressure 116/80. Subject B: lying heart rate 66, blood pressure 120/78; after one minute standing, heart rate 70, blood pressure 92/60, and he reports feeling lightheaded.\nWhich statement correctly interprets the two responses?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Subject A mounted a normal baroreflex response: sympathetic activation and vagal withdrawal raised heart rate and constricted vessels, so pressure was defended. Subject B's sympathetic response was inadequate, so pressure fell","why":"Standing pools blood in the legs, venous return and stroke volume fall, and arterial pressure starts to drop. Baroreceptors fire less, and the reflex answer is more sympathetic outflow and less vagal outflow: faster heart, stronger contraction, constricted arterioles and veins. Subject A shows exactly that, with a rise of 18 beats and a pressure held steady. Subject B raised his rate by only 4 beats and his pressure fell 28 mmHg systolic, so the sympathetic limb did not respond enough and his brain perfusion dipped."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a heart rate trace across several breathing cycles, with the breathing cycle drawn underneath on the same time axis. Show heart rate rising and falling against inspiration and expiration, using your own recorded data, and mark the beat to beat variation you measured. One line: name the division being withdrawn and restored fast enough to make that pattern, and say how the speed alone tells you."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a time axis running through one challenge you performed, cold pressor or standing up. Plot heart rate and blood pressure on it as two curves, before, during, and after, using your own numbers, and mark the moment of the challenge. One line: read your own curves and say which division took over, naming the receptor doing the work out at the effector."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Autonomic function testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Endocrine Principles","comps":[{"id":"w9-hormone-classes","name":"Hormone classes","can":"Classify hormones as peptide, steroid, or amine and compare the three by synthesis, storage, and receptor location.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A hormone binds a receptor inside the nucleus and changes the rate of transcription of specific genes. Which class is it most likely to be?\nA) Steroid  |  B) Catecholamine\nC) Prostaglandin  |  D) Peptide\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Steroid","why":"Only a lipid soluble hormone can reach an intranuclear receptor, and steroid is the class defined by that route, so the response is a change in the amount of new protein made. Peptides and catecholamines act at surface receptors and change the activity of proteins already present. Prostaglandins are local eicosanoids and are not part of the three class hormone scheme."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks all new gene transcription in a target cell. Which hormone response is blocked first?\nA) Epinephrine activating existing glycogen phosphorylase\nB) Cortisol raising the amount of gluconeogenic enzyme\nC) Insulin causing existing glucose transporters to move to the membrane\nD) Glucagon raising cyclic AMP in a liver cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cortisol raising the amount of gluconeogenic enzyme","why":"A steroid works by making new messenger RNA and new protein, so blocking transcription removes its whole mechanism. The other three act through second messengers on proteins the cell already has, which is why their effects appear within seconds and survive a transcription block. Those responses would eventually fade if the cell could never replace worn proteins, but that is a much slower effect."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why must insulin be injected rather than swallowed?\nA) It binds an intracellular receptor that the gut lining does not have\nB) It is a steroid and would be stored in fat before reaching the blood\nC) It is a peptide and would be digested into amino acids in the gut\nD) It is an amine and would be destroyed by stomach acid into cholesterol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It is a peptide and would be digested into amino acids in the gut","why":"Peptide hormones are proteins, and the digestive tract breaks proteins into amino acids, so an oral dose never arrives intact at the target. Steroids are the class that survives the gut and can be taken as a pill, which is why prednisone works orally. Insulin is not an amine, and the reason has nothing to do with receptor location, since insulin receptors are on the cell surface."},{"t":"mc","q":"A newly described hormone is lipid soluble, is not stored in the gland, and can be taken as a tablet. Which class fits best?\nA) Glycoprotein  |  B) Peptide\nC) Catecholamine  |  D) Steroid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Steroid","why":"Lipid solubility explains all three observations at once: the molecule leaves the cell as soon as it is made, so there is no storage pool, and it crosses the gut lining intact, so it survives an oral dose. Peptides and glycoproteins are water soluble, stored in vesicles, and digested if swallowed. Catecholamines are water soluble as well and act at surface receptors with a very short life in plasma."},{"t":"mc","q":"Thyroid hormone is built from tyrosine, yet it uses an intracellular receptor. Which property explains that?\nA) It is lipid soluble, so it can cross the plasma membrane\nB) It is a large glycoprotein that is taken in by endocytosis\nC) It is made from cholesterol despite its name\nD) It is stored in vesicles like a peptide\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is lipid soluble, so it can cross the plasma membrane","why":"Receptor location follows solubility, not the precursor, and thyroid hormone is lipid soluble enough to enter the cell and act on nuclear receptors like a steroid. That is why its effects are slow to start and long lasting. It is not a glycoprotein, and it is built from tyrosine and iodine rather than from cholesterol. Its storage is unusual, held extracellularly in thyroid colloid rather than in secretory vesicles."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Peptide hormones can be stored in vesicles and released within seconds of a stimulus, while steroid hormones must be synthesized each time they are needed.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Storage explains the speed of release. A beta cell can dump insulin in seconds because the vesicles are already loaded. An adrenal cortex cell has almost no cortisol on hand, so the plasma level rises only as fast as the synthetic pathway can run, which takes minutes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three production lines side by side, one peptide, one steroid, one amine. On each, draw the raw material it starts from, whether the cell stores it in vesicles or makes it on demand, how it gets out of the cell, and where its receptor sits, on the surface or inside. Keep all three at the same scale. One line: two of the three lines look nearly identical, so name which two and the single step where they diverge."},{"t":"draw","q":"The amine class does not behave as one class. Draw a catecholamine and a thyroid hormone side by side, both built from tyrosine, and follow each one out of its cell, through the blood, and to its receptor. Show the point where they part company. Beside each, write which of the other two classes it now resembles. One line: name the property of the molecule itself that decided which way each one went."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify hormones as peptide, steroid, or amine and compare the three by synthesis, storage, and receptor location."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone classes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-hormone-transport","name":"Hormone transport and half life","can":"Explain how protein binding in plasma affects the free hormone fraction and the half life and predict which hormone class circulates bound.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug displaces cortisol from corticosteroid binding globulin. What happens to the free cortisol concentration immediately after the drug is given?\nA) It falls  |  B) It is unchanged\nC) It falls, then total cortisol falls with it  |  D) It rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It rises","why":"Displacement moves hormone from the bound reserve into the free pool, so free cortisol rises at once and biological activity increases before feedback has time to respond. It cannot fall, because nothing is being removed from the plasma. Saying it is unchanged confuses total hormone, which barely moves at first, with the free fraction that actually acts."},{"t":"mc","q":"Pregnancy raises thyroxine binding globulin in plasma. What happens to total thyroxine in a woman whose thyroid gland is healthy?\nA) Total T4 rises while free T4 stays near normal\nB) Both total and free T4 fall\nC) Both total and free T4 rise well above normal\nD) Total T4 falls while free T4 rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Total T4 rises while free T4 stays near normal","why":"More carrier binds more hormone, so the total measured in plasma goes up, but feedback adjusts secretion until the free level is back where it started, which is what the hypothalamus and pituitary actually sense. Total falling would require fewer carriers, not more. If free hormone also rose and stayed high, the woman would be thyrotoxic, and negative feedback prevents that in a healthy gland."},{"t":"mc","q":"Liver disease lowers the plasma concentration of hormone binding proteins. Predict the effect on the half life of a highly bound steroid hormone.\nA) The half life lengthens, because less hormone reaches the tissues\nB) The half life shortens\nC) The half life lengthens\nD) The half life is unchanged, since half life depends only on the gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The half life shortens","why":"Fewer carriers means a larger share of hormone is free and therefore exposed to filtration and enzymatic breakdown, so it disappears from plasma faster. Lengthening would require more binding, not less. Half life is a property of clearance, not of secretion rate, and free hormone reaches tissues more readily rather than less."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which hormone would you expect to be measurable in the urine of a healthy person in the largest proportion of what was secreted?\nA) A small water soluble hormone that circulates free\nB) Thyroxine bound to thyroxine binding globulin\nC) A hormone bound to sex hormone binding globulin\nD) A steroid that is 98 percent bound to globulin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A small water soluble hormone that circulates free","why":"Free hormone is small enough to be filtered at the glomerulus, so an unbound water soluble hormone appears in urine, while carrier bound hormone stays in plasma because the protein cannot cross the filter. The three bound options all describe hormones shielded from filtration. This is the same protection that gives bound hormones their long half lives."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman in the third trimester of pregnancy has a total thyroxine (T4) of 16 micrograms/dL (normal 5 to 12). Her free T4 is 1.2 ng/dL (normal 0.8 to 1.8) and her TSH is 1.5 mU/L (normal 0.4 to 4.0). Pregnancy raises the liver's production of thyroxine binding globulin.\nWhich explanation fits all three values?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"More binding protein holds more T4 in the bound pool. The axis senses free T4, which is normal, so TSH is normal and her thyroid hormone action is normal","why":"Only free hormone leaves the blood, enters cells, and feeds back on the pituitary. When carrier protein rises, hormone shifts into the bound pool, free hormone dips for a moment, TSH nudges secretion up, and the free level returns to its set point with a larger bound reserve. The result is a high total, a normal free level, and a normal TSH. Nothing is wrong with her thyroid."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain why steroid hormones circulate mostly bound to plasma proteins, what binding does to their half life, which fraction of the hormone is active, and what happens to total and free hormone if the amount of carrier protein rises with feedback intact.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"steroids are lipophilic and poorly soluble in plasma, so they ride on carrier proteins; only the free (unbound) fraction can leave the capillary and enter target cells; bound hormone is protected from liver and kidney clearance, so the half life is long (hours to days); bound and free hormone are in equilibrium, as free hormone is used more is released from the carrier; peptide hormones circulate free and have short half lives (minutes); feedback loops sense the free hormone; more carrier protein raises total hormone, but free hormone returns to normal once feedback adjusts secretion","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a length of capillary with a steroid hormone and a peptide hormone both inside it. Draw the steroid mostly stuck to carrier proteins with a small free fraction, and the peptide traveling free. At the tissue, draw an arrow for which molecules can actually leave and act. At the kidney, draw which ones get filtered. One line: only free hormone acts and only free hormone is cleared, so say why binding makes a hormone last longer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw plasma concentration against time after one pulse of a bound hormone and one pulse of an unbound hormone on the same axes, and mark the half life on each. Then draw a third case, the bound hormone in a person whose carrier protein level has doubled. Show what happened to total hormone and what happened to free hormone. One line: the total went up but the person feels normal, so name the number the lab should have measured."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how protein binding in plasma affects the free hormone fraction and the half life and predict which hormone class circulates bound."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone transport and half life. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-hormone-receptors","name":"Hormone receptors and target response","can":"Explain why one hormone can produce different responses in different tissues and relate receptor number to target cell sensitivity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person has had very high insulin levels for years. Predict the change in insulin receptor number and in target cell sensitivity.\nA) Receptor number rises and sensitivity increases\nB) Receptor number falls and sensitivity decreases\nC) Receptor number falls and sensitivity increases\nD) Receptor number is unchanged and sensitivity decreases\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Receptor number falls and sensitivity decreases","why":"Sustained excess hormone triggers internalization and degradation of receptors, so fewer remain and a given insulin level does less, which is the core of insulin resistance. Rising receptor number is the answer to chronic hormone deficiency. A fall in receptors cannot raise sensitivity, and resistance in this setting is driven by the receptor change rather than occurring with receptor number held constant."},{"t":"mc","q":"What determines whether a given cell responds to a hormone circulating in the blood?\nA) Whether the cell is close to the gland that made the hormone\nB) Whether the cell expresses a receptor for that hormone\nC) Whether the hormone is present at a high enough total concentration\nD) Whether the cell has an open channel for the hormone to enter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Whether the cell expresses a receptor for that hormone","why":"Blood carries hormone to every cell, so the receptor is what selects the target and gives endocrine signaling its specificity. Distance from the gland does not matter once a hormone is in the circulation. Concentration changes how strong the response is in cells that already have receptors, and hormones do not enter through channels."},{"t":"mc","q":"What decides the nature of the response a target cell makes to a hormone?\nA) The gland that secreted the hormone\nB) The chemical structure of the hormone molecule by itself\nC) The receptor and signaling machinery of the target cell\nD) The distance the hormone traveled in the blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The receptor and signaling machinery of the target cell","why":"The hormone is only a key, and the response depends on what the cell it unlocks is equipped to do, which is why one molecule can trigger opposite effects in two tissues. The hormone structure determines which receptors it fits, not what those receptors then do. Travel distance and gland of origin have no bearing on the target cell's machinery."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does down regulation of a receptor mean?\nA) The gland decreases its secretion of the hormone\nB) The receptor moves from the membrane into the nucleus instead\nC) The hormone is broken down faster in the liver\nD) The target cell reduces the number of receptors for a hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The target cell reduces the number of receptors for a hormone","why":"Down regulation is a change in the target cell, which internalizes and degrades receptors so the same hormone concentration produces a smaller effect. Reduced secretion is a change in the gland and is a separate mechanism. Receptors do not migrate between the membrane and the nucleus, and faster hepatic breakdown changes hormone level rather than receptor number."},{"t":"mc","q":"Chronically elevated hormone levels usually produce which change in target cell receptors?\nA) Down regulation, so the cell becomes less sensitive\nB) No change, since receptor number is fixed at birth\nC) Conversion of surface receptors into intracellular receptors\nD) Up regulation, so the cell becomes more sensitive\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Down regulation, so the cell becomes less sensitive","why":"Persistent high hormone drives receptor internalization and degradation, which protects the cell from overstimulation and is a common route into hormone resistance. Up regulation is the opposite response and follows chronically low hormone. Receptor number is continually adjusted rather than fixed, and receptor type does not switch locations."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Epinephrine is infused into a volunteer. Blood flow to skeletal muscle rises 40 percent. Blood flow to the skin falls 30 percent. Heart rate rises from 65 to 95 beats per minute. The hormone reaches every tissue at the same blood concentration.\nWhy does one hormone produce opposite effects on the two vascular beds?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The response is decided by the target cell. Skeletal muscle arterioles express mostly beta 2 receptors, which relax the smooth muscle. Skin arterioles express mostly alpha 1 receptors, which contract it","why":"A hormone carries no instructions. It only carries a shape. What happens next depends on which receptor subtype the target cell expresses and what that receptor is wired to inside the cell. Beta 2 receptors couple to Gs and relax vascular smooth muscle. Alpha 1 receptors couple to Gq and contract it. The same molecule in the same blood gives opposite results because the two tissues read it differently."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one hormone molecule in the middle with arrows out to three different target tissues. Pick a hormone with genuinely different actions in different places, not insulin. In each tissue draw the receptor it meets and the response it gets, and make at least two of the three responses run in opposite directions. One line: the hormone was identical in all three, so name what was different and say where that difference physically lives."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one target cell three times: with a normal number of receptors, after weeks of very high hormone levels, and after weeks of very low hormone levels. Count receptors on the drawing rather than just labeling it. Beside the three cells, draw all three dose response curves on shared axes. One line: one of your cells now needs far more hormone for the same response, so name it and say what the body did to cause that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why one hormone can produce different responses in different tissues and relate receptor number to target cell sensitivity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone receptors and target response. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-hormone-interactions","name":"Hormone interactions","can":"Define synergism, permissiveness, and antagonism and identify each in a described hormone pair.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Hormone A alone raises a measured response by 10 units, hormone B alone by 10 units, and the two together by 45 units. Which interaction is this?\nA) Synergism  |  B) Permissiveness\nC) Antagonism  |  D) Additive effect\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Synergism","why":"Addition would predict 20 units, and 45 is far above that, so the two hormones amplify each other rather than simply summing. Permissiveness would show one hormone producing little or nothing alone, but here each produces 10 units. Antagonism would give a combined value below 20."},{"t":"mc","q":"Hormone C alone raises a response by 0 units, hormone D alone by 5 units, and the two together by 30 units. Which interaction is this?\nA) Additive effect  |  B) Permissiveness\nC) Synergism  |  D) Antagonism\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Permissiveness","why":"Hormone C does nothing on its own yet the response to hormone D is six times larger in its presence, which is the signature of a permissive hormone that prepares the tissue. Antagonism would lower the combined response below 5 units. An additive effect would give 5 units, and synergism is usually reserved for pairs that each produce a measurable effect alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Parathyroid hormone raises plasma Ca2+ and calcitonin lowers it. Which term describes this pair?\nA) Synergists  |  B) Trophic partners\nC) Permissive partners  |  D) Antagonists\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Antagonists","why":"The two hormones act on the same variable in opposite directions, so plasma Ca2+ settles where their opposing effects balance. Permissiveness would require one to enable the other. Synergism means pushing the same way beyond addition, and neither hormone stimulates the other's gland, which is what trophic would mean."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does synergism between two hormones mean?\nA) Their combined effect is greater than the sum of their separate effects\nB) One hormone must be present for the other to act at all\nC) One hormone opposes the effect of the other\nD) Their combined effect is exactly the sum of their separate effects\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Their combined effect is greater than the sum of their separate effects","why":"Synergism means the two together produce more than simple addition, usually because they act at different points of the same pathway and amplify one another. Requiring one hormone to be present for the other is permissiveness. Opposition is antagonism, and exact addition is an additive effect, which is the baseline that synergism exceeds."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does permissiveness between two hormones mean?\nA) The two hormones together produce more than the sum of their effects\nB) One hormone must be present for the second to exert its full effect\nC) The two hormones cancel each other out completely\nD) The two hormones bind the same receptor with equal affinity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) One hormone must be present for the second to exert its full effect","why":"A permissive hormone does little on its own but sets the stage, often by increasing the number of receptors or enzymes the second hormone needs. More than additive effect describes synergism, in which both hormones produce an effect on their own. Cancellation is antagonism, and permissiveness does not require the two to share a receptor."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Insulin and glucagon are antagonists because they have opposite effects on blood glucose.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Antagonistic pairs let the body push a variable in both directions and hold it steady. Insulin promotes glucose uptake and storage. Glucagon promotes glycogen breakdown and glucose release. The ratio of the two, not either alone, sets the blood glucose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same axes three times, hormone dose on the x and response on the y, and use them to show synergism, permissiveness, and antagonism. On each pair of axes plot hormone A alone, hormone B alone, and the two given together, so the shape of the interaction is visible. Label a real hormone pair on each. One line: in one of the three, hormone B does almost nothing by itself, so name that interaction and say why B still matters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick three hormone pairs, one synergistic, one permissive, one antagonistic, and draw each pair acting on the same target organ, with the organ drawn once per pair. Size the arrows into the organ to the effect each hormone has alone and to the effect the two have together. Then take one hormone out of each pair and draw what is left. One line: in one pair, removing the hormone that does nothing alone wrecked the response, so name that pair."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define synergism, permissiveness, and antagonism and identify each in a described hormone pair."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone interactions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-hormone-release","name":"Control of hormone release","can":"Classify a hormone stimulus as humoral, neural, or hormonal and trace an example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma K+ rises and the adrenal cortex secretes more aldosterone. Classify the stimulus.\nA) Hormonal, because K+ acts through renin\nB) Neural, because K+ depolarizes nerve endings on the gland\nC) Humoral, because the ion acts on the gland directly\nD) Hormonal, because K+ acts through ACTH\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Humoral, because the ion acts on the gland directly","why":"Zona glomerulosa cells are depolarized directly by extracellular K+, which is why potassium is one of the strongest aldosterone stimuli and needs no intermediary. The depolarization happens in the endocrine cell rather than in a nerve. ACTH has only a permissive role for aldosterone, and while renin drives the angiotensin route, potassium acts on the gland without it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Suckling at the breast leads to oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary. Classify the stimulus.\nA) Hormonal, since prolactin release triggers oxytocin release from the gland\nB) Humoral, since the fall in plasma volume triggers it\nC) Humoral, since milk components enter the blood\nD) Neural, since sensory nerves drive the hypothalamic neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Neural, since sensory nerves drive the hypothalamic neurons","why":"Mechanoreceptors in the nipple send action potentials to the hypothalamus, and the hypothalamic neurons release oxytocin from their endings in the posterior pituitary, so the whole loop is neural. Nothing dissolved in blood is being sensed, which rules out both humoral options. Prolactin is released by the same reflex but does not itself trigger oxytocin."},{"t":"mc","q":"TSH from the pituitary raises thyroid hormone output. Classify the stimulus acting on the thyroid gland.\nA) Hormonal  |  B) Neurohumoral\nC) Neural  |  D) Humoral\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Hormonal","why":"The thyroid follicular cell is responding to a hormone from another endocrine gland, which is the hormonal category by definition. It is not sensing an ion or nutrient in the blood, so humoral does not apply, even though iodine availability limits how much hormone it can make. No nerve fiber drives thyroid hormone secretion."},{"t":"mc","q":"Blood glucose falls during a fast and the alpha cell secretes glucagon. Classify the stimulus and name what the gland is sensing.\nA) Humoral, sensing the plasma Ca2+ concentration\nB) Neural, sensing sympathetic activity to the pancreas\nC) Humoral, sensing the plasma glucose concentration\nD) Hormonal, sensing the fall in plasma insulin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Humoral, sensing the plasma glucose concentration","why":"The alpha cell reads the nutrient level in blood directly, which is a humoral stimulus and lets the pancreas respond to fuel status without waiting for any other signal. Sympathetic input does modulate the islet during stress but is not the primary trigger during a simple fast. Falling insulin contributes but is not what the alpha cell is chiefly sensing, and Ca2+ is the humoral stimulus for the parathyroid rather than the alpha cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pathway would still work in an animal whose nerves to the endocrine gland have all been cut, with everything else intact?\nA) Suckling raising oxytocin from the posterior pituitary\nB) Cold air on the skin raising sympathetic output\nC) A startle raising epinephrine from the adrenal medulla\nD) Low plasma Ca2+ raising parathyroid hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Low plasma Ca2+ raising parathyroid hormone","why":"A humoral pathway needs only the blood and the gland, so parathyroid hormone release continues when the nerve supply is gone. The startle response and the suckling reflex both begin with sensory nerves and end with neural drive to secretory cells, so cutting nerves abolishes them. Cold sensing and sympathetic output are neural from start to finish."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Insulin release in response to rising blood glucose is an example of neural control of hormone release.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. It is an example of humoral control. The beta cell senses the glucose concentration in the blood directly, without a nerve or another hormone telling it to secrete.","why":"Classify a stimulus by what the endocrine cell actually detects. The beta cell takes up glucose, makes ATP, closes potassium channels, depolarizes, and releases insulin. No neuron is required, although nerves can adjust the response. That direct sensing of a blood chemical is the humoral pattern."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three trigger paths on one page, each ending at a gland letting go of its hormone. Make one humoral, driven by an ion or nutrient level in the blood, one neural, driven by a nerve firing onto the gland, and one hormonal, driven by another hormone. Use a real example for each and draw the actual stimulus, not the word for it. One line: name which of your three acts fastest and say what makes it fast."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw plasma calcium falling below its set point and trace the whole humoral loop it starts, gland, hormone, target tissues, and calcium returning, with a feedback arrow closing the loop. Beside it, draw the neural path to the adrenal medulla, from spinal cord to hormone in the blood, and note that this one has no releasing hormone anywhere in it. One line: say what the adrenal medulla is in tissue terms that lets a nerve release a hormone directly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a hormone stimulus as humoral, neural, or hormonal and trace an example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Control of hormone release. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-hypothalamic-pituitary","name":"Hypothalamic pituitary axes","can":"Trace a three tier axis from hypothalamic releasing hormone through the anterior pituitary trophic hormone to the peripheral gland and place the long and short loop negative feedback.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor secretes ACTH continuously and is not part of the pituitary. Predict what happens to CRH from the hypothalamus.\nA) CRH is unchanged, since the tumor is outside the axis\nB) CRH rises, because the adrenal gland is being overdriven\nC) CRH rises to match the ACTH output\nD) CRH falls, suppressed by both the high cortisol and ACTH\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) CRH falls, suppressed by both the high cortisol and ACTH","why":"High ACTH drives cortisol up, and cortisol suppresses the hypothalamus by long loop feedback while ACTH itself suppresses it by short loop feedback, so CRH is doubly inhibited. Rising CRH would require the feedback signals to be stimulatory. The tumor is outside the normal axis but its hormone is not, so the loops still respond."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient takes a high dose of a synthetic glucocorticoid for months. Predict the state of CRH, ACTH, and the adrenal cortex.\nA) CRH and ACTH are low and the adrenal cortex atrophies\nB) CRH is high, ACTH is low, and the cortex is normal\nC) CRH and ACTH are low and the adrenal cortex enlarges\nD) CRH and ACTH are high and the adrenal cortex enlarges\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) CRH and ACTH are low and the adrenal cortex atrophies","why":"The drug acts on the same receptors as cortisol, so the axis reads it as an excess and shuts down both upper tiers, and without ACTH the cortex loses its trophic support and shrinks. High CRH and ACTH would require the axis not to recognize the drug. A cortex cannot enlarge while its trophic hormone is suppressed, since ACTH is what maintains its mass."},{"t":"mc","q":"Growth hormone acts partly through IGF-1 made in the liver. Which molecule provides the long loop negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary in this axis?\nA) Somatostatin  |  B) Insulin\nC) GHRH  |  D) IGF-1\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) IGF-1","why":"IGF-1 is the peripheral product of the axis, so it plays the role cortisol or thyroid hormone plays elsewhere and inhibits both GHRH release and growth hormone release. GHRH is the tier one releasing hormone rather than a feedback signal. Somatostatin is an inhibiting hormone from the hypothalamus, part of the control rather than the feedback from the periphery, and insulin belongs to a different regulatory system."},{"t":"mc","q":"How do hypothalamic releasing hormones reach the anterior pituitary?\nA) By diffusion across the blood brain barrier\nB) Through the hypophyseal portal veins\nC) Through the general systemic circulation from the heart\nD) Down the axons of hypothalamic neurons into the anterior lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Through the hypophyseal portal veins","why":"A portal system carries blood from a capillary bed in the hypothalamus straight to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary, so tiny amounts of releasing hormone arrive undiluted. Going through the systemic circulation would dilute them past usefulness. Axonal transport into the lobe describes the posterior pituitary, and these hormones do not need to cross the blood brain barrier on this route."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has taken a high dose of prednisone, a synthetic glucocorticoid that acts like cortisol, every day for 6 months for an autoimmune disease. She stops it suddenly. Two days later she is weak, nauseated, and dizzy when she stands. Morning cortisol is 1 microgram/dL (normal 5 to 25). ACTH is 8 pg/mL (normal 10 to 60).\nTrace what has happened in the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Months of glucocorticoid suppressed CRH and ACTH by negative feedback, and the unstimulated adrenal cortex shrank. With the drug gone, neither ACTH nor cortisol can rise quickly, so both are low","why":"The axis cannot tell prednisone from cortisol. For six months the hypothalamus and pituitary saw plenty of glucocorticoid and shut off CRH and ACTH. An adrenal cortex without ACTH atrophies. When the drug stops, the suppressed upper tiers recover slowly and the shrunken gland could not respond quickly even if they did. Low cortisol with low ACTH is the secondary pattern, and it was caused by feedback, not by damage."},{"t":"bd","q":"Trace the thyroid axis from the hypothalamus to the thyroid gland, and explain where the long loop and short loop feedback act and what they do.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the hypothalamus releases TRH into the hypothalamic hypophyseal portal system; TRH stimulates the anterior pituitary to release TSH; TSH stimulates the thyroid to release T3 and T4; long loop: thyroid hormone feeds back negatively on the anterior pituitary and the hypothalamus; short loop: TSH feeds back negatively on the hypothalamus to reduce TRH; high thyroid hormone lowers TRH and TSH, low thyroid hormone raises them","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a three tier axis of your choosing, not the thyroid one, from hypothalamus to anterior pituitary to peripheral gland to final hormone. Draw the portal vessels between the first two tiers and systemic blood after that, because they are not the same route. Then draw the long loop feedback arrow and the short loop feedback arrow and land each one where it belongs. One line: say what the portal system buys the body that dumping the releasing hormone into the general circulation would not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one axis with its feedback loops and all three hormone levels marked normal. Then draw the same axis in a person who has taken a large daily dose of the final hormone from outside for six months. Show what happened at each tier and draw the peripheral gland at the size it ends up. One line: say what happens on the day that outside hormone is stopped suddenly, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace a three tier axis from hypothalamic releasing hormone through the anterior pituitary trophic hormone to the peripheral gland and place the long and short loop negative feedback."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hypothalamic pituitary axes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-endocrine-pathology","name":"Primary and secondary endocrine disorders","can":"Use hormone levels at two tiers of an axis to classify a disorder as primary or secondary and as hypersecretion or hyposecretion.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a low free T4 with a high TSH. Classify the disorder.\nA) Primary hyposecretion  |  B) Secondary hypersecretion\nC) Secondary hyposecretion  |  D) Primary hypersecretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Primary hyposecretion","why":"Low peripheral hormone sets the direction as hyposecretion, and the high trophic hormone shows that the pituitary is healthy and pushing hard on a gland that cannot answer, which places the defect in the thyroid itself. Secondary hyposecretion would show a low TSH alongside the low T4. Both hypersecretion answers are ruled out because the peripheral hormone is low, not high."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a high free T4 with a suppressed TSH. Classify the disorder.\nA) Secondary hyposecretion  |  B) Primary hypersecretion\nC) Secondary hypersecretion  |  D) Primary hyposecretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Primary hypersecretion","why":"High peripheral hormone sets the direction as hypersecretion, and the suppressed trophic hormone shows the pituitary is responding normally by shutting down, so the thyroid must be making hormone on its own. Secondary hypersecretion would show TSH high or inappropriately normal despite the high T4. Both hyposecretion answers are ruled out because the peripheral hormone is high."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a low morning cortisol with a markedly elevated ACTH. Where is the defect and in which direction?\nA) The adrenal cortex, with hyposecretion\nB) The adrenal cortex, with hypersecretion\nC) The anterior pituitary, with hypersecretion\nD) The anterior pituitary, with hyposecretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The adrenal cortex, with hyposecretion","why":"Cortisol is low, so the direction is hyposecretion, and the elevated ACTH proves the pituitary is working and being ignored, which places the failure in the cortex itself. A pituitary defect would leave ACTH low along with cortisol. Neither hypersecretion answer is possible when the peripheral hormone is low."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pituitary tumor destroys the corticotrophs while sparing the adrenal glands. Predict the ACTH and cortisol levels and classify the result.\nA) ACTH high and cortisol low, primary hyposecretion\nB) Both low, secondary hyposecretion\nC) ACTH low and cortisol high, primary hypersecretion\nD) Both high, secondary hypersecretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both low, secondary hyposecretion","why":"Losing the cells that make ACTH removes the trophic drive, and an adrenal cortex that is intact but unstimulated makes very little cortisol, so both tiers read low and the defect is one tier above the gland. High ACTH with low cortisol would require a healthy pituitary and a failing adrenal. The two options with a high hormone contradict the loss of secretory cells."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: Two patients have T4 and TSH measured. Patient 1: T4 2 micrograms/dL (normal 5 to 12), TSH 45 mU/L (normal 0.4 to 4.0). Patient 2: T4 2 micrograms/dL (normal 5 to 12), TSH 0.2 mU/L (normal 0.4 to 4.0).\nClassify each disorder and identify which tier of the axis is failing.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Patient 1 has primary hyposecretion: the thyroid is failing, and TSH is high because negative feedback is lost. Patient 2 has secondary hyposecretion: the pituitary or hypothalamus is failing, so TSH is low and the thyroid is unstimulated","why":"The same low T4 means two different things depending on the tier above it. A pituitary that sees low T4 and responds correctly pumps out TSH, so a high TSH means the pituitary is healthy and the thyroid is the problem. A pituitary that sees low T4 and does nothing is itself the problem, so a low TSH with low T4 points upstream."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient has a low plasma cortisol. Explain how measuring ACTH tells you whether the problem is in the adrenal cortex or in the pituitary, and predict the ACTH level in each case.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"primary hyposecretion means the peripheral gland (the adrenal cortex) is failing; in primary hyposecretion, low cortisol removes negative feedback, so ACTH is high; secondary hyposecretion means the pituitary (or hypothalamus) is failing; in secondary hyposecretion, ACTH is low and the adrenal is low because it is not being stimulated; the pattern of the two tiers together, not cortisol alone, identifies the failing tier; giving ACTH would raise cortisol in the secondary case but not in the primary case","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a two tier axis, the pituitary trophic hormone above and the peripheral gland hormone below, and draw it four times: primary hyposecretion, secondary hyposecretion, primary hypersecretion, secondary hypersecretion. In each, draw both levels as bars, high, normal, or low, and mark where the break sits. One line: state the rule all four of your drawings obey, in terms of whether the two bars move the same way or opposite ways."},{"t":"draw","q":"Your patient has low thyroid hormone. Draw the axis twice, once with the fault in the thyroid and once with the fault in the pituitary, and draw the TSH level as a bar in each. Then draw what happens to each patient's TSH once you give them thyroid hormone by mouth. One line: one lab value separated these two patients before you treated either of them, so name it and give its direction in each case."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to use hormone levels at two tiers of an axis to classify a disorder as primary or secondary and as hypersecretion or hyposecretion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Primary and secondary endocrine disorders. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Endocrine Glands","comps":[{"id":"w9-posterior-pituitary","name":"Posterior pituitary hormones","can":"Explain why the posterior pituitary is neural tissue and state the stimulus and target action of antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient secretes antidiuretic hormone continuously regardless of plasma osmolarity. Predict plasma sodium and urine osmolarity.\nA) Plasma sodium falls and urine osmolarity is high\nB) Plasma sodium rises and urine osmolarity is high\nC) Plasma sodium falls and urine osmolarity is low\nD) Plasma sodium rises and urine osmolarity is low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Plasma sodium falls and urine osmolarity is high","why":"Unregulated ADH keeps aquaporins in place, so water is retained and dilutes the plasma, dropping sodium, while the small urine that is passed is concentrated. Rising sodium with concentrated urine would mean water is being lost, not retained. Falling sodium with dilute urine is the pattern of drinking far too much water with ADH properly suppressed. Rising sodium with dilute urine is the picture of absent ADH."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student drinks two liters of water quickly. What happens to antidiuretic hormone release?\nA) It falls, because plasma osmolarity drops\nB) It rises, because plasma volume increased\nC) It rises, because the kidney must handle more water\nD) It is unchanged, since only sodium intake affects it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It falls, because plasma osmolarity drops","why":"Diluting the plasma lowers osmolarity, the osmoreceptors quiet down, ADH release falls, and the collecting duct removes aquaporins so a large dilute urine is passed. Increased volume does not raise ADH, it lowers it through the low pressure stretch receptors. The kidney handles the extra water by making less ADH, not more. Water alone changes osmolarity, so sodium intake is not the only input."},{"t":"mc","q":"A hiker has gone eight hours without water on a hot day. What is her urine like?\nA) Large in volume and dilute  |  B) Small in volume and concentrated\nC) Large in volume and concentrated  |  D) Small in volume and dilute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Small in volume and concentrated","why":"Water loss raises plasma osmolarity, ADH rises, aquaporins go into the collecting duct, and water is reclaimed so the urine is scant and concentrated. Large and dilute is the water loaded response, when ADH is low. Large and concentrated happens only when a solute such as glucose forces an osmotic diuresis. Small and dilute is not physiologically possible as a normal response, since low volume comes from reclaiming water."},{"t":"mc","q":"Alcohol suppresses antidiuretic hormone secretion. What does that predict about urine output?\nA) Urine volume falls and osmolarity rises\nB) Urine volume is unchanged but sodium loss rises\nC) Urine volume rises above the volume of fluid drunk\nD) Urine volume falls because plasma volume falls first\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Urine volume rises above the volume of fluid drunk","why":"With ADH suppressed the collecting duct stays water impermeable, so water that would normally be reclaimed is lost and the person can excrete more fluid than they drank, which is why alcohol dehydrates. Falling volume with rising osmolarity is the high ADH pattern. ADH controls water rather than sodium handling, so a pure sodium loss is not the prediction. Plasma volume falls as a consequence here, not as the cause."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient loses posterior pituitary function after surgery. What pattern of urine do you predict?\nA) Very small volumes of concentrated urine\nB) Very large volumes of concentrated urine\nC) Normal volume with glucose present\nD) Very large volumes of dilute urine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Very large volumes of dilute urine","why":"Without ADH the collecting duct cannot insert aquaporins, so water passes straight through and the person makes many liters of dilute urine each day, the picture called diabetes insipidus. Small concentrated volumes require ADH to be present and working. Glucose in the urine points to diabetes mellitus, a different problem entirely. Large volumes of concentrated urine would require a filtered solute load, which is not the case here."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A fall in plasma osmolarity stimulates ADH release, which causes the kidney to reabsorb more water.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A rise in plasma osmolarity stimulates ADH release. When osmolarity falls, ADH is suppressed and the kidney excretes the extra water.","why":"ADH exists to defend water balance. Concentrated plasma means the body needs water, so hypothalamic osmoreceptors fire, ADH is released, aquaporins go into the collecting duct, and water is saved. Dilute plasma means too much water, so ADH shuts off and dilute urine carries the excess out. The second half of the statement is right, the trigger is backwards."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the hypothalamus and both pituitary lobes in one sagittal section. For the posterior lobe, draw the neuron cell bodies up in the hypothalamus with their axons running down into the lobe and ending on capillaries. For the anterior lobe, draw the portal vessels instead. Shade the neural tissue and the glandular tissue differently. One line: say why cutting the stalk does different things to the two lobes' hormones."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person who has been out in the desert all day. Draw plasma osmolarity climbing above the normal 290 mOsm per liter, the osmoreceptor detecting it, the hypothalamic neuron firing, ADH leaving the posterior lobe, and water channels going into the collecting duct membrane. Draw the urine that results. Beside it, draw the oxytocin neuron and both of its targets. One line: both hormones leave the same lobe, so say what decides which one comes out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why the posterior pituitary is neural tissue and state the stimulus and target action of antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Posterior pituitary hormones. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-growth-hormone","name":"Growth hormone","can":"Describe the direct and insulin like growth factor mediated actions of growth hormone and predict the result of excess or deficiency before and after epiphyseal closure.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A child produces almost no growth hormone from infancy. What do you predict about his adult stature and body proportions?\nA) Short with a normal trunk and very short limbs\nB) Short with roughly normal proportions\nC) Tall with unusually long limbs\nD) Normal height with enlarged hands and jaw\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Short with roughly normal proportions","why":"Growth hormone deficiency slows growth at every plate at once, so the child ends up small but proportioned, the picture of pituitary dwarfism. Short limbs with a normal trunk is the pattern of a cartilage disorder such as achondroplasia, not a hormone deficiency. Tall with long limbs points to excess growth hormone or delayed plate closure. Normal height with enlarged hands and jaw is acromegaly, an adult onset excess."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to growth hormone secretion during a prolonged fast, and why does that make metabolic sense?\nA) It rises, mobilizing fat and sparing glucose\nB) It falls, reducing the demand for fuel\nC) It falls, allowing insulin to store the remaining glucose\nD) It rises, increasing glucose uptake into muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It rises, mobilizing fat and sparing glucose","why":"Fasting increases growth hormone secretion, which frees fatty acids for muscle and other tissues while limiting glucose uptake so the brain keeps its supply. A fall would remove that fuel switching signal at the moment it is most needed. Insulin secretion falls during a fast, so there is little glucose storage to permit. Growth hormone reduces rather than increases glucose uptake into muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"An adult with closed epiphyseal plates is given growth hormone. What effect on height do you predict?\nA) A gain of several centimeters of height over a year\nB) No increase in height, since no plate cartilage remains\nC) A gain in height only if plasma IGF-1 also rises\nD) A loss of height from increased bone resorption\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) No increase in height, since no plate cartilage remains","why":"Lengthening requires proliferating cartilage at the epiphyseal plate, and once that plate has been replaced by bone the substrate for length is simply gone. A gain of centimeters is impossible for the same reason. Raising IGF-1 does not help, because IGF-1 needs plate cartilage to act on. Growth hormone does not cause height loss, since it favors bone formation rather than resorption."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with a growth hormone secreting tumor is found to have a fasting plasma glucose of 130 mg/dL. Which growth hormone action explains this?\nA) Its IGF-1 mediated action on cartilage\nB) Its stimulation of insulin secretion by beta cells\nC) Its direct anti insulin action in muscle and fat\nD) Its inhibition of hepatic glucose output\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Its direct anti insulin action in muscle and fat","why":"Growth hormone directly opposes insulin, cutting glucose uptake into muscle and fat while the liver keeps releasing glucose, so fasting glucose drifts upward. The IGF-1 mediated action is on growth, not on glucose handling. Beta cells actually work harder to compensate here, but that is a response to the high glucose rather than the cause. Growth hormone raises hepatic glucose output rather than inhibiting it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 9 year old boy is the tallest child in his class by a wide margin. His growth hormone is 45 ng/mL (normal fasting less than 5) and his IGF-1 is well above the range for his age. An X ray shows open epiphyseal plates in his long bones.\nIf this hormone excess continued untreated for the next several years, what would happen to his height, and why?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"He would keep growing much taller than expected, because IGF-1 drives cartilage proliferation at plates that are still open","why":"Growth hormone, acting mostly through hepatic IGF-1, stimulates chondrocytes at the epiphyseal plate to divide and lay down cartilage that is then converted to bone. As long as the plates are open, more hormone means more linear growth. This is the mechanism of gigantism. Plate closure is driven by sex steroids at puberty, not by growth hormone, so nothing here stops the process until puberty does."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"An adult who develops a growth hormone secreting tumor will grow taller over the following years.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. After the epiphyseal plates have closed, bones cannot lengthen, so excess growth hormone in an adult thickens bones and enlarges soft tissue (acromegaly) without adding height.","why":"Growth hormone lengthens bones by stimulating cartilage proliferation at the epiphyseal plate, mostly through IGF-1. Once the plate has fused, that target is gone. The hormone still acts on bone thickness, on the hands, feet, jaw, and on soft tissues and metabolism, which is why the same excess produces gigantism in a child but acromegaly in an adult."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the anterior pituitary with two arrow paths leaving it. Path one goes straight to liver, muscle and adipose: label lipolysis, reduced glucose uptake, plasma glucose rising. Path two goes to the liver, makes insulin like growth factor 1, and that arrow lands on the epiphyseal plate of a long bone. Label which effects are direct and which are IGF mediated. One line: name the effect that would show up in a fasting glucose reading, and say which path it came from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four long bones side by side, each with its epiphyseal plate drawn open or fused. Bone one is normal. Bone two has excess growth hormone with the plate still open. Bone three has excess with the plate fused. Bone four has deficiency with the plate open. Sketch the shape change in each and name the condition. One line: say why bone three gets thicker and coarser instead of longer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the direct and insulin like growth factor mediated actions of growth hormone and predict the result of excess or deficiency before and after epiphyseal closure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Growth hormone. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-thyroid","name":"Thyroid hormone","can":"Trace thyroid hormone synthesis, state the metabolic actions of T3 and T4, and match hyperthyroid and hypothyroid signs to the underlying rate of metabolism.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has low plasma T4 and high plasma TSH. Where is the problem?\nA) In the hypothalamus  |  B) In the thyroid gland itself\nC) In the anterior pituitary  |  D) In the peripheral deiodinase enzymes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) In the thyroid gland itself","why":"A high TSH shows the pituitary is sensing low hormone and pushing hard, so the failure to respond must lie in the gland, which is what primary hypothyroidism means. A hypothalamic problem would lower TSH along with T4. A pituitary problem would also give a low TSH, since it could not produce the drive. A deiodinase problem would reduce T3 while leaving T4 normal or high."},{"t":"mc","q":"A different patient has low plasma T4 and low plasma TSH. What does this pattern indicate?\nA) The thyroid gland has been destroyed by autoantibodies\nB) Dietary iodine has been severely deficient in this patient\nC) The pituitary is not producing enough TSH to drive the gland\nD) Excess thyroid hormone is being taken by mouth daily\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The pituitary is not producing enough TSH to drive the gland","why":"When hormone is low, TSH should be high, so a low TSH means the drive itself has failed and the problem sits above the gland in the pituitary or hypothalamus. Destruction of the gland or iodine deficiency would both leave the pituitary intact and TSH would be high. Excess hormone taken by mouth would suppress TSH but plasma T4 would be high, not low."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has been taking too much thyroid hormone for months. Predict oxygen consumption, appetite, and body weight together.\nA) Oxygen consumption up, appetite up, weight down\nB) Oxygen consumption up, appetite down, weight down\nC) Oxygen consumption down, appetite up, weight up\nD) Oxygen consumption up, appetite up, weight up\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Oxygen consumption up, appetite up, weight down","why":"A raised metabolic rate burns more oxygen and more fuel, hunger rises in response, but intake usually fails to keep pace with expenditure so weight still falls. Appetite going down does not fit a high metabolic demand. Falling oxygen consumption is the hypothyroid pattern. Weight gain would require intake to exceed the increased expenditure, which is not the usual outcome."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person's diet has contained almost no iodine for a year. Predict plasma T4, plasma TSH, and gland size.\nA) T4 low, TSH high, gland enlarged\nB) T4 low, TSH low, gland shrunken\nC) T4 high, TSH low, gland enlarged\nD) T4 normal, TSH normal, gland enlarged\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) T4 low, TSH high, gland enlarged","why":"Without iodine the gland cannot make hormone, T4 falls, negative feedback is lost so TSH climbs, and sustained TSH stimulation makes the gland grow into a goiter. Low TSH with low T4 would point to a pituitary problem rather than a substrate shortage. High T4 is impossible without iodine. Normal values would not explain why the gland enlarged."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 45 year old woman feels cold all the time and has gained weight without eating more. Her free T4 is 0.4 ng/dL (normal 0.8 to 1.8) and her TSH is 32 mU/L (normal 0.4 to 4.0). In the lab her resting oxygen consumption is 0.17 L/min, well below the 0.25 L/min predicted for her size.\nWhat explains her low oxygen consumption, and what should happen to her TSH after she starts taking replacement T4?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Low thyroid hormone means fewer Na+/K+ ATPase pumps and less mitochondrial activity, so cells use less ATP and oxygen; TSH will fall as replacement restores negative feedback","why":"Thyroid hormone sets the basal rate of ATP use in nearly every cell by increasing the number of sodium pumps and mitochondrial enzymes. With too little hormone, cells run slower, consume less oxygen, and release less heat, which is why she is cold and why she gains weight on the same intake. The high TSH shows the pituitary is responding correctly to low hormone. Replacement T4 will be sensed by the pituitary and TSH will come back down toward normal."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a person with too much thyroid hormone loses weight despite eating more, feels hot in a cool room, and has a rapid resting heart rate. Also state what happens to TSH.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"thyroid hormone raises basal metabolic rate and oxygen consumption; more Na+/K+ ATPase pumps and mitochondrial enzymes in cells, so more ATP is used; the extra energy is released as heat, so the person feels hot and sweats; fuel is burned faster than it is eaten, so weight falls; thyroid hormone increases beta adrenergic receptors so the heart responds more to catecholamines; TSH is low because high thyroid hormone feeds back on the pituitary","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one thyroid follicle: follicular cells around a colloid center, with a capillary outside. Number the synthesis steps on the drawing: iodide pulled in at the blood side, iodide oxidized and attached to tyrosines on thyroglobulin at the colloid side, coupling to make T3 and T4, storage in colloid, endocytosis and release. Show where T4 becomes T3 in a target tissue. One line: say which step iodine deficiency blocks and what happens to follicle size."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a horizontal metabolic rate scale with normal in the middle, low at one end, high at the other. Hang at least four signs off each end, and pick things you can actually observe: heart rate, body temperature and heat tolerance, body weight on a steady diet, bowel activity, energy level. Draw a small arrow from each sign to the rate it reflects. One line: name one sign that is easy to misread, and say what else could cause it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace thyroid hormone synthesis, state the metabolic actions of T3 and T4, and match hyperthyroid and hypothyroid signs to the underlying rate of metabolism."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Thyroid hormone. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-adrenal-cortex","name":"Adrenal cortex","can":"Match the three cortical zones to aldosterone, cortisol, and the adrenal androgens and state the primary action of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma aldosterone rises sharply. What happens to plasma potassium?\nA) It rises, because potassium follows sodium into the blood\nB) It falls, because potassium is shifted into bone\nC) It is unchanged, because aldosterone only moves sodium\nD) It falls, because more potassium is secreted into the urine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls, because more potassium is secreted into the urine","why":"Aldosterone drives sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion at the same site, so more aldosterone means more potassium lost in the urine and a lower plasma level. Potassium moves opposite to sodium here, not with it. Aldosterone handles both ions, so no change is not the outcome. Bone is a calcium and phosphate store and does not buffer potassium in this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person loses blood volume during surgery. Trace the change to the zona glomerulosa.\nA) Renin rises, angiotensin II rises, and aldosterone secretion increases\nB) Renin falls, angiotensin II falls, and aldosterone secretion increases\nC) ACTH rises and aldosterone secretion increases with cortisol\nD) Plasma potassium falls and aldosterone secretion increases\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Renin rises, angiotensin II rises, and aldosterone secretion increases","why":"Reduced renal perfusion makes the kidney release renin, angiotensin II is generated, and it stimulates the glomerulosa to release aldosterone so sodium and therefore water are conserved. Falling renin cannot raise aldosterone, since it is the first step of the pathway. ACTH is not the main driver of the glomerulosa. Falling potassium reduces aldosterone rather than increasing it."},{"t":"mc","q":"The entire adrenal cortex is destroyed by autoimmune disease. Predict plasma sodium, plasma potassium, and blood pressure.\nA) Sodium high, potassium low, blood pressure high\nB) Sodium low, potassium high, blood pressure low\nC) Sodium low, potassium low, blood pressure high\nD) Sodium high, potassium high, blood pressure low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sodium low, potassium high, blood pressure low","why":"Losing aldosterone means sodium is wasted in the urine and potassium is retained, and the sodium loss takes water with it so volume and pressure fall. High sodium with low potassium is the pattern of aldosterone excess, the opposite problem. Low potassium cannot occur when the hormone that excretes it is gone. High sodium with high potassium does not fit a single failing pathway."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has had very high ACTH for months. Which change in the adrenal gland do you predict?\nA) The zona glomerulosa thickens and aldosterone output rises\nB) The adrenal medulla enlarges and epinephrine output rises\nC) The zona fasciculata thickens and cortisol output rises\nD) The whole gland shrinks from overuse\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The zona fasciculata thickens and cortisol output rises","why":"ACTH is both a secretory signal and a growth signal for the fasciculata, so chronic elevation makes that zone hypertrophy while cortisol output climbs. The glomerulosa is driven by angiotensin II and potassium, so it grows little under ACTH. The medulla answers to sympathetic nerves, not to ACTH. Glands enlarge under chronic trophic stimulation rather than shrinking."},{"t":"mc","q":"A seven year old girl has adrenal androgen secretion many times the normal level. What do you predict?\nA) Weight gain with a low blood glucose between meals\nB) Delayed puberty with short stature and absent pubic hair\nC) Salt wasting with a low blood pressure and a high plasma potassium\nD) Early appearance of pubic hair and other androgen driven features\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Early appearance of pubic hair and other androgen driven features","why":"Adrenal androgens are weak but at high concentration they produce the androgen dependent changes of puberty, so signs appear years early. Delayed puberty would follow from too little sex steroid, not too much. Salt wasting comes from aldosterone deficiency, a different zone. Weight gain with low glucose does not fit an androgen excess."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Aldosterone acts on the kidney to increase sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Aldosterone is the sodium saving hormone of the zona glomerulosa. Sodium retention brings water with it, so blood volume and pressure rise. The same cells that reclaim sodium secrete potassium, which is why too much aldosterone causes low plasma potassium and too little causes high plasma potassium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a wedge of adrenal gland in cross section from the capsule inward, with the three cortical zones at their real relative thicknesses and the medulla at the core. Label each zone with its hormone and, beside it, the one job that hormone is for: sodium retention, glucose mobilization, or androgen output. One line: one of these zones is not driven mainly by ACTH, so name it and say what does drive it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three targets on one page, a kidney tubule, a liver cell, and a whole body outline. Draw the hormone from the matching cortical zone arriving at each and show inside the target exactly what changes, which ion moves, which fuel gets made, which tissue responds. Then draw all three targets again with the entire cortex destroyed. One line: say which loss kills fastest and why it is the salt one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match the three cortical zones to aldosterone, cortisol, and the adrenal androgens and state the primary action of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Adrenal cortex. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-stress-response","name":"Cortisol and the stress response","can":"Describe the metabolic, immune, and cardiovascular actions of cortisol and explain the consequences of chronic elevation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person has eaten nothing for 36 hours. How does cortisol help keep plasma glucose from falling?\nA) By supplying amino acids from muscle for hepatic glucose synthesis\nB) By increasing glucose uptake into muscle and fat tissue\nC) By stimulating insulin release from the pancreatic beta cells\nD) By preventing the brain from taking up glucose from the blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) By supplying amino acids from muscle for hepatic glucose synthesis","why":"Cortisol breaks down muscle protein and the released amino acids become substrate for gluconeogenesis, so the liver can keep glucose in the blood well past the point where glycogen runs out. Increasing muscle uptake would lower plasma glucose, which is backwards. Insulin release falls during a fast rather than being stimulated by cortisol. Brain glucose uptake is insulin independent and is not blocked."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient takes high dose glucocorticoid medication daily for a year. Predict plasma ACTH and adrenal size.\nA) ACTH high and the adrenal cortex enlarged\nB) ACTH low and the adrenal cortex atrophied\nC) ACTH low and the adrenal cortex enlarged\nD) ACTH high and the adrenal cortex atrophied\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) ACTH low and the adrenal cortex atrophied","why":"The drug acts like cortisol at the feedback sites, so ACTH is suppressed, and without its trophic signal the fasciculata shrinks over months. High ACTH would require the feedback signal to be absent. An enlarged cortex needs ACTH stimulation, which is exactly what is missing. High ACTH with an atrophied cortex would describe a gland that cannot respond, not a suppressed axis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why do patients on long term high dose glucocorticoids get infections more easily?\nA) Cortisol reduces the number of red blood cells in the circulation\nB) Cortisol thins the blood and spreads the bacteria more quickly\nC) Cortisol suppresses lymphocyte function and the inflammatory response\nD) Cortisol lowers body temperature so that bacteria grow much better\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cortisol suppresses lymphocyte function and the inflammatory response","why":"Fewer and less active lymphocytes plus a blunted inflammatory response mean an invading organism meets weaker resistance and infections both start more easily and are noticed later. Red cell number is not meaningfully reduced by cortisol. Cortisol does not change blood viscosity in a way that spreads infection. Body temperature is not lowered enough to favor bacterial growth."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does chronic cortisol excess affect bone?\nA) Bone density rises because dietary protein is redirected to bone\nB) Bone density falls because parathyroid hormone secretion stops entirely\nC) Bone density is unchanged because bone is not a cortisol target tissue\nD) Bone density falls because formation is suppressed and absorption drops\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bone density falls because formation is suppressed and absorption drops","why":"Cortisol inhibits osteoblasts and reduces intestinal calcium absorption while increasing urinary calcium loss, so the balance tips toward bone loss over months. Bone gain would require an anabolic signal, and cortisol is catabolic. Bone is very much a cortisol target, which is why osteoporosis is a well known consequence. Parathyroid hormone does not stop, and if anything it rises in response to the calcium losses."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with chronic cortisol excess has thin arms and legs but increased fat over the trunk and face. Which cortisol action explains the thin limbs?\nA) Breakdown of skeletal muscle protein in the limbs\nB) Reduced blood flow to the muscles of the limbs\nC) Loss of subcutaneous fat only from the limbs\nD) Failure of growth hormone secretion from the pituitary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Breakdown of skeletal muscle protein in the limbs","why":"Cortisol is catabolic in skeletal muscle, and since the limbs are mostly muscle they visibly waste while fat is redistributed toward the trunk and face. Blood flow to the limbs is not meaningfully reduced by cortisol. Fat loss alone would not produce the weakness that goes with this pattern. Growth hormone secretion is somewhat blunted but muscle catabolism is the direct explanation."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 60 year old man has taken a high dose of prednisone, a synthetic glucocorticoid, every day for six months. His fasting glucose is 138 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99), his blood pressure is 152/94 mmHg (normal below 120/80), and a small skin wound has not healed in three weeks.\nWhich single mechanism best links the drug to all three findings?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Glucocorticoid stimulates gluconeogenesis and insulin resistance, is permissive for catecholamine vasoconstriction, and suppresses inflammation and collagen synthesis","why":"Each finding is a normal cortisol action that has gone on too long. Cortisol raises glucose by driving gluconeogenesis and making muscle and fat less responsive to insulin. It supports blood pressure by allowing catecholamines to constrict vessels and by some mineralocorticoid activity. And it suppresses the inflammatory and proliferative steps that wound healing depends on. Six months of a high dose produces the whole pattern at once."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the axis top to bottom: hypothalamus, anterior pituitary, adrenal cortex, cortisol, with the negative feedback arrows running back up. From cortisol draw three labeled arrows out: metabolic, immune, cardiovascular. Under each, draw the actual effect, gluconeogenesis and protein breakdown, suppressed inflammation, and vessels holding tone in response to catecholamines. One line: say what the feedback arrows are doing to the axis in someone taking a steroid drug for months."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline with the signs of months of high cortisol placed where they show: trunk, face, limb muscle, skin, bone. Beside each sign, draw a short arrow back to the cortisol action that caused it. Add a small glucose axis showing where plasma glucose sits. One line: pick the one sign a patient would notice first and say whether it is the one that matters most."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the metabolic, immune, and cardiovascular actions of cortisol and explain the consequences of chronic elevation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cortisol and the stress response. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-calcium-homeostasis","name":"Calcium homeostasis","can":"Trace the response to falling plasma calcium through parathyroid hormone, calcitriol, and the actions on bone, kidney, and intestine, and state the role of calcitonin.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a parathyroid tumor secreting hormone continuously. Predict plasma calcium, plasma phosphate, and the state of bone.\nA) Calcium high, phosphate low, bone resorbed\nB) Calcium high, phosphate high, bone resorbed\nC) Calcium low, phosphate high, bone dense\nD) Calcium high, phosphate low, bone dense\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Calcium high, phosphate low, bone resorbed","why":"Continuous parathyroid hormone drives bone resorption and renal calcium reabsorption so plasma calcium climbs, while the same hormone blocks phosphate reabsorption so phosphate is wasted in the urine and falls. High phosphate would require the renal phosphate action to be absent. Low calcium contradicts an excess of the hormone that raises it. Bone cannot become denser while resorption is being driven hard."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma calcium falls from 10 to 8 mg/dL. What happens to parathyroid hormone secretion?\nA) It decreases within minutes\nB) It increases within minutes\nC) It is unchanged until calcium falls below 6 mg/dL\nD) It increases only after calcitriol rises first\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It increases within minutes","why":"The calcium sensing receptor on chief cells detects small drops and releases the brake on secretion, so parathyroid hormone rises quickly and begins correcting the fall. A decrease would let the fall continue unopposed. The system responds to small deviations rather than waiting for a severe one. Calcitriol rises as a result of parathyroid hormone, not before it."},{"t":"mc","q":"The parathyroid glands are removed. Predict plasma calcium and the effect on nerve and muscle.\nA) Calcium rises and nerve and muscle become less excitable\nB) Calcium falls and nerve and muscle become less excitable\nC) Calcium rises and nerve and muscle become more excitable\nD) Calcium falls and nerve and muscle become more excitable\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Calcium falls and nerve and muscle become more excitable","why":"Without parathyroid hormone plasma calcium falls, and low extracellular calcium makes voltage gated sodium channels open more readily, so nerves fire spontaneously and muscles go into tetany. Reduced excitability with low calcium is the opposite of what is observed. Calcium cannot rise when the hormone that defends it is gone. High calcium is what makes tissue less excitable, and that is the other end of the range."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person gets almost no vitamin D and little sunlight for two years. Predict intestinal calcium absorption and the state of bone.\nA) Absorption falls and bone becomes poorly mineralized\nB) Absorption rises and the bone becomes denser\nC) Absorption falls and the bone becomes denser\nD) Absorption is unchanged and bone mineral is unaffected\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Absorption falls and bone becomes poorly mineralized","why":"Without calcitriol the intestine cannot take up dietary calcium efficiently, so parathyroid hormone rises and draws calcium out of bone to hold the plasma level, leaving bone soft and undermineralized. Absorption cannot rise when the hormone that drives it is missing. Bone loses mineral rather than gaining it under this pressure. No change would contradict the requirement for vitamin D in calcium uptake."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient in chronic kidney failure cannot make calcitriol. What happens to plasma calcium and parathyroid hormone?\nA) Calcium rises and parathyroid hormone falls\nB) Calcium falls and parathyroid hormone rises\nC) Calcium falls and parathyroid hormone falls\nD) Calcium rises and parathyroid hormone rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Calcium falls and parathyroid hormone rises","why":"Losing calcitriol cuts intestinal calcium uptake, plasma calcium drifts down, and the parathyroid glands respond by secreting more hormone in a sustained attempt to correct it. Rising calcium would require the intestinal arm to be intact. Parathyroid hormone cannot fall while calcium is low, because the calcium sensing receptor drives it up. Calcium and parathyroid hormone rising together would point to an autonomous gland, not to kidney failure."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace the whole body response to a fall in plasma calcium, naming what each of the three target organs does and what stops the response. Then say what calcitonin does and how important it is.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"parathyroid cells sense low calcium and release parathyroid hormone; bone: osteoclast resorption releases calcium and phosphate into plasma; kidney: more calcium reabsorbed in the distal nephron, less phosphate reabsorbed so phosphate is excreted; kidney: parathyroid hormone activates the enzyme that converts vitamin D to calcitriol; intestine: calcitriol increases calcium absorption from food; rising plasma calcium feeds back to shut off parathyroid hormone release; calcitonin lowers calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts but plays only a minor role in adults","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw plasma calcium as a bar with the normal band marked, about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL, and drop the level below it. Draw the parathyroid glands releasing PTH, then three arrows out to bone, kidney, and intestine. On the kidney arrow show calcium reabsorption up, phosphate reabsorption down, and the step that activates calcitriol. Route the intestinal arrow through calcitriol. One line: name the target PTH cannot reach without calcitriol, and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one calcium scale with two arrows off it, one for calcium falling and one for calcium rising, and put the hormone that answers each beside it, PTH from the parathyroids on one and calcitonin from the thyroid C cells on the other. Under each, draw its effect on the osteoclast. Then draw plasma calcium and nerve excitability after the parathyroids are removed in thyroid surgery. One line: say which of the two arms you could lose and barely notice."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the response to falling plasma calcium through parathyroid hormone, calcitriol, and the actions on bone, kidney, and intestine, and state the role of calcitonin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Calcium homeostasis. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-islet-hormones","name":"Pancreatic islet hormones","can":"Compare insulin and glucagon by stimulus and by effect on liver glycogen and on plasma glucose and identify the islet cell that secretes each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Thirty minutes after a large plate of pasta, what has happened to the insulin to glucagon ratio?\nA) It is unchanged, since both rise together\nB) It has fallen, favoring mobilization\nC) It has risen, favoring storage\nD) It has risen, favoring hepatic glucose release\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It has risen, favoring storage","why":"Absorbed glucose raises plasma glucose, which stimulates beta cells and inhibits alpha cells, so the ratio swings toward insulin and the body shifts into storing fuel. A falling ratio is the fasting pattern. The two hormones move in opposite directions rather than together. A high insulin ratio suppresses hepatic glucose release rather than promoting it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has not eaten since dinner and wakes at 7 am. Which hormone is holding plasma glucose up, and by what route?\nA) Insulin, through hepatic glucose uptake\nB) Glucagon, through glucose uptake into muscle\nC) Insulin, through GLUT4 recruitment in muscle\nD) Glucagon, through hepatic glycogen breakdown\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Glucagon, through hepatic glycogen breakdown","why":"Overnight the plasma glucose drifts down, alpha cells release glucagon, and the liver breaks down glycogen to keep the blood supplied for the brain. Insulin would lower glucose further, which is the wrong direction. GLUT4 recruitment moves glucose out of the blood into muscle. Glucagon does not act on skeletal muscle to take up glucose."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fasting person is given insulin. Predict plasma glucose and the alpha cell response.\nA) Glucose falls and glucagon secretion rises\nB) Glucose falls and glucagon secretion falls\nC) Glucose rises and glucagon secretion falls\nD) Glucose is unchanged and glucagon secretion rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Glucose falls and glucagon secretion rises","why":"Insulin drives glucose into muscle and fat and stops hepatic output, so plasma glucose drops, and the falling glucose is the very signal that turns on the alpha cells. Glucagon cannot fall while glucose is dropping, since that would remove the defense against hypoglycemia. Glucose cannot rise after insulin is given. An unchanged glucose would mean the insulin had no effect."},{"t":"mc","q":"Besides breaking down glycogen, what else does glucagon do in the liver during a long fast?\nA) It drives triglyceride storage and protein synthesis\nB) It drives gluconeogenesis and ketone body formation\nC) It drives glucose uptake and glycogen storage\nD) It drives conversion of glucose into amino acids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It drives gluconeogenesis and ketone body formation","why":"Once glycogen runs low, glucagon keeps glucose coming by building it from amino acids and glycerol, and it also sends fatty acids down the ketone pathway to supply an alternative fuel. Storage of triglyceride and protein synthesis are insulin driven processes. Glucose uptake and glycogen storage are the opposite of what glucagon does. Glucose is not converted into amino acids in any meaningful amount."},{"t":"mc","q":"An autoimmune process destroys the beta cells but leaves the alpha cells intact. Predict fasting plasma glucose.\nA) It falls, because the islet is damaged\nB) It stays normal, because alpha cells compensate\nC) It rises, because nothing opposes glucagon\nD) It falls, because glucagon secretion also stops\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises, because nothing opposes glucagon","why":"With no insulin the liver keeps releasing glucose and muscle and fat cannot take it up, so plasma glucose climbs even during a fast. Islet damage that removes insulin raises glucose rather than lowering it. Alpha cells raise glucose, so they cannot compensate for missing insulin. Glucagon secretion continues and in fact rises when insulin is absent."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A rise in plasma amino acids after a protein meal stimulates insulin release but suppresses glucagon release.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Amino acids stimulate both insulin and glucagon, and the glucagon prevents the insulin from driving plasma glucose too low after a meal that contains little carbohydrate.","why":"Glucose is the signal that separates the two hormones: it turns insulin on and glucagon off. Amino acids do not. They stimulate both islet cell types. That makes sense physiologically. Insulin is needed to move the amino acids into cells for protein synthesis, but a pure protein meal supplies little glucose, so without the glucagon the insulin would cause hypoglycemia."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one islet with alpha, beta and delta cells labeled and the hormone each secretes. Beside it draw one set of axes, plasma glucose on the horizontal and secretion rate on the vertical, and put insulin and glucagon on it as two curves running opposite ways. Mark roughly where a fasting glucose sits. One line: say what the crossing region of the two curves is doing for you between meals."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same hepatocyte twice. In the first, insulin has arrived: show glucose entering, glycogen granules being built, and the arrow direction for plasma glucose. In the second, glucagon has arrived: show glycogen being broken down, glucose leaving the cell, and the arrow for plasma glucose. Write the stimulus that released each hormone above its panel. One line: say which of the two the liver can keep doing after twenty four hours without food, and why the other one stops."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare insulin and glucagon by stimulus and by effect on liver glycogen and on plasma glucose and identify the islet cell that secretes each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pancreatic islet hormones. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-diabetes","name":"Diabetes mellitus","can":"Distinguish type 1 from type 2 diabetes by mechanism and explain how untreated hyperglycemia produces polyuria and ketoacidosis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why does a person with type 2 diabetes rarely develop ketoacidosis even with a plasma glucose over 300 mg/dL?\nA) Residual insulin is still enough to restrain lipolysis\nB) The liver in type 2 has lost the ability to make ketone bodies\nC) Glucagon secretion is absent in type 2 diabetes\nD) The kidney excretes ketone bodies faster in type 2\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Residual insulin is still enough to restrain lipolysis","why":"It takes very little insulin to hold hormone sensitive lipase in check, and type 2 patients usually still secrete some, so the massive fatty acid delivery that ketoacidosis requires never happens even when glucose is very high. The liver retains its ketogenic capacity in type 2. Glucagon is present and often elevated in type 2. Renal excretion of ketones is not accelerated enough to prevent an acidosis if one were being generated."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does the large urine volume of untreated hyperglycemia occur?\nA) Unreabsorbed glucose holds water in the tubule, an osmotic diuresis\nB) Antidiuretic hormone secretion is suppressed by the high glucose\nC) Glomerular filtration rate doubles when the plasma glucose is high\nD) Aldosterone secretion falls, so both sodium and water are lost\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Unreabsorbed glucose holds water in the tubule, an osmotic diuresis","why":"Glucose left in the tubular fluid is osmotically active, so water that would normally be reabsorbed stays with it and is excreted, which is what osmotic diuresis means. ADH is actually high here, because the patient is losing water and becoming concentrated. Filtration rate does not double. Aldosterone rises rather than falls as volume is lost."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a high plasma glucose together with a high plasma insulin. Which mechanism does this fit?\nA) Complete destruction of the beta cells\nB) Insulin resistance with beta cells compensating\nC) An insulin secreting tumor of the islets\nD) Excess glucagon with normal insulin sensitivity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Insulin resistance with beta cells compensating","why":"Plenty of insulin alongside a high glucose means the hormone is present but the target tissues are not responding, and the beta cells are working harder to make up for it, which is the type 2 pattern. Beta cell destruction would give high glucose with low insulin. An insulin secreting tumor would drive glucose down, not up. Excess glucagon with normal sensitivity would raise glucose but insulin would not be persistently elevated to this degree."},{"t":"mc","q":"Insulin is completely absent. What happens in adipose tissue?\nA) Triglyceride storage increases because glucose is plentiful\nB) Fat cells take up ketone bodies and store them\nC) Lipolysis runs unchecked and fatty acids flood the plasma\nD) Adipose tissue stops responding to any hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Lipolysis runs unchecked and fatty acids flood the plasma","why":"Insulin is the main brake on hormone sensitive lipase, so without it fat breakdown accelerates and the liver receives a large fatty acid load, which is the first step toward ketone production. Storage requires insulin, so it cannot increase when insulin is gone. Fat cells do not store ketone bodies. Adipose tissue still responds to catecholamines and cortisol, and both push toward breakdown here."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 19 year old with type 1 diabetes ran out of insulin three days ago. In the emergency room her plasma glucose is 480 mg/dL (normal 70 to 99), her arterial pH is 7.15 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), her bicarbonate is 8 mmol/L (normal 22 to 28), and her plasma ketones are markedly elevated. She is breathing deeply and rapidly.\nWhich mechanism links the absence of insulin to her low bicarbonate?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Without insulin, lipolysis is unrestrained, the liver converts the flood of fatty acids into ketoacids, and the ketoacids consume bicarbonate as they are buffered","why":"Insulin is the brake on hormone sensitive lipase. Remove it and adipose tissue releases fatty acids far faster than the liver can oxidize them completely, so the liver exports acetoacetate and beta hydroxybutyrate. These are strong acids. Each one releases a hydrogen ion that is buffered by bicarbonate, and the bicarbonate falls as the ketones rise. The deep breathing is the respiratory compensation, lowering carbon dioxide to limit the fall in pH."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a person with untreated type 1 diabetes produces large volumes of urine and becomes very thirsty. Start from the absence of insulin.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"without insulin, muscle and fat cannot take up glucose and the liver keeps releasing it, so plasma glucose rises; the filtered load of glucose exceeds the transport maximum of the proximal tubule; glucose left in the tubule holds water osmotically, so urine volume rises (osmotic diuresis); sodium and water are lost with the glucose; plasma volume falls and plasma osmolarity rises; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus trigger thirst and ADH release","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two panels, each with a beta cell, insulin in the blood, and a muscle or fat cell with its receptor. Panel one is type 1: show immune destruction of the beta cell and what that does to the insulin arrow. Panel two is type 2: show the beta cell working and the receptor failing to respond. Mark the plasma insulin level in each. One line: say which panel could still have a high plasma insulin measurement, and what that tells you."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two chains running down the page from one starting box, high plasma glucose. Chain one: glucose past the renal threshold near 180 mg per dL, glucose in the filtrate, water dragged with it, high urine volume, thirst. Chain two: cells short of glucose, unopposed fat breakdown, ketone acids in blood, plasma pH falling below 7.35, deep rapid breathing. One line: say which chain is far more typical of type 1 than type 2, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish type 1 from type 2 diabetes by mechanism and explain how untreated hyperglycemia produces polyuria and ketoacidosis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Diabetes mellitus. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-lab-glucose-tolerance","name":"Glucose tolerance testing","can":"Plot a glucose tolerance curve from measured or simulated data and distinguish a normal response from an impaired one.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A subject has a fasting glucose of 92 mg/dL, a 1 hour value of 155 mg/dL, and a 2 hour value of 120 mg/dL. How do you classify this curve?\nA) Normal  |  B) Impaired glucose tolerance\nC) Diabetic range  |  D) Uninterpretable without an insulin measurement\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Normal","why":"The fasting value is under 100 mg/dL and the two hour value is under 140 mg/dL, so both criteria are met and the peak at one hour is expected. Impaired tolerance would need a two hour value of 140 or more. The diabetic range starts at 200 mg/dL at two hours. Insulin measurements add information but are not needed to classify the glucose curve."},{"t":"mc","q":"One curve peaks higher than normal and returns to baseline much more slowly. What does the slow return say about insulin?\nA) Insulin is being cleared from plasma too slowly\nB) Insulin is being secreted too early\nC) Insulin is acting normally but absorption was slow\nD) Insulin action is inadequate for the load\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Insulin action is inadequate for the load","why":"The rate at which glucose comes back down reflects how effectively insulin moves it into tissue and shuts off hepatic output, so a slow return means the insulin response is too small, too late, or blunted by resistance. Early secretion would speed the return, not slow it. Slow absorption would flatten the peak instead of raising it. Slow insulin clearance would leave more insulin around and hasten the fall."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature of a glucose tolerance curve most directly reflects how well the tissues respond to insulin?\nA) How quickly glucose returns toward the fasting level\nB) The height of the fasting value at time zero\nC) The time at which the glucose drink was given\nD) The total volume of the glucose solution swallowed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) How quickly glucose returns toward the fasting level","why":"Clearance of the load is the functional test, so the downslope from peak back to baseline is where insulin sensitivity and secretion show themselves. The fasting value is informative about baseline hepatic output but not about the response to a challenge. The time the drink was given only sets the zero point on the axis. The volume of solution is standardized and does not vary with the subject."},{"t":"mc","q":"A subject ate breakfast an hour before arriving for the test. How does this affect the fasting sample?\nA) The baseline is falsely low, so the peak looks larger\nB) The baseline is falsely high, so the whole curve is shifted\nC) The baseline is unaffected, since the meal was small\nD) The baseline is unaffected, but the 2 hour value is falsely low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The baseline is falsely high, so the whole curve is shifted","why":"Absorbed carbohydrate from breakfast is still raising plasma glucose, so the sample labeled fasting is not a true baseline and every later value is being compared against the wrong starting point. Eating raises the baseline rather than lowering it. Even a small meal moves plasma glucose within an hour. The two hour value would tend to be falsely high, not low, since the subject started higher."},{"t":"mc","q":"A technician plots points at 0, 30, 60, 90, and 120 minutes. What units belong on the two axes?\nA) Minutes on the x axis and grams on the y axis\nB) mg/dL on the x axis and minutes on the y axis\nC) Minutes on the x axis and mg/dL on the y axis\nD) Hours on the x axis and mOsm/L on the y axis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Minutes on the x axis and mg/dL on the y axis","why":"The sampling times are in minutes and the measured quantity is a plasma glucose concentration in mg/dL, so those are the units on x and y. Reversing them puts the measurement on the time axis. Grams describe the dose swallowed, not the plasma concentration. Osmolarity in mOsm/L is a different measurement entirely."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time in hours from 0 to 3 on the horizontal, plasma glucose in mg per dL on the vertical. Mark the 75 gram drink at time zero. Plot three curves: a normal response, an impaired one, and a diabetic one. Mark the fasting line and the two hour line and write the cut values on both: fasting under 100 and two hour under 140 for normal, two hour at or over 200 for diabetes. One line: say which single point on the curve you would keep if you could only measure one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a stacked pair of graphs sharing one time axis: plasma glucose on top, plasma insulin underneath. Plot the normal pair first, insulin rising early and glucose coming back down by two hours. Then overlay in a second color the pair for someone with insulin resistance. One line: say what the insulin curve is doing in the second case while glucose is still high, and what that costs the beta cells over years."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to plot a glucose tolerance curve from measured or simulated data and distinguish a normal response from an impaired one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Glucose tolerance testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w9-lab-hormone-assay","name":"Hormone assay","can":"Run or simulate an immunoassay for a hormone and use the standard curve to determine an unknown concentration.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Standards read 0.40 absorbance at 10 ng/mL and 0.80 at 20 ng/mL. An unknown reads 0.60. What is its approximate concentration?\nA) About 5 ng/mL  |  B) About 15 ng/mL\nC) About 12 ng/mL  |  D) About 30 ng/mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 15 ng/mL","why":"An absorbance of 0.60 lies exactly halfway between the two standards, so on a linear portion of the curve the concentration is halfway between 10 and 20 ng/mL. Five is below the lower standard and would give a much smaller reading. Twelve does not sit at the midpoint of the bracket. Thirty is above the higher standard and would give a reading above 0.80."},{"t":"mc","q":"An unknown reads an absorbance higher than the highest standard. What should be done?\nA) Report it as equal to the highest standard\nB) Extend the straight line beyond the last point and read off a value\nC) Dilute the sample and run it again\nD) Report it as unmeasurable and discard the sample\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Dilute the sample and run it again","why":"Above the top standard the curve is no longer defined, so the sample must be diluted into the working range and the result multiplied back by the dilution factor. Reporting it as equal to the top standard understates the true value. Extending the line assumes linearity where the assay is usually flattening out. Discarding the sample throws away a result that a simple dilution can recover."},{"t":"mc","q":"A technician skips the wash steps before adding substrate. What happens to the readings?\nA) All wells read too high, because unbound label was not removed\nB) All wells read too low, because the antibody was diluted\nC) Only the blank reads too high, since it has no hormone\nD) Only the highest standard is affected, because it saturates\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) All wells read too high, because unbound label was not removed","why":"Washing removes labeled antibody that never bound the target, so skipping it leaves that label in every well and every reading is inflated by background. The antibody is not diluted by omitting a wash, so a low reading is not expected. The problem affects the whole plate rather than the blank alone. Saturation is a separate issue and does not confine the error to one well."},{"t":"mc","q":"A plasma sample is diluted 1 in 10, and the diluted sample reads 5 ng/mL from the standard curve. What was the concentration in the original plasma?\nA) 0.5 ng/mL  |  B) 50 ng/mL\nC) 5 ng/mL  |  D) 500 ng/mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 50 ng/mL","why":"A one in ten dilution reduces the concentration tenfold, so the original must be ten times the measured value, or 50 ng/mL. Half a nanogram per milliliter would come from multiplying in the wrong direction twice. Five is the diluted reading and ignores the dilution entirely. Five hundred applies a hundredfold correction that was not performed."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why are standards run on the same plate as the unknowns rather than on a plate from last week?\nA) Because the hormone changes structure over a week\nB) Because the plate reader can only store one curve\nC) Because reagent activity and conditions vary between runs\nD) Because antibodies bind more tightly on a fresh plate every time\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Because reagent activity and conditions vary between runs","why":"Enzyme activity, incubation temperature, and development time all differ from run to run, so a curve built under this run's conditions is the only valid reference for this run's unknowns. The hormone in a properly stored standard does not change structure that fast. Instrument storage capacity is not the reason. Binding affinity is a property of the antibody rather than of plate freshness."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the standard curve you would build, known hormone concentration on the x axis and measured signal on the y, with your standards as points and a line through them. Then put an unknown sample's signal on the y axis and draw the construction lines that read its concentration off the x. Add a second unknown that lands above your highest standard. One line: say what you do with that second sample and why you cannot simply extend the line."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the assay well in cross section at four stages: antibody stuck to the well, hormone captured, detection antibody added, color developed. Draw what a well holding a lot of hormone looks like beside an empty one. Then draw a well where a similar but wrong molecule got captured instead. One line: that last well still gives you a number, so say what the number is actually measuring and what it is not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone assay. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Reproductive Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w15-hpg-axis","name":"Hypothalamic pituitary gonadal axis","can":"Trace the gonadotropin releasing hormone axis to the gonads and place the feedback loops for both sexes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The same gonadotropin releasing hormone agonist can be used either to start puberty or to shut the axis down. What determines which effect occurs?\nA) Whether it is given before or after breakfast\nB) Whether it is delivered in pulses or continuously\nC) Whether the dose is high or low\nD) Whether the patient is male or female\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Whether it is delivered in pulses or continuously","why":"The gonadotrope reads the pattern of the signal, not just its presence: pulsatile delivery keeps receptors responsive and stimulates the axis, while continuous delivery downregulates them and suppresses it. Dose matters at the margins but cannot convert continuous exposure into a stimulating signal. The pattern rule holds in both sexes, since the same receptor is on the same cell type. Timing relative to meals has no bearing on GnRH receptor regulation."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient is given a gonadotropin releasing hormone agonist as a continuous infusion for several weeks. What happens to luteinizing hormone and follicle stimulating hormone?\nA) Both remain at their baseline levels  |  B) Both fall well below baseline\nC) Both rise and stay elevated  |  D) LH rises while FSH falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both fall well below baseline","why":"After a brief initial flare, continuous exposure downregulates and desensitizes the GnRH receptor on the gonadotrope, so LH and FSH fall and the gonads go quiet. Sustained elevation would be the answer if the receptor did not desensitize, but pulsatility is exactly what the gonadotrope requires. A split response with LH up and FSH down would need something acting selectively, such as inhibin. Unchanged levels would mean the receptor ignored a continuous agonist, which is not what happens."},{"t":"mc","q":"Both gonads are surgically removed from an adult. Predict what happens to luteinizing hormone and follicle stimulating hormone.\nA) Both fall, because there is no target organ\nB) Both stay the same, because the pituitary is intact\nC) LH falls while FSH rises\nD) Both rise, because negative feedback is lost\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Both rise, because negative feedback is lost","why":"Gonadal steroids and inhibin are the brake on the axis, so removing the gonads releases that brake and gonadotropins climb to high levels. Falling gonadotropins would require damage above the gonad, not below it, since the pituitary does not need a target to secrete. Unchanged levels would mean the axis has no feedback, which contradicts the whole design. A split response would require something suppressing LH selectively, and nothing here does that."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor secretes large amounts of inhibin into the circulation. Which pituitary hormone falls the most?\nA) Follicle stimulating hormone  |  B) Luteinizing hormone\nC) Thyroid stimulating hormone  |  D) Adrenocorticotropic hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Follicle stimulating hormone","why":"Inhibin acts on the gonadotrope to suppress FSH specifically, so FSH drops while LH is only modestly affected. LH answers mainly to GnRH pulse frequency and to steroid feedback, so it does not track inhibin closely. TSH and ACTH belong to separate axes with their own releasing hormones and their own feedback signals, and inhibin does not reach into them. This selective suppression is the reason FSH is the better clinical marker of gamete producing tissue."},{"t":"mc","q":"Estradiol from a dominant follicle stays above its threshold concentration for about two days. Predict the effect on luteinizing hormone.\nA) LH stays flat until progesterone rises\nB) LH oscillates around its previous mean\nC) LH rises steeply into a surge\nD) LH falls further, deepening the suppression\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) LH rises steeply into a surge","why":"Sustained high estradiol flips the hypothalamic and pituitary response from negative to positive feedback, and the result is the LH surge that triggers ovulation about a day later. Continued suppression is what happens at low or briefly elevated estradiol, which is the pattern for most of the follicular phase. Progesterone rises after ovulation as a consequence of the surge, so waiting for it reverses the order of events. Simple oscillation would mean estradiol carried no information about follicle maturity."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a woman, the high estrogen level sustained in the late follicular phase switches estrogen feedback from negative to positive and triggers the LH surge.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"This switch is the key event of the ovarian cycle. The dominant follicle makes more and more estrogen. Once the level is high enough for long enough, the hypothalamus and pituitary respond by increasing rather than decreasing LH. The surge causes ovulation. After ovulation, progesterone from the corpus luteum restores negative feedback."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the axis from GnRH pulses down through LH and FSH, then split it into two branches at the gonad, male and female. On the male branch, run LH to the Leydig cell and FSH to the Sertoli cell and draw what each produces. On the female branch, run LH to the theca cell and FSH to the granulosa cell and do the same. Draw the negative feedback arrows on both. One line: name the hormone that feeds back on FSH alone in both sexes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the female axis twice, once in the early follicular phase where moderate estradiol feeds back negatively, and once just before ovulation where high estradiol feeds back positively. Use the same diagram both times and change only the sign on the feedback arrow. Show what LH is doing in each. One line: the same hormone on the same axis flipped the sign, so say what was different about the estradiol signal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the gonadotropin releasing hormone axis to the gonads and place the feedback loops for both sexes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hypothalamic pituitary gonadal axis. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-male-reproductive","name":"Male reproductive physiology","can":"Explain the control of spermatogenesis by follicle stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and testosterone and state the roles of the Sertoli and Leydig cells.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A man has a normal testosterone, a normal luteinizing hormone, a high follicle stimulating hormone, and a very low sperm count. Which compartment is failing?\nA) The Sertoli cells and seminiferous tubules  |  B) The Leydig cells\nC) The anterior pituitary gonadotropes  |  D) The hypothalamic GnRH neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The Sertoli cells and seminiferous tubules","why":"Normal testosterone with normal LH says the Leydig compartment and its feedback loop are intact, while a high FSH with low sperm points to loss of inhibin from failing Sertoli cells and tubules. Leydig failure would show low testosterone and high LH instead. Pituitary failure would lower both gonadotropins rather than raise FSH. A hypothalamic lesion would also lower gonadotropins, so a high FSH argues against it. This is the pattern that shows why inhibin is worth learning as a separate signal."},{"t":"mc","q":"A man whose pituitary gonadotropes were destroyed is given testosterone replacement. Predict his libido and his sperm count.\nA) Libido stays low but the sperm count recovers\nB) Both stay low because testosterone cannot act without LH\nC) Libido is restored but the sperm count stays very low\nD) Both libido and sperm count are restored to normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Libido is restored but the sperm count stays very low","why":"Systemic testosterone is enough for libido, muscle, and secondary sexual characteristics, but it cannot recreate the intratesticular concentration that only LH driven Leydig cells produce, and it does nothing for the missing FSH signal to Sertoli cells. Full restoration would require replacing the gonadotropins themselves, which is why fertility treatment in this situation uses hCG and FSH rather than testosterone. Recovery of sperm without libido reverses which effect systemic testosterone can deliver. Testosterone acts perfectly well at its receptors without LH, so total failure is not the outcome."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sertoli cell function is selectively impaired so that these cells no longer respond to follicle stimulating hormone. Predict what happens to circulating inhibin and follicle stimulating hormone.\nA) Both inhibin and FSH fall  |  B) Both inhibin and FSH rise\nC) Inhibin falls and FSH rises  |  D) Inhibin rises and FSH falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Inhibin falls and FSH rises","why":"Inhibin is a Sertoli product, so losing Sertoli responsiveness lowers inhibin, and with the brake off FSH climbs. A rise in inhibin with a fall in FSH describes the healthy tubule doing its job, the opposite of this lesion. Both falling would require damage to the pituitary as well as the tubule. Both rising is impossible here, because inhibin cannot rise from a cell that is not responding."},{"t":"mc","q":"A man has failure of the Leydig cells but intact pituitary function. Predict his luteinizing hormone and testosterone levels.\nA) High LH with high testosterone  |  B) Low LH with high testosterone\nC) High LH with low testosterone  |  D) Low LH with low testosterone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) High LH with low testosterone","why":"The Leydig cell is the source of testosterone, so testosterone falls, and the lost negative feedback lets LH climb. Low LH with low testosterone would point to a hypothalamic or pituitary problem rather than a gonadal one, which is the key distinction. High LH with high testosterone would mean the feedback loop had stopped working at the pituitary end. Low LH with high testosterone is the pattern of exogenous androgen use, where the drug suppresses the axis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin is given to an adult man. Predict the effect on his testosterone.\nA) Testosterone rises, because hCG acts on the LH receptor\nB) Testosterone falls, because hCG suppresses the pituitary\nC) Testosterone is unchanged, because hCG acts only on ovarian tissue\nD) Testosterone falls, because hCG competes with LH at the receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Testosterone rises, because hCG acts on the LH receptor","why":"Human chorionic gonadotropin binds the same receptor as LH and has a longer half life, so it drives Leydig cells to make more testosterone. It does not act as a pituitary suppressant, and the resulting testosterone rise is what eventually lowers LH. The LH receptor is present on Leydig cells as well as on ovarian cells, so the effect is not sex specific. hCG is an agonist rather than a competitive blocker, so it activates the receptor rather than silencing it."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Injecting a man with large doses of testosterone increases his sperm production, because testosterone is required for spermatogenesis.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Outside testosterone lowers sperm production. It suppresses GnRH, LH, and FSH by negative feedback, so the Leydig cells stop making the very high local testosterone the tubules need and the Sertoli cells lose FSH support.","why":"Spermatogenesis needs testosterone inside the tubules at a concentration far above blood levels, and only LH driven Leydig cells can supply that. Injected testosterone raises the blood level, which the hypothalamus and pituitary read as too much, and the axis shuts down. Blood testosterone is fine, testicular testosterone is not, and sperm count falls."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cross section through one seminiferous tubule with a Leydig cell outside it in the interstitium. Bring FSH to the Sertoli cell and LH to the Leydig cell, and draw what each cell makes. Draw the tight junctions between Sertoli cells and the germ cells maturing from the outer wall inward toward the lumen. One line: name what those tight junctions keep apart and say why that separation matters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the axis from GnRH down to the testis, then draw both negative feedback arrows coming back, one carrying testosterone and one carrying inhibin, and land each on the tier it actually inhibits. Then draw the same axis in a man taking testosterone from outside. Show what happened to LH, to FSH, to the Leydig cells, and to sperm production. One line: he added the male hormone and his fertility fell, so name the step that explains it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain the control of spermatogenesis by follicle stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and testosterone and state the roles of the Sertoli and Leydig cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Male reproductive physiology. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-ovarian-cycle","name":"Ovarian cycle","can":"Order the follicular, ovulatory, and luteal phases and explain how the luteinizing hormone surge is triggered by positive feedback.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A woman has a consistent 35 day cycle. Which phase is longer than in a 28 day cycle?\nA) The luteal phase  |  B) Both phases are proportionally longer\nC) The ovulatory phase  |  D) The follicular phase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The follicular phase","why":"The luteal phase is fixed near 14 days by the corpus luteum's lifespan, so all the extra time in a long cycle sits in the follicular phase while a dominant follicle takes longer to mature. Extending the luteal phase would require rescuing the corpus luteum, which happens only in pregnancy. Proportional stretching of both phases contradicts the fixed luteal length that makes cycle length prediction possible. Ovulation is an event of hours and does not stretch to fill extra days."},{"t":"mc","q":"A woman has a regular 35 day cycle. On approximately which day does she ovulate?\nA) Day 28  |  B) Day 21\nC) Day 14  |  D) Day 17\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Day 21","why":"Counting back the fixed 14 day luteal phase from day 35 puts ovulation near day 21. Day 14 assumes a 28 day cycle and is the most common error, since it counts forward from day 1 rather than backward from the next menses. Day 17 would be the midpoint of the cycle, but ovulation is not anchored to the midpoint. Day 28 would leave only seven days for the corpus luteum, which is shorter than its natural lifespan."},{"t":"mc","q":"During the early follicular phase, estradiol is low and rising slowly. What effect does it have on luteinizing hormone?\nA) It raises LH slowly in proportion to its own rise\nB) It suppresses LH through negative feedback\nC) It drives an LH surge through positive feedback\nD) It has no effect until progesterone appears\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It suppresses LH through negative feedback","why":"At low concentrations and over short exposures, estradiol exerts classic negative feedback and keeps LH modest, which is the state for most of the follicular phase. Positive feedback appears only when estradiol is both high and sustained for a day or two, which is a late follicular condition. Progesterone is not required for estradiol to have an effect, and it stays low before ovulation. A smooth proportional rise would eliminate the sharp surge that ovulation depends on."},{"t":"mc","q":"Progesterone rises after ovulation. What does it do to gonadotropin releasing hormone pulse frequency?\nA) It speeds up the pulses\nB) It stops the pulses entirely\nC) It leaves the frequency unchanged but raises pulse height\nD) It slows the pulses\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It slows the pulses","why":"Progesterone slows the hypothalamic pulse generator, which lowers LH and favors FSH on a per pulse basis while total gonadotropin output stays down. Speeding the pulses is the late follicular pattern that helps build toward the surge, not the luteal pattern. Pulses do not stop completely, since some gonadotropin support is needed to maintain the corpus luteum. Raising pulse amplitude without changing frequency is not the described effect of luteal progesterone."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman takes a daily pill containing a steady dose of estrogen and a progestin (a synthetic progesterone) starting on day 1 of her cycle. On day 13 her LH is 4 IU/L (a normal midcycle surge reaches 40 to 100). Ultrasound shows only small follicles and no dominant follicle. Her own ovarian estradiol output is low.\nExplain why no LH surge occurs.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The steady steroid dose holds negative feedback on GnRH, LH, and FSH. Without gonadotropin support no follicle grows large enough to produce the sustained estrogen rise that would switch feedback to positive and trigger the surge","why":"The surge needs two things: a follicle that makes a lot of estrogen for a couple of days, and an axis free to respond to it with positive feedback. The pill takes away both. Steady estrogen and progestin act on the hypothalamus and pituitary as constant negative feedback, so FSH and LH stay low, follicles are not recruited to dominance, and the ovary's own estrogen never rises. Progesterone in particular holds the axis in the negative feedback state, as it does in the luteal phase."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain how the LH surge is triggered, why it is called positive feedback, and what stops the surge afterward.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"during most of the follicular phase, moderate estrogen exerts negative feedback on GnRH and LH; the dominant follicle produces steadily rising estrogen; when estrogen stays above a threshold for about two days, feedback switches from negative to positive; high estrogen increases GnRH release and pituitary sensitivity, so LH surges; it is positive feedback because the product (estrogen) increases the signal that stimulates more of the response, rather than shutting it down; the LH surge causes ovulation about 24 to 36 hours later; after ovulation, progesterone from the corpus luteum restores negative feedback and LH falls","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a 28 day timeline and put the follicle on it as a run of drawings: recruited follicle, dominant follicle, ovulation at about day 14, corpus luteum, corpus albicans. Under each drawing write the hormone that structure is making at that moment. One line: the follicular phase varies in length between women and the luteal phase does not, so name the structure whose lifespan is fixed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the positive feedback loop that fires the LH surge as a closed circle: the dominant follicle making estradiol, estradiol running high and staying high for about two days, the hypothalamus and pituitary flipping from inhibited to driven, LH climbing steeply, and that LH driving the follicle harder still. Mark on the circle where ovulation breaks it, about 36 hours after the surge begins. One line: positive feedback cannot stop itself, so name what ends this loop."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the follicular, ovulatory, and luteal phases and explain how the luteinizing hormone surge is triggered by positive feedback."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ovarian cycle. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-uterine-cycle","name":"Uterine cycle","can":"Correlate the menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases of the uterine cycle with the estrogen and progesterone levels that drive them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A progesterone receptor blocker is given during the mid secretory phase. Predict the effect on the endometrium.\nA) The lining continues to thicken through proliferation\nB) The lining becomes more secretory than usual\nC) The lining is unaffected because progesterone acts only on the ovary\nD) The lining loses its support and begins to break down\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The lining loses its support and begins to break down","why":"Progesterone is what holds the secretory endometrium together, so blocking its receptor mimics progesterone withdrawal and the lining sheds. Continued proliferation would need unopposed estrogen action, and the endometrium in this phase is already past that stage. Increased secretion would require more progesterone signaling, not less. Progesterone acts strongly on the endometrium, so calling its effect ovary only misplaces the target tissue."},{"t":"mc","q":"A woman receives estrogen for many months with no progesterone. Predict the state of her endometrium.\nA) It thickens continuously without becoming secretory\nB) It becomes thin and atrophic\nC) It becomes fully secretory and then sheds on schedule\nD) It stays exactly at its baseline thickness\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It thickens continuously without becoming secretory","why":"Unopposed estrogen drives proliferation with nothing to stop it or convert it, so the lining keeps thickening and never organizes into a secretory pattern. Thinning is what a progestin alone would eventually produce, which is the opposite exposure. A secretory change requires progesterone, which by definition is absent here. Baseline thickness would mean estrogen had no proliferative effect, contradicting its central role in the proliferative phase."},{"t":"mc","q":"A woman has cycles in which no follicle ever ovulates. Which uterine phase never occurs?\nA) The menstrual phase  |  B) All three phases occur normally\nC) The secretory phase  |  D) The proliferative phase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The secretory phase","why":"No ovulation means no corpus luteum, no progesterone, and therefore no secretory transformation, even though estrogen from developing follicles still drives proliferation. The proliferative phase does occur, because follicles produce estradiol whether or not they rupture. Bleeding still occurs, though it tends to be irregular breakthrough bleeding from an overgrown lining rather than true progesterone withdrawal menses. Saying all three occur ignores that the secretory phase has a strict hormonal prerequisite."},{"t":"mc","q":"What effect does luteal phase progesterone have on the myometrium?\nA) It has no effect on smooth muscle\nB) It increases oxytocin receptor density\nC) It quiets contractile activity\nD) It increases contractile activity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It quiets contractile activity","why":"Progesterone keeps the uterine muscle quiet, which protects a possible implantation and later maintains pregnancy. Increased contractility is the estrogen leaning state seen at term when the progesterone to estrogen balance shifts. Progesterone acts strongly on smooth muscle throughout the body, so no effect is wrong. Raising oxytocin receptor density is an estrogen effect near term, and progesterone tends to hold that number down."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cervical mucus is thin, watery, and stretchy at midcycle. Which hormonal state produces this?\nA) High estrogen with low progesterone\nB) High progesterone with low estrogen\nC) High progesterone with high estrogen\nD) Low estrogen with low progesterone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) High estrogen with low progesterone","why":"Peak estradiol just before ovulation thins cervical mucus so sperm can pass through it, which is the point of the change. High progesterone does the opposite, making mucus thick and hostile, which is a phase later and also part of how progestin contraceptives work. Both hormones high describes the mid luteal phase, where the progesterone effect dominates. Both low describes the menstrual phase, where mucus is scant rather than stretchy."},{"t":"bd","q":"Describe what happens to the endometrium across one 28 day cycle, name the hormone driving each phase, and explain what causes menstruation.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"menstrual phase: the functional layer of the endometrium is shed; proliferative phase: estrogen from growing follicles thickens the endometrium; ovulation marks the switch from proliferative to secretory; secretory phase: progesterone from the corpus luteum makes the glands secrete and prepares the lining for implantation; if no pregnancy, the corpus luteum degenerates after about 14 days and estrogen and progesterone fall; steroid withdrawal constricts the spiral arteries, the lining dies and sheds, which is menstruation","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the uterine lining in cross section at four points across 28 days: mid menstrual, mid proliferative, just after ovulation, and mid secretory. Draw the real thickness, the glands straight or coiled, and the spiral arteries. Underneath, run estrogen and progesterone curves along the same time axis so each drawing sits under its hormone level. One line: name which hormone built the lining and which one finished it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the endometrium at the end of the secretory phase, then draw the next three days as the corpus luteum dies. Show progesterone falling, the spiral arteries clamping down, and mark the layer that is shed and the layer that stays behind. Label both layers. One line: this lining is lost because a hormone went away, not because a hormone arrived, so say what that means for someone whose corpus luteum gets rescued."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to correlate the menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases of the uterine cycle with the estrogen and progesterone levels that drive them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Uterine cycle. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-pregnancy-hormones","name":"Pregnancy and placental hormones","can":"Explain how human chorionic gonadotropin rescues the corpus luteum and state the roles of placental estrogen, progesterone, and relaxin.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The corpus luteum is removed at week 12 of pregnancy. Predict the outcome.\nA) The pregnancy continues because the placenta now makes progesterone\nB) The pregnancy is lost because progesterone falls sharply\nC) The pregnancy continues only if hCG is given\nD) The pregnancy continues but relaxin excess causes early labor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pregnancy continues because the placenta now makes progesterone","why":"By week 12 the luteal placental shift is complete, so the placenta supplies enough progesterone and the corpus luteum is no longer required. Loss of the pregnancy is the week 6 answer, and the difference between the two timings is the point of this pair. Giving hCG would not help, since there is no corpus luteum left for it to act on. Removing the corpus luteum reduces rather than increases relaxin, so relaxin excess does not follow."},{"t":"mc","q":"Progesterone is elevated throughout pregnancy. Predict its effect on maternal ventilation and arterial PCO2.\nA) Ventilation is unchanged and PCO2 falls\nB) Ventilation increases and PCO2 falls\nC) Ventilation decreases and PCO2 rises\nD) Ventilation increases and PCO2 rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Ventilation increases and PCO2 falls","why":"Progesterone stimulates the respiratory center, so pregnant women hyperventilate mildly and arterial PCO2 settles around 30 mmHg, which also helps carbon dioxide move from fetus to mother. Reduced ventilation with a higher PCO2 would work against fetal gas exchange and is the opposite of the measured pattern. Ventilation up with PCO2 up is internally inconsistent, since more ventilation blows off carbon dioxide. Ventilation must change for PCO2 to fall, so unchanged ventilation with a low PCO2 does not follow."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which effect of progesterone explains the heartburn and constipation common in pregnancy?\nA) Increased gastric acid secretion by the stomach\nB) Increased intestinal motility\nC) Reduced blood flow to the wall of the gut\nD) Relaxation of gastrointestinal smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Relaxation of gastrointestinal smooth muscle","why":"Progesterone relaxes smooth muscle throughout the body, so the lower esophageal sphincter loosens and intestinal transit slows. Acid secretion does not rise in pregnancy, so the reflux comes from a weak sphincter rather than more acid. Increased motility would speed transit and relieve constipation rather than cause it. Gut blood flow is not reduced in pregnancy, and blood volume actually rises substantially."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tissue secretes human chorionic gonadotropin in early pregnancy?\nA) The anterior pituitary of the mother\nB) The endometrial glands of the uterine lining\nC) The syncytiotrophoblast of the embryo\nD) The corpus luteum of the ovary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The syncytiotrophoblast of the embryo","why":"The outer trophoblast layer of the implanting embryo makes hCG, which is why the hormone appears only after implantation and why it signals that an embryo is present. The corpus luteum is the target of hCG rather than its source. The maternal anterior pituitary makes LH and FSH, and its output actually falls during pregnancy. Endometrial glands secrete nutrient fluid but not gonadotropins."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which receptor does human chorionic gonadotropin act on?\nA) The luteinizing hormone receptor\nB) The follicle stimulating hormone receptor\nC) The progesterone receptor\nD) The oxytocin receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The luteinizing hormone receptor","why":"Human chorionic gonadotropin is structurally close enough to LH to bind and activate the LH receptor, which is exactly how it keeps the corpus luteum alive. The FSH receptor is a different glycoprotein hormone receptor and is not the target. Progesterone receptors are intracellular steroid receptors, and hCG is a peptide that cannot use them. The oxytocin receptor belongs to a separate family on uterine and breast tissue."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cycle ending the usual way with the corpus luteum dying, then draw a second version where a blastocyst has implanted. In the second, draw the trophoblast making hCG, the hCG reaching the corpus luteum, and the corpus luteum surviving. Run progesterone along the bottom of both. One line: hCG works by binding a receptor that was already sitting on the corpus luteum for a different hormone, so name that hormone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis from conception to birth and plot hCG, progesterone, and estrogen across the whole of it. Mark the handover in the first trimester where the placenta takes over progesterone production from the corpus luteum. Beside the axis, draw what progesterone, estrogen, and relaxin are each doing to the uterus, the cervix, and the pelvis. One line: say what would happen if that handover failed and the corpus luteum died on its usual schedule."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how human chorionic gonadotropin rescues the corpus luteum and state the roles of placental estrogen, progesterone, and relaxin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pregnancy and placental hormones. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-parturition-lactation","name":"Parturition and lactation","can":"Explain labor as a positive feedback loop driven by oxytocin and compare the hormonal control of milk production with milk ejection.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What ends the positive feedback loop of labor?\nA) Desensitization of myometrial oxytocin receptors\nB) Delivery of the fetus, which removes the cervical stretch\nC) Exhaustion of oxytocin stores in the posterior pituitary\nD) A rise in progesterone that quiets the myometrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Delivery of the fetus, which removes the cervical stretch","why":"Positive feedback loops run until something removes the stimulus, and here that is birth: once the fetus is out the cervix is no longer stretched, the afferent signal stops, and oxytocin falls. Store depletion is not how the loop terminates, and oxytocin is still needed after delivery for uterine involution. Progesterone falls at this stage rather than rising. Receptor desensitization is not the mechanism, and oxytocin remains effective postpartum for controlling bleeding."},{"t":"mc","q":"A dopamine agonist is given to a woman shortly after delivery. Predict the effect on milk production.\nA) Milk production rises, because dopamine increases oxytocin release\nB) Milk production falls, because prolactin is suppressed\nC) Milk production rises, because dopamine stimulates the alveolar cells\nD) Milk production is unchanged, but let down is blocked\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Milk production falls, because prolactin is suppressed","why":"Dopamine is the natural inhibitor of prolactin, so an agonist deepens that inhibition, prolactin falls, and milk synthesis stops. A rise would require dopamine to be stimulatory, which reverses its actual role at the lactotrope. Let down depends on oxytocin, which dopamine does not control, so blaming this drug for a blocked ejection reflex misassigns the target. Dopamine does not increase oxytocin release."},{"t":"mc","q":"A breastfeeding woman stops nursing entirely for several days. Predict prolactin and milk volume.\nA) Both prolactin and milk volume rise\nB) Prolactin falls but milk volume is maintained\nC) Prolactin rises but milk volume falls\nD) Both prolactin and milk volume fall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Both prolactin and milk volume fall","why":"Suckling is the stimulus that keeps prolactin elevated, so removing it lets dopamine inhibition dominate again, prolactin falls, and production winds down. A rise in either would require continued or increased stimulation. Milk volume tracks prolactin closely, so it cannot be maintained once prolactin falls. Prolactin cannot rise without the suckling stimulus that drives it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is oxytocin synthesized and from where is it released?\nA) Made and released entirely by the anterior pituitary\nB) Made in the uterine wall, released into uterine veins\nC) Made in the placenta, released into the maternal circulation\nD) Made in the hypothalamus, released by the posterior pituitary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Made in the hypothalamus, released by the posterior pituitary","why":"Hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and send it down their axons to nerve terminals in the posterior pituitary, where it enters the blood. The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones from glandular cells and does not store oxytocin. The uterus is a target of oxytocin, carrying receptors for it, rather than a source. The placenta makes many hormones but is not the origin of the oxytocin that drives labor."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which hormone drives milk synthesis in the mammary gland?\nA) Relaxin  |  B) Prolactin\nC) Oxytocin  |  D) Estrogen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Prolactin","why":"Prolactin from the anterior pituitary stimulates the alveolar cells to make milk, which is the production half of lactation. Oxytocin handles the other half, ejecting milk that has already been made. Estrogen helps the ducts develop during pregnancy but blocks actual milk production until it falls at delivery. Relaxin loosens pelvic connective tissue and has no lactogenic role."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman in labor has weak contractions every 3 minutes and her cervix is dilated 4 cm (full dilation is 10 cm). Her doctor starts an intravenous oxytocin infusion. Within 20 minutes the contractions are stronger and come every 2 minutes, and over the next hour the cervix dilates to 6 cm.\nExplain how the infusion fits the feedback loop of labor, and predict what happens to her own oxytocin release as the cervix stretches further.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Labor is a positive feedback loop. Cervical stretch sends sensory signals to the hypothalamus, oxytocin is released, contractions push the fetus against the cervix, and the stretch increases again. The infusion adds to the loop, and her own oxytocin release will keep rising until delivery removes the stretch","why":"Labor is the textbook example of positive feedback. Each contraction makes the next stimulus bigger instead of smaller, so the loop escalates until an outside event, delivery, ends it. Infused oxytocin simply supplies more of the loop's signal. Because her own release depends on cervical stretch and not on the plasma oxytocin level, it rises as dilation proceeds. Oxytocin also raises the uterus's sensitivity by increasing prostaglandin release, which strengthens the loop further."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw labor as a closed positive feedback circle: the fetal head pressing on the cervix, stretch receptors firing, the signal reaching the hypothalamus, oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary, the myometrium contracting, and the head driven harder into the cervix. Draw the loop three times round with the arrows getting thicker each turn. One line: name the event that breaks this loop and say why nothing short of it could."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the breast in cross section with the alveoli and the ducts, and run two separate control lines into it: prolactin from the anterior pituitary to the alveolar cells making the milk, and oxytocin from the posterior pituitary to the myoepithelial cells squeezing it out. Show suckling setting off both through the same afferent nerve. One line: a mother can be making plenty of milk and still not deliver it, so name which of the two failed and what stress has to do with it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain labor as a positive feedback loop driven by oxytocin and compare the hormonal control of milk production with milk ejection."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Parturition and lactation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-lab-cycle-graphs","name":"Hormone cycle graph interpretation","can":"Read a graph of gonadotropin and ovarian hormone levels across a cycle and identify the day of ovulation and the event driving each peak.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A hormone tracing shows the luteinizing hormone peak on day 18 in a woman with regular cycles. On approximately what day should her next menstrual period begin?\nA) About day 28  |  B) About day 25\nC) About day 36  |  D) About day 32 or 33\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About day 32 or 33","why":"Ovulation follows the surge by about a day, and the luteal phase then runs its fixed 14 days, so counting from day 19 gives menstruation near day 32 or 33. Day 28 assumes a standard cycle rather than reading this woman's own surge. Day 25 would give a luteal phase of only about six days, shorter than the corpus luteum's lifespan. Day 36 would stretch the luteal phase past its fixed length."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tracing shows a small follicle stimulating hormone bump at midcycle, at the same time as the luteinizing hormone spike. What drives it?\nA) The same sustained estradiol that drives the LH surge\nB) The fall in progesterone that follows soon after ovulation\nC) Inhibin released from the corpus luteum\nD) A separate hypothalamic hormone specific to FSH\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The same sustained estradiol that drives the LH surge","why":"One GnRH signal drives both gonadotropins, so the estradiol positive feedback that produces the LH surge also produces a smaller FSH rise at the same moment. Progesterone has not yet fallen at midcycle, since it has barely begun to rise. Inhibin lowers FSH rather than raising it, and the corpus luteum does not exist until after ovulation. There is no separate FSH releasing hormone, which is why the two gonadotropins move together here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two tracings are compared. One is from a 28 day cycle and one from a 35 day cycle. On which day does the luteinizing hormone peak appear in the 35 day tracing?\nA) Around day 14  |  B) Around day 17\nC) Around day 28  |  D) Around day 20\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Around day 20","why":"Counting the fixed 14 day luteal phase back from day 35 puts ovulation near day 21 and the surge about a day earlier, near day 20. Day 14 would only be right if every cycle were 28 days, which is the error this comparison is meant to expose. Day 17 is the midpoint of a 35 day cycle, but ovulation is anchored to the end rather than the middle. Day 28 would leave only a week for the luteal phase."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a standard graph of hormone levels across the menstrual cycle, which curve shows a tall narrow spike near the middle?\nA) Progesterone  |  B) Follicle stimulating hormone\nC) Inhibin  |  D) Luteinizing hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Luteinizing hormone","why":"The LH surge is brief and very large, so it appears as a sharp narrow spike around day 13 or 14 on a typical plot. Progesterone forms a broad rounded hill in the second half rather than a spike. FSH does show a small midcycle bump but it is much lower than the LH peak and is easy to distinguish by height. Inhibin is often not plotted at all and does not spike this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a standard cycle graph, which hormone forms a broad rounded peak near day 21?\nA) Progesterone  |  B) Luteinizing hormone\nC) Follicle stimulating hormone  |  D) Estradiol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Progesterone","why":"Progesterone climbs after ovulation as the corpus luteum matures, peaks in the mid luteal phase near day 21, and then falls, giving a wide hill rather than a spike. LH spiked a week earlier and has returned to baseline by day 21. FSH is at one of its lowest points in the mid luteal phase. Estradiol does rise again in the luteal phase but its larger and more recognizable peak is the one just before ovulation."},{"t":"bd","q":"Describe the pattern of LH, FSH, estrogen, and progesterone across a 28 day cycle. State the day ovulation occurs and explain what event drives each peak.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"estrogen rises through the follicular phase as the dominant follicle grows; the sustained estrogen peak just before midcycle triggers the LH surge by positive feedback; the LH surge (with a smaller FSH peak) occurs around day 13 to 14 and ovulation follows about a day later, around day 14; progesterone rises during the luteal phase because the corpus luteum makes it, peaking around day 21; FSH is somewhat elevated early in the follicular phase to recruit follicles, then is suppressed by estrogen and inhibin; in the late luteal phase the corpus luteum degenerates, both steroids fall, and the loss of feedback lets FSH rise to start the next cycle","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes covering 28 days and plot four curves on it, LH, FSH, estradiol, and progesterone, labeled along their length so they can be told apart. Drop a vertical line on the day of ovulation. Beside each peak, write the structure that was making that hormone at that moment. One line: name the peak you used to place ovulation, and say whether ovulation comes before or after it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the four curves for a normal cycle, then draw a second set of axes for a cycle in which ovulation never happened. Change only what has to change and leave the rest alone. Show which curve has lost its peak and which one never rises at all. One line: name the single hormone you would measure in the second half of a cycle to prove ovulation happened, and say what it should be doing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to read a graph of gonadotropin and ovarian hormone levels across a cycle and identify the day of ovulation and the event driving each peak."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormone cycle graph interpretation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Cardiac and Smooth Muscle","comps":[{"id":"w6-smooth-muscle","name":"Smooth muscle contraction mechanism","can":"Trace smooth muscle contraction through calcium entry and release to calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase and explain why the mechanism is slower and more economical than in skeletal muscle.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks L-type Ca2+ channels in the plasma membrane of an arteriole. What happens to myosin light chain kinase activity and to vessel tone?\nA) MLCK activity is unchanged because its Ca2+ comes from the SR\nB) MLCK activity falls but tone stays the same\nC) MLCK activity falls and the vessel dilates\nD) MLCK activity rises and the vessel constricts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) MLCK activity falls and the vessel dilates","why":"Blocking the channel cuts Ca2+ entry, less Ca2+ calmodulin forms, MLCK activity drops, and with the phosphatase still working the light chains are dephosphorylated and the vessel dilates. Tone cannot stay the same, because in smooth muscle tension tracks the fraction of phosphorylated light chains. The SR answer would be closer to the truth in skeletal muscle, where the store supplies nearly all the activator Ca2+ and channel blockers barely change force. Nothing in this pathway increases kinase activity."},{"t":"mc","q":"A signaling pathway inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase while cytosolic Ca2+ stays constant. What happens to tension?\nA) Tension briefly rises and then returns to baseline\nB) Tension rises at that same Ca2+ level\nC) Tension falls because the kinase is inhibited as well\nD) Tension is unchanged because Ca2+ alone sets tension\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Tension rises at that same Ca2+ level","why":"Tension follows the balance between the kinase adding phosphate and the phosphatase removing it, so slowing the phosphatase lets phosphorylated myosin accumulate even with no extra Ca2+. Physiologists call this Ca2+ sensitization, and it is how several vasoconstrictors work. The Ca2+ only answer is the common misreading: Ca2+ sets kinase activity, not tension directly. Nothing here touches the kinase, and the shift persists as long as the phosphatase is inhibited."},{"t":"mc","q":"In smooth muscle, which protein does calcium bind to in order to start contraction?\nA) Calmodulin  |  B) Tropomyosin\nC) Actin  |  D) Troponin C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Calmodulin","why":"Smooth muscle has no troponin, so the Ca2+ receptor is calmodulin, a soluble protein floating in the cytosol rather than sitting on the thin filament. Four Ca2+ bind calmodulin, and that complex is what switches on the next enzyme in the pathway. Troponin C is the Ca2+ receptor in skeletal and cardiac muscle, which is the single most useful contrast to carry out of this chapter. Tropomyosin blocks myosin binding sites in striated muscle but does not bind the activating Ca2+, and actin is the thin filament itself."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the Ca2+ calmodulin complex activate in smooth muscle?\nA) Phospholipase C  |  B) Myosin light chain phosphatase\nC) Protein kinase A  |  D) Myosin light chain kinase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Myosin light chain kinase","why":"Ca2+ calmodulin binds and activates myosin light chain kinase, the enzyme that turns a Ca2+ signal into a working cross bridge. Myosin light chain phosphatase does the opposite job and is not Ca2+ activated, so it runs steadily in the background and drives relaxation when the kinase quiets down. Protein kinase A is activated by cAMP, not by Ca2+ calmodulin, and in smooth muscle it tends to promote relaxation. Phospholipase C sits upstream: it makes IP3 that raises Ca2+ in the first place."},{"t":"mc","q":"Once activated, myosin light chain kinase phosphorylates what?\nA) The troponin I subunit on the thin filament\nB) A serine on the tropomyosin strand\nC) The regulatory light chain on the myosin head\nD) The actin monomer at the myosin binding site\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The regulatory light chain on the myosin head","why":"The target is a small regulatory light chain sitting on the neck of the myosin head, and phosphorylating it lets that head bind actin and cycle. Because the switch is on myosin, smooth muscle regulation is called thick filament regulation, in contrast to the thin filament regulation of striated muscle. Troponin I is not present in smooth muscle at all. Tropomyosin is present but is not the phosphorylation target, and actin itself is never phosphorylated to turn contraction on."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace smooth muscle contraction from a rise in cytosolic Ca2+ to crossbridge cycling, then explain why the process is slower and more economical than skeletal muscle contraction.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"Ca2+ comes from extracellular fluid through membrane channels and from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; Ca2+ binds calmodulin, not troponin; Ca2+ calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase; the kinase phosphorylates myosin light chains, and only phosphorylated myosin can bind actin and cycle; slower because contraction waits on an enzyme cascade and smooth muscle myosin ATPase is very slow; economical because slow cycling and the latch state hold tension with little ATP use; relaxation requires Ca2+ removal and dephosphorylation by myosin light chain phosphatase","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a smooth muscle cell and trace one pathway across it in order: calcium entering from outside and released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, calcium binding calmodulin, calcium calmodulin switching on myosin light chain kinase, the kinase phosphorylating the myosin light chain, and the head then cycling on actin. Draw the phosphate landing where it actually lands. One line: there is no troponin anywhere in this drawing, so name what took over its job."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw skeletal and smooth muscle contraction as two ladders side by side, both starting at calcium and both ending at tension, one step per rung. Count the rungs in each. Beside the smooth ladder, draw a tension trace that rises slowly and then holds for a long time on very little ATP. One line: say which part of your ladder explains the slow onset and which part explains the cheap holding."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace smooth muscle contraction through calcium entry and release to calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase and explain why the mechanism is slower and more economical than in skeletal muscle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Smooth muscle contraction mechanism. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-smooth-regulation","name":"Smooth muscle types and regulation","can":"Compare single unit and multi unit smooth muscle and list the stimuli that alter smooth muscle tone including stretch and local chemical signals.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug uncouples the gap junctions in the wall of the small intestine. What happens to peristalsis?\nA) Nothing changes, since each fiber is separately innervated\nB) The muscle becomes unable to contract at all\nC) Contractions become stronger and better synchronized\nD) Coordinated waves are lost and contraction becomes patchy\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Coordinated waves are lost and contraction becomes patchy","why":"Intestinal smooth muscle is single unit, so a pacemaker depolarization has to travel cell to cell through gap junctions to make a whole segment contract in sequence. Cut those connections and individual cells still contract, but not together, so propulsion fails. Separate innervation of every fiber describes multi unit muscle, not the gut. The cells retain their contractile machinery, so a complete loss of contraction is too strong a prediction."},{"t":"mc","q":"The ciliary muscle adjusts lens shape in small graded steps under autonomic control. Which classification does this behavior indicate?\nA) Single unit smooth muscle  |  B) Cardiac muscle\nC) Skeletal muscle under voluntary control  |  D) Multi unit smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Multi unit smooth muscle","why":"Fine grading requires that the nervous system be able to activate some fibers and not others, which is only possible when the fibers are not electrically coupled. That uncoupled, individually innervated design is the definition of multi unit smooth muscle. Single unit muscle would move as one sheet and could not focus a lens smoothly. Cardiac muscle contracts as a whole by design, and the ciliary muscle is not under voluntary control, which rules out skeletal muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are slow waves in single unit smooth muscle?\nA) Spontaneous rhythmic swings in membrane potential from pacemaker cells\nB) Long action potentials carried by a slow inward Ca2+ plateau current\nC) Slowly spreading waves of Ca2+ released from the internal stores\nD) Sustained depolarizations produced by continuous stretch of the wall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Spontaneous rhythmic swings in membrane potential from pacemaker cells","why":"Slow waves are subthreshold oscillations of the resting potential, set up by pacemaker cells such as the interstitial cells of Cajal and spread through gap junctions. When a wave crests above threshold it triggers a burst of spikes and a contraction, which is why gut motility has an underlying rhythm. A plateau bearing action potential describes cardiac muscle and some smooth muscle spikes, but it is not the pacemaker rhythm itself. Ca2+ waves are intracellular events, and stretch produces depolarization but not the intrinsic rhythm."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does an autonomic axon deliver its transmitter to smooth muscle?\nA) By direct current flow through gap junctions with the axon\nB) Through capillaries after the transmitter enters the blood\nC) Through a single tightly organized motor end plate per fiber\nD) From varicosities along the axon into the surrounding space\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) From varicosities along the axon into the surrounding space","why":"Autonomic axons run past smooth muscle cells and release transmitter from swellings called varicosities into a diffuse junction, so one axon can influence many cells and the transmitter reaches receptors across a wider gap. That diffuse arrangement is part of why smooth muscle responses are slower and more graded than a skeletal twitch. The tight motor end plate belongs to skeletal muscle. Neurons do not couple to smooth muscle electrically, and local transmitter is not delivered by way of the bloodstream, which is how hormones act."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the myogenic response of smooth muscle?\nA) Loss of tone when the muscle is held at a fixed length\nB) Relaxation of the muscle when it is stretched\nC) Contraction of the muscle in response to being stretched\nD) Contraction triggered by sympathetic nerve traffic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Contraction of the muscle in response to being stretched","why":"Stretch opens mechanically sensitive channels, the cell depolarizes, voltage gated Ca2+ channels open, and the muscle contracts. In an arteriole this is the basis of autoregulation, since a rise in pressure meets a rise in resistance. Relaxation to stretch is the opposite of what is observed and would let a vessel balloon. Sympathetic constriction is a neural stimulus, not the myogenic one, and the myogenic response is defined by a change in length, not by holding one."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Single unit smooth muscle cells cannot contract unless each cell receives its own input from an autonomic neuron.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Single unit smooth muscle cells are coupled by gap junctions and can be excited by neighboring cells, by stretch, or by pacemaker activity, so most cells contract without any direct nerve contact.","why":"The statement describes multi unit smooth muscle, where each cell is independently innervated. Single unit smooth muscle in the gut, uterus, and small blood vessels behaves as an electrical sheet. Depolarization started by a pacemaker cell, a stretch, or a neurotransmitter released near a few cells spreads through gap junctions to the whole tissue. Nerves and hormones adjust this activity but are not required to start it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a sheet of single unit smooth muscle with gap junctions between the cells and one pacemaker cell in it, then draw multi unit smooth muscle beside it with separate cells each carrying its own nerve ending. Name a real location in the body under each. Draw one stimulus spreading through both sheets. One line: say which arrangement lets a whole organ empty in one go, and which lets you grade the response finely."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a ring of smooth muscle in a vessel wall in the middle of your page, and bring an arrow into it from every stimulus that changes its tone: stretch of the wall, a sympathetic nerve ending, a circulating hormone, and a local chemical signal from the tissue outside. Mark each arrow tighten or relax. One line: pick the local chemical you drew and say what the surrounding tissue is asking the vessel to do by releasing it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare single unit and multi unit smooth muscle and list the stimuli that alter smooth muscle tone including stretch and local chemical signals."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Smooth muscle types and regulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-cardiac-muscle","name":"Cardiac muscle properties","can":"Explain how intercalated discs and gap junctions allow the myocardium to act as a functional syncytium and state why cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why can skeletal muscle be tetanized while cardiac muscle cannot?\nA) Cardiac muscle relaxes faster than skeletal muscle, preventing summation\nB) Cardiac muscle lacks the sarcoplasmic reticulum needed for summation\nC) Cardiac cells are refractory almost as long as the contraction lasts\nD) Cardiac muscle cannot be stimulated more than once per second\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cardiac cells are refractory almost as long as the contraction lasts","why":"In skeletal muscle a 2 ms action potential is long over before the twitch peaks, so a second stimulus can arrive while tension is still present and the twitches add. In cardiac muscle the long plateau keeps the cell refractory through nearly the entire contraction, so by the time another beat can be triggered the muscle has relaxed. Cardiac muscle has a working sarcoplasmic reticulum. It can be driven at three or four beats per second, just never fast enough to summate. Cardiac relaxation is slower, not faster, than skeletal."},{"t":"mc","q":"A second stimulus is delivered to a ventricular myocyte 100 ms after the first, during the plateau. What happens?\nA) Nothing, because the cell is absolutely refractory\nB) The plateau is cut short and the cell repolarizes early\nC) A second, smaller action potential is triggered\nD) A full second action potential is triggered and tension adds\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Nothing, because the cell is absolutely refractory","why":"At 100 ms the cell is still in the plateau with its fast Na+ channels inactivated, so no stimulus strength will produce another upstroke. That protection is what prevents summation of cardiac twitches. A smaller response would be possible only later, in the relative refractory period, once some Na+ channels have recovered. Tension cannot add during absolute refractoriness, and an ordinary stimulus does not truncate the plateau."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which component of the intercalated disc lets current pass directly from one cardiac cell to the next?\nA) Gap junctions  |  B) The fascia adherens\nC) Tight junctions  |  D) Desmosomes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gap junctions","why":"Gap junctions are pores built from connexon proteins that link the cytoplasm of neighboring myocytes, so ions carrying a depolarization flow straight into the next cell with no synapse in between. That low resistance path is what turns millions of separate cells into one conducting mass. Desmosomes and the fascia adherens are the mechanical halves of the disc: they hold cells together and transmit force. Tight junctions seal spaces between epithelial cells and are not a feature of the intercalated disc."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the intercalated disc keeps cardiac cells from being pulled apart during a forceful contraction?\nA) The sarcoplasmic reticulum terminal cisternae  |  B) The gap junctions\nC) The T tubules at the Z line  |  D) The desmosomes and fascia adherens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The desmosomes and fascia adherens","why":"Desmosomes anchor intermediate filaments between cells and the fascia adherens ties the thin filaments of one cell to those of the next, so tension generated in one myocyte is handed mechanically to its neighbor. Without them the electrical connection would tear apart under load. Gap junctions handle the electrical job and are far too small to carry much force. T tubules bring the action potential into the interior of a cell, and terminal cisternae store Ca2+; neither joins cell to cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ion current is chiefly responsible for the plateau phase of the ventricular action potential?\nA) K+ leaving through delayed rectifier channels\nB) Cl- entering through anion channels\nC) Ca2+ entering through L-type Ca2+ channels\nD) Na+ entering through fast Na+ channels\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Ca2+ entering through L-type Ca2+ channels","why":"During the plateau, slow L-type Ca2+ channels stay open and the inward Ca2+ current roughly balances the outward K+ current, holding the membrane depolarized for a couple hundred milliseconds. That Ca2+ does double duty, since it also triggers Ca2+ release from the SR. Fast Na+ channels drive the upstroke and then inactivate within a few milliseconds. Delayed rectifier K+ current is present but is what finally ends the plateau when it grows, and Cl- current is not the main plateau carrier."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An isolated strip of ventricular muscle has an action potential lasting 250 ms and a contraction lasting about 300 ms. A researcher stimulates it at 10 Hz, one stimulus every 100 ms, the same frequency that fuses a skeletal muscle strip from the same animal into a smooth tetanus.\nPredict the cardiac strip's tension record and explain the difference from skeletal muscle.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The strip responds only to stimuli that arrive after its refractory period ends, so it produces separate beats at a lower rate than 10 Hz and never fuses","why":"The skeletal action potential is over in 2 ms, so a stimulus every 100 ms finds the fiber excitable and still contracting, and the twitches summate. The cardiac cell is refractory for nearly the whole 250 ms plateau, so stimuli arriving at 100 ms intervals are simply ignored until the membrane has repolarized. By then the previous contraction is almost finished, so each accepted stimulus produces a fresh, separate beat. This long refractory period is what guarantees the heart relaxes and fills between beats."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two cardiac cells joined at an intercalated disc, big enough to show the desmosomes and the gap junctions as separate structures. Show what passes through the gap junctions and what force the desmosomes are holding against. Then draw four cells in a row and trace one signal through all of them with no nerve involved. One line: name what acting as a functional syncytium buys the ventricle that a set of independent cells could not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cardiac action potential with the tension it produces on the same time axis, lined up, then draw a skeletal action potential and its twitch below at the same time scale. Bracket the absolute refractory period on both. One line: skeletal muscle can be driven into tetanus and cardiac muscle cannot, so say what would happen to the output of a heart that could be."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how intercalated discs and gap junctions allow the myocardium to act as a functional syncytium and state why cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cardiac muscle properties. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w6-muscle-comparison","name":"Comparison of the three muscle types","can":"Build a comparison of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by calcium source, regulatory protein, control, and speed of contraction.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug binds troponin C and prevents Ca2+ from binding it. Which tissues lose the ability to contract?\nA) Cardiac and smooth muscle, with skeletal muscle unaffected\nB) All three muscle types\nC) Smooth muscle only\nD) Skeletal and cardiac muscle, with smooth muscle unaffected\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Skeletal and cardiac muscle, with smooth muscle unaffected","why":"The drug can only work where its target exists, and troponin is a thin filament protein of striated muscle. Smooth muscle has no troponin and routes Ca2+ through calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase instead, so it goes on contracting. Saying all three assumes a shared mechanism that the tissues do not share. The options that spare skeletal muscle or hit smooth muscle alone reverse which tissues carry troponin, which is the fact this card is asking you to reason from."},{"t":"mc","q":"In which muscle types does Ca2+ start contraction by binding troponin C on the thin filament?\nA) All three muscle types  |  B) Skeletal muscle only\nC) Skeletal and cardiac muscle  |  D) Cardiac and smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Skeletal and cardiac muscle","why":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle are both striated and both use the troponin tropomyosin complex, so Ca2+ binding troponin C swings tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites. Smooth muscle has no troponin at all, which is why it cannot be on this list, and it is the single most useful fact for telling the three tissues apart mechanistically. Answering skeletal only forgets that cardiac thin filaments carry the same regulatory proteins. Grouping cardiac with smooth mixes a thin filament system with a thick filament one."},{"t":"mc","q":"In which muscle type is the Ca2+ binding regulatory protein a soluble cytosolic protein rather than part of the thin filament?\nA) Cardiac muscle  |  B) Smooth muscle\nC) Cardiac and skeletal muscle  |  D) Skeletal muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Smooth muscle","why":"Smooth muscle uses calmodulin, which floats free in the cytosol and, once it has bound Ca2+, activates myosin light chain kinase. Because the switch ends up on the myosin head, smooth muscle regulation is called thick filament regulation. Skeletal and cardiac muscle both attach the Ca2+ sensor to the thin filament as troponin C, so the switch sits on actin rather than on myosin. That difference in where the switch lives predicts almost everything else about the three tissues."},{"t":"mc","q":"How much of the activator Ca2+ for a skeletal muscle twitch comes from outside the cell?\nA) Essentially none of it  |  B) None during a twitch, but all during tetanus\nC) Essentially all of it  |  D) About half of it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Essentially none of it","why":"In skeletal muscle the voltage sensor in the T tubule membrane opens the SR release channel by direct mechanical coupling, so no Ca2+ needs to cross the plasma membrane for a twitch to occur. That self sufficiency is why a skeletal fiber keeps twitching in a Ca2+ free bath and why Ca2+ channel blockers do not weaken it. Cardiac and smooth muscle are the tissues that depend on Ca2+ entry. Tetanus is repeated activation of the same mechanism, so the Ca2+ source does not change."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle types require Ca2+ to cross the plasma membrane for each contraction?\nA) Skeletal and smooth muscle  |  B) All three types equally\nC) Skeletal and cardiac muscle  |  D) Cardiac and smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cardiac and smooth muscle","why":"Cardiac muscle needs a trigger of entering Ca2+ to open ryanodine receptors, and smooth muscle relies on entering Ca2+ for most of its activator supply, so both tissues are sensitive to extracellular Ca2+ and to Ca2+ channel blockers. Skeletal muscle is the exception, since its T tubule voltage sensor opens the SR channel mechanically. Any option that includes skeletal muscle in the entry dependent group misses that mechanical coupling. The three are not equal here, and this is exactly the difference that experiments in low Ca2+ solutions expose."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle on four points: where the Ca2+ for contraction comes from, which protein Ca2+ binds, what controls contraction, and how fast contraction is.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"Ca2+ source: skeletal from the sarcoplasmic reticulum only; cardiac from extracellular entry that triggers SR release; smooth from both extracellular fluid and SR; regulatory protein: skeletal and cardiac use troponin on the thin filament; smooth uses calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase; control: skeletal contracts only when a somatic motor neuron fires; cardiac is autorhythmic and modulated by autonomic nerves and hormones; smooth is controlled by autonomic nerves, hormones, local signals, and stretch; speed: skeletal fastest, cardiac intermediate, smooth slowest; skeletal muscle can be tetanized; cardiac cannot because of its long refractory period; cardiac and single unit smooth muscle are coupled by gap junctions; skeletal fibers are electrically isolated from each other","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a three column chart, skeletal, cardiac, smooth, and fill it with drawings rather than words: one cell of each at the same magnification with striations present or absent, the calcium source drawn as arrows into the cytosol, the protein that calcium binds, and what gives the command. One line: two of the three share a regulatory protein, so name the odd one out and what it uses instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick one property, calcium source, regulatory protein, control, or speed, and draw all three muscle types along a single line ordered by that property, with the drawing that justifies each placement underneath. Then do it again on a second line using a different property. One line: your two orderings do not match, so say which property matters more for the job each muscle is actually asked to do."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to build a comparison of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by calcium source, regulatory protein, control, and speed of contraction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Comparison of the three muscle types. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Cardiac Electrophysiology","comps":[{"id":"w10-pacemaker-potential","name":"Pacemaker potential","can":"Explain the unstable pacemaker potential of the sinoatrial node by the funny current, calcium entry, and potassium exit and state what sets intrinsic heart rate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The sinoatrial node stops firing entirely. Which tissue is most likely to take over, and at roughly what rate?\nA) Purkinje fibers, at about 60 to 100 beats per minute\nB) Ordinary atrial muscle, at about 70 beats per minute\nC) The atrioventricular node, at 40 to 60 per minute\nD) The bundle branches, at about 90 beats per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The atrioventricular node, at 40 to 60 per minute","why":"The next fastest pacemaker takes over, and that is the atrioventricular junction, whose cells drift to threshold about 40 to 60 times a minute. Purkinje fibers can also pace, but far more slowly, at roughly 20 to 40 per minute, so the rate given in that option belongs to the sinoatrial node. Ordinary atrial and ventricular muscle have stable resting potentials and no funny current, so they cannot generate their own rhythm. Bundle branch tissue is part of the same slow His and Purkinje escape system and does not pace at 90."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these changes in a sinoatrial node cell would raise heart rate?\nA) A more negative maximum diastolic potential\nB) A steeper pacemaker potential\nC) A threshold shifted toward zero\nD) A longer nodal action potential\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A steeper pacemaker potential","why":"Rate is the inverse of the time from one upstroke to the next, and a steeper drift covers the distance to threshold in less time, so beats come closer together. A more negative maximum diastolic potential lengthens the distance the drift has to cover and slows the rate. A threshold shifted toward zero also lengthens that distance and slows the rate. A longer action potential adds time to each cycle before the drift can even begin, which slows the rate as well."},{"t":"mc","q":"During the early part of the sinoatrial node pacemaker potential, which ion moves into the cell through the funny channels?\nA) Na+  |  B) K+\nC) Ca2+  |  D) Cl-\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Na+","why":"The funny current is a slow inward Na+ current through HCN channels, and it starts the drift away from the most negative point of the cycle back toward threshold. K+ moves out of a pacemaker cell during repolarization, so it cannot be the ion driving the membrane in the depolarizing direction. Ca2+ entry is real but comes later in the pacemaker potential, first through T-type and then through L-type channels. Cl- movement plays no part in the sinoatrial pacemaker sequence."},{"t":"mc","q":"What opens the funny channels of a sinoatrial node cell?\nA) Depolarization of the membrane to about -40 mV\nB) Hyperpolarization after the last action potential\nC) Ca2+ binding to the channel from inside the cell\nD) Stretch of the nodal cell membrane during filling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hyperpolarization after the last action potential","why":"HCN channels are called funny because hyperpolarization opens them, the opposite of most voltage-gated channels, so the current switches on exactly when the cell is most negative near -60 mV. Depolarization to about -40 mV is threshold, which opens the L-type Ca2+ channels for the upstroke, not the funny channels. These channels are gated by voltage and tuned by cyclic AMP, not opened by intracellular Ca2+ binding, and they are not mechanically gated by stretch."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with a resting heart rate of 62 beats per minute (normal 60 to 100) is given atropine, a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Within two minutes the rate is 95 beats per minute. No epinephrine or norepinephrine was given.\nWhat happened in the sinoatrial node cells to raise the rate?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Blocking the resting vagal signal reduced K+ efflux and raised the funny current, so the pacemaker potential reached threshold sooner","why":"At rest the vagus is always releasing some acetylcholine onto the node. That acetylcholine, through muscarinic receptors, opens K+ channels and lowers the funny current, which holds the rate below the intrinsic 100 beats per minute. Atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors, so the brake comes off: K+ efflux falls, the funny current rises, the pacemaker slope steepens, and each cycle is shorter. The rate climbs toward the intrinsic rate of the node without any sympathetic help."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how a sinoatrial node cell generates its own rhythm, name the ion movement in each phase, and then explain why norepinephrine makes the heart beat faster.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the node has no stable resting potential; the membrane starts drifting upward from about -60 mV right after repolarization; the drift is the funny current, Na+ entering through channels that open on hyperpolarization; near -40 mV, L type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ entry produces the upstroke; K+ leaving the cell repolarizes the membrane back to about -60 mV; the intrinsic rate of the node is about 100 beats per minute; vagal tone holds the resting rate near 70; norepinephrine on beta 1 receptors raises cAMP, increases the funny current and Ca2+ current, steepens the slope, and threshold is reached sooner so rate rises","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the SA node membrane potential over two full cycles, voltage on the y axis and time on the x. Mark the most negative point near -60 mV and threshold near -40 mV. Along the slow drift upward, label the funny current sodium leak, then the calcium entry that carries the upstroke, then the potassium exit that brings it back down. One line: what sets the rate on this trace, and it is not the height of the spike."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three pacemaker slopes on one set of axes, all rising to the same threshold: normal, one steeper, one shallower. Mark where each fires and how far apart the firings are. Beside them draw a fourth that has the normal slope but starts from a more negative point. Write the normal sinus range, 60 to 100 beats per minute, next to the normal one. One line: two of your changes slow the heart by different means, so name which does which."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain the unstable pacemaker potential of the sinoatrial node by the funny current, calcium entry, and potassium exit and state what sets intrinsic heart rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pacemaker potential. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-cardiac-ap","name":"Contractile cell action potential","can":"Diagram the ventricular action potential and identify the ion movement responsible for the rapid upstroke, the plateau, and repolarization.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A toxin blocks fast voltage-gated Na+ channels in ventricular muscle. Predict the effect on phase 0.\nA) The upstroke becomes slower and smaller, because less Na+ current flows\nB) The upstroke becomes faster because K+ efflux is unopposed\nC) The upstroke is unchanged because Ca2+ carries it\nD) The upstroke reverses and becomes a hyperpolarization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The upstroke becomes slower and smaller, because less Na+ current flows","why":"Phase 0 is Na+ influx through fast channels, so blocking them reduces the inward current available and the membrane rises more slowly and to a lower peak, which also slows conduction to neighboring cells. K+ efflux opposes depolarization, so removing an inward current cannot make the upstroke faster. Assuming Ca2+ carries the upstroke confuses the contractile cell with the sinoatrial node. A blocked channel simply passes less current; it does not drive the membrane in the opposite direction."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks L-type Ca2+ channels in ventricular muscle. What happens to the action potential?\nA) The upstroke slows, but the overall duration is unchanged\nB) The plateau shortens, so the action potential is briefer\nC) The plateau lengthens, because K+ efflux is also reduced\nD) The resting potential becomes less negative than before\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The plateau shortens, so the action potential is briefer","why":"The plateau is held up by inward Ca2+ current, so with less of it the outward K+ current wins sooner, repolarization starts earlier, and total action potential duration falls. The upstroke is a Na+ event and is not affected by an L-type blocker. Blocking Ca2+ channels does nothing to K+ channels, so there is no reason for the plateau to lengthen. Resting potential is set by K+ leak conductance and is not shifted by a Ca2+ channel blocker."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks the delayed rectifier K+ channels of ventricular muscle. Predict the effect on the action potential.\nA) Repolarization is slowed, so the action potential lasts longer\nB) Repolarization is faster, so the action potential is shorter\nC) The upstroke is slowed, so the peak is lower\nD) The resting potential becomes more negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Repolarization is slowed, so the action potential lasts longer","why":"Phase 3 depends on outward K+ current, so reducing it means the membrane takes longer to fall back to -90 mV and the action potential, along with the QT interval on an ECG, is prolonged. Blocking a repolarizing current cannot speed repolarization up. The upstroke is a Na+ event, so its rate of rise is untouched. Less K+ conductance moves the resting membrane away from the K+ equilibrium potential, making it less negative rather than more."},{"t":"mc","q":"Extracellular K+ rises from 4 to 7 mEq/L. Which way does the resting membrane potential of a ventricular cell move?\nA) It becomes more negative, moving away from threshold\nB) It becomes less negative, moving toward threshold\nC) It does not change, because the Na+/K+ ATPase compensates immediately\nD) It becomes more negative, because more K+ leaves the cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It becomes less negative, moving toward threshold","why":"Resting potential tracks the K+ equilibrium potential, and raising extracellular K+ reduces the gradient driving K+ out, so the equilibrium potential and the resting potential both shift toward zero. Less negative means closer to threshold, which is why moderate hyperkalemia first makes cardiac cells more excitable before inactivating Na+ channels and making them less excitable. A smaller outward gradient cannot drive more K+ out, so the option reasoning from increased efflux has the gradient backwards. The pump adjusts over minutes and cannot hold the membrane potential fixed against an acute change."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with kidney failure has a plasma K+ of 7.2 mmol/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0). The ECG shows a QRS complex that has widened from 0.08 to 0.14 second (normal under 0.12).\nExplain the chain from the high plasma K+ to the wide QRS.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The resting potential of ventricular cells is less negative, so more fast Na+ channels are inactivated at rest, the upstroke is slower, and the wave of depolarization spreads more slowly through the ventricles","why":"The resting potential of a ventricular cell is set mostly by the K+ gradient. Raising extracellular K+ shrinks that gradient, so the resting potential moves from about -90 mV toward -70 mV. Fast Na+ channels are voltage sensitive, and at a less negative resting potential a fraction of them sit in the inactivated state. With fewer channels available, the upstroke of each cell is smaller and slower, each cell depolarizes its neighbor less effectively, and the wave of depolarization crawls through the ventricles instead of racing. Slower ventricular depolarization is a wider QRS."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, sketch a ventricular contractile cell action potential, label the ion movement behind the upstroke, the plateau, and repolarization, and explain why the plateau matters for contraction.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"resting potential near -90 mV, held by K+ permeability; upstroke: fast voltage gated Na+ channels open and Na+ rushes in; plateau: L type Ca2+ channels open and Ca2+ entry balances K+ exit, holding the membrane depolarized for 150 to 200 milliseconds; repolarization: Ca2+ channels close and K+ exit returns the membrane to -90 mV; the Ca2+ that enters during the plateau triggers Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (calcium induced calcium release); less plateau Ca2+ entry means less release and a weaker contraction","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the ventricular contractile cell action potential, voltage against time, resting near -90 mV, peaking near +20 mV, and lasting about 250 ms. Number the phases 0 through 4 and write on each the ion and the direction that makes it: fast sodium in, brief potassium out, calcium in balanced against potassium out, potassium out, rest. One line: which phase has no counterpart in a skeletal muscle fiber, and what the heart buys with it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a ventricular action potential and a skeletal muscle action potential on one time axis, drawn to their true durations, about 250 ms against about 2 ms. Directly under each, on the same axis, draw the twitch it produces. Keep the scale honest. One line: with the four traces drawn to scale in front of you, say what the heart cannot do that the skeletal fiber can."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to diagram the ventricular action potential and identify the ion movement responsible for the rapid upstroke, the plateau, and repolarization."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Contractile cell action potential. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-conduction-system","name":"Conduction system","can":"Trace an impulse through the sinoatrial node, internodal pathways, atrioventricular node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers and explain the purpose of the atrioventricular delay.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why do atrioventricular nodal cells conduct so slowly?\nA) They are wrapped in myelin, which slows the impulse between the nodes\nB) They are small, poorly coupled, and use a slow Ca2+ upstroke\nC) They lack a Na+/K+ ATPase, so their ion gradients are shallow\nD) They are separated by synapses that add a chemical transmission delay\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) They are small, poorly coupled, and use a slow Ca2+ upstroke","why":"Three features stack up: a narrow fiber offers high internal resistance, few gap junctions mean less current passes to the next cell, and a Ca2+ driven upstroke rises slowly compared with a Na+ driven one. Cardiac tissue has no myelin at all, which is a nervous system feature. Nodal cells have normal ion pumps and normal gradients. There are no synapses inside the conduction system; cardiac cells are electrically coupled directly through gap junctions."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does ventricular contraction begin at the apex and move toward the base?\nA) The apex has thicker muscle, so it depolarizes at a lower threshold\nB) Purkinje fibers reach the apex first, so blood is driven upward\nC) The atrioventricular valves open at the apex and pull the impulse down\nD) The base is insulated by the fibrous skeleton and cannot contract\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Purkinje fibers reach the apex first, so blood is driven upward","why":"The bundle branches run down the interventricular septum to the apex before the Purkinje fibers fan out, so the apex depolarizes and contracts first and the wave of contraction wrings blood upward toward the aortic and pulmonary valves. Wall thickness does not change threshold; depolarization order is set by the conduction pathway. Valve movement is a consequence of pressure changes and does not steer the impulse. The fibrous skeleton insulates the atria from the ventricles electrically, but the base of the ventricles is working muscle and contracts normally."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sympathetic stimulation acts on the atrioventricular node. Predict the effect on conduction through it.\nA) Conduction speeds up, so the delay is shorter\nB) Conduction slows, so the delay is longer\nC) Conduction is blocked entirely at high rates\nD) Conduction is unaffected, since only the sinoatrial node is innervated\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Conduction speeds up, so the delay is shorter","why":"Sympathetic fibers increase Ca2+ current in nodal tissue, so atrioventricular cells reach threshold and pass the impulse on faster, shortening the delay and the PR interval, which is useful at high heart rates when there is less time to spare. Slowed conduction with a longer delay is the parasympathetic effect. Block at high rates is what happens with excess vagal tone or disease, not with sympathetic drive. Both nodes are richly innervated by both branches of the autonomic nervous system."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which step in the conduction pathway accounts for most of the flat segment between the end of the P wave and the start of the QRS complex?\nA) Spread of the impulse through atrial muscle\nB) Conduction through the atrioventricular node\nC) Conduction through the Purkinje fibers\nD) Repolarization of the atria\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Conduction through the atrioventricular node","why":"The line is flat because the tissue conducting at that moment, the atrioventricular node, is too small to generate a voltage the surface electrodes can see, and its slow conduction fills most of that 100 ms. Atrial spread produces the P wave itself, which comes just before this segment. Purkinje conduction is extremely fast and occurs immediately before and during the QRS. Atrial repolarization does occur around this time but is small and buried under the QRS complex, and it does not create the delay."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does a normal cardiac impulse begin?\nA) In the sinoatrial node, high in the wall of the right atrium\nB) In the atrioventricular node, in the floor of the right atrium\nC) In the bundle of His, at the top of the interventricular septum\nD) In the left atrium, near where the pulmonary veins enter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the sinoatrial node, high in the wall of the right atrium","why":"The sinoatrial node sits high in the right atrial wall next to the superior vena cava, and because its cells drift to threshold fastest, that is where each normal beat starts. The atrioventricular node is downstream, in the floor of the right atrium near the tricuspid valve, and only paces if the sinoatrial node fails. The bundle of His is further downstream still, carrying the impulse into the septum. The left atrium receives the impulse through internodal and interatrial pathways rather than generating it."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a normal heart, the atrioventricular node is the only electrical connection between the atria and the ventricles.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Gap junctions let the impulse spread freely within the atria and within the ventricles, but the connective tissue ring around the valves blocks it from crossing directly. The atrioventricular node and bundle of His are the one bridge. That is what makes the nodal delay possible, since the impulse has no way around the slow cells. It is also why damage to the node produces heart block, and why an extra muscle bridge (an accessory pathway) lets the ventricles fire early."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the heart in outline with four chambers and place the conduction system in order: SA node in the right atrial wall, internodal pathways, AV node at the base of the interatrial septum, bundle of His, right and left bundle branches down the interventricular septum, and Purkinje fibers running from the apex up the ventricular walls. Number the arrows 1 through 6. One line: why the ventricles are wired to squeeze from the apex upward."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a horizontal time line for one beat, in milliseconds, and mark where each part of the conduction system fires. Draw the AV nodal delay as a labeled gap of about 100 ms. Above the line, draw what the atria are doing during that gap and what the ventricles are doing. One line: name one thing that would go wrong on the very next beat if the delay were zero."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace an impulse through the sinoatrial node, internodal pathways, atrioventricular node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers and explain the purpose of the atrioventricular delay."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Conduction system. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-cardiac-refractory","name":"Cardiac refractory period","can":"Explain why the long refractory period of cardiac muscle prevents tetanus and why that matters for pump function.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A second stimulus is delivered to a ventricular cell 100 ms after the upstroke, during the plateau. What happens?\nA) A second action potential of normal size occurs\nB) A second action potential of reduced size occurs\nC) Nothing, because the cell is absolutely refractory\nD) The cell hyperpolarizes and the contraction ends early\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Nothing, because the cell is absolutely refractory","why":"At 100 ms the cell is still in the plateau with its fast Na+ channels inactivated, so no stimulus of any size can produce another action potential. A normal sized second action potential would require full recovery, which does not happen until the cell has repolarized. A reduced action potential is possible in the relative refractory period later in phase 3, not during the plateau. A stimulus delivers depolarizing current and cannot hyperpolarize the cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why must the heart relax fully between beats?\nA) Ventricular filling happens during relaxation, so without it there is no volume to eject\nB) The Na+/K+ ATPase can only work while the muscle is relaxed\nC) The atrioventricular valves can only close while the ventricles are relaxed\nD) Action potentials cannot be conducted while the muscle is contracting\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Ventricular filling happens during relaxation, so without it there is no volume to eject","why":"Stroke volume is limited by end diastolic volume, and the ventricles only fill when their walls are relaxed and chamber pressure is below atrial pressure, so relaxation time directly limits pump output. The Na+/K+ ATPase runs continuously and does not pause for contraction. The atrioventricular valves close at the start of contraction, not during relaxation; they open during relaxation. Cardiac impulses conduct through contracting muscle perfectly well, as the spread of each normal beat shows."},{"t":"mc","q":"What defines the absolute refractory period of a cardiac muscle cell?\nA) No stimulus of any strength can trigger a second action potential\nB) A stronger than normal stimulus can trigger a second action potential\nC) The cell responds normally but contracts more weakly\nD) The cell is depolarized but cannot release Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) No stimulus of any strength can trigger a second action potential","why":"During the absolute refractory period the fast Na+ channels are inactivated and cannot reopen, so the cell simply will not fire again no matter how large the stimulus. A stimulus that is strong enough to work describes the relative refractory period, which follows the absolute one. A normal response with a weaker contraction is not a refractory state at all. Ca2+ release is happening vigorously during this period, which is why the cell is contracting."},{"t":"mc","q":"What makes the ventricular cell absolutely refractory for so long?\nA) The sarcoplasmic reticulum is empty and cannot release more Ca2+\nB) Fast Na+ channels stay inactivated until the membrane repolarizes\nC) The Na+/K+ ATPase has not yet restored the resting ion gradients\nD) Acetylcholine remains bound to the membrane throughout the contraction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Fast Na+ channels stay inactivated until the membrane repolarizes","why":"Inactivated Na+ channels only recover when the membrane returns toward its negative resting potential, and the long plateau holds the cell depolarized for about 200 ms, so recovery is postponed for most of the contraction. The sarcoplasmic reticulum still contains Ca2+; refractoriness is a membrane property, not a store depletion problem. The Na+/K+ ATPase restores gradients slowly and continuously and a single action potential barely dents them. Acetylcholine acts on nodal tissue and does not gate the ventricular refractory period."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient is started on a drug that blocks the K+ channels responsible for phase 3 repolarization in ventricular cells. The QT interval lengthens from 0.38 to 0.50 second (normal about 0.36 to 0.44).\nPredict what happens to the ventricular refractory period and to the fastest rate at which the ventricle can respond to stimulation.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The refractory period lengthens, so the maximum rate the ventricle can follow falls","why":"The refractory period lasts until enough Na+ channels have recovered from inactivation, and that recovery only happens once the membrane repolarizes. Slowing the K+ current stretches the action potential, so the membrane stays depolarized longer and the Na+ channels stay inactivated longer. A longer refractory period means a longer minimum gap between beats, so the highest rate the ventricle can respond to drops. Each cycle also has less of its time available for a new stimulus to fire a full beat."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized while skeletal muscle can, and explain why that difference is essential for the heart to work as a pump.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"in skeletal muscle the action potential ends in a few milliseconds, long before the twitch, so repeated stimuli sum into tetanus; in cardiac muscle the plateau keeps the action potential going for about 200 milliseconds; Na+ channels stay inactivated through the plateau, so the refractory period lasts almost as long as the contraction; a new action potential cannot start until the muscle is already relaxing, so twitches cannot sum; the ventricle must relax to fill; a tetanized ventricle would have no diastole and no stroke volume; the refractory period also limits how fast the ventricle can be driven, which protects filling time","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cardiac action potential and, directly under it on the same time axis, the twitch it produces. Shade the absolute refractory period across both drawings. Now stimulate again at three points, early, middle, and just after the shading ends, and draw what you get each time. One line: at which of the three could you get summation, and what that answer means for a chamber that has to refill between beats."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two panels on one time scale. Left, skeletal muscle: a train of stimuli, the brief action potentials, and the twitches fusing into tetanus. Right, cardiac muscle: the same stimulus train, the long action potentials, and what the twitches actually do. One line: name the single feature of the cardiac trace that makes the right panel look different, and say what a heart in tetanus could not do."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why the long refractory period of cardiac muscle prevents tetanus and why that matters for pump function."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cardiac refractory period. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-ecg-basics","name":"ECG waves and intervals","can":"Label the P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR interval, and QT interval and state the electrical event each represents.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Conduction through the atrioventricular node slows. Which ECG measurement lengthens?\nA) The QRS duration  |  B) The QT interval\nC) The PR interval  |  D) The T wave duration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The PR interval","why":"The PR interval contains the nodal delay, so anything that slows conduction between atria and ventricles stretches it while leaving the rest of the tracing untouched. QRS duration reflects how fast the ventricles depolarize once the impulse arrives, which is a His and Purkinje question, not a nodal one. The QT interval reflects ventricular depolarization and repolarization and is unaffected by how long the impulse waited at the node. The T wave reflects repolarization only."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug prolongs ventricular repolarization. Which ECG measurement lengthens?\nA) The P wave duration  |  B) The ST segment only, with no change in QT\nC) The PR interval  |  D) The QT interval\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The QT interval","why":"The QT interval covers depolarization through the end of repolarization, so a slower phase 3 pushes the end of the T wave later and lengthens QT. The PR interval measures conduction from atria to ventricles and is unchanged by a repolarization effect. P wave duration reflects atrial depolarization only. The ST segment may shift too, but the point of the QT interval is precisely that it captures repolarization, so it cannot stay constant while repolarization slows."},{"t":"mc","q":"Ventricular muscle mass increases substantially. What would you expect on the ECG?\nA) A shorter PR interval, because conduction distance is greater\nB) A smaller T wave, because repolarization requires less charge\nC) A taller P wave, because atrial pressure rises\nD) A taller QRS complex, because more muscle is depolarizing at once\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A taller QRS complex, because more muscle is depolarizing at once","why":"The size of a deflection reflects the amount of tissue depolarizing at that moment, so a thicker ventricular wall generates a larger voltage and a taller QRS. The P wave reflects atrial depolarization, which is not what has changed here. Greater distance would tend to lengthen conduction time, not shorten the PR interval, and the PR interval is dominated by the nodal delay in any case. More muscle repolarizing produces more voltage in the T wave, not less."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which phase of the ventricular action potential does the T wave correspond to?\nA) Phase 0, the upstroke  |  B) Phase 2, the plateau\nC) Phase 3, repolarization  |  D) Phase 4, the resting potential\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Phase 3, repolarization","why":"The T wave is the surface record of ventricular repolarization, which is phase 3, when K+ efflux is no longer balanced by inward Ca2+ current. Phase 0 in millions of cells at once produces the QRS complex. Phase 2 is the plateau, during which the ventricles are uniformly depolarized and the tracing is flat. Phase 4 is the interval between action potentials, which appears as the flat line from the end of the T wave to the next P wave."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which electrical event does the P wave represent?\nA) Atrial repolarization  |  B) Depolarization of the sinoatrial node itself\nC) Atrial contraction  |  D) Atrial depolarization\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Atrial depolarization","why":"The P wave is the voltage produced as depolarization sweeps across both atria, and atrial contraction follows it rather than being what the ECG records. An ECG shows electrical events only, so contraction is never what a wave represents. Atrial repolarization does happen, but it is small and hidden under the QRS complex. The sinoatrial node itself contains far too little tissue to produce a deflection at the body surface."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The T wave on the ECG represents repolarization of the atria.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The T wave represents repolarization of the ventricles. Atrial repolarization happens during the QRS complex and is hidden by it.","why":"Every wave on the ECG comes from a large mass of cells changing voltage at the same time. The atria are small, so their repolarization makes a tiny signal, and it happens while the huge ventricular depolarization signal is being written, so it disappears inside the QRS. The ventricles repolarize about 200 milliseconds after they depolarize, once the plateau ends, and that large signal is the T wave. A long QT interval therefore points at slow ventricular repolarization, not at anything atrial."},{"t":"draw","q":"Rule your own grid with small boxes of 0.04 s and draw a clean lead II tracing of two beats on it. Label the P wave, QRS complex and T wave, and beside each write the electrical event it is, not the mechanical one. Bracket the PR interval and the QT interval and write the normal duration of each. One line: name the electrical event that has no wave of its own, and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an ECG tracing across the top of your space, then under it on the same time axis draw the heart four times with the region that is depolarizing or repolarizing shaded: mid P, during QRS, during the ST segment, and during T. One line: say what the ventricles are doing electrically during the ST segment, and why that keeps the trace flat at baseline."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR interval, and QT interval and state the electrical event each represents."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ECG waves and intervals. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-ecg-interpretation","name":"ECG interpretation","can":"Calculate heart rate from an ECG strip and identify sinus rhythm, tachycardia, bradycardia, and a first degree or complete heart block.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An ECG shows 20 small boxes between consecutive R waves. What is the heart rate?\nA) 15 beats per minute  |  B) 60 beats per minute\nC) 75 beats per minute  |  D) 100 beats per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 75 beats per minute","why":"Dividing 1500 by 20 small boxes gives 75 beats per minute, and you can check it the long way: 20 boxes times 0.04 second is 0.80 second per beat, and 60 divided by 0.80 is 75. The value of 15 comes from applying the large box rule of 300 divided by boxes to small boxes instead. A rate of 60 would require 25 small boxes between beats. A rate of 100 would require 15 small boxes."},{"t":"mc","q":"An ECG shows exactly 5 large boxes between consecutive R waves. What is the heart rate?\nA) 50 beats per minute  |  B) 60 beats per minute\nC) 75 beats per minute  |  D) 300 beats per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 60 beats per minute","why":"Dividing 300 by 5 large boxes gives 60 beats per minute, which makes sense because five large boxes is exactly one second. A rate of 75 would correspond to 4 large boxes. A rate of 50 would correspond to 6 large boxes. The figure of 300 is the rate for a single large box between beats, which would be an extraordinarily fast rhythm."},{"t":"mc","q":"A six second rhythm strip contains 13 QRS complexes. What is the rate, and how is it classified?\nA) 130 beats per minute, which is normal\nB) 13 beats per minute, which is bradycardia\nC) 78 beats per minute, which is normal\nD) 130 beats per minute, which is tachycardia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 130 beats per minute, which is tachycardia","why":"Six seconds is a tenth of a minute, so 13 complexes times 10 gives 130 beats per minute, which is above the normal ceiling of 100 and therefore tachycardia. Multiplying by 6 instead of 10 gives 78 and treats the strip as ten seconds long. A rate of 130 cannot be called normal, since the normal range stops at 100. Reporting 13 forgets to scale the six second window up to a full minute."},{"t":"mc","q":"The interval between R waves on a strip is 1.2 seconds. What is the rate, and how is it classified?\nA) 50 beats per minute, which is bradycardia\nB) 72 beats per minute, which is normal\nC) 50 beats per minute, which is normal\nD) 120 beats per minute, which is tachycardia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 50 beats per minute, which is bradycardia","why":"Rate is 60 seconds divided by the cycle length, and 60 divided by 1.2 is 50 beats per minute, which falls below 60 and is therefore bradycardia. A rate of 72 would mean a cycle length of about 0.83 second. Calling 50 normal ignores the lower limit of the normal range. The figure of 120 comes from treating 1.2 as if it were the rate rather than the interval."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A lead II strip shows an upright P wave before every QRS complex. The R waves are 20 small boxes apart on every beat. The PR interval measures 0.26 second on every beat (normal 0.12 to 0.20).\nState the ventricular rate and explain what the PR interval tells you about conduction between atria and ventricles.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"75 beats per minute; every atrial impulse reaches the ventricles, but it is held in the atrioventricular node longer than normal (first degree block)","why":"Rate is 1500 divided by the small boxes between R waves, so 1500 divided by 20 is 75 beats per minute. A P wave before every QRS with a fixed PR interval means each atrial impulse is getting through to the ventricles. A PR interval above 0.20 second means the trip is taking too long, and since most of the PR interval is nodal delay, the slow step is the atrioventricular node. Every beat conducts, just late. That is a first degree block, a delay rather than a failure."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A strip with 15 small boxes between consecutive R waves, with an upright P wave before every QRS, shows a rate of 100 beats per minute at the upper limit of normal sinus rhythm.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"At standard paper speed, one minute is 1500 small boxes. Dividing 1500 by the number of small boxes between R waves gives the rate. Fifteen boxes is 0.60 second per beat, which is 100 beats per minute, right at the boundary between sinus rhythm and sinus tachycardia. The P wave in front of every QRS tells you the sinoatrial node is driving the ventricles. One box more (16) would give 94, one box fewer (14) would give 107 and be called sinus tachycardia."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four rhythm strips stacked on one ruled grid: normal sinus rhythm, sinus tachycardia, sinus bradycardia, and first degree block. Keep P, QRS and T in every one. Under each strip write the rate you get by counting boxes, 1500 divided by the small boxes between R waves, and the one feature that names it. One line: three of these are named by a rate and one by an interval, so say which and give the cutoff."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a complete heart block strip: P waves marching out at their own regular rate, QRS complexes marching out at a slower regular rate, no fixed relationship between them. Mark two places where a P falls inside a QRS. Beside the strip, draw where the conduction pathway is broken and which tissue is now pacing the ventricles. One line: say why the ventricular rate lands where it does and not somewhere else."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate heart rate from an ECG strip and identify sinus rhythm, tachycardia, bradycardia, and a first degree or complete heart block."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ECG interpretation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-lab-ecg","name":"ECG recording","can":"Record a lead II ECG, measure the intervals, and correlate each wave with the mechanical event that follows it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On your subject's lead II strip, the PR interval measures 4 small boxes. What is its duration and is it normal?\nA) 0.16 second, which is prolonged  |  B) 0.04 second, which is normal\nC) 0.40 second, which is prolonged  |  D) 0.16 second, which is normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 0.16 second, which is normal","why":"Four small boxes at 0.04 second each is 0.16 second, comfortably inside the normal PR range of 0.12 to 0.20 second. Calling 0.16 second prolonged applies the wrong cutoff, since prolongation begins above 0.20 second. Reporting 0.04 second uses the value of a single box instead of four. Reporting 0.40 second multiplies by 0.10 second per box, which is not the standard grid value."},{"t":"mc","q":"You measure a QRS complex spanning 2 small boxes. What is its duration and what does it tell you?\nA) 0.08 second, indicating that ventricular activation is abnormally slow\nB) 0.20 second, indicating a bundle branch block is present\nC) 0.02 second, indicating an unusually fast ventricular rate\nD) 0.08 second, indicating normal ventricular conduction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 0.08 second, indicating normal ventricular conduction","why":"Two small boxes is 0.08 second, which is under the 0.12 second ceiling, so the impulse is reaching the ventricular muscle through the fast bundle branch and Purkinje network as it should. A duration under the normal limit cannot indicate slow activation. Reading 2 boxes as 0.20 second uses 0.10 second per box, which is not the standard grid value. QRS duration measures how long ventricular depolarization takes, not how often it happens, so it says nothing about rate."},{"t":"mc","q":"You measure the QT interval as 9 small boxes. What is its duration?\nA) 0.09 second  |  B) 0.18 second\nC) 0.36 second  |  D) 0.45 second\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 0.36 second","why":"Nine small boxes at 0.04 second each is 0.36 second, a normal QT interval at an ordinary resting rate. Reporting 0.09 second treats each box as 0.01 second. Reporting 0.18 second uses 0.02 second per box. Reporting 0.45 second uses 0.05 second per box, which is close to the correct value but not the standard grid."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a simultaneous ECG and heart sound recording, when does the first heart sound occur?\nA) At the peak of the T wave, as the ventricles begin to relax\nB) Midway between the T wave and the next P wave\nC) Just after the P wave, as the atria begin to contract\nD) Just after the QRS complex, as the atrioventricular valves close\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Just after the QRS complex, as the atrioventricular valves close","why":"The first heart sound is the atrioventricular valves closing, which happens as soon as ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, a few tens of milliseconds after the QRS depolarizes the ventricles. Atrial contraction after the P wave is quiet in a healthy heart. Valve closure at the end of the T wave produces the second heart sound, not the first. The gap between the T wave and the next P wave is diastole, when both valve sets are quiet."},{"t":"mc","q":"On the same recording, when does the second heart sound occur?\nA) At the start of the QRS complex, as the ventricles depolarize\nB) In the middle of the PR interval, as the impulse crosses the atrioventricular node\nC) At the end of the T wave, as the semilunar valves close\nD) Just before the P wave, as the ventricles finish filling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At the end of the T wave, as the semilunar valves close","why":"The second heart sound is aortic and pulmonary valve closure, which happens when ventricular pressure falls below arterial pressure at the end of ejection, and that coincides closely with the end of the T wave. The QRS marks the start of ventricular systole, which is where the first heart sound belongs. Nodal conduction during the PR interval is silent, both electrically at the surface and mechanically. The interval before the P wave is late diastole, when the ventricles are filling quietly."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"On a lead II recording, the QRS complex is a direct record of the ventricular muscle shortening.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The QRS complex records ventricular depolarization, an electrical event. The mechanical contraction follows it by a few tens of milliseconds and is not recorded by the ECG at all.","why":"The ECG picks up voltage changes at the skin, so it only sees electrical events. Depolarization triggers Ca2+ entry, Ca2+ release, and cross bridge cycling, and those steps take time, so tension develops after the QRS is finished. The same is true for every wave: atrial contraction follows the P wave, and ventricular relaxation follows the T wave. A heart can show a normal QRS and still contract poorly, which is why the ECG alone cannot tell you about pump function."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, describe what you would measure on a lead II ECG strip from a resting subject, and for the P wave, QRS complex, and T wave state the mechanical event that follows each one and why it follows rather than coincides.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"measure the R to R spacing and compute rate as 1500 divided by small boxes (or 300 divided by large boxes); measure the PR interval (normal 0.12 to 0.20 second), the QRS duration (under 0.12 second), and the QT interval (about 0.36 to 0.44 second); P wave is atrial depolarization; atrial contraction follows and finishes ventricular filling; QRS is ventricular depolarization; ventricular contraction, atrioventricular valve closure, and the first sound follow; T wave is ventricular repolarization; ventricular relaxation, semilunar valve closure, and the second sound follow; the ECG records electrical events only; excitation contraction coupling takes time, so each mechanical event lags its wave","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you record anything, draw the lead II tracing you expect from your own resting heart, with the electrode placement sketched beside it. Record, then draw the real tracing directly under your prediction at the same scale, with your measured PR, QRS and QT written on it. One line: say where prediction and recording disagreed, and whether the cause was your heart, your technique, or the equipment."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw your recorded lead II strip across the page. Under it, on the same time axis, draw a row of small panels for the mechanical event that follows each wave: atrial contraction after P, ventricular contraction beginning after QRS, ventricular relaxation after T. Put S1 and S2 on the same axis. One line: every mechanical mark sits to the right of its electrical trigger, so say what fills that gap."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ECG recording. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Cardiac Mechanics","comps":[{"id":"w11-cardiac-cycle","name":"Cardiac cycle","can":"Order the phases of the cardiac cycle and state the pressure relationship that opens and closes the atrioventricular and semilunar valves in each phase.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Left ventricular pressure is 15 mmHg and rising, left atrial pressure is 8 mmHg, and aortic pressure is 80 mmHg. Which phase is this?\nA) Rapid ventricular filling  |  B) Isovolumetric contraction\nC) Atrial systole  |  D) Ventricular ejection\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Isovolumetric contraction","why":"Compare each valve separately. The ventricle at 15 mmHg beats the atrium at 8 mmHg, so the mitral valve is shut, and the ventricle is far below the aorta at 80 mmHg, so the aortic valve is also shut. Both valves closed with pressure climbing is isovolumetric contraction. Filling and atrial systole both require the atrium to exceed the ventricle, and ejection requires the ventricle to exceed the aorta."},{"t":"mc","q":"Left atrial pressure is 10 mmHg and left ventricular pressure is 4 mmHg. What is the state of the mitral valve and the ventricular volume?\nA) Closed, with volume constant  |  B) Open, with volume decreasing\nC) Closed, with volume decreasing  |  D) Open, with volume increasing\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Open, with volume increasing","why":"The atrium exceeds the ventricle by 6 mmHg, so the mitral valve is held open and blood runs down the gradient into the ventricle, which means volume is rising. Closed with constant volume would describe either isovolumetric phase, but those require the ventricle to hold the higher pressure. Volume can only decrease during ejection, which needs an open semilunar valve, and an open mitral valve with falling volume would mean blood was running backward into the atrium."},{"t":"mc","q":"Heart rate climbs from 70 to 160 beats per minute. Which part of the cardiac cycle is shortened the most?\nA) Isovolumetric contraction  |  B) Ventricular ejection\nC) Ventricular filling  |  D) Isovolumetric relaxation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Ventricular filling","why":"Systole is relatively protected as rate climbs because contraction and relaxation are driven by fixed calcium handling steps, so most of the lost time comes out of diastole, and filling is by far the largest piece of diastole. Isovolumetric contraction and relaxation are already only a few hundredths of a second each and have little to give up. Ejection shortens somewhat but far less than filling, which is why very fast rates lower end diastolic volume."},{"t":"mc","q":"The dicrotic notch on an aortic pressure tracing marks which event?\nA) Closure of the mitral valve  |  B) Opening of the aortic valve\nC) Closure of the aortic valve  |  D) Opening of the mitral valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Closure of the aortic valve","why":"As the ventricle relaxes, a brief backflow toward the ventricle dips aortic pressure, then the aortic cusps catch that flow and slam shut, producing the small rebound notch. Mitral closure happens at the start of systole and is not visible on an aortic tracing. Aortic valve opening produces the sharp upstroke at the beginning of ejection, not a notch on the downslope. Mitral opening occurs later, once ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient's atria have stopped contracting in a coordinated way, so there is no atrial kick. At rest her heart rate is 72 beats per minute and her left ventricular end diastolic volume is 115 mL (previously 130 mL). She then climbs three flights of stairs and her rate reaches 150 beats per minute.\nPredict how the loss of atrial contraction affects end diastolic volume at rest compared with during the stair climb, and explain why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The loss matters more at 150 beats per minute, because the short diastole cuts passive filling and the ventricle depends more on the atrial kick to finish filling","why":"At a resting rate diastole is long, about two thirds of the cycle, and passive filling has time to bring the ventricle close to its full volume before the atria contract. The atrial kick adds only the last 20 to 30 percent, which is why her end diastolic volume fell modestly, from 130 to 115 mL. At 150 beats per minute the cycle is 0.40 second and most of the lost time comes out of diastole, so passive filling is cut short. In a normal heart the atrial kick then becomes a much larger fraction of filling. Without it, end diastolic volume and stroke volume fall much more during exercise than at rest."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, walk through one left ventricular cardiac cycle starting with atrial contraction. For each phase, state which valves are open and the pressure comparison that opens or closes them.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"atrial contraction: mitral valve open, aortic valve closed; atrial pressure above ventricular pressure pushes in the last 20 to 30 percent of filling; isovolumetric contraction: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, mitral valve closes (first sound); both valves closed and volume does not change; ejection: ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure (about 80 mmHg), aortic valve opens, blood leaves; isovolumetric relaxation: ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure, aortic valve closes (second sound); both valves closed and volume does not change; ventricular filling: ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure, mitral valve opens, passive filling supplies most of end diastolic volume; valves are passive and open or close only by the pressure difference across them","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis for a single beat and stack three pressure curves on it: left ventricular, aortic, and left atrial. Mark the four moments a valve moves, and at each one write the pressure inequality that caused it, which chamber is higher than which. Add ventricular volume as a fourth curve underneath. One line: name the two phases in which all four valves are shut, and say what the volume is doing then."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the cardiac cycle as five panels in a row: atrial systole, isovolumetric contraction, ventricular ejection, isovolumetric relaxation, ventricular filling. In each panel draw the heart with every valve shown open or shut and an arrow for where blood is moving. Under each panel write which pressure exceeds which. One line: say which comes first, the pressure change or the valve movement, and point to the panel that proves it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the phases of the cardiac cycle and state the pressure relationship that opens and closes the atrioventricular and semilunar valves in each phase."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cardiac cycle. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-pv-loop","name":"Pressure volume loop","can":"Label the four phases of a ventricular pressure volume loop and predict how the loop changes with increased preload, increased afterload, or increased contractility.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Only preload is increased. Which corner of the loop moves first?\nA) The upper left corner, at end systolic volume\nB) The lower left corner, where the mitral valve opens\nC) The lower right corner, at end diastolic volume\nD) The top of the ejection segment, at peak pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lower right corner, at end diastolic volume","why":"Preload is defined by how full the ventricle is when contraction begins, so the first thing to move is the point where filling ends and the mitral valve closes, the lower right corner. The upper left corner is set mainly by contractility and afterload and shifts little with pure preload change. The lower left corner is where the previous beat ended. Peak ejection pressure follows from the arterial load rather than from filling volume alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two loops from the same ventricle share the same end diastolic volume, but one turns and opens the aortic valve at 80 mmHg and the other at 120 mmHg. Which variable differs?\nA) Preload  |  B) Afterload\nC) Contractility  |  D) Ventricular compliance\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Afterload","why":"The pressure at which the loop turns from the vertical limb into ejection is the arterial pressure the ventricle must overcome, which is afterload, so a 40 mmHg difference in that threshold is an afterload difference. Preload is identical here, because both loops share an end diastolic volume. Contractility would show as a difference in where ejection stops rather than where it starts. Compliance would show as a different height of the filling curve along the bottom."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a ventricular pressure volume loop, what does the width of the loop measure?\nA) Stroke volume  |  B) End diastolic volume\nC) End systolic volume  |  D) Stroke work\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Stroke volume","why":"The loop runs from end diastolic volume on the right to end systolic volume on the left, so the horizontal distance between those corners is exactly the volume ejected, which is stroke volume. End diastolic volume is the position of the right edge, not the width, and end systolic volume is the position of the left edge. Stroke work is the area enclosed by the loop, which depends on both width and height."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which segment of the pressure volume loop is vertical with pressure rising and volume unchanged?\nA) Ventricular filling  |  B) Ejection\nC) Isovolumetric contraction  |  D) Isovolumetric relaxation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Isovolumetric contraction","why":"A vertical segment means volume is fixed, which happens only when all valves are shut, and rising pressure identifies the contracting side of the loop. Isovolumetric relaxation is also vertical but pressure is falling there, so it is the descending limb on the left. Filling is the roughly horizontal bottom of the loop where volume rises at low pressure. Ejection is the top of the loop where volume falls."},{"t":"mc","q":"The lower right corner of the pressure volume loop marks which event?\nA) Aortic valve closure at end systolic volume\nB) Aortic valve opening at end diastolic volume\nC) Mitral valve closure at end diastolic volume\nD) Mitral valve opening at end systolic volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Mitral valve closure at end diastolic volume","why":"Filling runs rightward along the bottom of the loop and stops at the largest volume of the cycle, where the ventricle starts to contract and the mitral valve shuts, so that corner is end diastolic volume. Aortic valve closure sits at the upper left corner at end systolic volume. Aortic valve opening is at the top of the vertical contraction limb, at end diastolic volume but at high pressure. Mitral valve opening is the lower left corner where filling begins."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient is given a drug that dilates the veins and pools blood in the venous system. Before the drug, the left ventricular pressure volume loop ran from an end diastolic volume of 130 mL to an end systolic volume of 50 mL. After the drug, end diastolic volume is 105 mL and end systolic volume is still about 50 mL. Aortic pressure and heart rate are unchanged.\nDescribe the change in the loop and calculate the change in stroke volume.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The right edge of the loop moves left and the loop narrows; stroke volume falls from 80 to 55 mL, because preload fell and contractility did not change","why":"Venous dilation lowers venous return, so the ventricle fills less and the loop starts contracting from a smaller volume. That is a preload change, and on the loop it shows as the right edge (end diastolic volume) moving leftward. The ventricle still empties to the same end systolic volume because contractility and afterload are unchanged, so the left edge stays put and the loop gets narrower. Stroke volume before was 130 minus 50, or 80 mL. After it is 105 minus 50, or 55 mL."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a left ventricular pressure volume loop, volume on the x axis running from about 50 to 120 mL, pressure in mmHg on the y. Travel around it in the correct direction, label the four phases, and label the four corners where a valve opens or shuts. Mark EDV, ESV and stroke volume on the volume axis. One line: two sides of your loop are isovolumetric, so say how you can spot them by eye alone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the normal loop once, then draw three altered loops over it in different colors: increased preload, increased afterload, and increased contractility. For each, show what happened to the width of the loop and to ESV, and label the corner that moved. One line: only two of the three raised stroke volume, so name the one that did not and say what it raised instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the four phases of a ventricular pressure volume loop and predict how the loop changes with increased preload, increased afterload, or increased contractility."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pressure volume loop. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-heart-sounds","name":"Heart sounds","can":"Explain the origin of the first and second heart sounds and relate a murmur to valve stenosis or regurgitation by its timing.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A murmur is heard clearly between the first and second heart sounds. Which pair of lesions could produce it?\nA) Aortic regurgitation or mitral stenosis\nB) Aortic stenosis or mitral regurgitation\nC) Aortic stenosis or mitral stenosis\nD) Aortic regurgitation or mitral regurgitation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Aortic stenosis or mitral regurgitation","why":"During systole the semilunar valves are open and carrying flow, so narrowing there is noisy, while the AV valves are shut, so leaking there is noisy. That gives aortic stenosis and mitral regurgitation. Aortic regurgitation and mitral stenosis are both diastolic for the mirror image reason. The mixed options each pair one systolic lesion with one diastolic lesion, so they cannot both fall in the same window."},{"t":"mc","q":"A murmur begins right after the second heart sound and fades before the next first sound. Which lesion fits?\nA) Aortic stenosis  |  B) Mitral regurgitation\nC) Aortic regurgitation  |  D) Pulmonic stenosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Aortic regurgitation","why":"The window after the second sound is diastole, when the aortic valve should be sealed, so blood running back from the aorta into the relaxing ventricle produces exactly this decreasing murmur. Aortic stenosis and pulmonic stenosis both make noise while their valves carry ejected blood, so both are systolic. Mitral regurgitation is also systolic, because the mitral valve is shut only while the ventricle contracts."},{"t":"mc","q":"What produces the first heart sound?\nA) Closure of the atrioventricular valves\nB) Closure of the semilunar valves\nC) Opening of the semilunar valves\nD) Contraction of the atrial muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Closure of the atrioventricular valves","why":"As the ventricles begin to contract, ventricular pressure passes atrial pressure, the mitral and tricuspid valves shut, and the vibration of the sealed valve and the blood behind it makes the first sound. Semilunar closure makes the second sound at the end of ejection. Valve opening is normally silent because it does not create the abrupt tensioning that generates sound. Atrial contraction is also normally silent in a compliant ventricle."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a low pitched rumbling murmur that begins after the second heart sound and continues until just before the next first sound. Catheter measurements during diastole show a left atrial pressure of 24 mmHg (normal 4 to 12) and a left ventricular pressure of 6 mmHg (normal 4 to 12).\nWhat is producing the sound, and what is happening to left ventricular filling?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Blood is being forced through a narrowed mitral valve during diastole; the 18 mmHg gradient drives turbulent flow and the ventricle fills slowly and incompletely","why":"The timing places the murmur in diastole, when the only valve that should have flow through it on the left side is the mitral valve. In a healthy heart, atrial and ventricular pressures are nearly equal during filling because the open mitral valve offers almost no resistance. Here the atrium is at 24 mmHg while the ventricle sits at 6 mmHg, so there is an 18 mmHg drop across a valve that should be wide open. That gradient means the orifice is narrowed. Blood squeezing through a small opening is turbulent, which is the rumble, and the ventricle fills slowly, so end diastolic volume and stroke volume are reduced."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A murmur heard between the first and second heart sounds could be caused by a narrowed mitral valve.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A murmur between the first and second sounds is systolic, when the mitral valve should be closed, so it comes from a mitral valve that leaks (regurgitation) or an aortic valve that is narrowed (stenosis). A narrowed mitral valve makes noise during diastole, after the second sound, when blood is flowing through it.","why":"A murmur is turbulent flow. To place a murmur, ask which valves have blood moving through them at that moment. Between the first and second sounds the ventricle is ejecting, so the aortic valve is carrying flow and the mitral valve is supposed to be sealed. Turbulence then means either flow through a narrowed aortic valve or backward flow through a mitral valve that fails to seal. A narrowed mitral valve can only make noise when blood is trying to cross it, which is during ventricular filling."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what produces the first and second heart sounds, then explain how you would use the timing of a murmur (before or after the second sound) to decide whether a given valve is narrowed or leaking.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the first sound is the atrioventricular valves closing when ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure at the start of systole; the second sound is the semilunar valves closing when ventricular pressure falls below arterial pressure at the start of diastole; a murmur is turbulent flow through a valve that is either too narrow (stenosis) or does not seal (regurgitation); a systolic murmur (between the first and second sounds) means aortic or pulmonary stenosis, or mitral or tricuspid regurgitation; a diastolic murmur (after the second sound) means mitral or tricuspid stenosis, or aortic or pulmonary regurgitation; the rule: ask which valves should be open and which should be closed in that phase; noise from an open valve is stenosis, noise from a closed valve is regurgitation","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one cardiac cycle with ventricular and aortic pressure curves on a single time axis. Mark S1 and S2 exactly where they fall and run an arrow from each to the valve closure that made it. Bracket systole on the axis, between the two sounds. One line: the sound comes from closing and not from opening, so say what is actually vibrating to make it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four timing diagrams in a row, each with S1 and S2 marked and the murmur shaded in where it falls: aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation. Label each as systolic or diastolic. One line: write, in your own words, the rule that takes you from timing plus valve to stenosis or regurgitation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain the origin of the first and second heart sounds and relate a murmur to valve stenosis or regurgitation by its timing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Heart sounds. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-stroke-volume","name":"Stroke volume and ejection fraction","can":"Calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction from end diastolic and end systolic volumes and interpret a reduced ejection fraction.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A dilated ventricle has an end diastolic volume of 250 mL and an ejection fraction of 28 percent. What are its stroke volume and end systolic volume?\nA) 70 mL and 180 mL  |  B) 180 mL and 70 mL\nC) 28 mL and 222 mL  |  D) 70 mL and 250 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 70 mL and 180 mL","why":"Twenty eight percent of 250 mL is 70 mL ejected, and 250 minus 70 leaves 180 mL behind, so the stroke volume is normal even though the ejection fraction is severely low, because the ventricle enlarged to compensate. Reversing to 180 and 70 assigns the retained volume to ejection. Treating 28 percent as 28 mL confuses a fraction with a volume. Listing 250 mL as end systolic volume ignores the 70 mL that left."},{"t":"mc","q":"End diastolic volume is 130 mL and end systolic volume is 60 mL. What is the stroke volume?\nA) 190 mL  |  B) 60 mL\nC) 70 mL  |  D) 130 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 70 mL","why":"Stroke volume is 130 minus 60, which is 70 mL. The value 190 mL comes from adding the two volumes rather than subtracting. The value 60 mL is the end systolic volume itself, the blood that stayed behind. The value 130 mL is the end diastolic volume, which the ventricle held before it contracted."},{"t":"mc","q":"A ventricle with an end diastolic volume of 160 mL ejects 80 mL. What are its end systolic volume and ejection fraction?\nA) 240 mL and 50 percent  |  B) 80 mL and 50 percent\nC) 80 mL and 100 percent  |  D) 160 mL and 50 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 80 mL and 50 percent","why":"If 80 mL of a 160 mL filling volume left the chamber, 80 mL remains, so end systolic volume is 80 mL and the ejection fraction is 80 divided by 160, or 50 percent. The value 240 mL comes from adding the ejected volume to the filled volume, which double counts. Calling it 100 percent divides stroke volume by end systolic volume rather than end diastolic volume. Reporting 160 mL as end systolic volume ignores that any blood left at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"A ventricle has an end diastolic volume of 120 mL and an ejection fraction of 60 percent. What are its stroke volume and end systolic volume?\nA) 72 mL and 48 mL  |  B) 60 mL and 60 mL\nC) 48 mL and 72 mL  |  D) 72 mL and 120 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 72 mL and 48 mL","why":"Sixty percent of 120 mL is 72 mL ejected, and the remaining forty percent, 48 mL, stays behind as end systolic volume. Splitting the volume evenly into 60 and 60 would require an ejection fraction of exactly 50 percent. Reversing the numbers to 48 and 72 assigns the ejected share to the leftover. Listing 120 mL as end systolic volume would mean nothing was ejected at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"How is stroke volume calculated?\nA) End diastolic volume minus end systolic volume\nB) End systolic volume minus end diastolic volume\nC) End diastolic volume divided by end systolic volume\nD) End diastolic volume plus end systolic volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) End diastolic volume minus end systolic volume","why":"Stroke volume is simply how much smaller the ventricle got during ejection, so it is the volume at the end of filling minus the volume left at the end of contraction. Reversing the subtraction gives a negative number, which no ejected volume can be. Dividing the two gives a unitless ratio rather than a volume. Adding them describes no physiological quantity at all."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: An echocardiogram gives a left ventricular end diastolic volume of 180 mL (normal about 100 to 140) and an end systolic volume of 120 mL (normal about 40 to 60). Resting heart rate is 80 beats per minute.\nCalculate stroke volume and ejection fraction, and explain what the pair of numbers says about this ventricle.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Stroke volume 60 mL, ejection fraction 33 percent; the ventricle is weak and has dilated, so it maintains a near normal stroke volume only by starting from a much larger volume","why":"Stroke volume is 180 minus 120, which is 60 mL, a value near the low end of normal. Ejection fraction is 60 divided by 180, which is 0.33, far below the normal 55 to 70 percent. The two numbers together tell the story. This ventricle ejects only a third of what it holds, so contractility is poor. It still puts out about 60 mL per beat because it has stretched to hold 180 mL, and the Frank Starling stretch on those enlarged fibers partly makes up for the weak contraction. Stroke volume alone would hide the problem; ejection fraction exposes it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a ventricle three times in a row: at end diastole, at end systole, and with the difference between the two shaded. Put real numbers on the drawing, EDV about 120 mL and ESV about 50 mL, and work stroke volume and ejection fraction out beside it with the arithmetic showing. One line: redraw or describe what an ejection fraction of 30 percent would do to those same three pictures."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two patients side by side, both with a stroke volume of 55 mL. Give the first an EDV of 110 mL and the second an EDV of 180 mL, and draw the two ventricles at those relative sizes. Compute each ejection fraction on the page. One line: the stroke volumes are identical, so say which heart you would rather have and what the ratio caught that the volume missed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction from end diastolic and end systolic volumes and interpret a reduced ejection fraction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Stroke volume and ejection fraction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-cardiac-output","name":"Cardiac output","can":"Calculate cardiac output and cardiac reserve and predict the effect of a change in heart rate or stroke volume on each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Heart rate is 72 beats per minute and stroke volume is 70 mL. What is the cardiac output?\nA) About 0.5 L/min  |  B) About 5.0 L/min\nC) About 50 L/min  |  D) About 1.0 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 5.0 L/min","why":"Multiplying 72 by 70 mL gives 5040 mL/min, which is 5.04 L/min once you divide by 1000. The other three answers each miss by a factor of ten or more, which usually comes from mishandling the conversion between milliliters and liters. Checking that the result lands near the familiar resting value of 5 L/min is a quick way to catch that error."},{"t":"mc","q":"During exercise, heart rate is 150 beats per minute and stroke volume is 100 mL. What is the cardiac output?\nA) 1.5 L/min  |  B) 25 L/min\nC) 150 L/min  |  D) 15 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 15 L/min","why":"Multiplying 150 by 100 mL gives 15000 mL/min, which is 15 L/min, about three times the resting value and a reasonable moderate exercise number. The value 1.5 L/min is off by a factor of ten and would be incompatible with life during exercise. The value 25 L/min comes from adding the two figures. The value 150 L/min skips the milliliter to liter conversion entirely."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cardiac output is 6.0 L/min and heart rate is 75 beats per minute. What is the stroke volume?\nA) 45 mL  |  B) 450 mL\nC) 125 mL  |  D) 80 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 80 mL","why":"Rearranging the equation, stroke volume is cardiac output divided by heart rate, so 6000 mL/min divided by 75 beats per minute is 80 mL per beat. The value 125 mL comes from dividing 75 into 1000 by mistake rather than into the full output. The values 45 mL and 450 mL do not follow from either operation and are off by a factor of ten from each other, which is a sign that the unit conversion went astray."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student has a resting cardiac output of 5 L/min and a maximum cardiac output of 25 L/min. What is the cardiac reserve?\nA) 5 L/min  |  B) 25 L/min\nC) 20 L/min  |  D) 30 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 20 L/min","why":"Cardiac reserve is the maximum minus the resting output, so 25 minus 5 is 20 L/min of extra flow available on demand. The value 25 L/min is the maximum itself, which counts the resting output as though it were reserve. The value 5 L/min is the resting output. The value 30 L/min adds the two rather than subtracting."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient's atrioventricular node has stopped conducting, and a ventricular escape pacemaker is driving the ventricles at 38 beats per minute (normal 60 to 100). An echocardiogram shows a stroke volume of 95 mL (normal about 70 mL).\nCalculate the cardiac output, and explain why the stroke volume is above normal even though the ventricle has not become stronger.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 3.6 L/min; the long diastole lets the ventricle fill to a larger end diastolic volume, and the extra stretch raises stroke volume by the Frank Starling relationship, but not enough to offset the slow rate","why":"Cardiac output is 38 beats per minute times 95 mL, which is 3610 mL/min, or about 3.6 L/min, well below a normal resting 5 L/min. At 38 beats per minute each cycle lasts about 1.6 seconds, and almost all of that extra time is diastole. The ventricle fills to a larger end diastolic volume, the fibers are stretched more, and they contract harder, so stroke volume rises from about 70 to 95 mL. That is the Frank Starling mechanism doing what it can. The gain in stroke volume (about 35 percent) is much smaller than the loss in rate (about 50 percent), so output still falls."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During exercise, cardiac output rises only because heart rate rises; stroke volume stays at its resting value.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. During exercise both heart rate and stroke volume rise. Sympathetic input raises contractility and the muscle pump raises venous return, so stroke volume climbs (often from about 70 mL to 100 mL or more) before very high rates begin to limit filling.","why":"Cardiac output is rate times stroke volume, and exercise pushes on both. Norepinephrine and epinephrine raise contractility, so the ventricle empties further and end systolic volume falls. Contracting skeletal muscle and deeper breathing squeeze more blood back to the heart, so end diastolic volume is maintained or rises even though diastole is shorter. The result is a stroke volume well above resting. Only at rates near maximum does filling time get short enough for stroke volume to plateau or fall."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the cardiac output equation as a picture rather than algebra: a heart with a rate written on it, a stroke volume drawn as a shaded slug of blood, and the product leaving as a labeled flow per minute. Use 70 beats per minute and 70 mL and show the arithmetic to about 5 L/min. Draw the same picture again at maximum exercise and label the gap between them as cardiac reserve. One line: which of the two variables has more room to grow."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a row of three small graphs, cardiac output on the y axis of each. First, output against heart rate with stroke volume held fixed. Second, output against stroke volume with rate fixed. Third, output against heart rate again, but let the axis run past 180 beats per minute and draw what the curve really does up there. One line: name what falls at very high rates and which part of the cycle gets squeezed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate cardiac output and cardiac reserve and predict the effect of a change in heart rate or stroke volume on each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cardiac output. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-frank-starling","name":"Frank Starling relationship","can":"State the Frank Starling law and explain how venous return and end diastolic volume determine the force of the next contraction.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Venous return increases while heart rate and contractility are held constant. Predict end diastolic volume and stroke volume.\nA) End diastolic volume rises and stroke volume rises\nB) End diastolic volume rises and stroke volume falls\nC) End diastolic volume falls and stroke volume rises\nD) Both are unchanged, because contractility did not change\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) End diastolic volume rises and stroke volume rises","why":"More blood returning fills the ventricle further, and the extra stretch produces a stronger contraction, so both quantities rise together. Stroke volume cannot fall on greater filling unless something also opposed ejection. End diastolic volume cannot fall when more blood is arriving. Contractility is not the only route to a larger stroke volume, and treating it as such is exactly the confusion the Frank Starling mechanism resolves."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student stands up quickly and blood pools in the leg veins. Before any reflex responds, what happens to stroke volume?\nA) It rises, because gravity assists ejection\nB) It is unchanged, because total blood volume did not change\nC) It falls, because less blood returns to fill the ventricle\nD) It rises, because heart rate increases immediately\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It falls, because less blood returns to fill the ventricle","why":"Pooling traps blood in the compliant leg veins, so venous return drops, end diastolic volume falls, and the less stretched ventricle ejects less on the next beat. Gravity does not assist ejection from the left ventricle in any meaningful way. Total blood volume is indeed unchanged, but its distribution is what matters for filling, and that is the point students often miss. The reflex rise in heart rate comes afterward and is a response to the fall in output rather than a cause of a rise."},{"t":"mc","q":"The heart rate slows from 80 to 55 beats per minute with venous return unchanged. Predict end diastolic volume and stroke volume.\nA) Both rise, because longer diastole allows more filling\nB) Both fall, because fewer beats deliver less blood\nC) End diastolic volume rises but stroke volume falls\nD) Both are unchanged, because venous return did not change\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Both rise, because longer diastole allows more filling","why":"A slower rate lengthens diastole, the AV valve stays open longer, more blood accumulates in the ventricle, and the extra stretch produces a bigger ejection. Fewer beats per minute does lower cardiac output if stroke volume did not compensate, but that is a per minute statement rather than a per beat one. Stroke volume cannot fall while stretch increases, unless afterload also rose. Unchanged venous return per minute is precisely why the extra time concentrates more blood into each beat."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A healthy volunteer lying flat is tilted so her legs are raised above her heart. Over the next several beats, right ventricular stroke volume rises from 70 to 80 mL. Her heart rate and autonomic output do not change during the first few beats.\nPredict what happens to left ventricular stroke volume over the next several beats, and explain the mechanism that links the two ventricles.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Left ventricular stroke volume rises to match about 80 mL, because the extra blood the right ventricle pumps into the lungs returns to the left ventricle, raises its end diastolic volume, and the extra stretch increases its force of contraction","why":"Raising the legs pushes pooled venous blood back to the right heart, so the right ventricle fills more and by the Frank Starling law ejects more. That extra 10 mL per beat goes into the pulmonary circulation and, a few beats later, arrives at the left atrium and left ventricle. The left ventricle now fills more, stretches more, and contracts harder, so its stroke volume climbs until it matches the right side. No nerve is needed, because the response is built into the muscle. This is how the two pumps in series stay balanced; without it, blood would pile up in the lungs or in the systemic veins within minutes."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Within physiological limits, an increase in venous return raises the stroke volume of the next beat even with no change in autonomic input to the heart.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Cardiac muscle is length sensitive. A longer starting length improves cross bridge overlap and makes the fibers more sensitive to Ca2+, so a fuller ventricle contracts harder without any nerve or hormone involved. This is how the two sides of the heart keep their outputs matched: whatever volume the right side sends into the lungs arrives at the left side, stretches it, and gets ejected. A denervated transplanted heart still shows this response, which is the proof it does not need the autonomic system."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, a person lying flat suddenly stands up, and then a few minutes later lies down again. Explain, using the Frank Starling law, what happens to left ventricular stroke volume in each case and why no nerves are needed for the change.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"standing pools blood in the leg veins, so venous return falls; less venous return means a smaller end diastolic volume and less stretch on the ventricular fibers; less stretch means a weaker contraction and a smaller stroke volume on the next beat; lying down returns the pooled blood, raises end diastolic volume, and stroke volume rises again; the Frank Starling law: force of contraction is proportional to the stretch of the muscle at the end of diastole, within limits; the response is a property of the muscle itself (length dependent Ca2+ sensitivity and overlap), so it happens in a denervated heart","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the Frank Starling curve, end diastolic volume on the x axis and stroke volume on the y. Put three dots on it: at rest, after a rapid fluid infusion, and after standing still for ten minutes in the heat. Beside each dot, draw a sarcomere at that length with the thick and thin filament overlap shown. One line: say what the sarcomere drawings explain that the curve on its own does not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a closed loop: venous return feeding end diastolic volume, feeding force of contraction, feeding stroke volume, feeding back into what the veins deliver. Now run one beat in which the right ventricle ejects more than the left, and draw where blood piles up over the next two or three beats. One line: say what this loop guarantees about the two sides of the heart with no nerve or hormone involved."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the Frank Starling law and explain how venous return and end diastolic volume determine the force of the next contraction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Frank Starling relationship. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-preload-afterload","name":"Preload afterload and contractility","can":"Distinguish preload, afterload, and contractility and predict how a change in each alters stroke volume.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Preload increases while afterload and contractility are held constant. What happens to stroke volume?\nA) It rises, because greater stretch produces a stronger contraction\nB) It falls, because the ventricle is overfilled\nC) It is unchanged, because contractility did not change\nD) It rises, because afterload falls when the ventricle is fuller\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It rises, because greater stretch produces a stronger contraction","why":"More filling stretches the sarcomeres closer to their optimal length and raises troponin C sensitivity to Ca2+, so the ventricle ejects more without any change in its contractile state. Overfilling only reduces output in a failing or extremely dilated ventricle, not within the normal working range. Assuming stroke volume can only rise if contractility rises is the error this card targets. Afterload was specified as constant, so it cannot be the explanation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Afterload increases while preload and contractility are held constant. What happens to stroke volume and end systolic volume on that beat?\nA) Stroke volume rises and end systolic volume falls  |  B) Both fall\nC) Stroke volume falls and end systolic volume rises  |  D) Both rise\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Stroke volume falls and end systolic volume rises","why":"A higher pressure to eject against means the ventricle shortens less before the valve closes, so less blood leaves and more stays behind. Since preload is fixed, whatever is not ejected must remain, so the two move in opposite directions. Both falling would require blood to disappear from the chamber, and both rising would require more blood to be present than the fixed end diastolic volume allows. A rise in stroke volume with a smaller residual is the contractility response, which is the confusion worth guarding against here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Contractility increases while preload and afterload are held constant. What happens to stroke volume and end systolic volume?\nA) Stroke volume rises and end systolic volume rises\nB) Stroke volume rises and end diastolic volume rises\nC) Stroke volume is unchanged and end systolic volume falls\nD) Stroke volume rises and end systolic volume falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Stroke volume rises and end systolic volume falls","why":"A stronger beat empties the ventricle further at the same load, so less blood remains and more is ejected, which is the fingerprint that separates a contractility change from a preload change. End systolic volume cannot rise when the muscle contracts more forcefully at the same load. End diastolic volume was held constant, so it cannot be the source of the extra output. Stroke volume must change if end systolic volume falls at a fixed end diastolic volume, since the two differ by exactly that amount."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient receives a rapid intravenous infusion of saline. Which variable rises first?\nA) Contractility  |  B) Ejection fraction\nC) Afterload  |  D) Preload\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Preload","why":"Extra fluid expands blood volume, raises venous return, and fills the ventricle more, which is a preload increase. Contractility is unchanged because nothing has altered myocyte calcium handling. Arterial pressure may rise a little as output increases, so afterload is a later and secondary effect. Ejection fraction may change slightly but is a computed ratio rather than a variable an infusion acts on directly."},{"t":"mc","q":"Aortic valve stenosis narrows the outflow orifice. Which variable does it primarily increase?\nA) Preload  |  B) Contractility\nC) Venous return  |  D) Afterload\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Afterload","why":"A narrowed valve means the ventricle must generate a much higher pressure than the aorta itself carries in order to push blood through, which is a large increase in the load opposing ejection. Preload may rise later as end systolic volume accumulates, but the primary lesion is on the ejection side. Contractility is a myocyte property and is not changed by the valve. Venous return is set by the systemic circulation upstream, not by the outflow valve."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one ventricle three times. On the first, mark preload with the thing that actually stretches it before it contracts. On the second, mark afterload with what it must push against, and put a pressure value on the aorta. On the third, show contractility as more force at the same starting length. Under each, an arrow for what a rise in that variable does to stroke volume. One line: name the one that can rise while the other two hold still, and what does that in the body."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a normal ventricle in the middle of your space and four altered versions around it: hemorrhage, severe untreated hypertension, a sympathetic surge, and an intravenous fluid overload. On every version, draw the filling volume, the pressure it ejects against, and the strength of the squeeze as three things you can see. One line: pick the version where two of the three moved at once and say which one decided stroke volume."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish preload, afterload, and contractility and predict how a change in each alters stroke volume."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Preload afterload and contractility. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-cardiac-regulation","name":"Autonomic regulation of the heart","can":"Explain how sympathetic and parasympathetic input alter heart rate and contractility at the receptor and second messenger level.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"How does increased cAMP in SA node cells raise heart rate?\nA) It lowers the threshold voltage needed for the action potential\nB) It lengthens the plateau phase of the action potential\nC) It makes the maximum diastolic potential more negative than usual\nD) It steepens the slope of the pacemaker potential toward threshold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It steepens the slope of the pacemaker potential toward threshold","why":"More cAMP opens more HCN channels and increases Ca2+ entry during diastolic depolarization, so the membrane drifts upward faster and each interval between beats shortens. Threshold voltage itself is a property of the channels and is not the main variable moved here. A more negative maximum diastolic potential would lengthen the climb and slow the rate, which is what vagal stimulation does. The SA node action potential has no true plateau, so lengthening one is not the mechanism."},{"t":"mc","q":"Protein kinase A also phosphorylates phospholamban. What does this accomplish?\nA) It inhibits SERCA, so Ca2+ lingers and contraction is prolonged\nB) It blocks the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, so Ca2+ accumulates in the cytosol\nC) It opens ryanodine receptors, so Ca2+ release continues through diastole\nD) It releases the brake on SERCA, so Ca2+ is pumped back faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It releases the brake on SERCA, so Ca2+ is pumped back faster","why":"Unphosphorylated phospholamban restrains SERCA, and phosphorylating it lifts that restraint so calcium is cleared from the cytosol faster, shortening relaxation and preserving filling time at high heart rates. Inhibiting SERCA would prolong contraction, the opposite of the sympathetic effect. Ryanodine receptors govern release from the store rather than reuptake into it. The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger removes calcium from the cell and is not the target of this phosphorylation step."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks muscarinic receptors in the heart. Predict the effect on heart rate.\nA) Heart rate falls, because the sympathetic input is blocked\nB) Heart rate rises, because contractility increases directly\nC) Heart rate is unchanged, because the SA node sets its own rate\nD) Heart rate rises, because vagal tone is removed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Heart rate rises, because vagal tone is removed","why":"The resting heart is held below its intrinsic rate by continuous vagal activity, so blocking the muscarinic receptors lifts that brake and the rate climbs toward roughly 100 beats per minute. Sympathetic transmission uses adrenergic receptors and is untouched by a muscarinic blocker. The SA node does have an intrinsic rate, but the resting rate is not that rate, which is why the drug has an effect at all. Ventricular contractility is only lightly influenced by vagal input, so it is not the route to the rate change."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient starts a beta 1 receptor blocker. Before the drug, resting heart rate was 72 beats per minute and maximum exercise heart rate was 185. After the drug, resting rate is 64 and maximum exercise rate is 130.\nExplain, at the receptor and second messenger level, why the drug changed the exercise rate so much more than the resting rate.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"At rest the rate is set mostly by vagal tone, which acts through muscarinic receptors the drug does not touch; during exercise the rise depends on norepinephrine raising cAMP through beta 1 receptors, and that is exactly the pathway the drug blocks","why":"Resting heart rate sits below the intrinsic 100 beats per minute because the vagus is releasing acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors, and only a small amount of sympathetic tone is present. Blocking beta 1 receptors removes that small sympathetic contribution, so rest drops a few beats. Exercise is different: the rise from about 100 to 185 is almost entirely norepinephrine and epinephrine acting on beta 1 receptors, raising cAMP, and increasing the funny current and Ca2+ current in the node. With the receptor blocked, the second messenger never rises, and the rate can only reach about 130, roughly what vagal withdrawal plus whatever escapes the block allows."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart mainly by reducing the contractility of the ventricles.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart by acting on the sinoatrial node, where acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors opens K+ channels and reduces the funny current, so the pacemaker potential takes longer to reach threshold. The ventricles receive little parasympathetic innervation.","why":"Heart rate is set by the pacemaker, so anything that changes rate has to act on the node. Acetylcholine hyperpolarizes nodal cells and flattens the pacemaker slope, and that lengthens each cycle. The vagus nerve reaches the sinoatrial node, the atrial muscle, and the atrioventricular node, but very few fibers reach the ventricles, so parasympathetic input has little direct effect on ventricular force. Contractility is the sympathetic side of the story, through beta 1 receptors and cAMP."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare how the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions change heart rate and contractility. Name the transmitter, the receptor, the G protein and second messenger, and the ion channel effect for each.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"sympathetic: norepinephrine (and circulating epinephrine) on beta 1 receptors, Gs, cAMP rises, protein kinase A; in the node cAMP increases the funny current and Ca2+ current, steepens the pacemaker slope, and heart rate rises; in contractile cells protein kinase A increases Ca2+ entry and Ca2+ release, so contractility rises and end systolic volume falls; parasympathetic: acetylcholine on muscarinic (M2) receptors, Gi, cAMP falls, and K+ channels open; K+ efflux hyperpolarizes the node and the funny current falls, so the slope flattens and heart rate falls; the ventricles have little parasympathetic innervation, so contractility is barely affected by the vagus","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the SA node cell twice, side by side. Left, sympathetic: norepinephrine, the beta 1 receptor, the G protein, cAMP, and what that does to the funny current and to calcium channels. Right, parasympathetic: the vagus, acetylcholine, the M2 muscarinic receptor, and what that does to cAMP and to potassium channels. Under each, draw the pacemaker slope it produces. One line: one of them hyperpolarizes the cell, so say why that alone slows the rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a pacemaker trace with three versions on the same axes: resting with normal vagal tone, with the vagus cut, and under maximal sympathetic drive. Beside it, draw a ventricular cell showing the two places sympathetic signaling raises contractility, calcium entry and the calcium pump of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. One line: the vagus does little to the ventricles, so say what that predicts about slowing a heart against weakening one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how sympathetic and parasympathetic input alter heart rate and contractility at the receptor and second messenger level."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Autonomic regulation of the heart. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-lab-heart-sounds","name":"Heart sound and pulse correlation","can":"Auscultate the heart sounds, locate the valve areas, and correlate the sounds with the pulse and with the cardiac cycle phases.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"During auscultation, a sound is heard at the same moment the carotid pulse is felt. Which sound is it?\nA) The second heart sound  |  B) The first heart sound\nC) A third heart sound  |  D) A fourth heart sound\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The first heart sound","why":"The carotid upstroke is produced by ejection, which begins a fraction of a second after the AV valves close, so the sound that coincides with the pulse is the first sound. The second sound ends ejection and arrives after the carotid upstroke has already passed. A third sound falls in early diastole, well after the pulse. A fourth sound falls just before the first sound and therefore just before the pulse."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student feels the carotid pulse while listening and hears a murmur that occupies the interval containing that pulse. How should the murmur be classified?\nA) Diastolic  |  B) Systolic\nC) Presystolic  |  D) Continuous\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Systolic","why":"The carotid upstroke happens during ejection, which is part of ventricular systole, so a murmur filling that same interval is systolic. A diastolic murmur would fall in the interval after the pulse has subsided and before the next upstroke. Presystolic refers to the moment just before the first sound, during atrial contraction. A continuous murmur would not stop at the second sound and would span both intervals."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is each valve area located downstream of the valve rather than directly over it?\nA) The stethoscope only detects sound at the skin surface over bone\nB) The ribs block sound directly over each valve\nC) The valves are too deep to hear at all\nD) Sound travels in the direction that blood is flowing\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sound travels in the direction that blood is flowing","why":"Vibrations generated at a valve are carried along the moving column of blood, so the loudest point on the chest wall lies in the path of that flow, which is why aortic sounds project up and right and mitral sounds project down and left. The ribs do transmit sound imperfectly, but the listening areas were chosen by flow direction rather than by rib position. The valves are certainly audible, which is the premise of the exercise. Sound is transmitted through soft tissue as well as bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the radial pulse a poor choice for timing heart sounds at a high heart rate?\nA) The radial artery does not pulsate strongly during systole\nB) The radial artery is compressed by the stethoscope tubing\nC) The radial pulse reflects diastolic pressure rather than systolic\nD) The pressure wave takes time to reach the wrist, so it lags\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The pressure wave takes time to reach the wrist, so it lags","why":"The pulse wave has to travel from the aortic root all the way down the arm, and by the time it arrives the heart may already be well into the next part of the cycle, so at fast rates the lag can point you at the wrong sound. The radial artery does pulsate with each ejection, so a claim that it does not is simply incorrect. The pulse reflects the systolic upstroke, not diastolic pressure. The stethoscope is on the chest and does not compress the wrist."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pulse deficit is found when the apical rate exceeds the radial rate. What does this indicate?\nA) Some contractions are too weak to produce a palpable pulse\nB) The radial artery is partly occluded by atherosclerosis\nC) The examiner is counting the second heart sound as a separate beat\nD) The heart is beating faster on the left than on the right\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Some contractions are too weak to produce a palpable pulse","why":"Every audible beat at the apex represents a ventricular contraction, but a contraction that follows too soon after the previous one has had little filling time and may eject too little blood to raise a palpable wave at the wrist, so the counts diverge. A localized arterial blockage is possible but would be an unusual cause and would not fluctuate beat to beat. Miscounting sounds is an error to rule out but is not what the term describes. The two ventricles necessarily beat at the same rate."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The first heart sound is produced by the semilunar valves closing at the start of ventricular contraction.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The first heart sound is produced by the atrioventricular valves closing at the start of ventricular contraction. The semilunar valves close at the end of ejection and produce the second sound.","why":"When the ventricle starts to contract, its pressure rises above atrial pressure almost at once and the atrioventricular valves slam shut. That vibration is the first sound. The semilunar valves are still closed at that moment because ventricular pressure has not yet reached arterial pressure. They open during ejection and close when the ventricle relaxes, which gives the second sound. Remembering which valves are open in each phase is the key: the semilunar valves cannot close at the start of systole because they were not open."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the chest with the four auscultation areas in their real positions: aortic at the second right intercostal space beside the sternum, pulmonic at the second left, tricuspid at the lower left sternal border, mitral at the fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line. Run an arrow from each spot to the valve it reports on, and note that the spot is not sitting on top of the valve. One line: say what carries the sound from the valve to the spot."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one long time axis and stack four traces on it: the ECG, the heart sounds, left ventricular pressure, and the radial pulse under your fingers. Line up S1, S2 and the pulse wave against the electrical and pressure events above them. One line: the radial pulse arrives after S1, so say what that delay is made of."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Heart sound and pulse correlation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Vascular Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w11-flow-resistance","name":"Pressure flow and resistance","can":"Apply the relationship among flow, pressure gradient, and resistance and predict the effect of a change in vessel radius, blood viscosity, or vessel length on flow.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A vessel constricts so its radius is halved, and at the same time the pressure gradient across it is doubled. Compared with the starting condition, flow through the vessel is now what?\nA) One eighth of the original flow  |  B) One half of the original flow\nC) One sixteenth of the original flow  |  D) Twice the original flow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One eighth of the original flow","why":"Halving radius raises resistance sixteenfold, so flow would fall to one sixteenth on its own, and doubling the gradient then multiplies that by two, leaving one eighth. One sixteenth ignores the doubled gradient. One half accounts for the gradient but treats radius as a first power term. Twice the original ignores the radius change entirely. Doubling the driving pressure cannot rescue flow through a vessel that has narrowed this much, which is the core problem in arterial stenosis."},{"t":"mc","q":"An arteriole constricts so that its radius falls to half its original value. By what factor does its resistance change?\nA) Resistance increases fourfold  |  B) Resistance increases sixteenfold\nC) Resistance increases twofold  |  D) Resistance increases eightfold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Resistance increases sixteenfold","why":"Resistance goes as 1 over radius to the fourth power, so cutting radius in half multiplies resistance by two to the fourth power, which is sixteen. A fourfold answer comes from squaring instead of raising to the fourth. A twofold answer treats radius as if it acted the way length does, in simple proportion. An eightfold answer comes from cubing. This steep dependence is exactly why arterioles are such powerful flow regulators."},{"t":"mc","q":"A vascular bed has a pressure gradient of 100 mmHg across it and a resistance of 20 mmHg per L/min. What is the flow?\nA) 2 L/min  |  B) 5 L/min\nC) 50 L/min  |  D) 20 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 5 L/min","why":"Flow equals the pressure gradient divided by resistance, so 100 divided by 20 gives 5 L/min, a value close to a normal resting cardiac output. Twenty comes from dividing the gradient by five instead. Fifty comes from misplacing a decimal. Two comes from dividing resistance into the wrong quantity, that is, inverting the ratio."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man with polycythemia has a hematocrit of 62 percent (normal 40 to 52). His blood is roughly twice as viscous as normal. His arteriole radii and mean arterial pressure are unchanged.\nWhat happens to blood flow through his tissues, and what would restore it?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Flow falls to about half; restoring it requires arteriolar dilation to lower resistance or a higher pressure gradient","why":"Resistance is directly proportional to viscosity. Doubling viscosity doubles resistance, and at the same pressure gradient flow falls by half. To bring flow back the body must either lower resistance somewhere else, which means widening arterioles, or raise the driving pressure. This is why polycythemia strains the heart and why removing blood to lower the hematocrit is a treatment."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"If the radius of an arteriole doubles and the pressure gradient is unchanged, flow through it doubles.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. If the radius doubles at an unchanged pressure gradient, resistance falls to one sixteenth and flow rises about sixteenfold.","why":"Resistance depends on radius to the fourth power. Doubling the radius divides resistance by two to the fourth, or sixteen. Since flow is the gradient divided by resistance, flow rises sixteenfold. This is why arterioles need only small changes in radius to make large changes in organ blood flow. Treating radius as a linear term is the most common error in this topic."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a change in arteriole radius is the body's main tool for controlling blood flow to an organ. Include what happens to flow when the radius is halved and the pressure gradient does not change.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"flow equals the pressure gradient divided by resistance; resistance is inversely proportional to radius to the fourth power; halving the radius raises resistance about sixteenfold; flow falls to about one sixteenth at the same gradient; arterioles have thick smooth muscle that can change radius quickly; vessel length and blood viscosity do not change on a moment to moment basis","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a straight tube with a pressure at each end and flow through it, and write on the drawing that flow equals the pressure gradient divided by resistance. Below it draw three more tubes: one twice as long, one with the radius halved, and one carrying blood of twice the viscosity. Under each, write what flow does and by what factor. One line: name the one of the three the body actually adjusts moment to moment."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one arteriole in cross section at three radii, normal, constricted, and dilated, drawn so the lumen areas look honest. Under each, draw a bar for the flow through it, sized by the fourth power relationship. Beside the three, draw the smooth muscle layer and what makes it change. One line: a tiny change in radius does something huge, so say what that costs you in fine control."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to apply the relationship among flow, pressure gradient, and resistance and predict the effect of a change in vessel radius, blood viscosity, or vessel length on flow."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pressure flow and resistance. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-vessel-function","name":"Vessel structure and function","can":"Match arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins to their role in pressure buffering, resistance, exchange, and capacitance.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug produces a large fall in total peripheral resistance with almost no change in how much blood the venous system holds. Which vessels does it most likely act on?\nA) Large veins  |  B) Capillaries\nC) Arterioles  |  D) Elastic arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Arterioles","why":"Arterioles set most of total peripheral resistance, so a drug that lowers resistance sharply without shifting stored volume is acting there. A drug acting on large veins would move stored volume and change venous return, which is not what was described. Capillaries have no smooth muscle of their own and cannot be dilated directly. Elastic arteries are already wide and low resistance, so relaxing them changes buffering more than resistance."},{"t":"mc","q":"Across which vascular segment does mean pressure fall the most steeply?\nA) The aorta and large arteries  |  B) The arterioles\nC) The capillaries  |  D) The large veins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The arterioles","why":"Mean pressure drops from roughly 90 mmHg at the start of the arterioles to about 35 mmHg at the capillary entrance, the largest fall anywhere in the circuit, because that is where resistance is concentrated. The aorta and large arteries lose only a few mmHg over their length. Capillaries drop pressure further but over a smaller range. Large veins operate near right atrial pressure already and have little left to lose."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician wants to lower the pressure the left ventricle must overcome to eject blood. Which vessels should the drug target?\nA) Arterioles, to lower total peripheral resistance\nB) Capillaries, to increase filtration\nC) Large veins, to increase capacitance\nD) Pulmonary veins, to lower left atrial pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Arterioles, to lower total peripheral resistance","why":"The load against which the left ventricle ejects is set mainly by arterial pressure, and arterial pressure is set by cardiac output and arteriolar resistance, so dilating arterioles is the direct route. Dilating large veins increases capacitance and lowers the volume returning to the heart, which reduces preload rather than the ejection load. Capillaries cannot be dilated directly and changing filtration there does not change ejection pressure. Lowering left atrial pressure affects filling, again a preload effect."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessels serve as the capacitance vessels of the circulation?\nA) The veins  |  B) The arterioles\nC) The capillaries  |  D) The elastic arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The veins","why":"Veins are thin walled and highly distensible, so they hold roughly two thirds of the total blood volume at low pressure and act as the reservoir the circulation draws on. Elastic arteries hold only a small volume at high pressure and act as buffers rather than reservoirs. Arterioles are the resistance vessels. Capillaries hold a very small fraction of blood volume at any instant and exist for exchange."},{"t":"mc","q":"Exchange of gases, nutrients, and fluid between blood and tissue takes place mainly across which vessels?\nA) The muscular arteries  |  B) The arterioles\nC) The capillaries  |  D) The large veins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The capillaries","why":"Capillary walls are a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, which is thin enough for diffusion and filtration to occur across it, and they are so numerous that nearly every cell sits within a short diffusion distance of one. Arterioles have a smooth muscle coat that makes them too thick for meaningful exchange, though some exchange does begin there. Muscular arteries and large veins have multilayered walls built for conduction and capacitance, not transfer."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Blood velocity is lowest in the capillaries because their combined cross sectional area is the largest of any vessel type.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Flow through the whole system is the same at every level, equal to cardiac output. When that flow spreads over a very wide total area, the blood moves slowly, like a river widening into a delta. The slow transit through capillaries gives time for oxygen, nutrients, and waste to exchange. Velocity picks up again in the veins as the vessels merge and the total area shrinks."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the circuit as one loop: aorta, arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins, vena cava. Directly under the loop, on the same horizontal axis, draw the pressure curve falling from about 120 over 80 in the aorta to near zero at the right atrium. Beside the loop, draw each vessel type's wall in cross section. One line: name the segment with the steepest pressure drop and say what makes it steep."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four boxes headed pressure buffering, resistance, exchange, and capacitance. In each, draw the vessel that does the job with the one wall feature that lets it: elastic sheets, thick smooth muscle, a single endothelial layer, a thin distensible wall with valves. Then shade the share of total blood volume sitting in the capacitance vessels at rest, about 60 percent. One line: say what happens to that reservoir when the sympathetic system fires."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins to their role in pressure buffering, resistance, exchange, and capacitance."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Vessel structure and function. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-blood-pressure","name":"Arterial blood pressure","can":"Define systolic, diastolic, pulse, and mean arterial pressure, calculate mean arterial pressure, and explain how it is determined by cardiac output and total peripheral resistance.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In septic shock, total peripheral resistance falls to half of normal while cardiac output rises to 1.5 times normal. If mean arterial pressure started at 100 mmHg, what is it now?\nA) About 150 mmHg  |  B) About 100 mmHg, since the two changes cancel\nC) About 75 mmHg  |  D) About 50 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 75 mmHg","why":"Mean arterial pressure is the product of the two terms, so multiplying by 1.5 and then by 0.5 gives 0.75 of the starting value, or 75 mmHg. The changes do not cancel, because a halving is a larger fractional change than a fifty percent rise. One hundred fifty would follow if only the output change were counted. Fifty would follow if only the resistance change were counted. This is why a septic patient can be hypotensive despite a high cardiac output."},{"t":"mc","q":"During heavy exercise, cardiac output rises from 5 to 20 L/min while total peripheral resistance falls to one third of its resting value. If resting mean arterial pressure was 90 mmHg, what is it during exercise?\nA) About 30 mmHg  |  B) About 90 mmHg, since the changes offset exactly\nC) About 360 mmHg  |  D) About 120 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 120 mmHg","why":"Cardiac output rises by a factor of four and resistance falls to one third, so the product changes by four thirds, and 90 times four thirds is 120 mmHg, a modest rise that matches what is measured during exercise. Three hundred sixty counts only the output change. Thirty counts only the resistance change. The changes nearly offset but not exactly, and the small net rise is what allows muscle to receive enormous flow without the arterial pressure either collapsing or spiking dangerously."},{"t":"mc","q":"A blood pressure reads 120 over 80 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure?\nA) About 93 mmHg  |  B) 100 mmHg\nC) About 87 mmHg  |  D) 40 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 93 mmHg","why":"Pulse pressure is 120 minus 80, or 40 mmHg, and one third of 40 is about 13, so the mean is 80 plus 13, roughly 93 mmHg. One hundred is the simple midpoint, which overestimates because it ignores the longer duration of diastole. Eighty seven comes from subtracting rather than adding the third. Forty is the pulse pressure itself, not the mean."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's blood pressure is 150 over 90 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure?\nA) 120 mmHg  |  B) 60 mmHg\nC) 100 mmHg  |  D) 110 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 110 mmHg","why":"The pulse pressure is 60 mmHg, one third of that is 20, and adding 20 to the diastolic value of 90 gives 110 mmHg. One hundred twenty is the midpoint between the two readings and overestimates the true mean. One hundred comes from adding only ten. Sixty is the pulse pressure, which describes the swing rather than the average."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In a clinic, a 75 year old man reads 160 over 60 mmHg and a 35 year old woman reads 130 over 100 mmHg (normal below 120 over 80).\nCalculate each person's pulse pressure and mean arterial pressure and compare them.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Man: pulse pressure 100 mmHg, mean about 93 mmHg. Woman: pulse pressure 30 mmHg, mean 110 mmHg. The woman has the higher mean pressure despite the lower systolic value","why":"Mean arterial pressure is diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure. For the man, pulse pressure is 160 minus 60, or 100, and one third of that is about 33, so the mean is about 93 mmHg. For the woman, pulse pressure is 130 minus 100, or 30, one third is 10, and the mean is 110 mmHg. The man's stiff aorta gives a high systolic and a low diastolic, but because diastole dominates the average, his mean is lower. The woman's high diastolic pressure points to high resistance and a high mean."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Mean arterial pressure is the simple average of systolic and diastolic pressure.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Mean arterial pressure is closer to diastolic pressure and is estimated as diastolic pressure plus one third of pulse pressure.","why":"At resting heart rates the heart spends about twice as long in diastole as in systole, so the pressure in the arteries sits near the diastolic value for most of each cycle. Averaging the two extremes overestimates the mean. For 120 over 80, the simple average is 100 mmHg but the true mean is about 93 mmHg. The mean is the number that matters for organ perfusion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an aortic pressure trace over three beats, with the systolic peak at 120 and the diastolic trough at 80 marked on a y axis in mmHg. Bracket and label pulse pressure. Then compute mean arterial pressure on the page with the arithmetic showing, diastolic plus one third of pulse pressure. One line: the mean is not the midpoint of 120 and 80, so say what about the shape of the trace pulls it lower."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw mean arterial pressure as a product of two things, a pump for cardiac output and a set of narrowed vessels for total peripheral resistance. Then draw three altered versions: output up with resistance unchanged, resistance up with output unchanged, and output up with resistance down by the same proportion. Show what mean pressure does in each. One line: the third case is exercise, so say why the pressure barely shifts."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define systolic, diastolic, pulse, and mean arterial pressure, calculate mean arterial pressure, and explain how it is determined by cardiac output and total peripheral resistance."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Arterial blood pressure. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-local-blood-flow","name":"Local control of blood flow","can":"Explain myogenic, metabolic, and endothelial control of arteriolar tone and predict the local flow response to increased tissue metabolism.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Increased endothelin 1 release in a vascular bed has what effect on resistance and local flow?\nA) Resistance rises and local flow falls\nB) Resistance falls and local flow rises\nC) Resistance rises and local flow rises\nD) Resistance is unchanged and only capillary permeability rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Resistance rises and local flow falls","why":"Endothelin constricts arteriolar smooth muscle, narrowing the vessel, and since resistance climbs steeply as radius falls, flow through that bed drops. Falling resistance would follow from nitric oxide instead. Resistance and flow cannot rise together at a fixed pressure gradient, since flow is pressure divided by resistance. Permeability is governed by other mediators and is not the main action of endothelin."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks the enzyme that makes nitric oxide in endothelial cells. What happens to arteriolar tone and arterial pressure?\nA) Tone decreases and arterial pressure falls\nB) Tone increases and arterial pressure rises\nC) Tone is unchanged because nitric oxide acts only on veins\nD) Tone increases but arterial pressure falls because flow is redistributed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Tone increases and arterial pressure rises","why":"Endothelium releases nitric oxide continuously, so blocking its synthesis removes a background dilator signal, arteriolar smooth muscle contracts more, total peripheral resistance rises, and with cardiac output roughly unchanged mean arterial pressure climbs. Falling tone would require adding a dilator rather than removing one. Nitric oxide acts throughout the arterial tree, not only on veins. Pressure follows the product of output and resistance, so rising resistance cannot lower pressure while output holds."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which metabolite is released by working cells and is considered a major local dilator in cardiac and skeletal muscle?\nA) Adenosine  |  B) Albumin\nC) Endothelin 1  |  D) Angiotensin II\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Adenosine","why":"When ATP use outruns ATP production, adenosine appears in the interstitium and relaxes nearby arteriolar smooth muscle, which is why it is one of the strongest links between cardiac work and coronary flow. Albumin is a plasma protein that holds fluid in the capillary and has no role in setting tone. Endothelin and angiotensin II are both constrictors, so they would reduce flow to a working muscle rather than increase it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab, a cuff on a student's upper arm is inflated to 200 mmHg, well above her systolic pressure of 118 mmHg, and held for 3 minutes. When the cuff is released her forearm turns pink and feels warm for about a minute, then returns to normal.\nWhat explains the flush after the cuff is released?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"During the occlusion, oxygen fell and metabolites accumulated, so the arterioles dilated; on release, flow through the dilated bed is far above normal until the metabolites wash out","why":"This is reactive hyperemia. With no flow for three minutes the tissue used up its oxygen and built up carbon dioxide, hydrogen ion, adenosine, and potassium. Those signals relaxed the arteriolar smooth muscle. When the cuff comes off, blood pours through vessels that are wide open, so flow overshoots. As the metabolites are washed out the arterioles regain their tone and flow settles."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When a rise in blood pressure stretches an arteriole, the myogenic response is dilation, which relieves the stretch.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. When a rise in pressure stretches an arteriole, the myogenic response is constriction, which raises resistance and keeps flow to the tissue roughly constant.","why":"Stretching arteriolar smooth muscle opens mechanically gated channels, calcium enters, and the muscle contracts. The vessel narrows, resistance rises, and flow does not climb with the pressure. This is one of the mechanisms of autoregulation in organs such as the brain and kidney. If the response were dilation, every rise in pressure would flood the tissue with flow."},{"t":"bd","q":"A skeletal muscle starts contracting. Explain how the arterioles in that muscle know to dilate, what happens to flow, and what the response is called.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"called active hyperemia; increased metabolism raises carbon dioxide, hydrogen ion, adenosine, and potassium and lowers oxygen in the tissue; these metabolites relax arteriolar smooth muscle; resistance falls and flow rises; flow rises in proportion to metabolic demand; local signals override sympathetic vasoconstriction in the working muscle","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one arteriole three times, each answering a different local signal. First myogenic, with a sudden pressure rise stretching the wall and the smooth muscle response. Second metabolic, with a working muscle beside it releasing carbon dioxide, hydrogen ion, potassium and adenosine, and the arteriole response. Third endothelial, with the endothelial cell releasing nitric oxide. One line: the first two point opposite ways during exercise, so say which one wins locally."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a tissue at rest and the same tissue working hard, each with its feeding arteriole and the capillary bed beyond it drawn with the number of open capillaries you would expect. Between the two drawings, draw the chain of signals that took you from the first to the second, in order. One line: say what would hold flow steady instead if you kept the metabolism fixed and only changed the perfusion pressure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain myogenic, metabolic, and endothelial control of arteriolar tone and predict the local flow response to increased tissue metabolism."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Local control of blood flow. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-capillary-exchange","name":"Capillary exchange and Starling forces","can":"Identify the four Starling forces at a capillary and determine the net direction of fluid movement at the arterial and venous ends.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which Starling force is the main driver of fluid out of the capillary?\nA) Interstitial hydrostatic pressure\nB) Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure\nC) Capillary colloid osmotic pressure\nD) Capillary hydrostatic pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Capillary hydrostatic pressure","why":"The blood pressure inside the capillary pushes fluid through the wall into the interstitium, and it is the largest of the outward forces. Interstitial hydrostatic pressure pushes in the opposite direction, back into the capillary, and is normally very small. Capillary colloid osmotic pressure pulls fluid inward and is the main opposing force. Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure does favor filtration but is much smaller than the hydrostatic push."},{"t":"mc","q":"Capillary colloid osmotic pressure is generated mainly by what?\nA) Sodium and chloride dissolved in plasma\nB) Red blood cells suspended in plasma\nC) Glucose dissolved in plasma\nD) Plasma proteins, especially albumin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Plasma proteins, especially albumin","why":"Albumin is small enough to be abundant but too large to cross the capillary wall in quantity, so it stays in the plasma and holds water there, accounting for most of the colloid osmotic pressure. Sodium, chloride, and glucose all cross the capillary wall freely, so their concentrations equalize and they exert no sustained osmotic pull across it. Red cells are confined to the vessel but are far too few in number to generate meaningful osmotic pressure."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate value of capillary colloid osmotic pressure in a healthy person?\nA) About 5 mmHg  |  B) About 25 mmHg\nC) About 60 mmHg  |  D) About 100 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 25 mmHg","why":"Plasma proteins generate roughly 25 mmHg of inward pull, a value that stays nearly constant from one end of the capillary to the other. Five mmHg is closer to the interstitial colloid osmotic pressure in many tissues. Sixty mmHg is far above what plasma protein concentrations can produce. One hundred mmHg is near mean arterial pressure and has nothing to do with the protein osmotic force."},{"t":"mc","q":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure changes in which way from the arterial end of a capillary to the venous end?\nA) It rises, from roughly 15 mmHg to roughly 35 mmHg\nB) It falls, from roughly 90 mmHg to roughly 40 mmHg\nC) It stays constant at roughly 25 mmHg along the whole length\nD) It falls, from roughly 35 mmHg to roughly 15 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls, from roughly 35 mmHg to roughly 15 mmHg","why":"Pressure drops as blood moves along the capillary because of the resistance of the vessel itself, so the outward push weakens from about 35 mmHg at the start to about 15 mmHg at the end, and that fall is what reverses the net direction of fluid movement. Rising pressure would require an upstream source partway along, which does not exist. Staying constant would leave the direction unchanged along the whole length. Values near 90 mmHg belong to arteries, upstream of the arterioles that drop pressure so steeply."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"At the venous end of a typical capillary, plasma colloid osmotic pressure exceeds capillary hydrostatic pressure, so the net movement of fluid is into the capillary.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Fluid leaks out at the arterial end, where hydrostatic pressure is high, and most of it is pulled back in at the venous end, where hydrostatic pressure has dropped below the osmotic pull of the plasma proteins. The small amount not reabsorbed, about 3 liters a day, is returned by the lymphatics. Anything that lowers plasma protein removes this reabsorbing end and produces edema."},{"t":"bd","q":"List the four Starling forces, say which direction each pushes fluid, and explain why fluid leaves the capillary at the arterial end but returns at the venous end.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"capillary hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out; plasma colloid osmotic pressure pulls fluid in; interstitial hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid in; interstitial colloid osmotic pressure pulls fluid out; capillary hydrostatic pressure falls along the capillary while plasma colloid osmotic pressure stays about the same; net filtration at the arterial end and net reabsorption at the venous end; fluid not reabsorbed is returned by the lymphatics","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one capillary running from its arterial end to its venous end, wide enough to write inside. Put all four Starling forces on it as arrows with their true directions, and write the values: capillary hydrostatic pressure about 35 mmHg at the arterial end falling to about 15 mmHg at the venous end, plasma colloid osmotic pressure about 25 mmHg the whole way. Work out the net direction at each end. One line: not everything filtered comes back, so say where the rest goes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same capillary as a graph instead. Distance along the capillary on the x axis, pressure in mmHg on the y, hydrostatic pressure as a falling line and colloid osmotic pressure as a flat one. Mark where the lines cross and shade the filtration region and the reabsorption region. One line: say what dropping the flat line by 10 mmHg does to the crossing point, and name a condition that drops it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four Starling forces at a capillary and determine the net direction of fluid movement at the arterial and venous ends."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Capillary exchange and Starling forces. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-edema","name":"Edema","can":"Explain how increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, reduced plasma protein, increased permeability, or lymphatic blockage each produce edema and match a clinical scenario to its mechanism.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with right heart failure has swollen ankles and distended neck veins. Which mechanism of edema fits best?\nA) Reduced plasma protein from impaired synthesis\nB) Lymphatic obstruction from scarred lymph channels\nC) Increased capillary permeability from inflammatory mediators\nD) Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous congestion","why":"A right ventricle that cannot keep up lets pressure rise behind it, which is visible in the neck veins and transmitted back to the systemic capillaries, raising the outward push. Impaired albumin synthesis is a liver problem and would not distend the neck veins. Inflammatory permeability produces local, warm, red swelling rather than symmetric dependent edema with venous congestion. Lymphatic obstruction is usually confined to the drainage territory of the blocked vessels."},{"t":"mc","q":"Minutes after a bee sting, the skin around the site becomes red, warm, and swollen. Which mechanism produced the swelling?\nA) Reduced plasma protein from renal loss\nB) Increased capillary permeability caused by histamine release\nC) Lymphatic obstruction caused by the venom\nD) Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from venous obstruction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Increased capillary permeability caused by histamine release","why":"Histamine from mast cells dilates arterioles, which brings the redness and warmth, and separates endothelial cells, which lets protein and fluid escape, so swelling appears within minutes. Renal protein loss takes days to lower albumin and would cause generalized rather than local swelling. Venom does not physically block lymphatics. Venous obstruction would produce a cool, dusky limb rather than a hot red area."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot in a deep leg vein is followed by swelling of that leg. Which mechanism of edema does this represent?\nA) Increased capillary permeability from clot proteins\nB) Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from blocked venous outflow\nC) Lymphatic obstruction from the clot\nD) Reduced plasma protein from consumption of clotting factors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure from blocked venous outflow","why":"A clot obstructing the vein raises the pressure everywhere upstream of it, including the capillaries, so the outward push climbs and fluid accumulates in that leg. Clot proteins do not open the capillary wall. The clot sits in a vein and does not block lymphatic vessels, which run alongside but separately. Clotting factor consumption does not lower total plasma protein enough to change colloid osmotic pressure meaningfully."},{"t":"mc","q":"Edema is defined as what?\nA) An excess accumulation of fluid inside cells\nB) An excess accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space\nC) An increase in circulating plasma volume\nD) An increase in the protein concentration of plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) An excess accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space","why":"Edema is fluid that has left the capillaries and collected between the cells, so it is an interstitial problem rather than an intracellular or intravascular one. Swelling inside cells is cellular edema and follows osmotic shifts across the cell membrane rather than the Starling balance. A larger plasma volume can contribute to edema by raising capillary pressure, but it is not the same thing. Plasma protein concentration usually falls rather than rises in the forms of edema caused by protein loss."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman had the lymph nodes removed from her left armpit during breast cancer surgery. Months later her left arm is swollen while the right arm is normal. Her serum albumin is 4.2 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0), her heart function is normal, and the arm is not red or hot.\nWhich mechanism explains the swelling, and what happens to the protein in the interstitial fluid of that arm over time?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Lymphatic drainage is blocked, so filtered fluid and escaped protein cannot leave; interstitial protein accumulates, raises interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, and pulls in still more fluid","why":"The swelling is on one side only, the albumin is normal, the heart is normal, and there is no inflammation. That leaves the one mechanism that is regional and permanent: the lymphatics of that arm drain through the nodes that were removed. Lymphatics normally carry away both the fluid that is not reabsorbed and the small amount of protein that leaks out. With them blocked, protein builds up in the tissue, its osmotic pull grows, and the arm draws in more fluid. This is why lymphedema tends to worsen rather than resolve."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain the four ways edema can form. For each, give the Starling force that changes and one example of a condition that causes it.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, as in heart failure or venous obstruction; decreased plasma protein and plasma colloid osmotic pressure, as in liver failure, kidney protein loss, or malnutrition; increased capillary permeability lets protein leak out and raises interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, as in inflammation or burns; lymphatic blockage stops the return of filtered fluid and protein, as in filariasis or lymph node removal; edema forms when filtration exceeds lymphatic return","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four capillaries in a row, each with the interstitial space around it. In each, break one Starling factor: raise capillary hydrostatic pressure, drop plasma protein, open the wall to protein leak, block the lymphatic. Draw the fluid piling up in each and mark whether interstitial protein went up or stayed put. One line: two of the four raise interstitial protein, so name them and say why that changes what you would do about it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline for each of four patients and shade where the swelling actually sits: right heart failure, liver failure with low albumin, a bee sting on the forearm, and lymph node removal from one armpit. Beside each, draw the capillary picture that produced it. One line: pick the one where a diuretic would help least and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, reduced plasma protein, increased permeability, or lymphatic blockage each produce edema and match a clinical scenario to its mechanism."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Edema. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-lymph-return","name":"Lymphatic return","can":"State the volume of fluid the lymphatic system returns each day and trace lymph from an interstitial space to the subclavian vein.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Lymph formed in the right hand returns to the blood by which route?\nA) Thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein\nB) Thoracic duct into the right subclavian vein\nC) Right lymphatic duct into the superior vena cava directly\nD) Right lymphatic duct into the right subclavian vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Right lymphatic duct into the right subclavian vein","why":"The right upper limb is within the territory of the right lymphatic duct, which empties near the right subclavian vein. The thoracic duct serves the rest of the body and drains on the left, so it is the wrong duct and the wrong side for the right hand. Lymph does not enter the superior vena cava directly, since each duct joins at a subclavian junction. Pairing the thoracic duct with the right subclavian mixes the two systems."},{"t":"mc","q":"Lymph formed in the left foot returns to the blood by which route?\nA) Right lymphatic duct into the right subclavian vein\nB) Thoracic duct into the right subclavian vein\nC) Left lymphatic duct into the inferior vena cava\nD) Thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein","why":"Both lower limbs drain upward through the abdominal trunks into the thoracic duct, which ascends through the chest and empties on the left side. The right lymphatic duct serves only the upper right quadrant, not the legs. The thoracic duct empties on the left, not the right. There is no vessel called the left lymphatic duct, and lymph does not enter the inferior vena cava."},{"t":"mc","q":"About how much fluid does the lymphatic system return to the bloodstream each day?\nA) About 3 liters  |  B) About 0.3 liters\nC) About 30 liters  |  D) About 100 liters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 3 liters","why":"Roughly three liters of filtered fluid are collected from the interstitium and returned to the venous circulation each day, which is close to the entire plasma volume, so the system is not a minor accessory. Three tenths of a liter would be far too little to keep the interstitium from flooding. Thirty liters overshoots by an order of magnitude and exceeds the surplus that filtration actually leaves behind. One hundred liters is not a plausible figure for any daily fluid movement in the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Of the fluid filtered out of systemic capillaries each day, roughly how much is reabsorbed directly back into the capillaries?\nA) About 3 of the roughly 20 liters filtered\nB) All 20 liters, leaving nothing for the lymphatics\nC) About 17 of the roughly 20 liters filtered\nD) About 10 of the roughly 20 liters filtered\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 17 of the roughly 20 liters filtered","why":"Around twenty liters filter out and about seventeen return at the venous end of the capillaries, leaving the three liter surplus that the lymphatics must carry. Reversing the numbers would make the lymphatic load nearly six times what it actually is. If all twenty returned there would be no lymph at all, and no route for escaped protein to get back. Ten liters would leave a surplus far larger than the lymphatic system carries."},{"t":"mc","q":"The right lymphatic duct drains which region?\nA) The right leg, the right side of the abdomen, and the right side of the pelvis\nB) The right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax\nC) The entire right half of the body\nD) The right lung only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax","why":"The right lymphatic duct serves only the upper right quadrant, a relatively small territory, and empties near the right subclavian vein. The right leg and right abdomen drain into the thoracic duct instead. The whole right half of the body would include those lower regions, which is why the split is by quadrant rather than by side. The right lung is part of the territory but not the whole of it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a capillary bed with filtration and reabsorption marked, then show the roughly 3 liters a day that is not reabsorbed leaving as lymph. Follow it from the interstitial space into a lymphatic capillary (draw the overlapping endothelial flaps that let fluid in but not out), through a node, into a trunk, into the thoracic duct, and into the vein at the base of the neck. One line: name the one thing lymph carries back that the blood capillary could not take."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the body divided into the drainage territory of the thoracic duct and that of the right lymphatic duct, shaded differently, and mark where each one empties into the venous system. Then draw an arm whose axillary nodes have been removed and follow the fluid balance in that limb over weeks. One line: lymph has no central pump, so say what moves it and what that predicts for a limb that cannot move."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the volume of fluid the lymphatic system returns each day and trace lymph from an interstitial space to the subclavian vein."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Lymphatic return. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-venous-return","name":"Venous return","can":"Explain the skeletal muscle pump, the respiratory pump, and venoconstriction and predict the effect of prolonged standing on venous return and stroke volume.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the function of the valves in peripheral veins?\nA) They regulate the resistance of the vein by opening and closing partially\nB) They generate the pressure that drives venous return\nC) They separate oxygenated from deoxygenated blood within the vein\nD) They prevent backflow so that each compression moves blood toward the heart\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They prevent backflow so that each compression moves blood toward the heart","why":"Venous valves are passive one way flaps, so when a muscle squeezes a vein the blood can only go forward, which converts random compression into directed flow. They are not resistance regulators, since they are either open or shut rather than graded. All blood in a systemic vein is deoxygenated, so there is nothing to separate. Valves direct flow but generate no pressure of their own."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate normal right atrial pressure in a healthy person lying down?\nA) About 0 mmHg  |  B) About 25 mmHg\nC) About 60 mmHg  |  D) About 90 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 0 mmHg","why":"Right atrial pressure sits near zero, essentially atmospheric, which is what makes it the low pressure end of the systemic circuit and gives blood somewhere to flow toward. Twenty five mmHg is closer to pulmonary artery systolic pressure. Sixty and ninety mmHg belong to the arterial side, and pressures that high in the right atrium would abolish the gradient driving venous return."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A healthy 20 year old stands motionless in a hot room at 32 degrees C (90 degrees F) for 20 minutes. Her heart rate rises from 70 to 95 beats/min (normal 60 to 100) and she feels lightheaded. She begins walking in place and feels better within a minute.\nExplain what happened to her stroke volume during standing and after she began walking.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Standing pooled blood in her leg veins, so venous return, end diastolic volume, and stroke volume fell; the faster heart rate did not fully make up the lost output; walking squeezed the leg veins and restored venous return, so stroke volume recovered","why":"Without muscle contraction there is no muscle pump, so gravity fills the compliant veins in the legs. Heat makes it worse by dilating skin vessels and veins. Less blood returns to the heart, the ventricle fills less, and by the Frank Starling relationship it ejects less. The baroreflex raises heart rate, but rate cannot fully compensate when each beat is small, so cerebral perfusion dips. Walking in place squeezes the deep leg veins and the valves send that blood toward the heart, so filling and stroke volume come back."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During inspiration intrathoracic pressure falls, and as a result venous return to the heart decreases.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. During inspiration intrathoracic pressure falls and abdominal pressure rises, which widens the pressure gradient toward the chest and increases venous return.","why":"The great veins in the chest are exposed to intrathoracic pressure. When the diaphragm drops, pressure in the chest falls and pressure in the abdomen rises, so blood is pulled from the abdominal veins into the thoracic veins and right atrium. This is the respiratory pump. It is why deep breathing helps return during exercise and why a Valsalva strain, which raises chest pressure, does the opposite."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain what happens to venous return, stroke volume, and blood pressure when a person stands motionless for a long time, and describe the mechanisms that normally keep venous return up.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"gravity distends the compliant leg veins and blood pools there; venous return falls; end diastolic volume falls, so stroke volume falls by the Frank Starling relationship; cardiac output and mean arterial pressure fall; skeletal muscle pump with one way valves pushes blood toward the heart during movement; respiratory pump pulls blood into the chest during inspiration; sympathetic venoconstriction lowers venous capacitance and pushes stored blood forward","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a leg vein in a standing person with its valves and the blood in it, three times over: calf muscle relaxed, calf contracting, calf relaxing again. Show which valve is open in each. Beside it, draw the thorax during inspiration with the pressure change that pulls blood toward the heart. One line: standing at attention shuts one of these pumps off, so say which and what the body does instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person standing still for ten minutes, with the blood pooled in the leg veins shaded. Then follow it through a chain of small panels: venous return, end diastolic volume, stroke volume by Frank Starling, cardiac output, and blood pressure at the head. Draw what happens in the last panel. One line: say what one calf squeeze would do to the whole chain and how fast."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain the skeletal muscle pump, the respiratory pump, and venoconstriction and predict the effect of prolonged standing on venous return and stroke volume."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Venous return. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-lab-bp-measurement","name":"Blood pressure measurement","can":"Measure blood pressure by auscultation, explain the origin of the Korotkoff sounds, and record the response to a postural or exercise challenge.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What produces the Korotkoff sounds heard during blood pressure measurement?\nA) Closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves transmitted down the arm\nB) Contraction of the arterial smooth muscle beneath the cuff\nC) Turbulent flow through an artery that the cuff has partially compressed\nD) Friction between the cuff and the skin as pressure is released\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Turbulent flow through an artery that the cuff has partially compressed","why":"Squeezing the artery to a narrow opening speeds the blood passing through it and breaks the smooth laminar pattern, and that turbulence is audible. Valve closure produces the heart sounds heard over the chest, not the sounds heard at the elbow. Arterial smooth muscle contraction is silent. Cuff friction would be heard regardless of cuff pressure, whereas Korotkoff sounds appear and disappear at specific pressures."},{"t":"mc","q":"The first Korotkoff sound heard as cuff pressure falls marks what?\nA) Diastolic pressure  |  B) Systolic pressure\nC) Mean arterial pressure  |  D) Pulse pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Systolic pressure","why":"The first sound appears the moment cuff pressure drops just below the peak arterial pressure, so a small jet of blood forces its way through at the top of each beat. Diastolic pressure is marked by the disappearance of the sounds, not their onset. Mean arterial pressure is calculated from the two readings rather than heard directly. Pulse pressure is the difference between them and likewise is not a moment in the auscultation."},{"t":"mc","q":"The disappearance of the Korotkoff sounds as the cuff continues to deflate marks what?\nA) Mean arterial pressure  |  B) The point of maximum turbulence\nC) Systolic pressure  |  D) Diastolic pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Diastolic pressure","why":"Once cuff pressure falls below the lowest arterial pressure, the artery stays open through the entire cycle, flow becomes laminar again, and the sounds stop. Systolic pressure is marked by the first sound rather than the last. Mean arterial pressure is derived from both endpoints and has no audible marker. Maximum turbulence occurs somewhere in the middle of the range, where the sounds are loudest, not where they vanish."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which artery is auscultated during a standard blood pressure measurement?\nA) The brachial artery at the antecubital fossa\nB) The radial artery at the wrist\nC) The carotid artery in the neck\nD) The femoral artery in the groin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The brachial artery at the antecubital fossa","why":"The cuff wraps the upper arm and the stethoscope is placed over the brachial artery just below the cuff, where the compressed vessel produces its sounds. The radial artery is used to palpate a pulse but lies too far distal for the cuff to compress it directly. The carotid cannot be compressed by an arm cuff and should never be occluded. The femoral artery requires a thigh cuff and is not the standard site."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A student inflates a cuff on her partner's arm to 180 mmHg and lets it fall slowly while listening over the brachial artery. The first tapping sound appears at 128 mmHg and the sounds disappear at 84 mmHg (normal below 120 over 80).\nWhat produces the sounds between 128 and 84 mmHg, and why do they stop below 84 mmHg?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Between those pressures the cuff squeezes the artery partly shut and it opens only at the peak of each pulse, so blood spurts through in a turbulent jet that is audible; below 84 the artery stays open through the whole cycle, flow is smooth, and there is nothing to hear","why":"Smooth laminar flow is silent. Above 128 mmHg the cuff holds the artery closed and there is no flow at all. Just below the systolic peak, the artery pops open for an instant each beat and blood squirts through the narrowed segment at high velocity, which makes turbulence and sound. As cuff pressure drops the artery stays open longer each cycle. Once cuff pressure is below diastolic, the artery never closes, the flow is laminar again, and the sound disappears. So the first sound marks systolic pressure and the last sound marks diastolic pressure."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab a subject reads 118 over 76 mmHg lying down with a heart rate of 68 beats/min (normal below 120 over 80, heart rate 60 to 100). One minute after standing she reads 112 over 82 mmHg with a heart rate of 84 beats/min.\nCalculate pulse pressure and mean arterial pressure in both positions. What does the pattern tell you about her cardiovascular reflexes?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Pulse pressure narrowed from 42 to 30 mmHg while mean pressure stayed near 90 mmHg; stroke volume fell on standing and the baroreflex raised heart rate and resistance to hold the mean, which is a normal response","why":"Lying down, pulse pressure is 118 minus 76, or 42, and the mean is 76 plus 14, or 90 mmHg. Standing, pulse pressure is 112 minus 82, or 30, and the mean is 82 plus 10, or 92 mmHg. The narrower pulse pressure tells you stroke volume fell, which is expected when blood pools in the legs. The higher diastolic pressure and faster heart rate tell you the baroreflex answered with vasoconstriction and a faster heart. The mean is unchanged, so the reflex did its job."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Korotkoff sounds are heard while cuff pressure is above systolic pressure, because the artery is completely closed.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Korotkoff sounds are heard while cuff pressure is between systolic and diastolic pressure, because the artery opens briefly at each pulse and the turbulent spurt of blood makes a sound.","why":"Above systolic pressure the cuff shuts the artery completely, there is no flow, and there is nothing to hear. As cuff pressure falls just below systolic, the artery opens only at the peak of each pulse and blood squirts through in a turbulent jet, which is the first tapping sound. Below diastolic pressure the artery stays open all the time, flow becomes smooth and laminar, and the sounds disappear."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the arm, the cuff, and the artery under it at three cuff pressures: above systolic, between systolic and diastolic, and below diastolic. In each, draw the vessel cross section, whether blood is moving, and whether the flow is smooth or turbulent. Mark where the first Korotkoff sound appears and where the last one vanishes, and write a real reading. One line: the sounds come from turbulence, so say why silence means two opposite things at the two ends."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one axis of pressure in mmHg against time in minutes and plot systolic and diastolic for a subject who lies down, stands up suddenly, rests, then steps for two minutes. Mark each transition. Include the brief dip on standing and the recovery after it. One line: name the reflex that closed that dip and say how many seconds you would give it before you worried."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Blood pressure measurement. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Cardiovascular Regulation","comps":[{"id":"w11-baroreflex","name":"Baroreceptor reflex","can":"Trace the baroreceptor reflex from the carotid sinus and aortic arch through the medulla to the heart and vessels and state the response to a fall in blood pressure.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Mean arterial pressure rises to 120 mmHg. What happens to vagal traffic to the heart, and what does that do?\nA) It is unchanged, since only sympathetic outflow is adjusted\nB) It falls, letting the intrinsic SA node rate emerge\nC) It rises, slowing the SA node\nD) It falls, slowing the SA node\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises, slowing the SA node","why":"Higher pressure means more stretch, more afferent firing, and a medullary response that raises vagal output while lowering sympathetic output, so the SA node slows. Falling vagal traffic would unmask the faster intrinsic pacemaker rate near 100 beats per minute, which is what happens when pressure drops, not when it rises. Pairing falling vagal traffic with a slower SA node joins the right effect to the wrong cause, since less vagal traffic can only speed the heart. Both limbs are adjusted reciprocally in this reflex, so leaving the vagus out misses half the response."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient takes a beta-1 blocker. Which part of the baroreflex response to a fall in blood pressure is blunted?\nA) The constriction of capacitance veins\nB) The rise in heart rate and contractility\nC) The constriction of systemic arterioles\nD) The fall in baroreceptor firing rate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The rise in heart rate and contractility","why":"Beta-1 receptors are the cardiac targets of sympathetic outflow, so blocking them removes the chronotropic and inotropic part of the response while everything else still works. Arteriolar and venous constriction run through alpha-1 receptors and are untouched by a beta-1 blocker. Baroreceptor firing is a sensory event in the vessel wall and is not adrenergic at all, so no adrenergic blocker changes it."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an animal experiment all baroreceptor afferents are cut. What does the medulla do, and why?\nA) Raises vagal outflow, reading silence as low pressure\nB) Makes no change, since the reflex needs an active signal\nC) Raises sympathetic outflow, reading silence as low pressure\nD) Lowers sympathetic outflow, reading silence as high pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Raises sympathetic outflow, reading silence as low pressure","why":"Ongoing baroreceptor firing is a tonic brake on the sympathetic center, so cutting the afferents removes that brake entirely and mean arterial pressure rises and becomes unstable. Lowering sympathetic outflow reverses the sign, treating no signal as if it meant a strong one. Raising vagal outflow would be the response to more firing, not less, so that answer keeps the right premise and draws the wrong conclusion. Expecting no change at all misses that the resting reflex is always firing, which makes the absence of a signal informative in itself."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient is given a drug that constricts arterioles and has no direct effect on the heart. Mean arterial pressure rises from 90 to 120 mmHg (normal 70 to 105). Within a minute heart rate falls from 72 to 50 beats/min (normal 60 to 100).\nWhy did the heart rate fall?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The rise in pressure stretched the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors, their firing increased, the medulla raised vagal output and lowered sympathetic output, and the SA node slowed","why":"The drug raised resistance, and mean pressure equals cardiac output times resistance, so pressure rose. The baroreceptors reported the higher pressure by firing faster. That afferent traffic drives the medulla to increase parasympathetic output to the SA node and to reduce sympathetic output. The heart slows and cardiac output falls, which partly offsets the rise in pressure. This reflex bradycardia is the standard sign that the baroreflex is intact."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When arterial pressure rises, baroreceptor firing decreases and the medulla responds by raising sympathetic output.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. When arterial pressure rises, baroreceptor firing increases and the medulla responds by lowering sympathetic output and raising parasympathetic output, which slows the heart and dilates arterioles.","why":"Baroreceptors are stretch receptors, so more pressure means more stretch and more action potentials. That increased afferent traffic inhibits the sympathetic centers and excites the vagal center. Heart rate, contractility, and resistance all fall, bringing pressure back down. The statement reverses both the sensor and the response, which would make the reflex a positive feedback loop."},{"t":"bd","q":"Trace the baroreceptor reflex from a sudden drop in arterial pressure to the return of pressure toward normal. Name the sensors, the afferent path, the integrating center, the efferent paths, and the effector responses.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"stretch receptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch; less stretch, so afferent firing rate falls; afferents travel in the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves to the medulla; medulla raises sympathetic output and lowers parasympathetic output; heart rate and contractility rise, so cardiac output rises; arteriolar constriction raises total peripheral resistance and venoconstriction raises venous return; mean arterial pressure rises back toward the set point, a negative feedback loop","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the reflex as a labeled loop with every step in it: baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and the aortic arch, the afferent nerves (glossopharyngeal from the carotid, vagus from the arch), the cardiovascular center in the medulla, and both efferent arms, sympathetic and vagal, running to the SA node, the ventricles, the arterioles and the veins. Run a fall in pressure around the loop and write the direction of change at every step. One line: name the step where the signal is less firing, not more."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same loop skeleton twice in different colors, once for a fall in blood pressure and once for a rise. On each, mark with up or down arrows the receptor firing rate, sympathetic outflow, vagal outflow, heart rate, contractility, and arteriolar tone. Below them, draw a time axis comparing how fast this reflex acts against the hormonal control of pressure. One line: this reflex resets over days, so say what it cannot fix."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the baroreceptor reflex from the carotid sinus and aortic arch through the medulla to the heart and vessels and state the response to a fall in blood pressure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Baroreceptor reflex. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-cv-hormonal","name":"Hormonal control of blood pressure","can":"Describe the roles of angiotensin II, aldosterone, antidiuretic hormone, and atrial natriuretic peptide in long term blood pressure control.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A hiker drinks nothing for eight hours in the heat. Plasma osmolarity is 302 mOsm/L and blood volume is down about 6 percent. Which set of changes fits?\nA) Hormone up, aldosterone down, urine volume up\nB) Hormone down, aldosterone up, urine volume down\nC) Hormone down, aldosterone down, urine volume up\nD) Hormone up, aldosterone up, urine volume down\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Hormone up, aldosterone up, urine volume down","why":"Sweating loses more water than salt, so osmolarity rises and drives antidiuretic hormone, while the volume loss lowers renal perfusion and drives renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone. Both hormones conserve fluid, so the urine becomes small in volume and concentrated. The answers that raise one hormone while lowering the other split a pair that moves together, since in dehydration the osmotic and volume signals point the same way. Suppressing both with a large urine volume describes the state after drinking a lot of water, which is the opposite challenge."},{"t":"mc","q":"A narrowing of one renal artery lowers perfusion pressure to that kidney. Predict renin release and systemic blood pressure.\nA) Renin rises and systemic pressure falls\nB) Renin falls and systemic pressure rises\nC) Renin falls and systemic pressure falls\nD) Renin rises and systemic pressure rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Renin rises and systemic pressure rises","why":"The stenosed kidney senses a low perfusion pressure and cannot tell that the rest of the body is normal, so it secretes renin, the cascade generates angiotensin II and aldosterone, and systemic pressure climbs. The kidney is defending its own perfusion at the cost of the whole circulation, which is why this is a recognized cause of secondary hypertension. Any answer with renin falling misreads the trigger, and any answer with pressure falling ignores what angiotensin II and aldosterone do once they are made."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient takes a drug that inhibits angiotensin converting enzyme. Predict plasma angiotensin II and aldosterone.\nA) Angiotensin II is unchanged, and aldosterone falls\nB) Angiotensin II falls, and aldosterone falls with it\nC) Angiotensin II falls, but aldosterone rises instead\nD) Angiotensin II rises, because renin is inhibited\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Angiotensin II falls, and aldosterone falls with it","why":"Blocking the converting enzyme stops the production of angiotensin II, and because angiotensin II is the main stimulus for aldosterone secretion, aldosterone falls as a consequence. That is one hormone pulling the other, not two independent effects. Aldosterone cannot rise when its main stimulus is removed, unless plasma K+ climbs enough to drive it directly. Saying angiotensin II rises because renin is inhibited confuses the two enzymes, since renin is upstream and untouched by this drug, so angiotensin I accumulates while angiotensin II falls."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which hormone retains water without a matching gain of sodium, and therefore lowers plasma osmolarity?\nA) Aldosterone  |  B) Atrial natriuretic peptide\nC) Angiotensin II  |  D) Antidiuretic hormone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Antidiuretic hormone","why":"Antidiuretic hormone opens a water pathway in the collecting duct while leaving solute handling alone, so pure water is added to the body and plasma osmolarity falls. Aldosterone reabsorbs sodium with water following, which expands volume while keeping osmolarity close to normal, and that is the distinction between the two. Atrial natriuretic peptide removes sodium and water rather than retaining either. Angiotensin II raises pressure mainly through vasoconstriction and by driving the other two hormones, so its water effect is indirect."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma osmolarity rises from 290 to 302 mOsm/L. Predict antidiuretic hormone secretion and urine concentration.\nA) Hormone falls, urine becomes more concentrated\nB) Hormone falls, urine becomes more dilute\nC) Hormone rises, urine becomes more concentrated\nD) Hormone rises, urine becomes more dilute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Hormone rises, urine becomes more concentrated","why":"Hypothalamic osmoreceptors are sensitive to shifts of only one or two percent, so a 12 mOsm/L rise strongly stimulates antidiuretic hormone release, water is reabsorbed from the collecting duct, and a small volume of concentrated urine is produced. The two options with the hormone rising but urine diluting or the hormone falling but urine concentrating break the link between the hormone and its own renal action. Falling hormone with dilute urine is the correct response to drinking a large volume of water, which is the opposite stimulus."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman with a weak heart has a mean arterial pressure of 75 mmHg (normal 70 to 105) and reduced blood flow to her kidneys. Her plasma renin, aldosterone, and antidiuretic hormone are all elevated. Over the next month she gains 4 kg and her ankles swell, although her serum albumin is normal at 4.0 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.0).\nTrace how the hormone pattern leads to the weight gain and ankle swelling.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Low renal perfusion releases renin; angiotensin II and aldosterone increase sodium and water retention and ADH adds water retention; blood volume and venous pressure rise, capillary hydrostatic pressure rises, and fluid filters into the tissues","why":"The kidney reads a weak heart as low volume, because renal perfusion is poor. It releases renin, which produces angiotensin II, which raises aldosterone and, with the low pressure, ADH. All three hormones tell the kidney to hold onto salt and water. That would be the right response to bleeding, but here the heart cannot pump the extra volume, so it backs up in the veins. Venous pressure rises, capillary hydrostatic pressure rises, and fluid leaks into the tissues, mostly in the legs where gravity adds to the pressure. The weight gain is retained water."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a kidney, a liver, a lung, an adrenal gland, a posterior pituitary and a cardiac atrium on one page and connect them into the renin angiotensin aldosterone pathway in order, labeling where each conversion happens and what does it. Draw angiotensin II leaving for four destinations at once. Then draw atrial natriuretic peptide from the stretched atrium pushing the other way. One line: pick the arm you would block to lower pressure and say what you break along with it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person who has lost a liter of blood, then draw four hormones converging on that person's blood pressure: angiotensin II, aldosterone, antidiuretic hormone, and atrial natriuretic peptide. For each, draw whether it rises or falls and what it moves, water, sodium, or vessel diameter. Put a time axis under the drawing running from minutes to hours. One line: three of the four pull the same way and one is quiet here, so name it and say why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the roles of angiotensin II, aldosterone, antidiuretic hormone, and atrial natriuretic peptide in long term blood pressure control."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hormonal control of blood pressure. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-exercise-cv","name":"Cardiovascular response to exercise","can":"Predict the change in heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, total peripheral resistance, and regional blood flow distribution during moderate exercise.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A runner has a heart rate of 150 beats/min and a stroke volume of 120 mL. What is cardiac output?\nA) 1.8 L/min  |  B) 30 L/min\nC) 18 L/min  |  D) 12.5 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 18 L/min","why":"Multiply 150 beats per minute by 120 mL per beat to get 18000 mL/min, which is 18 L/min, a realistic value for hard exercise. The 12.5 L/min answer comes from dividing rather than multiplying. The 1.8 L/min answer is a decimal slip of one place and is well below even resting output, which is a useful check on any result. The 30 L/min answer exceeds what all but an elite endurance athlete can reach."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cardiac output during exercise is 20 L/min and heart rate is 160 beats/min. What is stroke volume?\nA) 12.5 mL  |  B) 125 mL\nC) 80 mL  |  D) 320 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 125 mL","why":"Rearranging cardiac output equals rate times stroke volume gives 20000 mL divided by 160 beats, which is 125 mL per beat. The 80 mL answer is a resting stroke volume and would give only 12.8 L/min at this rate. The 320 mL answer comes from multiplying instead of dividing and exceeds the volume a ventricle can hold. The 12.5 mL answer is off by a factor of ten and is far too small to support any exercise."},{"t":"mc","q":"Skeletal muscle receives about 20 percent of cardiac output at rest. What happens to muscle blood flow during heavy exercise?\nA) Share rises but total muscle flow stays about the same\nB) Share is unchanged while total cardiac output rises\nC) Share rises only because cardiac output falls elsewhere\nD) Share rises above 80 percent as total output rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Share rises above 80 percent as total output rises","why":"Two things multiply together: cardiac output can rise from 5 to 20 L/min or more, and muscle takes a much larger slice of it, so absolute muscle flow can rise more than twentyfold. If the share rose while flow stayed flat, the extra output would have to go somewhere else, and no other bed expands like this. An unchanged share would mean muscle gained only as much as everything else, which cannot support the observed oxygen consumption. Cardiac output does not fall anywhere during exercise, so explaining the larger share that way contradicts the central fact of the response."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A trained runner at rest has a heart rate of 50 beats/min and a stroke volume of 100 mL, with a mean arterial pressure of 90 mmHg (normal 70 to 105). At a moderate running pace her heart rate is 150 beats/min, her stroke volume is 130 mL, and her mean arterial pressure is 105 mmHg.\nCalculate her cardiac output at rest and during running. What must have happened to total peripheral resistance?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Cardiac output rose from 5 to about 19.5 L/min, almost fourfold, while mean pressure rose only 17 percent, so total peripheral resistance must have fallen to roughly a third of its resting value","why":"Cardiac output is heart rate times stroke volume. At rest, 50 times 100 mL is 5000 mL/min, or 5 L/min. Running, 150 times 130 mL is 19,500 mL/min, or 19.5 L/min. Mean pressure equals cardiac output times total peripheral resistance, so resistance is pressure divided by output. At rest that is 90 divided by 5, or 18 units. Running, it is 105 divided by 19.5, or about 5.4 units. Resistance fell to about 30 percent of resting. The fall comes from metabolic dilation of the arterioles in her working muscle."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During moderate exercise total peripheral resistance rises, because sympathetic outflow constricts arterioles in most organs.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. During moderate exercise total peripheral resistance falls, because metabolic dilation of arterioles in the large mass of working muscle outweighs sympathetic constriction in the gut and kidney.","why":"Sympathetic outflow does rise during exercise and does constrict the arterioles of the splanchnic and renal beds. But skeletal muscle is a large fraction of the body, and its arterioles dilate strongly in response to local metabolites, overriding the sympathetic signal. The net effect is a fall in total resistance. That fall is why mean arterial pressure rises only modestly even when cardiac output triples."},{"t":"bd","q":"Explain how the cardiovascular system redistributes blood during moderate exercise, and why mean arterial pressure rises only a little even though cardiac output may triple.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"sympathetic output rises and parasympathetic output falls, so heart rate and contractility rise; muscle pump raises venous return, so stroke volume rises; cardiac output rises several fold; arterioles in working muscle dilate because of local metabolites; sympathetic constriction of gut and kidney arterioles diverts flow to muscle, and skin flow rises to lose heat; total peripheral resistance falls because muscle dilation outweighs constriction elsewhere; mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output times resistance, so it rises only modestly","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw five small graphs in a row, all with time on the x axis running from rest through moderate exercise into recovery: heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, total peripheral resistance, and mean arterial pressure. Get the real shape of each, including the plateau in one and the fall in another. One line: output rose and resistance fell, so say what that combination did to mean pressure and why it is not a contradiction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline twice, at rest and during moderate exercise, and shade the share of cardiac output going to skeletal muscle, the gut and kidneys, the skin, the brain, and the heart muscle in each. Write the total output beside each figure, about 5 L/min at rest. One line: one of the five gets about the same absolute flow in both drawings, so name it and say what protects it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the change in heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, total peripheral resistance, and regional blood flow distribution during moderate exercise."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Cardiovascular response to exercise. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w11-shock-compensation","name":"Compensation in hemorrhage and shock","can":"Sequence the compensatory responses to blood loss and explain the point at which compensation fails.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Put the compensations for a 20 percent blood loss in the order in which they take effect.\nA) Baroreflex, fluid refill, salt and water hormones, red cells\nB) Salt and water hormones, baroreflex, red cells, fluid refill\nC) Fluid refill, red cells, baroreflex, salt and water hormones\nD) Red cells, salt and water hormones, baroreflex, fluid refill\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Baroreflex, fluid refill, salt and water hormones, red cells","why":"Neural signaling is fastest and acts in seconds, movement of interstitial fluid into the capillaries begins within minutes and continues for hours, renal salt and water conservation builds over minutes to hours and holds the gains, and red cell replacement takes days to weeks. The hormonal and fluid steps do overlap in time, but the fluid shift starts first and needs no new hormone to begin. Every other ordering puts a slow mechanism before a fast one, which would leave a gap of seconds to minutes during which pressure was undefended."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has lost 15 percent of blood volume. Predict heart rate, total peripheral resistance, and urine output.\nA) Heart rate up, resistance down, urine output up\nB) Heart rate down, resistance up, urine output down\nC) Heart rate up, resistance up, urine output up\nD) Heart rate up, resistance up, urine output down\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Heart rate up, resistance up, urine output down","why":"Baroreceptor firing falls, sympathetic outflow rises, and the result is tachycardia, widespread vasoconstriction including the renal arterioles, and a sharp drop in urine output that is reinforced by aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone. Falling resistance would give away the pressure the reflex is trying to defend. A slower heart rate would be the response to a pressure that was too high, not too low. Rising urine output would waste the very volume the body is trying to keep."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does urine output fall sharply after hemorrhage?\nA) Renal constriction plus antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone\nB) The kidneys are permanently damaged by the fall in pressure\nC) Antidiuretic hormone secretion is suppressed by the volume loss\nD) Filtered sodium is excreted faster than the tubules reabsorb it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Renal constriction plus antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone","why":"Three mechanisms point the same way: sympathetic constriction of the afferent arteriole lowers filtration, antidiuretic hormone reabsorbs water, and aldosterone reabsorbs sodium with water following. Low urine output is therefore an early and sensitive marker of hypovolemia. Acute kidney injury can follow prolonged hypotension, but the initial fall in output is a normal defense, not damage. Volume loss stimulates antidiuretic hormone rather than suppressing it, and sodium is conserved rather than dumped."},{"t":"mc","q":"Immediately after acute blood loss the hematocrit is nearly normal, but it falls over the next several hours. Why?\nA) The bone marrow halts red cell production immediately\nB) Plasma proteins leave the circulation faster than cells do\nC) Whole blood is lost first, then interstitial fluid enters\nD) Red cells are destroyed by the sympathetic response\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Whole blood is lost first, then interstitial fluid enters","why":"Hemorrhage removes plasma and red cells in the same proportion, so the ratio between them is unchanged at first, and only when transcapillary refill adds cell free fluid does the hematocrit drop. This is why an early normal hematocrit never rules out significant bleeding. Catecholamines do not lyse red cells, and the marrow responds to blood loss by making more cells, not fewer. Protein loss would raise the hematocrit by concentrating the cells rather than diluting them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why are cerebral and coronary blood flow relatively spared while the gut and kidneys are constricted?\nA) Their arterioles are too large in diameter to constrict\nB) Blood is redirected to them through the venous system\nC) They autoregulate and resist sympathetic constriction\nD) They receive extra sympathetic vasodilator fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They autoregulate and resist sympathetic constriction","why":"Brain and heart hold their own flow through local metabolic and myogenic control and carry sparse alpha-1 innervation, so the systemic vasoconstriction that shifts blood away from other organs passes them by. This is why a patient can be cold and oliguric while still awake. There is no sympathetic vasodilator supply to these beds in humans. Their arterioles are ordinary resistance vessels with normal smooth muscle, and blood cannot be routed to an organ through veins, which carry flow away."},{"t":"bd","q":"A person loses about 30 percent of their blood volume. Describe the compensations in the order they take effect, and explain why compensation can eventually fail.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"baroreflex within seconds: tachycardia, arteriolar constriction, venoconstriction; lower capillary hydrostatic pressure pulls interstitial fluid into the plasma over minutes to hours; renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone, and ADH conserve salt and water and stimulate thirst over hours; red cells and plasma proteins are replaced over days to weeks; if perfusion stays low, the heart and vessel smooth muscle become underperfused and weaken; falling cardiac output lowers perfusion further, a positive feedback loop that leads to irreversible shock","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a time axis running from seconds to days after a serious blood loss and place each compensation where it acts: the baroreceptor reflex, fluid shifting from the interstitium back into the capillaries, thirst and antidiuretic hormone, the renin angiotensin aldosterone system, and erythropoiesis. Draw the blood pressure trace above the axis. One line: only one of these restores oxygen carrying rather than volume, so name it and say how long it needs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the compensation first as a negative feedback loop that is holding, then redraw the same loop as a positive feedback spiral: falling pressure, falling coronary and cerebral perfusion, a weaker pump and a failing vasomotor center, falling pressure again. Mark the point where you would say compensation has failed. One line: name the sign at the bedside that tells you the patient has crossed it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to sequence the compensatory responses to blood loss and explain the point at which compensation fails."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Compensation in hemorrhage and shock. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Blood","comps":[{"id":"w10-plasma","name":"Plasma and blood functions","can":"State the three functional categories of blood, transport, regulation, and protection, and name the major plasma proteins and the function of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Insulin travels from the pancreas through the blood to liver and muscle cells. Which functional category of blood does this illustrate?\nA) Coagulation  |  B) Transport\nC) Regulation  |  D) Protection\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Transport","why":"Carrying a hormone from the gland that makes it to the tissue that responds is a delivery job, and delivery is transport. Regulation refers to blood stabilizing pH, temperature and fluid volume rather than to carrying a messenger. Protection covers clotting and immune defense. Coagulation is one specific mechanism inside protection, not a category of its own."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tube of blood is allowed to clot and the fluid above the clot is analyzed. Which measurement will come back essentially zero?\nA) Albumin  |  B) Glucose\nC) Fibrinogen  |  D) Sodium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Fibrinogen","why":"Clotting converts fibrinogen into the fibrin strands of the clot, so the serum left above contains almost none of it. Sodium and glucose are small solutes that stay dissolved in the fluid regardless of clotting. Albumin is not consumed by the clotting reaction and remains at its usual concentration. If you need a fibrinogen level, the sample has to be drawn into an anticoagulant so that plasma, not serum, is obtained."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma bicarbonate takes up a hydrogen ion produced by exercising muscle, keeping arterial pH near 7.4. Which functional category of blood is this?\nA) Regulation  |  B) Transport\nC) Protection  |  D) Filtration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Regulation","why":"Buffering hydrogen ions to hold pH in a narrow range is a homeostatic job, and homeostatic jobs of blood are filed under regulation. Transport would describe carrying the carbon dioxide that generated the hydrogen ion, a separate step. Protection covers clotting and immune defense against injury and pathogens. Filtration is a kidney process, not a category of blood function."},{"t":"mc","q":"An antibody in plasma binds to a bacterial toxin and prevents it from attaching to a host cell. Which functional category of blood does this belong to?\nA) Buffering  |  B) Protection\nC) Regulation  |  D) Transport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Protection","why":"Immune defense and clotting are both grouped under protection, because both defend the body against a threat. Regulation is about holding pH, temperature and fluid volume steady. Transport is the delivery of gases, nutrients and hormones. Buffering is one specific mechanism inside regulation, not a category."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the three functional categories of blood?\nA) Transport, regulation, and protection\nB) Filtration, secretion, and absorption\nC) Conduction, contraction, and secretion\nD) Digestion, storage, and excretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Transport, regulation, and protection","why":"Physiology texts group everything blood does into transport of gases, nutrients and wastes, regulation of pH, temperature and fluid balance, and protection through clotting and immunity. Filtration, secretion and absorption are the operations of the kidney tubule and gut, not categories of blood function. Conduction and contraction belong to excitable tissues. Digestion, storage and excretion describe organ systems that blood serves rather than what blood itself does."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and is the main contributor to plasma colloid osmotic pressure.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Colloid osmotic pressure depends on the number of protein particles, not their size. Albumin is both abundant and relatively small, so it wins on particle count. Lose albumin, as in liver failure or nephrotic syndrome, and fluid leaks out of the capillaries into tissue. Students sometimes credit fibrinogen or globulins, but those are larger and far less numerous."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a centrifuged blood tube with the layers to scale: plasma at about 55 percent, the buffy coat as a thin line, packed red cells at about 45 percent. Beside the plasma layer, draw its contents split by volume, mostly water with a protein slice, and name the three protein groups with the job of each. One line: name the protein group that holds fluid inside the vessels and say what happens to that fluid when it runs low."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three columns headed transport, regulation, and protection, and under each draw two things your blood is doing right now with the component doing it, a cell, a protein, or an ion. Use real cargo: oxygen, glucose, heat, hydrogen ion, a clotting factor, an antibody. One line: name the single job that would kill you fastest if blood stopped doing it, and say roughly how fast."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the three functional categories of blood, transport, regulation, and protection, and name the major plasma proteins and the function of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Plasma and blood functions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-hematocrit","name":"Hematocrit and formed elements","can":"Define hematocrit and interpret a value as normal or abnormal in the context of anemia and dehydration.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An adult male has a hematocrit of 30 percent. How should this be interpreted?\nA) It is within the normal range for an adult male\nB) It is above the normal range and indicates polycythemia\nC) It is below normal and indicates dehydration\nD) It is below the normal range and indicates anemia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It is below the normal range and indicates anemia","why":"Thirty percent sits well under the 42 to 52 percent male range, so the blood is carrying fewer red cells than it should and oxygen capacity is reduced. It is not within the male range, and it is not even within the female range. Polycythemia would require a value well above the range, not below it. Dehydration concentrates the blood and raises hematocrit, so it moves the number in the opposite direction."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does hematocrit measure?\nA) The percentage of whole blood volume occupied by plasma\nB) The mass of hemoglobin in a given volume of blood\nC) The number of erythrocytes in a microliter of blood\nD) The percentage of whole blood volume occupied by erythrocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The percentage of whole blood volume occupied by erythrocytes","why":"Hematocrit is a packed cell volume: spin the sample and read what fraction of the column is red cells. The plasma fraction is the remainder, roughly 55 percent, and is not what the number reports. Hemoglobin mass per volume is a separate measurement reported in g/dL. Red cell count per microliter is a third measurement, reported in millions per microliter, and it can move independently of hematocrit if cell size changes."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the normal hematocrit range for an adult male?\nA) About 42 to 52 percent  |  B) About 30 to 38 percent\nC) About 55 to 65 percent  |  D) About 12 to 18 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 42 to 52 percent","why":"Adult males run higher than females because testosterone stimulates erythropoiesis, giving a typical range in the low to mid forties. A value of 30 to 38 percent would be anemia in a male. A value of 55 to 65 percent would be polycythemia, from dehydration, chronic hypoxia or a marrow disorder. The 12 to 18 range is hemoglobin in g/dL, not hematocrit."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a centrifuged blood sample, what is contained in the thin buffy coat?\nA) Albumin and fibrinogen  |  B) Bilirubin and free hemoglobin\nC) Leukocytes and platelets  |  D) Reticulocytes and young erythrocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Leukocytes and platelets","why":"White cells and platelets are less dense than red cells but denser than plasma, so they settle into a narrow pale band at the interface, usually about 1 percent of the volume. Reticulocytes are young red cells and pack down with the rest of the red cell column. Albumin and fibrinogen are dissolved in the plasma above. Bilirubin and free hemoglobin, if present, also stay in the plasma and would tint it yellow or pink."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a normal centrifuged sample, about what fraction of whole blood volume is plasma?\nA) About 45 percent  |  B) About 92 percent\nC) About 1 percent  |  D) About 55 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 55 percent","why":"Plasma and formed elements split whole blood roughly 55 to 45, which is why a normal hematocrit sits in the forties. The 45 percent figure is the formed element fraction, the hematocrit itself. The 92 percent figure is the water content of plasma, a fraction of a fraction. The 1 percent figure is the buffy coat."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four spun hematocrit tubes of equal length, filled to the right level: a normal man at about 45 percent, a normal woman at about 40 percent, someone with iron deficiency anemia, and someone badly dehydrated. Write the reference ranges beside them, about 42 to 52 percent in men and 37 to 47 percent in women. One line: hematocrit is a ratio, so name the tube that reads abnormal while the number of red cells is normal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a blood sample as one tall bar, split into plasma and formed elements, then split the formed elements into red cells, white cells and platelets at their true relative numbers, with red cells swamping the other two. Beside it, draw the same bar for the same person after two liters of intravenous saline. One line: say what the saline did to the hematocrit and what it did not do to the red cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define hematocrit and interpret a value as normal or abnormal in the context of anemia and dehydration."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hematocrit and formed elements. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-rbc-hemoglobin","name":"Erythrocyte structure and hemoglobin","can":"Relate the biconcave shape and absent nucleus of the erythrocyte to its function and describe how the four heme groups of hemoglobin carry oxygen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the absence of mitochondria in the erythrocyte an advantage?\nA) The cell can synthesize new hemoglobin as it ages\nB) The cell becomes rigid enough to resist capillary pressure\nC) The cell cannot consume the oxygen it is transporting\nD) The cell can divide more rapidly in the circulation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The cell cannot consume the oxygen it is transporting","why":"A red cell running oxidative phosphorylation would burn some of its own cargo on every trip; relying on glycolysis instead means every oxygen molecule it picks up is delivered. Mature red cells cannot divide at all, having lost the nucleus. They cannot synthesize hemoglobin either, for the same reason. Losing mitochondria makes the cell more deformable, not more rigid, and rigidity would be a disadvantage in capillaries."},{"t":"mc","q":"If each gram of hemoglobin can carry 1.34 mL of oxygen and a patient has 15 g/dL of hemoglobin, what is the oxygen carrying capacity of 100 mL of blood?\nA) About 200 mL of oxygen  |  B) About 10 mL of oxygen\nC) About 20 mL of oxygen  |  D) About 2 mL of oxygen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 20 mL of oxygen","why":"Multiply 15 g/dL by 1.34 mL per gram to get about 20.1 mL of oxygen per 100 mL of blood, the standard figure for a healthy adult. About 2 mL would be off by a factor of ten and is closer to the amount dissolved in plasma. About 200 mL is a factor of ten too large. About 10 mL would correspond to a hemoglobin of roughly 7.5 g/dL, that is, a markedly anemic patient."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many oxygen molecules can one fully saturated hemoglobin molecule carry?\nA) Two  |  B) Eight\nC) Four  |  D) One\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Four","why":"Hemoglobin has four globin chains, each holding one heme group with one iron atom, and each iron binds one oxygen molecule, giving four per hemoglobin. One is the number per heme group, not per hemoglobin. Two is the number of chains of each type, alpha and beta, not the oxygen count. Eight would require two oxygen molecules per iron, which the heme pocket cannot accommodate."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which atom sits at the center of a heme group and binds oxygen?\nA) Magnesium in the divalent state, Mg2+\nB) Copper in the cupric state, Cu2+\nC) Zinc in the divalent state, Zn2+\nD) Iron in the ferrous state, Fe2+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Iron in the ferrous state, Fe2+","why":"Each heme is a porphyrin ring holding one Fe2+, and oxygen binds reversibly to that ferrous iron without oxidizing it. Magnesium sits at the center of chlorophyll, not heme. Copper is the oxygen carrier metal in some invertebrate hemocyanins and a cofactor in cytochrome oxidase, but not in human hemoglobin. Zinc is a common enzyme cofactor, for example in carbonic anhydrase, but it does not bind oxygen in blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"What globin chains make up one molecule of normal adult hemoglobin?\nA) Two alpha chains and two beta chains\nB) Four identical alpha chains\nC) Two alpha chains and two gamma chains\nD) One alpha chain and three beta chains\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Two alpha chains and two beta chains","why":"Adult hemoglobin, HbA, is a tetramer of two alpha and two beta globins, each carrying one heme. Four identical chains would not match the two gene families that produce hemoglobin. Two alpha and two gamma chains describe fetal hemoglobin, HbF, which has a higher oxygen affinity than the adult form. A one to three arrangement does not occur."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Oxygen binds to the globin protein chains of hemoglobin; the iron in heme only gives blood its red color.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Oxygen binds reversibly to the iron atom at the center of each of the four heme groups, so one hemoglobin molecule can carry up to four oxygen molecules.","why":"The globin chains are the scaffold. They hold the heme groups in place and shift shape as oxygen loads, which raises affinity for the next oxygen, but the actual binding site is the iron. This matters clinically: without enough iron the marrow cannot make working hemoglobin no matter how much globin it produces, and carbon monoxide poisons oxygen transport by taking the iron site. The color change from dark to bright red is a side effect of iron binding oxygen, not the point of it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a red cell in face view and in cross section, biconcave, about 7.5 micrometers across, with no nucleus and no mitochondria. Beside each feature write what it buys: short diffusion distance, surface area, room for hemoglobin, and the ability to fold through a capillary narrower than the cell. One line: it has no mitochondria, so say how it makes ATP and why that is convenient given what it is carrying."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one hemoglobin molecule big, with its four globin chains, a heme group on each, and the iron atom at the center of each heme. Show how many oxygen molecules it holds when fully loaded. Then draw the same molecule at three loading states, empty, half, and full. One line: say why loading the first oxygen makes the next one easier, and what that does to the shape of the loading curve."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate the biconcave shape and absent nucleus of the erythrocyte to its function and describe how the four heme groups of hemoglobin carry oxygen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Erythrocyte structure and hemoglobin. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-erythropoiesis","name":"Erythropoiesis and its regulation","can":"Trace the erythropoietin negative feedback loop from tissue hypoxia to increased red cell production and state the nutrients required.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with chronic iron deficiency has anemia. What is characteristic of the red cells produced?\nA) They are small and pale, because each cell contains less hemoglobin\nB) They are large and deeply colored, because hemoglobin is concentrated\nC) They are normal in size and color but shorter lived\nD) They contain nuclei, because maturation is arrested\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) They are small and pale, because each cell contains less hemoglobin","why":"Iron is the core of heme, so when it runs short the precursors divide an extra time while filling with less hemoglobin, giving microcytic hypochromic cells. Large deeply colored cells are not produced, since less hemoglobin means less color. Size and color both change, which is what distinguishes this from vitamin deficiency and from blood loss. Nucleated red cells appear in the blood only under extreme marrow stress and are not the defining feature here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which organ secretes most of the erythropoietin in an adult?\nA) The spleen  |  B) The kidney\nC) The liver  |  D) The red bone marrow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The kidney","why":"Interstitial cells of the kidney sense oxygen delivery and release about 90 percent of circulating erythropoietin, which is why kidney disease causes anemia. The liver makes a small share in adults and is the main source in the fetus, but it cannot compensate for failed kidneys. The marrow is the target of erythropoietin, not its source. The spleen destroys old red cells rather than stimulating new ones."},{"t":"mc","q":"What stimulus triggers erythropoietin release?\nA) A rise in arterial carbon dioxide\nB) A fall in plasma potassium\nC) Reduced oxygen delivery to the kidney\nD) A rise in plasma iron concentration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Reduced oxygen delivery to the kidney","why":"The kidney responds to hypoxia, whether from anemia, altitude, or lung disease, by increasing erythropoietin so that more oxygen carriers are built. Plasma iron is a raw material for hemoglobin and does not itself signal the kidney. Carbon dioxide drives ventilation through central and peripheral chemoreceptors, not erythropoietin release. Potassium regulates aldosterone secretion, a different loop entirely."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with long standing kidney disease has a hematocrit of 27 percent (normal 40 to 54 for males), a reticulocyte count of 0.3 percent (normal 0.5 to 1.5), normal iron stores, and a normal arterial PO2 of 95 mmHg (normal 80 to 100).\nWhich mechanism best explains why the hematocrit is low, and what does the low reticulocyte count tell you?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The damaged kidney is not secreting enough erythropoietin, so the marrow is under stimulated; the low reticulocyte count shows that few new cells are being made","why":"The loop is broken at the sensor and signal step. Tissue oxygen delivery is low because there are too few red cells, but the diseased kidney cannot answer with erythropoietin, so the marrow never gets the message. Reticulocytes are red cells released in the last day or two, so a low count means low production. A marrow that was responding to anemia would push reticulocytes well above normal. Giving erythropoietin restores the signal and the count rises."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Erythropoietin is released by the bone marrow when it senses that oxygen delivery has fallen.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Erythropoietin is released mainly by the kidney in response to low tissue oxygen, and it travels in the blood to the bone marrow, which is the target that responds by making more red cells.","why":"The feedback loop has a sensor, a signal, and an effector, and they are in different organs. The kidney senses oxygen and makes the hormone. The marrow receives the hormone and makes the cells. Keeping them separate explains a common clinical picture: a patient with failing kidneys becomes anemic even though the marrow is healthy, because the signal is missing, and injecting erythropoietin fixes it."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace what happens in the erythropoietin feedback loop over the first month after a person moves from sea level to 3,500 m altitude. Include what turns the loop on, what turns it off, and what the marrow needs to respond.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"low atmospheric oxygen lowers tissue oxygen delivery (hypoxia); the kidney senses hypoxia and secretes erythropoietin; erythropoietin acts on the bone marrow to increase red cell production; new red cells take days to mature, so hematocrit rises over one to several weeks; the marrow requires iron, folate, and vitamin B12 to make the new cells; as red cell mass rises, oxygen delivery improves and erythropoietin secretion falls (negative feedback)","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the erythropoietin loop closed: a tissue low in oxygen, the kidney sensing it, erythropoietin traveling in blood, the red bone marrow, new red cells, oxygen carrying restored, and the signal switching off. Label the sensor, the integrator and the effector on your drawing. Beside the marrow, draw the raw materials it needs, iron, vitamin B12, folate and amino acids. One line: name the step that breaks in chronic kidney disease and say what that does to the loop."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the maturation series from stem cell down to reticulocyte and mature red cell, showing the nucleus shrinking and being ejected while hemoglobin fills in. Beside it, draw three failure points: no iron, no vitamin B12, and no erythropoietin, with the kind of cell each one leaves you holding. One line: two of these give cells of the wrong size in opposite directions, so say which is small and which is large."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the erythropoietin negative feedback loop from tissue hypoxia to increased red cell production and state the nutrients required."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Erythropoiesis and its regulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-rbc-destruction","name":"Red cell destruction and bilirubin","can":"Trace the fate of hemoglobin after red cell breakdown and explain how failure to clear bilirubin produces jaundice.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Unconjugated bilirubin is not water soluble. How does it travel through plasma to the liver?\nA) Bound to albumin  |  B) Dissolved freely in plasma water\nC) Inside erythrocytes  |  D) Bound to transferrin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Bound to albumin","why":"Albumin has hydrophobic binding pockets that carry poorly soluble molecules such as unconjugated bilirubin, fatty acids and many drugs. Free dissolution is exactly what its insolubility prevents. It is not carried inside red cells, since it is produced after those cells have been destroyed. Transferrin is specific for iron and does not carry bilirubin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where are worn out erythrocytes removed from the circulation?\nA) By macrophages in the spleen and liver\nB) By filtration in the kidney glomerulus\nC) By neutrophils in the capillary bed\nD) By the red bone marrow that produced them\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) By macrophages in the spleen and liver","why":"Aged, stiff red cells fail to squeeze through narrow splenic channels and are engulfed by resident macrophages there and in the liver, which is why the spleen is sometimes called the red cell graveyard. The glomerulus does not filter whole cells, and hemoglobin in the urine is a sign that something has gone wrong. Neutrophils attack bacteria rather than clearing senescent host cells. The marrow builds red cells and does not destroy them under normal conditions."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a macrophage breaks down hemoglobin, what does the heme portion become, once the iron is removed?\nA) Ferritin, which is stored in the liver\nB) Biliverdin, which is then converted to bilirubin\nC) Urea, which is excreted by the kidney\nD) Amino acids, which are returned to the pool\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Biliverdin, which is then converted to bilirubin","why":"The porphyrin ring is opened to green biliverdin and quickly reduced to yellow bilirubin, the pigment that must then be handled by the liver. Urea comes from protein nitrogen metabolism, not from the heme ring. Amino acids come from the globin chains, the other half of the molecule. Ferritin is the storage protein for the iron that was pulled out before the ring was opened."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which plasma protein transports iron released from broken down hemoglobin?\nA) Albumin  |  B) Haptoglobin\nC) Transferrin  |  D) Ferritin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Transferrin","why":"Transferrin binds free iron in the plasma and delivers it to the marrow or to storage, keeping reactive iron from circulating unbound. Ferritin is the intracellular storage form, not the transport form. Albumin carries fatty acids, drugs and unconjugated bilirubin but not iron. Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin released when cells lyse inside vessels."},{"t":"mc","q":"In what form is iron stored inside liver and spleen cells?\nA) As transferrin  |  B) As heme\nC) As bilirubin  |  D) As ferritin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) As ferritin","why":"Ferritin is a hollow protein shell that holds thousands of iron atoms in a safe, non reactive form and releases them when the marrow needs them. Transferrin is the plasma carrier that moves iron between sites. Heme is iron already built into a porphyrin ring inside hemoglobin. Bilirubin is the waste pigment left after the iron has been removed and contains no iron at all."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a red cell at the end of its 120 days being taken apart by a macrophage in the spleen, and follow all three products out on separate arrows: globin to amino acids, iron bound and shipped back to the marrow, heme to biliverdin to bilirubin. Carry the bilirubin to the liver, into bile, into the gut, and out. Label what colors the stool and the urine. One line: almost nothing here is wasted, so say what actually leaves the body and why it has to."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three jaundiced patients with the block in a different place in each: massive red cell breakdown, liver cell failure, and a stone in the bile duct. Draw the same pathway under each and dam it at the right point. Show what backs up and what color the stool is in each. One line: one of the three has pale stool, so name it and say why the color locates the block."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the fate of hemoglobin after red cell breakdown and explain how failure to clear bilirubin produces jaundice."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Red cell destruction and bilirubin. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-leukocytes","name":"Leukocytes","can":"Match neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils to their defensive function and to the condition that raises each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has acute appendicitis from a bacterial infection. Which leukocyte will be most elevated?\nA) Basophils  |  B) Lymphocytes\nC) Neutrophils  |  D) Eosinophils\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Neutrophils","why":"Neutrophils are recruited within hours to bacterial sites and the marrow releases its reserve pool, so acute bacterial infection typically shows neutrophilia. Eosinophils rise with parasites and allergy, not with ordinary bacterial infection. Basophils are few in number and rise mainly with allergic and some chronic conditions. Lymphocytes lead the response to viral infection instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student has infectious mononucleosis, a viral illness. Which leukocyte pattern is expected?\nA) Neutrophilia, an increase in neutrophils\nB) Eosinophilia, an increase in eosinophils\nC) Basophilia, an increase in basophils\nD) Lymphocytosis, an increase in lymphocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Lymphocytosis, an increase in lymphocytes","why":"Viruses replicate inside host cells, so the response depends on lymphocytes that recognize infected cells and produce antibody, and their numbers climb. Neutrophils dominate bacterial infections and are often unremarkable or even reduced in viral illness. Eosinophils track parasites and allergy. Basophils are too scarce to define a response pattern."},{"t":"mc","q":"A traveler returns with an intestinal roundworm infection. Which leukocyte count rises?\nA) The monocyte count  |  B) The eosinophil count\nC) The basophil count  |  D) The neutrophil count\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The eosinophil count","why":"Worms are far too large to phagocytose, so the body sends eosinophils to coat them and release destructive granule proteins onto their surface, and the blood count rises markedly. Basophils act in immediate allergic reactions by releasing histamine. Neutrophils engulf bacteria and small particles. Monocytes and their macrophages handle chronic intracellular infections and tissue debris."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which leukocyte is the most abundant in normal blood?\nA) The monocyte  |  B) The eosinophil\nC) The neutrophil  |  D) The lymphocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The neutrophil","why":"Neutrophils are roughly 60 to 70 percent of circulating white cells, matching their role as the first responders to bacterial invasion. Lymphocytes are second at about 25 percent. Monocytes are about 5 percent. Eosinophils are only 2 to 4 percent and rise mainly with parasites and allergy."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which leukocyte leaves the blood and matures into a tissue macrophage?\nA) The neutrophil  |  B) The basophil\nC) The lymphocyte  |  D) The monocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The monocyte","why":"Monocytes circulate briefly and then enter tissue, where they enlarge into long lived macrophages that phagocytose debris and present antigen. Neutrophils also phagocytose but die at the site within a day or two and do not become macrophages. Basophils release histamine and heparin rather than transforming. Lymphocytes mediate specific immunity and do not become phagocytes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the five white cells at their true relative numbers, so neutrophils crowd your field and basophils are one or two cells. Draw each with the nucleus shape and granules you would use to identify it under the microscope. Beside each, write its defensive job and one condition that raises it. One line: you are handed a differential with high eosinophils, so name the two causes you would consider first."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four panels: a bacterial infection, a viral infection, a parasitic worm, and an allergic reaction. In each, draw which white cell arrives and what it does on arrival, engulfing, killing an infected host cell, or emptying granules onto a surface. One line: name the panel where the cell cannot swallow its target and say what it does instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils to their defensive function and to the condition that raises each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Leukocytes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-hemostasis","name":"Hemostasis","can":"Order the three phases of hemostasis, vascular spasm, platelet plug formation, and coagulation, and identify the role of von Willebrand factor and thromboxane.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What exposed material in the vessel wall triggers platelet adhesion?\nA) Subendothelial collagen  |  B) Prostacyclin\nC) Fibrin  |  D) Elastin in the tunica media\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Subendothelial collagen","why":"Endothelium normally hides the collagen underneath it, so a break uncovers a surface that von Willebrand factor and platelet receptors bind avidly, marking the exact site of injury. Prostacyclin is an endothelial inhibitor of adhesion, the opposite signal. Fibrin appears later, after coagulation has been triggered, so it is a consequence and not the trigger. Elastin gives the vessel recoil but is not the adhesion signal."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the first phase of hemostasis after a vessel is cut?\nA) Vascular spasm  |  B) Platelet plug formation\nC) Coagulation  |  D) Fibrinolysis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Vascular spasm","why":"Injury makes the vessel wall smooth muscle contract immediately, narrowing the lumen and cutting blood loss before anything else has time to happen. Platelet plug formation comes second, once collagen is exposed and platelets can adhere. Coagulation is third and builds the fibrin mesh that stabilizes the plug. Fibrinolysis is the later removal of the clot, not part of the three phases of stopping bleeding."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the correct order of the three phases of hemostasis?\nA) Vascular spasm, coagulation, platelet plug formation\nB) Vascular spasm, platelet plug formation, coagulation\nC) Platelet plug formation, vascular spasm, coagulation\nD) Coagulation, vascular spasm, platelet plug formation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Vascular spasm, platelet plug formation, coagulation","why":"Each phase buys time for the next: spasm reduces flow in seconds, the platelet plug seals small breaks in a minute or two, and coagulation reinforces the plug with fibrin over several minutes. Starting with the platelet plug skips the immediate muscular response. Coagulation cannot come first because it needs a platelet surface and exposed tissue factor to get going. Putting coagulation before the platelet plug reverses the scaffold and the reinforcement."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does von Willebrand factor do?\nA) It causes vascular smooth muscle to relax\nB) It dissolves the fibrin strands of the clot once healing is complete\nC) It bridges platelets to collagen exposed in the damaged vessel wall\nD) It converts fibrinogen into fibrin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It bridges platelets to collagen exposed in the damaged vessel wall","why":"Von Willebrand factor is a large adhesive protein that grips subendothelial collagen at one end and a platelet surface receptor at the other, making adhesion possible under flowing blood. Converting fibrinogen to fibrin is the job of thrombin. Relaxing vascular smooth muscle is the opposite of what injury calls for and describes nitric oxide. Dissolving fibrin is the work of plasmin during fibrinolysis."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does thromboxane A2 released by activated platelets do?\nA) It inhibits platelet aggregation and dilates the vessel\nB) It converts prothrombin into thrombin\nC) It cross links fibrin strands\nD) It promotes platelet aggregation and constricts the vessel\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It promotes platelet aggregation and constricts the vessel","why":"Thromboxane A2 is made from arachidonic acid by cyclooxygenase in the platelet and does two pro hemostatic jobs at once, recruiting more platelets and narrowing the vessel. Inhibiting aggregation and dilating the vessel describes prostacyclin, the endothelial counterpart that keeps the plug from spreading. Converting prothrombin to thrombin is done by factor Xa in the common pathway. Cross linking fibrin is the job of factor XIII."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Vascular spasm is the first response to a vessel injury and reduces blood flow through the wound before a platelet plug has formed.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The three phases of hemostasis overlap but start in order: spasm within seconds, platelet plug within a minute or two, and fibrin over several minutes. The spasm is not enough on its own, but it drops the pressure and flow at the wound so that platelets can stick rather than being washed away. Thromboxane A2 and serotonin from activated platelets later reinforce it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cut vessel across three panels in time order: vascular spasm, platelet plug, coagulation. In the middle panel, draw the exposed collagen, von Willebrand factor bridging a platelet to it, and the platelets releasing ADP and thromboxane A2 to recruit more. Show what thromboxane does to the vessel itself. One line: the plug builds by positive feedback, so say what stops it spreading down the whole vessel."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same cut vessel three times, with one phase disabled in each: no vascular spasm, no functioning platelets, no coagulation factors. Draw what the bleeding looks like in each and how quickly it shows. One line: say which failure you would notice first from a paper cut and which you would only notice from a deep injury."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the three phases of hemostasis, vascular spasm, platelet plug formation, and coagulation, and identify the role of von Willebrand factor and thromboxane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hemostasis. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-coagulation","name":"Coagulation cascade","can":"Compare the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways to the common pathway and identify the roles of thrombin, fibrin, calcium, and vitamin K.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a prolonged prothrombin time with a normal partial thromboplastin time. Where is the defect?\nA) In the common pathway, most likely factor X\nB) In platelet function, most likely von Willebrand factor\nC) In the extrinsic pathway, most likely factor VII\nD) In the intrinsic pathway, most likely factor VIII\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In the extrinsic pathway, most likely factor VII","why":"Only one test is abnormal, so the defect must lie in a factor unique to that pathway, and factor VII is the only factor tested exclusively by prothrombin time. A factor VIII problem would prolong the partial thromboplastin time instead, since factor VIII is unique to the intrinsic route. A common pathway defect would prolong both tests, because both routes pass through it. Platelet defects do not lengthen either cascade test."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pathway generates fibrin faster after a traumatic injury, and why?\nA) The intrinsic pathway, because it needs no external trigger\nB) The extrinsic pathway, because it does not require calcium\nC) The intrinsic pathway, because factor XII is the most abundant factor\nD) The extrinsic pathway, because it takes fewer steps to reach factor X\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The extrinsic pathway, because it takes fewer steps to reach factor X","why":"Tissue factor plus factor VII activates factor X almost directly, so fibrin appears in about fifteen seconds, while the intrinsic pathway needs a longer chain of activations and takes several minutes. Needing no external trigger does not make a pathway faster; it makes it slower here because more steps are involved. Both pathways require calcium. Abundance of a factor does not determine the speed of the sequence."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with advanced liver disease bleeds easily. What is the mechanism?\nA) The liver stores platelets, so the platelet count falls to zero\nB) The liver produces calcium for the cascade, so calcium falls\nC) The liver secretes plasmin, so clots dissolve too fast\nD) The liver synthesizes most clotting factors, so factor levels fall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The liver synthesizes most clotting factors, so factor levels fall","why":"Fibrinogen, prothrombin and nearly every other factor are hepatocyte products, so failing liver tissue reduces the supply and the cascade runs short of substrate. The spleen, not the liver, sequesters platelets, and the count falls but not to zero. Plasma calcium is regulated by parathyroid hormone and vitamin D, not by liver synthesis. Plasmin is generated from plasminogen by tissue plasminogen activator, and the liver actually makes plasminogen rather than secreting active plasmin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which laboratory test evaluates the extrinsic and common pathways?\nA) The prothrombin time  |  B) The activated partial thromboplastin time\nC) The bleeding time  |  D) The platelet count\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The prothrombin time","why":"Prothrombin time is run by adding tissue factor to plasma, so it measures how fast the extrinsic route reaches fibrin and is the test that responds to warfarin and to factor VII deficiency. The activated partial thromboplastin time uses a contact activator and tests the intrinsic and common pathways instead. Bleeding time assesses platelet function rather than the cascade. The platelet count is a number of cells and says nothing about factor activity."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A blood sample is drawn into a tube containing sodium citrate, which binds calcium ions. After an hour at room temperature the blood has not clotted. A technician adds calcium chloride to a portion of the sample, and it clots within a few minutes.\nWhat does this experiment show about the role of calcium in coagulation?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Calcium is a required cofactor at several steps in the cascade, so removing it stops the cascade at those steps and restoring it lets the cascade run","why":"Several clotting factors, including the vitamin K dependent ones, must bind calcium to attach to the phospholipid surface of platelets where the cascade assembles. Take calcium away and the enzymes are intact but cannot assemble, so nothing happens. Add it back and they work immediately. This is why citrate and EDTA tubes keep blood liquid in the laboratory, and why the same sample can be clotted on demand to run a coagulation test."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways each get started, where they join, and what thrombin does once it is formed. Include the roles of calcium and vitamin K.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"extrinsic pathway is triggered by tissue factor from damaged tissue outside the vessel; intrinsic pathway is triggered by contact of factor XII with exposed collagen; both pathways activate factor X, which starts the common pathway; activated factor X converts prothrombin to thrombin; thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin and activates factor XIII to cross link the fibrin mesh; calcium is needed at multiple steps of the cascade; vitamin K is needed by the liver to make working factors II, VII, IX, and X","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the cascade as two branches meeting one trunk. On one side the intrinsic pathway starting from contact with exposed collagen, on the other the extrinsic pathway starting from tissue factor released by damaged tissue, joining at factor X. Then draw the common trunk: prothrombin to thrombin, fibrinogen to fibrin, and the mesh. Mark every point where calcium is required. One line: the two branches start differently, so say which is faster and what it starts from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the finished clot as a fibrin mesh with red cells caught in it, then work backwards up the cascade, writing at each step the factor that made it happen. Circle the four factors the liver cannot make without vitamin K (II, VII, IX and X) and mark where each sits in your drawing. One line: a patient on warfarin has a normal platelet count, so say what part of your drawing still works and what does not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways to the common pathway and identify the roles of thrombin, fibrin, calcium, and vitamin K."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Coagulation cascade. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-fibrinolysis","name":"Clot limitation and fibrinolysis","can":"Explain how anticoagulants and plasmin limit and remove a clot and predict the effect of a drug that blocks vitamin K or platelet aggregation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a markedly elevated D-dimer. What does this indicate?\nA) Platelet count is elevated\nB) Vitamin K stores are depleted\nC) A clot has formed somewhere and plasmin is breaking it down\nD) The patient cannot form clots at all, anywhere in the circulation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A clot has formed somewhere and plasmin is breaking it down","why":"D-dimer is released only when cross linked fibrin is digested, so its presence proves both that clotting occurred and that fibrinolysis is under way. An inability to clot would produce no fibrin and therefore no degradation fragments. Platelet count is measured separately and is not reflected in this fragment. Vitamin K status is assessed through the prothrombin time, not through fibrin fragments."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme digests the fibrin mesh of a clot?\nA) Factor XIIIa  |  B) Carbonic anhydrase\nC) Plasmin  |  D) Thrombin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Plasmin","why":"Plasmin is a protease that cleaves fibrin into soluble fragments, gradually dismantling the clot as the vessel heals. Thrombin does the opposite, generating fibrin from fibrinogen. Factor XIIIa strengthens fibrin by cross linking it. Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the reaction between carbon dioxide and water and has no role in clotting."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the inactive precursor of plasmin?\nA) Prothrombin  |  B) Fibrinogen\nC) Prekallikrein  |  D) Plasminogen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Plasminogen","why":"Plasminogen circulates inactive and is trapped inside the clot as it forms, so the tool for taking the clot apart is built into the clot itself. Prothrombin is the precursor of thrombin, which builds the clot. Fibrinogen is the precursor of fibrin, the clot's structural protein. Prekallikrein participates in contact activation of the intrinsic pathway rather than in fibrinolysis."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient starts warfarin on Monday. On Tuesday morning the prothrombin time is 12 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5). On Friday morning it is 28 seconds. The dose has not changed.\nWhy did the test take several days to lengthen, when the drug reached the liver within hours of the first dose?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Warfarin only blocks the production of new functional factors II, VII, IX, and X; the factors already circulating keep working until they are cleared over the following days","why":"Warfarin does not touch factors that already exist. It blocks the vitamin K cycle in the liver, so every factor made from now on lacks the calcium binding groups it needs to work. The prothrombin time depends on the factors present in plasma, and those have half lives from several hours (factor VII) to a few days (prothrombin). The test lengthens as the old, working factors are used up and replaced by defective ones. This is why heparin is often given at the same time for the first few days."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Plasmin is an anticoagulant that prevents fibrin from forming in the first place.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Plasmin is a fibrinolytic enzyme. It digests fibrin that has already formed, dissolving the clot once the vessel has healed. Anticoagulants such as antithrombin and heparin act earlier, by blocking thrombin and other enzymes so fibrin is not made.","why":"There are two ways the body keeps clotting under control, and they act at different times. Anticoagulants limit how much clot forms and keep it from spreading along healthy vessel. Fibrinolysis removes the clot afterward. Plasminogen is built into the clot as it forms and is activated later by tissue plasminogen activator from healthy endothelium. Drugs that activate plasminogen dissolve existing clots; drugs that boost antithrombin, like heparin, prevent new ones."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the body keeps a clot from spreading along a healthy vessel and how it removes the clot once the vessel has healed. Then predict what a drug that blocks vitamin K does to each of these processes.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"healthy endothelium releases prostacyclin and nitric oxide that inhibit platelets; antithrombin (boosted by heparin) inactivates thrombin and other clotting enzymes; plasminogen is trapped in the clot and later activated to plasmin by tissue plasminogen activator; plasmin digests fibrin and dissolves the clot; a vitamin K blocker (warfarin) reduces functional factors II, VII, IX, and X, so less thrombin and fibrin form; the drug slows new clot formation but does not itself dissolve existing clots","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one clot at three times: fresh, held in check, and being dissolved. In the middle drawing show what stops it growing, the healthy endothelium beside it and the antithrombin in the plasma. In the third, show plasminogen already trapped inside the mesh, tissue plasminogen activator turning it into plasmin, and the mesh being cut apart. One line: say why plasminogen is built into the clot from the start rather than delivered later."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two patients bleeding from the same cut. Give one a drug that blocks vitamin K and the other a drug that blocks thromboxane and platelet aggregation. Draw exactly which half of the clot fails in each, the mesh or the plug. One line: both bleed, so say which one you could reverse with vitamin K and which you would have to wait out, and roughly how long."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how anticoagulants and plasmin limit and remove a clot and predict the effect of a drug that blocks vitamin K or platelet aggregation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Clot limitation and fibrinolysis. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-blood-types","name":"ABO and Rh blood types","can":"Predict agglutination for any donor and recipient ABO and Rh combination and explain the mechanism of hemolytic disease of the newborn.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Type O red cells are transfused into a type A recipient. What happens and why?\nA) No agglutination, because type A recipients have no antibodies\nB) No agglutination, because the donor cells carry neither A nor B antigen\nC) Agglutination, because the recipient's anti-B attacks the donor cells\nD) Agglutination, because type O cells carry both A and B antigens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) No agglutination, because the donor cells carry neither A nor B antigen","why":"The recipient's anti-B has nothing to bind on a type O cell, since those cells display no A or B antigen, which is what makes O the universal red cell donor. Anti-B is present in the type A recipient, but it needs a B antigen to attack and there is none. Type O cells lack both antigens rather than carrying both. Type A recipients do have an antibody, anti-B, so the last option is wrong about the mechanism even though the outcome is right."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's plasma agglutinates both type A and type B test cells. What is the patient's ABO type?\nA) Type B  |  B) Type O\nC) Type AB  |  D) Type A\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Type O","why":"Agglutinating both means the plasma contains both anti-A and anti-B, and only a type O person carries both, because they carry neither antigen. Type AB plasma has no ABO antibody and would agglutinate neither. Type A plasma has only anti-B and would agglutinate B cells alone. Type B plasma has only anti-A and would agglutinate A cells alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person with type A blood has which antigen on their erythrocytes and which antibody in their plasma?\nA) A antigen on the cells and anti-B antibody in the plasma\nB) A antigen on the cells and anti-A antibody in the plasma\nC) B antigen on the cells and anti-A antibody in the plasma\nD) No antigen on the cells and anti-B antibody in the plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A antigen on the cells and anti-B antibody in the plasma","why":"The rule is that you carry antibodies against the ABO antigen you lack, so a type A person makes anti-B and tolerates A. Having anti-A along with A antigen would mean attacking your own cells, which does not happen in a healthy person. B antigen with anti-A describes type B, not type A. No antigen with anti-B alone matches no ABO type, since a person lacking both antigens has both antibodies."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 24 year old woman typed as A negative is bleeding after an accident. No Rh negative blood is available, and she is given two units of O positive red cells. There is no immediate transfusion reaction.\nWhy is there no reaction now, and what is the risk that follows from this transfusion?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Her anti-B has nothing to bind on O cells, so no ABO reaction occurs; but the Rh positive cells will sensitize her to D, endangering a future Rh positive fetus or transfusion","why":"ABO and Rh are independent systems. Her plasma has anti-B only, and O cells have neither A nor B antigen, so there is nothing for it to bind. Rh is different: an Rh negative person has no anti-D until first exposed. The D antigen on the donor cells is that first exposure, and over the following weeks she makes anti-D and memory cells. That antibody will attack Rh positive cells in any later transfusion and, because it is IgG, will cross the placenta in a later Rh positive pregnancy. Anti-D immune globulin can be given to try to prevent sensitization."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A type O positive patient with severe burns needs plasma, not red cells. The blood bank supplies plasma from a type AB donor.\nPredict what happens when this plasma is infused, and explain why this pairing works even though AB red cells would not.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"No reaction; AB plasma contains neither anti-A nor anti-B, so there is nothing to bind the recipient's O cells, whereas AB red cells would carry both antigens that the O recipient's antibodies attack","why":"For a red cell transfusion you worry about the recipient's antibodies meeting the donor's antigens. For a plasma transfusion the roles flip: you worry about the donor's antibodies meeting the recipient's antigens. AB donors have both antigens on their cells and therefore make neither antibody, so their plasma is safe for anyone. O donors are the mirror image: their cells are safe for anyone, but their plasma carries both antibodies and would attack A, B, or AB recipients."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A person with type O blood has neither A nor B antigens on their red cells and therefore makes neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A person with type O blood has no A or B antigens but has both anti-A and anti-B antibodies in the plasma, because the immune system makes antibodies against whichever ABO antigens the person lacks.","why":"The rule is that you carry antibodies against the antigens you do not have. Type O lacks both, so it makes both. That is why type O red cells can go to anyone (no antigen to be attacked) but type O plasma cannot, and why a type O recipient can receive red cells only from another type O donor. Reversing the logic, type AB has both antigens and makes neither antibody."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain the mechanism of hemolytic disease of the newborn. Say which parent is which Rh type, when the mother becomes sensitized, why the first pregnancy is usually spared, and what happens to the fetus in a later pregnancy.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"mother is Rh negative and the fetus is Rh positive; fetal red cells enter the maternal circulation, usually at delivery; the mother makes anti-D antibodies after that first exposure, so the first baby is already born; in a later Rh positive pregnancy maternal anti-D (IgG) crosses the placenta; the antibody binds fetal red cells and causes hemolysis, anemia, and high bilirubin; anti-D immune globulin given to the mother clears fetal cells before she is sensitized","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four red cells in a row, A, B, AB and O, with the antigens on the surface of each. Under each cell, draw the antibodies floating in that person's plasma. Then draw a grid of donor against recipient and mark every combination that agglutinates. One line: O is called the universal donor, so say what your own grid shows about O as a recipient."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an Rh negative mother and an Rh positive fetus across two pregnancies. In the first, show where fetal cells get into the maternal circulation and what the mother makes in response. In the second, draw that antibody crossing the placenta and what it does to fetal red cells. Mark where an injection of anti D immunoglobulin breaks the sequence. One line: say why the first baby is usually fine and the second is not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict agglutination for any donor and recipient ABO and Rh combination and explain the mechanism of hemolytic disease of the newborn."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: ABO and Rh blood types. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-lab-hematocrit","name":"Hematocrit and hemoglobin determination","can":"Determine hematocrit and hemoglobin from a sample or simulation and evaluate the result against reference ranges.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student measures the packed red cell column as 21 mm and the total column of blood as 50 mm. What is the hematocrit?\nA) 21 percent  |  B) 50 percent\nC) 71 percent  |  D) 42 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 42 percent","why":"Divide the red cell column by the total column and multiply by 100, so 21 divided by 50 is 0.42, or 42 percent, a normal adult value. Reporting 21 mistakes the raw length for the ratio. Reporting 50 gives the total column length rather than the fraction. Adding the two numbers gives 71 and has no meaning as a ratio."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student includes the buffy coat in the packed red cell column when reading the tube. What is the effect on the reported hematocrit?\nA) It is slightly falsely high  |  B) It is slightly falsely low\nC) It is unaffected  |  D) It is falsely high by about half\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is slightly falsely high","why":"Adding the white cell and platelet layer lengthens the numerator without changing the denominator, so the percentage comes out a point or so above the true value. It cannot be falsely low, since nothing was subtracted. It is not unaffected, because the numerator changed. The buffy coat is only about 1 percent of the column, so the error is small rather than enormous."},{"t":"mc","q":"In what units is hematocrit reported?\nA) In grams per deciliter\nB) In cells per microliter\nC) In millimeters of packed column\nD) As a percentage of total blood volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) As a percentage of total blood volume","why":"Hematocrit is a ratio of the packed red cell column to the whole column, so it is expressed as a percent and has no mass or count units. Grams per deciliter is the unit for hemoglobin concentration. Cells per microliter is the unit for a red cell count. Column length in millimeters is what you measure, but you must divide it by the total length to get the reported value."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the capillary tube used for a microhematocrit coated with heparin?\nA) To bind hemoglobin so it can be measured on the same tube\nB) To prevent the sample from clotting before it can be spun\nC) To lyse the white cells so the buffy coat disappears\nD) To make the red cells denser so they pack faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) To prevent the sample from clotting before it can be spun","why":"A clotted sample cannot be separated into layers, so the tube carries an anticoagulant that keeps the cells suspended until centrifugation. Heparin does not lyse white cells, and the buffy coat should still be visible. It does not change red cell density. Hemoglobin is measured by a separate method on a separate portion of sample."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is one end of the filled capillary tube sealed with clay before spinning?\nA) To mark which end contains the plasma\nB) To absorb the plasma so only cells remain\nC) To keep the sample from being thrown out of the tube during centrifugation\nD) To provide the calcium that the red cells need in order to pack down tightly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) To keep the sample from being thrown out of the tube during centrifugation","why":"Spinning generates a large outward force, and an unsealed tube would lose sample, giving an inaccurate and messy result. The clay is inert and supplies no reagent to the reaction. Layer identification comes from the visible color boundaries, not from the seal. Nothing is absorbed by the clay; the plasma remains in the tube above the cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Before you spin anything, draw the tube you predict for yourself with a percentage on it. Spin, read, and draw the real tube beside your prediction with the layers measured. Write the reference range for your sex next to it. Do the same for hemoglobin in g/dL and check whether your hemoglobin is roughly a third of your hematocrit. One line: if those two numbers break that rule, say which measurement you would repeat first and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a row of four spun tubes for four blind samples, at 30, 42, 52 and 60 percent, and beside each write the hemoglobin you get from the rule of three. Under each tube, draw the person it could belong to and write one plausible cause. One line: one of these could be an ordinary person who is simply dehydrated, so name it and say what you would measure next to check."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to determine hematocrit and hemoglobin from a sample or simulation and evaluate the result against reference ranges."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Hematocrit and hemoglobin determination. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w10-lab-blood-typing","name":"Blood typing","can":"Perform simulated ABO and Rh typing and determine compatible donor types for the result obtained.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A sample agglutinates with anti-B and anti-D but not with anti-A. What is the full type?\nA) B negative  |  B) AB positive\nC) B positive  |  D) A positive\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) B positive","why":"Clumping with anti-B places the B antigen on the cells, no clumping with anti-A rules out A, and clumping with anti-D makes it Rh positive. A positive would have required a reaction with anti-A instead. B negative would have shown no reaction in the anti-D well. AB positive would have clumped with anti-A as well."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sample agglutinates with anti-A and anti-D but not with anti-B. Which donor red cell types are compatible for this patient?\nA) A positive, B positive, AB positive, and O positive\nB) A negative and O negative only\nC) Only A positive, since the type must match exactly\nD) A positive, A negative, O positive, and O negative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A positive, A negative, O positive, and O negative","why":"The patient is A positive, so their plasma has anti-B and any unit carrying B is excluded, while their own D antigen means Rh positive units are also acceptable. Including B positive and AB positive units would supply the B antigen that anti-B will attack. Restricting to Rh negative units alone is safe but unnecessarily narrow for a patient who already has the D antigen. Requiring an exact match ignores the fact that O cells carry no A or B antigen and are therefore compatible."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sample shows no reaction with anti-A or anti-B but agglutinates with anti-D. What is the full type, and what does the plasma contain?\nA) O positive, with both anti-A and anti-B in the plasma\nB) O negative, with both anti-A and anti-B in the plasma\nC) AB positive, with neither anti-A nor anti-B in the plasma\nD) O positive, with anti-D in the plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) O positive, with both anti-A and anti-B in the plasma","why":"No ABO antigens plus the D antigen makes this O positive, and because the person lacks both A and B they carry antibodies against both. O negative would have shown no reaction with anti-D, which contradicts the result. AB positive would have clumped with both ABO reagents. An Rh positive person does not make anti-D, since D is their own antigen."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient types as AB negative. Which donor red cell types are compatible?\nA) Every type, since AB is the universal recipient\nB) AB negative, A negative, B negative, and O negative\nC) AB positive, A positive, B positive, and O positive\nD) AB negative and O negative only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) AB negative, A negative, B negative, and O negative","why":"Having no ABO antibodies opens every ABO type, but lacking the D antigen means only Rh negative units avoid the risk of sensitizing the patient to D. The positive units all carry D and are therefore not the safe choice for an Rh negative recipient. Limiting to AB negative and O negative leaves out A negative and B negative units that would not be attacked, since the patient has no ABO antibodies. The universal recipient label applies fully only to AB positive."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which blood type can donate red cells to the largest number of recipients, and why?\nA) O negative, because its cells display neither A, nor B, nor D antigen\nB) AB positive, because its plasma contains no ABO antibodies\nC) O positive, because it is the most common type in the population\nD) AB negative, because it carries both ABO antigens but not D\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) O negative, because its cells display neither A, nor B, nor D antigen","why":"Red cell compatibility depends on what the donor cell displays, and an O negative cell displays none of the three common antigens, so no recipient antibody has a target. Having no ABO antibodies makes AB positive the best recipient, not the best donor, because compatibility for receiving depends on plasma rather than cells. Frequency in the population affects supply but not compatibility. AB negative cells carry both ABO antigens and would be attacked by anti-A or anti-B in most recipients."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: In lab a student tests a simulated blood sample. Anti-A serum: agglutination. Anti-B serum: agglutination. Anti-D serum: no agglutination. The controls behave as expected.\nWhich red cell donor types could this person receive, and what is the physiological reason for each restriction?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"A negative, B negative, AB negative, or O negative; the recipient has no anti-A or anti-B to attack any ABO type, but should avoid Rh positive cells to prevent becoming sensitized to D","why":"Clumping with anti-A and anti-B means both antigens are present, so the type is AB, and no clumping with anti-D means Rh negative. An AB person makes neither anti-A nor anti-B, so red cells of any ABO type carry nothing their plasma will attack. The Rh restriction is different in kind: the person has no anti-D now, but Rh positive cells would sensitize them, and the antibody they then make would cause a reaction on a later exposure."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what you are looking for on a blood typing slide when you add anti-A, anti-B, and anti-D serum, and how you get from the pattern of agglutination to the list of red cell donor types the person can safely receive.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"agglutination (clumping) in a well means the antibody found its antigen on the red cells; the antigens present define the type, for example clumping with anti-B only is type B; clumping with anti-D means Rh positive, no clumping means Rh negative; the person's plasma contains antibodies against whichever A or B antigens they lack; safe donor red cells carry no antigen that the recipient's antibodies would attack; an Rh negative recipient should receive only Rh negative cells to avoid sensitization","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three wells for one sample, labeled anti A, anti B and anti D, and fill in what you actually saw, clumped or smooth, for the sample you were given. Then draw the full grid of well patterns for all eight types, so any future result can be read straight off it. One line: name the pattern that is easiest to misread and say what you would do to be sure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw your typed result as a patient on an emergency table, then draw the units of donor blood you could safely hang and the ones you could not, with the antigen and antibody reason drawn on every rejected bag. Then draw what actually happens inside a vessel during an incompatible transfusion. One line: say where the clinical damage comes from, and it is not the clumping by itself."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Blood typing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Immune Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w15-innate-immunity","name":"Innate defenses","can":"Describe the physical, chemical, and cellular innate defenses and explain what makes them nonspecific and immediate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A child cannot make functional neutrophils. Which problem should you expect?\nA) Failure to produce antibody after a routine childhood vaccination\nB) Inability to raise body temperature at all during an infection\nC) Loss of the skin barrier with widespread water loss through it\nD) Repeated bacterial infections with little pus at the infected site\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Repeated bacterial infections with little pus at the infected site","why":"Neutrophils do the bulk of early bacterial phagocytosis, and pus is largely spent neutrophils, so losing them means both frequent bacterial infection and an infected site that looks quiet rather than purulent. Antibody production is a B cell job and would be unaffected. Fever is set by the hypothalamus in response to pyrogens from macrophages and does not require neutrophils. The skin barrier is structural and independent of leukocyte counts."},{"t":"mc","q":"A large burn destroys the epidermis over a wide area. Which innate defense is lost first?\nA) The physical barrier that keeps microbes out of the tissue\nB) The cellular defense provided by natural killer cells\nC) The complement proteins circulating in the plasma\nD) The chemical barrier provided by gastric acid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The physical barrier that keeps microbes out of the tissue","why":"Intact skin is the first line, a mechanical seal that microbes cannot cross, and a burn that removes the epidermis opens a wide door before any other defense is touched. Gastric acid guards the swallowed route and is unrelated to skin. NK cells and complement are still present and functional after a burn, which is why burn patients still mount responses once organisms get in; the problem is that organisms now get in easily."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient is given interferon during a viral illness. Which cells does it act on to slow the infection?\nA) The B cells, which it drives to secrete antibody more quickly\nB) The uninfected cells nearby, which it readies against the virus\nC) The already infected cells, which it destroys outright\nD) The virus particles in plasma, which it binds and neutralizes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The uninfected cells nearby, which it readies against the virus","why":"Interferon protects the cells that have not yet been infected by inducing enzymes that shut down viral protein synthesis if the virus arrives. It does not kill the cell that released it, and it does not bind virus particles, which is antibody work. Driving antibody secretion is a cytokine effect of helper T cells, not the antiviral action interferon is named for."},{"t":"mc","q":"A splinter drives bacteria into the dermis. Which cell meets them first, before any new cells arrive from the blood?\nA) The helper T cell waiting in the nearest lymph node\nB) The macrophage already resident in the tissue\nC) The neutrophil arriving from the bloodstream\nD) The plasma cell secreting antibody into the wound\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The macrophage already resident in the tissue","why":"Tissues are patrolled by resident macrophages that are on site at time zero, which is why they are the cells that detect the invasion and release the signals that call everyone else. Neutrophils are fast but still have to marginate and cross the vessel wall, which takes an hour or more. Plasma cells and helper T cells belong to the adaptive response and act days later."},{"t":"mc","q":"What makes the innate defenses nonspecific?\nA) They grow faster and stronger with each repeat exposure to the same microbe\nB) They are aimed at one antigen selected from a very large receptor library\nC) They respond the same way to any invader, with no prior exposure needed\nD) They act only against an invader the body has already met once before\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They respond the same way to any invader, with no prior exposure needed","why":"Innate defenses use a fixed set of recognition patterns shared by many microbes, so the response to a first encounter and a fiftieth encounter looks the same and starts within minutes. Requiring a prior encounter and growing stronger with repetition describe adaptive memory, which innate defenses do not have. Choosing one antigen from a large receptor library describes lymphocyte clonal selection, the defining feature of the specific arm."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Fever, inflammation, and phagocytosis are innate defenses because they respond the same way regardless of which pathogen is present.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The dividing line between innate and adaptive immunity is specificity and memory. Innate responses use receptors for common microbial patterns, so they need no time to learn and no prior exposure. The cost is that they cannot get better. Adaptive responses are slow the first time because a specific lymphocyte clone must be found and expanded, but they leave memory behind."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline and one bacterium trying three routes in: through the skin, through the airway, and in swallowed food. On each route draw the barrier it meets and name it properly, keratinized skin with its acid film, mucus with cilia sweeping upward, and stomach acid near pH 2. Then draw what waits inside if it gets past. One line: say what makes all of these nonspecific, in terms of what they actually recognize."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a splinter carrying bacteria into the dermis, then follow the innate response as a row of small panels from the first minutes onward: the chemicals released, the phagocytes arriving, complement punching a hole in a bacterial membrane, and a natural killer cell handling a host cell that has gone wrong. Put a rough time on each panel. One line: adaptive immunity needs days, so say what innate defense is buying with them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the physical, chemical, and cellular innate defenses and explain what makes them nonspecific and immediate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Innate defenses. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-inflammation","name":"Inflammation and fever","can":"Order the events of the inflammatory response and explain how the cardinal signs arise and why fever aids defense.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why does an inflamed area look red and feel warm?\nA) Arteriolar dilation increases blood flow, bringing more warm blood to it\nB) Fluid leaking into the tissue space raises the local temperature directly\nC) Neutrophils generate heat as a byproduct of digesting the bacteria\nD) Blood flow slows down, so blood lingers long enough to warm the tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Arteriolar dilation increases blood flow, bringing more warm blood to it","why":"Redness and heat are one mechanism seen two ways: more blood in the area means more hemoglobin visible through the skin and more core temperature blood delivered to a surface that is normally cooler. Edema fluid arrives at the same temperature as the blood it came from and adds no heat of its own. Phagocytosis does not generate measurable local warming, and slowed flow would make an area look dusky and feel cool."},{"t":"mc","q":"A splinter enters the dermis. Which sequence of events follows?\nA) Vasodilation, chemical release, leukocyte exit, increased permeability\nB) Leukocyte exit, chemical release, vasodilation, increased permeability\nC) Increased permeability, leukocyte exit, vasodilation, chemical release\nD) Chemical release, vasodilation, increased permeability, leukocyte exit\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Chemical release, vasodilation, increased permeability, leukocyte exit","why":"Damaged cells and resident mast cells release mediators first, and everything downstream is a response to those signals: vessels dilate, then they leak, then leukocytes stick and cross the wall. Any order that puts a vascular change before the chemical signal reverses cause and effect. Leukocyte exit has to come last of the four, because the cells need the adhesion signals and the widened gaps in order to leave."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does a moderate fever aid defense?\nA) It speeds immune cell activity and slows the growth of many pathogens\nB) It denatures every bacterial protein once temperature passes 39 C\nC) It removes the need for phagocytes to enter the infected tissue\nD) It stops histamine release and so brings the swelling to an end\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It speeds immune cell activity and slows the growth of many pathogens","why":"A few degrees raises the rate of nearly every enzymatic and cellular process, including phagocyte movement and lymphocyte proliferation, while many pathogens replicate best at 37 C and do less well above it. Bacterial proteins are not denatured at 39 C; temperatures that would do that would kill the patient first. Fever supports phagocytes rather than replacing them, and it does not end the swelling."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the four classic cardinal signs of acute inflammation?\nA) Bruising, heat, dryness, and weakness\nB) Redness, heat, swelling, and pain\nC) Pallor, coolness, swelling, and numbness\nD) Redness, coolness, shrinkage, and itching\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Redness, heat, swelling, and pain","why":"Each sign traces to a vascular change: redness and heat from arteriolar dilation, swelling from increased capillary permeability, and pain from mediators plus pressure on nerve endings. Pallor and coolness would mean reduced blood flow, the opposite of what inflammation does. Shrinkage and dryness are not features of an inflamed tissue, which gains fluid rather than losing it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with a bacterial infection has a temperature of 39.4 C (102.9 F). He is shivering under two blankets and his hands are cold and pale even though his core temperature is high.\nWhat mechanism raised his temperature, and why is he shivering while already hot?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Pyrogens from macrophages and bacteria raised the hypothalamic set point; the body treats its current temperature as too cold and shivers and vasoconstricts to reach the new set point","why":"Fever is a regulated rise, not a failure of regulation. Interleukin 1 and other pyrogens act on the hypothalamus and reset its thermostat higher. At that moment the body's actual temperature is below the new target, so the hypothalamus does what it always does when cold: it constricts skin vessels to conserve heat and triggers shivering to generate it. Once the new set point is reached the shivering stops. When the set point returns to normal the patient sweats and flushes. The higher temperature slows bacterial growth and speeds immune cell activity."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, put the events of the inflammatory response in order after a splinter breaks the skin, and explain how each of the four cardinal signs (redness, heat, swelling, pain) arises from those events.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"damaged cells and mast cells release histamine and other mediators; arterioles dilate, increasing local blood flow, which causes redness and heat; capillary permeability increases, so plasma and protein leak into the tissue, causing swelling; swelling and chemical mediators stimulate pain receptors; neutrophils then monocytes are attracted by chemotaxis and squeeze out of capillaries (diapedesis); phagocytes engulf bacteria and debris, and tissue repair follows","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a small injury with the tissue around it and build the cardinal signs from the mechanism rather than the word: redness and heat from vasodilation, swelling from increased permeability and fluid leaving the capillary, pain from the chemicals released and the pressure. Draw the vessel changes that make each one. One line: swelling both helps and hurts, so name one thing it does that is genuinely useful."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a temperature axis from 37 degrees Celsius upward against time and plot a fever from onset through plateau to breaking. On the drawing mark the pyrogen arriving, the hypothalamic set point moving up, the shivering and vasoconstriction while the body chases the new set point, and the sweating when the set point drops back. One line: you feel cold while your temperature is climbing, so say what in your drawing explains that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the events of the inflammatory response and explain how the cardinal signs arise and why fever aids defense."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Inflammation and fever. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-adaptive-immunity","name":"Adaptive immunity","can":"Compare humoral and cell mediated immunity by the cell responsible, the target, and the mechanism of elimination.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell is responsible for humoral immunity?\nA) The cytotoxic T lymphocyte  |  B) The tissue macrophage\nC) The natural killer cell  |  D) The B lymphocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The B lymphocyte","why":"Humoral immunity is named for the body fluids, and its agent is the B cell, whose descendants pour antibody into blood and lymph. The cytotoxic T cell carries out cell mediated immunity instead, killing infected cells directly. Macrophages and NK cells contribute to defense but belong to the innate side and do not produce antibody."},{"t":"mc","q":"An activated B cell divides. What do most of its daughter cells become, and what do they secrete?\nA) Plasma cells, which secrete antibody\nB) Cytotoxic cells, which secrete perforin\nC) Memory T cells, which secrete cytokines\nD) Macrophages, which secrete digestive enzymes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Plasma cells, which secrete antibody","why":"The effector form of a B cell is the plasma cell, packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum and dedicated to antibody export; a smaller share of the daughters become memory B cells instead. Perforin comes from cytotoxic T cells and NK cells. A B cell cannot become a T cell or a macrophage, since those arise from separate lineages."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which targets does humoral immunity handle best?\nA) Cells of a transplanted organ from another person\nB) Bacteria and toxins free in the blood and in the lymph\nC) Viruses that are already replicating inside a body cell\nD) Tumor cells growing in the middle of a solid tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Bacteria and toxins free in the blood and in the lymph","why":"Antibody is a soluble protein that circulates in body fluids, so it can only reach a target that is also out in those fluids: extracellular bacteria, free virus particles between cells, and dissolved toxins. Anything hidden inside a cell, including a replicating virus, a tumor cell, or a graft cell, has to be handled by cell mediated immunity, because antibody cannot cross the plasma membrane to reach it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell directly kills a virus infected body cell?\nA) The cytotoxic T cell  |  B) The plasma cell\nC) The helper T cell  |  D) The B cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cytotoxic T cell","why":"The cytotoxic T cell is the killer of the adaptive system: it inspects a cell's surface display and destroys the cell if the display includes foreign fragments. Plasma cells and B cells work through secreted antibody and never touch a target cell. Helper T cells direct the response with cytokines but do not deliver the lethal hit themselves."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A boy has an inherited condition in which B cells never mature. His serum IgG is 60 mg/dL (normal 700 to 1600) and IgM is undetectable. His T cell counts and function are normal. He is exposed in the same week to tetanus toxin from a dirty wound and to a virus that infects cells in the throat.\nPredict how his immune system handles each threat.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"He cannot neutralize the tetanus toxin because that requires antibody, but his cytotoxic T cells can still kill the virus infected throat cells","why":"The two arms of adaptive immunity handle different kinds of target. A toxin dissolved in body fluid is outside any cell, and only antibody can bind and neutralize it. T cells do not recognize free toxin at all. A virus inside a cell is hidden from antibody, and the job of finding and killing that cell belongs to cytotoxic T cells, which are intact here. So the boy is in danger from the toxin and from bacterial infections in general, while cell mediated defense against the viral infection goes ahead."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare humoral and cell mediated immunity. For each, name the lymphocyte responsible, what kind of target it handles, and how the target is eliminated. Include where the helper T cell fits.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"humoral immunity uses B cells that become plasma cells and secrete antibodies; antibodies act on targets outside cells: bacteria, toxins, and free virus in body fluids; antibodies neutralize, agglutinate, opsonize, or activate complement, and other cells do the killing; cell mediated immunity uses cytotoxic T cells; cytotoxic T cells kill the body's own cells that are infected by virus or are cancerous; helper T cells release cytokines that activate both B cells and cytotoxic T cells","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a line down the middle of your space. On the left draw humoral immunity: the B cell, the plasma cell, antibodies leaving, and a target they can reach, a toxin or a bacterium out in the fluid. On the right draw cell mediated immunity: the helper T cell, the cytotoxic T cell, and a target they have to reach, a virus infected cell or a tumor cell, with the killing mechanism shown. One line: say why an antibody cannot solve the right hand problem."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one virus and follow it through both arms of the adaptive response on a single page, first while it is still outside a cell, then after it has got inside one. Show which cell handles it at each stage and what becomes of it. Put the helper T cell where it belongs in both halves. One line: untreated HIV infection destroys helper T cells, so say what that costs each arm."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare humoral and cell mediated immunity by the cell responsible, the target, and the mechanism of elimination."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Adaptive immunity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-antibodies","name":"Antibody structure and function","can":"Relate antibody structure to antigen binding and name the mechanisms by which antibodies neutralize or mark a target.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why can a single antibody molecule link two separate bacterial cells together?\nA) Because complement joins two antibody molecules end to end\nB) Because it has two identical binding sites, one on each arm\nC) Because its stem region binds a second bacterium directly\nD) Because it changes shape to grip a new epitope after the first\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Because it has two identical binding sites, one on each arm","why":"Both arms of a monomer carry the same specificity, so if two cells display the same epitope one antibody can hold one on each arm, and many antibodies doing this at once build a lattice. The stem binds phagocyte receptors and complement, not antigen. An antibody does not change its specificity after binding, and complement joining molecules together is not how a lattice forms."},{"t":"mc","q":"Antibody coats a bacterium. What does a neutrophil bind in order to engulf it more efficiently?\nA) The bare bacterial surface between antibodies\nB) The hinge segments where the arms bend\nC) The stem regions of the bound antibodies\nD) The variable tips that are holding the antigen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The stem regions of the bound antibodies","why":"Once the arms are occupied by antigen, the stems all point outward, and the phagocyte has receptors for exactly that region, so the coated bacterium is easy to grasp. That is opsonization by antibody, and it is why coating a slippery encapsulated organism makes such a difference. The variable tips are already engaged with the antigen, and the bare surface is what the phagocyte struggled with in the first place."},{"t":"mc","q":"Antibody binds a bacterial surface and complement is then activated at that spot. What happens to the bacterium?\nA) Complement assembles a pore in its membrane, and it lyses\nB) Complement digests the bacterial DNA and stops replication\nC) Complement pulls the antibody off and recycles it for reuse\nD) Complement blocks the bacterium from binding to host cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Complement assembles a pore in its membrane, and it lyses","why":"Antibody bound to a surface exposes a site on its stem that starts the classical complement pathway, and the cascade ends in a membrane attack complex that opens the cell to water and ions. Complement acts on the membrane, not on DNA. It does not remove the antibody, and blocking attachment to host cells is neutralization, which the antibody accomplishes on its own without complement."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mother's milk protects the lining of her infant's gut. Which antibody class does that, and where does it act?\nA) IgM, acting on the lymph draining away from the intestine\nB) IgA, acting on the mucosal surface facing the gut lumen\nC) IgG, acting inside the infant's bloodstream after absorption\nD) IgE, acting on mast cells within the wall of the intestine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) IgA, acting on the mucosal surface facing the gut lumen","why":"IgA is the class exported into secretions, including milk, saliva, and gut and airway mucus, and it works on the surface by blocking pathogens from ever attaching to the epithelium. IgG is the class transferred across the placenta into fetal blood, which is a different route for a different fluid. IgE arms mast cells for allergic responses, and IgM stays largely within the bloodstream."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's mast cells release histamine seconds after an antigen appears. Which antibody class was already bound to those mast cells?\nA) IgM  |  B) IgA\nC) IgE  |  D) IgG\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) IgE","why":"IgE is the one class whose stem binds mast cells and basophils, so it sits waiting on the cell surface long before the antigen arrives; when antigen links two of these antibodies together the cell degranulates immediately. IgG and IgM are free in plasma and produce slower responses involving complement and phagocytes. IgA is in secretions and does not arm mast cells."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The constant region of an antibody determines which antigen the antibody will bind.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The variable regions at the tips of the two arms determine which antigen an antibody binds. The constant region determines the antibody class and which effector mechanisms it can recruit.","why":"The two ends of an antibody do two different jobs. The variable regions differ between antibodies and fit one antigen. The constant region is shared across all antibodies of the same class and is read by complement and by receptors on phagocytes, mast cells, and the placenta. Class switching during an immune response swaps the constant region while keeping the same variable region, so the antibody keeps its target but changes what it can do."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one antibody large enough to label properly: two heavy chains, two light chains, the disulfide bonds, the variable regions forming two antigen binding sites at the tips, and the constant stem. Mark which part decides what it binds and which part decides what happens next. Draw an antigen sitting in one of the two sites. One line: it has two binding sites, so say what a population of these can do to a suspension of bacteria."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one antigen in the middle of your space with four antibody mechanisms acting on it around the edges: neutralization of a toxin or a viral binding site, agglutination of several cells into a clump, complement activated on the surface, and opsonization marking it for a phagocyte. Draw the outcome of each. One line: some of these destroy the target and some only label it, so say which only label it and who finishes the job."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate antibody structure to antigen binding and name the mechanisms by which antibodies neutralize or mark a target."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Antibody structure and function. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-immune-memory","name":"Immune memory and immunization","can":"Compare the primary and secondary antibody responses and explain how active and passive immunization produce protection.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A child recovers from chickenpox and is protected for decades afterward. Which type of immunity did the illness produce?\nA) Natural passive immunity  |  B) Artificial active immunity\nC) Artificial passive immunity  |  D) Natural active immunity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Natural active immunity","why":"The child's own lymphocytes met the virus, expanded, and left memory cells behind, so the immunity is active, and because the exposure came from ordinary infection rather than a clinic, it is natural. Passive labels would require antibody arriving from someone else, which would not last decades. Artificial active would mean the same memory produced by a vaccine instead of by the disease."},{"t":"mc","q":"About how long after a first exposure does antibody become measurable in the plasma?\nA) About 6 months  |  B) Within a few minutes\nC) About 3 to 6 days  |  D) About 6 to 8 weeks\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 3 to 6 days","why":"The lag of the primary response is the time needed to find the one matching lymphocyte, activate it, expand the clone, and differentiate plasma cells, which takes several days and peaks around day 10 to 14. Minutes would describe an innate response or a preformed passive antibody. Weeks and months are far too long, which matters because it means a first infection is often over before antibody peaks."},{"t":"mc","q":"How quickly does a secondary response produce antibody after a repeat exposure?\nA) After about two weeks of steady exposure\nB) Only after a full month has passed\nC) Within hours to two or three days\nD) After the same 3 to 6 day lag as the first time\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Within hours to two or three days","why":"Memory cells are already numerous and already specific, so the slow search and expansion phase of the primary response is skipped and antibody rises almost immediately. Repeating the original lag would mean no memory had formed at all. Longer delays describe no immune system a person could survive with, and the whole point of vaccination is buying this speed in advance."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A nurse who completed a hepatitis B vaccine series six years ago has an antibody level of 8 mIU/mL (protective level 10 or above). She receives a single booster dose. Five days later her level is 350 mIU/mL, and nearly all of it is IgG.\nWhy did the antibody level rise so fast and so high after one dose, when the original series needed three doses over six months?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The original series left memory B and T cells that are numerous and already class switched, so the booster triggered a secondary response with a short lag and a high IgG peak","why":"Circulating antibody fades, but memory cells persist for years. A serum level below the protective cutoff says little about memory. On re exposure, memory cells need no long search and no class switch, so antibody appears within days instead of weeks and rises to a much higher level, mostly IgG. The size and speed of this response is itself proof that the original immunization worked, and it is why a single booster restores protection while an unvaccinated person would need the whole series."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Passive immunization, such as an injection of preformed antibodies, gives long lasting protection because it creates memory cells.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Passive immunization gives immediate but temporary protection that lasts only as long as the injected antibodies survive, a few weeks to months, because the recipient's own lymphocytes were never activated and no memory cells are formed.","why":"Memory requires that the recipient's own B and T cells meet antigen and divide. Antibody alone contains no antigen, so nothing is learned. This is why antivenom, rabies immune globulin, and the antibodies a newborn gets across the placenta all wear off. Active immunization delivers antigen, so it is slow to work but leaves memory. The two are often given together to cover both the short term and the long term."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, compare the primary and secondary antibody responses in timing, size, and antibody class, explain what accounts for the difference, and state how a vaccine uses this.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"primary response has a lag of several days, a low peak, and mostly IgM; secondary response starts within a day or two, peaks much higher, and is mostly IgG; the difference is memory B and T cells left from the first exposure, more numerous and already class switched; a vaccine supplies antigen without disease, so it triggers a primary response and leaves memory; on real exposure the memory cells produce a secondary response fast enough to prevent illness; boosters raise the number of memory cells and antibody levels","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, antibody concentration on the y and time in days on the x, and plot the primary response to a first exposure and the secondary response to the same antigen weeks later. Get all three differences right, the lag before the rise, the height of the peak, and how long it stays up. Mark where IgM leads and where IgG does. One line: say which of the three differences is the one a vaccine is really buying you."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four people getting protection four different ways: a natural infection, a vaccine, a fetus receiving antibodies across the placenta, and a patient given an antivenom injection. Under each, draw whether memory cells were made and a bar for how long the protection lasts. One line: two of these run out, so name them and say what that person would need to be protected again."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the primary and secondary antibody responses and explain how active and passive immunization produce protection."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Immune memory and immunization. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-immune-dysfunction","name":"Immune dysfunction","can":"Classify allergy, autoimmunity, and immunodeficiency by what the immune system is doing wrong in each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the immune system doing wrong in an allergy?\nA) It is mounting a strong response against a harmless foreign substance\nB) It is attacking the body's own tissues as though they were foreign\nC) It is failing to respond because a needed component is missing\nD) It is responding normally but far too slowly to be of any use\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is mounting a strong response against a harmless foreign substance","why":"In allergy the machinery works, the specificity works, and the target is genuinely foreign; the error is that the antigen posed no threat, so the damage comes from the response rather than from the pollen or the peanut. Attacking self tissue is autoimmunity and failing for lack of a component is immunodeficiency. Sorting these three by what has gone wrong is the whole of this competency."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to blood vessels and airways during anaphylaxis?\nA) Vessels dilate and airway smooth muscle relaxes at the same time\nB) Vessels constrict and leak while airway smooth muscle constricts\nC) Vessels dilate and leak while airway smooth muscle constricts\nD) Vessels constrict while airway smooth muscle relaxes and opens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Vessels dilate and leak while airway smooth muscle constricts","why":"Anaphylaxis is a local allergic response occurring body wide, so the histamine effects you would see in one swollen finger happen everywhere at once: widespread vasodilation and leak drop blood pressure while bronchoconstriction closes the airway. Vasoconstriction and bronchodilation are the opposite pattern and are what treatment aims to restore. The danger comes from both systems failing at once."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell does HIV infect and destroy?\nA) The red blood cell  |  B) The helper T cell\nC) The plasma cell  |  D) The neutrophil\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The helper T cell","why":"HIV enters cells carrying the CD4 surface protein, and the helper T cell is the main one, which is why a virus that infects a single cell type dismantles so much of the immune system. Plasma cells and neutrophils are not the virus target, though both function poorly once helper T support is gone. Red cells have no nucleus and cannot support viral replication."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In an allergy, symptoms arise because the immune system fails to respond to a harmful antigen.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. In an allergy, symptoms arise because the immune system responds strongly to a substance that is harmless in itself, and the response, not the substance, causes the damage.","why":"Allergy is a problem of aim, not of strength. The mechanisms involved, IgE, mast cells, histamine, and eosinophils, are normal tools for fighting parasites. Turned against pollen or peanut protein they produce swelling, airway narrowing, and in the worst case a fall in blood pressure. Failure to respond to a real threat is immunodeficiency, a different category."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three panels, one per fault, and in each show the immune system doing the wrong thing: allergy responding hard to a harmless antigen, autoimmunity attacking a self tissue, and immunodeficiency failing to answer a real pathogen. In each, draw the target and the cell or antibody doing the damage. One line: pick your own autoimmune example, not type 1 diabetes, and say what self molecule is being mistaken for foreign."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a dial with the normal setting in the middle, turned too far up on one side and too far down on the other, then draw a third fault that is not about the amount at all but about the target. Place allergy, autoimmunity and immunodeficiency on your drawing and sketch a real clinical picture beside each. One line: one of the three is something a drug is given to produce on purpose, so name it and say when you would want that."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify allergy, autoimmunity, and immunodeficiency by what the immune system is doing wrong in each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Immune dysfunction. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Digestive Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w14-digestive-processes","name":"Four digestive processes","can":"Distinguish motility, secretion, digestion, and absorption and identify where in the tract each dominates.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A parietal cell in the stomach wall moves H+ into the gastric lumen. Which digestive process is this?\nA) Absorption  |  B) Secretion\nC) Digestion  |  D) Motility\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Secretion","why":"Anything a cell releases into the lumen is secretion, regardless of what it later does there. The acid then supports digestion by activating pepsinogen, but the release itself is the secretory step. Absorption would be H+ moving from lumen into blood, the opposite direction. Motility describes the churning that mixes the acid with food."},{"t":"mc","q":"Rings of contraction appear at intervals along the small intestine, relax, and reappear between the old sites, sloshing chyme back and forth with little forward movement. Which process is this?\nA) Secretion  |  B) Motility\nC) Absorption  |  D) Digestion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Motility","why":"This describes segmentation, a motility pattern whose job is mixing rather than propulsion. It supports digestion and absorption by keeping chyme in contact with enzymes and the brush border, but the contractions themselves are muscle work. Digestion is enzymatic bond breaking, and secretion is the release of the enzymes doing that work."},{"t":"mc","q":"Glucose crosses the apical membrane of an enterocyte on SGLT1 and leaves the cell into a capillary. Which process does this represent?\nA) Secretion  |  B) Mechanical digestion\nC) Chemical digestion  |  D) Absorption\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Absorption","why":"Nutrient moving from lumen through the epithelium into blood is the definition of absorption. Secretion would be glucose moving the other way. Chemical digestion is what produced the free glucose in the first place, when brush border maltase cleaved a disaccharide, but the transport step itself is absorption. No muscle is involved, so nothing here is mechanical digestion."},{"t":"mc","q":"Pepsin cleaves peptide bonds inside a protein chain in the stomach lumen. Which process is this?\nA) Absorption  |  B) Mechanical digestion\nC) Secretion  |  D) Chemical digestion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Chemical digestion","why":"Breaking a covalent bond with a hydrolytic enzyme is chemical digestion by definition. The release of pepsinogen from chief cells was secretion, an earlier and separate step. Absorption comes later, once peptides are reduced to amino acids and small peptides at the brush border. Mechanical digestion would be the churning that exposed more of the protein to the enzyme."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing correctly matches a region of the tract with the process that dominates there?\nA) Colon and chemical digestion of dietary protein\nB) Esophagus and secretion of digestive enzymes\nC) Mouth and absorption of most dietary nutrients\nD) Stomach and mechanical mixing with acid secretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Stomach and mechanical mixing with acid secretion","why":"The stomach churns food into chyme while parietal and chief cells secrete acid and pepsinogen, so motility and secretion dominate there. The mouth secretes and chews but absorbs essentially nothing. Protein digestion is complete before the colon, which handles water and electrolytes. The esophagus performs peristalsis and secretes only lubricating mucus, not enzymes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the tract as one long tube from mouth to anus with the named regions marked. Along it, draw four labeled bands showing where motility, secretion, digestion and absorption each dominate, and let them overlap where they truly do. Put a real example in each band, a named enzyme, a named secretion, a named nutrient crossing. One line: name the region where absorption dominates and say what its wall does to make that possible."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cross section of the gut wall with the four layers, mucosa to serosa. Place each of the four processes on the layer that carries it out, with the actual structure doing the work: smooth muscle sheets, glands and gland cells, enzymes in the lumen, epithelial transporters. Draw the lumen contents changing as you move along. One line: say which of the four processes is the only one that moves a molecule into your body, and where the other three stop."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish motility, secretion, digestion, and absorption and identify where in the tract each dominates."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Four digestive processes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-gi-motility","name":"Motility patterns","can":"Compare peristalsis, segmentation, and the migrating motor complex by pattern and purpose.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A recording from the jejunum shows rings of contraction that appear, relax, and reappear between the previous sites, with contents moving only a few centimeters over several minutes. Which pattern is this?\nA) Segmentation  |  B) Phase III of the migrating motor complex\nC) Mass movement  |  D) Peristalsis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Segmentation","why":"Alternating, nonprogressive rings with almost no net transit is the signature of segmentation, the dominant fed pattern in the small intestine. Peristalsis would show a wave traveling steadily in one direction. Phase III of the migrating motor complex is a strong sweeping series that moves contents rapidly and appears only in fasting. Mass movement is a colonic event that pushes a large volume over a long distance at once."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fasting volunteer shows a burst of strong contractions that begins in the stomach and travels to the terminal ileum, then falls quiet for about 90 minutes before repeating. What is being recorded?\nA) Receptive relaxation  |  B) Segmentation\nC) The gastrocolic reflex  |  D) The migrating motor complex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The migrating motor complex","why":"A propagating burst that starts proximally, sweeps to the ileum, and cycles every 90 minutes in a fasting subject is the migrating motor complex. Segmentation is nonpropagating and belongs to the fed state. The gastrocolic reflex is triggered by a meal and acts on the colon. Receptive relaxation is a brief gastric event as food arrives and produces no traveling contraction."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma motilin rises during a fast. What does this predict?\nA) Contraction of the gallbladder and release of pancreatic enzymes\nB) Relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter\nC) Onset of a migrating motor complex\nD) A burst of segmentation in the jejunum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Onset of a migrating motor complex","why":"Motilin peaks cyclically during fasting and initiates phase III of the migrating motor complex, which is why motilin receptor agonists speed gastric emptying. Segmentation is the fed pattern and is not motilin driven. Gallbladder contraction and enzyme release are cholecystokinin effects triggered by fat and protein in the duodenum. Lower esophageal sphincter relaxation is a swallow triggered vagal event."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the primary purpose of peristalsis?\nA) Clearing residue from the tract between meals\nB) Propulsion of contents along the tract in one direction\nC) Mixing of contents with little net movement\nD) Grinding of solid particles against a closed sphincter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Propulsion of contents along the tract in one direction","why":"Peristalsis is a progressive wave of contraction behind the bolus and relaxation ahead of it, so material is squeezed forward. Mixing with little net movement is segmentation. Grinding against a closed pylorus is gastric retropulsion, a specialized stomach behavior. Clearing residue between meals is the job of the migrating motor complex."},{"t":"mc","q":"When does the migrating motor complex occur?\nA) Continuously, whether fed or fasting\nB) Immediately after a meal enters the stomach\nC) During fasting, between meals\nD) Only during sleep\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) During fasting, between meals","why":"The migrating motor complex is the fasting pattern; feeding abolishes it and replaces it with the fed pattern of segmentation and short peristaltic waves. It is not restricted to sleep, though it is easier to record at night. It is not continuous, which is the point: it is a cycle that appears only when the tract is empty enough to need sweeping."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Segmentation contractions in the small intestine mix chyme with digestive secretions but produce little net forward movement.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Mixing gives enzymes time to act and brings digested nutrients into contact with the brush border. If the small intestine only pushed chyme forward, absorption would fall because contact time would be too short. This is why loss of segmentation or an overly fast transit leads to malabsorption."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three horizontal strips of gut, each with three snapshots in time left to right. Strip one is peristalsis: show the contracted ring behind and the relaxed segment ahead, and the net movement. Strip two is segmentation: show alternating contractions with no net movement. Strip three is the migrating motor complex sweeping down an empty tract. Label the region and the timing of each. One line: say which pattern you would lose if food never left the stomach, and what would collect there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a timeline across the page: a meal at the left, then the hours after it, then the fasted state. Above the line, draw which motility pattern dominates at each point and in which region. Below the line, draw what the pattern is doing to the contents, moving them, mixing them with secretions, or clearing debris. One line: say what an antibiotic or a drug that stops the fasting sweep would let build up in the small intestine."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare peristalsis, segmentation, and the migrating motor complex by pattern and purpose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Motility patterns. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-gi-regulation","name":"Neural and hormonal regulation","can":"Explain short and long reflex control by the enteric nervous system and match gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, and glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide to their stimulus and action.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a meal high in cream and meat, the gallbladder contracts and the pancreas releases lipase and trypsinogen. Which hormone is responsible?\nA) Cholecystokinin  |  B) Motilin\nC) Secretin  |  D) Gastrin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cholecystokinin","why":"Fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum stimulate I cells to release cholecystokinin, which contracts the gallbladder and stimulates acinar enzyme secretion, exactly the two things a fatty, proteinaceous meal needs. Secretin would supply bicarbonate instead. Gastrin acts back in the stomach on acid. Motilin drives the fasting migrating motor complex, not a fed response."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks the gastrin receptor on the parietal cell. Predict the effect on gastric pH and on circulating gastrin.\nA) Gastric pH rises and circulating gastrin rises\nB) Gastric pH rises and circulating gastrin falls\nC) Gastric pH falls and circulating gastrin rises\nD) Gastric pH falls and circulating gastrin falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gastric pH rises and circulating gastrin rises","why":"Blocking the receptor removes a major drive on acid secretion, so less HCl is made and luminal pH climbs. Antral G cells are inhibited by low pH, so when acid falls that brake is released and gastrin secretion actually rises, a pattern seen with any effective acid suppressing drug. The two options predicting falling pH have the direct effect backward, and the option predicting falling gastrin misses the loss of negative feedback."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cells of the gastric mucosa secrete gastrin?\nA) Parietal cells  |  B) G cells\nC) I cells  |  D) S cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) G cells","why":"G cells in the gastric antrum release gastrin into the blood in response to peptides and distension. Parietal cells are the target of gastrin, not its source, and they secrete acid and intrinsic factor. I cells and S cells sit in the duodenal mucosa and release cholecystokinin and secretin respectively."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the principal action of gastrin?\nA) It stimulates insulin release before glucose is absorbed\nB) It increases pancreatic bicarbonate secretion\nC) It increases gastric acid secretion\nD) It causes the gallbladder to contract\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It increases gastric acid secretion","why":"Gastrin drives the parietal cell to secrete HCl, both directly and by stimulating histamine release from enterochromaffin like cells, and it also promotes gastric motility and mucosal growth. Pancreatic bicarbonate is the secretin response. Gallbladder contraction is a cholecystokinin effect. Anticipatory insulin release is the job of glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A research volunteer is given a drug that blocks cholecystokinin receptors and then eats a meal high in fat. Ultrasound measures gallbladder volume, a duodenal tube samples pancreatic enzyme output, and a marker tracks gastric emptying.\nCompared with the same meal without the drug, predict the gallbladder, the pancreatic enzyme output, and the rate of gastric emptying.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The gallbladder fails to contract, enzyme output is much lower, and gastric emptying is faster than normal","why":"Fat and protein in the duodenum release cholecystokinin, and cholecystokinin does three things: contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi so bile enters the duodenum, stimulates acinar cells to secrete enzymes, and slows gastric emptying so fat is delivered at a rate the duodenum can handle. Block the receptor and all three responses are lost. Losing the brake means the stomach empties fat faster, which is the opposite of what a fatty meal normally does."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Secretin is released when acidic chyme enters the duodenum, and it stimulates the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"S cells in the duodenal mucosa sense low pH and release secretin into the blood. Secretin acts on pancreatic duct cells to increase bicarbonate secretion and also inhibits gastric acid secretion and emptying. This is a clean negative feedback loop: acid triggers the response that removes the acid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a length of gut wall with the two enteric plexuses in place, the submucosal and the myenteric, and name what each controls. On it, draw a short reflex arc that never leaves the gut wall, sensor to plexus to effector. Then draw a long reflex looping out to the brainstem and back down the vagus. Mark the stimulus that starts each. One line: say which reflex still works in a transplanted gut with the vagus cut, and what that gut cannot do."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the stomach and duodenum, and place four hormone secreting cells where they actually sit: G cells, S cells, I cells and K cells. For each, draw an arrow in for its stimulus and an arrow out to its target with the action written on it. Use real stimuli, peptides and stretch, acid, fat and protein, glucose. One line: pick the hormone whose job is to protect a downstream organ rather than to digest anything, and say what it protects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain short and long reflex control by the enteric nervous system and match gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, and glucose dependent insulinotropic peptide to their stimulus and action."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Neural and hormonal regulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-digestive-phases","name":"Phases of digestive control","can":"Describe the cephalic, gastric, and intestinal phases and state the dominant control mechanism in each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Chyme with a pH of 2 and a high fat content arrives in the duodenum. Predict the effect on the rate of gastric emptying and name the phase.\nA) Emptying slows, in the cephalic phase\nB) Emptying speeds up, in the gastric phase\nC) Emptying slows, in the intestinal phase\nD) Emptying speeds up, in the intestinal phase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Emptying slows, in the intestinal phase","why":"Acid and fat in the duodenum are the two strongest signals for the enterogastric reflex and for secretin and cholecystokinin, all of which restrain the pylorus so the duodenum is not overwhelmed. Speeding emptying under those conditions would deliver more acid and fat than the duodenum could neutralize or emulsify. The cephalic phase has ended by this point, and the gastric phase concerns stimuli inside the stomach."},{"t":"mc","q":"Receptive relaxation allows the stomach to accept a large meal with little rise in pressure. Which phase and mechanism does it belong to?\nA) The gastric phase, through a local myenteric reflex\nB) The cephalic phase, through a vagovagal reflex\nC) The intestinal phase, through cholecystokinin\nD) The gastric phase, through gastrin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cephalic phase, through a vagovagal reflex","why":"Swallowing triggers a vagovagal reflex that relaxes the gastric fundus before food arrives, which is why volume can rise sharply without much pressure change. A local myenteric reflex cannot anticipate food that has not arrived. Cholecystokinin acts later and mainly on the pancreas and gallbladder. Gastrin increases secretion and motility rather than producing relaxation."},{"t":"mc","q":"What triggers the cephalic phase of digestive control?\nA) Distension of the stomach wall as the swallowed meal arrives in it\nB) The sight, smell, taste, or thought of food before it is swallowed\nC) Arrival of acidic and fatty chyme in the duodenum\nD) A rise in blood glucose after nutrients are absorbed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sight, smell, taste, or thought of food before it is swallowed","why":"The cephalic phase is anticipatory: signals from the head prepare the stomach before food arrives. Gastric distension starts the gastric phase. Acidic chyme in the duodenum starts the intestinal phase. A rise in blood glucose is a metabolic consequence of absorption, well after all three phases are underway."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which nerve carries the efferent signal of the cephalic phase to the stomach?\nA) The greater splanchnic nerve  |  B) The vagus nerve\nC) The phrenic nerve  |  D) The pelvic nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The vagus nerve","why":"The vagus is the parasympathetic outflow to the foregut and midgut and is the entire efferent limb of the cephalic phase, which is why cutting it abolishes anticipatory acid secretion. The greater splanchnic nerve is sympathetic and generally inhibits digestion. The phrenic nerve serves the diaphragm. The pelvic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon and rectum, not the stomach."},{"t":"mc","q":"What initiates the gastric phase of digestive control?\nA) A fall in duodenal pH as acid crosses the pylorus\nB) Fatty acids and amino acids arriving in the duodenal lumen\nC) Distension of the stomach and peptides in the gastric lumen\nD) The taste and smell of food while it is chewed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Distension of the stomach and peptides in the gastric lumen","why":"Stretch receptors and chemoreceptors in the stomach wall respond to the arrival of the meal itself, launching local reflexes and gastrin release. Fatty acids in the duodenum and a fall in duodenal pH are intestinal phase stimuli. Taste belongs to the cephalic phase, which has already peaked by the time the meal is in the stomach."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During the intestinal phase, acid and fat arriving in the duodenum speed up gastric emptying so the meal moves through faster.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Acid and fat in the duodenum slow gastric emptying, through secretin, cholecystokinin, and enteric reflexes, so chyme arrives no faster than the duodenum can neutralize and digest it.","why":"The intestinal phase is a feedback brake. The duodenum is small and can only neutralize and digest so much per minute. Sensing acid, fat, and hypertonic chyme, it sends hormonal and neural signals back to the stomach to reduce motility and tighten the pylorus. A fatty meal stays in the stomach longer for exactly this reason."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time on the horizontal, gastric acid secretion rate on the vertical, and mark the moment food is swallowed. Divide the curve into cephalic, gastric and intestinal phases with vertical lines. Under each phase write the trigger and the dominant control mechanism, neural or hormonal. Show the intestinal phase pulling the curve down. One line: say what is stimulating secretion during the phase before any food reaches the stomach."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the stomach three times in a row. In the first, food is still in the mouth or only smelled: draw the vagal pathway arriving and what it turns on. In the second, food is in the stomach: draw distension receptors, peptides in the lumen, gastrin in the blood. In the third, chyme has entered the duodenum: draw the reflex and the hormones sending inhibition back. One line: say why the third panel exists at all, given the stomach is done with the meal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the cephalic, gastric, and intestinal phases and state the dominant control mechanism in each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Phases of digestive control. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-gastric-secretion","name":"Gastric secretion and mucosal protection","can":"Trace the production of hydrochloric acid by the parietal cell and explain how the mucosal barrier protects the stomach and how ulcers develop when it fails.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug irreversibly inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase. Predict the effect on gastric pH and on pepsin activity.\nA) Gastric pH is unchanged because histamine bypasses the pump\nB) Gastric pH rises and pepsin activity falls\nC) Gastric pH rises and pepsin activity rises\nD) Gastric pH falls and pepsin activity falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Gastric pH rises and pepsin activity falls","why":"The proton pump is the final common step for all three stimulatory pathways, so blocking it raises luminal pH regardless of how the cell is stimulated. Pepsinogen requires an acid environment both to be converted to pepsin and for pepsin to work, so activity falls with the acid. Histamine acts upstream on the parietal cell receptor and cannot bypass a blocked pump."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which gastric cell secretes hydrochloric acid?\nA) The parietal cell  |  B) The mucous neck cell\nC) The enterochromaffin like cell  |  D) The chief cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The parietal cell","why":"Parietal cells, also called oxyntic cells, contain the H+/K+ ATPase that pumps protons into the lumen, and they also make intrinsic factor. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Mucous neck cells secrete the mucus and bicarbonate of the protective barrier. Enterochromaffin like cells release histamine, which stimulates the parietal cell but does not itself secrete acid."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pump moves H+ across the apical membrane of the parietal cell into the gastric lumen?\nA) The Na+/H+ exchanger  |  B) The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger\nC) The Na+/K+ ATPase  |  D) The H+/K+ ATPase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The H+/K+ ATPase","why":"The H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump, exchanges luminal K+ for cytoplasmic H+ using ATP directly, and it is the final common step that drugs called proton pump inhibitors block. The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane and maintains the Na+ gradient for other transport. The Na+/H+ exchanger handles pH regulation in many cells but is not the acid secreting pump here. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is basolateral and produces the alkaline tide."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme inside the parietal cell generates the H+ used for acid secretion?\nA) Pepsinogen  |  B) Carbonic anhydrase\nC) Enteropeptidase  |  D) Alcohol dehydrogenase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Carbonic anhydrase","why":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 and water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ for secretion and HCO3- that leaves across the basolateral membrane. Pepsinogen is the inactive protein digesting enzyme secreted by chief cells. Enteropeptidase is a duodenal brush border enzyme that activates trypsinogen. Alcohol dehydrogenase in gastric mucosa metabolizes ethanol and has nothing to do with acid production."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 70 year old woman has taken high doses of ibuprofen, a drug that blocks prostaglandin synthesis, for a year for arthritis. She now has a gastric ulcer. Her gastric acid output when tested is 20 mmol per hour (normal stimulated output 15 to 25).\nHer acid output is normal. Why did an ulcer form anyway?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Blocking prostaglandins reduced mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, so a normal amount of acid reached and damaged an unprotected epithelium","why":"The stomach survives its own acid because prostaglandins keep the defenses running: the mucus layer, the bicarbonate trapped inside it, and the blood flow that carries away any acid that leaks through. Nonsteroidal anti inflammatory drugs shut off prostaglandin synthesis, so those defenses fade while the acid stays the same. An ulcer forms when attack exceeds defense, and here the defense side dropped."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace how a parietal cell makes hydrochloric acid, then explain why the stomach lining does not digest itself.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"carbonic anhydrase combines carbon dioxide and water to make hydrogen ion and bicarbonate; the hydrogen potassium ATPase pumps hydrogen ion into the lumen in exchange for potassium; chloride leaves the cell through apical channels to join the hydrogen ion; bicarbonate exits into the blood, the alkaline tide; a mucus and bicarbonate layer keeps the pH at the cell surface near neutral; tight junctions block acid from entering between cells and damaged cells are replaced quickly; pepsin is released as inactive pepsinogen and prostaglandins maintain mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a strip of gastric mucosa with its defenses drawn to scale: the mucus layer, the bicarbonate trapped in it, the tight junctions between surface cells, and the blood supply underneath. Write the luminal pH above the mucus and the pH at the cell surface below it. Then draw the same strip after the barrier fails, with acid and pepsin reaching the cells. One line: name one thing that breaks this barrier and say which defense it takes out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace the production of hydrochloric acid by the parietal cell and explain how the mucosal barrier protects the stomach and how ulcers develop when it fails. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the production of hydrochloric acid by the parietal cell and explain how the mucosal barrier protects the stomach and how ulcers develop when it fails."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Gastric secretion and mucosal protection. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-pancreatic-bile-secretion","name":"Pancreatic and biliary secretion","can":"State the components of pancreatic juice and bile and explain how bicarbonate neutralizes chyme and how bile salts emulsify fat.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A stone completely blocks the common bile duct. Predict the effect on fat in the stool and on stool color.\nA) Fat in the stool decreases and the stool becomes pale\nB) Fat in the stool increases and the stool becomes pale\nC) Fat in the stool increases and the stool becomes dark\nD) Fat in the stool is unchanged and the stool becomes pale\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Fat in the stool increases and the stool becomes pale","why":"Without bile salts the fat is poorly emulsified and poorly carried to the brush border in micelles, so it passes into the stool, and without bilirubin reaching the intestine the pigment that normally colors stool is missing. Darkening would require more pigment, not less. Fat absorption cannot be unchanged when the emulsifier and micelle carrier are both absent, and it certainly does not improve."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cells of the pancreas secrete the bicarbonate rich component of pancreatic juice?\nA) Alpha cells of the islets  |  B) Acinar cells\nC) Duct cells  |  D) Beta cells of the islets\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Duct cells","why":"Duct cells respond to secretin by secreting a watery, bicarbonate rich fluid that carries the enzymes and neutralizes chyme. Acinar cells make the enzymes themselves under cholecystokinin control. Islet beta and alpha cells secrete insulin and glucagon into blood and have no exocrine duct at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of the following is a pancreatic enzyme secreted as an inactive zymogen?\nA) Trypsinogen  |  B) Pancreatic lipase\nC) Ribonuclease  |  D) Pancreatic amylase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Trypsinogen","why":"The proteases are secreted as zymogens, trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen and procarboxypeptidase, so that they cannot digest the pancreas itself. Amylase, lipase, and the nucleases are secreted in active form because starch, fat, and nucleic acids are not structural components of the pancreatic cell in a way that would be attacked. Activation of trypsinogen in the duodenum is the switch that turns the whole proteolytic system on."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme activates trypsinogen, and where is it located?\nA) Enteropeptidase, on the duodenal brush border\nB) Carbonic anhydrase, inside the acinar cell\nC) Amylase, in pancreatic juice\nD) Pepsin, in the stomach lumen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Enteropeptidase, on the duodenal brush border","why":"Enteropeptidase, also called enterokinase, is anchored to the duodenal brush border and cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the remaining zymogens including more trypsinogen. Putting the activator outside the pancreas is the safety feature. Pepsin works in the stomach on dietary protein. Carbonic anhydrase supplies bicarbonate in duct cells, and amylase digests starch."},{"t":"mc","q":"From what molecule does the liver synthesize bile salts?\nA) Phospholipid  |  B) Bilirubin\nC) Cholesterol  |  D) Triglyceride\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cholesterol","why":"Hepatocytes convert cholesterol to bile acids, which are then conjugated with glycine or taurine to form bile salts, and this is one of the body's few routes for disposing of cholesterol. Bilirubin is a separate waste product from heme, excreted in bile but not a precursor of bile salts. Triglyceride and phospholipid are lipids in their own right, not bile salt precursors, though lecithin is secreted alongside bile salts."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 50 year old man has a gallstone lodged in the common bile duct, above the point where the pancreatic duct joins it, so pancreatic juice still reaches the duodenum but bile does not. His stools are pale and greasy. His prothrombin time is 22 seconds (normal 11 to 13.5), meaning his blood clots slowly.\nExplain the greasy stool and the slow clotting.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Without bile salts, fat is not emulsified and micelles do not form, so lipase works poorly and fat and the fat soluble vitamin K are lost in the stool, leaving the liver short of vitamin K for clotting factors","why":"Bile salts do two jobs in fat absorption: they emulsify fat so lipase has enough surface area, and they form micelles that ferry the products to the brush border. Without them, lipase still works but very inefficiently, and the products cannot reach the mucosa, so fat leaves in the stool. Vitamin K travels with the fat and is lost with it. The liver needs vitamin K to make several clotting factors, so clotting slows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the duodenum with the pancreatic duct and the bile duct arriving at it. On the pancreas draw an acinar cell and a duct cell, and label what each secretes, enzymes from one and a watery bicarbonate fluid from the other. Draw a pH scale along the duodenum showing chyme arriving acidic and being neutralized. Mark the hormone that drives each secretion. One line: say what happens to pancreatic enzyme activity if the bicarbonate fails, and why the enzymes care."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one large fat droplet in watery chyme, then draw it again after bile salts arrive, now as many small droplets with bile salt molecules at each surface. Show the polar and nonpolar ends of one bile salt. Beside the two pictures, draw a bar for total droplet surface area in each case. Then draw lipase working on a surface. One line: say whether bile salts break any chemical bonds, and if not, name what they actually changed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the components of pancreatic juice and bile and explain how bicarbonate neutralizes chyme and how bile salts emulsify fat."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pancreatic and biliary secretion. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-carb-protein-absorption","name":"Carbohydrate and protein digestion and absorption","can":"Trace a starch and a protein from mouth to bloodstream naming the enzymes at each step and the transport mechanism at the brush border.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme begins starch digestion, and where does it act?\nA) Salivary amylase, in the mouth  |  B) Maltase, on the brush border\nC) Pepsin, in the stomach  |  D) Pancreatic amylase, in the duodenum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Salivary amylase, in the mouth","why":"Salivary amylase starts cleaving the internal bonds of starch while food is still being chewed, though it accounts for only a modest share of total starch digestion. Pancreatic amylase continues the job in the duodenum. Maltase is a brush border enzyme that works on the products of amylase, not on intact starch. Pepsin digests protein and has no action on carbohydrate."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to salivary amylase when a bolus reaches the stomach?\nA) It is converted to pancreatic amylase\nB) It is activated by gastric acid and works faster\nC) It is inactivated by gastric acid\nD) It is absorbed intact across the gastric mucosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It is inactivated by gastric acid","why":"Salivary amylase works best near neutral pH and is denatured as the bolus mixes with acid, which is why starch digestion pauses in the stomach and resumes in the duodenum. Acid activates pepsinogen, not amylase, and the two enzymes have opposite pH preferences. Enzymes are proteins and are digested rather than absorbed intact. Pancreatic amylase is a separate gene product made by the pancreas."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the main products of pancreatic amylase acting on starch?\nA) Branched glycogen and cellulose fibers\nB) Free glucose and free galactose molecules only\nC) Maltose, maltotriose, and alpha limit dextrins\nD) The disaccharides sucrose and lactose\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Maltose, maltotriose, and alpha limit dextrins","why":"Amylase is an endoenzyme that cuts internal alpha 1,4 bonds but cannot cut the alpha 1,6 branch points, so it leaves short oligosaccharides that brush border enzymes finish. Free monosaccharides are the product of the brush border step, not of amylase. Sucrose and lactose are dietary disaccharides, not products of starch digestion. Glycogen is a storage polysaccharide and cellulose is indigestible fiber."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme is located on the brush border rather than being secreted into the lumen?\nA) Lactase  |  B) Pepsin\nC) Pancreatic amylase  |  D) Trypsin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lactase","why":"Lactase, along with sucrase, maltase, and alpha dextrinase, is anchored in the apical membrane of the enterocyte so the monosaccharides it releases appear right at the transporter that takes them up. Pancreatic amylase and trypsin are secreted into the lumen from the pancreas. Pepsin works in the gastric lumen. This membrane bound arrangement is why brush border damage causes carbohydrate malabsorption."},{"t":"mc","q":"By which transporter do glucose and galactose cross the apical membrane of the enterocyte?\nA) A primary active ATPase specific for hexose sugars\nB) GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion down a gradient\nC) SGLT1, a Na+ dependent secondary active cotransporter\nD) GLUT2, by facilitated diffusion down a gradient\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) SGLT1, a Na+ dependent secondary active cotransporter","why":"SGLT1 uses the inward Na+ gradient to drag glucose or galactose into the cell against its own concentration gradient, which is why these sugars can be absorbed almost completely. GLUT5 is the fructose carrier on the same membrane. GLUT2 is the basolateral exit route. There is no ATPase that pumps hexoses directly; the energy is spent by the Na+/K+ ATPase and used indirectly."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace a dietary protein from the stomach to the portal blood. Name the enzymes at each step, how they are activated, and how the products cross the brush border.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"pepsin, activated from pepsinogen by stomach acid, begins protein digestion; pancreatic trypsin and chymotrypsin continue digestion in the small intestine; trypsinogen is activated by enteropeptidase on the brush border and trypsin activates the other zymogens; brush border peptidases cut oligopeptides to amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides; amino acids enter the enterocyte by sodium dependent cotransport; dipeptides and tripeptides enter by hydrogen ion dependent cotransport and are broken down inside the cell; amino acids leave the basolateral membrane and travel in the portal vein to the liver","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one starch molecule and follow it left to right across the page: mouth, stomach, small intestine lumen, brush border, enterocyte, blood. At each step name the enzyme and what it leaves behind, and mark where salivary amylase stops working and why. At the brush border draw the apical transporter that moves glucose in with sodium and the basolateral carrier that moves it out. One line: say which single transport step in your drawing is the one that actually needs energy, and where that energy comes from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a protein chain and follow it from stomach to blood. In the stomach show pepsinogen becoming pepsin and mark the pH it needs. In the small intestine show the pancreatic enzymes arriving inactive, enteropeptidase at the brush border switching on trypsin, and trypsin switching on the rest. At the enterocyte, draw two apical routes in, one for single amino acids and one for short peptides. One line: say why the pancreas ships these enzymes out inactive, and what goes wrong when they activate early."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace a starch and a protein from mouth to bloodstream naming the enzymes at each step and the transport mechanism at the brush border."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Carbohydrate and protein digestion and absorption. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-lipid-absorption","name":"Lipid digestion and absorption","can":"Trace a triglyceride through emulsification, lipase action, micelle formation, and chylomicron packaging to the lymphatic route and explain why fat takes a different path than glucose.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient absorbs fat poorly because bile salt concentration in the duodenum is too low to form micelles. Which additional deficiency should you expect?\nA) Deficiency of vitamin C and the B vitamins\nB) Deficiency of iron and calcium only\nC) Deficiency of vitamin B12 alone\nD) Deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K","why":"The fat soluble vitamins dissolve in the lipid phase and depend on micelles to reach the brush border, so they are lost whenever micelle formation fails. Vitamin C and the B vitamins are water soluble and are absorbed by their own transporters independent of bile. Iron and calcium have dedicated transport systems and are not micelle dependent. Vitamin B12 requires intrinsic factor and the ileal receptor, a separate pathway."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does plasma appear milky a few hours after a very fatty meal?\nA) Undigested triglyceride is absorbed directly into the portal blood\nB) Free fatty acids bound to albumin scatter light\nC) Chylomicrons entering the blood from the thoracic duct scatter light\nD) Bile salts absorbed from the ileum accumulate in plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Chylomicrons entering the blood from the thoracic duct scatter light","why":"Chylomicrons are large enough to scatter visible light, and they enter the venous blood at the end of the lymphatic route a few hours after a fatty meal, before lipoprotein lipase clears them. Albumin bound fatty acids are molecularly dispersed and do not cloud plasma. Bile salts recirculate at low plasma concentration and are not turbid. Intact triglyceride is not absorbed into portal blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"What do bile salts do to dietary fat in the duodenum?\nA) They convert fatty acids directly into chylomicrons\nB) They transport intact triglyceride into the enterocyte\nC) They hydrolyze the ester bonds of the triglyceride\nD) They emulsify it, breaking large droplets into small ones\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They emulsify it, breaking large droplets into small ones","why":"Bile salts are amphipathic, so they coat droplet surfaces and prevent the small droplets from merging back together, multiplying the surface available to lipase. Hydrolysis of ester bonds is what lipase does, and bile salts have no catalytic activity at all. Chylomicron assembly happens inside the enterocyte. Triglyceride is digested before absorption, not carried in intact."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which protein must bind to pancreatic lipase so that it can work at the surface of a bile salt coated fat droplet?\nA) Colipase  |  B) Apoprotein B 48\nC) Intrinsic factor  |  D) Enteropeptidase\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Colipase","why":"Bile salts on the droplet surface would otherwise displace lipase, and colipase anchors the enzyme to the lipid interface so hydrolysis can proceed. Apoprotein B 48 is a structural protein of the chylomicron, added later inside the cell. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12. Enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen and has no lipid role."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 30 year old woman had 100 cm of terminal ileum removed after an injury. Her pancreas and liver are normal. Two months later she has diarrhea after every meal, and a stool test shows fat at 20 g per day (normal less than 7 g per day).\nWhy is fat appearing in her stool when her lipase and bile production are both normal?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Bile salts are no longer reabsorbed in the ileum, so the bile salt pool shrinks and there are not enough to emulsify fat and form micelles, and the unabsorbed fat and bile salts entering the colon draw in water and cause diarrhea","why":"Fat digestion and most fat absorption happen in the duodenum and jejunum, which she still has. What she lost is the site that recovers bile salts. Every meal now loses most of the pool to the colon, and the liver cannot synthesize replacement fast enough. With too few bile salts, emulsification and micelle formation fail, and fat is left behind. In the colon, the fatty acids and bile salts stimulate secretion, and the osmotic load holds water, so she gets diarrhea."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why a dietary triglyceride ends up in the lymph while a dietary glucose molecule ends up in the portal blood. Trace each step for the fat.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"bile salts emulsify fat into small droplets; pancreatic lipase breaks triglyceride into monoglycerides and free fatty acids; products are carried in micelles to the brush border and diffuse into the enterocyte; triglyceride is rebuilt inside the cell and packaged with protein into chylomicrons; chylomicrons are too large to enter blood capillaries so they enter lacteals and reach blood through the thoracic duct; glucose is small and water soluble, crosses on SGLT1 and GLUT2, and enters capillaries leading to the portal vein","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one triglyceride and follow it across the page in five stages: emulsified into small droplets by bile salts, cut by pancreatic lipase into free fatty acids and monoglyceride, carried in a micelle to the brush border, rebuilt into triglyceride inside the enterocyte, and packaged with protein into a chylomicron. End with the chylomicron leaving the basolateral side. One line: say what the micelle is for, given the products can cross the membrane on their own once they get there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a single villus with a blood capillary and a lacteal inside it. Send glucose in one color from the enterocyte into the capillary and on to the hepatic portal vein and liver. Send a chylomicron in another color into the lacteal, up the lymph, and into the venous blood near the neck. Mark the two entry points into the circulation. One line: say what a chylomicron cannot get through that glucose can, and what that means for when the liver first sees dietary fat."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace a triglyceride through emulsification, lipase action, micelle formation, and chylomicron packaging to the lymphatic route and explain why fat takes a different path than glucose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Lipid digestion and absorption. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-liver-function","name":"Liver function and enterohepatic circulation","can":"State the metabolic, storage, and detoxification functions of the liver and trace the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with severe liver failure bruises easily and bleeds after minor cuts. Which liver function has failed?\nA) Phagocytosis by Kupffer cells  |  B) Storage of vitamin B12\nC) Synthesis of clotting factors  |  D) Conjugation of bilirubin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Synthesis of clotting factors","why":"The liver makes fibrinogen, prothrombin, and most of the other clotting factors, so hepatocyte failure lengthens clotting time within days because several of these proteins have short half lives. B12 storage failure would take years to appear and would cause anemia, not bleeding. Failed bilirubin conjugation causes jaundice. Loss of Kupffer cell function raises infection risk rather than bleeding risk."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which liver function is being used when a person who has not eaten for 24 hours maintains a normal blood glucose?\nA) Bile salt synthesis and secretion\nB) Kupffer cell phagocytosis\nC) Bilirubin conjugation and excretion\nD) Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","why":"After a day without food the liver first breaks down its glycogen and then makes new glucose from lactate, glycerol, and amino acids, keeping plasma glucose in range for the brain. Bilirubin handling is a waste disposal function. Bile salt synthesis serves fat digestion and continues, but it does not defend blood glucose. Kupffer cells are immune cells."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which molecule does the liver store and release to keep blood glucose stable between meals?\nA) Bilirubin  |  B) Triglyceride\nC) Glycogen  |  D) Albumin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Glycogen","why":"The liver stores glucose as glycogen after a meal and breaks it back down during fasting, which is the main short term buffer for blood glucose. Triglyceride is stored mainly in adipose tissue, and liver triglyceride accumulation is a pathological finding rather than a glucose reserve. Albumin is a protein the liver exports, not a glucose store. Bilirubin is a waste product excreted in bile."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which plasma protein made by the liver is the main determinant of plasma colloid osmotic pressure?\nA) Hemoglobin  |  B) Albumin\nC) Fibrin  |  D) Intrinsic factor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Albumin","why":"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and, because it stays in the capillary, it generates most of the osmotic force that pulls fluid back from the interstitium. Hemoglobin is inside red cells and made in bone marrow. Fibrin is the insoluble clot protein formed from fibrinogen, which is a liver product but a small fraction of plasma protein. Intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells."},{"t":"mc","q":"The liver converts ammonia from amino acid breakdown into which molecule for excretion?\nA) Urea  |  B) Creatinine\nC) Bilirubin  |  D) Uric acid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Urea","why":"The urea cycle in hepatocytes converts toxic ammonia into urea, which the kidneys then excrete, and this is the body's main route for disposing of amino acid nitrogen. Uric acid comes from purine breakdown. Creatinine comes from muscle creatine phosphate. Bilirubin comes from heme and has nothing to do with nitrogen from amino acids."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Most of the bile salts secreted into the intestine during a meal are lost in the feces, so the liver must synthesize a new pool every day.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. About 95 percent of bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in the portal vein, and the liver replaces only the small fraction lost in feces.","why":"Synthesizing bile salts from cholesterol is slow, and a meal needs far more than the liver could make on demand. The enterohepatic circulation solves this by recycling the same pool several times per meal. This is also why drugs that bind bile salts in the gut lower plasma cholesterol: the liver has to pull cholesterol out of the blood to replace what is lost."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the loop as a closed circuit: liver makes bile salts, bile duct to gallbladder, gallbladder to duodenum, bile salts work on fat down the small intestine, reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, hepatic portal vein back to the liver. Put an arrow at each leg and mark the recycled fraction as most of the pool. Add a small leak arrow for what is lost in faeces. One line: say what happens to fat digestion after the terminal ileum is surgically removed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a hepatocyte with the sinusoid on one side and the bile canaliculus on the other. Around it draw four labeled jobs with a real example in each: a metabolic job, a storage job, a synthetic job, and a detoxification job. Show which side of the cell each product leaves by. One line: pick the liver job whose failure shows up first in a blood test, and name the substance you would measure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the metabolic, storage, and detoxification functions of the liver and trace the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Liver function and enterohepatic circulation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-large-intestine","name":"Large intestine and microbiome","can":"Describe water and electrolyte absorption in the colon, the contribution of the microbiome, and the defecation reflex.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which molecule serves as the preferred fuel for colonic epithelial cells, and where does it come from?\nA) Glucose, delivered to the cell from arterial blood\nB) Butyrate, made by bacterial fermentation of fiber\nC) Glutamine, produced by colonic bacteria from urea\nD) Ketone bodies, delivered from the liver in portal blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Butyrate, made by bacterial fermentation of fiber","why":"Colonocytes take butyrate directly from the lumen and oxidize it, which is one reason fiber intake supports colonic mucosal health. They can use glucose from blood but butyrate is the preferred substrate. Glutamine is a major fuel for small intestinal enterocytes and comes from blood, not from bacterial urea metabolism. Portal blood flows away from the gut toward the liver, so the liver does not supply the colon by that route."},{"t":"mc","q":"About how much fluid enters the colon each day, and about how much leaves in the stool?\nA) About 1.5 liters enters and about 100 to 150 mL leaves\nB) About 500 mL enters and about 400 mL leaves\nC) About 3 liters enters and about 1.5 liters of it leaves\nD) About 9 liters enters and about 1 liter leaves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 1.5 liters enters and about 100 to 150 mL leaves","why":"The small intestine has already recovered most of the roughly 9 liters that pass through the tract, so the colon receives about 1.5 liters and returns almost all of it, leaving stool about three quarters water by weight but only 100 to 150 mL in volume. Nine liters entering the colon would describe the total load on the whole tract. The other figures either understate the colon's daily load or overstate normal stool water."},{"t":"mc","q":"By which channel does Na+ enter the colonic epithelial cell from the lumen?\nA) The Na+/H+ exchanger driven by luminal acid\nB) SGLT1, cotransported with glucose\nC) The epithelial Na+ channel, ENaC\nD) The Na+/K+ ATPase on the apical membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The epithelial Na+ channel, ENaC","why":"Colonic Na+ absorption is largely through ENaC in the apical membrane, driven by the low intracellular Na+ that the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase maintains, and it is the step that aldosterone upregulates. SGLT1 is a small intestinal carrier and there is little glucose left in the colon anyway. The Na+/K+ ATPase is basolateral, never apical. The Na+/H+ exchanger contributes in the small intestine but is not the main colonic route."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which hormone increases Na+ and water absorption in the colon?\nA) Cholecystokinin  |  B) Gastrin\nC) Secretin  |  D) Aldosterone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Aldosterone","why":"Aldosterone acts on colonic epithelium much as it does on the distal nephron, increasing the number of ENaC channels and Na+/K+ ATPase units so more Na+, and therefore more water, is recovered. Secretin and cholecystokinin are duodenal hormones acting on the pancreas, gallbladder, and stomach. Gastrin acts on the parietal cell and gastric motility."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ion does the colon secrete into the lumen rather than absorb?\nA) Cl-  |  B) K+\nC) HCO3- is neither secreted nor absorbed  |  D) Na+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) K+","why":"The colon absorbs Na+ and Cl- and secretes K+ and HCO3-, which is why prolonged diarrhea causes both potassium loss and a fall in blood bicarbonate. Na+ and Cl- are the ions being recovered. Bicarbonate is very much secreted here, exchanged for the Cl- being absorbed, so calling it neither is incorrect."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The colon absorbs water mainly by absorbing sodium, with water following osmotically.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Sodium enters colonocytes through apical channels and is pumped out the basolateral side by the Na+/K+ ATPase. The solute movement makes the interstitium slightly hypertonic and water follows through the cells and between them. Aldosterone increases the number of apical sodium channels and so increases colonic water absorption, which is one reason volume depletion produces firm stool."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a length of colon in cross section with the lumen contents drawn wet at the entry end and formed at the exit end. Show sodium being pumped out of the lumen across the epithelium and water following it osmotically. Add the microbiome in the lumen with two things it makes for you and one thing it makes that you notice. One line: say what happens to stool water content when transit through the colon is too fast, and name one cause."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the rectum and both anal sphincters, and label which is smooth muscle and which is skeletal. Draw the reflex in sequence: stool distends the rectum, stretch receptors fire, the spinal reflex relaxes the internal sphincter, and a voluntary pathway from the brain holds or releases the external one. Mark which limb you control. One line: say what the voluntary limb is doing when you delay, and where the stool goes in the meantime."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe water and electrolyte absorption in the colon, the contribution of the microbiome, and the defecation reflex."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Large intestine and microbiome. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w14-lab-digestion-enzymes","name":"Digestive enzyme experiment","can":"Test the effect of pH, temperature, and substrate on a digestive enzyme and relate the results to the region of the tract where that enzyme works.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate optimal pH for pepsin?\nA) About 2  |  B) About 5\nC) About 7  |  D) About 9\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 2","why":"Pepsin evolved to work in gastric juice, so its activity peaks near pH 2 and falls to nearly nothing above pH 5. A pH near 7 is where salivary amylase works best. A pH near 9 suits the pancreatic proteases in the duodenum. Matching an enzyme's optimum to a region of the tract is the whole point of the experiment."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a starch digestion experiment, what does a drop of iodine solution detect?\nA) Reducing sugars, by turning orange  |  B) Starch, by turning blue black\nC) Protein, by turning purple  |  D) Fat, by turning the solution cloudy\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Starch, by turning blue black","why":"Iodine forms a blue black complex with the helical structure of starch, so a tube that stays dark still contains starch and a tube that turns pale has had its starch digested. Reducing sugars are detected by Benedict solution, which turns orange or red. Protein is detected by the biuret test, which turns purple. Fat is not detected by iodine."},{"t":"mc","q":"At what temperature do human digestive enzymes work best in a laboratory assay?\nA) About 60 C  |  B) About 4 C\nC) About 25 C  |  D) About 37 C\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About 37 C","why":"Enzyme optima match the environment the enzyme evolved in, and for a human enzyme that is core body temperature near 37 C. At 4 C molecular motion is too slow and the reaction nearly stops, though the enzyme is undamaged. Room temperature at 25 C gives a slow but measurable reaction. By 60 C most human enzymes are denaturing rather than accelerating."},{"t":"mc","q":"An enzyme acts on starch but produces no change when incubated with a protein solution. What property does this demonstrate?\nA) Denaturation  |  B) Substrate specificity\nC) Temperature dependence  |  D) Competitive inhibition\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Substrate specificity","why":"An active site has a shape and chemistry that fit one substrate or one class of bond, so amylase cleaves alpha 1,4 glycosidic bonds and simply cannot bind a polypeptide. Temperature dependence would be shown by varying heat, not substrate. Competitive inhibition requires a molecule that binds the active site and blocks it. Denaturation would abolish activity on all substrates, including starch."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an experiment testing amylase at pH 2, 7, and 9 at 37 C, what is the independent variable?\nA) pH  |  B) The amount of starch digested\nC) The concentration of amylase  |  D) Temperature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) pH","why":"The independent variable is the one the experimenter deliberately changes across tubes, which here is pH, while temperature and enzyme concentration are held constant as controlled variables. The amount of starch digested is what you measure, making it the dependent variable. Naming these correctly is what lets you attribute a difference between tubes to the factor you varied."},{"t":"bd","q":"You have amylase solution, starch solution, iodine, buffers at pH 2, pH 7, and pH 9, and a 37 C (98.6 F) water bath. Without notes, describe how you would test the effect of pH on amylase, the result you expect, and how the result relates to where amylase works in the body.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"equal amounts of amylase and starch in each tube, with only the pH different; same temperature and same incubation time for every tube; iodine test: blue black means starch remains, no color change means starch was digested; expect the most digestion near pH 7 and little or none at pH 2; amylase works in the mouth and duodenum, both near neutral pH; salivary amylase stops working when it reaches the acid of the stomach","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, pH from 1 to 9 on the horizontal, enzyme activity on the vertical. Plot three curves with their peaks in the right places: salivary amylase, pepsin, and a pancreatic enzyme working in the duodenum. Under the axis, draw the region of the tract that matches each pH so the curves line up with the anatomy. One line: say what happens to the amylase curve when the bolus reaches the stomach, and whether that change reverses."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a rack of test tubes for an experiment on one enzyme you pick, not amylase. Draw every tube you would need, including the controls, and write inside each what it contains and what you changed. Draw the expected result you would see in each tube. Beside the rack, sketch the graph you would build from those tubes. One line: name the tube that tells you the result came from the enzyme and not from the conditions alone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to test the effect of pH, temperature, and substrate on a digestive enzyme and relate the results to the region of the tract where that enzyme works."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Digestive enzyme experiment. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Respiratory Mechanics","comps":[{"id":"w12-respiratory-functions","name":"Functions and functional zones","can":"State the functions of the respiratory system and distinguish the conducting zone from the respiratory zone by structure and by role in gas exchange.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person breathes cold dry air at 5 C. What is the condition of that air by the time it reaches the alveoli?\nA) Still near 5 C but fully saturated with water vapor\nB) Warmed to about 37 C and fully saturated with water vapor\nC) Warmed to about 37 C but still dry\nD) Unchanged in temperature and in water content\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Warmed to about 37 C and fully saturated with water vapor","why":"The conducting zone is built to condition air, so by the carina and certainly by the alveolus the air is at body temperature and 100 percent humidified, which protects the delicate exchange surface. Air that stayed at 5 C would injure the alveoli and would also be unable to hold much water. Warm but dry air would dehydrate the surface, and truly unchanged air would do both kinds of damage."},{"t":"mc","q":"Tidal volume is 500 mL and anatomic dead space is 150 mL. How much of one breath actually reaches the respiratory zone?\nA) 150 mL  |  B) 500 mL\nC) 650 mL  |  D) 350 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 350 mL","why":"Fresh air reaching exchange surface is tidal volume minus dead space, so 500 minus 150 gives 350 mL per breath. The 150 mL figure is the part that stays in the conducting airways and never exchanges. The full 500 mL ignores dead space entirely, and 650 mL adds the two numbers instead of subtracting."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tracheostomy tube bypasses the nose, mouth, and pharynx. What is the immediate effect on the anatomic dead space?\nA) It decreases, because part of the conducting zone is bypassed\nB) It increases, because a new airway is added in series\nC) It is unchanged, because dead space is fixed by lung size\nD) It falls to zero, because air enters the trachea directly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It decreases, because part of the conducting zone is bypassed","why":"Anatomic dead space is the volume of all conducting airways, so removing the upper airway from the path takes roughly half of it away. Adding tubing outside the body would increase dead space, but a tracheostomy shortens the path rather than lengthening it. Dead space is not fixed, and it cannot reach zero because the trachea and bronchi still conduct air."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which change would most directly slow oxygen diffusion into the blood?\nA) Loss of cartilage from a large bronchus\nB) Thickening of the alveolar and capillary walls\nC) Increased mucus production in the trachea\nD) Weakness of the external intercostal muscles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Thickening of the alveolar and capillary walls","why":"Diffusion rate falls as the barrier gets thicker, so any process that adds tissue between alveolar air and capillary blood directly slows gas transfer. Loss of bronchial cartilage and extra tracheal mucus both affect conduction of air, not the exchange barrier itself. Weak intercostals reduce how much air arrives but do not change how fast gas crosses the membrane."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mucus plug completely blocks one terminal bronchiole. What happens to the alveoli downstream of the plug?\nA) They stop being perfused but remain ventilated\nB) They continue to exchange gas normally\nC) They stop being ventilated but remain perfused\nD) They immediately fill with fluid from the capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They stop being ventilated but remain perfused","why":"A terminal bronchiole is the sole air supply to its cluster of alveoli, so blocking it cuts off ventilation while pulmonary capillary blood keeps flowing past, creating blood that leaves the lung without picking up oxygen. Perfusion is set by the pulmonary arteries, not by the airway. Normal exchange is impossible without fresh air, and capillary fluid does not pour into a blocked alveolus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the airway as a branching tree from the nose down to the alveoli, with the named levels marked. Draw a line across it at the terminal bronchiole and label the conducting zone above and the respiratory zone below. Beside the conducting zone write its jobs and its volume, about 150 mL of air that never exchanges. Beside the respiratory zone draw an alveolus against a capillary. One line: say what the conducting zone is doing to the air that the alveoli would suffer without."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw five boxes for five jobs the respiratory system does, and in each draw the structure that does it, not just the word. Include gas exchange, the fast control of blood pH, sound production, smell, and defense against what you inhale. Mark which of the five happen in the respiratory zone and which do not. One line: name the job that is not usually listed as respiratory but kills you fastest when it fails, and say why it belongs here."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the functions of the respiratory system and distinguish the conducting zone from the respiratory zone by structure and by role in gas exchange."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Functions and functional zones. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-ventilation-mechanics","name":"Pressure gradients and airflow","can":"Apply Boyle's law to inspiration and expiration and relate alveolar pressure to atmospheric pressure at each point in a quiet breath.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A climber breathes at an altitude where atmospheric pressure is 500 mmHg. What must alveolar pressure be for air to flow into the lungs?\nA) Slightly below 760 mmHg, for example 759 mmHg\nB) Exactly 500 mmHg, since flow depends on volume not pressure\nC) Slightly above 500 mmHg, for example 501 mmHg\nD) Slightly below 500 mmHg, for example 499 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Slightly below 500 mmHg, for example 499 mmHg","why":"What matters is the difference between alveolar and ambient pressure, so at altitude the alveolus must simply be a little below the local 500 mmHg for air to move in. Using 759 mmHg applies sea level numbers to a place where they no longer hold, and that alveolar pressure would push air out at altitude. Equal pressures give zero flow, and a pressure above ambient drives expiration."},{"t":"mc","q":"At the exact instant airflow stops at the end of inspiration, what is alveolar pressure?\nA) Below atmospheric pressure  |  B) Above atmospheric pressure\nC) Equal to atmospheric pressure  |  D) Equal to intrapleural pressure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Equal to atmospheric pressure","why":"Airflow is driven by a pressure difference, so flow reaches zero exactly when that difference reaches zero, which means alveolar pressure has climbed back to atmospheric as air filled the lung. A pressure still below atmospheric would keep pulling air in. A pressure above atmospheric would start expiration, and alveolar pressure never equals the much lower intrapleural pressure in a lung that is still inflated."},{"t":"mc","q":"How do the external intercostal muscles contribute to inspiration?\nA) They lift the ribs upward and outward, widening the thorax\nB) They pull the ribs downward and inward, narrowing the thorax\nC) They pull the diaphragm downward from above\nD) They compress the abdominal contents against the diaphragm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) They lift the ribs upward and outward, widening the thorax","why":"Their fibers run downward and forward, so contraction swings each rib up and out in a bucket handle motion that increases the side to side and front to back dimensions of the chest. Pulling ribs down and in is the action of the internal intercostals during forced expiration. The intercostals do not attach to the diaphragm, and abdominal compression pushes the diaphragm up, which empties the lung."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscles are recruited during forced expiration?\nA) The diaphragm and the external intercostals\nB) The abdominal muscles and the internal intercostals\nC) The scalenes and the sternocleidomastoid\nD) The diaphragm and the abdominal muscles together\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The abdominal muscles and the internal intercostals","why":"Abdominal contraction drives the viscera and the diaphragm upward while internal intercostals pull the ribs down, and together they shrink the thorax faster than recoil alone could. The diaphragm and external intercostals are the inspiratory pair. Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid are accessory inspiratory muscles, and the diaphragm never helps empty the lung because its contraction always enlarges the cavity."},{"t":"mc","q":"Atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg and alveolar pressure is 759 mmHg. Which way is air moving?\nA) Out of the lungs, because alveolar pressure is lower\nB) No air moves, because the difference is too small\nC) Into the lungs, because alveolar pressure is lower\nD) Into the lungs, because alveolar pressure is higher\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Into the lungs, because alveolar pressure is lower","why":"Gas flows from higher to lower pressure, so a 1 mmHg deficit inside the alveolus pulls air inward, which is precisely the gradient of a normal quiet inspiration. Air would leave the lung only if alveolar pressure exceeded atmospheric. A 1 mmHg difference is small but entirely sufficient because airway resistance is low, and describing 759 as higher than 760 misreads the numbers."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A student breathes quietly at sea level, where atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg. Midway through inspiration a probe reads alveolar pressure at 759 mmHg. At the end of inspiration, with the chest still expanded and the diaphragm still contracted, alveolar pressure reads 760 mmHg.\nWhy has airflow stopped at the end of inspiration even though the chest is still held in the expanded position?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Alveolar pressure now equals atmospheric pressure, so there is no pressure gradient to drive flow","why":"Air moves only down a pressure gradient. Expanding the chest lowered alveolar pressure below 760, air flowed in, and that inflow raised alveolar pressure back up. Once alveolar pressure matches atmospheric, the gradient is zero and flow stops, even though the lung is now larger. Flow depends on the pressure difference, not on how big the lung is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time across one quiet breath on the horizontal, pressure on the vertical, with atmospheric pressure drawn as a flat line at 760 mmHg. Plot alveolar pressure through the whole cycle: about one below atmospheric during inspiration, equal at the end of inspiration, about one above during expiration, equal again at rest. Under the trace, draw the thorax expanding and recoiling at the matching moments. One line: say at which two moments in the cycle no air is moving, and why."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a sealed container with a movable wall and a fixed amount of gas in it, in two states: small volume with the molecules crowded, large volume with them spread out. Write the pressure comparison between them. Then draw the thorax twice beside it, at rest and mid inspiration, with the diaphragm and rib position in each and the resulting alveolar pressure. Draw the airflow arrow. One line: say which happens first in inspiration, the volume change or the pressure change, and how you know."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to apply Boyle's law to inspiration and expiration and relate alveolar pressure to atmospheric pressure at each point in a quiet breath."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Pressure gradients and airflow. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-intrapleural-pressure","name":"Intrapleural pressure","can":"Explain why intrapleural pressure is subatmospheric and predict what happens to the lung and chest wall when the pleural seal is broken.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Alveolar pressure is 0 mmHg and intrapleural pressure is 4 mmHg below atmospheric. What is transpulmonary pressure?\nA) 4 mmHg  |  B) 0 mmHg\nC) Negative 4 mmHg  |  D) 8 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 4 mmHg","why":"Subtracting negative 4 from 0 gives positive 4 mmHg, a distending pressure that keeps the lung inflated at the resting volume. A value of 0 would mean no distending force and the lung would collapse. Negative 4 comes from reversing the subtraction, and 8 mmHg would require an intrapleural pressure of negative 8."},{"t":"mc","q":"At the peak of inspiration alveolar pressure is 1 mmHg below atmospheric and intrapleural pressure is 6 mmHg below atmospheric. What is transpulmonary pressure?\nA) 7 mmHg  |  B) 5 mmHg\nC) Negative 5 mmHg  |  D) 1 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 5 mmHg","why":"Negative 1 minus negative 6 equals positive 5 mmHg, larger than the resting value of 4, which is exactly why the lung is more inflated at this moment. Adding the two magnitudes gives 7 and ignores that both numbers are negative. Negative 5 reverses the subtraction, and 1 mmHg is just the alveolar value on its own."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does a pneumothorax on the right side usually leave the left lung inflated?\nA) The left lung is supported by the heart\nB) The left lung has stronger elastic recoil\nC) Each lung sits in its own separate pleural cavity\nD) The mediastinum seals the airways on the left\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Each lung sits in its own separate pleural cavity","why":"The two pleural sacs do not communicate, so air entering one leaves the pressure in the other untouched and that lung keeps its normal transpulmonary pressure. The heart does not hold the lung open. Stronger recoil would make a lung collapse more readily, not less, and the mediastinum does not seal off airways."},{"t":"mc","q":"What normally prevents the lungs from collapsing to their own resting volume inside the chest?\nA) Cartilage rings that hold the alveoli open\nB) Continuous low level contraction of the diaphragm muscle between breaths\nC) The positive pressure of the pulmonary arteries\nD) The subatmospheric intrapleural pressure created by the pleural seal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The subatmospheric intrapleural pressure created by the pleural seal","why":"Because pleural pressure is lower than alveolar pressure, there is a net outward distending force on the lung at all times, and that is why an excised lung shrinks but an intact one does not. Cartilage supports conducting airways and never reaches alveoli. The diaphragm relaxes between breaths, and pulmonary arterial pressure acts inside vessels rather than distending air spaces."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: At the end of a quiet expiration, a subject is relaxed with no respiratory muscle activity and no airflow. A probe reads alveolar pressure at 0 cmH2O (equal to atmospheric) and intrapleural pressure at minus 5 cmH2O.\nWhat is holding the lung inflated at this moment, given that no muscle is contracting and no air is flowing?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The transpulmonary pressure of 5 cmH2O, alveolar minus intrapleural, holds the lung open against its elastic recoil","why":"The lung is always trying to collapse. What stops it is the pressure difference across its wall: alveolar pressure inside minus intrapleural pressure outside. At rest that is 0 minus (minus 5), or 5 cmH2O pushing outward, and it exactly balances the inward recoil at functional residual capacity. No muscle work is needed; the sealed pleural space does the holding."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Intrapleural pressure is below atmospheric pressure because the lung pulls inward and the chest wall pulls outward on a sealed, fluid filled space.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The lung wants to collapse and the chest wall wants to spring outward. The pleural fluid between them is a closed system with no air, so the two pulls put it under a slight vacuum, about minus 3 to minus 5 cmH2O at rest. That negative pressure is what holds the lung against the chest wall. Open the seal and both structures move to their resting positions: the lung collapses and the chest wall springs out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cross section of one lung inside the chest wall with the pleural space between them, drawn thin and holding a film of fluid. Draw two opposing arrows on the space: the lung recoiling inward, the chest wall springing outward. Write the pressures at rest, alveolar equal to atmospheric and intrapleural about 4 mmHg below it, and write what those two become at peak inspiration. One line: say what holds the two pleural layers together despite the arrows pulling opposite ways."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same chest twice. On the left, intact, with the lung filling the cavity and the subatmospheric pleural pressure marked. On the right, a hole has let air into the pleural space and the pressure there is now atmospheric. Draw what the lung does and what the chest wall does, and draw the other lung in the same picture. One line: say why one lung can collapse while the other keeps working, and what that tells you about the pleural spaces."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain why intrapleural pressure is subatmospheric and predict what happens to the lung and chest wall when the pleural seal is broken."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Intrapleural pressure. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-compliance","name":"Compliance and elastic recoil","can":"Define lung compliance and elastic recoil and predict the effect of fibrosis and of emphysema on each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A lung takes in 500 mL when transpulmonary pressure rises by 2 cmH2O. What is its compliance?\nA) 250 mL per cmH2O  |  B) 1000 mL per cmH2O\nC) 0.004 mL per cmH2O  |  D) 100 mL per cmH2O\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 250 mL per cmH2O","why":"Compliance is 500 divided by 2, giving 250 mL per cmH2O, which is at the upper end of the normal adult range near 200. The value 1000 comes from multiplying the two numbers instead of dividing. The tiny value inverts the ratio into elastance units, and 100 would require a 5 cmH2O pressure change."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which patient must generate a larger inspiratory pressure to take in a 500 mL breath?\nA) The patient with emphysema, because high compliance resists inflation\nB) The patient with fibrosis, because low compliance resists inflation\nC) The patient with emphysema, because low compliance resists inflation\nD) Neither, because compliance affects only expiration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The patient with fibrosis, because low compliance resists inflation","why":"Low compliance means each milliliter of inflation costs more pressure, so the fibrotic patient does far more inspiratory work for a normal sized breath. High compliance makes inflation easier, not harder, which is why the emphysema patient struggles on the way out rather than the way in. Emphysema raises compliance rather than lowering it, and compliance governs inflation directly."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does lung compliance measure?\nA) The change in lung volume produced by a given change in distending pressure\nB) The change in distending pressure produced by a given change in volume\nC) The speed at which air flows through the conducting airways\nD) The force with which the lung returns to its resting volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The change in lung volume produced by a given change in distending pressure","why":"Compliance is delta V divided by delta P, so it answers how much the lung inflates per unit of pressure applied, which is a measure of stretchability. The reversed ratio is elastance, the stiffness of the tissue. Airflow speed depends on airway resistance rather than tissue stretch, and the force of return to resting volume is elastic recoil."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does elastic recoil of the lung describe?\nA) The ease with which the lung can be inflated\nB) The tendency of the stretched lung to return to its smaller resting volume\nC) The resistance of the airways to airflow during expiration\nD) The pressure needed to overcome the surface tension of the fluid in the trachea\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The tendency of the stretched lung to return to its smaller resting volume","why":"Elastic recoil is stored energy in stretched elastin and in the alveolar surface film that pulls the lung back down in volume, and it is what powers quiet expiration. Ease of inflation is compliance, the opposite property. Airway resistance is a separate flow property, and the surface tension that matters acts at the alveolar air liquid interface rather than in the trachea."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which expression correctly gives lung compliance?\nA) Change in transpulmonary pressure divided by change in volume\nB) Change in volume multiplied by change in transpulmonary pressure\nC) Change in volume divided by change in transpulmonary pressure\nD) Change in volume divided by change in airflow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Change in volume divided by change in transpulmonary pressure","why":"Compliance is volume gained per unit of pressure, written as delta V over delta P, and normal lung values are near 200 mL per cmH2O. Inverting the ratio gives elastance, which rises when compliance falls. Multiplying the two has no physiological meaning, and dividing volume by airflow gives a time, not a stretch property."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 62 year old patient is tested. A change in transpulmonary pressure of 10 cmH2O produces a change in lung volume of only 300 mL, giving a compliance of 30 mL per cmH2O (normal about 200 mL per cmH2O).\nPredict this patient's elastic recoil and which phase of breathing demands the most extra work.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Recoil is high, and inspiration is hard because the lung resists being stretched","why":"Compliance of 30 means the lung is stiff: a lot of pressure buys very little volume. Stiffness and strong recoil are the same property viewed from two sides. A stiff lung springs back easily, so expiration is quick and passive, but every inspiration requires the muscles to generate a large negative pressure. This is the restrictive picture seen in pulmonary fibrosis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, transpulmonary pressure on the horizontal, lung volume on the vertical, and plot three curves: a normal lung, a fibrotic lung, and an emphysematous lung. Make the slopes right, since the slope is compliance. Mark on each curve which is hard to inflate and which is hard to empty. One line: say which patient works harder breathing in and which works harder breathing out, and point at the slope that told you."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two balloons side by side to represent the two failures. One is stiff walled and takes real effort to inflate but snaps shut on its own. One is stretched out and floppy, easy to inflate and slow to empty. Label which lung disease each represents and what happened to the elastic fibers in the wall. Draw the breathing pattern each patient adopts. One line: say which one traps air and roughly where that air sits at the end of a breath."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define lung compliance and elastic recoil and predict the effect of fibrosis and of emphysema on each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Compliance and elastic recoil. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-surfactant","name":"Surfactant and surface tension","can":"Explain how surfactant lowers surface tension and why its absence causes alveolar collapse in the premature newborn.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Two alveoli have the same surface tension, but one has half the radius of the other. How do their collapsing pressures compare?\nA) The smaller alveolus has half the collapsing pressure\nB) The smaller alveolus has twice the collapsing pressure\nC) The smaller alveolus has four times the collapsing pressure\nD) The two have equal collapsing pressures\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The smaller alveolus has twice the collapsing pressure","why":"Laplace's law puts radius in the denominator, so halving the radius doubles the pressure when surface tension is held constant. Halving the pressure would require the radius to double instead. A fourfold change would happen only if pressure varied with the square of the radius, which it does not, and equal pressures would require equal radii."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to the work of breathing when surfactant is absent?\nA) It increases, because much more pressure is needed to inflate each alveolus\nB) It decreases, because the alveoli are already partly collapsed\nC) It is unchanged, because tidal volume is set by the respiratory centers\nD) It decreases, because surface tension helps pull air inward\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It increases, because much more pressure is needed to inflate each alveolus","why":"With surface tension unopposed, every breath must generate a large transpulmonary pressure just to reopen collapsed alveoli, so muscular work rises sharply and the infant tires. Partly collapsed alveoli make breathing harder, not easier, because they must be reinflated from nothing each time. Work depends on the mechanics of the lung, not only on the neural drive, and surface tension always opposes inflation."},{"t":"mc","q":"A premature infant receives exogenous surfactant into the airway. Predict the effect on surface tension and compliance.\nA) Surface tension rises and compliance falls\nB) Surface tension falls and compliance falls\nC) Surface tension falls and compliance rises\nD) Surface tension rises and compliance rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Surface tension falls and compliance rises","why":"Replacing the missing phospholipid restores the low tension film, so the lung takes in more volume for the same pressure and becomes easier to inflate. Rising surface tension is what the treatment is designed to prevent. Falling tension cannot lower compliance, since tension is one of the forces opposing inflation, and tension and compliance move in opposite directions."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to alveoli at the end of expiration when surfactant is missing?\nA) They stay open but become overinflated\nB) Many collapse completely and must be reopened on the next breath\nC) They fill with surfactant from neighboring alveoli\nD) They remain exactly at their end inspiratory volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Many collapse completely and must be reopened on the next breath","why":"End expiration is when alveolar radius is smallest, and Laplace's law makes the collapsing pressure highest at exactly that moment, so without surfactant the alveoli close and the next breath must generate a very high opening pressure. Overinflation is the opposite of what high surface tension causes. Surfactant does not migrate between alveoli to rescue them, and volume necessarily falls during expiration."},{"t":"mc","q":"An alveolus with a radius of 100 micrometers shrinks to a radius of 25 micrometers while surface tension stays constant. By what factor does the collapsing pressure change?\nA) It decreases fourfold  |  B) It increases twofold\nC) It increases sixteenfold  |  D) It increases fourfold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It increases fourfold","why":"Pressure varies as one over the radius, so cutting the radius to one quarter multiplies the pressure by four. A decrease would require the alveolus to enlarge instead. A twofold rise corresponds to halving the radius, and a sixteenfold change would need pressure to depend on radius to the fourth power, which is the rule for airway resistance rather than for surface tension."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how surfactant keeps alveoli from collapsing, and then explain why a baby born at 28 weeks has stiff lungs and labored breathing.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"water lining the alveolus creates surface tension that pulls the alveolus inward; surfactant from type II cells sits between water molecules and lowers surface tension; by Laplace's law smaller alveoli have higher collapsing pressure, and surfactant lowers tension most in small alveoli, equalizing pressures; lower surface tension lowers recoil and raises compliance; surfactant production is not adequate until about 34 weeks of gestation; without it alveoli collapse at end expiration, compliance falls, and each breath needs much more pressure; the result is high work of breathing and low arterial PO2","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two alveoli of different sizes sharing one duct, first with the same surface tension in both. Show where the air goes and what happens to the small one. Then draw the same pair with surfactant, and show it concentrated more in the small alveolus so its tension falls further. Draw the outcome now. Beside it, draw a premature newborn's alveoli without surfactant. One line: say why the premature infant has to generate so much more pressure for every single breath."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Explain how surfactant lowers surface tension and why its absence causes alveolar collapse in the premature newborn. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how surfactant lowers surface tension and why its absence causes alveolar collapse in the premature newborn."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Surfactant and surface tension. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-airway-resistance","name":"Airway resistance","can":"Identify the main determinant of airway resistance and predict the effect of bronchoconstriction, mucus, and sympathetic stimulation on airflow.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An airway radius is reduced to half its original value while the patient doubles the driving pressure. What happens to airflow compared with the original?\nA) It rises to twice the original\nB) It falls to one half of the original\nC) It falls to one eighth of the original\nD) It stays the same, since the two effects cancel\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It falls to one eighth of the original","why":"Halving the radius raises resistance sixteenfold, and flow equals pressure over resistance, so doubling the pressure gives a flow of two divided by sixteen, which is one eighth. Flow cannot rise when resistance climbs sixteen times faster than the driving pressure. A halving would occur only if resistance had merely quadrupled, and cancellation would require resistance to change by only a factor of two."},{"t":"mc","q":"An airway narrows so that its radius is halved. By what factor does its resistance change?\nA) It increases twofold  |  B) It increases fourfold\nC) It increases eightfold  |  D) It increases sixteenfold\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It increases sixteenfold","why":"Resistance varies as one over the radius to the fourth power, and two raised to the fourth is sixteen, so halving the radius makes the airway sixteen times harder to move air through. A twofold rise assumes a simple inverse relationship. Fourfold assumes a square relationship, and eightfold assumes a cube relationship."},{"t":"mc","q":"During an asthma attack the driving pressure stays the same while airway smooth muscle contracts. What happens to airflow?\nA) It rises, because resistance rises\nB) It falls, because resistance rises\nC) It is unchanged, because the driving pressure did not change\nD) It falls, because lung compliance falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It falls, because resistance rises","why":"Flow equals pressure divided by resistance, so with the numerator fixed and the denominator climbing steeply, airflow drops, which is why an asthmatic must work harder to move the same volume. Rising resistance can never raise flow. Flow depends on both terms rather than on driving pressure alone, and asthma is an airway caliber problem rather than a compliance problem."},{"t":"mc","q":"Thick mucus partly fills the lumen of a bronchus. How does this raise resistance?\nA) It reduces the effective radius available for airflow\nB) It increases the length of the airway\nC) It lowers the compliance of the surrounding alveoli\nD) It raises the viscosity of the inspired air\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It reduces the effective radius available for airflow","why":"Anything occupying the lumen leaves a narrower channel for air, and because resistance depends on radius to the fourth power even a modest layer of mucus has a large effect. Airway length is set by anatomy and is unchanged by secretions. Alveolar compliance is a tissue property, and mucus does not mix into the air to change its viscosity."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: During an asthma attack a patient's bronchioles have an average radius of 1.0 mm. After an inhaled beta 2 agonist the average radius increases to 1.2 mm, a 20 percent increase. Airway length and gas viscosity do not change.\nPredict the change in airway resistance produced by the drug.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Resistance falls to about half its original value","why":"Resistance is proportional to 1 over radius to the fourth power. Raising radius by a factor of 1.2 raises radius to the fourth by 1.2 times 1.2 times 1.2 times 1.2, about 2.07. Resistance therefore falls to about 1 over 2.07, roughly half. This is why a modest change in bronchiolar diameter has such a large effect on airflow, in both directions."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Sympathetic stimulation constricts bronchiolar smooth muscle and raises airway resistance.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Sympathetic stimulation, through epinephrine acting on beta 2 receptors, relaxes bronchiolar smooth muscle, widens the airways, and lowers resistance.","why":"Bronchioles carry no cartilage, so their smooth muscle sets their radius. Epinephrine and beta 2 agonist drugs relax that muscle; parasympathetic acetylcholine and histamine contract it. Because resistance depends on the fourth power of radius, even a small change in bronchiolar diameter makes a large change in airflow. This is why albuterol opens an asthmatic airway."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two airways in cross section, one normal and one with the radius halved, and write what that does to resistance given that resistance changes with the fourth power of the radius. Then draw a row of four small airway cross sections: normal, bronchoconstricted, plugged with mucus and swollen, and under sympathetic stimulation. Draw the airflow arrow through each, sized to match. One line: say which of the four you can reverse with an inhaler, and name what the drug is imitating."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an asthma attack as a sequence down the page: trigger, airway smooth muscle contracting, wall swelling, mucus in the lumen, radius falling, resistance rising, airflow falling. Draw the airway cross section changing at each step. Beside it, draw a forced expiration trace for that patient against a normal one on the same axes. One line: say why expiration is harder than inspiration for this patient, and what happens to airway radius as the chest squeezes down."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the main determinant of airway resistance and predict the effect of bronchoconstriction, mucus, and sympathetic stimulation on airflow."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Airway resistance. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-lung-volumes","name":"Lung volumes and capacities","can":"Define tidal volume, inspiratory and expiratory reserve volume, residual volume, vital capacity, and total lung capacity and read each from a spirogram.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student has a tidal volume of 500 mL, an inspiratory reserve volume of 3000 mL, and an expiratory reserve volume of 1200 mL. What is the vital capacity?\nA) 4700 mL  |  B) 4200 mL\nC) 3500 mL  |  D) 5900 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 4700 mL","why":"Vital capacity is 500 plus 3000 plus 1200, which is 4700 mL. Leaving out the tidal volume gives 4200. Leaving out the expiratory reserve gives 3500, and 5900 would be total lung capacity once a residual volume of 1200 mL is added."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a vital capacity of 4700 mL and a residual volume of 1200 mL. What is the total lung capacity?\nA) 3500 mL  |  B) 5900 mL\nC) 4700 mL  |  D) 6100 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 5900 mL","why":"Total lung capacity is vital capacity plus residual volume, so 4700 plus 1200 gives 5900 mL. Subtracting instead of adding gives 3500. Reporting 4700 ignores the residual volume entirely, and 6100 does not follow from these two numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an expiratory reserve volume of 1200 mL and a residual volume of 1300 mL. What is the functional residual capacity?\nA) 1300 mL  |  B) 100 mL\nC) 2500 mL  |  D) 3000 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 2500 mL","why":"Functional residual capacity is the air still in the lung at the end of a quiet expiration, which is the expiratory reserve plus the residual volume, so 1200 plus 1300 gives 2500 mL. Reporting 1300 counts only the residual volume and leaves out the reserve that can still be forced out. Subtracting gives 100, which would leave the lung nearly empty between breaths, and 3000 does not match either number."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person has a tidal volume of 500 mL and an inspiratory reserve volume of 3100 mL. What is the inspiratory capacity?\nA) 2600 mL  |  B) 3100 mL\nC) 500 mL  |  D) 3600 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 3600 mL","why":"Inspiratory capacity is the most that can be inhaled starting from the end of a quiet expiration, so it is the tidal volume plus the inspiratory reserve, giving 3600 mL. Subtracting gives 2600. Reporting either component alone leaves out the other half of the maneuver."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a total lung capacity of 6000 mL and a vital capacity of 4500 mL. What is the residual volume?\nA) 1500 mL  |  B) 10500 mL\nC) 4500 mL  |  D) 3000 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 1500 mL","why":"Residual volume is the part of total lung capacity that cannot be exhaled, so it is 6000 minus 4500, which is 1500 mL. Adding the two gives 10500, far beyond any human lung. The value 4500 is the vital capacity itself, and 3000 does not follow from the subtraction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a spirogram trace across the page: several quiet tidal breaths, then a maximal inspiration, then a maximal expiration, then back to quiet breathing. Bracket and label tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume, expiratory reserve volume, residual volume, vital capacity and total lung capacity, and write an approximate value beside each. Draw residual volume as a band the trace never enters. One line: say which one of these a spirometer can never measure, and say why not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a single tall column representing total lung capacity, divided into four stacked volumes in true proportion, labeled and valued. Beside it, draw the same column for a patient with severe fibrosis and a third for a patient with severe emphysema, keeping the same scale so the heights differ. Mark residual volume in all three. One line: say which patient has a larger total lung capacity than normal and why that is not good news."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define tidal volume, inspiratory and expiratory reserve volume, residual volume, vital capacity, and total lung capacity and read each from a spirogram."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Lung volumes and capacities. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-spirometry-patterns","name":"Obstructive and restrictive patterns","can":"Use the ratio of forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity to classify a spirometry result as obstructive or restrictive.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a forced expiratory volume in one second of 1.8 L and a forced vital capacity of 3.0 L. Calculate the ratio and classify the pattern.\nA) 0.60, a restrictive pattern  |  B) 1.67, an obstructive pattern\nC) 0.60, an obstructive pattern  |  D) 0.60, a normal result\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 0.60, an obstructive pattern","why":"Dividing 1.8 by 3.0 gives 0.60, well under the 0.70 threshold, so airflow is limited and the pattern is obstructive. Restriction would show a preserved or raised ratio. The value 1.67 comes from inverting the fraction, and 0.60 is clearly below the normal range rather than within it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Predict the spirometry ratio in a patient with advanced emphysema.\nA) The ratio is raised, because elastic recoil is increased\nB) The ratio is normal, because emphysema affects only gas exchange\nC) The ratio is reduced, because airflow is limited more than lung volume\nD) The ratio is reduced, because total lung capacity is decreased\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The ratio is reduced, because airflow is limited more than lung volume","why":"Emphysema destroys the elastic tissue that tethers small airways open, so they collapse during forced expiration and the first second delivers much less air than it should, dropping the ratio. Elastic recoil in emphysema is reduced, not increased. The disease clearly affects mechanics as well as exchange, and total lung capacity actually rises because of air trapping."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does forced vital capacity measure?\nA) The volume exhaled in the first second of a forced expiration\nB) The total volume exhaled as forcefully and completely as possible after a maximal inspiration\nC) The volume remaining in the lung after a forced expiration\nD) The volume of air exhaled per minute at rest\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The total volume exhaled as forcefully and completely as possible after a maximal inspiration","why":"Forced vital capacity is the whole amount a person can move out in one maximal effort, so it reflects how much lung is available rather than how quickly it empties. The first second alone is forced expiratory volume in one second. Air left behind is residual volume, and air moved per minute is minute ventilation."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate normal value for the ratio of forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity in a healthy young adult?\nA) About 0.40  |  B) About 0.60\nC) About 0.80  |  D) About 1.00\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 0.80","why":"A healthy adult blows out roughly 80 percent of the whole forced vital capacity in the first second, which is why values below about 0.70 are treated as abnormal. Ratios of 0.40 and 0.60 indicate obstruction. A ratio of 1.00 would mean the entire capacity left the lung within one second, which does not occur in normal lungs."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 55 year old patient has a forced expiratory volume in one second of 3.0 L and a forced vital capacity of 3.4 L, giving a ratio of 0.88 (normal about 0.75 to 0.80). Her predicted forced vital capacity is 5.0 L.\nWhich change in lung mechanics best explains this pair of results?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The lung cannot be expanded to normal size, so volumes are small but air leaves fast","why":"Her forced vital capacity is only 68 percent of predicted, so volume is clearly reduced. Yet 88 percent of it comes out in the first second, which is faster than normal. Small volume with fast emptying is the restrictive pattern: a stiff lung or weak chest wall that cannot be filled, with strong recoil that empties it quickly. Obstruction would show a low ratio."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A restrictive lung disease such as fibrosis lowers the ratio of forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity below 0.70.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Restriction leaves the ratio normal or high, because both the one second volume and the total volume shrink together and the stiff lung actually empties quickly; a low ratio marks obstruction.","why":"The ratio asks how fast air leaves, not how much. In fibrosis the lung is small but its strong recoil pushes air out quickly, so a large fraction of the reduced vital capacity comes out in the first second and the ratio is often above 0.80. In obstruction the airways are narrow, air leaves slowly, and the first second captures a smaller fraction, giving a ratio below 0.70. To spot restriction you look at the total volumes, not the ratio."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time in seconds on the horizontal, volume expired on the vertical, and plot three forced expiration curves from a full breath: normal, obstructive, restrictive. Mark the one second point on all three and the final forced vital capacity on all three. Write the ratio underneath each, with the normal ratio near 0.8 and the obstructive under 0.7. One line: say which pattern has a low volume and a normal ratio, and what that ratio is telling you about the airways."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a table on the page as a grid you sketch yourself, with obstructive down one side and restrictive down the other, and three columns: what happened to the lung, what happens to forced vital capacity, what happens to the ratio. Fill each cell with a small drawing rather than only words, an airway for one row and a stiff lung wall for the other. Name one real disease in each row. One line: say which measurement alone would mislead you, and what second number you would demand."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to use the ratio of forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity to classify a spirometry result as obstructive or restrictive."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Obstructive and restrictive patterns. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-dead-space","name":"Alveolar ventilation and dead space","can":"Calculate minute ventilation and alveolar ventilation and explain why slow deep breathing ventilates the alveoli better than rapid shallow breathing.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A person breathes 500 mL fourteen times per minute. What is the minute ventilation?\nA) 514 mL/min  |  B) 4900 mL/min\nC) 6000 mL/min  |  D) 7000 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 7000 mL/min","why":"Minute ventilation is 500 times 14, which is 7000 mL/min or 7 L/min. Adding the two numbers gives 514, which is not a rate. The value 4900 would be the alveolar ventilation once a 150 mL dead space is subtracted, and 6000 corresponds to twelve breaths rather than fourteen."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person breathes 500 mL twelve times per minute with a dead space of 150 mL. What is the alveolar ventilation?\nA) 4200 mL/min  |  B) 6000 mL/min\nC) 1800 mL/min  |  D) 5850 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 4200 mL/min","why":"Each breath delivers 500 minus 150, or 350 mL, and twelve of those give 4200 mL/min. The value 6000 is the minute ventilation, which ignores dead space. The value 1800 is the dead space ventilation, and 5850 comes from subtracting the dead space only once instead of from every breath."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient breathes 400 mL fifteen times per minute with a dead space of 150 mL. What is the alveolar ventilation?\nA) 6000 mL/min  |  B) 3750 mL/min\nC) 2250 mL/min  |  D) 5850 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 3750 mL/min","why":"Fresh air per breath is 400 minus 150, or 250 mL, and multiplying by 15 gives 3750 mL/min. The value 6000 is the minute ventilation before dead space is removed. The value 2250 is dead space ventilation, and 5850 subtracts the dead space only once from the whole minute."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a minute ventilation of 6 L/min at a rate of 12 breaths per minute. What is the tidal volume?\nA) 72 mL  |  B) 350 mL\nC) 500 mL  |  D) 720 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 500 mL","why":"Rearranging minute ventilation as tidal volume times frequency gives 6000 divided by 12, which is 500 mL. The value 72 comes from dividing the wrong way. The value 350 is the fresh air per breath after a 150 mL dead space is subtracted, not the tidal volume itself."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient on a ventilator has an anatomic dead space of 150 mL. Setting A delivers 400 mL breaths at 15 per minute. Setting B delivers 600 mL breaths at 10 per minute. Both settings give a minute ventilation of 6000 mL per minute.\nWhich setting will produce the lower arterial PCO2, and why?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Setting B, because alveolar ventilation is 4500 mL per minute versus 3750 for setting A","why":"Carbon dioxide removal depends on alveolar ventilation, not minute ventilation. Setting A: (400 minus 150) times 15 gives 3750 mL per minute. Setting B: (600 minus 150) times 10 gives 4500 mL per minute. The larger breath pays the 150 mL dead space toll fewer times, so more fresh gas reaches the alveoli each minute and arterial PCO2 falls lower."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Two people with the same minute ventilation always have the same alveolar ventilation.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Alveolar ventilation is (tidal volume minus dead space) times rate, so at the same minute ventilation the person taking slower, deeper breaths delivers more fresh air to the alveoli.","why":"Every breath wastes about 150 mL in the conducting airways. That cost is paid per breath, not per liter. Twelve breaths of 500 mL and twenty four breaths of 250 mL both move 6 L per minute, but the first delivers 350 times 12, or 4200 mL per minute, to the alveoli, and the second delivers 100 times 24, or 2400 mL per minute. Minute ventilation counts all the air moved; alveolar ventilation counts only what reaches the exchange surface."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the airway with the conducting zone shaded as dead space, about 150 mL, and the alveoli beyond it. Then work three cases across the page, each with the same minute ventilation of 6000 mL per minute: 500 mL twelve times, 250 mL twenty four times, and 1000 mL six times. For each, draw the tidal volume as a bar split into dead space and fresh alveolar air, then write the alveolar ventilation. One line: say which case ventilates the alveoli worst and name the part of the bar that did not change."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one tidal breath as a moving column of air in three frames: end of expiration with dead space full of stale air, inspiration with that stale air pushed into the alveoli first and fresh air behind it, and end of inspiration. Show where the fresh air actually ended up. Then draw the same three frames for a very shallow breath. One line: say what a panicking patient breathing fast and shallow is doing to their alveolar ventilation, and to their arterial carbon dioxide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate minute ventilation and alveolar ventilation and explain why slow deep breathing ventilates the alveoli better than rapid shallow breathing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Alveolar ventilation and dead space. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-lab-spirometry","name":"Spirometry","can":"Record a spirogram, calculate the lung volumes and capacities available from it, and compare the results with predicted values.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student measures a vital capacity of 3.6 L against a predicted value of 4.5 L. What is the percent predicted?\nA) 80 percent  |  B) 125 percent\nC) 90 percent  |  D) 20 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 80 percent","why":"Dividing 3.6 by 4.5 gives 0.80, so the student reached 80 percent of the predicted value. Inverting the fraction gives 125 percent and would suggest a larger than expected lung. The value 20 percent is the shortfall rather than the result, and 90 percent does not follow from these numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student measures a forced expiratory volume in one second of 2.0 L against a predicted 3.2 L. What is the percent predicted?\nA) 160 percent  |  B) 62.5 percent\nC) 37.5 percent  |  D) 64 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 62.5 percent","why":"Dividing 2.0 by 3.2 gives 0.625, so the measured value is 62.5 percent of predicted. The value 160 percent comes from inverting the fraction. The value 37.5 percent is the shortfall from predicted rather than the percent achieved, and 64 percent does not match the division."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a recorded spirogram, from which point does the expiratory reserve volume excursion begin?\nA) From the peak of a maximal inspiration\nB) From the lowest point of a maximal expiration\nC) From the peak of a quiet inspiration\nD) From the resting level at the end of a quiet expiration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) From the resting level at the end of a quiet expiration","why":"Expiratory reserve volume is the extra air that can be forced out after an ordinary breath has already been let go, so the trace must start at the resting end expiratory level and go down from there. Starting at maximal inspiration would trace the vital capacity instead. The lowest point of the trace is the end of that excursion, and starting from a quiet inspiration would include the tidal volume."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student stops exhaling early during the vital capacity maneuver. What is the effect on the recorded value?\nA) Vital capacity is underestimated\nB) Vital capacity is overestimated\nC) Vital capacity is unaffected because the maneuver is standardized\nD) Vital capacity is unaffected but residual volume appears larger\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Vital capacity is underestimated","why":"The instrument records only the air that actually passed the mouthpiece, so stopping short leaves part of the expiratory reserve volume in the lung and the number comes out too small. It cannot be overestimated, since no extra air was moved. Standardizing the instructions does not guarantee full effort, and residual volume is not recorded by a spirometer in the first place."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does a spirometer record?\nA) The volume of air moved into and out of the lungs over time\nB) The pressure inside the pleural cavity during each breath\nC) The percentage of oxygen in the exhaled air\nD) The rate of blood flow through the pulmonary capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The volume of air moved into and out of the lungs over time","why":"A spirometer measures moving volume against time, which is why every value it reports is either a volume or a flow derived from one. Pleural pressure requires an esophageal balloon or a direct measurement, not a mouthpiece. Exhaled gas fractions come from a gas analyzer, and pulmonary blood flow is not measured by this instrument at all."},{"t":"bd","q":"A student's spirogram shows a tidal volume of 450 mL, an inspiratory reserve volume of 2500 mL, and an expiratory reserve volume of 1100 mL. The predicted vital capacity is 4500 mL. Calculate vital capacity, inspiratory capacity, and percent of predicted, interpret the result, and state which volumes cannot be obtained from this trace and why.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"vital capacity is 450 plus 2500 plus 1100, which is 4050 mL; inspiratory capacity is 450 plus 2500, which is 2950 mL; percent predicted is 4050 divided by 4500, about 90 percent; 90 percent is within the normal range (above about 80 percent); residual volume cannot be read because that air never leaves the lung and the spirometer only measures air that moves; functional residual capacity and total lung capacity therefore cannot be calculated from the spirogram alone","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the spirogram you would produce yourself, quiet breathing then a maximal inspiration and a maximal forced expiration, with volume on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal. Read off and bracket every volume and capacity the trace actually gives you, and shade the region it cannot give you. Beside the trace, draw a second axis for predicted values and show yours as a percentage of predicted. One line: say what a result at 85 percent of predicted means, and whether that is a diagnosis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two traces on one set of axes from the same person: one recorded sitting upright and one recorded lying flat, or one before and one after hard exercise, your choice. Label which volumes changed and which did not. Beside them draw the anatomical reason for the change, the diaphragm and the abdominal contents in each position. One line: say which volume moved most and whether the change was in the lung itself or in what surrounds it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Spirometry. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Gas Exchange and Transport","comps":[{"id":"w12-partial-pressures","name":"Partial pressures","can":"Apply Dalton's law to calculate the partial pressure of oxygen in inspired and alveolar air and state the normal partial pressures in alveolar air, arterial blood, and venous blood.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A sedated patient hypoventilates until alveolar PCO2 reaches 60 mmHg while breathing room air at sea level. Using a respiratory exchange ratio of 0.8, what is the approximate alveolar PO2?\nA) About 40 mmHg  |  B) About 75 mmHg\nC) About 90 mmHg  |  D) About 110 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 75 mmHg","why":"Humidified inspired PO2 is about 150 mmHg, and the alveolar gas equation subtracts PCO2 divided by 0.8, so 150 minus 75 gives roughly 75 mmHg. About 90 mmHg would follow if carbon dioxide were subtracted one for one, which understates its dilution effect. About 110 mmHg is higher than normal alveolar PO2 and cannot occur when carbon dioxide is accumulating, and about 40 mmHg would be a near lethal alveolar value not produced by this degree of hypoventilation."},{"t":"mc","q":"A climber at a barometric pressure of 380 mmHg hyperventilates until alveolar PCO2 falls to 20 mmHg. Using a respiratory exchange ratio of 0.8, what is her approximate alveolar PO2?\nA) About 80 mmHg  |  B) About 45 mmHg\nC) About 70 mmHg  |  D) About 25 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 45 mmHg","why":"Humidified inspired PO2 at this pressure is (380 minus 47) times 0.21, about 70 mmHg, and subtracting 20 divided by 0.8 leaves roughly 45 mmHg. About 70 mmHg is the inspired value before any carbon dioxide dilution. About 25 mmHg subtracts the inspired value from the carbon dioxide term in the wrong direction, and about 80 mmHg ignores humidification and the carbon dioxide correction together."},{"t":"mc","q":"Alveolar air at sea level contains oxygen at 104 mmHg, carbon dioxide at 40 mmHg, and water vapor at 47 mmHg. What partial pressure does nitrogen exert?\nA) About 569 mmHg  |  B) About 616 mmHg\nC) About 713 mmHg  |  D) About 600 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 569 mmHg","why":"The partial pressures of all gases present must sum to the total pressure, so nitrogen is 760 minus the sum of 104, 40, and 47, which is about 569 mmHg. About 616 mmHg leaves out the water vapor term. About 713 mmHg is the total dry gas pressure, which still includes oxygen and carbon dioxide, and about 600 mmHg does not follow from these numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Dry room air at sea level is 21 percent oxygen and the barometric pressure is 760 mmHg. What is the PO2 of that air?\nA) 104 mmHg  |  B) 100 mmHg\nC) 160 mmHg  |  D) 150 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 160 mmHg","why":"Dalton's law gives 0.21 times 760, which is about 160 mmHg for dry air that has not yet been humidified. 150 mmHg is the answer after subtracting the 47 mmHg of water vapor added in the airways. 104 mmHg is alveolar PO2 after carbon dioxide dilution and oxygen uptake, and 100 mmHg is arterial PO2."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient at sea level (barometric pressure 760 mmHg) breathes 40 percent oxygen through a mask. Water vapor pressure at body temperature, 37 C (98.6 F), is 47 mmHg. Her arterial PCO2 is 40 mmHg (normal 35 to 45), and her respiratory exchange ratio is 0.8.\nWhat is her approximate alveolar PO2?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 235 mmHg","why":"First humidify the air: 760 minus 47 is 713 mmHg. Then apply Dalton's law: 713 times 0.40 is about 285 mmHg for inspired PO2. In the alveolus, carbon dioxide takes up space, so subtract PCO2 divided by the exchange ratio: 40 divided by 0.8 is 50. That leaves about 235 mmHg. Raising inspired oxygen raises alveolar PO2 a great deal, which is why oxygen therapy works so well for diffusion problems."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Alveolar PO2 is about 150 mmHg, the same as humidified inspired air, because air flows straight into the alveoli.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Alveolar PO2 is about 100 mmHg, lower than the inspired 150 mmHg, because oxygen keeps leaving the alveolus into the blood and carbon dioxide keeps entering, and each breath only partly refreshes the large volume already there.","why":"Humidified room air at sea level has a PO2 of (760 minus 47) times 0.21, about 150 mmHg. But a tidal breath of 500 mL, minus 150 mL of dead space, mixes with about 2400 mL of stale gas already in the lung, and the alveolus is constantly trading oxygen for carbon dioxide with the capillary blood. The steady state that results is roughly 100 mmHg oxygen and 40 mmHg carbon dioxide. Hyperventilation pushes alveolar PO2 up toward 150 but never past it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a vertical bar for atmospheric air at sea level, total 760 mmHg, split into slices for nitrogen, oxygen and the trace gases in true proportion, with the oxygen slice labeled about 160 mmHg. Beside it draw the bar for air in the alveoli, now with water vapor taking about 47 mmHg and carbon dioxide taking a real slice, and the oxygen slice down near 100 mmHg. One line: say what happened between the two bars to knock oxygen down by that much, and name both causes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a circuit: alveolus, pulmonary capillary, systemic artery, tissue capillary, systemic vein, back to the alveolus. Write the partial pressure of oxygen and of carbon dioxide at each station, using the standard values, 100 and 40 in alveolar air and arterial blood, 40 and 46 in venous blood. Draw the gradient arrows at the two exchange sites, sized to the gradient. One line: say which gas has the smaller gradient at the lung and why it still crosses fast enough."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to apply Dalton's law to calculate the partial pressure of oxygen in inspired and alveolar air and state the normal partial pressures in alveolar air, arterial blood, and venous blood."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Partial pressures. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-gas-diffusion","name":"Diffusion at the respiratory membrane","can":"Apply the determinants of diffusion to the respiratory membrane and predict the effect of edema, fibrosis, and emphysema on gas transfer.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In pulmonary fibrosis, collagen is deposited in the alveolar walls. What is the immediate effect on gas transfer?\nA) Transfer rises because the stiffer wall holds alveoli open\nB) Transfer is unchanged because the gradient is unchanged\nC) Transfer falls because the diffusion distance is longer\nD) Transfer falls because the alveolar PO2 has dropped\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Transfer falls because the diffusion distance is longer","why":"Scarring thickens the barrier between alveolar gas and capillary blood, and a longer path means fewer molecules cross per second. Alveolar PO2 is determined by ventilation and inspired gas, so it is not lowered by the scarring itself. Stiffer walls do resist collapse, but they also resist inflation and do nothing to speed diffusion, and an unchanged gradient does not rescue transfer when the distance term has grown."},{"t":"mc","q":"A disease process doubles the thickness of the respiratory membrane with no other change. What happens to the rate of oxygen diffusion?\nA) It falls to about one quarter  |  B) It falls by about 20 percent\nC) It doubles  |  D) It falls to about half\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It falls to about half","why":"Rate is inversely proportional to thickness, so doubling the distance halves the transfer rate for a given gradient. A fall to one quarter would require the rate to vary with the square of thickness, which it does not. A 20 percent fall understates the effect of doubling, and the rate cannot rise when the barrier has grown."},{"t":"mc","q":"What supplies the driving force for a gas to diffuse across the respiratory membrane?\nA) The hydrostatic pressure difference across the capillary wall\nB) The partial pressure difference for that gas across the membrane\nC) The total barometric pressure on the two sides of the membrane\nD) The concentration of hemoglobin in the pulmonary capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The partial pressure difference for that gas across the membrane","why":"Gases diffuse down their own partial pressure gradients, so only the difference in that gas between alveolus and blood drives net transfer. Total barometric pressure is the same on both sides and creates no gradient for any single gas. Hemoglobin keeps the gradient from collapsing by removing dissolved oxygen, but it is not the driving force itself, and hydrostatic pressure drives bulk fluid movement, which is filtration rather than diffusion."},{"t":"mc","q":"If the surface area available for gas exchange doubles and nothing else changes, what happens to the rate of diffusion?\nA) It is unchanged  |  B) It falls to one quarter\nC) It doubles  |  D) It halves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It doubles","why":"Diffusion rate is directly proportional to surface area, so doubling the area doubles the number of molecules crossing per unit time. Halving would be the effect of doubling membrane thickness, since thickness is in the denominator. Nothing about area leaves the rate unchanged, and a fall to one quarter does not match any single change in the diffusion relationship."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with heart failure has fluid accumulating in the alveolar walls, roughly doubling the thickness of the respiratory membrane. Arterial blood gas: PO2 58 mmHg (normal 80 to 100), PCO2 36 mmHg (normal 35 to 45). Respiratory rate is 26 per minute (normal 12 to 20).\nWhy is oxygen transfer failing while carbon dioxide transfer is not?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Carbon dioxide is about 20 times more soluble in the membrane fluid, so it still crosses the thicker barrier fast enough, and the extra ventilation keeps PCO2 down","why":"Doubling thickness halves the diffusion rate of both gases. Oxygen has little reserve, so its transfer falls behind and arterial PO2 drops. Carbon dioxide's high solubility gives it about a twenty fold advantage, so even at half speed it equilibrates. Meanwhile the low PO2 stimulates breathing, and the extra alveolar ventilation lowers PCO2 slightly below normal. Hypoxemia with normal or low PCO2 is the signature of a diffusion problem."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Pulmonary edema lowers oxygen transfer across the respiratory membrane more than it lowers carbon dioxide transfer.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"Diffusion rate depends on surface area, thickness, the partial pressure gradient, and solubility. Edema fluid thickens the barrier for both gases equally, but carbon dioxide's much higher solubility means its diffusion has a large reserve. In practice a patient with pulmonary edema has a low arterial PO2 while PCO2 is normal or even low, because hypoxia drives extra ventilation. Carbon dioxide retention appears only when the lung is very badly damaged."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one pulmonary capillary as a long channel with a red cell traveling along it, and plot the partial pressure of oxygen in that red cell against distance traveled, from 40 mmHg at the arterial end up to 100 mmHg. Show it reaching equilibrium well before the end of the capillary. Then draw the same plot for a thickened membrane, and again for that patient during exercise when transit time is shorter. One line: say why the damaged lung can look normal at rest and fail during exercise."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Apply the determinants of diffusion to the respiratory membrane and predict the effect of edema, fibrosis, and emphysema on gas transfer. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to apply the determinants of diffusion to the respiratory membrane and predict the effect of edema, fibrosis, and emphysema on gas transfer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Diffusion at the respiratory membrane. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-va-q-matching","name":"Ventilation perfusion matching","can":"Explain how local hypoxic vasoconstriction and bronchiolar responses match ventilation to perfusion and predict the consequence of a mismatch.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"What is the normal overall ventilation perfusion ratio for the whole lung?\nA) About 3.0  |  B) About 0.8\nC) About 1.5  |  D) About 0.2\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 0.8","why":"Alveolar ventilation of roughly 4 L/min divided by pulmonary blood flow of roughly 5 L/min gives about 0.8 for the lung as a whole. About 1.5 is higher than any whole lung average and describes an overventilated region. About 0.2 describes a poorly ventilated region close to shunt, and about 3.0 is the ratio at the apex of an upright lung, not the average."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the ventilation perfusion ratio, what does the ventilation term actually refer to?\nA) Alveolar ventilation, the fresh gas reaching exchange surfaces\nB) Total minute ventilation, including gas in the conducting airways\nC) Tidal volume measured at the mouth\nD) The respiratory rate in breaths per minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Alveolar ventilation, the fresh gas reaching exchange surfaces","why":"Only gas that reaches perfused alveoli can participate in exchange, so the ratio uses alveolar ventilation, which is minute ventilation minus the dead space contribution. Total minute ventilation includes air that never reaches an alveolus and would overstate the ratio. Tidal volume and respiratory rate are the two components of minute ventilation, and neither is the ventilation term on its own."},{"t":"mc","q":"How do pulmonary arterioles respond when the alveolar PO2 in their region falls?\nA) They constrict, but only when arterial PCO2 also rises\nB) They do not respond, because the response is confined to bronchioles\nC) They constrict, diverting blood toward better ventilated regions\nD) They dilate, increasing blood flow to the hypoxic region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They constrict, diverting blood toward better ventilated regions","why":"Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction moves blood away from an alveolus that has little oxygen to offer and toward alveoli that are well ventilated, which protects arterial PO2. Dilating in response to hypoxia is the systemic pattern and would send more blood past useless alveoli. The response works on alveolar PO2 directly and does not require a change in PCO2, and bronchioles respond as well but the vascular response is real and central."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does the pulmonary vascular response to low PO2 compare with the systemic vascular response?\nA) It is opposite: pulmonary vessels dilate while systemic vessels constrict\nB) It is opposite: pulmonary vessels constrict while systemic vessels dilate\nC) It is the same: both constrict to conserve oxygen\nD) It is the same: both dilate to restore flow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is opposite: pulmonary vessels constrict while systemic vessels dilate","why":"A hypoxic systemic tissue needs more blood, so its arterioles dilate, while a hypoxic alveolus has no oxygen to give, so its arterioles constrict and send blood elsewhere. Both constricting would starve hypoxic tissues throughout the body. Both dilating would waste pulmonary blood flow on unventilated alveoli, and the reversed version has the pulmonary and systemic behaviors swapped."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A mucus plug completely blocks the bronchus to the right lower lobe of an otherwise healthy patient. Blood flow to that lobe continues at first. The lobe normally receives about 20 percent of pulmonary blood flow.\nPredict the local vascular response in that lobe over the next several minutes and the effect on arterial PO2.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Arterioles in the lobe constrict in response to low alveolar PO2, diverting blood to ventilated lobes, so arterial PO2 falls only modestly","why":"Once ventilation stops, the alveolar gas in the lobe equilibrates with venous blood and its PO2 falls. Pulmonary arterioles respond to low local PO2 by constricting, which is the opposite of systemic vessels. Blood is redirected to lobes that are still ventilated, so the shunt fraction stays small and arterial PO2 is protected. The match between ventilation and perfusion is restored by moving the blood, since the air cannot be moved."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"When a region of lung is poorly ventilated, its arterioles dilate to send more blood there and pick up whatever oxygen is available.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Pulmonary arterioles constrict in response to low alveolar PO2, sending blood away from the poorly ventilated region toward alveoli that are still ventilated.","why":"The pulmonary circulation is the opposite of the systemic circulation. In muscle, low oxygen dilates arterioles to bring more blood. In the lung, low alveolar oxygen constricts the local arteriole, which is called hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Blood is diverted to alveoli that are getting fresh air, so ventilation and perfusion stay matched and arterial PO2 is protected. Dilating the vessel would create a shunt and lower arterial PO2."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two alveoli side by side, each with its own bronchiole and its own pulmonary arteriole. In the first, a mucus plug has blocked the bronchiole, so alveolar oxygen falls: draw what the arteriole does to send blood elsewhere. In the second, a clot has blocked the arteriole, so alveolar carbon dioxide falls: draw what the bronchiole does. Label which gas is sensed by which vessel. One line: say what the blood leaving the first alveolus would do to arterial oxygen if the arteriole did nothing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a lung with three regions and give each a different ratio of ventilation to perfusion: one matched, one ventilated but not perfused, one perfused but not ventilated. Under each, write what the blood leaving it looks like compared with mixed arterial blood. Draw all three streams merging into the pulmonary vein. One line: say which mismatch giving extra oxygen would fix and which it would barely touch, and explain the difference."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how local hypoxic vasoconstriction and bronchiolar responses match ventilation to perfusion and predict the consequence of a mismatch."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ventilation perfusion matching. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-oxygen-transport","name":"Oxygen transport and the oxyhemoglobin curve","can":"Explain the sigmoid shape of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve and state the physiological advantage of the steep and the flat portions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"At rest, arterial blood is about 98 percent saturated and mixed venous blood is about 75 percent saturated. What fraction of the carried oxygen do tissues extract?\nA) About 75 percent  |  B) About 5 percent\nC) About 25 percent  |  D) About 50 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 25 percent","why":"The difference between 98 and 75 percent is roughly a quarter of the oxygen carried, so resting extraction is about 25 percent and the remaining 75 percent is a reserve that exercise can draw on. About 50 percent and about 75 percent extraction occur only in heavily exercising muscle. About 5 percent would leave venous blood nearly as saturated as arterial blood, which is not the case."},{"t":"mc","q":"A climber has an arterial PO2 of 60 mmHg. What is her approximate arterial oxygen saturation?\nA) About 60 percent  |  B) About 98 percent\nC) About 90 percent  |  D) About 75 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 90 percent","why":"A PO2 of 60 mmHg sits at the shoulder where the curve begins to steepen, and saturation is still about 90 percent, which is why moderate hypoxemia is tolerated surprisingly well. About 75 percent corresponds to 40 mmHg. About 60 percent would require a PO2 near 30 mmHg, and about 98 percent requires a PO2 near 100 mmHg."},{"t":"mc","q":"How much oxygen is carried dissolved in 100 mL of arterial plasma at a PO2 of 100 mmHg?\nA) About 15 mL  |  B) About 0.3 mL\nC) About 3 mL  |  D) About 20 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 0.3 mL","why":"Dissolved oxygen is only 0.003 mL per 100 mL of blood for each mmHg, so 100 mmHg gives about 0.3 mL, a trivial amount next to the roughly 20 mL bound to hemoglobin. About 3 mL is ten times too high. About 20 mL is total arterial content including hemoglobin bound oxygen, and about 15 mL is closer to normal venous content."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many oxygen molecules can one hemoglobin molecule carry when fully saturated?\nA) Two  |  B) One\nC) Eight  |  D) Four\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Four","why":"Hemoglobin has four globin chains, each with one heme group holding one iron atom, and each iron binds one oxygen molecule, so four is the maximum. Two and one understate the number of heme groups. Eight would be the number of oxygen atoms rather than molecules, which is a common way to double count."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does cooperative binding mean in hemoglobin?\nA) Oxygen binding requires a second hemoglobin molecule to be present\nB) Binding one oxygen raises the affinity of the remaining sites\nC) Binding one oxygen lowers the affinity of the remaining sites\nD) All four sites must fill at the same instant\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Binding one oxygen raises the affinity of the remaining sites","why":"The first oxygen shifts hemoglobin toward its relaxed conformation, and each subsequent oxygen binds more readily than the last, which is what bends the curve into its sigmoid shape. Falling affinity with successive binding would give a curve that flattens early rather than steepening. The sites fill in sequence rather than simultaneously, and one hemoglobin binds oxygen without needing a partner molecule."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve is S shaped and what advantage the flat part and the steep part each give the body.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"hemoglobin has four binding sites and binding one oxygen raises the affinity of the remaining sites (cooperativity); cooperativity makes saturation rise slowly at low PO2, then steeply, then level off, which is the S shape; the flat part is above a PO2 of about 60 mmHg; the flat part means arterial saturation stays high even if alveolar PO2 drops, as at altitude or in mild lung disease; the steep part is between a PO2 of about 20 and 40 mmHg, where tissues sit; on the steep part a small fall in tissue PO2 releases a lot of oxygen, so active tissue can extract much more","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the curve properly: partial pressure of oxygen in mmHg on the horizontal from 0 to 100, percent saturation of hemoglobin on the vertical from 0 to 100, sigmoid in shape. Mark the arterial point near 100 mmHg and about 98 percent, and the resting venous point near 40 mmHg and about 75 percent. Shade the steep portion and the flat portion in different colors. One line: say what the flat top buys you when someone's arterial oxygen falls from 100 to 70 mmHg, and read the saturation off your own curve to prove it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four hemoglobin molecules in a row, each with four binding sites, holding nothing, one, three and four oxygens. Beside each, mark how easy the next oxygen is to bind, showing the shape change that makes the later sites bind more readily. Underneath, draw the curve those four points trace out and mark the shallow foot, the steep middle and the flat top. One line: say what the curve would look like if the four sites did not talk to each other, and what you would lose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain the sigmoid shape of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve and state the physiological advantage of the steep and the flat portions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Oxygen transport and the oxyhemoglobin curve. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-hb-curve-shifts","name":"Curve shifts","can":"Predict the direction of the oxyhemoglobin curve shift produced by a change in pH, carbon dioxide, temperature, or 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate and state what the shift means for tissue oxygen delivery.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient is deliberately cooled to 30 C during surgery. What happens to the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve?\nA) It shifts left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly\nB) It shifts right, so hemoglobin releases oxygen more readily\nC) It shifts left, so P50 rises above normal\nD) It flattens, so maximum saturation falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It shifts left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly","why":"Cooling strengthens the oxygen hemoglobin bond, raising affinity and moving the curve left, which reduces unloading, though tissue oxygen demand is also much lower at that temperature. A rightward shift would follow fever rather than cooling. A leftward shift lowers P50, so pairing left with a rising P50 is contradictory, and temperature moves the curve sideways rather than lowering its plateau."},{"t":"mc","q":"A leftward shift of the oxyhemoglobin curve has what combined effect on loading in the lung and unloading at the tissue?\nA) Loading is impaired and unloading is slightly favored\nB) Both loading and unloading are impaired\nC) Both loading and unloading are favored\nD) Loading is slightly favored and unloading is impaired\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Loading is slightly favored and unloading is impaired","why":"Higher affinity means hemoglobin picks oxygen up more eagerly and lets go of it more reluctantly, so the lung end benefits a little and the tissue end suffers. The reversed version describes a rightward shift. A shift cannot impair both ends, since affinity acts in opposite directions at the two ends, and for the same reason it cannot favor both."},{"t":"mc","q":"A rise in PCO2 shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve in which direction?\nA) It does not move the curve  |  B) To the right\nC) To the left  |  D) Downward without a horizontal shift\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) To the right","why":"Carbon dioxide lowers hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, both directly and through the hydrogen ions it generates, and lower affinity is drawn as a rightward shift. A leftward shift is what falling carbon dioxide produces. The curve shifts sideways rather than dropping vertically, since the maximum saturation is unchanged, and carbon dioxide is one of the four classic modifiers rather than a neutral factor."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fall in blood pH shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve in which direction?\nA) To the right\nB) To the left\nC) To the right only if temperature also falls\nD) It does not move the curve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) To the right","why":"A lower pH means more hydrogen ions, which bind hemoglobin and stabilize the form that holds oxygen loosely, so the curve moves right and oxygen comes off more readily. A leftward shift follows a rise in pH, that is alkalosis. Acidosis shifts the curve right on its own without needing a temperature change, and pH is one of the strongest modifiers rather than a neutral one."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A trauma patient receives four units of red cells that have been stored for three weeks. Stored red cells lose most of their 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate. After transfusion his hemoglobin is 10 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5) and his arterial saturation is 98 percent. His tissue PO2 is a normal 40 mmHg.\nPredict how the transfused cells will behave at the tissues in the first hours, before they rebuild 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Their curve is shifted left, so at a tissue PO2 of 40 mmHg they hold on to oxygen and unload less than normal cells","why":"2,3 BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and lowers oxygen affinity. Without it, hemoglobin binds oxygen tightly, which is a left shift and a lower P50. Loading in the lung is fine, and may even be slightly better, but at a tissue PO2 of 40 mmHg the transfused cells stay more saturated than normal cells and give up less oxygen. The patient's tissues get less benefit from the transfusion than the hemoglobin number suggests until the cells restore their 2,3 BPG over about a day."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A rise in red cell 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the left and raises hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A rise in 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate shifts the curve to the right and lowers affinity, so hemoglobin releases more oxygen at any given tissue PO2.","why":"2,3 BPG is made by red cells during glycolysis and binds in the central pocket of deoxyhemoglobin, stabilizing the low affinity form. More of it means oxygen comes off more easily. Red cells make more in chronic hypoxia, at altitude, and in anemia, which is a useful adaptation. Stored blood loses 2,3 BPG and shifts left, holding oxygen more tightly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw exercising skeletal muscle in one panel and the lung capillary in the other, and put the local conditions in each: temperature, pH, carbon dioxide. Draw the curve that hemoglobin sees in each panel, shifted in the right direction, and mark the loading and unloading points. Show the same red cell passing through both. One line: say why the shift that helps unloading at the muscle does not hurt loading at the lung, and use the shape of the curve to say it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Predict the direction of the oxyhemoglobin curve shift produced by a change in pH, carbon dioxide, temperature, or 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate and state what the shift means for tissue oxygen delivery. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the direction of the oxyhemoglobin curve shift produced by a change in pH, carbon dioxide, temperature, or 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate and state what the shift means for tissue oxygen delivery."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Curve shifts. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-co2-transport","name":"Carbon dioxide transport","can":"Name the three forms in which carbon dioxide is carried, state the approximate percentage of each, and trace the chloride shift in a systemic capillary.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Carbon dioxide diffuses from a metabolically active tissue into a red blood cell. What is the next step?\nA) It is converted straight to bicarbonate without an intermediate\nB) Carbonic anhydrase combines it with water to form carbonic acid\nC) It binds directly to the heme iron in place of oxygen\nD) It is pumped into the plasma by band 3\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Carbonic anhydrase combines it with water to form carbonic acid","why":"Inside the cell the enzyme catalyzes the hydration of carbon dioxide to carbonic acid, which then dissociates almost instantly into hydrogen ion and bicarbonate. Carbon dioxide does not bind heme iron, since it uses globin amine groups instead. Band 3 exports bicarbonate rather than carbon dioxide, and the carbonic acid intermediate does exist even though it is short lived."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which form of carbon dioxide does a blood gas analyzer actually report when it gives a PCO2?\nA) The bicarbonate concentration converted into pressure units\nB) The carbamino bound fraction only\nC) Only the dissolved carbon dioxide\nD) The total of all three transport forms\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Only the dissolved carbon dioxide","why":"Partial pressure describes gas molecules in solution exerting pressure, so PCO2 reflects only the dissolved fraction, and the far larger bicarbonate and carbamino pools are invisible to that measurement. Total carbon dioxide content is a separate quantity reported in mL/dL or mmol/L. Bicarbonate is reported as its own concentration, and the carbamino fraction is not measured directly at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"In what form is most carbon dioxide carried in the blood?\nA) As carbaminohemoglobin bound to hemoglobin\nB) As carbon dioxide dissolved in plasma\nC) As carbonic acid circulating in plasma\nD) As bicarbonate ion dissolved in plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) As bicarbonate ion dissolved in plasma","why":"About 70 percent of carbon dioxide travels as bicarbonate, produced inside the red cell and then exported to plasma, which is why the blood's carbon dioxide store is also its main pH buffer. Carbaminohemoglobin accounts for roughly 23 percent, and dissolved carbon dioxide for about 7 percent. Carbonic acid is only a fleeting intermediate and is present in negligible amounts."},{"t":"mc","q":"About what percentage of carbon dioxide is carried as bicarbonate?\nA) About 70 percent  |  B) About 23 percent\nC) About 7 percent  |  D) About 45 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 70 percent","why":"Roughly 70 percent is converted to bicarbonate inside red cells by carbonic anhydrase and then exported to plasma. About 23 percent is the carbamino fraction bound to hemoglobin. About 7 percent is the dissolved fraction, and about 45 percent does not correspond to any of the three transport forms."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which enzyme converts carbon dioxide and water into carbonic acid, and where is it concentrated?\nA) Carbonic anhydrase, inside the red blood cell\nB) Carbonic anhydrase, in the plasma\nC) Carboxypeptidase, inside the red blood cell\nD) Carbonic anhydrase, on the alveolar epithelial surface\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Carbonic anhydrase, inside the red blood cell","why":"Carbonic anhydrase sits in high concentration inside red cells, which is why bicarbonate formation happens there rather than in plasma and why the chloride shift is needed to move the product out. Plasma has almost no carbonic anhydrase, so the uncatalyzed reaction there is far too slow to matter. Carboxypeptidase is a digestive protease with no role in gas transport, and the alveolar surface is not where the reaction is catalyzed."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, trace carbon dioxide from a working muscle cell into the blood of a systemic capillary. Name the three forms it is carried in, give the approximate share of each, and describe the chloride shift step by step.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"carbon dioxide diffuses from the cell into plasma and then into the red cell; about 7 percent stays dissolved in plasma; about 23 percent binds amino groups on the globin chains as carbaminohemoglobin; about 70 percent becomes bicarbonate; inside the red cell carbonic anhydrase converts carbon dioxide and water to carbonic acid, which splits into hydrogen ion and bicarbonate; hemoglobin buffers the hydrogen ion; bicarbonate leaves the red cell through band 3 in exchange for chloride entering (the chloride shift)","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same red cell twice, once in a tissue capillary and once in a lung capillary, and run every reaction in the correct direction in each. Show carbon dioxide entering and bicarbonate forming on one side, and bicarbonate re entering and carbon dioxide leaving on the other. Draw the chloride moving the opposite way in both. Mark where carbonic anhydrase sits. One line: say which single enzyme makes the whole system fast enough to work in the second a red cell spends in a capillary."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three forms in which carbon dioxide is carried, state the approximate percentage of each, and trace the chloride shift in a systemic capillary. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three forms in which carbon dioxide is carried, state the approximate percentage of each, and trace the chloride shift in a systemic capillary."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Carbon dioxide transport. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-bohr-haldane","name":"Bohr and Haldane effects","can":"Distinguish the Bohr effect from the Haldane effect and explain how the two work together at the tissue and at the lung.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A rise in tissue PCO2 increases the amount of oxygen released from hemoglobin. Classify this observation.\nA) Haldane effect  |  B) Chloride shift\nC) Cooperative binding  |  D) Bohr effect\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bohr effect","why":"Carbon dioxide acting on oxygen affinity is the Bohr effect, whichever of the two signals, carbon dioxide or hydrogen ion, is named. The Haldane effect would be the reverse arrangement, oxygen state acting on carbon dioxide carriage. The chloride shift moves bicarbonate out of the red cell and does not itself change oxygen release, and cooperative binding is the interaction among the four heme sites that shapes the curve."},{"t":"mc","q":"Deoxygenated blood in a systemic capillary takes up more carbon dioxide than fully oxygenated blood would at the same PCO2. Classify this observation.\nA) Reverse chloride shift  |  B) Increased 2,3 BPG binding\nC) Haldane effect  |  D) Bohr effect\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Haldane effect","why":"The oxygenation state of hemoglobin setting its carbon dioxide capacity is the Haldane effect, and it is why the carbon dioxide dissociation curve for venous blood lies above the arterial one. The Bohr effect goes the other way, with carbon dioxide acting on oxygen. The reverse chloride shift is the lung end of bicarbonate handling, and 2,3 BPG changes oxygen affinity rather than carbon dioxide carriage."},{"t":"mc","q":"The Bohr effect acts on which property of hemoglobin?\nA) Its rate of synthesis in the bone marrow  |  B) Its affinity for oxygen\nC) Its affinity for carbon dioxide  |  D) Its total oxygen carrying capacity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Its affinity for oxygen","why":"The Bohr effect changes how tightly hemoglobin holds oxygen, which is why it is drawn as a shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve. Affinity for carbon dioxide is what the Haldane effect changes. Carrying capacity depends on how much hemoglobin is present, not on affinity, and synthesis in the marrow is regulated by erythropoietin over days."},{"t":"mc","q":"The Haldane effect acts on which property of hemoglobin?\nA) Its ability to carry carbon dioxide and to buffer acid\nB) Its ability to bind oxygen at the iron of the heme group\nC) Its ability to bind 2,3 BPG in its own central cavity\nD) Its ability to release iron for recycling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Its ability to carry carbon dioxide and to buffer acid","why":"The Haldane effect describes how the oxygenation state of hemoglobin sets how much carbon dioxide it can carry, both as carbamino compounds and indirectly through buffering the hydrogen ion from bicarbonate formation. Oxygen binding at the heme iron is what the Bohr effect modulates. 2,3 BPG binding is a separate regulator of oxygen affinity, and iron recycling happens when the cell is broken down, not during gas transport."},{"t":"mc","q":"At a systemic capillary, the Bohr effect shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve in which direction?\nA) To the right  |  B) To the left\nC) To the right only during exercise  |  D) It does not shift the curve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) To the right","why":"Tissue carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion lower affinity, so the curve shifts right and hemoglobin releases more oxygen exactly where metabolism is producing those signals. A leftward shift would hold oxygen on and defeat the purpose. The effect operates at rest as well as in exercise, just more strongly in exercise, and a shift is precisely what the Bohr effect produces."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, define the Bohr effect and the Haldane effect so that a classmate could tell them apart, then explain how the two cooperate in a capillary in exercising muscle.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"Bohr effect: carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion lower hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen (curve shifts right); Haldane effect: deoxygenated hemoglobin binds more carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion than oxygenated hemoglobin; Bohr is carbon dioxide acting on oxygen binding; Haldane is oxygen state acting on carbon dioxide binding; in muscle, carbon dioxide enters the red cell and forms hydrogen ion, which drives oxygen off hemoglobin (Bohr); the deoxyhemoglobin formed then takes up more carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion (Haldane); each effect promotes the other, so oxygen unloading and carbon dioxide loading reinforce each other; in the lung both run in reverse: oxygen loading drives carbon dioxide off","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a tissue capillary and a lung capillary side by side, with one red cell in each. In the tissue, draw carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion arriving and show what they do to hemoglobin's grip on oxygen and which way the curve shifts. In the lung, draw oxygen arriving and show what that does to hemoglobin's willingness to hold carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion. Label which effect is which. One line: say which effect describes a gas changing oxygen carriage and which describes oxygen changing gas carriage."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one hemoglobin molecule at the center of a loop, and run it around the circuit: tissue, vein, lung, artery, back to tissue. At each station draw what it is carrying, oxygen, hydrogen ion, carbon dioxide as carbamino, and show the handoffs. Mark the two points where the two effects are helping each other rather than competing. One line: say what happens to carbon dioxide unloading at the lung in someone whose hemoglobin cannot be fully oxygenated."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the Bohr effect from the Haldane effect and explain how the two work together at the tissue and at the lung."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Bohr and Haldane effects. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-oxygen-content","name":"Oxygen content versus partial pressure","can":"Distinguish oxygen content from oxygen partial pressure and saturation and explain why anemia and carbon monoxide poisoning reduce delivery differently.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a hemoglobin of 15 g/dL and an arterial saturation of 100 percent. Ignoring dissolved oxygen, what is the arterial oxygen content?\nA) About 10 mL per 100 mL  |  B) About 27 mL per 100 mL\nC) About 20 mL per 100 mL  |  D) About 15 mL per 100 mL\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 20 mL per 100 mL","why":"Multiply 1.34 mL per gram by 15 g/dL and by a saturation of 1.0, which gives about 20 mL per deciliter. About 15 mL per deciliter would be the answer if the hemoglobin concentration were used directly as a volume. About 10 mL per deciliter corresponds to half this hemoglobin concentration, and about 27 mL per deciliter is higher than normal blood can carry."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does a standard pulse oximeter read close to normal in carbon monoxide poisoning?\nA) It cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin\nB) It measures dissolved oxygen, which is unaffected by carbon monoxide\nC) It measures the total hemoglobin concentration, which is unchanged\nD) It corrects automatically for any abnormal hemoglobin present\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin","why":"A two wavelength oximeter reads carboxyhemoglobin much like oxyhemoglobin, so it reports a saturation that overstates how much oxygen is actually bound, and the number is falsely reassuring. Dissolved oxygen is measured by a blood gas analyzer, not by an oximeter. Total hemoglobin concentration is a separate laboratory measurement, and a standard oximeter has no way to correct for abnormal hemoglobins."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a patient with pure anemia, which of these values is abnormal?\nA) Arterial oxygen content  |  B) Arterial PO2\nC) Arterial oxygen saturation  |  D) Alveolar PO2\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Arterial oxygen content","why":"Anemia lowers the number of carriers, so content per deciliter falls while every pressure and percentage measurement stays normal, which is the whole point of separating content from partial pressure. Arterial PO2 depends on lung function and is unaffected. Saturation is the percentage of the hemoglobin that is present, so it remains normal, and alveolar PO2 depends on ventilation and inspired gas."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an arterial oxygen content of 20 mL per 100 mL and a cardiac output of 5 L/min. What is oxygen delivery to the body?\nA) About 1000 mL/min  |  B) About 100 mL/min\nC) About 250 mL/min  |  D) About 4000 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 1000 mL/min","why":"Delivery is content times cardiac output, and 20 mL per deciliter is 200 mL per liter, so five liters per minute carries about 1000 mL/min. About 100 mL/min would be far below resting oxygen consumption of roughly 250 mL/min. About 250 mL/min is that consumption figure rather than delivery, and about 4000 mL/min overstates delivery several fold."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man is brought in from a house with a faulty furnace. Arterial PO2 is 95 mmHg (normal 80 to 100). A standard pulse oximeter reads 98 percent. A blood test shows 40 percent of his hemoglobin sites are occupied by carbon monoxide. Hemoglobin concentration is 15 g/dL (normal 13.5 to 17.5).\nHis tissues are severely short of oxygen. Which statement explains why, given the normal PO2?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"PO2 measures only dissolved oxygen; 40 percent of his hemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so content is low, and carbon monoxide also shifts the curve left so the remaining oxygen unloads poorly","why":"The lungs are working: dissolved oxygen equilibrates normally, so PO2 is 95. But carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin about 200 times more tightly than oxygen, and 40 percent of the sites are gone. Arterial content drops to about 60 percent of normal, similar to a hemoglobin of 9 g/dL. Worse, the bound carbon monoxide shifts the remaining sites to a higher affinity, so even the oxygen that is carried is released poorly at tissue PO2. The pulse oximeter cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, so its 98 percent is misleading."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A patient with anemia can have a normal arterial PO2 and a normal hemoglobin saturation while arterial oxygen content is low.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The lungs of an anemic patient work fine. Dissolved oxygen equilibrates to a normal PO2 near 95 mmHg, and every hemoglobin molecule present gets loaded to about 98 percent. The problem is that there are fewer hemoglobin molecules, so the total oxygen carried per 100 mL of blood is low. A pulse oximeter and a blood gas can both look normal while delivery to tissue is poor. Content is what the tissue actually receives."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three test tubes of arterial blood side by side: a healthy person, a person with half the normal hemoglobin, and a person who has breathed carbon monoxide. Under each, draw three separate scales and fill them in: partial pressure of oxygen, percent saturation, and total oxygen content in mL per 100 mL of blood, with normal content near 20. Draw the red cells to match. One line: say which of the three numbers looks completely normal in the anemic patient, and why a blood gas machine can miss it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, partial pressure of oxygen on the horizontal and total oxygen content on the vertical, and plot three curves: normal blood, anemic blood, and blood with carbon monoxide bound to some of the hemoglobin. Draw the carbon monoxide curve shifted left as well as lowered. Mark the arterial point on all three. One line: say why carbon monoxide is worse than losing the same number of binding sites to anemia, and point at the feature of the curve that shows it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish oxygen content from oxygen partial pressure and saturation and explain why anemia and carbon monoxide poisoning reduce delivery differently."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Oxygen content versus partial pressure. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Control of Ventilation","comps":[{"id":"w12-respiratory-centers","name":"Respiratory centers","can":"Identify the medullary and pontine respiratory centers and explain how the basic breathing rhythm is generated and modified.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient in pulmonary edema breathes rapidly and shallowly even though arterial PCO2 is low. Which receptors best account for the pattern?\nA) Slowly adapting stretch receptors in the airway smooth muscle\nB) Juxtacapillary J receptors in the alveolar walls\nC) Carotid body chemoreceptors responding to arterial H+\nD) Central chemoreceptors in the medulla\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Juxtacapillary J receptors in the alveolar walls","why":"J receptors sit next to the pulmonary capillaries and fire when the interstitium swells with fluid, producing the rapid shallow breathing seen in edema, and they explain why ventilation is high while PCO2 is low. Central chemoreceptors would be inhibited, not excited, by a low PCO2, so they cannot be the driver here. Stretch receptors act to shorten inspiration during large breaths, and these breaths are small. Carotid bodies respond to acidosis, but blowing off CO2 has made the blood alkalotic."},{"t":"mc","q":"An opioid drug strongly inhibits neurons of the pre-Botzinger complex. Predict the immediate effect on breathing.\nA) Breathing frequency rises while tidal volume falls\nB) Tidal volume rises with no change in frequency\nC) Expiration becomes active while inspiration is unchanged\nD) Breathing frequency falls and breaths may become irregular\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Breathing frequency falls and breaths may become irregular","why":"The pre-Botzinger complex sets the burst rate, so inhibiting it slows and destabilizes the rhythm, which is the classic pattern of opioid respiratory depression. A rise in frequency would require excitation of that pacemaker, not inhibition. A pure rise in tidal volume points to loss of the pontine off switch instead. Active expiration comes from recruiting the expiratory neurons of the ventral respiratory group, which inhibiting the rhythm generator does not do."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a complete spinal cord injury at C6. Predict the effect on the diaphragm.\nA) The diaphragm works but the medulla can no longer set its rhythm\nB) The diaphragm is completely paralyzed on both sides\nC) The diaphragm still works, because the phrenic roots leave above C6\nD) The diaphragm is paralyzed on one side only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The diaphragm still works, because the phrenic roots leave above C6","why":"The phrenic nerve arises from C3 to C5, so a lesion at C6 leaves that outflow connected to the brainstem and the diaphragm keeps contracting, although intercostal and abdominal muscles below the lesion are lost. Complete paralysis would require a lesion above C3. A one sided result would require damage to one phrenic nerve rather than a complete cord lesion. The medulla still sets the rhythm, because the descending pathway to C3 through C5 is intact."},{"t":"mc","q":"At the end of a quiet inspiration, the inspiratory neurons stop firing. What produces expiration?\nA) Elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall\nB) A burst of firing from the pontine respiratory group to the diaphragm\nC) Contraction of the abdominal muscles\nD) Contraction of the internal intercostal muscles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall","why":"Quiet expiration is passive, so once the diaphragm relaxes the stretched elastic tissue and the surface tension of the alveoli pull the lung back down and push air out. The abdominal muscles and internal intercostals do drive expiration, but only when breathing is forced, such as during exercise or coughing. The pontine respiratory group modifies inspiratory timing and has no motor projection of its own to the diaphragm."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student voluntarily holds her breath during a lab exercise. Which pathway carries that command?\nA) Fibers from the cortex that directly excite the pre-Botzinger complex\nB) Sympathetic fibers from the hypothalamus to the diaphragm\nC) Vagal fibers from the cortex to the lung stretch receptors\nD) Corticospinal fibers running to the respiratory motor neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Corticospinal fibers running to the respiratory motor neurons","why":"Voluntary control descends in the corticospinal tracts straight to the phrenic and intercostal motor neurons, bypassing the medullary rhythm generator, which is why a person can override breathing for a while yet cannot suppress it indefinitely. The cortex does not send fibers to lung stretch receptors, which are sensory endings. Exciting the pre-Botzinger complex would speed breathing rather than stop it. The diaphragm is skeletal muscle with somatic innervation, so no sympathetic pathway drives it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time on the horizontal, activity in the inspiratory neurons on the vertical, and plot quiet breathing as a repeating ramp that rises then switches off. Beneath it, on the same time axis, draw the resulting lung volume trace. Then draw the same pair for forced breathing, showing the expiratory group now firing too. One line: say which part of the trace lengthens when the pontine input is removed, and what breathing would look like."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the medullary and pontine respiratory centers and explain how the basic breathing rhythm is generated and modified. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the medullary and pontine respiratory centers and explain how the basic breathing rhythm is generated and modified."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Respiratory centers. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-chemoreceptors","name":"Chemoreceptor control","can":"Compare central and peripheral chemoreceptors by stimulus and explain why carbon dioxide is the primary minute to minute drive to breathe.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Arterial PCO2 rises from 40 to 45 mmHg in a healthy person. Which receptor group produces most of the resulting increase in ventilation?\nA) The peripheral chemoreceptors\nB) The lung stretch receptors\nC) The two chemoreceptor groups contribute equally\nD) The central chemoreceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The central chemoreceptors","why":"About 70 percent of the ventilatory response to a rise in CO2 comes from the central chemoreceptors, because the extra CO2 crosses into the brain fluid and drives H+ up there. The peripheral chemoreceptors do respond to CO2 as well, and they respond faster, but they supply the smaller share of the steady response. Stretch receptors have no chemical sensitivity at all. An equal split understates how much of the CO2 response is central."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does a rise in arterial PCO2 lower cerebrospinal fluid pH more than it lowers arterial blood pH?\nA) Cerebrospinal fluid has a higher starting bicarbonate concentration\nB) Cerebrospinal fluid has very little protein buffer\nC) Cerebrospinal fluid lacks carbonic anhydrase, so CO2 accumulates\nD) Cerebrospinal fluid receives H+ directly from arterial blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cerebrospinal fluid has very little protein buffer","why":"Blood carries hemoglobin and plasma proteins that soak up H+, while cerebrospinal fluid is nearly protein free, so the same rise in CO2 produces a bigger pH swing there and the central chemoreceptors get a strong signal. Cerebrospinal fluid bicarbonate is not higher than plasma bicarbonate in a way that would explain this. Carbonic anhydrase is present in the surrounding cells, and its absence would blunt the response rather than sharpen it. H+ cannot move directly from blood into cerebrospinal fluid, which is exactly why the CO2 route matters."},{"t":"mc","q":"A healthy person at rest switches from room air to 100 percent oxygen. Predict the change in ventilation.\nA) A large decrease, because the hypoxic drive is removed\nB) Almost no change, because CO2 and not oxygen is the resting drive\nC) An increase in rate with a matching fall in tidal volume\nD) A large increase, because more oxygen reaches the chemoreceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Almost no change, because CO2 and not oxygen is the resting drive","why":"At a normal arterial PO2 of about 100 mmHg the peripheral chemoreceptors are contributing very little, so raising PO2 further removes almost nothing and ventilation is essentially unchanged apart from a small transient dip. A large decrease is what you would see in someone whose ventilation had come to depend on hypoxia, such as a chronic CO2 retainer, so that option is right under different conditions. Extra oxygen does not excite any receptor, and there is no mechanism to trade rate against depth here."},{"t":"mc","q":"Ventilation rises by roughly 2 L/min for each 1 mmHg rise in arterial PCO2. If resting ventilation is 6 L/min and PCO2 goes from 40 to 43 mmHg, what is the new ventilation?\nA) About 12 L/min  |  B) About 18 L/min\nC) About 24 L/min  |  D) About 8 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 12 L/min","why":"A 3 mmHg rise at 2 L/min per mmHg adds 6 L/min to the starting 6 L/min, giving about 12 L/min, and this steepness is why arterial PCO2 normally stays within a mmHg or two. About 8 L/min would follow from adding only 2 L/min total instead of 2 per mmHg. About 18 comes from adding 2 L/min per mmHg to a starting value of 12, and 24 comes from multiplying rather than adding. The point of the number is how small a CO2 change it takes to double ventilation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these changes would raise ventilation the most in a healthy person?\nA) A rise in arterial PCO2 from 40 to 45 mmHg\nB) A rise in arterial PO2 from 100 to 300 mmHg\nC) A fall in arterial PCO2 from 40 to 37 mmHg\nD) A fall in arterial PO2 from 100 to 85 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A rise in arterial PCO2 from 40 to 45 mmHg","why":"A 5 mmHg rise in PCO2 roughly doubles ventilation, which is why CO2 is called the minute to minute drive to breathe. Dropping PO2 from 100 to 85 mmHg stays on the flat part of the oxygen curve and barely moves the peripheral chemoreceptors, so the ventilatory effect is small. Raising PO2 above normal does nothing, since the receptors are already quiet. Lowering PCO2 reduces the drive and would decrease ventilation, not raise it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with severe long standing lung disease has an arterial PCO2 of 65 mmHg (normal 35 to 45), a PO2 of 52 mmHg (normal 80 to 100), and a pH of 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45). He is given high flow oxygen. Within 30 minutes his PO2 is 120 mmHg, his breathing has become slow and shallow, and his PCO2 has risen to 80 mmHg.\nWhich mechanism best explains why his ventilation fell when oxygen was given?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"His central chemoreceptors had adapted to chronic high PCO2, leaving low PO2 at the peripheral chemoreceptors as his main drive; raising PO2 removed that drive","why":"Normally PCO2 is the main drive, sensed as hydrogen ion in the fluid around the medulla. In chronic carbon dioxide retention, bicarbonate moves into that fluid over days and its pH returns toward normal, so the central chemoreceptors stop responding to the high PCO2. What is left is the peripheral chemoreceptor response to low PO2. Raising PO2 above about 60 mmHg silences the carotid bodies, ventilation falls, and PCO2 climbs. Oxygen in such a patient is given carefully, aiming for a modest PO2 rather than a normal one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two graphs side by side, both with ventilation on the vertical. On the first, arterial carbon dioxide on the horizontal near the normal 40 mmHg, plotted as a steep line that responds to small changes. On the second, arterial oxygen on the horizontal from 100 down to 30 mmHg, plotted as a line that stays flat until it falls off below about 60. Mark normal on both. One line: say which stimulus is doing the minute to minute work, and say what has to happen before the other one matters."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare central and peripheral chemoreceptors by stimulus and explain why carbon dioxide is the primary minute to minute drive to breathe. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare central and peripheral chemoreceptors by stimulus and explain why carbon dioxide is the primary minute to minute drive to breathe."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Chemoreceptor control. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-ventilation-adaptation","name":"Ventilation in exercise and at altitude","can":"Predict the ventilatory response to exercise and to acute and chronic altitude exposure and explain the acclimatization changes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"At an altitude where barometric pressure is 380 mmHg, what is the partial pressure of oxygen in warmed humidified inspired air? Water vapor pressure is 47 mmHg and oxygen is 21 percent of dry air.\nA) About 100 mmHg  |  B) About 50 mmHg\nC) About 70 mmHg  |  D) About 80 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 70 mmHg","why":"Subtract the water vapor first, since airway gas is fully humidified, giving 380 minus 47 equals 333 mmHg of dry gas, and 21 percent of that is about 70 mmHg. About 100 mmHg is the sea level value for this calculation and shows the size of the altitude penalty. About 80 mmHg comes from taking 21 percent of 380 without removing water vapor. About 50 mmHg is closer to a normal venous PO2 and does not come from this computation."},{"t":"mc","q":"After several weeks at altitude, hematocrit rises from 45 to 58 percent. Predict the paired consequences.\nA) Oxygen carrying capacity rises and blood viscosity falls\nB) Oxygen carrying capacity falls and blood viscosity rises\nC) Oxygen carrying capacity rises and blood viscosity rises\nD) Oxygen carrying capacity is unchanged and blood viscosity rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Oxygen carrying capacity rises and blood viscosity rises","why":"More red cells means more hemoglobin per unit of blood, which raises how much oxygen the blood can carry, but the same crowding thickens the blood so the heart must work harder to move it. This is why altitude polycythemia is a trade rather than a pure gain. Viscosity cannot fall when cell concentration rises, and carrying capacity cannot fall or stay flat when hemoglobin per unit volume increases."},{"t":"mc","q":"What happens to ventilation in the first few seconds after exercise begins?\nA) It falls briefly and then rises once lactate appears\nB) It stays at the resting level until oxygen consumption doubles\nC) It rises abruptly, before blood gases have changed\nD) It rises slowly over about two minutes as PCO2 climbs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises abruptly, before blood gases have changed","why":"Ventilation jumps almost the instant movement starts, well before arterial blood has time to circulate and report any change, so the rise cannot be caused by a chemical signal. A slow rise driven by climbing PCO2 describes what students often assume but not what recordings show, and in moderate exercise PCO2 barely moves. Ventilation does not dip at the start, and it does not wait for lactate, which appears only in heavier work."},{"t":"mc","q":"What causes the abrupt jump in ventilation at the very start of exercise?\nA) A sudden fall in arterial PO2 sensed by the carotid and aortic bodies\nB) Feedforward signals from the motor cortex and limb proprioceptors\nC) A sudden rise in the H+ concentration of the cerebrospinal fluid\nD) Stretch receptor firing produced by the deeper breaths\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Feedforward signals from the motor cortex and limb proprioceptors","why":"The motor cortex sends collateral signals to the respiratory centers as it commands the muscles, and moving joints and muscles add proprioceptive input, so breathing is turned up in anticipation rather than in response. Arterial PO2 does not fall in moderate exercise, so the carotid bodies have nothing new to report. Cerebrospinal fluid H+ changes too slowly to explain a response measured in seconds. Stretch receptors respond to the deeper breaths but do not initiate them."},{"t":"mc","q":"During moderate steady state exercise, what happens to arterial PCO2?\nA) It stays close to its resting value\nB) It rises for a minute and then climbs further as work continues\nC) It rises steadily as muscle CO2 production climbs\nD) It falls steadily because breathing increases first\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It stays close to its resting value","why":"Ventilation increases in proportion to CO2 production, so the extra CO2 is cleared as fast as it is made and arterial PCO2 is held near 40 mmHg, which is one of the most striking pieces of matching in the whole system. A steady rise would mean ventilation was lagging behind metabolism, which happens only in disease or very heavy work. A steady fall is seen above the lactate threshold in heavy exercise, so that answer is correct under different conditions."},{"t":"bd","q":"A student flies from sea level to a research station at 3500 m. Without notes, predict what happens to her ventilation, arterial PCO2, and arterial pH in the first hour, then explain what changes over the next two weeks and why each change helps.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"low arterial PO2 stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors and ventilation rises; hyperventilation lowers PCO2 and raises pH (respiratory alkalosis); the alkalosis quiets the central chemoreceptors and limits the initial rise in ventilation; over days the kidneys excrete bicarbonate, pH returns toward normal, and ventilation rises further; the kidney releases erythropoietin, so red cell mass and hemoglobin rise, raising oxygen content; red cell 2,3 BPG rises, shifting the curve right to improve unloading at the tissue","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time on the horizontal through rest, the start of exercise, steady exercise, and recovery, with ventilation on the vertical. Plot the abrupt jump at the moment work begins, the plateau, and the fall at the end. On the same time axis underneath, plot arterial carbon dioxide staying close to normal through moderate exercise. One line: say why the ventilation jump cannot have been caused by a rise in carbon dioxide, and name what you think triggered it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Predict the ventilatory response to exercise and to acute and chronic altitude exposure and explain the acclimatization changes. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to predict the ventilatory response to exercise and to acute and chronic altitude exposure and explain the acclimatization changes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ventilation in exercise and at altitude. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w12-lab-ventilation-response","name":"Ventilatory response testing","can":"Measure the change in breathing rate and depth after hyperventilation, breath holding, and rebreathing and explain each result by the change in carbon dioxide.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student feels lightheaded with tingling fingers after hyperventilating in lab. What explains those symptoms?\nA) Low PCO2 causes cerebral vasoconstriction and alkalosis\nB) High PCO2 causes cerebral vasodilation and acidosis\nC) Low arterial PO2 starves the brain of oxygen\nD) High arterial PO2 causes oxygen toxicity in the nerves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Low PCO2 causes cerebral vasoconstriction and alkalosis","why":"CO2 is a powerful cerebral vasodilator, so blowing it off narrows brain vessels and reduces cerebral blood flow, which produces the lightheadedness, while the alkalosis increases nerve excitability and causes the tingling. High PCO2 would dilate rather than constrict and is the opposite of what hyperventilation produces. Arterial PO2 actually rises slightly during hyperventilation, so hypoxia is not the cause, and the small rise in PO2 from breathing room air is nowhere near toxic."},{"t":"mc","q":"A subject breathes 10 times a minute at 600 mL at rest, then 16 times a minute at 750 mL near the end of rebreathing. How much did minute ventilation increase?\nA) From 6 to 15 L/min  |  B) From 6 to 18 L/min\nC) From 6 to 9 L/min  |  D) From 6 to 12 L/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) From 6 to 12 L/min","why":"Minute ventilation is rate times tidal volume, so 10 times 600 is 6000 mL/min or 6 L/min, and 16 times 750 is 12000 mL/min or 12 L/min, a doubling produced by the rising CO2. About 9 L/min is what you get by letting the rate rise to 16 while holding tidal volume at the resting 600 mL, so it counts only half the change. The 15 and 18 L/min answers overshoot, and neither can be produced by pairing the rate and depth that were actually recorded."},{"t":"mc","q":"After hyperventilation the subject shows 25 seconds of apnea. What ends the apnea?\nA) Oxygen stores fall low enough to excite the carotid bodies\nB) The lung stretch receptors stop firing at low lung volume\nC) CO2 accumulates until the chemoreceptor threshold is reached again\nD) The cortex takes over and forces a voluntary breath\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) CO2 accumulates until the chemoreceptor threshold is reached again","why":"Metabolism keeps producing CO2 during the pause, so PCO2 climbs back toward 40 mmHg and the chemoreceptors resume driving the respiratory centers, which restarts breathing without any conscious effort. Oxygen would take much longer than 25 seconds to fall to a level that excites the carotid bodies, so hypoxia is not the trigger here. Stretch receptors report inflation rather than initiate breaths. The apnea ends automatically, which is why the subject cannot prevent it by choosing not to breathe."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does one minute of voluntary hyperventilation do to arterial PCO2?\nA) It raises it above 40 mmHg\nB) It lowers it below 40 mmHg\nC) It leaves it unchanged, since PCO2 is tightly defended\nD) It raises it briefly and then lowers it below 40 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It lowers it below 40 mmHg","why":"Hyperventilation moves more air than metabolism requires, so CO2 is cleared from the alveoli faster than the blood delivers it and arterial PCO2 drops, often into the twenties. A rise would require moving less air, not more. PCO2 is defended by the reflex system, but voluntary cortical drive overrides that defense, which is the whole point of the maneuver. There is no initial rise, because clearance increases from the very first deep breath."},{"t":"mc","q":"What breathing pattern is expected immediately after a bout of voluntary hyperventilation?\nA) A single very deep breath followed by normal breathing\nB) A period of apnea or clearly reduced breathing\nC) A period of even faster and deeper breathing\nD) Breathing that returns instantly to the resting pattern\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A period of apnea or clearly reduced breathing","why":"PCO2 has been driven below the threshold that normally keeps the respiratory centers firing, so once the voluntary effort stops there is little chemical drive and breathing pauses or becomes shallow until CO2 rebuilds. Faster breathing would require more drive, not less. An instant return to the resting pattern would only happen if PCO2 had not changed. A single deep breath is the pattern of a sigh, not of recovery from hypocapnia."},{"t":"bd","q":"In lab, a subject held her breath for 45 seconds after normal breathing, 90 seconds after one minute of hyperventilation, and only 20 seconds after one minute of rebreathing into a paper bag. Without notes, explain all three results in terms of carbon dioxide, and explain why the hyperventilation result is dangerous before swimming underwater.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"a breath hold ends when arterial PCO2 rises to the breakpoint that the chemoreceptors will not tolerate; hyperventilation lowers the starting PCO2, so it takes longer to reach the breakpoint and the hold is longer; rebreathing raises the starting PCO2, so the breakpoint is reached sooner and the hold is shorter; hyperventilation adds almost no oxygen because hemoglobin is already about 98 percent saturated; central chemoreceptors sense the hydrogen ion formed from carbon dioxide, which is the main drive; while swimming, oxygen is used up before PCO2 reaches the breakpoint, so PO2 can fall low enough to cause blackout without any urge to breathe","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one prediction chart before the data: three columns for hyperventilation, breath holding and rebreathing, and three rows for arterial carbon dioxide, plasma pH, and the ventilation that follows. Draw an up arrow, a down arrow, or no change in every cell, with a small sketch beside each. Then mark the one cell you are least sure about. One line: name the cell you would need a measurement to settle, and say what instrument would settle it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Measure the change in breathing rate and depth after hyperventilation, breath holding, and rebreathing and explain each result by the change in carbon dioxide. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Ventilatory response testing. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Renal Physiology","comps":[{"id":"w13-kidney-functions","name":"Kidney functions","can":"List the homeostatic functions of the kidney including water and electrolyte balance, acid base regulation, waste excretion, blood pressure control, and hormone production.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with long standing kidney failure is anemic despite normal iron stores. Which lost kidney function best explains this?\nA) Reduced erythropoietin production  |  B) Reduced urea excretion\nC) Reduced renin release  |  D) Reduced calcitriol production\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Reduced erythropoietin production","why":"Damaged kidneys make less erythropoietin, so marrow gets a weaker signal and red cell production falls even when iron is available. Poor urea excretion raises blood urea nitrogen but does not by itself stop erythropoiesis. Low renin lowers blood pressure and sodium retention, a separate consequence. Low calcitriol produces low calcium and bone disease, not anemia."},{"t":"mc","q":"Kidney function falls to about fifteen percent of normal. Which acid base disturbance would you expect?\nA) Respiratory acidosis  |  B) Respiratory alkalosis\nC) Metabolic acidosis  |  D) Metabolic alkalosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Metabolic acidosis","why":"The kidney is the route for excreting the fixed acid produced daily by metabolism and for making new bicarbonate, so failing kidneys let acid accumulate and bicarbonate fall. Respiratory disturbances arise from changes in ventilation and PCO2, not from tubular transport. Metabolic alkalosis would require gaining bicarbonate or losing acid, the opposite of what a failing kidney does."},{"t":"mc","q":"Mean arterial pressure falls after blood loss. What is the kidney's immediate endocrine response?\nA) Granular cells decrease renin secretion\nB) Granular cells increase renin secretion\nC) The tubule releases atrial natriuretic peptide\nD) The kidney stops releasing erythropoietin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Granular cells increase renin secretion","why":"Low perfusion pressure at the afferent arteriole is a direct stimulus for renin release, which raises angiotensin II and aldosterone and defends pressure and volume. Renin falls when pressure is high, which is the opposite situation. Atrial natriuretic peptide comes from stretched atria and would worsen the volume loss. Erythropoietin release actually tends to rise with low oxygen delivery, and in any case it acts over days."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does chronic kidney disease tend to lower plasma calcium?\nA) Erythropoietin normally drives calcium uptake into blood\nB) Renin normally drives calcium reabsorption in the loop of Henle\nC) More calcium is filtered because the filtration membrane is damaged\nD) Less calcitriol is made, so the intestine absorbs less calcium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Less calcitriol is made, so the intestine absorbs less calcium","why":"The failing kidney cannot complete the 1 alpha hydroxylation step, so calcitriol falls and the intestine takes up less dietary calcium. Filtration of calcium is not the main problem, and filtration overall falls rather than rises in kidney disease. Erythropoietin acts on marrow and has no role in calcium transport. Renin acts on sodium and pressure through angiotensin, not on calcium handling."},{"t":"mc","q":"A hiker goes twelve hours without water. What happens to the volume and osmolarity of the urine the kidney makes?\nA) Volume rises and osmolarity falls  |  B) Volume falls and osmolarity falls\nC) Volume falls and osmolarity rises  |  D) Volume rises and osmolarity rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Volume falls and osmolarity rises","why":"Water conservation means reabsorbing water while still excreting the same solute load, so a small volume of concentrated urine leaves. Rising volume would waste the water the body is short of. Falling osmolarity with falling volume is impossible while solute output is maintained. Rising volume with rising osmolarity would mean excreting far more solute than was consumed."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The kidney releases erythropoietin when oxygen delivery to its tissue falls, and this increases red blood cell production in bone marrow.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The kidney is a good oxygen sensor because its blood flow is high and steady, so a drop in oxygen content reflects the blood rather than the flow. This is why chronic kidney disease causes anemia and why people living at altitude make more red cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one kidney large in the middle of the page with six arrows leaving it, one per job: water balance, electrolyte balance, acid base balance, waste excretion, blood pressure control, and hormone production. At the end of each arrow draw the actual thing moved or made, a water molecule, a sodium ion, a hydrogen ion, a urea molecule, renin, erythropoietin. One line: pick the job whose failure you would notice first in a patient, and name the sign."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a body outline for a patient whose kidneys have failed and mark the consequences on it rather than listing them: where fluid collects, what plasma potassium does, what arterial pH does, what builds up in the blood, and what happens to the red cell count. Beside each mark write which kidney job was lost to cause it. One line: dialysis replaces some of these and not others, so name one it does not replace."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to list the homeostatic functions of the kidney including water and electrolyte balance, acid base regulation, waste excretion, blood pressure control, and hormone production."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Kidney functions. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-nephron-function","name":"Nephron structure and function","can":"Match each nephron segment to its dominant transport function and distinguish a cortical from a juxtamedullary nephron by capability.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A nephron has a short loop that barely enters the medulla. Which capability is most limited?\nA) Secreting H+ into the tubular fluid\nB) Filtering plasma at the glomerulus\nC) Reabsorbing filtered glucose\nD) Producing a maximally concentrated urine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Producing a maximally concentrated urine","why":"Concentrating urine requires a steep medullary gradient, and that gradient is built by long loops, so a short looped cortical nephron contributes little to it. Glucose reabsorption happens in the proximal tubule and is unaffected by loop length. H+ secretion happens proximally and in the collecting duct. Filtration occurs at the renal corpuscle and does not depend on how far the loop descends."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient cannot concentrate urine because the medullary osmotic gradient has collapsed. Which segment's transport function is most likely impaired?\nA) The proximal convoluted tubule  |  B) The descending thin limb\nC) The thick ascending limb  |  D) The distal convoluted tubule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The thick ascending limb","why":"The thick ascending limb pumps salt into the medullary interstitium without letting water follow, and that single asymmetry is what builds the gradient. The proximal tubule reabsorbs isosmotically and adds nothing to medullary tonicity. The descending thin limb responds to the gradient rather than creating it. The distal convoluted tubule lies in the cortex, away from the medullary interstitium."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks the Na+ K+ 2Cl- symporter. What happens to the medullary osmotic gradient?\nA) It becomes shallower\nB) It becomes steeper\nC) It is unchanged because the gradient depends on urea alone\nD) It reverses so the medulla becomes dilute relative to cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It becomes shallower","why":"Blocking the symporter stops the thick ascending limb from loading salt into the interstitium, so medullary osmolarity falls toward plasma values and concentrating ability drops. The gradient cannot steepen when its main pump is off. Urea recycling contributes to the gradient but cannot maintain it without the salt component. Reversal would require actively pumping salt out of the medulla, which no segment does."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the fluid leaving the thick ascending limb hypo osmotic compared with plasma?\nA) Urea is secreted into the tubular fluid\nB) Both salt and water are removed in equal proportion\nC) Water is added to the tubular fluid from the interstitium\nD) Salt is removed while water cannot follow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Salt is removed while water cannot follow","why":"The segment is water impermeable, so pumping out Na+, K+ and Cl- lowers tubular osmolarity to roughly 100 mOsm/L, earning the name diluting segment. Water cannot move in because the segment blocks water flux in either direction. Urea secretion happens deeper in the medullary collecting duct and would not dilute the fluid. Removing salt and water together is isosmotic reabsorption, which is what the proximal tubule does instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"What do macula densa cells sense in the tubular fluid?\nA) The concentration of urea  |  B) The delivery rate of NaCl\nC) The concentration of filtered albumin  |  D) The partial pressure of oxygen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The delivery rate of NaCl","why":"Macula densa cells sit where the thick ascending limb touches its own glomerulus and read NaCl delivery as a proxy for filtration rate, which drives tubuloglomerular feedback. Albumin should not be present in filtrate in appreciable amounts, so it carries no useful signal. Oxygen is sensed by peritubular cells for erythropoietin release. Urea concentration varies with water handling and is not the feedback signal."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The descending limb of the loop of Henle actively pumps sodium chloride out of the tubular fluid into the medullary interstitium.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The descending limb has no salt pump; it is permeable to water, so water leaves and the fluid concentrates passively. The thick ascending limb is the segment that pumps sodium chloride out.","why":"The two limbs divide the work. The descending limb equilibrates with the salty medulla by losing water. The thick ascending limb pumps salt out but holds water in, which is what makes the medulla salty and the fluid dilute. Students often put the pump on the wrong limb because the descending limb is the one that gets concentrated."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one nephron stretched left to right from glomerulus to collecting duct, with cortex and medulla as bands behind it. Label Bowman's capsule, proximal tubule, descending limb, thick ascending limb, distal tubule, and collecting duct. Under each segment write its dominant transport job in a few words. One line: name the segment that stays impermeable to water no matter what the hormones are doing, and say what that buys the kidney."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a wedge of kidney with cortex and medulla, then draw a cortical nephron and a juxtamedullary nephron inside it at true relative depth and loop length. Add peritubular capillaries to one and vasa recta to the other. Mark roughly what fraction of nephrons is each type. One line: say which type you need to make urine more concentrated than plasma, and what would happen if you had only the other kind."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match each nephron segment to its dominant transport function and distinguish a cortical from a juxtamedullary nephron by capability."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Nephron structure and function. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-renal-processes","name":"Three renal processes","can":"Define filtration, reabsorption, secretion, and excretion and write the equation that relates them for any solute.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Sodium is filtered at 17.5 mEq/min and excreted at 0.175 mEq/min. What fraction of the filtered sodium is reabsorbed?\nA) About 99 percent  |  B) About 50 percent\nC) About 65 percent  |  D) About 90 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 99 percent","why":"Excretion divided by filtered load is 0.175 divided by 17.5, which is 0.01 or one percent, so 99 percent is reabsorbed across the whole nephron. Sixty five percent is the proximal tubule's share alone, not the total. Ninety percent would leave ten times more sodium in urine than the numbers give. Fifty percent would represent a sodium loss incompatible with volume balance."},{"t":"mc","q":"For substance X, glomerular filtration rate is 100 mL/min, plasma concentration is 2 mg/mL, urine flow is 2 mL/min, and urine concentration is 150 mg/mL. What is the net tubular handling?\nA) Net secretion of 300 mg/min  |  B) Neither reabsorption nor secretion\nC) Net reabsorption of 100 mg/min  |  D) Net secretion of 100 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Net secretion of 100 mg/min","why":"The filtered load is 100 mL/min times 2 mg/mL, which is 200 mg/min, and excretion is 2 mL/min times 150 mg/mL, which is 300 mg/min, so the tubule added 100 mg/min. Reabsorption would require excretion below 200 mg/min. Three hundred is the total excretion rate, not the secreted increment. Equality of filtration and excretion would require an excretion rate of 200 mg/min."},{"t":"mc","q":"A solute is filtered at 100 mg/min and excreted at 20 mg/min, and it is not secreted. How much is reabsorbed?\nA) 100 mg/min  |  B) 120 mg/min\nC) 20 mg/min  |  D) 80 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 80 mg/min","why":"With no secretion, excretion equals filtration minus reabsorption, so reabsorption is 100 minus 20, which is 80 mg/min. Getting 120 comes from adding rather than subtracting the excreted amount. Twenty is the excretion rate itself, not the amount recovered. One hundred would mean everything filtered was reabsorbed and nothing appeared in urine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Glomerular filtration rate is 125 mL/min and plasma glucose is 100 mg/dL. What is the filtered load of glucose?\nA) 125 mg/min  |  B) 100 mg/min\nC) 12.5 mg/min  |  D) 1250 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 125 mg/min","why":"A plasma glucose of 100 mg/dL is 1 mg/mL, so 125 mL/min times 1 mg/mL gives 125 mg/min. Getting 12.5 misplaces the decimal by treating plasma glucose as 0.1 mg/mL. Getting 1250 treats it as 10 mg/mL. One hundred simply repeats the plasma concentration without multiplying by the filtered volume."},{"t":"mc","q":"Urine flow is 1 mL/min and urine sodium concentration is 100 mEq/L. What is the sodium excretion rate?\nA) 100 mEq/min  |  B) 1 mEq/min\nC) 0.1 mEq/min  |  D) 10 mEq/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 0.1 mEq/min","why":"One mL/min is 0.001 L/min, and 0.001 L/min times 100 mEq/L gives 0.1 mEq/min. One mEq/min would require ten times the urine flow or concentration. Ten and 100 mEq/min ignore the liter to milliliter conversion, and either value would represent a sodium loss the body could not sustain."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"If a solute's excretion rate is lower than its filtered load, the tubule net reabsorbed that solute.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The mass balance equation is the whole tool. Filtration loads the tubule, and comparing what comes out with what went in tells you the net direction of tubular handling. Excretion below the filtered load means net reabsorption; above it means net secretion; equal means no net handling, which is how inulin behaves."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a nephron with its capillaries and put the three arrows in the right places and directions: filtration at the glomerulus into the capsule, reabsorption from tubule back to blood, secretion from blood into tubule. Write the equation for excretion in terms of the other three beside the drawing. One line: pick a substance that is filtered and then almost entirely reabsorbed, and say what its excretion rate looks like."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same nephron three times in a row, once for glucose, once for creatinine, and once for a secreted drug such as penicillin. On each copy draw only the arrows that actually happen for that substance, with arrow thickness showing how much. One line: say which of the three leaves in the urine at a higher rate than it was filtered, and what that proves about a step."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define filtration, reabsorption, secretion, and excretion and write the equation that relates them for any solute."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Three renal processes. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-filtration-membrane","name":"Filtration membrane and selectivity","can":"Describe the three layers of the filtration membrane and predict which plasma components appear in the filtrate based on size and charge.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Three neutral molecules have radii of 1.5, 3.0, and 4.5 nanometers. Rank their filterability from highest to lowest.\nA) 4.5 nanometers, then 3.0, then 1.5\nB) 3.0 nanometers, then 1.5, then 4.5\nC) 1.5 nanometers, then 3.0, then 4.5\nD) All three are filtered equally because none carries a charge\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 1.5 nanometers, then 3.0, then 4.5","why":"With charge held constant, filterability falls steadily as radius rises, because the slit diaphragm and basement membrane mesh impose a size limit. Ranking largest first reverses the physical barrier. Placing the middle size at the top has no mechanistic basis. Neutrality removes the charge penalty but does nothing to remove the size restriction."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of the following is filtered most freely at the glomerulus?\nA) A red blood cell  |  B) Albumin\nC) Glucose  |  D) Hemoglobin bound to haptoglobin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Glucose","why":"Glucose has a molecular weight of only 180 daltons and no charge barrier acting against it, so it enters filtrate at the same concentration as plasma. Red blood cells are stopped at the endothelium. Albumin is stopped by both size and negative charge. Hemoglobin bound to haptoglobin forms a complex too large to cross, which is why free hemoglobin is filtered but the bound form is not."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two molecules have the same radius, but one is negatively charged and one is neutral. Which is filtered more readily?\nA) Neither is filtered, since charged molecules block the pores\nB) The neutral molecule\nC) The negatively charged molecule\nD) They are filtered equally because size alone matters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The neutral molecule","why":"The fixed negative charge of the basement membrane repels anions, so at equal size the neutral molecule crosses more easily. The anion is the one held back, not favored. Charge does matter alongside size, which is why the two barriers are described separately. Charged molecules do not block pores, and small cations actually filter slightly better than neutral ones of the same size."},{"t":"mc","q":"A disease strips the negative charge from the glomerular basement membrane but leaves pore size normal. What appears in the urine?\nA) Glucose  |  B) Nothing new, since pore size is unchanged\nC) Red blood cells  |  D) Albumin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Albumin","why":"Albumin sits close to the size limit and is held back mainly by charge repulsion, so removing charge alone lets it through. Red blood cells are excluded by size at the endothelium, which is intact. Glucose is already freely filtered and is fully reabsorbed proximally, so it does not appear. Charge loss by itself is enough to cause proteinuria, so nothing new is not correct."},{"t":"mc","q":"About forty percent of plasma calcium is bound to albumin. What happens to that bound fraction at the glomerulus?\nA) It is filtered only when plasma calcium is low\nB) It is filtered normally, since calcium ions are small\nC) It is not filtered, because the albumin carrying it cannot cross\nD) It is filtered and then completely secreted back\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It is not filtered, because the albumin carrying it cannot cross","why":"Filterability depends on the size of the whole complex, so calcium riding on albumin stays in the capillary while free ionized calcium crosses easily. The small size of a calcium ion does not help once it is bound. Secretion is a tubular process and does not undo filtration at the glomerulus. Plasma calcium level changes how much is free but does not change the rule for the bound fraction."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Albumin is kept out of the filtrate only because it is too large to fit through the filtration slits.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Albumin is close to the size cutoff and would partly pass on size alone; it is also repelled by the negative charges on the basement membrane and podocytes, so charge and size together keep it out.","why":"The barrier is a charge filter as well as a sieve. Neutral molecules the size of albumin are filtered a little, but albumin's negative charge cuts that to almost nothing. When glomerular disease strips the negative charge, albumin leaks even though the pores have not enlarged, which is why proteinuria can appear before structural damage."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a cross section through the filtration membrane with blood on one side and capsular space on the other, showing all three layers: fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte foot processes with slits between them. Mark the negative charge on the basement membrane. Then draw four things arriving, water, glucose, albumin, and a red blood cell, and show how far each gets. One line: say why albumin is held back for two separate reasons, not one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two filtration membranes side by side, a normal one and one whose basement membrane has lost its negative charge. Send the same set of plasma components at both: water, sodium, glucose, a small protein, albumin, and a red cell. Show what crosses in each. Beside the damaged one write the urinalysis result you expect. One line: say what weeks of this does to plasma colloid osmotic pressure, and what that does to tissue fluid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the three layers of the filtration membrane and predict which plasma components appear in the filtrate based on size and charge."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Filtration membrane and selectivity. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-gfr-forces","name":"Net filtration pressure and GFR","can":"Calculate net filtration pressure from glomerular hydrostatic pressure, capsular pressure, and colloid osmotic pressure and predict the effect of each change on glomerular filtration rate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The filtration coefficient is 12.5 mL/min per mmHg and net filtration pressure is 12 mmHg. What is the glomerular filtration rate?\nA) 104 mL/min  |  B) 1.04 mL/min\nC) 125 mL/min  |  D) 150 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 150 mL/min","why":"Filtration rate is the coefficient times the net pressure, so 12.5 times 12 is 150 mL/min. One hundred twenty five would correspond to a net pressure of 10 mmHg, the normal value rather than the one given. One hundred four comes from dividing the coefficient into the pressure product incorrectly. Dividing 12.5 by 12 gives about 1.04, which inverts the relationship."},{"t":"mc","q":"Glomerular hydrostatic pressure is 60 mmHg, capsular pressure is 18 mmHg, and colloid osmotic pressure is 28 mmHg. What is the net filtration pressure?\nA) 70 mmHg  |  B) 32 mmHg\nC) 14 mmHg  |  D) 42 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 14 mmHg","why":"Net filtration pressure is 60 minus 18 minus 28, which is 14 mmHg. Forty two comes from subtracting only the capsular pressure. Seventy comes from adding the opposing pressures instead of subtracting them. Thirty two comes from subtracting only the colloid osmotic pressure."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stone partly obstructs the ureter, raising capsular hydrostatic pressure from 15 to 25 mmHg with glomerular hydrostatic pressure at 55 mmHg and colloid osmotic pressure at 30 mmHg. What is the new net filtration pressure?\nA) 20 mmHg  |  B) 5 mmHg\nC) 10 mmHg  |  D) 0 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 0 mmHg","why":"The calculation is 55 minus 25 minus 30, which is exactly zero, so filtration stops in that kidney. Ten mmHg was the value before the obstruction. Twenty mmHg would require the capsular pressure to fall rather than rise. Five mmHg does not follow from any correct arrangement of these three numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Severe dehydration raises plasma protein concentration. What happens to net filtration pressure and glomerular filtration rate?\nA) Both rise\nB) Both fall\nC) Net filtration pressure rises and filtration rate falls\nD) Net filtration pressure falls and filtration rate rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both fall","why":"More concentrated plasma protein raises colloid osmotic pressure, which is subtracted in the net filtration equation, so the net force and the filtration rate both drop. A rise would require protein to become more dilute. Net filtration pressure and filtration rate move together when the filtration coefficient is unchanged, so answers that split them apart are inconsistent."},{"t":"mc","q":"Advanced liver disease lowers plasma albumin substantially. What happens to net filtration pressure at the glomerulus?\nA) It rises, because colloid osmotic pressure is lower\nB) It rises, because capsular pressure is lower\nC) It is unchanged, because filtration depends only on blood pressure\nD) It falls, because colloid osmotic pressure is lower\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It rises, because colloid osmotic pressure is lower","why":"Colloid osmotic pressure is one of the two subtracted terms, so lowering it leaves a larger net force pushing fluid out. Falling net pressure would require the opposing forces to grow. Capsular pressure is set by filtrate outflow and is not changed by albumin levels. Filtration depends on all three pressures, not on blood pressure alone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a glomerular capillary with three arrows drawn to scale: glomerular hydrostatic pressure about 55 mmHg pushing out, capsular hydrostatic pressure about 15 mmHg pushing in, and blood colloid osmotic pressure about 30 mmHg pulling in. Work the net filtration pressure out on the page and mark normal GFR near 125 mL/min beside it. One line: say which single one of those three pressures the body actually adjusts, and how it does it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the same three pressure arrows three more times, once for a stone blocking the ureter, once for liver failure with low plasma protein, and once for hemorrhage dropping arterial pressure. In each, redraw only the arrow that changes and write the new net filtration pressure and what GFR does. One line: two of these three move GFR the same direction, so name the odd one out and say why it goes the other way."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate net filtration pressure from glomerular hydrostatic pressure, capsular pressure, and colloid osmotic pressure and predict the effect of each change on glomerular filtration rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Net filtration pressure and GFR. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-gfr-regulation","name":"Regulation of GFR","can":"Compare myogenic autoregulation, tubuloglomerular feedback, sympathetic control, and hormonal control and predict the effect of afferent and efferent arteriolar constriction on filtration rate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The afferent arteriole constricts. What happens to glomerular hydrostatic pressure and glomerular filtration rate?\nA) Both rise\nB) Both fall\nC) Glomerular hydrostatic pressure rises and filtration rate falls\nD) Glomerular hydrostatic pressure falls and filtration rate rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both fall","why":"Constricting the vessel that feeds the glomerulus reduces inflow, so pressure inside the capillary bed drops and filtration falls with it. Both rising would describe efferent constriction instead. Glomerular pressure and filtration move in the same direction when the other Starling forces are unchanged, so the two split answers are internally inconsistent."},{"t":"mc","q":"The efferent arteriole constricts to a moderate degree. What happens to glomerular hydrostatic pressure and glomerular filtration rate?\nA) Both rise\nB) Neither changes, because the constriction is downstream of the glomerulus\nC) Both fall\nD) Glomerular hydrostatic pressure falls and filtration rate rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Both rise","why":"The efferent arteriole is the outflow resistor, so narrowing it dams blood in the glomerular capillaries, raises hydrostatic pressure there, and raises filtration rate. A fall in both would describe afferent constriction. Glomerular pressure cannot fall when outflow is restricted. Being downstream is exactly why the constriction raises upstream pressure rather than leaving it unchanged."},{"t":"mc","q":"The afferent arteriole dilates. What happens to glomerular filtration rate?\nA) It is unchanged  |  B) It falls at first and then rises above baseline\nC) It rises  |  D) It falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It rises","why":"Widening the inflow vessel lets more of the arterial pressure reach the glomerular capillaries, raising hydrostatic pressure and filtration. A fall would follow afferent constriction, not dilation. No change would require an opposing adjustment elsewhere that the question does not describe. There is no biphasic reversal here, since dilation acts in one direction only."},{"t":"mc","q":"The efferent arteriole dilates. What happens to glomerular hydrostatic pressure and glomerular filtration rate?\nA) Both rise\nB) Both fall\nC) Glomerular hydrostatic pressure rises and filtration rate falls\nD) Only renal blood flow changes, with no effect on filtration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both fall","why":"Opening the outflow resistor lets blood escape the glomerulus more easily, so capillary pressure drops and filtration falls even though total renal blood flow may increase. A rise in both would follow efferent constriction. Pressure cannot rise when outflow resistance is reduced. Renal blood flow and filtration can move in opposite directions here, which is exactly why filtration is affected."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has lost about two liters of blood. Mean arterial pressure is 60 mmHg (normal 70 to 105), heart rate is 130 beats per minute, and the skin is cold and pale. Urine output over the last hour is 5 mL (normal above 30 mL per hour).\nWhich mechanism best explains the near shutdown of urine production?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Intense sympathetic constriction of the afferent arterioles overrides autoregulation, glomerular capillary pressure falls, and filtration rate falls","why":"Autoregulation holds filtration steady for pressures down to about 80 mmHg, and even then it is an intrinsic mechanism that a strong sympathetic drive can override. In hemorrhage the sympathetic nervous system clamps the afferent arterioles to redirect blood to heart and brain, so glomerular pressure and filtration collapse. Angiotensin II and antidiuretic hormone add to the water retention."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, predict what happens to glomerular filtration rate when the afferent arteriole constricts and when the efferent arteriole constricts, and explain the two intrinsic mechanisms that keep filtration rate steady when arterial pressure changes.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"afferent constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, so filtration rate falls; afferent dilation raises it; moderate efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure, so filtration rate rises; myogenic response: when arterial pressure rises, afferent arteriole smooth muscle stretches and contracts, holding capillary pressure steady; tubuloglomerular feedback: the macula densa senses sodium chloride delivery in the distal tubule; high sodium chloride delivery means filtration is too high, so the macula densa signals the afferent arteriole to constrict (via adenosine or ATP); low sodium chloride delivery allows afferent dilation and stimulates renin release; together these hold filtration rate nearly constant over mean arterial pressures of about 80 to 180 mmHg","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a glomerulus with its afferent and efferent arterioles clearly distinct, then draw four copies: afferent constricted, afferent dilated, efferent constricted, efferent dilated. On each mark what happened to glomerular hydrostatic pressure and to GFR with an up or down arrow. One line: two of the four raise GFR, so name them, and say which of those two cuts total renal blood flow at the same time."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw an arterial pressure trace that dips and then climbs above normal, and off it draw three controls responding: the myogenic response in afferent smooth muscle, tubuloglomerular feedback from the macula densa back to the afferent arteriole, and sympathetic outflow during hemorrhage. Show what each does to arteriole diameter. One line: say which of these gives up when arterial pressure falls far enough, and what takes over when it does."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare myogenic autoregulation, tubuloglomerular feedback, sympathetic control, and hormonal control and predict the effect of afferent and efferent arteriolar constriction on filtration rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Regulation of GFR. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-renal-clearance","name":"Renal clearance","can":"Calculate the clearance of a substance and use inulin and creatinine clearance to estimate glomerular filtration rate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's plasma creatinine rises from 1.0 to 2.0 mg/dL over six months while muscle mass and creatinine production stay constant. Baseline filtration rate was 120 mL/min. What is the approximate current filtration rate?\nA) About 30 mL/min  |  B) About 240 mL/min\nC) About 60 mL/min  |  D) About 120 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 60 mL/min","why":"With constant production, filtration rate and plasma creatinine are inversely related at steady state, so a doubling of plasma creatinine corresponds to a halving of filtration rate, from 120 to about 60 mL/min. Two hundred forty inverts the relationship. One hundred twenty ignores the change entirely. Thirty would require plasma creatinine to have risen four fold, to 4.0 mg/dL."},{"t":"mc","q":"Para aminohippurate clearance is 625 mL/min and inulin clearance is 125 mL/min. What is the filtration fraction?\nA) 5.0  |  B) 0.80\nC) 0.50  |  D) 0.20\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 0.20","why":"Filtration fraction is filtration rate divided by renal plasma flow, and since para aminohippurate clearance estimates plasma flow, the calculation is 125 divided by 625, which is 0.20. Five inverts the ratio and would mean more plasma is filtered than arrives. Point eight is the fraction that leaves in the efferent arteriole unfiltered, not the fraction filtered. Point five would require plasma flow of only 250 mL/min."},{"t":"mc","q":"A 24 hour urine collection totals 2160 mL with a creatinine concentration of 80 mg/dL. Plasma creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL. What is the creatinine clearance?\nA) 120 mL/min  |  B) 100 mL/min\nC) 150 mL/min  |  D) 15 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 150 mL/min","why":"Urine flow is 2160 divided by 1440 minutes, which is 1.5 mL/min, and the urine to plasma ratio is 80 divided by 0.8, which is 100, so the clearance is 100 times 1.5, or 150 mL/min. One hundred twenty would follow from a urine flow of 1.2 mL/min. One hundred uses the ratio without the flow rate. Fifteen misplaces the decimal in the ratio."},{"t":"mc","q":"Urine inulin is 100 mg/mL, urine flow is 1.5 mL/min, and plasma inulin is 1.2 mg/mL. What is the glomerular filtration rate?\nA) 150 mL/min  |  B) 125 mL/min\nC) 180 mL/min  |  D) 80 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) 125 mL/min","why":"Excretion rate is 100 times 1.5, which is 150 mg/min, and dividing by the plasma concentration of 1.2 mg/mL gives 125 mL/min. One hundred fifty is the excretion rate in mg/min, not the clearance. One hundred eighty comes from multiplying by the plasma concentration instead of dividing. Eighty does not follow from any arrangement of these numbers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Urine concentration of a substance is 30 mg/mL, urine flow is 2 mL/min, and plasma concentration is 0.5 mg/mL. What is its clearance?\nA) 15 mL/min  |  B) 30 mL/min\nC) 60 mL/min  |  D) 120 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 120 mL/min","why":"Excretion rate is 30 times 2, which is 60 mg/min, and dividing by 0.5 mg/mL gives 120 mL/min. Thirty repeats the urine concentration. Sixty is the excretion rate in mg/min before dividing by plasma concentration. Fifteen comes from multiplying by 0.5 instead of dividing."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient collects urine for 24 hours. Urine flow works out to 1.2 mL/min and urine creatinine is 100 mg/dL. Plasma creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (normal 0.6 to 1.2).\nWhat is the creatinine clearance, and what does the number tell you?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"120 mL/min, which is close to glomerular filtration rate because creatinine is freely filtered and only slightly secreted","why":"Clearance is urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration: 100 times 1.2 divided by 1.0 equals 120 mL/min. Creatinine is filtered freely and not reabsorbed, so its clearance tracks filtration rate. A small amount is secreted, so the estimate runs a little high, but it is the standard bedside measure of filtration rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the clearance equation as a picture rather than symbols: a urine collection with its concentration and its volume per minute, an arrow to a plasma sample with its concentration, and the volume of plasma cleared per minute coming out. Then draw a nephron with inulin passing through, filtered, not reabsorbed, not secreted. Write inulin's clearance next to normal GFR near 125 mL/min. One line: say why inulin has to be infused while creatinine does not."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two nephrons side by side, one handling inulin and one handling creatinine, with every arrow that actually happens on each. Creatinine gets a small secretion arrow. Under each, draw a bar for its measured clearance against a marked line at the true GFR. One line: say which direction creatinine clearance is wrong as a GFR estimate, and whether that error makes a failing kidney look better or worse than it is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate the clearance of a substance and use inulin and creatinine clearance to estimate glomerular filtration rate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Renal clearance. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-clearance-inference","name":"Clearance and handling inference","can":"Use a clearance value relative to glomerular filtration rate to determine whether a substance is net reabsorbed or net secreted.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Glomerular filtration rate is 120 mL/min and the clearance of substance A is 20 mL/min. How is A handled?\nA) It is net reabsorbed  |  B) It is neither reabsorbed nor secreted\nC) It is not filtered  |  D) It is net secreted\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is net reabsorbed","why":"A clearance of 20 mL/min against a filtration rate of 120 mL/min means only about one sixth of the filtered load reached the urine, so five sixths was reabsorbed. Secretion would raise the clearance above 120. Equality at 120 would indicate no tubular handling. Being unfiltered would give a clearance near zero unless secretion occurred."},{"t":"mc","q":"Inulin clearance is 125 mL/min and the clearance of substance C is also 125 mL/min. What can you conclude about C?\nA) It is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule and secreted in the distal tubule\nB) It is freely filtered and not handled by the tubule\nC) It is filtered and then equally secreted and metabolized\nD) It is heavily protein bound\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is freely filtered and not handled by the tubule","why":"Matching inulin exactly is the signature of a substance that enters by filtration and is then left alone all the way down the nephron. Secretion balanced by metabolism is possible in principle but is not the simple interpretation and would be unusual physiology. Heavy protein binding would lower the filtered load and give a clearance well below the inulin value. Equal reabsorption and secretion in different segments is speculative and not what the matching numbers indicate."},{"t":"mc","q":"The fractional excretion of sodium is 0.5 percent. What fraction of the filtered sodium is reabsorbed?\nA) 99.5 percent  |  B) 0.5 percent\nC) 95 percent  |  D) 50 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 99.5 percent","why":"Fractional excretion is the share that leaves in urine, so the rest is reclaimed, and 100 minus 0.5 gives 99.5 percent reabsorbed. Fifty percent confuses the decimal point in the percentage. Half a percent is the excreted share, not the reabsorbed share. Ninety five percent would correspond to a fractional excretion of 5 percent."},{"t":"mc","q":"A substance is freely filtered but its clearance is zero. What is the tubule doing?\nA) Reabsorbing half the filtered load\nB) Secreting the substance heavily\nC) Reabsorbing all of the filtered load\nD) Blocking it at the filtration membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Reabsorbing all of the filtered load","why":"Zero clearance means nothing appears in urine, and since the substance did enter the tubule by filtration, all of it must have been reabsorbed, which is the normal fate of glucose. Secretion would produce a large clearance. Blockage at the membrane is ruled out by the statement that it is freely filtered. Reabsorbing only half would leave a measurable clearance."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A new drug is being studied. Plasma concentration is 0.5 mg/mL, urine concentration is 20 mg/mL, and urine flow is 1 mL/min. Inulin clearance in the same patient is 100 mL/min.\nWhat is the net tubular handling of this drug?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Net reabsorption of 30 mg/min; its clearance of 40 mL/min is below the filtration rate","why":"Filtered load is 100 mL/min times 0.5 mg/mL, which is 50 mg/min. Excretion is 20 mg/mL times 1 mL/min, which is 20 mg/min. The tubule took back 30 mg/min. Clearance is 20 times 1 divided by 0.5, which is 40 mL/min, well below the inulin value of 100, which confirms net reabsorption."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A substance whose clearance is higher than inulin clearance is being net reabsorbed by the tubule.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Clearance above inulin clearance means net secretion; reabsorption pulls clearance below the inulin value.","why":"Inulin clearance equals glomerular filtration rate because inulin is filtered and then left alone. Any substance cleared faster than that must have had more added to the urine than filtration supplied, which is secretion. Any substance cleared slower had some taken back. Glucose, at zero clearance, is the extreme of reabsorption; para aminohippurate, cleared at nearly the whole plasma flow, is the extreme of secretion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a number line for clearance in mL/min with GFR marked at about 125. Place inulin, glucose, sodium, creatinine, and PAH on it. Above the line write net reabsorbed, neither, or net secreted over the right zones. Beside each substance draw the single nephron arrow that puts it where you placed it. One line: pick the substance whose position moves in a diabetic with plasma glucose near 300 mg/dL, and say which way it moves."},{"t":"draw","q":"You are handed three unknowns with clearances of 0, 125, and 400 mL/min in a person with normal GFR. Draw a nephron for each and put in only the arrows those numbers force. Beside each, name a real substance that behaves that way. One line: a clearance of 60 mL/min is ambiguous, so give the two different tubular stories that could both produce it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to use a clearance value relative to glomerular filtration rate to determine whether a substance is net reabsorbed or net secreted."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Clearance and handling inference. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-proximal-reabsorption","name":"Proximal tubule reabsorption","can":"Explain how the sodium gradient drives reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, and water in the proximal tubule and state why reabsorption there is described as isosmotic.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks the Na+/K+ ATPase in proximal tubule cells. What happens to glucose reabsorption?\nA) It rises, because more sodium stays inside the cell to drive the carrier\nB) It falls, because the sodium gradient that powers SGLT collapses\nC) It is unchanged, because SGLT uses ATP directly\nD) It falls, because glucose can no longer be filtered\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It falls, because the sodium gradient that powers SGLT collapses","why":"SGLT depends on sodium being much lower inside the cell than in the tubular fluid, and the pump is what maintains that difference, so blocking the pump removes the driving force. Sodium building up inside the cell weakens the inward gradient rather than strengthening it. SGLT does not hydrolyze ATP, so it is not independent of the pump. Filtration of glucose is pressure driven at the glomerulus and is unaffected by a tubular pump."},{"t":"mc","q":"Amino acid reabsorption in the proximal tubule depends on which gradient?\nA) The calcium gradient across the basolateral membrane\nB) The chloride gradient established by the loop of Henle\nC) The inward sodium gradient across the apical membrane\nD) The outward potassium gradient across the apical membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The inward sodium gradient across the apical membrane","why":"Amino acids are pulled into the cell by sodium coupled symporters, the same strategy used for glucose, so both share a dependence on the sodium gradient. Potassium moves outward and is not coupled to amino acid uptake. Calcium gradients drive signaling, not bulk amino acid recovery. The loop of Henle acts downstream and cannot supply a gradient to the proximal tubule."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the osmolarity of tubular fluid at the end of the proximal tubule compared with plasma?\nA) Much higher, near 1200 mOsm/L  |  B) Slightly higher, near 600 mOsm/L\nC) Much lower, near 100 mOsm/L  |  D) About the same, near 300 mOsm/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) About the same, near 300 mOsm/L","why":"Isosmotic reabsorption removes solute and water in nearly equal proportion, so the volume shrinks while the concentration stays near plasma values. One hundred mOsm/L is what leaves the thick ascending limb, the diluting segment. Twelve hundred mOsm/L is reached at the bend of a long loop. Six hundred mOsm/L would require water to be reabsorbed faster than solute, which this segment does not do."},{"t":"mc","q":"If 125 mL/min is filtered, about how much fluid per minute leaves the proximal tubule and enters the loop of Henle?\nA) About 1 mL/min  |  B) About 81 mL/min\nC) About 44 mL/min  |  D) About 125 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 44 mL/min","why":"With about 65 percent reabsorbed, roughly 35 percent of 125 mL/min remains, which is about 44 mL/min. Eighty one mL/min is the amount reabsorbed, not the amount remaining. One hundred twenty five mL/min would mean no reabsorption occurred at all. One mL/min is the final urine flow after the entire nephron has acted."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has a proximal tubule disorder that impairs the energy supply of the basolateral sodium potassium ATPase. Urinalysis shows glucose, amino acids, phosphate and bicarbonate in the urine. Plasma glucose is 95 mg/dL (normal 70 to 100).\nWhy do all of these substances spill into the urine together?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Their reabsorption depends on the sodium gradient set by the basolateral pump, so one failure lets all of them escape","why":"Glucose, amino acids, phosphate and bicarbonate are all recovered by transporters that use sodium moving down its gradient into the cell. That gradient exists only because the basolateral pump keeps intracellular sodium low. Starve the pump and every sodium coupled cotransporter slows at once, so the substances that ride on sodium spill together, even at normal plasma levels."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose, amino acids, and water even though it has no pump for any of them, and why the fluid leaving the proximal tubule is still about 300 mOsm/L.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the basolateral sodium potassium ATPase keeps intracellular sodium low, creating a gradient for sodium to enter from the lumen; sodium enters the cell through apical cotransporters that carry glucose or amino acids with it (secondary active transport); glucose and amino acids are moved against their gradients using the energy of the sodium gradient, then leave the cell across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; solute reabsorption makes the fluid on the blood side slightly more concentrated than the lumen; the proximal epithelium is highly water permeable (aquaporin 1 and leaky junctions), so water follows the solute by osmosis; water follows solute in proportion, so the tubular fluid osmolarity stays at about 300 mOsm/L (isosmotic reabsorption); about two thirds of filtered sodium and water and essentially all glucose and amino acids are reabsorbed here","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one proximal tubule cell with lumen on one side and blood on the other. Put the sodium potassium ATPase on the basolateral membrane and show the sodium gradient it creates. On the apical membrane draw the cotransporters that ride that gradient, sodium with glucose and sodium with an amino acid. Then draw water following. One line: say what happens to glucose reabsorption within minutes if you poison that pump, given that glucose never touches it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the proximal tubule as a tube and plot two things along its length: the volume of fluid remaining in it, and the osmolarity of that fluid starting near 290 mOsm/L. Show roughly two thirds of the water and sodium gone by the end and the osmolarity line flat. Draw where the water left. One line: say what would have to be true of the wall for that osmolarity line to climb instead, and name a segment where it does."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the sodium gradient drives reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, and water in the proximal tubule and state why reabsorption there is described as isosmotic."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Proximal tubule reabsorption. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-renal-threshold","name":"Transport maximum and renal threshold","can":"Distinguish transport maximum from renal threshold and explain the appearance of glucose in urine when plasma glucose is elevated.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Glomerular filtration rate is 100 mL/min, plasma glucose is 600 mg/dL, and the transport maximum is 300 mg/min. What is the rate of glucose excretion?\nA) 0 mg/min  |  B) 600 mg/min\nC) 300 mg/min  |  D) 100 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 300 mg/min","why":"A plasma glucose of 600 mg/dL is 6 mg/mL, so the filtered load is 6 times 100, which is 600 mg/min, and subtracting the 300 mg/min that the carriers can reclaim leaves 300 mg/min excreted. Six hundred is the filtered load before any reabsorption. One hundred is the filtration rate in mL/min, not a mass rate. Zero would require the filtered load to stay below the transport maximum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Plasma glucose is 400 mg/dL, glomerular filtration rate is 125 mL/min, and the transport maximum is 375 mg/min. What is the glucose excretion rate?\nA) 0 mg/min  |  B) 500 mg/min\nC) 375 mg/min  |  D) 125 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 125 mg/min","why":"A plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL is 4 mg/mL, so the filtered load is 4 times 125, which is 500 mg/min, and subtracting the 375 mg/min the carriers can recover leaves 125 mg/min in the urine. Five hundred is the filtered load before reabsorption. Three hundred seventy five is the amount reabsorbed. Zero would require the filtered load to stay under the transport maximum."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the transport maximum for a substance describe?\nA) The plasma concentration at which the substance first appears in urine\nB) The fastest rate at which the tubular carriers can move that substance\nC) The highest concentration the substance can reach in the filtrate\nD) The amount of the substance filtered each minute\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The fastest rate at which the tubular carriers can move that substance","why":"Transport maximum is a saturation point of the carrier system, expressed as an amount per unit time, and once it is reached extra substance stays in the tubule. The plasma concentration at which the substance first appears in urine is the renal threshold, a related but different idea. Filtrate concentration matches plasma for a freely filtered solute and has no ceiling of this kind. The amount filtered each minute is the filtered load."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate transport maximum for glucose in a healthy adult?\nA) About 125 mg/min  |  B) About 180 mg/min\nC) About 375 mg/min  |  D) About 600 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 375 mg/min","why":"The glucose carriers saturate at roughly 375 mg/min, which is about three times the filtered load at normal plasma glucose. One hundred twenty five mg/min is the filtered load at a normal plasma glucose of 100 mg/dL. One hundred eighty is the renal threshold in mg/dL, a concentration rather than a rate. Six hundred mg/min overstates the capacity of the transporters."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the approximate renal threshold for glucose?\nA) About 180 mg/dL of plasma glucose  |  B) About 375 mg/dL of plasma glucose\nC) About 600 mg/dL of plasma glucose  |  D) About 100 mg/dL of plasma glucose\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) About 180 mg/dL of plasma glucose","why":"Glucose starts appearing in urine when plasma glucose climbs above roughly 180 mg/dL. One hundred mg/dL is a normal fasting plasma glucose, well below the threshold, so no glucose appears. Three hundred seventy five is the transport maximum in mg/min, not a plasma concentration. Six hundred mg/dL is far above the threshold and would produce heavy glucose loss."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with untreated diabetes has a plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL (normal 70 to 100). Glomerular filtration rate is 125 mL/min and the glucose transport maximum is 375 mg/min.\nPredict the rate of glucose excretion and the effect on urine volume.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"About 125 mg/min of glucose is excreted, and urine volume rises because the glucose holds water in the tubule","why":"Plasma glucose of 400 mg/dL is 4 mg/mL, so the filtered load is 4 times 125, which is 500 mg/min. The carriers can reclaim at most 375 mg/min, so 125 mg/min escapes. That unreabsorbed glucose is an osmotically active solute that keeps water in the tubule, producing the large urine volume of untreated diabetes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes with plasma glucose on the x and rate on the y, and plot three curves: filtered load rising as a straight line, reabsorption rising then flattening at the transport maximum near 375 mg/min, and excretion flat at zero then lifting off. Mark the renal threshold near 180 mg/dL where excretion leaves zero. One line: say why the threshold sits below the plasma level at which every transporter is finally saturated."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the proximal tubule wall with a fixed number of glucose carriers on it, say six, and draw three cases passing by: normal plasma glucose, plasma glucose near 200 mg/dL, and plasma glucose near 400 mg/dL. Show carriers occupied, glucose left in the lumen, and water dragged along with it. One line: say why the patient in the third panel is thirsty and urinating heavily, and name the force pulling that water."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish transport maximum from renal threshold and explain the appearance of glucose in urine when plasma glucose is elevated."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Transport maximum and renal threshold. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-tubular-secretion","name":"Tubular secretion","can":"State the purpose of tubular secretion and name substances secreted including hydrogen ion, potassium, and organic anions such as drugs.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's blood pH falls during a period of heavy exercise. How does the tubule respond?\nA) It secretes less H+ and more HCO3-\nB) It stops secreting K+ and secretes Na+ instead\nC) It reabsorbs more H+ from the tubular fluid\nD) It secretes more H+ into the tubular fluid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It secretes more H+ into the tubular fluid","why":"Acidosis means excess H+ in the blood, and the tubule responds by pushing more H+ into the lumen so the acid leaves in the urine while HCO3- is returned to blood. Secreting bicarbonate is the response to alkalosis, when the body has too much base. Reabsorbing H+ would worsen the acidosis by adding acid back to the blood. Na+ is reabsorbed, not secreted, and swapping it for K+ secretion is not how the tubule handles an acid load."},{"t":"mc","q":"A substance has a renal clearance greater than the glomerular filtration rate. What does this tell you about its handling?\nA) It is reabsorbed after filtration\nB) It is neither filtered nor secreted\nC) It is filtered only, with no secretion or reabsorption\nD) It is secreted in addition to being filtered\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It is secreted in addition to being filtered","why":"Clearance above the filtration rate means more of the substance leaves the blood than filtration alone could account for, so the tubule must be adding it to the urine by secretion. Reabsorption pulls the substance back and drops clearance below the filtration rate. A clearance exactly equal to the filtration rate is the signature of a purely filtered marker such as inulin. A substance that is neither filtered nor secreted would have a clearance near zero."},{"t":"mc","q":"In tubular secretion, which way does a substance move?\nA) From tubular fluid into peritubular capillary blood\nB) From glomerular capillary blood into the capsular space\nC) From peritubular capillary blood into the tubular fluid\nD) From tubular fluid into the interstitium and then into the loop of Henle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) From peritubular capillary blood into the tubular fluid","why":"Secretion adds material to the forming urine after filtration, so the substance leaves the blood of the peritubular capillaries and crosses the tubule cells into the lumen. Movement from tubular fluid back into blood is reabsorption, the opposite process. Movement from glomerular blood into the capsular space is filtration, which happens upstream at the renal corpuscle. The fourth option runs the wrong way and puts the fluid back into an earlier segment, which the nephron does not do."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ion is secreted by the tubule to help regulate blood pH?\nA) H+  |  B) Ca2+\nC) Cl-  |  D) Na+\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) H+","why":"Secreting H+ into the tubular fluid removes acid from the body and is the kidney's main lever on blood pH. Ca2+ handling is dominated by reabsorption under parathyroid hormone control, not by secretion for pH. Cl- follows Na+ during reabsorption and is not the acid the kidney excretes. Na+ is heavily reabsorbed rather than secreted, since losing it would cost the body water and blood volume."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tubule cells carry out most potassium secretion?\nA) Intercalated cells of the collecting duct\nB) Cells of the thick ascending limb\nC) Cells of the descending limb of the loop of Henle\nD) Principal cells of the collecting duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Principal cells of the collecting duct","why":"Principal cells have apical potassium channels and basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, so they load K+ from blood and let it leak into the lumen, and this step is the one aldosterone controls. Intercalated cells are the acid and base handlers, secreting H+ or HCO3-. The thick ascending limb reabsorbs K+ through the NKCC2 cotransporter rather than secreting it. The descending limb moves water, not K+."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Organic anions such as penicillin leave the body only by filtration, so their clearance can never exceed glomerular filtration rate.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Organic anions are actively secreted by the proximal tubule from the peritubular blood, so their clearance can be much higher than glomerular filtration rate.","why":"Only about a fifth of the plasma entering the kidney is filtered. Secretory carriers in the proximal tubule reach into the other four fifths and pull organic anions and cations into the lumen. That is why para aminohippurate is cleared almost completely in one pass and why penicillin doses had to be given so often before drugs were found to block the carrier."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a distal nephron with its peritubular capillary alongside and four secretion arrows crossing from blood into lumen: hydrogen ion, potassium, ammonium, and an organic anion such as penicillin. Beside each arrow write why that substance is secreted rather than simply left in the filtrate. One line: say what secretion can do for a drug bound to plasma protein that filtration cannot do at all."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a secreted drug that is not penicillin and draw the nephron twice for it. In the first copy show its route through filtration and secretion into the urine. In the second, block the secretion transporter and draw a small graph of what its plasma level does over the next few hours. One line: say what dose change you would make for that second patient, and in which direction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the purpose of tubular secretion and name substances secreted including hydrogen ion, potassium, and organic anions such as drugs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Tubular secretion. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-countercurrent","name":"Countercurrent mechanism","can":"Explain how the countercurrent multiplier of the loop of Henle and the countercurrent exchanger of the vasa recta build and preserve the medullary osmotic gradient.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Blood flow through the vasa recta increases sharply. Predict the effect on the medullary gradient.\nA) The gradient becomes steeper\nB) The gradient is unaffected because the vasa recta are impermeable\nC) The gradient washes out and weakens\nD) The gradient shifts to the cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The gradient washes out and weakens","why":"Slow flow is what lets the descending and ascending vasa recta trade solute and water with each other, and fast flow carries medullary solute away before it can be handed back, which is called medullary washout. Faster flow removes solute rather than concentrating it, so the gradient cannot steepen. The vasa recta are quite permeable to water and solute, which is exactly why flow rate matters. The gradient lives in the medulla by anatomy and does not relocate."},{"t":"mc","q":"An animal is bred with unusually short loops of Henle. What would you predict about its maximum urine concentration?\nA) It would be higher than normal\nB) It would be unchanged because the vasa recta compensate\nC) It would be unchanged because urea compensates\nD) It would be lower than normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It would be lower than normal","why":"Longer loops multiply the single effect over more vertical distance, so shorter loops build a shallower gradient and the collecting duct has less osmotic pull to concentrate urine against. Shorter loops cannot produce a deeper gradient. Urea recycling depends on the concentrating machinery of the loop and collecting duct, so it cannot make up the shortfall. The vasa recta only preserve a gradient that already exists and cannot create one."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the permeability of the thin descending limb of the loop of Henle?\nA) Impermeable to water and permeable to NaCl\nB) Permeable to water and largely impermeable to NaCl\nC) Permeable to both water and NaCl\nD) Impermeable to both water and NaCl\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Permeable to water and largely impermeable to NaCl","why":"Water leaves the descending limb into the salty medulla while the salt stays behind, which is why the fluid at the hairpin turn is the most concentrated in the nephron. Being impermeable to water and permeable to NaCl describes the ascending limb, the opposite half of the loop. If both moved freely, no osmotic difference could build between lumen and interstitium. If neither moved, the loop could not concentrate anything at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle do?\nA) It allows water out of the lumen and holds NaCl inside the tubule\nB) It pumps NaCl out into the interstitium and is impermeable to water\nC) It pumps NaCl into the lumen and lets water follow it osmotically\nD) It is freely permeable to water, NaCl, and urea in both directions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It pumps NaCl out into the interstitium and is impermeable to water","why":"Active NaCl transport out of a water tight tube is the single effect that makes the interstitium salty and the tubular fluid dilute, and it is the engine of the whole multiplier. Letting water out while holding salt in describes the descending limb. Pumping NaCl into the lumen would dilute the medulla rather than concentrate it. Free permeability to everything would let all gradients collapse immediately."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The vasa recta build the medullary osmotic gradient by actively pumping salt into the interstitium.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The vasa recta do no pumping; they are a passive countercurrent exchanger whose hairpin shape keeps blood from washing away the gradient that the loop of Henle builds.","why":"Building the gradient takes energy, and that energy comes from the sodium pumps of the thick ascending limb. The vasa recta only preserve it. Blood going down picks up salt and loses water, then blood coming back up gives the salt back and regains water, so the blood leaving the medulla is only slightly more concentrated than the blood entering it."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the loop of Henle builds the osmotic gradient of the renal medulla, and how the vasa recta keep blood flow from washing that gradient away.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"the thick ascending limb actively pumps sodium chloride into the interstitium and is impermeable to water; the descending limb is permeable to water, so water leaves and its fluid becomes more concentrated as it descends; the concentrated fluid delivered to the ascending limb lets the single pumping step be repeated and stacked along the loop (multiplication); the result is about 300 mOsm/L at the top of the medulla and about 1200 mOsm/L at the tip; urea recycled from the collecting duct adds to the gradient in the inner medulla; the vasa recta are hairpin loops, so solute that enters the descending vessel diffuses back out of the ascending vessel and water does the reverse; blood leaving the medulla is only slightly more concentrated than blood entering, so the gradient is preserved; slow flow helps","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a loop of Henle in the medulla with an osmolarity scale down the side, about 300 mOsm/L at the cortex to about 1200 mOsm/L at the papilla. Mark the descending limb as permeable to water and not solute, and the thick ascending limb as pumping sodium chloride out while staying water impermeable. Write fluid osmolarity at the top, the bend, and the exit. One line: fluid leaving the ascending limb is more dilute than plasma, so say what that buys."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the vasa recta as a hairpin running alongside the loop, blood flowing down and back up through the gradient. Show water and solute crossing at each level, and mark the osmolarity of blood entering and leaving. Then draw what the medullary gradient would look like if that vessel ran straight through instead of hairpinning. One line: say which structure is the multiplier and which is only the exchanger, and what the difference between those two words is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how the countercurrent multiplier of the loop of Henle and the countercurrent exchanger of the vasa recta build and preserve the medullary osmotic gradient."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Countercurrent mechanism. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-adh-water-balance","name":"Antidiuretic hormone and urine concentration","can":"Trace the response to dehydration from osmoreceptor firing through antidiuretic hormone release to aquaporin insertion and concentrated urine.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient produces large volumes of dilute urine, and giving synthetic antidiuretic hormone corrects it. Which problem does this identify?\nA) The collecting duct cannot respond to the hormone\nB) The medullary gradient has been washed out\nC) The pituitary is not releasing enough hormone\nD) The glomerular filtration rate is abnormally high\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The pituitary is not releasing enough hormone","why":"Responding to the replacement hormone proves the kidney machinery works and the missing piece was the hormone itself, which is central diabetes insipidus. If the collecting duct could not respond, giving more hormone would change nothing, which is the nephrogenic form. A washed out gradient would also blunt the response to given hormone. A high filtration rate is not corrected by antidiuretic hormone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Antidiuretic hormone is secreted in very large amounts inappropriately. Predict the plasma sodium concentration.\nA) It rises, because sodium is reabsorbed with the water\nB) It is unchanged, because the hormone moves only water\nC) It falls, because water is retained and dilutes the plasma\nD) It rises, because the medullary gradient pulls sodium into the blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It falls, because water is retained and dilutes the plasma","why":"The hormone moves water without sodium, so retained water dilutes the sodium already in the plasma and the measured concentration drops. Sodium reabsorption is the job of aldosterone, not of this hormone. Moving only water is exactly why sodium concentration changes; the numerator stays put while the denominator grows. The medullary gradient pulls water out of the collecting duct, not sodium into the blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where are the osmoreceptors that detect a rise in plasma osmolarity?\nA) In the carotid sinus  |  B) In the posterior pituitary\nC) In the juxtaglomerular apparatus  |  D) In the hypothalamus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) In the hypothalamus","why":"Hypothalamic osmoreceptors shrink when plasma osmolarity rises and increase their firing, which is the first step of the dehydration response. The carotid sinus holds baroreceptors for arterial pressure and chemoreceptors for blood gases, not osmoreceptors. The juxtaglomerular apparatus senses perfusion pressure and distal NaCl for renin release. The posterior pituitary stores and releases the hormone but does not do the sensing."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A hiker has had no water for eight hours on a 35 degrees Celsius (95 degrees Fahrenheit) day. Plasma osmolarity is 298 mOsm/kg (normal 280 to 295), plasma sodium is 148 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), urine osmolarity is 1000 mOsm/kg, and urine output is 20 mL per hour (normal above 30).\nWhich sequence explains the concentrated, low volume urine?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Hypothalamic osmoreceptors shrink and fire, the posterior pituitary releases antidiuretic hormone, aquaporin 2 is inserted in the collecting duct, and water is drawn into the hypertonic medulla","why":"Sweat is hypotonic, so losing it concentrates the plasma. Osmoreceptor cells in the hypothalamus lose water and shrink, which increases their firing, and the posterior pituitary releases antidiuretic hormone. The hormone acts on V2 receptors in collecting duct principal cells, aquaporin 2 channels move into the apical membrane, and water follows the medullary gradient back into blood. A small volume of very concentrated urine is the result."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Antidiuretic hormone concentrates the urine by making the ascending limb of the loop of Henle permeable to water.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Antidiuretic hormone acts on the collecting duct, where it inserts aquaporin 2 channels so water can leave the tubule and enter the hyperosmotic medulla; the ascending limb stays water impermeable no matter what.","why":"The ascending limb has to stay watertight or the gradient could never form, because water would follow the pumped salt right back out. The hormone's target is the last segment, the collecting duct, which runs through the salty medulla. With aquaporins in place water is drawn out and the urine concentrates; without them it stays dilute."},{"t":"bd","q":"A person works outdoors on a 38 degrees Celsius (100 degrees Fahrenheit) day and sweats heavily without drinking. Without notes, trace the response from the change in plasma to the change in urine, naming the sensor, the hormone, its target, and the channel involved.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"sweat loss raises plasma osmolarity (and lowers volume); hypothalamic osmoreceptors shrink and fire more; antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) is released from the posterior pituitary; thirst is also stimulated; the hormone binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells; aquaporin 2 water channels are inserted into the apical membrane; water leaves the collecting duct into the hyperosmotic medulla and returns to blood; a small volume of concentrated urine is produced and plasma osmolarity falls back toward normal","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the dehydration chain as boxes and arrows: plasma osmolarity climbing above about 290 mOsm/L, hypothalamic osmoreceptors firing, ADH released from the posterior pituitary, and the collecting duct cell responding. Draw that last cell in detail, with aquaporins moving from vesicles into the apical membrane and water following. Show the urine leaving, small and concentrated. One line: name the second stimulus that can release ADH, and give a case where it overrides osmolarity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one collecting duct twice running down through the medullary gradient, once with maximal ADH and once with none. On each draw the aquaporins present or absent, the water arrows, and the volume and osmolarity of urine leaving the bottom. Use real urine osmolarity numbers if you can. One line: the medullary gradient is identical in both drawings, so say exactly what changed and where it changed."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the response to dehydration from osmoreceptor firing through antidiuretic hormone release to aquaporin insertion and concentrated urine."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Antidiuretic hormone and urine concentration. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-raas","name":"Renin angiotensin aldosterone system","can":"Trace the renin angiotensin aldosterone pathway from the stimulus for renin release to the effects of angiotensin II and aldosterone on vessels, tubules, and blood pressure.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An adrenal tumor secretes aldosterone continuously and independently of angiotensin II. Predict the pattern of renin, plasma potassium, and blood pressure.\nA) Renin high, potassium high, blood pressure low\nB) Renin high, potassium low, blood pressure high\nC) Renin low, potassium low, blood pressure high\nD) Renin low, potassium high, blood pressure low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Renin low, potassium low, blood pressure high","why":"Unregulated aldosterone retains sodium and water, which raises pressure, and the higher pressure feeds back on the granular cells to shut renin off, while the same aldosterone drives potassium into the urine. High renin would mean the pathway is being driven from above, which is not the case with an autonomous tumor. Potassium cannot be high while aldosterone is high, since the hormone promotes its secretion. Blood pressure rises rather than falls with sodium retention."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drug blocks angiotensin converting enzyme. Predict the effect on blood pressure and on aldosterone.\nA) Blood pressure falls and aldosterone falls\nB) Blood pressure rises and aldosterone rises\nC) Blood pressure falls and aldosterone rises\nD) Blood pressure rises and aldosterone falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Blood pressure falls and aldosterone falls","why":"Without the converting enzyme, angiotensin II is not made, so the vasoconstriction is lost and the adrenal stimulus for aldosterone disappears, and both pressure and aldosterone fall together. Blocking the pathway cannot raise pressure. Aldosterone cannot rise when its main driver is removed, unless plasma potassium climbs enough to stimulate it separately. Both effects follow from the same missing molecule, so they move in the same direction."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient is put on a very low sodium diet. Predict the change in renin and aldosterone.\nA) Both fall, because there is less sodium to handle\nB) Renin rises but aldosterone falls\nC) Both rise, to conserve the sodium that is available\nD) Renin falls but aldosterone rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both rise, to conserve the sodium that is available","why":"Low sodium intake lowers extracellular volume and reduces sodium chloride at the macula densa, both of which increase renin, and the extra angiotensin II then drives aldosterone up so the kidney holds onto sodium. Falling levels would waste the scarce sodium. Aldosterone follows angiotensin II in this setting, so renin and aldosterone move together. Renin falling with low sodium would be the reverse of the sensing mechanism."},{"t":"mc","q":"Besides its vascular and adrenal effects, angiotensin II acts on the brain. What does that produce?\nA) Suppression of thirst and reduced antidiuretic hormone release\nB) Increased respiratory rate and reduced thirst\nC) Thirst and increased antidiuretic hormone release\nD) Reduced sympathetic outflow to the heart\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Thirst and increased antidiuretic hormone release","why":"Angiotensin II acts at the hypothalamus to make the person drink and to increase water retention, both of which add volume and support blood pressure. Suppressing thirst would work against the purpose of the pathway. Angiotensin II does not act as a respiratory stimulant. It raises sympathetic outflow rather than reducing it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with high blood pressure starts a drug that blocks angiotensin converting enzyme. Two weeks later blood pressure has fallen from 160/100 to 130/85 mmHg, and plasma potassium has risen from 4.0 to 5.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0).\nWhich mechanism explains both the fall in pressure and the rise in potassium?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Less angiotensin II means less arteriolar constriction and less aldosterone, so sodium retention and vasoconstriction fall, and the collecting duct secretes less potassium","why":"Angiotensin II does two things that matter here: it constricts arterioles and it drives aldosterone release. Block its formation and peripheral resistance falls, so pressure falls. With less aldosterone the collecting duct principal cells reabsorb less sodium, the lumen is less negative, and less potassium is secreted, so plasma potassium drifts up. This is the expected side effect of the drug class."},{"t":"bd","q":"A person donates a unit of blood and their blood pressure dips. Without notes, trace the renin angiotensin aldosterone response from the signals that release renin to the recovery of blood pressure.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"renin is released by granular cells of the afferent arteriole in response to low pressure, sympathetic stimulation, and low sodium chloride at the macula densa; renin converts angiotensinogen (from the liver) to angiotensin I; angiotensin converting enzyme in the lungs converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II; angiotensin II constricts arterioles, raising peripheral resistance and blood pressure, and constricts the efferent arteriole to defend filtration; angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex, plus antidiuretic hormone release and thirst; aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption (and potassium secretion) in the distal tubule and collecting duct, and water follows the sodium; volume and pressure rise, which removes the stimulus for renin release","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the chain starting at the stimulus, low blood pressure or low sodium chloride sensed at the macula densa, then granular cells releasing renin, angiotensinogen from the liver becoming angiotensin I, and ACE in the lung making angiotensin II. From angiotensin II draw four arrows to arterioles, adrenal cortex, hypothalamus, and proximal tubule, with the effect at each. One line: name the step a common blood pressure drug blocks, and say what happens downstream of it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a collecting duct principal cell with aldosterone arriving, entering the nucleus, and increasing two proteins: the apical sodium channel and the basolateral sodium potassium pump. Show sodium moving, potassium moving, and water following. Then draw the same cell in someone whose adrenal cortex has failed. One line: predict that person's blood pressure and plasma potassium, and say which of the two would kill them first."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the renin angiotensin aldosterone pathway from the stimulus for renin release to the effects of angiotensin II and aldosterone on vessels, tubules, and blood pressure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Renin angiotensin aldosterone system. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-natriuretic-peptides","name":"Natriuretic peptides","can":"Explain how atrial natriuretic peptide opposes the renin angiotensin aldosterone system and state the stimulus for its release.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient receives a large intravenous saline infusion. Predict the change in atrial natriuretic peptide and in urine sodium.\nA) The peptide falls and urine sodium falls\nB) The peptide rises and urine sodium rises\nC) The peptide rises and urine sodium falls\nD) The peptide falls and urine sodium rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The peptide rises and urine sodium rises","why":"Extra volume stretches the atria, release goes up, and the kidney answers by dumping sodium, which pulls water with it and returns volume toward normal. A fall in the peptide would follow volume loss, not volume loading. High peptide levels with low urine sodium contradicts the hormone's main action. Urine sodium rising while the peptide falls would need a different explanation entirely."},{"t":"mc","q":"Atrial natriuretic peptide and aldosterone are compared side by side. Which pair of effects is correct?\nA) Both increase urinary sodium loss\nB) The peptide increases urinary sodium loss while aldosterone decreases it\nC) Both decrease urinary sodium loss\nD) The peptide decreases urinary sodium loss while aldosterone increases it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The peptide increases urinary sodium loss while aldosterone decreases it","why":"The two hormones are opposites on sodium handling, which is what lets the body correct volume in either direction. If both increased sodium loss, nothing would defend blood volume when it fell. If both decreased it, volume overload could not be corrected. The last option reverses each hormone's action."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cells release atrial natriuretic peptide?\nA) Granular cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus\nB) Endothelial cells of the renal arteries\nC) Myocytes of the cardiac atria\nD) Cells of the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Myocytes of the cardiac atria","why":"Atrial muscle cells store this peptide in granules and release it when the atrial wall is stretched, which makes the heart itself an endocrine sensor of blood volume. Renal endothelium releases nitric oxide and other local vasodilators, not this peptide. Granular cells release renin, the hormone this peptide opposes. The zona glomerulosa releases aldosterone, which this peptide inhibits."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the stimulus for atrial natriuretic peptide release?\nA) A fall in blood volume that reduces venous return\nB) A rise in plasma potassium above the normal range\nC) Stretch of the atrial wall from increased blood volume\nD) A fall in arterial PO2 sensed by chemoreceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Stretch of the atrial wall from increased blood volume","why":"More volume returning to the heart stretches the atria further, and that stretch is the signal to release the peptide so the kidney can shed the extra salt and water. A fall in blood volume does the opposite, reducing release. Plasma potassium is the direct stimulus for aldosterone from the adrenal cortex. Arterial PO2 is monitored by chemoreceptors that adjust ventilation."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the word natriuretic mean?\nA) Promoting sodium retention in the blood\nB) Promoting sodium loss in the urine\nC) Promoting potassium loss in the urine\nD) Promoting water retention without sodium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Promoting sodium loss in the urine","why":"Natrium is sodium and uresis is urination, so a natriuretic agent makes the kidney put sodium into the urine, and water follows it out. Retaining sodium is what aldosterone does, which is the opposite. Potassium loss in the urine is kaliuresis, a different term. Retaining water without sodium describes the action of antidiuretic hormone."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Atrial natriuretic peptide is released when blood volume falls and the atria are less stretched.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Atrial natriuretic peptide is released when blood volume rises and stretches the atria; it then promotes sodium and water loss to bring volume back down.","why":"The peptide is the volume overload signal. Falling volume does the opposite: atrial stretch falls, the peptide falls, and the renin angiotensin aldosterone system takes over to conserve salt and water. Getting the stimulus backwards flips every downstream prediction."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a heart with the atria stretched by high blood volume, releasing atrial natriuretic peptide. From it draw arrows to four places and show the effect at each: the afferent and efferent arterioles, the collecting duct sodium channels, the granular cells making renin, and the adrenal cortex making aldosterone. Show what ends up in the urine. One line: state the release stimulus in one phrase, and say why the atrium is a sensible place to sense it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two columns, renin angiotensin aldosterone on one side and atrial natriuretic peptide on the other, and run four rows across both: stimulus, effect on arteriole tone, effect on sodium reabsorption, effect on blood volume. Draw every cell as a small picture rather than a sentence. One line: a person in heart failure has both running high at once, so say which one is winning and what that does to their fluid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how atrial natriuretic peptide opposes the renin angiotensin aldosterone system and state the stimulus for its release."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Natriuretic peptides. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-potassium-handling","name":"Potassium handling","can":"Explain how aldosterone and plasma potassium regulate potassium secretion in the collecting duct and predict the effect of a potassium wasting diuretic.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's aldosterone level is very low after adrenal damage. Predict their plasma sodium and potassium.\nA) Sodium high and potassium low  |  B) Sodium low and potassium high\nC) Both sodium and potassium high  |  D) Both sodium and potassium low\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sodium low and potassium high","why":"Without aldosterone the collecting duct cannot reclaim sodium or push potassium out, so sodium is lost in the urine and potassium builds up in the blood. High sodium with low potassium is the pattern of excess aldosterone, not deficiency. The two ions move in opposite directions here because the same transport step handles both. Both falling together would not fit a loss of sodium reabsorption alone."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the normal range of plasma potassium?\nA) 135 to 145 mEq/L  |  B) 8 to 10 mEq/L\nC) 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L  |  D) 0.5 to 1.5 mEq/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L","why":"Plasma potassium is kept in a narrow band around 4 mEq/L, and even small deviations disturb the resting membrane potential of heart and nerve. The range of 135 to 145 mEq/L is plasma sodium. Values of 8 to 10 mEq/L would be dangerously high and are not a normal range. Values below 1.5 mEq/L are far too low to sustain excitable tissue."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is most of the body's potassium located?\nA) In the plasma  |  B) In bone mineral\nC) Inside cells  |  D) In the interstitial fluid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Inside cells","why":"About 98 percent of body potassium sits in the intracellular compartment, which is why the small plasma fraction is so sensitive to shifts across the membrane. Plasma holds only a tiny fraction of the total. Bone mineral is the reservoir for calcium and phosphate, not potassium. Interstitial fluid, like plasma, is part of the small extracellular pool."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell type secretes potassium into the collecting duct lumen?\nA) Principal cells  |  B) Intercalated cells\nC) Macula densa cells  |  D) Podocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Principal cells","why":"Principal cells pump potassium into themselves from the blood side and let it leak into the lumen through apical channels, and this is the step aldosterone regulates. Intercalated cells handle acid and base and can reabsorb potassium when the body is depleted. Macula densa cells sense sodium chloride and signal renin release. Podocytes are part of the filtration barrier and do no secretion."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient with heart failure is started on spironolactone, a drug that blocks the aldosterone receptor. One week later plasma potassium is 5.8 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0) and plasma sodium is 134 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145). Urine output has increased.\nWhich mechanism explains the rise in plasma potassium?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Without aldosterone signaling, principal cells have fewer sodium channels and pumps, less sodium enters, the lumen is less negative, and less potassium is secreted","why":"Aldosterone normally tells principal cells to make more apical sodium channels, more apical potassium channels, and more basolateral sodium potassium pumps. Sodium entering through those channels makes the lumen negative, and that electrical pull is what drives potassium out. Block the receptor and the whole machine slows, so potassium stays in the body. This is why aldosterone antagonists are called potassium sparing diuretics."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain what controls how much potassium the collecting duct secretes, then predict what a loop diuretic such as furosemide does to plasma potassium and explain each step.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"potassium is secreted by principal cells of the collecting duct; sodium entry through apical sodium channels makes the lumen negative, which drives potassium out through apical potassium channels; aldosterone increases the number of sodium channels, potassium channels, and basolateral sodium potassium pumps, so secretion rises; high plasma potassium directly stimulates aldosterone release and directly increases pump activity; higher flow and sodium delivery to the collecting duct increase potassium secretion; a loop diuretic blocks salt reabsorption in the ascending limb, so more sodium and fluid reach the collecting duct, and the volume loss raises aldosterone; potassium secretion rises and plasma potassium falls (hypokalemia)","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a collecting duct principal cell with the sodium potassium pump on the blood side and the potassium channel on the lumen side. Draw it three times: normal plasma potassium, high plasma potassium, and high aldosterone. Show how much potassium is secreted in each. One line: name the loop that lets high plasma potassium raise aldosterone as well as acting on this cell directly, and say why that makes the response larger."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a nephron and mark where a loop diuretic blocks sodium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb. Then draw the collecting duct downstream and show what the extra sodium delivery and faster flow do to potassium secretion there. Plot plasma potassium across a week on the drug. One line: say what you would see on that patient's ECG by the end of the week, and what you would add to the prescription."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how aldosterone and plasma potassium regulate potassium secretion in the collecting duct and predict the effect of a potassium wasting diuretic."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Potassium handling. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-micturition","name":"Micturition","can":"Trace the micturition reflex and explain how the internal and external sphincters allow voluntary control.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Bladder volume rises from 100 mL to 400 mL. Predict what happens to stretch receptor firing and to the urge to void.\nA) Firing increases and the urge becomes stronger\nB) Firing decreases and the urge fades\nC) Firing is unchanged until the bladder is completely full\nD) Firing increases but the urge decreases as the bladder adapts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Firing increases and the urge becomes stronger","why":"Stretch receptors are graded, so more wall stretch means a higher firing frequency and a stronger conscious signal, which is why the urge builds gradually. Firing does not fall as the bladder fills. There is no threshold at complete fullness; the first urge appears around 200 mL. Some accommodation occurs, but the urge grows rather than shrinks as volume climbs."},{"t":"mc","q":"During bladder filling, sympathetic activity to the bladder is high. What does that accomplish?\nA) It contracts the detrusor and opens the internal sphincter\nB) It relaxes both the detrusor and the internal sphincter\nC) It relaxes the detrusor and keeps the internal sphincter closed\nD) It contracts the external sphincter directly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It relaxes the detrusor and keeps the internal sphincter closed","why":"Sympathetic outflow through the hypogastric nerve lets the bladder expand at low pressure while holding the outlet shut, which is exactly what storage requires. Contracting the detrusor and opening the outlet is the voiding pattern driven by the parasympathetic system. Relaxing both would let urine escape as the bladder filled. The external sphincter is skeletal muscle and takes somatic, not sympathetic, commands."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the receptor that starts the micturition reflex?\nA) Chemoreceptors sensing urine osmolarity\nB) Osmoreceptors in the urethra\nC) Pressure receptors in the renal pelvis\nD) Stretch receptors in the bladder wall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Stretch receptors in the bladder wall","why":"As urine accumulates, the bladder wall is stretched, and those mechanoreceptors fire more and more as volume rises, which is the trigger for the reflex. Urine osmolarity is not monitored in the bladder, since the composition of urine is already fixed by the time it arrives. Renal pelvis pressure receptors do not initiate voiding. There are no osmoreceptors in the urethra."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle contracts to empty the bladder?\nA) The external urethral sphincter  |  B) The detrusor muscle\nC) The levator ani  |  D) The psoas major\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The detrusor muscle","why":"The detrusor is the smooth muscle of the bladder wall, and its contraction raises bladder pressure and expels urine. The external urethral sphincter must relax rather than contract for voiding to occur. The levator ani supports the pelvic floor but is not the muscle that empties the bladder. The psoas major is a hip flexor with no urinary role."},{"t":"mc","q":"What type of muscle is the internal urethral sphincter, and is it under voluntary control?\nA) Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control\nB) Cardiac muscle, not under voluntary control\nC) Smooth muscle, not under voluntary control\nD) Smooth muscle, under voluntary control\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Smooth muscle, not under voluntary control","why":"The internal sphincter is a thickening of bladder smooth muscle controlled by the autonomic nervous system, so it opens and closes without conscious command. Skeletal muscle under voluntary control describes the external sphincter, the one you can hold. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart. Smooth muscle is by definition not under direct voluntary control here."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"Voluntary control of urination works by keeping the skeletal muscle of the external sphincter contracted until the person chooses to relax it.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The reflex itself is involuntary: stretch triggers parasympathetic detrusor contraction and the internal smooth muscle sphincter opens with it. The one skeletal muscle in the system, the external sphincter, is the point of voluntary control. Higher centers also inhibit the reflex directly, but the sphincter is the final gate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the bladder filling, with stretch receptors in the wall, the sensory path to the sacral spinal cord, the parasympathetic motor path back to the detrusor muscle, and both sphincters. Mark the internal sphincter as smooth muscle and involuntary, the external as skeletal muscle on the pudendal nerve. Show the state of each during filling and during voiding. One line: say which sphincter a toddler has to learn to control, and why the other needs no training."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the reflex twice, once intact with the descending path from the pons included, and once with the spinal cord cut above the sacral segments. In the second, show which parts of the loop still work and which are gone, then draw the bladder behavior that results. One line: say why that patient still empties the bladder but cannot choose when, and name the piece that was lost."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the micturition reflex and explain how the internal and external sphincters allow voluntary control."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Micturition. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-lab-urinalysis","name":"Urinalysis","can":"Perform a urinalysis measuring specific gravity, pH, glucose, protein, ketones, and blood and relate any abnormal finding to the renal process that failed.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A urine sample shows a strongly positive protein result. Which renal process most likely failed?\nA) The filtration barrier lost its ability to hold back plasma proteins\nB) The collecting duct stopped secreting protein into the blood\nC) The loop of Henle stopped concentrating the tubular fluid properly\nD) The bladder failed to empty completely between voids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The filtration barrier lost its ability to hold back plasma proteins","why":"Protein in the urine means proteins that should have stayed in the glomerular capillary got through, usually because the podocyte slits or the charge barrier are damaged. The collecting duct does not secrete protein, so there is no such process to fail. A concentrating failure shows up as a low specific gravity, not as protein. Incomplete bladder emptying changes volume, not protein content."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's urine pH is consistently 5.0. Which renal activity is this most consistent with?\nA) Loss of the medullary osmotic gradient\nB) Bicarbonate secretion by intercalated cells in response to alkalosis\nC) Failure of the proximal tubule to reabsorb sodium\nD) Active hydrogen ion secretion by the tubule in response to an acid load\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Active hydrogen ion secretion by the tubule in response to an acid load","why":"An acidic urine means the tubule is dumping H+ into the lumen, which is what the kidney does when the body has acid to get rid of. Bicarbonate secretion would raise urine pH toward 8, not lower it. Proximal sodium handling does not set urine pH directly. The medullary gradient governs concentration, which is read from specific gravity rather than pH."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does urine specific gravity measure?\nA) The acidity of urine compared with the acidity of plasma\nB) The density of urine compared with the density of pure water\nC) The volume of urine produced in one hour of collection\nD) The total concentration of protein dissolved in urine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The density of urine compared with the density of pure water","why":"Specific gravity is a ratio of densities, and because dissolved solute adds mass, it serves as a quick proxy for how concentrated the urine is. Urine pH is measured separately by the dipstick pad. Volume per hour is a flow rate, measured with a graduated cylinder and a clock. Protein has its own dipstick pad and is only one of many solutes contributing to density."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the normal range of urine specific gravity?\nA) 2.00 to 3.00  |  B) 0.900 to 1.000\nC) 1.100 to 1.200  |  D) 1.001 to 1.035\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 1.001 to 1.035","why":"Urine is always at least slightly denser than water, and the wide range reflects the kidney's ability to make either very dilute or very concentrated urine. Values below 1.000 would mean urine less dense than water, which cannot happen with solute dissolved in it. Values above 1.100 are far beyond what the kidney can produce. Values of 2.00 or 3.00 are not in the physiological range at all."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the typical pH of urine in a person on a mixed diet?\nA) About 7.4  |  B) About 6.0\nC) About 3.0  |  D) About 9.0\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) About 6.0","why":"Urine is normally slightly acidic because the kidney is excreting the daily acid load from metabolism, and about 6 is a common value. A pH of 7.4 is the tightly regulated value of arterial blood, not urine. A pH of 3.0 is below the lower limit of about 4.5 the kidney can reach. A pH of 9.0 is above the upper limit of about 8.0."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has eaten nothing for three days and has been drinking very little. Urinalysis: specific gravity 1.035 (normal 1.005 to 1.030), pH 5.0 (normal 4.5 to 8.0), glucose negative, protein negative, blood negative, ketones moderate.\nWhich explanation best links the abnormal findings to what the kidney is doing?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Fasting drives fat breakdown, ketoacids are filtered faster than the tubule can reabsorb them, the acid load is excreted as hydrogen ion, and antidiuretic hormone is concentrating the urine because water intake is low","why":"Nothing here is broken. Three days of fasting makes the liver produce ketoacids, and their plasma level rises until the filtered load outruns proximal reabsorption, so ketones appear in the urine. They also add acid, which the kidney secretes as hydrogen ion, giving the low pH. Little water intake means high antidiuretic hormone and a concentrated urine, hence the high specific gravity. All three findings match a normal kidney responding to a starved, dry patient."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a dipstick with its pads in a row and write the normal result on each: specific gravity 1.003 to 1.030, pH about 4.5 to 8.0, and negative for glucose, protein, ketones, and blood. Beside every pad draw the nephron step that keeps that pad negative in a healthy person. One line: name the pad that turns positive first in uncontrolled diabetes, and the second one that follows it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three urine samples with their dipstick results filled in: one from an untreated type 1 diabetic, one from someone with glomerular damage, and one from someone who ran a hot marathon without drinking. Mark abnormal pads and specific gravity on each, and beside each draw the nephron arrow that failed or was pushed. One line: one of these three kidneys is entirely healthy, so name it and say what its dipstick is really reporting."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Urinalysis. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-lab-renal-calculation","name":"Renal function calculation","can":"Calculate glomerular filtration rate, clearance, and filtered load from a data set and interpret the values in a clinical case.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a plasma glucose of 4 mg/mL and a glomerular filtration rate of 125 mL/min. The tubular transport maximum for glucose is 375 mg/min. How much glucose appears in the urine each minute?\nA) 125 mg/min  |  B) 500 mg/min\nC) 375 mg/min  |  D) 0 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) 125 mg/min","why":"The filtered load is 125 times 4, which is 500 mg/min, and the carriers can move only 375 mg/min, so the surplus of 125 mg/min stays in the tubule and is excreted. A value of 500 mg/min is the whole filtered load, which ignores the substantial reabsorption still happening. A value of 375 mg/min is the amount reabsorbed, not the amount excreted. Zero would be correct only if the filtered load stayed below the transport maximum."},{"t":"mc","q":"A substance has a plasma concentration of 4 mg/mL and a clearance of 25 mL/min in a patient whose glomerular filtration rate is 125 mL/min. At what rate is the tubule reabsorbing it?\nA) 100 mg/min  |  B) 25 mg/min\nC) 500 mg/min  |  D) 400 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 400 mg/min","why":"The filtered load is 125 times 4, or 500 mg/min, the excretion rate is clearance times plasma concentration, or 25 times 4, which is 100 mg/min, and the difference of 400 mg/min must have been reabsorbed. A value of 100 mg/min is the excretion rate rather than the reabsorption rate. A value of 500 mg/min is the whole filtered load, which would mean nothing at all was excreted. A value of 25 mg/min mistakes the clearance number for an amount."},{"t":"mc","q":"Inulin is infused to steady state. Plasma inulin is 1 mg/mL, urine inulin is 100 mg/mL, and urine flow is 1.25 mL/min. What is the glomerular filtration rate?\nA) 80 mL/min  |  B) 100 mL/min\nC) 125 mL/min  |  D) 1.25 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 125 mL/min","why":"Clearance is 100 mg/mL times 1.25 mL/min divided by 1 mg/mL, which is 125 mL/min, and because inulin is neither secreted nor reabsorbed this equals the filtration rate. A value of 80 mL/min would come from arithmetic that lost the flow rate multiplication. A value of 100 mL/min is the urine concentration copied over without completing the calculation. A value of 1.25 mL/min is the urine flow rate itself."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a glomerular filtration rate of 125 mL/min and a plasma glucose of 1 mg/mL. What is the filtered load of glucose?\nA) 1 mg/min  |  B) 125 mL/min\nC) 125 mg/min  |  D) 250 mg/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 125 mg/min","why":"Filtered load is 125 mL/min multiplied by 1 mg/mL, giving 125 mg of glucose presented to the tubule each minute, comfortably below the transport maximum of about 375 mg/min. A result in mL/min would be a volume rate, not an amount. A value of 1 mg/min ignores the filtration rate entirely. A value of 250 mg/min would require either double the filtration rate or double the plasma concentration."},{"t":"mc","q":"Urine creatinine is 60 mg/dL, urine flow is 2 mL/min, and plasma creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL. What is the creatinine clearance?\nA) 50 mL/min  |  B) 120 mL/min\nC) 100 mL/min  |  D) 72 mL/min\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) 100 mL/min","why":"Multiply 60 by 2 to get 120, then divide by 1.2 to get 100 mL/min; the dL units cancel so no conversion is needed. A value of 50 mL/min results from dividing before multiplying by the flow rate. A value of 120 mL/min is the numerator left undivided. A value of 72 mL/min does not follow from these numbers under any correct grouping."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient's plasma inulin is 1 mg/mL, urine inulin is 120 mg/mL, and urine flow is 1 mL/min. Plasma glucose is 100 mg/dL. Without notes, calculate the glomerular filtration rate and the filtered load of glucose, explain why inulin clearance gives you the filtration rate, and predict how much glucose is in this patient's urine and why.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration; inulin clearance is 120 times 1 divided by 1, which is 120 mL/min; inulin clearance equals glomerular filtration rate because inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted; filtered load equals glomerular filtration rate times plasma concentration; plasma glucose 100 mg/dL is 1 mg/mL, so the filtered load of glucose is 120 mg/min; 100 mg/dL is below the renal threshold (about 180 mg/dL), so the filtered load is below the transport maximum; all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, so urine glucose is essentially zero","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Work a case on the page. Choose plausible values for plasma creatinine, urine creatinine, and urine flow rate, then draw the clearance calculation as a labeled flow of numbers with units carried through every step. Use your answer to calculate the filtered load of sodium at a plasma sodium of 140 mEq/L. One line: compare that filtered load against the sodium actually excreted in a day, and say what the gap tells you about the tubule."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a two column comparison of two patients with the same urine output, one with plasma creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL and one at 4.0 mg/dL. Calculate each one's clearance and draw it as a bar against a marked normal near 125 mL/min. Then draw what the nephrons of the second patient are doing to produce that number. One line: say whether a fourfold rise in plasma creatinine means a fourfold fall in GFR, and why that relationship is not a straight line."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to calculate glomerular filtration rate, clearance, and filtered load from a data set and interpret the values in a clinical case."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Renal function calculation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Acid Base and Fluid Balance","comps":[{"id":"w13-buffer-systems","name":"Buffer systems","can":"Compare the bicarbonate, phosphate, and protein buffer systems by location and speed and explain why bicarbonate is the dominant extracellular buffer.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the bicarbonate system a poor buffer for a rise in carbon dioxide itself?\nA) It raises H+ and bicarbonate together, so the ratio still falls\nB) Carbon dioxide is a base rather than an acid in this reaction\nC) Carbon dioxide inactivates carbonic anhydrase at high partial pressure\nD) Bicarbonate cannot accept a hydrogen ion once carbon dioxide is high\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It raises H+ and bicarbonate together, so the ratio still falls","why":"Hydrating carbon dioxide produces one H+ and one HCO3- from the same molecule, so the base cannot mop up the acid it just created, and pH is set by the ratio of the two. That is why proteins, especially hemoglobin, do the real buffering of a respiratory acid load by taking up the H+ and leaving the bicarbonate free. Carbonic anhydrase is not inactivated by high carbon dioxide. Bicarbonate still binds H+ chemically; the problem is bookkeeping, not chemistry. Carbon dioxide behaves as the acid member of the pair."},{"t":"mc","q":"A load of fixed acid enters the blood. What happens to plasma bicarbonate and to carbon dioxide before the lungs respond?\nA) Bicarbonate falls and carbon dioxide rises\nB) Bicarbonate rises and carbon dioxide falls\nC) Both bicarbonate and carbon dioxide rise\nD) Both bicarbonate and carbon dioxide fall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Bicarbonate falls and carbon dioxide rises","why":"The added H+ combines with bicarbonate to make carbonic acid, which breaks down to water and carbon dioxide, so the base is consumed and the gas accumulates until ventilation clears it. Bicarbonate cannot rise while it is being spent on the acid load. Carbon dioxide cannot fall, because it is the product of the buffering reaction. Both falling would require an entirely different disturbance, such as hyperventilation on top of the acid load."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does buffering a fixed acid with bicarbonate depend on normal lung function?\nA) The lungs deliver the oxygen that binds the excess hydrogen ion\nB) The lungs manufacture the bicarbonate used in the reaction\nC) The lungs supply the carbonic anhydrase for the reaction\nD) The carbon dioxide produced must be exhaled or buffering stalls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The carbon dioxide produced must be exhaled or buffering stalls","why":"Each H+ buffered turns into a molecule of carbon dioxide, and if that gas accumulates the equilibrium pushes back and the buffer loses its power. The kidney, not the lung, makes bicarbonate. Carbonic anhydrase is inside cells such as the erythrocyte and the tubule cell rather than being supplied by the lung. Oxygen does not bind H+ and plays no part in the buffering reaction."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's plasma albumin falls to half its normal value. What happens to the buffering power of plasma?\nA) Bicarbonate buffering rises to compensate exactly\nB) Buffering is unchanged because albumin is not a buffer\nC) Buffering rises because more free H+ can reach bicarbonate\nD) The non bicarbonate buffering power of plasma falls\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The non bicarbonate buffering power of plasma falls","why":"Albumin supplies most of the protein buffering in plasma through its histidine side chains, so losing half of it removes a real share of the total buffer capacity. Bicarbonate concentration is set by the kidney and does not automatically rise to cover the loss. Albumin is very much a buffer, which is why low albumin also lowers the measured anion gap. More free H+ reaching bicarbonate means a larger pH swing, not better buffering."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why can the kidney excrete far more acid when phosphate is present in the tubular fluid?\nA) Buffered H+ keeps urine pH from falling, so secretion continues\nB) Phosphate is converted to bicarbonate inside the tubule cell\nC) Phosphate raises the number of H+ ATPase pumps in the membrane\nD) Phosphate carries H+ across the luminal membrane by symport\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Buffered H+ keeps urine pH from falling, so secretion continues","why":"The H+ pumps stall once urine pH reaches about 4.4, and a buffer soaks up secreted H+ so the free concentration stays low and the gradient stays workable. Phosphate does not carry H+ across the luminal membrane; the H+ ATPase and the Na+/H+ exchanger do that. Phosphate is not converted into bicarbonate, although trapping H+ on phosphate does let the cell add new bicarbonate to the blood. Pump number is regulated by acidosis and hormones, not by the amount of filtered phosphate."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The phosphate buffer system is the most important buffer in plasma because of its high plasma concentration.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Bicarbonate is the most important plasma buffer; phosphate is present in plasma at only about 1 mmol/L and does its main work inside cells and in the urine.","why":"A buffer can only handle acid in proportion to how much of it is present. Plasma bicarbonate sits at about 24 mmol/L, and the carbon dioxide it forms can be exhaled, so it never saturates. Phosphate has a pKa near 6.8, close to body pH, which makes it a good buffer chemically, but its plasma concentration is too low to matter there. Inside cells and in the renal tubule its concentration is much higher, and that is where it works."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a pH scale from 6.8 to 7.8 with the normal arterial range 7.35 to 7.45 shaded. Draw an acid load arriving and show it being handled in three stages down the page: chemical buffering in seconds, the lungs changing ventilation in minutes, and the kidneys changing bicarbonate and acid excretion over hours to days. Draw the pH recovering a little at each stage. One line: say which stage actually removes hydrogen ion from the body, and what the other two did instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare the bicarbonate, phosphate, and protein buffer systems by location and speed and explain why bicarbonate is the dominant extracellular buffer. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the bicarbonate, phosphate, and protein buffer systems by location and speed and explain why bicarbonate is the dominant extracellular buffer."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Buffer systems. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-respiratory-ph-control","name":"Respiratory control of pH","can":"Explain how a change in ventilation shifts the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate equilibrium and therefore plasma pH.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A student hyperventilates for one minute, then holds their breath as long as possible. What happens to pH across the two phases?\nA) It stays constant, then falls sharply\nB) It rises during both phases\nC) It falls, then rises above normal\nD) It rises, then falls back through normal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It rises, then falls back through normal","why":"Blowing off carbon dioxide first drives PCO2 down and pH up, then during the breath hold metabolism keeps making carbon dioxide with no route out, so PCO2 climbs and pH falls back toward and past normal. The order cannot be reversed, since hyperventilation always comes first here. pH cannot stay constant while PCO2 is changing. It cannot rise in both phases, because a breath hold retains acid rather than removing it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student breathes rapidly and deeply for two minutes during an anxiety episode. Predict the changes in PCO2 and pH.\nA) PCO2 falls and pH rises  |  B) PCO2 falls and pH falls\nC) PCO2 rises and pH falls  |  D) PCO2 rises and pH rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) PCO2 falls and pH rises","why":"Blowing off carbon dioxide faster than it is made lowers arterial PCO2, removes acid from the blood, and drives pH up, which is respiratory alkalosis. PCO2 and pH move in opposite directions for a respiratory problem, so falling PCO2 with falling pH is not possible from hyperventilation alone. Rising PCO2 with falling pH describes hypoventilation, the opposite disturbance. Rising PCO2 with rising pH breaks the chemistry in both directions."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person takes a sedative that slows the respiratory rate. Predict what happens to arterial PCO2 and pH.\nA) PCO2 rises and pH falls  |  B) PCO2 and pH both stay normal\nC) PCO2 falls and pH rises  |  D) PCO2 rises and pH rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) PCO2 rises and pH falls","why":"Less ventilation lets metabolically produced carbon dioxide accumulate, and the extra carbonic acid drives pH down, which is respiratory acidosis. Falling PCO2 would require more ventilation, not less. A rise in PCO2 can never raise pH, because carbon dioxide is the acid member of the buffer pair. Values cannot stay normal while carbon dioxide production continues and removal has slowed."},{"t":"mc","q":"Adding carbon dioxide to blood shifts the reaction CO2 + H2O to H2CO3 to H+ plus HCO3- in which direction, and what happens to bicarbonate?\nA) To the right, and bicarbonate rises slightly\nB) To the right, and bicarbonate falls slightly\nC) To the left, and bicarbonate rises slightly\nD) To the left, and bicarbonate falls slightly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) To the right, and bicarbonate rises slightly","why":"Extra reactant pushes the equilibrium toward products, so both H+ and HCO3- are produced and measured bicarbonate rises a little even though pH is falling. The reaction cannot run to the left when carbon dioxide is being added. Bicarbonate cannot fall while it is being generated by the same reaction. This is also why a rising bicarbonate on a blood gas does not automatically mean a metabolic alkalosis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Alveolar ventilation doubles while carbon dioxide production stays the same. What happens to arterial PCO2?\nA) It stays the same because production is unchanged\nB) It falls to roughly half its previous value\nC) It falls by roughly 10 percent\nD) It doubles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It falls to roughly half its previous value","why":"Arterial PCO2 is set by carbon dioxide production divided by alveolar ventilation, so doubling the denominator with the numerator fixed roughly halves the value, taking 40 mmHg toward 20 mmHg. A ten percent fall would need only a small change in ventilation. PCO2 doubles when ventilation is halved, not doubled. Unchanged production is precisely why the ventilation change is free to move PCO2."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has a pH of 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and a plasma bicarbonate of 12 mmol/L (normal 22 to 26). She is breathing deeply and rapidly. Without notes, use the carbon dioxide bicarbonate equation to explain how her breathing pattern changes her pH, what triggers it, and why it cannot fully fix the problem.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"carbon dioxide plus water forms carbonic acid, which splits into hydrogen ion plus bicarbonate; the extra hydrogen ion stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors, so ventilation rises within minutes; hyperventilation lowers PCO2; lowering PCO2 pulls the reaction toward carbon dioxide, which consumes hydrogen ion; hydrogen ion falls, so pH rises toward normal; pH depends on the ratio of bicarbonate to PCO2, and lowering PCO2 partly restores the ratio; compensation is partial: the ketoacids are still there, bicarbonate is still low, and the drive fades as pH improves","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Explain how a change in ventilation shifts the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate equilibrium and therefore plasma pH. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain how a change in ventilation shifts the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate equilibrium and therefore plasma pH."},{"t":"draw","q":"Trace the cause and effect. Draw a flow chart for this topic: Respiratory control of pH. Show each step in order, with an arrow for what causes what. Explain every arrow out loud."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Respiratory control of pH. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-renal-ph-control","name":"Renal control of pH","can":"Describe how the kidney secretes hydrogen ion and reclaims or generates bicarbonate and explain why renal compensation is slower and more complete than respiratory compensation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with chronic lung disease has had a PCO2 near 60 mmHg for several years. Predict plasma bicarbonate and urine pH.\nA) Bicarbonate is high and the urine is alkaline\nB) Bicarbonate is high and the urine is acidic\nC) Bicarbonate is low and the urine is alkaline\nD) Bicarbonate is normal and the urine is alkaline\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Bicarbonate is high and the urine is acidic","why":"Years of carbon dioxide retention drive the kidney to secrete H+ and generate new bicarbonate, so plasma bicarbonate climbs into the thirties while the acid leaving in the urine keeps urine pH low. A low bicarbonate would make the acidemia worse rather than compensating for it. A normal bicarbonate would mean no compensation at all, which is not possible after years. Alkaline urine would mean the kidney was dumping the very bicarbonate it needs to retain."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has been acidotic for two days. Predict what has happened to renal H+ secretion and to ammonium production.\nA) H+ secretion decreased and ammonium production increased\nB) Both have increased\nC) Both have decreased\nD) H+ secretion increased and ammonium production decreased\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both have increased","why":"Acidosis upregulates the luminal Na+/H+ exchanger and H+ ATPase and also induces the enzymes of glutamine metabolism, so the kidney both pumps more acid and supplies more ammonia to carry it out. Both falling is the response to alkalosis, not acidosis. The two processes are regulated together because H+ secretion is useless without a buffer to trap it, so a split answer in either direction misses how the system is coupled."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a metabolic alkalosis that has lasted three days. What is the kidney doing with bicarbonate?\nA) Holding bicarbonate constant while excreting more chloride\nB) Reclaiming all filtered bicarbonate and generating more\nC) Excreting bicarbonate in the urine, making it alkaline\nD) Converting bicarbonate to ammonium for excretion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Excreting bicarbonate in the urine, making it alkaline","why":"With plasma bicarbonate high, the filtered load exceeds what the proximal tubule can reclaim and type B intercalated cells actively secrete bicarbonate, so the urine turns alkaline and plasma bicarbonate is dragged back down. Reclaiming and generating more bicarbonate is the acidosis response and would worsen the alkalosis. Bicarbonate is not converted into ammonium; ammonium comes from glutamine. Chloride excretion alone would not correct the base excess."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man with severe chronic lung disease has had a PCO2 near 58 mmHg (35 to 45) for years. His plasma bicarbonate is 34 mEq/L (22 to 26) and his pH is 7.37 (7.35 to 7.45). He is placed on a ventilator that brings his PCO2 down to 40 mmHg within an hour.\nPredict his arterial pH at the end of that hour and explain why.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"pH rises above 7.45, because the bicarbonate the kidney built up over years is still high while the carbon dioxide is gone","why":"The kidney spent days raising bicarbonate to offset the high PCO2. The ventilator removes the PCO2 in an hour, but the kidney needs days to excrete the extra bicarbonate. For that hour the patient has a normal PCO2 and a high bicarbonate, which is the picture of a metabolic alkalosis, and pH overshoots into the alkaline range. This is why chronic carbon dioxide retainers are ventilated gently."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman has had heavy diarrhea for five days. Her plasma bicarbonate is 16 mEq/L (22 to 26) and her pH is 7.33 (7.35 to 7.45). Her urine pH is 5.0 (normal range 4.5 to 8.0) and her urine ammonium excretion is three times normal.\nWhat is her kidney doing, and what does it accomplish for her plasma?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Secreting H+ that leaves the body as ammonium, which generates new bicarbonate to replace what was lost in the stool","why":"The diarrhea took bicarbonate out of the body, so reclaiming the filtered load is not enough. The kidney has responded by making more ammonia, which traps secreted H+ in the urine as ammonium. Every H+ that leaves this way leaves one new bicarbonate in the blood. The acid urine and the high ammonium excretion are the signs that this net acid excretion is running hard."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"The kidney compensates for a respiratory acidosis by excreting bicarbonate in the urine.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The kidney compensates for a respiratory acidosis by secreting more H+ and generating new bicarbonate, which raises plasma bicarbonate.","why":"In a respiratory acidosis the problem is too much carbon dioxide, and the only way the kidney can pull pH back up is to add base. It does that by secreting H+ into the urine as ammonium and phosphate, and each secreted H+ leaves a new bicarbonate in the blood. Excreting bicarbonate is what the kidney does in an alkalosis, and it would make an acidosis worse."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how the kidney raises plasma bicarbonate in a patient who has been retaining carbon dioxide for a week, and why the process takes days rather than minutes.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"high PCO2 raises carbon dioxide inside tubule cells, and carbonic anhydrase produces H+ and bicarbonate; H+ is secreted into the lumen (Na+/H+ exchanger and H+ ATPase); secreted H+ is trapped in the urine as ammonium or bound to phosphate, so it leaves the body; each H+ excreted this way leaves one new bicarbonate that enters the blood; the urine becomes acidic and plasma bicarbonate climbs above normal; the delay comes from having to synthesize more transporters and more ammonia in the tubule cells, which takes days","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two tubule cells side by side. In the first, a proximal tubule cell reclaiming filtered bicarbonate: hydrogen ion secreted into the lumen, carbonic anhydrase there, carbon dioxide crossing into the cell, bicarbonate returned to blood. In the second, an intercalated cell generating new bicarbonate by pumping hydrogen ion onto phosphate or ammonia buffer. One line: say which of the two can push plasma bicarbonate above where it started, and why the other one cannot."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one set of axes, time in hours to days on the x and arterial pH on the y with the band 7.35 to 7.45 marked. Load the patient with metabolic acid at time zero, then plot two correction curves, the respiratory response and the renal response, each starting when it really starts and reaching as far as it really gets. One line: say why the fast one cannot finish the job, and name what the slow one is physically excreting."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe how the kidney secretes hydrogen ion and reclaims or generates bicarbonate and explain why renal compensation is slower and more complete than respiratory compensation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Renal control of pH. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-acid-base-disorders","name":"The four acid base disorders","can":"Classify a disorder as respiratory or metabolic and as acidosis or alkalosis from pH, partial pressure of carbon dioxide, and bicarbonate.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A blood gas shows pH 7.11, PCO2 55 mmHg, HCO3- 17 mEq/L. Why can this not be a simple disorder with compensation?\nA) The pH is too low for any compensation to have started\nB) Bicarbonate and PCO2 can never be abnormal at the same time\nC) Both values are pushing pH down, so neither is compensating\nD) Compensation never changes bicarbonate, only PCO2\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Both values are pushing pH down, so neither is compensating","why":"A high PCO2 acidifies and a low bicarbonate also acidifies, so both a respiratory and a metabolic acidosis are present and there is no compensating move in either direction, which is why this reads as a mixed acidosis. Compensation absolutely does change bicarbonate; that is how the kidney compensates for a respiratory problem. A very low pH does not prevent compensation from starting, and in a simple disorder you would expect to see it. Both values are abnormal at once in every compensated disorder, so that alone is not the problem here."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient vomits gastric contents repeatedly for two days, losing hydrochloric acid. Predict bicarbonate and pH, then classify the disorder.\nA) Bicarbonate rises, pH falls, respiratory acidosis\nB) Bicarbonate falls, pH rises, respiratory alkalosis\nC) Bicarbonate falls, pH falls, metabolic acidosis\nD) Bicarbonate rises, pH rises, metabolic alkalosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bicarbonate rises, pH rises, metabolic alkalosis","why":"Every H+ secreted into the stomach leaves a bicarbonate behind in the blood, so removing that acid by vomiting leaves the base unopposed, bicarbonate climbs, and pH rises, which is a metabolic alkalosis. Bicarbonate cannot fall when the acid that would have neutralized it is being discarded. A rising bicarbonate with a falling pH would require a large simultaneous rise in PCO2, which vomiting does not cause. Losing bicarbonate is what happens with diarrhea, from the other end of the gut."},{"t":"mc","q":"Classify this blood gas: pH 7.25, PCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L.\nA) Respiratory acidosis  |  B) Respiratory alkalosis\nC) Metabolic alkalosis  |  D) Metabolic acidosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Respiratory acidosis","why":"The pH of 7.25 is acidemic, and the PCO2 of 60 mmHg is high, which is the direction that makes blood more acidic, so the respiratory system is the cause. Bicarbonate at 26 mEq/L is at the top of normal and cannot explain an acidemia, so metabolic acidosis is ruled out. Both alkalosis labels are impossible with a pH this low."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A hiker drives from sea level to a mountain hut at 4,000 meters and six hours later has a blood gas drawn: pH 7.50 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 28 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 23 mEq/L (22 to 26), PO2 55 mmHg (80 to 100).\nClassify the disorder and explain the mechanism that produced it.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Respiratory alkalosis, because the low PO2 stimulated the peripheral chemoreceptors and the resulting hyperventilation blew off carbon dioxide","why":"At altitude the arterial PO2 falls low enough to fire the carotid bodies, and the hiker breathes more than his carbon dioxide production requires. PCO2 falls, pH rises, and the bicarbonate is still normal because six hours is too short for the kidney to respond. That makes this an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. Over the next days the kidney will excrete bicarbonate and bring the pH down."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man with a blocked small intestine has been vomiting for three days and now has a tube draining his stomach. Blood gas: pH 7.52 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 47 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 37 mEq/L (22 to 26).\nClassify the disorder and explain what produced each abnormal value.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Metabolic alkalosis: losing gastric H+ left bicarbonate behind, and the lungs are compensating by slowing ventilation so PCO2 rises","why":"The pH is above 7.40, so the primary disorder is an alkalosis. The high bicarbonate is the value that raises pH, so it is metabolic, and it came from days of losing gastric acid. The high PCO2 would lower pH, so it is the compensation: the chemoreceptors have let ventilation fall so carbon dioxide accumulates and pulls pH partly back down."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A high arterial PCO2 always means that the patient has a respiratory acidosis.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. A high PCO2 is a respiratory acidosis only when the pH is on the acid side. With an alkaline pH, a high PCO2 is the lung compensating for a metabolic alkalosis.","why":"The same abnormal value can be the cause or the compensation, and only the pH tells you which. In a metabolic alkalosis the lungs slow down to hold carbon dioxide, so PCO2 rises above normal, but the pH stays above 7.40. Calling that a respiratory acidosis would have you treating the compensation and missing the real problem."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, walk through the steps you use to classify an acid base disorder, using this blood gas as your example: pH 7.30 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 14 mEq/L (22 to 26).\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"look at pH first: 7.30 is below 7.40, so the primary disorder is an acidosis; ask which value would lower pH: a low bicarbonate lowers pH, so the disorder is metabolic; the low PCO2 would raise pH, so it is not the cause, it is compensation; conclusion: metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation; pH is still outside the normal range, so compensation is partial, not complete","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a two by two grid, respiratory and metabolic down the side, acidosis and alkalosis across the top. Write the normal values once at the top: pH 7.35 to 7.45, PCO2 35 to 45 mmHg, bicarbonate 22 to 26 mEq/L. In every cell draw three arrows for pH, PCO2, and bicarbonate pointing the right way, and circle the one that is the primary disturbance. One line: state the single rule that identifies the primary value, using the direction of pH."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw four patients, one per disorder, each with the organ or process that caused it and the blood gas numbers it would produce. Do not use diabetic ketoacidosis, pick your own metabolic acidosis. Mark the primary change on the drawing itself so it is visible without reading. One line: two of your four have a low pH, so name the single value that separates them and say which way it points in each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a disorder as respiratory or metabolic and as acidosis or alkalosis from pH, partial pressure of carbon dioxide, and bicarbonate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: The four acid base disorders. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-acid-base-compensation","name":"Compensation","can":"Determine whether an acid base disorder is uncompensated, partially compensated, or fully compensated and name the system doing the compensating.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A blood gas shows pH 7.43, PCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 19 mEq/L. Name the primary disorder and the compensating system.\nA) A metabolic alkalosis compensated by the lung\nB) A respiratory acidosis compensated by the kidney\nC) A metabolic acidosis compensated by the lung\nD) A respiratory alkalosis compensated by the kidney\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A respiratory alkalosis compensated by the kidney","why":"The pH is normal but sits above 7.40, so the primary problem was an alkalosis, and the low PCO2 is the value that raises pH, making it respiratory, with the low bicarbonate representing renal compensation. A metabolic acidosis would put pH below 7.40 and would be driven by the low bicarbonate instead. A metabolic alkalosis would require a high bicarbonate. A respiratory acidosis would require a high PCO2 and a pH below 7.40."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has had a stable PCO2 of 60 mmHg for two weeks. Which bicarbonate value fits full renal compensation, and what pH would you expect?\nA) About 18 mEq/L, with a pH near 7.10\nB) About 24 mEq/L, with a pH near 7.22\nC) About 34 mEq/L, with a pH just under 7.40\nD) About 34 mEq/L, with a pH just over 7.45\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) About 34 mEq/L, with a pH just under 7.40","why":"Two weeks is long enough for full renal compensation, which means bicarbonate rises far enough to restore the ratio and bring pH back inside the normal range but not past 7.40, and a bicarbonate near 34 mEq/L against a PCO2 of 60 mmHg does exactly that. A bicarbonate of 24 mEq/L would mean no compensation at all, leaving pH near 7.22. A pH above 7.45 would be an overshoot, which compensation does not produce. A bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L would push pH lower still and would indicate an added metabolic acidosis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Determine the compensation status: pH 7.32, PCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 30 mEq/L.\nA) Uncompensated  |  B) Partially compensated\nC) Fully compensated  |  D) No compensation is possible for this disorder\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Partially compensated","why":"The pH of 7.32 is still acidemic, the high PCO2 identifies a respiratory acidosis, and the bicarbonate of 30 mEq/L is above normal, which is the kidney moving in the right direction but not far enough yet. Uncompensated would require a bicarbonate still inside 22 to 26 mEq/L. Full compensation would require the pH back inside 7.35 to 7.45. Renal compensation is very much possible here; it is simply incomplete."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A teenager with untreated type 1 diabetes arrives breathing deeply and rapidly. Blood gas: pH 7.20 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 22 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 8 mEq/L (22 to 26). Blood glucose is 480 mg/dL (70 to 100).\nWhat is the compensation status, and which system is doing the compensating?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Partially compensated: the lungs are blowing off carbon dioxide to raise pH, but the pH is still well below normal","why":"Ketoacids consumed the bicarbonate, so this is a metabolic acidosis. The carotid bodies sense the H+ and drive ventilation up, which is the deep rapid breathing you can see at the bedside. That lowers PCO2 far below normal and raises pH from where it would otherwise be, but not into the normal range. Compensation is present and partial, and the lungs are doing it."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A woman suddenly became short of breath 20 minutes ago from a clot in a lung artery. She is breathing 32 times per minute. Blood gas: pH 7.49 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 22 mEq/L (22 to 26).\nWhat is the compensation status now, and if the fast breathing continued for three days, how would the bicarbonate and pH change?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Uncompensated now; over three days the kidney would excrete bicarbonate, bicarbonate would fall below normal, and pH would move back toward 7.45","why":"The low PCO2 raised pH, and bicarbonate has not moved because 20 minutes is too short for the kidney. That is an uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. Given days, the kidney compensates for an alkalosis by secreting less H+ and letting bicarbonate escape in the urine. Plasma bicarbonate drops below normal, which pulls pH back toward the top of the normal range."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a fully compensated acid base disorder, the pH returns to the normal range but stays on the side of 7.40 that matches the primary problem.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"True.","why":"The signal that drives compensation is the abnormal pH itself. As pH gets closer to normal that signal fades, so the response levels off with pH just inside the range. A fully compensated respiratory acidosis lands around 7.36 to 7.39, and a compensated alkalosis around 7.41 to 7.44. If pH crossed to the other side, a second disorder would have to be present."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain why the lungs compensate for an acid base disorder quickly but incompletely, while the kidney compensates slowly but can bring pH almost all the way back.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"chemoreceptors change ventilation within minutes, so PCO2 changes fast; the changed PCO2 reduces the chemoreceptor stimulus that drove the change, so the response levels off with pH still abnormal; the lungs can only adjust carbon dioxide, they cannot remove fixed acid or base; the kidney must increase transporter numbers and ammonia production, which takes hours to days; the kidney can excrete the actual excess acid (as ammonium and titratable acid) or excrete excess bicarbonate; because it removes the load itself, renal compensation can return pH close to normal and hold it","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis for a patient in respiratory acidosis with three vertical slices on it, uncompensated, partially compensated, and fully compensated. At each slice draw pH, PCO2, and bicarbonate as small bars against their normal bands. Show bicarbonate climbing while PCO2 stays high, and pH walking back into range. One line: at the fully compensated slice two values are still abnormal, so say which one still tells you what the original problem was."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw two blood gas sets that both read pH 7.32, one a respiratory acidosis partly compensated by the kidney and one a metabolic acidosis partly compensated by the lung. Draw the compensating organ in each with the actual action it is taking, retaining bicarbonate or blowing off carbon dioxide. Mark on each which value is the disturbance and which is the response. One line: say why compensation never carries pH past 7.4 to the other side."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to determine whether an acid base disorder is uncompensated, partially compensated, or fully compensated and name the system doing the compensating."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Compensation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-lab-abg-interpretation","name":"Arterial blood gas interpretation","can":"Interpret a set of arterial blood gas values and match the result to a plausible clinical cause.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Two patients both have a PCO2 of 60 mmHg. One has a pH of 7.24 and the other a pH of 7.38. What accounts for the difference?\nA) The first has a higher PO2, which acidifies the blood\nB) The first has a larger dead space in the lung\nC) The second has a lower rate of carbon dioxide production\nD) The second has had time for the kidney to raise bicarbonate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The second has had time for the kidney to raise bicarbonate","why":"pH is set by the ratio of bicarbonate to dissolved carbon dioxide, so with PCO2 identical the only way to hold pH near normal is a higher bicarbonate, which takes two to three days of renal compensation to build. Carbon dioxide production is not the issue, since PCO2 is already fixed at the same value in both. PO2 does not enter the Henderson Hasselbalch relationship and does not acidify blood. Dead space would change PCO2 itself rather than the pH at a given PCO2."},{"t":"mc","q":"A blood gas shows pH 7.33, PCO2 55 mmHg, HCO3- 28 mEq/L. Which cause is most plausible?\nA) Two days of vomiting gastric contents\nB) Anxiety with rapid and deep breathing\nC) Long standing airflow obstruction\nD) Three days of profuse watery diarrhea\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Long standing airflow obstruction","why":"The acidemic pH with a high PCO2 makes this a respiratory acidosis, and the raised bicarbonate shows the kidney has had time to begin compensating, which fits long standing airflow obstruction. Anxious hyperventilation lowers PCO2 and would give the opposite picture. Diarrhea loses bicarbonate and produces a metabolic acidosis with a low bicarbonate. Vomiting loses acid and produces a metabolic alkalosis with a high pH."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has had watery diarrhea for three days. Which set of arterial values fits best?\nA) pH 7.32, PCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 30 mEq/L\nB) pH 7.46, PCO2 28 mmHg, HCO3- 19 mEq/L\nC) pH 7.50, PCO2 48 mmHg, HCO3- 36 mEq/L\nD) pH 7.28, PCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 14 mEq/L\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) pH 7.28, PCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 14 mEq/L","why":"Losing bicarbonate rich intestinal fluid lowers plasma bicarbonate and pH, and the low PCO2 shows respiratory compensation already underway, so a low pH with low bicarbonate and low PCO2 is the expected pattern. A pH of 7.50 with a bicarbonate of 36 mEq/L is a metabolic alkalosis, which is what vomiting produces, not diarrhea. A PCO2 of 60 mmHg makes a set a respiratory acidosis, which needs a lung problem rather than a gut one. A pH of 7.46 with a PCO2 of 28 mmHg is a respiratory alkalosis, which diarrhea does not cause."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A 68 year old woman has had profuse watery diarrhea for four days. Blood gas: pH 7.30 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 30 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 14 mEq/L (22 to 26). Plasma potassium is 3.0 mmol/L (3.5 to 5.0). She is breathing 26 times per minute.\nWhich mechanism explains her low PCO2?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"The extra H+ in her plasma stimulates the carotid body chemoreceptors, which raise ventilation above what her carbon dioxide production requires","why":"Her diarrhea took bicarbonate with it and left a metabolic acidosis. The peripheral chemoreceptors respond to arterial H+ and drive the medullary respiratory centers, so she breathes more than her metabolism requires and her PCO2 falls. The low PCO2 is what is holding her pH at 7.30 instead of something lower."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man has a generalized seizure lasting three minutes, during which he does not breathe effectively and all of his skeletal muscles contract violently. Blood drawn right after: pH 7.15 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 55 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 19 mEq/L (22 to 26), lactate 8 mmol/L (0.5 to 2.2).\nWhich two processes together explain these numbers?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Reduced ventilation during the seizure raised PCO2, and intense anaerobic muscle contraction produced lactic acid that consumed bicarbonate","why":"Both abnormal values push pH down, so neither one is compensating for the other. This is a respiratory acidosis from three minutes of poor ventilation stacked on top of a metabolic acidosis from lactic acid. The lactate value confirms the second half. Once he breathes normally the PCO2 will fall within minutes, and the liver will clear the lactate over the next hour or two."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis breathes deeply and rapidly because the acidosis is respiratory in origin.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The deep, rapid breathing is respiratory compensation for a metabolic acidosis, driven by H+ acting on the peripheral chemoreceptors.","why":"Ketoacids consume bicarbonate, so this is a metabolic acidosis. The carotid bodies sense the extra H+ and drive ventilation up, which lowers PCO2 and pulls pH partly back toward normal. The breathing pattern is the response, not the cause. A respiratory acidosis would have a high PCO2 and a patient who is breathing too little, not too much."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient found after an opioid overdose has a blood gas of pH 7.22 (7.35 to 7.45), PCO2 65 mmHg (35 to 45), HCO3- 26 mEq/L (22 to 26). Without notes, explain the chain from the drug to these numbers, and predict how the numbers would change over three days if the hypoventilation continued.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"opioids depress the medullary respiratory centers, so alveolar ventilation falls; carbon dioxide is produced faster than it is removed, so PCO2 rises; carbon dioxide combines with water to form H+ and bicarbonate, and the added H+ lowers pH; this is an acute respiratory acidosis; bicarbonate is normal because the kidney has not had time to respond, so it is uncompensated; over days the kidney secretes more H+ and generates new bicarbonate, so bicarbonate rises above 30 mEq/L; pH climbs back toward the low end of normal while PCO2 stays high","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a decision tree you could use cold, with a blood gas at the top. Branch first on pH, low, high, or in range. Branch next on which of PCO2 and bicarbonate moved the way that would cause that pH. Branch last on whether the other moved to oppose it. End every path in a named disorder and its compensation state, then run one set of numbers down your tree. One line: say what a pH of exactly 7.40 with both other values abnormal means."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw three patients with a short clinical picture each: a severe asthma attack, two days of vomiting, and shock with poor tissue perfusion. Beside each draw pH, PCO2, and bicarbonate as arrows against their normal bands, and mark which value is the primary disturbance. One line: pick the one where you would treat the underlying cause rather than the number, and say what would go wrong if you chased the number instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to interpret a set of arterial blood gas values and match the result to a plausible clinical cause."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Arterial blood gas interpretation. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w13-volume-osmolarity","name":"Volume and osmolarity disturbances","can":"Classify a disturbance as volume depletion or overload and as hypoosmotic, isosmotic, or hyperosmotic and predict the resulting fluid shift between compartments.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"One liter of isotonic saline is infused. Classify the disturbance and predict the fluid shift.\nA) Isosmotic volume expansion, with no shift across the cell membrane\nB) Hyperosmotic volume expansion, with water leaving cells\nC) Isosmotic volume depletion, with no shift across the cell membrane\nD) Hypoosmotic volume expansion, with water entering cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Isosmotic volume expansion, with no shift across the cell membrane","why":"Isotonic saline has the same effective osmolarity as body fluid, so it stays where it is put, expanding the extracellular compartment while osmolarity and cell volume are unchanged. Water would enter cells only if extracellular osmolarity had fallen. Water would leave cells only if extracellular osmolarity had risen. Adding fluid is expansion, so depletion is ruled out at the start."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person drinks two liters of plain water. Classify the disturbance and predict the fluid shift.\nA) Hypoosmotic volume expansion, with water entering cells\nB) Isosmotic volume expansion, with no shift\nC) Hyperosmotic volume expansion, with water leaving cells\nD) Hypoosmotic volume depletion, with water entering cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Hypoosmotic volume expansion, with water entering cells","why":"Pure water adds volume without solute, so extracellular osmolarity falls below intracellular, water crosses into cells, and both compartments end larger and more dilute. An isosmotic result would require the added fluid to carry solute at 290 mOsm/L. Water cannot leave cells when the outside has become more dilute. Drinking adds volume, so this cannot be depletion."},{"t":"mc","q":"One liter of hypertonic saline is infused. Classify the disturbance and predict the fluid shift.\nA) Hyperosmotic volume depletion, with water leaving cells\nB) Hypoosmotic volume expansion, with water entering cells\nC) Isosmotic volume expansion, with no shift\nD) Hyperosmotic volume expansion, with water leaving cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Hyperosmotic volume expansion, with water leaving cells","why":"Concentrated salt raises extracellular osmolarity, so water is pulled out of cells, and the extracellular compartment grows by more than the volume infused while cells shrink. Water moves into cells only when the outside is more dilute. An isosmotic result would need the infusion to match body osmolarity. Total body volume rises here, so this is expansion rather than depletion."},{"t":"mc","q":"An athlete sweats heavily for three hours and drinks nothing. Classify the disturbance and predict the fluid shift.\nA) Hyperosmotic volume depletion, with water leaving cells\nB) Hyperosmotic volume expansion, with water leaving cells\nC) Isosmotic volume depletion, with no shift\nD) Hypoosmotic volume depletion, with water entering cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Hyperosmotic volume depletion, with water leaving cells","why":"Sweat is hypotonic, so more water than salt is lost, extracellular osmolarity climbs while extracellular volume falls, and water is drawn out of cells so both compartments shrink. An isosmotic loss would require sweat to match plasma osmolarity, which it does not. Water cannot enter cells when the outside has become more concentrated. Losing fluid is depletion, not expansion."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient loses two liters of blood through hemorrhage. Classify the disturbance and predict the immediate fluid shift across cell membranes.\nA) Hyperosmotic volume depletion, with water leaving cells\nB) Hypoosmotic volume depletion, with water entering cells\nC) Isosmotic volume expansion, with no shift\nD) Isosmotic volume depletion, with no immediate shift\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Isosmotic volume depletion, with no immediate shift","why":"Whole blood is lost at the osmolarity of body fluid, so the extracellular compartment shrinks while osmolarity stays at 290 mOsm/L and cells are initially untouched. A hyperosmotic result would require losing relatively more water than solute, as in sweating. A hypoosmotic result would require losing relatively more solute than water. Losing volume is depletion, so expansion is ruled out."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A patient has had vomiting and diarrhea for three days and has lost about four liters of fluid. Plasma sodium is 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), plasma osmolarity is 290 mOsm/kg (normal 280 to 295), blood pressure is 90/60 mmHg, and heart rate is 120 beats per minute.\nClassify the disturbance and predict the shift of water between the extracellular and intracellular compartments.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Isosmotic volume depletion; extracellular volume is down but there is no net water shift into or out of cells","why":"The fluid lost from the gut carried salt and water in roughly the same proportion as the extracellular fluid, so what remains has the same osmolarity, as the normal sodium and osmolarity show. With no osmotic gradient, cells neither gain nor lose water. The whole deficit sits in the extracellular compartment, which is why blood pressure is low and heart rate is high."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw the fluid compartments as two boxes sharing a wall, intracellular at about two thirds of body water and extracellular at about one third, with height for volume and width for osmolarity starting near 290 mOsm/L. Redraw the pair for three cases: hemorrhage, heavy sweating without drinking, and swallowing several liters of plain water fast. Show which wall moved and which way water shifted. One line: name the case where cells swell, and the tissue where that is dangerous."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a two by three grid of compartment pictures, volume depletion and volume overload down the side, hypoosmotic, isosmotic, and hyperosmotic across the top. Fill every cell with the two boxes drawn to the right shape, and write a real cause under each. One line: in exactly two cells the extracellular and intracellular volumes move in opposite directions, so name those two and say what drives water the way it goes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a disturbance as volume depletion or overload and as hypoosmotic, isosmotic, or hyperosmotic and predict the resulting fluid shift between compartments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Volume and osmolarity disturbances. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]},{"system":"Integration","comps":[{"id":"w15-integration-case","name":"Multisystem integration case","can":"Given a clinical scenario, trace the disturbance through at least three organ systems and identify the compensatory responses in the order they occur.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient vomits repeatedly for two days, losing gastric HCl. Which arterial values fit that disturbance plus its expected compensation?\nA) pH 7.50, HCO3- 34 mEq/L, PCO2 46 mmHg\nB) pH 7.28, HCO3- 16 mEq/L, PCO2 30 mmHg\nC) pH 7.50, HCO3- 23 mEq/L, PCO2 30 mmHg\nD) pH 7.30, HCO3- 30 mEq/L, PCO2 60 mmHg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) pH 7.50, HCO3- 34 mEq/L, PCO2 46 mmHg","why":"Losing H+ in vomit leaves excess HCO3- behind, so pH and HCO3- both rise, which is metabolic alkalosis. The lung compensates by slowing ventilation and holding on to CO2, so PCO2 climbs above 40 mmHg. Low pH with a low HCO3- and a low PCO2 is metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, the mirror image of this case. High pH with a normal HCO3- and a low PCO2 is a primary respiratory alkalosis. Low pH with a high PCO2 and a high HCO3- is respiratory acidosis with renal compensation."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person loses 700 mL of blood over a few minutes. What happens to carotid sinus baroreceptor firing?\nA) It falls, because the vessel wall is stretched less\nB) It rises, because the heart is beating faster\nC) It rises, because blood shifts centrally and stretches the sinus\nD) It is unchanged, because these endings sense plasma osmolarity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It falls, because the vessel wall is stretched less","why":"Baroreceptors report wall stretch, so a smaller volume at a lower pressure means fewer action potentials traveling up the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves. The medulla reads that drop as low pressure and raises sympathetic outflow, which is why heart rate goes up. Heart rate does not drive baroreceptor firing; the reflex runs the other way, from sensor to effector. Osmolarity is the business of hypothalamic osmoreceptors, not of these endings."},{"t":"mc","q":"During the sympathetic response to hemorrhage, what does venoconstriction accomplish?\nA) It raises total peripheral resistance and so raises afterload\nB) It lowers venous compliance so less blood reaches the right atrium\nC) It shifts stored blood toward the heart and raises preload\nD) It raises capillary hydrostatic pressure and speeds up filtration\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It shifts stored blood toward the heart and raises preload","why":"About two thirds of the blood volume sits in veins, so squeezing them pushes volume centrally, raising venous return, end diastolic volume, and stroke volume through the Frank-Starling mechanism. Raising resistance and afterload is what arteriolar constriction does, a different vessel and a different purpose. Lower venous compliance moves blood toward the heart, not away from it. Capillary hydrostatic pressure actually falls in hemorrhage, which pulls interstitial fluid into the plasma and helps refill it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A frightened person hyperventilates for several minutes. What happens to arterial PCO2 and pH?\nA) PCO2 falls and pH falls  |  B) PCO2 falls and pH rises\nC) PCO2 rises and pH falls  |  D) PCO2 rises and pH rises\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) PCO2 falls and pH rises","why":"Blowing off CO2 faster than metabolism makes it lowers PCO2, which pulls the CO2 and water reaction backward and removes H+, so pH rises. That is respiratory alkalosis. Rising PCO2 with falling pH is the opposite disturbance, respiratory acidosis, and it comes from hypoventilation. When the lung is the source of the problem, PCO2 and pH always move in opposite directions."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A man's heart has been weakened by a heart attack and pumps out only about half the normal fraction of blood with each beat. Over the following month his heart rate rises to 95 beats per minute, his ankles swell, and his urine sodium falls to 10 mmol/L (normal on a regular diet 40 to 220).\nTrace the chain of responses that connects the weak heart to the swollen ankles and the low urine sodium, in the order they occur.\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Low cardiac output unloads the baroreceptors, sympathetic drive raises heart rate and releases renin, angiotensin II and aldosterone plus ADH retain sodium and water, and the expanded volume raises venous pressure and pushes fluid into the tissues","why":"The body reads a weak heart as a low volume state, because the arterial side is underfilled. The baroreflex and the kidney respond as they would to hemorrhage: sympathetic drive, renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone and ADH all rise, and the kidney holds sodium and water. The retained fluid cannot raise cardiac output because the heart cannot pump it, so it backs up on the venous side, raises capillary pressure, and filters out into the tissues."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"After a sudden hemorrhage, ADH and aldosterone restore blood pressure before the baroreceptor reflex has time to act.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. The baroreceptor reflex acts within seconds and is the first line of defense. ADH rises over minutes, and aldosterone driven volume recovery takes hours.","why":"A neural reflex needs only nerve conduction and synaptic delay, so heart rate and vasoconstriction change within a heartbeat or two. Hormones must be released, carried in the blood, and act on their targets, and aldosterone must also change gene expression in tubule cells. Any order that puts a hormone ahead of the reflex has the timing backwards."},{"t":"bd","q":"A patient has vomited repeatedly for three days. Without notes, trace this disturbance through the digestive, cardiovascular, respiratory, and renal systems, in the order the responses appear, and explain why the kidney keeps the urine acidic even though the blood is alkaline.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"loss of gastric HCl removes H+ and leaves bicarbonate behind, producing a metabolic alkalosis; loss of fluid lowers blood volume and pressure, and the baroreflex raises heart rate and constricts vessels within seconds; within minutes the lungs slow ventilation, PCO2 rises, and pH is pulled partly back; low volume raises renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone and ADH over minutes to hours, so the kidney retains sodium and water; aldosterone and sodium reabsorption drive H+ secretion, so the kidney keeps reclaiming bicarbonate and the urine stays acidic; the alkalosis cannot resolve until volume is replaced, because the kidney puts volume ahead of pH","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick a disturbance from this term, not dehydration, and draw it crossing at least three organ systems. Set the page up as a chain of organs rather than a list: the insult, the first system to respond, what it changes, and the next system reacting to that change. Number every arrow in the order things actually happen and mark where a slow step lags. One line: name the point where a compensation creates a second problem, and say what that second problem is."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a person who has lost about a liter of blood, then draw the response across four panels on one page, seconds, minutes, hours, and days. Seconds gets the baroreceptor reflex. Minutes gets fluid shifting from interstitium into the capillaries. Hours gets renin angiotensin aldosterone and ADH acting on the kidney. Days gets erythropoietin and the marrow. One line: say which panel restores blood pressure and which restores oxygen carrying, and why those are not the same problem."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Multisystem integration case. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-exercise-integration","name":"Exercise as an integrated response","can":"Build an integrated account of moderate exercise across the muscular, cardiovascular, respiratory, endocrine, and renal systems.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"At steady state a subject consumes 3.0 L of oxygen per minute with a cardiac output of 20 L/min. If arterial oxygen content is 200 mL per liter, what is the mixed venous oxygen content?\nA) 150 mL of O2 per liter  |  B) 100 mL of O2 per liter\nC) 20 mL of O2 per liter  |  D) 50 mL of O2 per liter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 50 mL of O2 per liter","why":"By the Fick principle the arteriovenous difference is 3000 mL/min divided by 20 L/min, which is 150 mL per liter, so venous content is 200 minus 150, or 50 mL per liter. Choosing 150 mL per liter reports the difference itself rather than what is left in venous blood. 100 mL per liter is close to resting mixed venous blood, where extraction is far lower. Whole body venous blood never falls to 20 mL per liter, since not every tissue extracts at the rate working muscle does."},{"t":"mc","q":"At rest a person has a cardiac output of 5 L/min and consumes 250 mL of oxygen per minute. What is the arteriovenous oxygen difference?\nA) 25 mL of O2 per liter of blood  |  B) 125 mL of O2 per liter of blood\nC) 200 mL of O2 per liter of blood  |  D) 50 mL of O2 per liter of blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) 50 mL of O2 per liter of blood","why":"The Fick relation says oxygen consumption equals cardiac output times the arteriovenous oxygen difference, so 250 mL/min divided by 5 L/min gives 50 mL per liter. 200 mL per liter is the oxygen content of arterial blood, not the amount removed by the tissues. 125 mL per liter is closer to the extraction seen in heavy exercise. 25 mL per liter would mean the tissues took only half of what they actually consume."},{"t":"mc","q":"During upright exercise stroke volume rises. What is the main reason?\nA) Greater afterload from a rising arterial pressure\nB) Shorter filling time as heart rate climbs\nC) Reduced contractility at the higher heart rate\nD) Greater venous return raises end diastolic volume\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Greater venous return raises end diastolic volume","why":"The skeletal muscle pump, deeper breathing and venoconstriction all push blood back to the heart, so preload rises and the Frank-Starling mechanism raises stroke volume, with sympathetic contractility adding to it. Rising afterload opposes ejection and would lower stroke volume if nothing else changed. Filling time does shorten at high rates, which is why stroke volume plateaus, but that limits the rise rather than causing it. Contractility increases during exercise rather than falling."},{"t":"app","q":"Case: A runner is measured during a steady 10 kilometer race. Her heart rate is 165 per minute (rest 60), cardiac output is 20 liters per minute (rest 5), and mean arterial pressure is 105 mmHg (rest 88). Her core temperature is 38.8 degrees Celsius (101.8 degrees Fahrenheit) and her skin is flushed.\nHer cardiac output has risen fourfold, but her mean arterial pressure has risen only modestly. What explains that?\nDraw the mechanism and explain your reasoning out loud.","ans":"Total peripheral resistance has fallen, because arterioles in working muscle and in the skin have dilated","why":"Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output times total peripheral resistance. Output went up four times, so resistance must have fallen to roughly a quarter of its resting value for pressure to rise only a little. Muscle arterioles are dilated by local metabolites, and skin arterioles are dilated to dump heat, which is why she is flushed. Those two huge beds pull resistance down even though the gut and kidney are constricted."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"During moderate exercise, arterial PO2 falls sharply, and that fall is the main signal that drives ventilation up.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. Arterial PO2 and PCO2 stay close to normal in moderate exercise. Ventilation is driven up by central command from the motor cortex and by feedback from muscle receptors, not by a fall in arterial oxygen.","why":"If ventilation waited for arterial gases to change, it could not keep up with the tissues. Instead the motor cortex signals the respiratory centers at the same time it signals the muscles, and mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors in the working muscle add to the drive. The result is ventilation that rises in step with metabolism, so arterial PO2 and PCO2 barely move. A large fall in PO2 would only occur in someone with lung disease."},{"t":"bd","q":"Without notes, explain how cardiac output and the distribution of blood flow change in the first minute of moderate exercise, and name the signal that triggers each change.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"central command from the motor cortex reaches the medulla, withdrawing vagal tone and raising sympathetic output; heart rate rises within seconds, and contractility and stroke volume rise with sympathetic drive; the skeletal muscle pump and respiratory pump raise venous return, supporting stroke volume; local metabolites (adenosine, H+, K+, low PO2) dilate arterioles in working muscle; sympathetic vasoconstriction narrows gut, kidney and resting muscle arterioles, diverting flow; total peripheral resistance falls, so mean arterial pressure rises only modestly despite the large rise in cardiac output","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw one time axis from rest through twenty minutes of moderate exercise and stack five curves on it: heart rate, cardiac output, ventilation, muscle blood flow, and renal blood flow. Get the directions and the relative sizes right, including the one that falls. Mark where each begins to move. One line: total peripheral resistance drops while mean arterial pressure rises, so say how both of those can be true at the same time."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw a working muscle in the middle of the page with five systems running into it as spokes: cardiovascular delivering oxygen, respiratory loading it, endocrine supplying fuel with the hormones named, renal conserving salt and water, and the muscle itself putting out heat and carbon dioxide. On every spoke draw the actual molecule moving and its direction. One line: pick the system that sets the limit on how long you can hold this intensity, and say what runs out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to build an integrated account of moderate exercise across the muscular, cardiovascular, respiratory, endocrine, and renal systems."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Exercise as an integrated response. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]},{"id":"w15-drawing-synthesis","name":"Drawing based synthesis check","can":"Draw and annotate a homeostatic pathway from memory and explain each step aloud or in writing without notes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In a drawing of a negative feedback loop, what is the correct order of the labeled boxes?\nA) Stimulus, effector, integrating center, sensor, response\nB) Sensor, stimulus, effector, integrating center, response\nC) Stimulus, sensor, integrating center, effector, response\nD) Stimulus, integrating center, sensor, effector, response\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Stimulus, sensor, integrating center, effector, response","why":"The pathway runs from the change itself, to a sensor that detects it, along an afferent path to an integrating center that compares the value with a set point, then along an efferent path to an effector that produces the response. Putting the effector before the integrating center takes away the step where the decision is made. Starting with the sensor leaves it nothing to detect. The sensor must come before the integrating center, because the center can only act on what the sensor sends it."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a negative feedback diagram, what must the arrow returning from the response to the stimulus show?\nA) That the response reduces the original stimulus\nB) That the response increases the original stimulus\nC) That the response leaves the original stimulus unchanged\nD) That the sensor reports the response to the integrating center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) That the response reduces the original stimulus","why":"Negative feedback means the output feeds back to shut off its own trigger, so the return arrow has to carry a minus sign or the word decreases. An arrow that increases the stimulus describes positive feedback, as in oxytocin during labor. An arrow with no effect describes an open loop, which cannot regulate anything. The path from sensor to integrating center is the afferent limb, drawn earlier in the loop rather than at its close."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a drawn homeostatic loop, where does the set point belong?\nA) With the sensor, as the value it is able to detect\nB) With the effector, as the level of output it can produce\nC) With the integrating center, as the value it compares against\nD) With the response, as the value the variable finally reaches\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) With the integrating center, as the value it compares against","why":"The set point is a reference held by the integrating center, and the error signal is the difference between the sensed value and that reference. Sensors report values; they do not hold references to compare against. Effectors act on commands, and their maximum output has nothing to do with the set point. The value actually reached is the regulated variable, which oscillates around the set point rather than defining it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student is drawing the baroreceptor reflex. What belongs in the sensor box?\nA) The cardiovascular center in the medulla oblongata\nB) The SA node and the ventricular myocardium\nC) The granular cells of the afferent arteriole\nD) Stretch receptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Stretch receptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch","why":"The reflex begins at stretch sensitive nerve endings in the walls of the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which report arterial pressure moment to moment. The medullary cardiovascular center is the integrating center in this loop, not the sensor. The SA node and the myocardium are effectors, changing rate and force on command. Granular cells belong to the renal pathway for long term volume regulation, a slower loop that deserves its own diagram."},{"t":"tf","q":"True or false? \"In a correct drawing of a homeostatic negative feedback loop, the arrow from the effector back to the regulated variable points in the same direction as the original disturbance.\"\nSay which it is. If it is false, fix it. Then draw and explain why.","ans":"False. In negative feedback the effector arrow points in the opposite direction from the disturbance, pushing the variable back toward its set point.","why":"A loop where the response reinforces the disturbance is positive feedback, and it drives the variable further away until something outside the loop stops it. Homeostasis depends on the response opposing the change. When you draw a loop, check the last arrow: if a rise leads to responses that cause a fall, the loop is negative and will hold the variable steady."},{"t":"bd","q":"From memory, draw and label the pathway that returns plasma osmolarity to normal after you drink a liter of water. Then explain each step in writing without notes.\nUse a drawing to help you explain.","ans":"absorbed water lowers plasma osmolarity; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the fall; ADH release from the posterior pituitary is reduced; fewer aquaporin channels are inserted in the collecting duct, so water reabsorption falls; a large volume of dilute urine is produced; plasma osmolarity rises back to the set point, and the loop is negative feedback","why":""},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick any homeostatic loop from this term that you have not already drawn twice, close everything, and draw it complete from memory: stimulus, receptor, afferent path, integrating center, efferent path, effector, response, and the arrow closing back onto the stimulus. Name the real anatomical structure at every position rather than a generic box. One line: mark the single step you were least certain about while drawing, and say what you would check to settle it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Pick one regulated variable, your choice, and draw it pushed off its set point and then corrected twice on the same time axis, once by a fast neural reflex and once by a slow hormonal loop. Show the two responses starting at different times and correcting by different amounts. Label the receptor, center, and effector for each. One line: say what the fast loop buys you given that the slow one was going to fix it anyway."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to draw and annotate a homeostatic pathway from memory and explain each step aloud or in writing without notes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Predict a change. Draw a model of this topic: Drawing based synthesis check. Then change one part of it (block it, raise it, or lower it) and show what happens next. Explain why."}]}]}]};
